(window.ASH_QB=window.ASH_QB||{})["pharmacology"]=[
 {"q":"Drug of choice for anaphylaxis is:","o":["Hydrocortisone","Adrenaline (IM)","Chlorpheniramine","Salbutamol"],"a":1,"e":"IM adrenaline is first-line.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which antitubercular drug causes optic neuritis?","o":["Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"a":2,"e":"Ethambutol: dose-related optic neuritis, red-green colour loss.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Fundoscopic evaluation in an HIV patient showed CMV retinitis. He was started on intravenous foscarnet, a highly ionized drug. Which of the following statements is true about the drug?","o":["Excreted mainly by the kidneys","Can cross the blood brain barrier easily","Well absorbed from the intestine","Accumulates in the cellular lipids"],"a":0,"e":"Highly ionized drugs like foscarnet are mainly excreted by the kidneys.\n\nThey are not reabsorbed from the renal tubules and are hence excreted unchanged by glomerular filtration. Dose reduction is necessary in renal impairment to avoid the accumulation of the drug and associated toxicity.\n\nThe non-ionized forms of weak acids and bases undergo tubular reabsorption.\n\nOption B: The blood-brain barrier limits the entry of ionized, non-lipid soluble drugs.\n\nOption C: A drug is more readily absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract when it is in its unionized, more lipophilic form.\n\nOption D: As the ionized form has low lipid solubility, it cannot penetrate cell membranes easily.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Alkalinization of the urine is done in case of toxicity with which of the following substances?","o":["Barbiturate","Amphetamine","Heparin","Morphine"],"a":0,"e":"Alkalinization of the urine is done in barbiturate poisoning. When the urine is alkaline, weakly acidic drugs like barbiturates are present in highly ionized form and excreted more rapidly.\n\nWhen the tubular urine is made more alkaline, weak acids like barbiturates become highly ionized and are excreted to a greater extent. Similarly, acidification of urine increases the excretion of weak bases. This property is used in the management of drug overdose.\n\nDrug\nTreatment of overdose\n\nWeak acids\n(e.g., phenobarbital, aspirin)\nAlkalinization of urine with sodium bicarbonate\n\nWeak bases\n(e.g., amphetamine)\nAcidification of urine with ammonium chloride","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with nephrotic syndrome, drug toxicity is least likely with which of the following drugs?","o":["Efavirenz","Morphine","Diazepam","Valproate"],"a":1,"e":"In a patient with nephrotic syndrome, drug toxicity is least likely with morphine.\n\nAcidic drugs generally bind to plasma albumin while basic drugs bind to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. Nephrotic syndrome reduces plasma albumin levels, therefore there is a decrease in the protein-bound drug fraction and an increase in the free form, resulting in drug toxicity.\n\nThis is less likely with drugs having low plasma protein binding such as morphine (only around 35%).\n\nEfavirenz, diazepam, and valproate have a very high plasma protein binding of >90% and predominantly bind to albumin and are associated with toxicity in hypoalbuminemia.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"What does time for peak plasma concentration (Tmax) indicate?","o":["Rate of elimination","Rate of absorption","Duration of effect","Intensity of effect"],"a":1,"e":"The time for peak plasma concentration (Tmax) indicates the rate at which the drug is absorbed from the administered site. It depends on both the administered site and the drug formulation.\n\nThis is used to determine the timing of the administration of hypnotics with respect to sleep timings. A hypnotic with a higher rate of absorption might be preferred to induce sleep at a shorter latency as the target concentration can be achieved earlier.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"The pharmacokinetics of an experimental drug is being studied. If its apparent volume of distribution is 6L/kg, which of the following is true regarding its distribution?","o":["Highly bound to plasma proteins","Confined to vascular compartment","Sequestered in body tissues","Distributed in extracellular fluid volume"],"a":2,"e":"In the present case, the volume of distribution of the drug is 6L/kg (420 L in a normal adult with reference weight 70kg) which is very high when compared to the total body water of 42L. Therefore, it can be implied that the drug is having high sequestration in tissues.\n\nThe apparent volume of distribution (Vd) is an imaginary volume throughout which the drug is distributed if the plasma concentration remains constant throughout.\n\nVd = Dose administered IV/ plasma concentration\n\nThe difference in the volume of distribution based on drug type:\n\n• Low Vd\n\n• Restricted to the intravascular compartment\n\n• Seen in drugs with high molecular weight (e.g., heparin) or high protein binding (e.g., warfarin - 9.8L)\n\n• Medium Vd\n\n• Lipid insoluble drugs (e.g., gentamicin - 20L) do not enter the cells and are distributed in the extracellular fluid volume\n\n• Small water-soluble molecules (e.g., ethanol - 42L) are distributed in the total body water\n\n• High Vd\n\n• Sequestered and accumulated in the tissues\n\n• Small lipophilic molecules (e.g., chloroquine - 13000L)","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about first-order kinetics?","o":["A constant amount of drug is eliminated in unit time","The half life increases with an increase in dose","The rate of elimination is constant","The rate of elimination is proportional to the plasma concentration"],"a":3,"e":"The rate of elimination is proportional to plasma concentration in first-order kinetics.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"You would consider administering a loading dose for a patient if the drug has","o":["High clearance","High volume of distribution","Long plasma half life","Short plasma half life"],"a":1,"e":"The volume of distribution of the drug determines the loading dose of the drug.\n\nLoading dose is a relatively large dose of the drug given initially to rapidly achieve the target plasma concentration. It is given by the formula\n\nLoading Dose = Vol. of Distribution x Desired Steady-state plasma Concentration.\n\nThe bioavailability determines the route of administration. Though bioavailability may affect the dose necessary, it will not be the primary factor considered during the calculation of the loading dose.\n\nOption A: Clearance is used to determine the maintenance dose.\nMaintenance dose = Clearance x Desired Steady-state plasma concentration.\n\nOptions C and D: Plasma half-life primarily determines the frequency of dosing.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs approach zero-order kinetics at higher therapeutic levels?","o":["Penicillin","Phenytoin","Valproate","Carbamazepine"],"a":1,"e":"Phenytoin kinetics changes from first-order to zero-order kinetics at higher therapeutic levels.\n\nTrue zero-order kinetics is shown only by ethanol.\n\nSome drugs have first-order kinetics at low plasma concentrations while at higher plasma concentration (within therapeutic range), the mechanism for elimination gets saturated and they change over to zero-order kinetics.\n\nDrugs that show early saturation kinetics include:\n\n• Warfarin\n\n• Aspirin\n\n• Theophylline\n\n• Verapamil\n\n• Fluoxetine\n\n• Phenytoin\n\nSome drugs show zero-order kinetics at much higher (non-therapeutic / toxic) levels.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"In the metabolism of xenobiotics, all of the following are phase 1 reactions except?","o":["Oxidation","Reduction","Acetylation","Hydrolysis"],"a":2,"e":"Acetylation is a phase 2 reaction.\n\nMetabolizing enzymes in hepatic cells:\n\n• Microsomal enzymes - present in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n\n• Non-microsomal enzymes - present in mitochondria and cytoplasm","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Sulphonamides undergo which of the following metabolic reactions?","o":["Acetylation","Methylation","Hydroxylation","Oxidation"],"a":0,"e":"Sulphonamides undergo acetylation. It is a phase II metabolic reaction where compounds having amino or hydrazine residues are conjugated with the help of acetyl coenzyme-A.\n\nDrugs that undergo acetylation are:\n\n• Sulphonamides\n\n• Hydralazine\n\n• Isoniazid\n\n• PAS\n\n• Dapsone\n\n• Clonazepam\n\n• Procainamide","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"All of the following can cause lupus-like syndromes except:","o":["Isoniazid","Penicillin","Hydralazine","Sulphonamide"],"a":1,"e":"Penicillin is not associated with a lupus-like syndrome.\n\nDrug-induced lupus is a consequence of genetic polymorphism of N-acetyl transferase 2 (NAT2) enzyme which results in slow acetylators and fast acetylators:\n\n• Slow acetylators of isoniazid, hydralazine, and sulphonamides can have increased toxicity that includes lupus-like syndrome\n\n• Fast acetylators can have decreased efficacy as a result of faster metabolism of the active drug\n\nDrugs that may result in lupus-like syndrome (The SHIP MeChaniQ has lupus-like syndrome) are:\n\n• Sulphonamides/ Sulfasalazine\n\n• Hydralazine\n\n• Isoniazid\n\n• Procainamide, Para-aminosalicylic acid\n\n• Methyldopa\n\n• Chlorpromazine\n\n• Quinidine","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A patient on long-term anticoagulation therapy with warfarin would need an increased dose when coadministered with which of the following drugs?","o":["Captopril","Erythromycin","Rifampicin","Cimetidine"],"a":2,"e":"A patient on long-term anticoagulation therapy with warfarin would need an increased dose when coadministered with rifampicin. This is because rifampicin is a potent microsomal enzyme inducer and can decrease the effect of warfarin.\n\nRifampicin induces CYP3A and CYP2D6 enzymes.\n\nEnzyme inducers:\n\n• CYP3A\n\n• Carbamazepine\n\n• Phenobarbitone (CYP2B1)\n\n• Rifampicin (CYP2D6)\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Glucocorticoids\n\n• CYP2E1\n\n• Isoniazid\n\n• Chronic alcoholism\n\nNote: Isoniazid is an inducer for CYP2E1 but an inhibitor for CYP3A4","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Contraceptive failure while on oral contraceptive pills is unlikely to occur with which of the following drugs?","o":["Valproate","Rifampicin","Phenytoin","Phenobarbitone"],"a":0,"e":"Contraceptive failure in patients on the oral contraceptive pill is unlikely to occur with valproate. It is an enzyme inhibitor that inhibits the degradation of OCPs and increases their duration of action.\n\nRifampicin, phenytoin, and phenobarbitone are enzyme inducers and can cause increased metabolism of OCPs leading to contraceptive failure.\n\nEnzyme inducers\nEnzyme inhibitors\n\nMnemonic: CRAP GPS\n\n• Carbamezepine\n\n• Rifampicin\n\n• Alcohol (chronic)\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Griseofulvin\n\n• Phenobarbitone\n\n• Sulfonylureas\n\nMnemonic: RED VICK\n\n• Ritonavir\n\n• Erythromycin\n\n• Diltiazem\n\n• Verapamil, Valproate\n\n• Itraconazole\n\n• Clarithromycin, Cimetidine\n\n• Ketoconazole","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following microsomal enzymes is important for the activation of procarcinogens?","o":["CYP3A4","CYP2C8/9","CYP2C19","CYP1A1/2"],"a":3,"e":"CYP1A1/2 enzymes are important in the activation of procarcinogens.\n\nThey are involved in the metabolism of few drugs like theophylline, caffeine, paracetamol, and carbamazepine.\n\nInducers of CYP1A1/2:\n\n• Polycyclic hydrocarbons\n\n• Cigarette smoking\n\n• Charbroiled meat\n\n• Rifampicin\n\n• Carbamazepine","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Cytochrome enzymes are not involved in which of the following processes?","o":["Oxidation","Production of bile acids","Synthesis of steroids","Acetylation"],"a":3,"e":"Cytochrome enzymes are not involved in acetylation. Cytochrome enzymes such as CYP450 are involved in phase 1 reactions such as oxidation.\n\nThey are also involved in bile acid production from cholesterol; synthesis of steroids and fatty acid signaling molecules like epoxyeicosatrienoic acids.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A patient is brought to the emergency in an unresponsive state. There is an alleged history of consumption of multiple prescription tablets in an attempt to commit suicide. After the drug was conclusively identified, the doctor recommends dialysis for definitive management. The drug most likely is:","o":["Digoxin","Verapamil","Diazepam","Lithium"],"a":3,"e":"The drug most likely is lithium as it is the only one amongst the given drugs that can be removed by dialysis. It is the treatment of choice in case of severe lithium toxicity (>4mEq/L).\n\nThe distribution of lithium is mainly restricted to the vascular compartment and it is not bound to any plasma proteins. Dialysis is effective in removing the drugs if they are sufficiently present in the free form in the plasma. Therefore, lithium can be removed by dialysis during an overdose.\n\nDrugs not suitable for dialysis are those with:\n\n• High volume of distribution - digoxin, verapamil, and organophosphates\n\n• High level of plasma protein binding - diazepam","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a prodrug?","o":["Enalapril","Imipramine","Sulfasalazine","Cyclophosphamide"],"a":1,"e":"Imipramine is not a prodrug. It is a tricyclic antidepressant and has therapeutic activity. It also forms an active metabolite, desipramine.\n\nProdrugs are inactive precursors of active forms. They require metabolic alteration in the body to become active.\n\nExamples of prodrugs:\n\n• Prednisone\n\n• Levodopa\n\n• ACE inhibitors (except lisinopril and captopril)\n\n• Sulfasalazine\n\n• Methyldopa\n\n• Cyclophosphamide\n\n• Clopidogrel","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"You prescribe an antibiotic course for a patient following cesarean section. What advice would you give regarding breastfeeding?","o":["No advice as most of drug are not present in breast milk","Avoid breastfeeding during the antibiotic course","Medications to be taken 3-4 hours before next feeding","She can take medications anytime"],"a":2,"e":"If the lactating mother needs to take medications that are deemed safe, the dosing optimally should be 30-60 minutes after nursing and at least 3-4 hours before the next feeding.\n\nA minimum interval of 3-4 hours allows time for partial clearance of the drug. This ensures the infants are not exposed to the peak maternal plasma concentration that may translate to higher levels secreted into milk.\n\nOption A: Most drugs administered are detected in breast milk but most of them are excreted in very small quantities that do not cause any effect in the infants.\n\nOption B: Breastfeeding should not be avoided altogether for all medications. Intake of cytotoxic agents and immunomodulatory agents are contraindications for breastfeeding.\n\nOption D: The timing of medication should preferably be at an interval where the infants are not exposed to higher maternal plasma concentrations.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old patient is on omeprazole for gastroesophageal reflux disease. She is now to be started on dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and clopidogrel. You decide to switch to pantoprazole because of the action of omeprazole on which enzyme?","o":["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP2C20"],"a":2,"e":"Omeprazole is replaced with another proton pump inhibitor (PPI) during coadministration with clopidogrel because of the action on CYP2C19.\n\nClopidogrel is a prodrug that is activated to its active metabolite by the liver enzyme CYP2C19. Apart from CYP2C19, CYP3A4, and CYP2C9 are also involved to a certain extent.\n\nOmeprazole serves as both substrate and inhibitor for CYP2C19. It may inhibit the activation of clopidogrel and decrease its antiplatelet action. Hence, they are not prescribed together.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Subfamily of CYP3A4 is _______","o":["CYP","A","3","4"],"a":1,"e":"The sub-family of CYP3A4 is A.\n\nIn CYP enzymes nomenclature, the root CYP is followed by a number designating the family, a letter denoting the subfamily and another number designating the gene. Therefore, in CYP3A4:\n\n• 3 - family\n\n• A - subfamily A\n\n• 4 - individual gene coding for one specific isoenzyme\n\nThere are numerous CYP isoenzymes in humans. Among them, CYP3A4 is the most abundant and carries out the metabolism of nearly 50% of clinically used drugs.","t":"Pharmacokinetics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Neurotransmitters like noradrenaline, adrenaline, and dopamine act on:","o":["Single-pass transmembrane receptors","Nuclear receptors","Seven-pass transmembrane receptors","Ligand-gated receptors"],"a":2,"e":"Noradrenaline, adrenaline, and dopamine act through a bundle of seven alpha-helicase trans-membrane receptors which are the G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs).","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4c6a2d30d5c46dc9b527577f6591a2ax1280x1087.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about G-protein-coupled receptors?","o":["G proteins bind to hormones on the cell surface.","All the three subunits alpha, beta and gamma should bind to each other for G proteins to act.","G proteins act as inhibitory and excitatory because of differences in the alpha subunit.","G protein is bound to GTP in resting state."],"a":2,"e":"G proteins act as inhibitory and excitatory because of differences in the alpha subunit.\n\n• Excitatory - Gs and Gq\n\n• Inhibitory - Gi\n\nIn the resting-state:\n\n• G protein is a heterotrimer consisting of alpha, beta, and gamma subunits. It forms a complex with G-protein-coupled receptors at the intracellular domain. The extracellular domain of GPCR has the binding domain for hormones. G protein subunits do not directly bind them.\n\n• The alpha subunit is bound to GDP.\n\nBinding of ligand results in activation of GPCR, following which:\n\n• Alpha subunit undergoes a conformational change\n\n• Release of GDP and binding of GTP occurs\n\n• GTP bound alpha subunit dissociates from beta-gamma subunit\n\n• Both alpha subunit and beta-gamma subunit bind to and regulate effectors only when they are separated.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b43b24ca1f7244c8b612a11f90812f45x1279x1346.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an ion channel receptor?","o":["Nicotinic cholinergic receptor","Muscarinic cholinergic receptor","Beta adrenergic receptors","Opioid receptors"],"a":0,"e":"Nicotinic cholinergic receptors (both Nm and Nn) are of ligand-gated ion channel type.\n\nThe binding of ligand alters the permeability of ion channels directly and brings about biological action.\n\nMuscarinic cholinergic, beta-adrenergic, and opioid receptors are G protein-coupled receptors.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33cfea1bc6f2417cbc712880cb653c8ex1280x1500.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9c1a274c2964d6f80153a48471ace73x1279x1086.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"All of the following agents act by intracellular receptors except ______","o":["Thyroid hormones","Vitamin D","Insulin","Steroid"],"a":2,"e":"Insulin receptors belong to the class of trans-membrane enzyme-linked receptors.\n\nTransmembrane enzyme-linked receptors consist of a large extracellular ligand-binding domain. It is connected to an intracellular subunit having enzymatic property through a single transmembrane helical peptide chain. The enzyme on the cytosolic side is usually a protein kinase (tyrosine) or membrane-bound GC (guanylyl cyclase).\n\nPhysiological ligands for transmembrane enzyme-linked receptors are mostly growth factors and chemokines. Other receptors belonging to this class include:\n\n• Receptor tyrosine kinases - Growth factors (PDGF, EGF, VEGF); insulin\n\n• Membrane-bound GC - Natriuretic peptides\n\n• Receptor serine-threonine kinases - TGF-β\n\nThyroid hormones, vitamin D, and steroids act via intracellular receptors.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not a second messenger?","o":["Cyclic AMP","Guanylyl cyclase","Diacylglycerol","Inositol triphosphate"],"a":1,"e":"Guanylyl cyclase is an enzyme that converts GTP to cGMP. It is not a second messenger.\n\nSecond messengers are:\n\n• Diacylglycerol\n\n• Inositol triphosphate\n\n• Cyclic AMP\n\n• Cyclic GMP\n\n• Calcium\n\n• Nitric oxide","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following hormones act through the receptor shown in the image below?","o":["Insulin","Somatostatin","Cholecystokinin","Vasopressin (V1 receptor)"],"a":0,"e":"The receptor in the given image uses tyrosine kinase signaling. Insulin acts through this receptor to exert its functions.\n\nOther hormones acting via tyrosine kinase signaling:\n\n• Growth hormone\n\n• Growth factors like VEGF, PDGF, FGF, NGF\n\n• Prolactin\n\n• Leptin","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/137bbde364aa4bcfbec1d567af3f5776.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A patient with congestive heart failure is being treated with an intravenous infusion of nesiritide. Which of the following receptors does this drug act through?","o":["G-protein coupled receptors","Tyrosine kinase receptors","JAK-STAT receptors","Ligand-gated guanylyl cyclase receptor"],"a":3,"e":"Nesiritide is recombinant B-type natriuretic peptide. Natriuretic peptide receptors are transmembrane ligand-gated guanylyl cyclase receptors.\n\nNatriuretic peptides:\n\n• Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP): released from atrial storage granules in response to an increase in intravascular volume. It decreases blood pressure.\n\n• Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP): released from ventricular tissue in response to volume overload. Along with a decrease in blood pressure, it is known to reduce cardiac hypertrophy and fibrosis.\n\n• C- type natriuretic peptide (CNP): synthesized in the brain and endothelial tissues and functions to stimulate long bone growth.\n\nANP and BNP act on the NPR-A receptor.\n\nCNP acts on the NPR-B receptor.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"JAK/STAT is a signal transducer for which of the following receptors?","o":["G protein coupled receptors","Ion channels","Transmembrane receptors","Nuclear receptors"],"a":2,"e":"JAK/STAT is a signal transducer for transmembrane receptors lacking intrinsic enzymatic activity.\n\nInterleukin and other cytokines act through such receptors.\n\nOn binding of the ligand to such receptors:\n\n• Receptor dimerization occurs\n\n• Increase in affinity for cytosolic JAK (Janus kinase)\n\n• Activation of JAK results in phosphorylation of tyrosine residues of the receptor\n\n• Binding of signal transducer and activator of transcription (STAT) and subsequent phosphorylation of STAT\n\n• STAT dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus to regulate gene expression, resulting in a biological response","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80e08aee5f19404abcb79703932759f4x1280x2227.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Adrenaline is used to treat histamine-induced bronchoconstriction in a patient with brittle asthma. What is this type of antagonism called?","o":["Physiological antagonism","Chemical antagonism","Competitive antagonism","Uncompetitive antagonism"],"a":0,"e":"Adrenaline and histamine are physiological antagonists.\n\nAdrenaline and histamine act on bronchial muscles. Histamine acts on H1 receptors and produces bronchoconstriction. Adrenaline acts on β2 receptors and produces bronchodilation. Two drugs acting on different receptors and resulting in opposite responses means having pharmacological effects in the opposite direction. This is called physiological antagonism or functional antagonism.\n\nOption B: Chemical antagonism refers to a reaction between two chemicals to neutralize their effects, such as is seen with chelation therapy.\n\nOption C: Competitive antagonism is a type of receptor antagonism that occurs when the agonist and antagonist compete for the same binding site on the receptor.\n\nOption D: In uncompetitive inhibition, inhibitors form a complex with an intermediate complex of the substrate and transporter. This inhibits subsequent translocation.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Letrozole, a competitive inhibitor of the enzyme aromatase is prescribed to a patient with breast cancer. Which of the following is true about this type of antagonism?","o":["Antagonism cannot be completely reversed by increased dose of the agonist","An agonist cannot displace an antagonist from the receptor","Agonists and antagonists bind to the same receptor","Dose response curve shifts to the left in the presence of an antagonist"],"a":2,"e":"In competitive antagonism, the drug and the antagonist compete to bind to the same site of the receptor.\n\nAgonists if present in high concentration, can displace the antagonists from the active site and overcome the competitive antagonism completely.\n\nThe dose-response curve in the case of competitive antagonism shifts to the right.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a true statement regarding inverse agonists?","o":["Binds to the receptor and causes maximum action","Binds to the receptor and causes opposite action to that of agonist","Binds to the receptor and causes no action","Binds to the receptor and causes submaximal action"],"a":1,"e":"Inverse agonists bind to the receptor and cause an action opposite to that of an agonist.\n\nType of drugs\n\nIntrinsic activity\n\nAgonists\n\n1\n\nPartial agonists\n\n0 to 1\n\nCompetitive antagonists\n\n0\n\nInverse agonists\n\n0 to -1\n\nThe ability of the drug to bind with a receptor is called affinity.\n\nThe capacity of a drug to induce a functional change in the receptor is called intrinsic activity or efficacy.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A patient with symptoms of psychosis is prescribed aripiprazole. Which of the following best describes this drug?","o":["No intrinsic activity and high affinity for D2 receptor","High intrinsic activity and no affinity for D2 receptor","Low intrinsic activity and high affinity for D2 receptor","High intrinsic activity and low affinity for D2 receptor"],"a":2,"e":"Aripiprazole is a D2 receptor partial agonist. Partial agonists have submaximal or low intrinsic activity and high affinity.\n\nPartial agonists can behave as an antagonist in the presence of an agonist. Partial agonists block the binding of the agonist to the receptor thereby inhibiting a complete response.\n\nAripiprazole has lower activity than dopamine at D2 receptors. By occupying and blocking the action of dopamine on D2 receptors, it acts similar to an antagonist. It is used in conditions like schizophrenia where dopamine antagonist activity is required.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"What type of antagonism is shown by drugs A and B in the following dose-response curve?","o":["Competitive antagonism","Noncompetitive antagonism","Uncompetitive antagonism","Cannot be determined"],"a":0,"e":"The parallel rightward shift of the agonist dose-response curve (DRC) is seen with competitive antagonism.\n\nIncreasing the dose of the agonist can result in the same maximal response.\n\nIn non-competitive antagonism, there will be flattening of the agonist DRC.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/70f71508943a49488767fd1ac5304e71.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6211b2beb2fc444faebd7f4ab50b4f84x1280x1167.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about the given dose-response curve?","o":["Drug B is the most potent.","Drug B is the most efficacious.","Drug C is more efficacious than drug D.","Drug A is more potent than drug B."],"a":0,"e":"The more the curve is on the left in a dose-response curve (DRC), the greater is its potency. Hence, Drug B is the most potent.\n\nPotency:\n\n• It is defined as the amount of drug needed to produce a given response.\n\n• A highly potent drug evokes a given response at low concentrations and vice-versa.\n\n• In the above image, the potency is Drug B> Drug A > Drug C > Drug D.\n\nEfficacy:\n\n• It is defined as the maximum effect produced by the drug. It reflects the capacity of the drug to activate a receptor and produce the response.\n\n• In the log dose-response curve, the perpendicular height represents the drug efficacy.\n\n• Thus, in the above image, the efficacy is Drug A = Drug C =Drug D > Drug B.\n\n• Drugs having a similar pharmacological effect can have different efficacies e.g furosemide and metolazone are both diuretics but furosemide has greater efficacy than metolazone.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a55588364afa413da3c7cb8df6bfc3f5.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the potency and efficacy of a drug is not true?","o":["In a clinical setup, efficacy is more important than potency","In the log dose response curve, the height of the curve corresponds with efficacy","ED50 of the drug corresponds to the efficacy","Drugs that produce a similar pharmacological effect can have different levels of efficacy"],"a":2,"e":"ED50 is a measure of the drug potency, not efficacy.\n\nPotency refers to the amount of the drug required to produce a certain response. The x coordinates (dose axis) on the log dose-response curve (DRC) of a drug represent the potency of the drug. A right shift in the DRC indicates a lower potency and vice versa.\n\nPotency\n\n• It is defined as the amount of drug needed to produce a given response.\n\n• A highly potent drug evokes a given response at low concentrations and vice-versa.\n\n• The efficacy of a drug is a more decisive factor in the choice of a drug in the clinical setting\n\n• The more the curve shifts towards the left in a dose-response curve (DRC), the greater is its potency.\n\n• Thus, in the above image, Drug A is more potent. The potency is Drug A > Drug B > Drug C > Drug D.\n\nEfficacy\n\n• It is defined as the maximum effect produced by the drug.\n\n• It expresses the effect produced by the receptors when bound by a certain drug.\n\n• It is mainly a factor in choosing the dose of a drug.\n\n• In the log dose-response curve, the perpendicular height represents the drug efficacy.\n\n• Thus, in the above image, efficacy is Drug A >B > C > D.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12fda29ce9db479e99be322d78892dc3x1280x1196.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/202d58649a0544a9ba61ef918f0a6f03x1280x1167.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following shows a U-shaped dose-response curve?","o":["Vitamins","Anti-cancer drugs","Steroids","Chelators"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamins and essential metals show a U-shaped dose-response curve.\n\nAt a low dose, adverse effects are observed because there is a deficiency of these nutrients to maintain homeostasis. As dose increases, homeostasis is achieved, and the bottom of the U-shaped dose-response curve is reached. As the dose increases to surpass the amount required to maintain homeostasis, toxicity can ensue.\n\nThus, adverse effects are seen at both low and high doses.\n\nHockey stick-shaped dose-response curve for toxicants that are also metabolic by-products.\n\nInverted U-shaped dose-response curve for ligands that downregulate their receptors.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cbb79628483a49e5bce96265d68a0673x1280x1154.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0db32ffbadd42dd9c69b75a9398ff60x1280x1420.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23699bc3b29d46ed9c13c1f6a2abef2cx1280x1282.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"What does the area between the dotted lines in this graph indicate?","o":["Median effective dose","Median lethal dose","Therapeutic index","Therapeutic window"],"a":3,"e":"The area between the dotted lines in the graph shows the therapeutic window. It is the range of steady-state concentrations of a drug that provides therapeutic efficacy with minimal toxicity.\n\nThe therapeutic index (TI) describes a relationship between the doses of a drug that causes lethal or toxic effects with the dose that causes therapeutic effects. It is also called the therapeutic ratio.\n\nMathematically, you can calculate TI by following way:\n\nTherapeutic Index = LD50/ED50 (or) Therapeutic Index = TD50/ED50\n\nWhere,\n\n• LD50 (Median Lethal dose) is the minimum amount of drug that causes adverse effects in 50% of the population. LD50 could also be replaced with Toxic dose (TD50).\n\n• ED50 (Median Effective dose) is the quantity of a drug that can produce desired therapeutic effects in 50% of the population.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13fba814599e4468b06f6230f1b67101.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Therapeutic index is calculated with which of the following equations?","o":["ED50/LD50","LD50/ED50","ED50-LD50","ED50 X LD50"],"a":1,"e":"Therapeutic index is calculated with the following equation = LD50/ED50.\n\nTherapeutic index is used to assess the safety of the drug administered.\n\nLD50 (median lethal dose) refers to the dose of the drug at which 50% of the experimental animals die. Larger the LD50, the safer the drug. In humans, TD50 is considered. TD50 refers to the dose of the drug that causes a toxic response in 50% of the population.\n\nED50 (median effective dose) refers to the dose of the drug at which 50% of the experimental animals show the specific or desired effect\n\nClinically, the therapeutic range bound by the dose that produces the minimal necessary therapeutic effect and the dose that produces the maximal acceptable adverse effect is more relevant.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1355a1cb0a574d2e9c6bd3789144d0e4x1280x1420.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Therapeutic drug monitoring is required in all except ______.","o":["Phenytoin","Metformin","Tacrolimus","Cylcosporine"],"a":1,"e":"Metformin does not require therapeutic monitoring because it does not have a narrow therapeutic index and the effect on blood sugar levels can be easily measured.\n\nPhenytoin, tacrolimus, and cyclosporine have the narrow therapeutic index and therapeutic monitoring is necessary to avoid adverse effects.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about pharmacogenetics is correct?","o":["It refers to the effect of drugs on genetic expression","It refers to the genetic basis of variability in drug response","It is not synonymous with pharmacogenomics","Drug metabolism is not a highly heritable trait"],"a":1,"e":"Pharmacogenetics is the study of the genetic basis for variability in drug action.\n\nGenetic differences cause variability in the dose-response curve (DRC) due to alteration in drug action and disposition in the body.\n\nThe term is used interchangeably with pharmacogenomics.\n\nDrug metabolism is highly heritable, as assessed using drug exposure studies in monozygotic versus fraternal twins and from other related subjects.","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old male is diagnosed with G6PD deficiency. Which of the following drugs is not contraindicated in his condition?","o":["Cotrimoxazole","Proguanil","Nalidixic acid","Ceftriaxone"],"a":3,"e":"Ceftriaxone is not contraindicated in G6PD deficient individuals as it does not possess oxidizing property, hence there is no hemolysis.\n\nDrugs having the potential to cause hemolysis in G6PD deficient individuals:\n\n• Anti-malarials - Primaquine, chloroquine, quinine, pyrimethamine, proguanil\n\n• Sulfonamides - Cotrimoxazole\n\n• Fluoroquinolones - Nalidixic acid\n\n• Nitro furan antibacterial - Furazolidone, nitrofurantoin\n\n• Anti-hypertensives - Methyldopa, hydralazine\n\n• Anti-convulsants - Phenytoin\n\n• Anti-arrythmics - Procainamide","t":"Pharmacodynamics","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Pharmacovigilance refers to:","o":["Monitoring of drug safety","Monitoring of unethical trade of drugs","Monitoring of drug potency","Monitoring of drug efficacy"],"a":0,"e":"Pharmacovigilance deals with the monitoring of drug safety.\n\nWHO definition of pharmacovigilance:\n\n\"Science and activities related to the detection, assessment, understanding, and prevention of adverse effects or any other drug-related problems”.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following organizations regulates the clinical trials and approval of investigational new drugs in India?","o":["CPCSEA","CDSCO","FDA","DCGI"],"a":1,"e":"Central Drug Standard Control Organisation (CDSCO) regulates the clinical trials and approval of investigational new drugs in India.\n\nThe proposal for initiating trials, review of clinical trial results, and recommendations for marketing authorization is done through a committee called the Investigational New Drug (IND) Committee formed by CDSCO.\n\nPreclinical trials are performed on animals, and they are regulated by guidelines prescribed by the Committee for the Purpose of the Control and Supervision of Experiments on Animals (CPCSEA) in India.\n\nThe Drugs Controller General of India (DCGI) is the head of the CDSCO, under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare in India.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"You are working as a research associate on clinical trials of a new drug for migraine. What is the dose of the test drug that is usually not exceeded in phase 0 studies?","o":["1000 mcg","1000 mg","100 mcg","100 mg"],"a":2,"e":"The dose of the test drug in phase 0 studies usually does not exceed 100mcg. Phase 0 studies are also called micro-dosing studies.\n\nVery low doses of drugs, usually 1/100th of the estimated human dose or a maximum of 100 mcg is used.\n\nThey are done to obtain pharmacokinetic data in humans at a very early stage of drug development. The micro-dosing stage is not mandatory and not clearly categorized by regulatory authorities yet.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Good clinical practice is not required in which phase of clinical trials?","o":["Preclinical phase","Phase 1 trial","Phase 2 trial","Phase 3 trial"],"a":0,"e":"Good clinical practice is not required in the preclinical phase.\n\nGood clinical practice (GCP) is an international ethical and scientific quality standard for the design, conduct, performance, monitoring, auditing, recording, analyses, and reporting of clinical trials. It is primarily for the conduct of clinical trials in humans.\n\nPreclinical trials are performed on animals and they are regulated by guidelines prescribed by the CPCSEA (Committee for the Purpose of the Control and Supervision of Experiments on Animals) in India. The guidelines are laid down in good laboratory practice (GLP).","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a primary endpoint in phase 1 studies?","o":["Safety","Tolerability","Pharmacokinetic parameters","Efficacy"],"a":3,"e":"Efficacy is not a primary endpoint in phase 1 studies. Phase 1 studies are generally done in healthy volunteers. Efficacy can be a secondary endpoint in some studies where patients are recruited.\n\nThe primary endpoints are:\n\n• Safety\n\n• Tolerability of single/multiple doses\n\n• Pharmacokinetic parameters\n\nThe primary endpoint denotes the purpose of the study for which it is primarily designed. The secondary endpoint denotes the measure of the valuable outcome of the study design.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"You read a news alert that mentions a novel drug has received marketing approval for diabetes mellitus and that it has shown positive results in a pivotal trial. This corresponds to which phase of clinical trials?","o":["Phase I","Phase II","Phase III","Phase IV"],"a":2,"e":"Phase III trials are usually considered pivotal trials and are necessary for the approval of the majority of drugs. They are also called multi-site trials and are done for 3–5 years.\n\nWhen the initial Phase III trials are complete, the sponsor (usually a pharmaceutical company) applies to the FDA for approval to market the drug. This application is called either a New Drug Application (NDA) or a Biologics License Application (BLA).\n\nIn some drugs related to rare diseases or malignancies, phase II studies are considered as pivotal, and the drug may be approved on the basis of phase II studies.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A pharmaceutical company has set up a team of physicians to oversee the Phase IV clinical trial of a new drug for SARS-CoV2 infection, to identify possible drug interactions. Which is the most appropriate statement about this phase of the clinical trial?","o":["Done in a group of 50-500 patients","Done after a drug is approved and marketed","Done for drugs used in rare diseases","Done for formulating guidelines for therapeutic use"],"a":1,"e":"Phase IV study is conducted after the drug is approved and marketed by regulatory authorities. It includes post-marketing surveillance and other studies that complement the earlier findings of the drug.\n\nAfter the drug is marketed for general use, practicing physicians are identified through whom data about the efficacy, acceptability, and adverse effects of the drug are collected on a structured proforma. Patients treated in the normal course form the study population. Therefore the study population is much larger. Uncommon adverse effects or those that occur only after long-term use and unsuspected drug interactions are detected at this stage.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Orphan drugs are drugs that are not used anymore.\n\n2. Orphan drugs are drugs for rare diseases with little commercial value for pharmaceutical companies.\n\n3. Orphan drugs act on orphan receptors.\n\n4. Orphan receptors are receptors that are discovered yet no known ligands are identified.\n\n5. Orphan receptors are receptors that do not serve any purpose.","o":["1, 3 and 4","2 and 4","2, 4 and 5","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"e":"Orphan drugs are for the treatment or prevention of rare diseases with little commercial value for pharmaceutical companies. Orphan receptors are receptors that are discovered yet no known ligands are identified.\n\nThere are two types of drug development. They are:\n\n• Traditional drug development: Initial drug discovery is followed by the characterization of the receptors the drugs are used to act on.\n\n• Contemporary drug development: Due to advances in molecular biology and genome sequencing, receptors are identified initially. The drugs are identified later using chemical screening later resulting in the discovery of orphan receptors.\n\nSpecial incentives are given by the government to pharmaceutical companies for the development and marketing of orphan drugs.\n\nFew examples of orphan drugs are:\n\n• Liposomal amphotericin B\n\n• Digoxin antibody (Digibind)\n\n• Fomepizole","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A pharmacologically inert substance used along with an active ingredient in a formulation is known as the:","o":["Placebo","Nocebo","Excipient","Inducer"],"a":2,"e":"An excipient is a pharmacologically inert substance used along with an active ingredient in a formulation. e.g., starch, lactose, FDKP\n\nRole of excipients:\n\n• Add bulk to the active drug if used in small quantities\n\n• Increase the stability of the formulation\n\n• Mask unpleasant taste\n\nInhaled insulin (Afrezza) consists of 2- to 2.5-μm crystals of the excipient, fumaryl diketopiperazine (FDKP), that provides a large surface area for adsorption of proteins like insulin.\n\nOption A: Placebo is an inert substance given in the form of medication. It is generally not combined with the active ingredient.\n\n• Endorphins may play a role in the placebo effect especially for analgesic action.\n\n• It is used in the controls of clinical trials to elucidate the actual drug effect.\n\n• Not all but only a few patients respond to the placebo.\n\nOption B: Nocebo is the opposite of the placebo effect where the patient experiences a negative psychodynamic effect due to their pessimistic attitude towards the medications or healthcare professionals.\n\nOption C: Inducers refer to agents that increase the expression of cytochrome enzymes involved in the metabolism of drugs.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following are not reasons for the alteration of drug dosage in the elderly?","o":["They have decreasing renal function with age","They have loss of lean body mass","They have increased baroreceptor sensitivity","Their total body water is decreased"],"a":2,"e":"The reason for the alteration of drug dosage in the elderly is decreased baroreceptor sensitivity due to age-related vascular stiffening.\n\nReasons for the alteration of drug dosage in the elderly are:\n\n• Progressive decline of renal function\n\n• Loss of lean body mass and an increased ratio of fat to muscle, leading to an increased volume of distribution of lipophilic drugs\n\n• Decreased body water - causing volume of distribution (Vd) of hydrophilic drugs decreases.\n\n• Slower absorption due to reduced gut motility.\n\n• Plasma albumin decreases with aging which leads to decreased plasma protein binding.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A patient started on an anti-epileptic for epilepsy developed toxic epidermal necrolysis. It was documented as a type A adverse drug reaction. Which of the following is not true regarding this adverse effect?","o":["These reactions occur due to genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a drug.","These reactions can be predicted from the drug’s known pharmacological or toxicological effects","These reactions are generally dose-dependent","These reactions are usually common"],"a":0,"e":"Type A (augmented) adverse drug reactions (ADR) are not based upon the qualitatively abnormal responses to the drug. It is based on the pharmacological properties of the drug.\n\nExamples of type A ADR:\n\n• Side effects\n\n• Toxic effects\n\n• Consequences of drug withdrawal.\n\nThey are common, reversible, and preventable.\n\nIdiosyncrasy refers to the genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a drug.\n\nAdverse drug reactions can be classified into two types:\n\n1.\n\nType A reactions – sometimes referred to as augmented reactions – are dose-dependent and predictable based on the pharmacology of the drug\n\n2.\n\nType B reactions – bizarre reactions – which are idiosyncratic and not predictable on the basis of drug pharmacology. They can be based on the characteristics of patients.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs if taken during pregnancy can lead to this adverse effect?","o":["Tetracycline","Thalidomide","Warfarin","Alcohol"],"a":1,"e":"The baby has features suggestive of phocomelia and it results due to the consumption of thalidomide during pregnancy.\n\nPhocomelia is associated with underdeveloped limbs and absent pelvic bones. Both upper and lower limbs are affected and a part of the limbs may be missing. Fused fingers and missing thumbs are also seen.\n\nThalidomide was originally used for the treatment of pregnancy-associated morning sickness but was withdrawn due to this adverse effect.\n\nIt re-entered the market for the treatment of erythema nodosum leprosum. It also has antiangiogenic and immunomodulatory effects.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90d8d6a4f5ef49289dcafc48bbfd1efd.PNG"],"t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can be used in the first trimester of pregnancy?","o":["ACE inhibitors","Angiotensin receptor blockers","Propylthiouracil","Statins"],"a":2,"e":"Propylthiouracil can be used in the first trimester of pregnancy. It is indicated in the management of hyperthyroidism.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs cannot cross the placenta and can safely be used in a pregnant woman?","o":["Phenytoin","Diazepam","Morphine","Heparin"],"a":3,"e":"Heparin is a large molecule and cannot cross the placenta. Hence, heparin can be used safely in a pregnant woman.\n\nUnfractionated or low molecular weight heparin is the preferred anticoagulant in pregnancy.\n\nOption A: Phenytoin crosses the placenta and can cause fetal hydantoin syndrome.\n\nOptions B and C: Diazepam and morphine are lipophilic molecules and can cross the placenta.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"'Me-too drugs' do not differ significantly in which of the following pharmacological features as compared to the prototypical drug?","o":["Absorption","Metabolism","Mechanism of action","Elimination"],"a":2,"e":"Me-too drugs are chemically related to the prototype or other chemical compounds that have an identical mechanism of action.\n\nVarious statins present in the market can be considered as \"Me-too drugs\". The pharmacokinetic parameters (absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination) vary significantly between them (atorvastatin and rosuvastatin have a very long duration of action), but the core mechanism of action remains the same: HMG CoA reductase inhibition and decrease in cholesterol synthesis.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"As a medical officer in a PHC, you anticipate a rise in infections following the worsening flood in your area. You write a letter to the district collector requesting to provide an additional supply of essential medicines. Which of the following best describes these medicines?","o":["These are drugs needed to treat emergency conditions","These are drugs needed to treat serious diseases","These are drugs that satisfy the priority health needs of the population","These are drugs that have been in use for a long time"],"a":2,"e":"Essential medicines are those medicines that satisfy the priority health care needs of the population.\n\nThey are selected with due regard to:\n\n• Public health relevance\n\n• Evidence on efficacy and safety\n\n• Comparative cost-effectiveness\n\nWHO publishes a model list of essential medicines that provides a framework for individual nations to draw their own national list. The 21st Essential Medicines List, published in April 2019 brings the total to 460 drugs deemed essential for addressing the most important public health needs globally.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"The schedule that deals with prescription drugs under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act is ______.","o":["Schedule P","Schedule H","Schedule Y","Schedule D"],"a":1,"e":"Schedule H deals with prescription drugs. All the drugs listed under schedule H should be provided to the patient only with the prescription from a registered medical practitioner.\n\nAll the drugs under the list must have the warning mentioned below, on their packaging listed prominently.\n\n\"Schedule H drug. Warning: To be sold by retail on the prescription of a registered medical practitioner only\"\n\nSchedule H1: To be sold on the production of a valid prescription, but the chemist will maintain a separate register where the identity of the patient, contact details of the prescribing doctor, and the name and dispensed quantity of the drug will be recorded. The dispensing quantity should be strictly according to the valid prescription. It is a recently introduced list with the objective of curtailing indiscriminate use of antimicrobials.\n\nSchedule X: To be sold with a valid prescription. Prescription in duplicate, separate license requirements, meticulous storage, and dispensing records must be maintained by the chemist. The drugs that have high abuse liability are included in schedule X.","t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A first-year resident administers an intramuscular injection to a patient by the Z track technique. What is the advantage of this technique?","o":["Increase absorption rate","Prevent staining of skin","Reduce allergic manifestations","Longer duration of action"],"a":1,"e":"The advantage of the 'Z-track technique' for intramuscular administration is that it prevents skin staining.\n\nZ-track technique procedure:\n\n1. Stretch the skin by pulling it laterally away from the intended point of injection.\n\n2. Insert the needle into the site at a 90˚ angle, aspirate by pulling back to see if the needle has a penetrated vessel.\n\n3. If blood is aspirated withdraw the needle, and repeat the procedure at another site with new equipment and medication.\n\n4. If no blood is aspirated administer the injection.\n\n5. Wait for 10 seconds and then withdraw the needle, and release the skin allowing the displaced tissue to seal the needle track.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n• The 'Z-track' prevents leakage of the medication into the subcutaneous tissue and skin staining through the tract to the surface of the skin\n\n• Pain intensity is also reduced","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23b7eb6ea07e4ccd8689c15d21f4d601x1280x2717.JPEG"],"t":"Clinical Trials And Miscellaneous","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"The elimination after 3 half-lives in first-order kinetics is around:","o":["12.5%","75%","87.5%","94%"],"a":2,"e":"After each half-life, 50% of the drug is eliminated or the plasma concentration becomes half the original value.\n\nTherefore, after,\n\n• 1 half-life = 50% drug is eliminated\n\n• 2 half-lives = 75% drug is eliminated\n\n• 3 half-lives = 87.5% is eliminated\n\n• 4 half-lives = 93% is eliminated","t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"You have to give 180 mg of ceftriaxone to a patient in a 2 ml syringe that has 10 divisions per ml. The concentration of the drug in the vial is 500 mg/5 ml. How many divisions should be filled in 2 ml of the syringe to give 180 mg?","o":["18","1.8","2","20"],"a":0,"e":"The syringe should be filled up to 18 divisions to deliver 180 mg ceftriaxone to the patient.\n\nConcentration of drug: 500 mg/5 mL → 100 mg/1 mL\n\nThe syringe has 10 divisions per mL.\n\nHence, each division corresponds to 1/10 = 0.1 mL\n\nThe required dosage for the patient is 180 mg that is present in 1.8 mL.\n\n0.1mL =1 division, 1.8mL = 18 divisions.","t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"500mg of a drug was administered to a patient. 100mg was cleared in 1 hour. How much of the drug would be left in his body after 3 hours if the excretion of the drug follows zero-order kinetics?","o":["300 mg","200 mg","400 mg","100 mg"],"a":1,"e":"In zero-order kinetics, a constant amount of drug is eliminated in unit time.\n\nIn this question, 100 mg of the drug is eliminated in 1 hour.\n\nAfter 3 hours,\n\n3 x 100 = 300 mg would be eliminated.\n\n500-300 = 200 mg will be the remaining drug in the body.","t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"200 mg of a drug is administered to a patient, of which 20 mg is excreted in 90 minutes. How much of the drug will remain after 3 hours if it follows first-order kinetics?","o":["162 mg","145.8 mg","50 mg","40 mg"],"a":0,"e":"After 3 hours, 162mg of the drug will remain.\n\nIn first-order kinetics, a constant fraction of the drug is excreted in a given time.\n\nIn the given question,\n\n20mg out of 200mg is excreted in 90 minutes,\n\n20/200 = 0.1 fraction is eliminated every 90 minutes.\n\nAfter 90 minutes, 200mg - 20 mg = 180 mg remains\n\nThe next 90 minutes (i.e. after 180 minutes) = 180 x 0.1 = 18 mg is eliminated.\n\nTherefore, 180 - 18 = 162 mg remains.\n\nSimpler way:\n\n1/10 fraction of the drug is excreted in 90 minutes → 9/10 remains after 90 minutes.\n\n3 hours (180 min) is 2 times 90 minutes.\n\nSo 200 x (9/10)(9/10) = 162 mg remains after 180 minutes","t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Calculate the half-life of a drug X administered to a patient if it has a volume of distribution of 50 liters and clearance of 0.1 L/min.","o":["500 minutes","440 minutes","420 minutes","350 minutes"],"a":3,"e":"The half-life of this drug is 350 minutes.\n\nIn first-order kinetics:\n\nTherefore,\n\nt1/2 ~ 0.7 x 50/0.1 = 0.7 x 500\n\nt1/2 ~ 350 minutes\n\nWhere,\n\n• t1/2 - Plasma half-life\n\n• Vd - Volume of distribution (50)\n\n• CL - Clearance (0.1)","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b90167f94b3b478fb3d9bdf163b48edfx1280x361.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Calculate hepatic clearance of a drug with hepatic blood flow of 2000 mL/min and hepatic extraction ratio of 0.5.","o":["4000 mL/min","2000 mL/min","1500 mL/min","1000 mL/min"],"a":3,"e":"The hepatic clearance is 1000 mL/min.\n\nClearance of a drug by an organ is the difference between the rate of presentation of the drug to the organ and the rate of exit of the drug from the organ. It is calculated by the equation:\n\nCLorgan = Q x E\n\nWhere,\n\n• Q - Organ blood flow\n\n• E - Extraction ratio (organ-specific)\n\nIn the question, it is given that the hepatic blood flow (Q) is 2000 mL/min and the hepatic extraction ratio (E) is 0.5.\n\nSubstituting the values in the above equation,\n\nCLorgan = 2000 X 0.5 = 1000 mL/min\n\nNote: The extraction ratio (E) is the fraction of the drug that is removed from the blood by the eliminating organ.\n\nExtraction ratio (E) = CA-CV/CA\n\nWhere,\n\n• CA is the concentration of drug presented to the organ\n\n• CA-CV is the drug removed by the organ","t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A patient is administered drug X that follows first-order kinetics. 40 mg intravenous dose results in a plasma concentration of 2mg/L initially and 1mg/L, 4 hours later. What is the clearance of drug X?","o":["5 L/hr","10 L/hr","3.5 L/hr","7 L/hr"],"a":2,"e":"Time taken for the plasma concentration to drop to half,\n\n2 mg/L to 1mg/L = 4 hours (plasma half life-t1/2)\n\nAmount of drug in the body = 40\nInitial plasma concentration = 2\n\nTherefore,\n\nVd = 40/2 = 20 L\n\nClearance = 0.693 x Vd / t1/2\n\nClearance ~ 0.7 x 20/4\n\nClearance ~ 3.5 L/hour","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59ac4b5998694ff49b010e93aeede415x1280x362.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"A male patient weighing 50 kg has acute severe asthma, for which theophylline infusion is planned. The target plasma concentration is 10 mg/L. The volume of distribution of theophylline is 0.6 L/kg. What will be the loading dose in this patient for intravenous administration?","o":["200 mg","250 mg","300 mg","350 mg"],"a":2,"e":"The loading dose in this patient for intravenous administration is 300mg.\n\nTarget plasma concentration (Cp) = 10 mg/L\n\nVolume of distribution (Vd) = 0.6 L/kg\n\nIn the above patient, total Vd = 0.6 x 50 = 30 L\n\nFor intravenous route; F=1\n\nWhere,\n\n• Vd - Volume of distribution (liters)\n\n• Cp - target serum level (mcg/L)\n\n• F - bioavailability factor\n\nLoading dose = 10 mg/L x 30L = 300 mg\n\nNote: The loading dose of 300 mg is usually not given as a single intravenous injection; it would be given as an infusion or as divided doses administered over a short period to reduce the toxicity associated with high plasma levels.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2daab0c99dbc4a309b3638916161d3dbx1280x361.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"Calculate maintenance dose for a drug M with 100% oral bioavailability, which needs a target concentration of 2 mg/L and clearance of 20L/hr and dosed QID.","o":["300 mg","160 mg","250 mg","240 mg"],"a":3,"e":"The maintenance dose of the drug is 240 mg.\n\nTarget plasma concentration (Cp) = 2 mg/L\n\nClearance (CL) = 20L/hr\n\nAssuming dosing interval for QID = every 6th hour = x 6 (since the clearance stated is per hour)\n\nBioavailability (100%), F= 1\n\nMaintenance dose = 2 x 20 x 6/ 1 = 240 mg\n\nNote: QID corresponds to 4 times a day. Hence, the dosing interval is 24/4 = 6 hours.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41b2b6037db546789cb27ecb131fc3afx1280x407.JPEG"],"t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"You have started an infusion of drug Y in a patient at a rate of 200 mg/hr. Drug Y has Vd of 100 liters and clearance of 100 ml/min. What will be the drug concentration at a steady state?","o":["50 mg/h","25 mg/h","36.66 mg/L","33.33 mg/L"],"a":3,"e":"Steady state concentration (Css) for this drug is 33.33mg/L.\n\nSteady state concentration (Css) = Rate of infusion / clearance.\n\nRate of infusion = 200 mg/h\n\nVd = 100 L\n\nClearance = 100 mL/min\n\nClearance = 100 x 60 mL/hr = 6000 mL/hr = 6L/h (converted to L/h)\n\nCss = (200 mg/hr) / (6L/hr) = 100/3 = 33.33 mg/L","t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"The clearance of a drug which follows first-order kinetics (in one compartment model) is 0.2 L/min and volume of distribution is 40 L. What is the t1/2 of that drug?","o":["140 min","220 min","240 min","180 min"],"a":0,"e":"The t1/2 of the drug would be 140 minutes.\n\nt1/2 is the time required for the plasma concentration or the amount of a drug in the body to be reduced by 50%. It depends on its clearance and volume of distribution.\n\nt1/2 = 0.693 x Vd/Cl\n\nwhere,\n\n• Vd - volume of distribution\n\n• Cl - clearance.\n\nWe can take 0.693 approximately as 0.7\n\nSubstituting,\n\nt1/2 = 0.7 x 40 /0.2\n\nt1/2 = 0.7 x 200\n\nt1/2 = 140 minutes\n\nClinical implications: Half-life determines the duration of the drug effect. It also indicates whether the accumulation of the drug will occur under multiple dosage regimens thereby making it is essential to decide on the appropriate dosing interval.","t":"Pharmacokinetics   Calculations","c":"General Pharmacology"},
 {"q":"What is the principal post-ganglionic neurotransmitter of the parasympathetic system?","o":["Dopamine","Nor-epinephrine","Acetylcholine","GABA"],"a":2,"e":"The principal post-ganglionic neurotransmitter of the parasympathetic system is acetylcholine.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"While conducting animal studies, a scientist is experimenting with different properties of drugs acting on the ANS. He identifies a drug that inhibits the rate-limiting step in acetylcholine synthesis in nerve terminals. What is this drug?","o":["Choline","Hemicholinium","Acetylcholine esterase","Botulinium toxin"],"a":1,"e":"The rate-limiting step in acetylcholine synthesis is the active uptake of choline into the nerve terminal, which is inhibited by hemicholinum.\n\nOption A: Choline is the precursor of acetylcholine and is taken up from the synaptic cleft into the nerve terminal.\n\nOption C: Acetylcholine esterase is involved in the breakdown of acetylcholine after it is released into the synaptic cleft.\n\nOption D: Botulinum toxin inhibits the release of acetylcholine from nerve terminals.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c164d17405742588be93f8c5a097090x1280x2124.JPEG"],"t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"What is the drug that acts on SNARE?","o":["Hemicholinum","Botulinum toxin","Hexamethonium","Acetylcholinesterase"],"a":1,"e":"The drug which acts on SNARE (Soluble N-ethylmalimide Sensitive Factor (NSF) Attachment protein Receptor) is botulinum toxin.\n\nSNARE proteins play a critical role in the fusion of the vesicle with the cell membrane and the exocytosis of the neurotransmitter from the pre-synaptic membrane. Inhibition of these SNARE proteins inhibits the release of acetylcholine from synaptic terminals.\n\nIt is useful to treat spastic and neurological conditions associated with increased cholinergic activity like:\n\n• Blepharospasm\n\n• Spastic cerebral palsy\n\n• Torticollis\n\n• Chronic resistant migraine\n\nOption A: Hemicholinum inhibits the uptake of choline into nerve terminals.\n\nOption C: Hexamethonium is a ganglionic blocker.\n\nOption D: Acetylcholine esterase is involved in the breakdown of acetylcholine after it is released into the synaptic cleft.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2acfd56c030143abb12025c6fd80fcebx1280x2124.JPEG"],"t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements regarding acetylcholine is incorrect ?","o":["It has no systemic therapeutic use","It is metabolised by both acetylcholine esterase and butyrylcholine esterase","It is used topically during ophthalmic surgery","It has good CNS penetration"],"a":3,"e":"Acetylcholine does not have a good CNS penetration as it is a quaternary ammonium compound.\n\nNeostigmine and glycopyrrolate are also quaternary ammonium compounds and their entry into CNS is limited. Tertiary ammonium compounds like physostigmine have good CNS penetration.\n\nOptions A and B: Acetylcholine has virtually no systemic use because of its diffuse action and rapid hydrolysis by both acetylcholine esterase and plasma butyrylcholine esterase.\n\nOption C: Acetylcholine is used during ophthalmic surgery for producing rapid miosis.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You notice your senior performing a vagal maneuver in a stable patient with PSVT. Which of the following muscarinic receptors mediate the therapeutic effect of this maneuver?","o":["M1","M2","M3","M4"],"a":1,"e":"In vagal maneuver, the parasympathetic nervous system mediates activity on the heart via the M2 muscarinic receptor (most predominant subtype).\n\nThe receptors in the autonomic nervous system (ANS) are of 2 types:\n\n• Muscarinic:\n\n• 5 Subtypes - M1, M2, M3, M4, M5\n\n• M1, M3, M5 are Gq coupled receptors\n\n• M2, M4 are Gi/Go coupled receptors\n\n• Nicotinic:\n\n• 2 Subtypes - Nm (Neuromuscular junction), Nn (CNS, Ganglion, Adrenal glands)\n\n• Nicotinic receptors are ion channels\n\nReceptors of Parasympathetic system\nSubtype\nSite\n\nNicotinic\nNm\nNeuromuscular junction\n\nNn\nCNS, ganglia, Adrenal gland\n\nMuscarinic\nM1\nCNS, Ganglia, Salivary, and gastric glands\n\nM2\nHeart\n\nM3\nSmooth muscles, secretory glands, eyes - pupils, and blood vessels\n\nM4\nCNS\n\nM5\nCNS","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old woman who was diagnosed with Sjogren's syndrome is on treatment with cevimeline. On which receptor does this drug mainly act for the expulsion of secretions from the salivary gland?","o":["M1","M2","M3","M4"],"a":2,"e":"Cevimeline is an M3 receptor agonist resulting in the expulsion of the secretions from the salivary gland. This drug also enhances expulsion of lacrimal secretions in Sjögren’s syndrome.\n\nBoth M1 and M3 receptors are present in a salivary gland. Activation of M1 increases secretion and M3 causes smooth muscle contraction and expulsion of the secretions.\n\nReceptors of Parasympathetic system\nSubtype\nSite\n\nNicotinic\nNm\nNeuromuscular junction\n\nNn\nCNS, ganglia, Adrenal gland\n\nMuscarinic\nM1\nCNS, Ganglia, Salivary, and gastric glands\n\nM2\nHeart\n\nM3\nSmooth muscles, secretory glands, eyes - pupils, and blood vessels\n\nM4\nCNS\n\nM5\nCNS","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You are asked to prescribe a cholinergic agent to a patient suffering from Alzheimer's disease. Which of the following drugs cannot be chosen?","o":["Galantamine","Donepezil","Tacrine","Memantine"],"a":3,"e":"Memantine is an NMDA receptor antagonist (not a cholinergic agent) indicated for the management of moderate to severe Alzheimer’s disease.\n\nDonepezil, galantamine, and tacrine are centrally acting cholinesterase inhibitors indicated for Alzheimer’s disease.\n\nNote: In this scenario, you are asked to prescribe a cholinergic drug. Tacrine, a cholinergic drug, although hepatotoxic, is still approved for use in Alzheimer's disease. Hence, memantine is the best answer.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions should cholinomimetic drugs be avoided?","o":["Closed angle glaucoma","Bradycardia","Cobra bite","Myasthenia gravis"],"a":1,"e":"Cholinomimetics cannot be used in bradycardia. They cause bradycardia by its action on M2 receptors on the heart.\n\nIn bradycardia, an anticholinergic agent like atropine is indicated.\n\nOption A - Cholinomimetics like pilocarpine are used in both open-angle and closed-angle glaucoma.\n\nOption C - Cobra venom contains a curare-like neurotoxin. Although antivenom is the specific antidote, symptoms can be controlled by a combination of neostigmine and atropine.\n\nOption D - Cholinesterase inhibitors like neostigmine and pyridostigmine are indicated in myasthenia gravis.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Neostigmine is administered to a post-op patient to reverse neuromuscular blockade. What is its mechanism of action?","o":["It blocks the action of acetylcholine","It interferes with the action of choline transferase","It interferes with the action of choline acetyl transferase","It interferes with the action of acetylcholine esterase"],"a":3,"e":"Muscle weakness is improved by neostigmine as it interferes with the action of acetylcholine esterase.\n\nNeostigmine is a cholinesterase inhibitor used mainly to improve muscle tone in people with myasthenia gravis, and also to reverse the effects of non-depolarizing muscle relaxants such as rocuronium and vecuronium at the end of an operation.\n\nInhibition of acetylcholine esterase increases the acetylcholine levels in the synapse that can act on receptors over the large surface area and for longer periods of time, reducing the weakness.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17de91627d8047d984a749fcc6eac33fx1280x2234.JPEG"],"t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A first-year anesthesia resident administered neostigmine to a patient who was accidentally given a slightly excessive bolus dose of suxamethonium. What is the most probable effect this is likely to produce?","o":["Complete reversal","Incomplete reversal","No effect","Enhanced relaxation"],"a":3,"e":"Neostigmine prolongs the muscle relaxation caused by suxamethonium (succinylcholine) instead of reversing the block.\n\nThe contraction of a muscle is achieved by a cycle of ACh binding and its degradation. Depolarising neuromuscular blockers (such as suxamethonium) acts by binding to the receptors much longer than ACh and causing a prolonged depolarization, resulting in an eventual blocking of neuromuscular transmission and flaccid paralysis.\n\nAChE metabolizes ACh and plasma cholinesterase metabolizes suxamethonium (SCh). Neostigmine blocks both enzymes. By inhibiting AChE, neostigmine increases the ACh concentration in the synaptic cleft. But this cannot reverse the block as the depolarized nerves are unresponsive to any stimuli during this period. However, the simultaneous inhibition of plasma cholinesterase decreases the degradation of SCh and therefore potentiates the neuromuscular block of suxamethonium, resulting in prolonged muscle relaxation.\n\nNeostigmine can be used only as a reversal agent for neuromuscular blockade caused by competitive non-depolarising blockers such as vecuronium.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9ffc23f906f4e298f36f21819ea8f70x1280x2234.JPEG"],"t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman presented to the medicine OPD with complaints of muscle weakness and blurring of vision following exertion. On examination, the patient had ptosis. What is the drug that can be used in the diagnosis of this condition?","o":["Physostigmine","Edrophonium","Neostigmine","Pyridostigmine"],"a":1,"e":"Edrophonium, a short-acting anticholinesterase agent is used for diagnosing myasthenia gravis. It is also called the tensilon test.\n\nInitially, 2 mg is administered. If improvements of symptoms do not occur within 45 seconds, an additional 8 mg can be administered. Improvement of strength unaccompanied by lingual fasciculations (generally present in non-myasthenics) is a positive test.\n\nEdrophonium is short-acting because:\n\n• The binding site of edrophonium to acetylcholine esterase - choline subsite of the active center\n\n• No reaction with the enzyme - reversible binding only\n\n• Quaternary structure - rapid elimination\n\nNote: An excessive dose of anti-cholinesterase drugs results in a cholinergic crisis.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs would you use in the treatment of a patient with myasthenia gravis?","o":["d-Tubocurarine","Hexamethonium","Ambenonium","Gallamine"],"a":2,"e":"Ambenonium, pyridostigmine, and neostigmine are the anti-cholinesterase drugs indicated in the treatment of myasthenia gravis.\n\nNeostigmine and its congeners can improve muscle contractions by allowing released acetylcholine to accumulate and act on the receptors over a large area. They also have an additional direct depolarizing effect on the motor endplate.\n\nOptions A and D: d-Tubocurarine and gallamine are non-depolarising neuromuscular blockers and can exacerbate myasthenia.\n\nOption B: Hexamethonium is a ganglionic blocker and not indicated for myasthenia gravis.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"What is the mechanism of action of atropine in the management of organophosphate poisoning?","o":["Reactivates acetylcholineesterase","Competes with acetylcholine release","Binds with both nicotinic and muscarinic acetylcholine receptors","Is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine"],"a":3,"e":"Atropine is useful in organophosphate poisoning because it is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine.\n\nAn acetylcholinesterase enzyme has an esteratic and an anionic site. The organophosphates bind to the esteratic (active) site of AChEs (acetylcholine esterases) and deactivate it.\n\nThis results in excessive Ach causing increased muscarinic activity like increased tracheobronchial and salivary secretions, bronchoconstriction, miosis, bradycardia, and to a lesser extent peripheral ganglionic and CNS actions. It has relatively insignificant action on nicotinic receptors.\n\nAtropine antagonizes these parasympathetic effects by competitive inhibition of acetylcholine at the muscarinic receptors.\n\nPralidoxime reactivates the acetylcholinesterase, which had been deactivated in organophosphate poisoning. Pralidoxime attaches to the anionic site of the enzyme that remains unoccupied. Then it reacts with the phosphorus atom of the organophosphorous compound which is attached to the esteric site to form a phospho-oxime complex. It then dissociates from the enzyme, regenerating the active enzyme.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A sanitation worker presented with complaints of blurred vision, breathing difficulty and muscle weakness. He gave a history of spraying malathion this morning. You administer pralidoxime as an antidote. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Stimulation of ACh receptors","Inhibition of breakdown of ACh","Blockade of ACh receptors","Reactivation of AChE enzyme"],"a":3,"e":"Pralidoxime reactivates cholinesterase, which would have been deactivated by phosphorylation caused by malathion.\n\nPralidoxime attaches to the anionic site of the enzyme that remains unoccupied. Then it reacts with the phosphorus atom of the organophosphorous compound which is attached to the esteric site to form a phospho-oxime complex. It then dissociates from the enzyme, regenerating the active enzyme.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A young man is brought to the ER with a history of ingestion of carbamate-based insecticide. He is agitated with excessive sweating, lacrimation, and salivation. Which of the following should not be used for treatment?","o":["Pralidoxime","Atropine","Magnesium sulfate purgative","Gastric lavage with activated charcoal"],"a":0,"e":"Pralidoxime may be hazardous in oral poisoning with carbamate insecticides. It is not effective in carbamate anti-ChE poisoning as the anionic site required for attachment of oximes is occupied.\n\nOximes are usually contraindicated in carbamate poisoning, as they do not reactivate the enzyme. They also have anti-ChE activity of their own that may worsen the condition.\n\nOption B: Atropine is the mainstay in the management of carbamate poisoning.\n\nOptions C and D: Magnesium sulfate purgative and gastric lavage with activated charcoal can be tried in oral carbamate poisoning.","t":"Ans Introduction & Parasympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following inhibits the rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine synthesis?","o":["Tyrosine","Metyrosine","Propranolol","Valbenazine"],"a":1,"e":"The rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine synthesis is tyrosine hydroxylase and is inhibited by metyrosine (alpha-methyl-p-tyrosine).\n\nTyrosine hydroxylase converts tyrosine to dopa (rate-limiting step). Dopa is then converted to dopamine by dopa decarboxylase.\n\nMetyrosine is indicated preoperatively in pheochromocytoma and in inoperable cases.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57a5fe6f90964779a2fd3090f2b4ebcdx1280x2291.JPEG"],"t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"An asthmatic patient has been taking a salbutamol inhaler to control his wheezing episodes. Which of the following is not an effect seen on stimulation of the receptor involved?","o":["Inhibition of lipolysis","Increased hepatic gluconeogenesis","Increased muscle glycogenolysis","Smooth muscle relaxation"],"a":0,"e":"Salbutamol is a short-acting, selective β2-adrenergic receptor agonist used in the treatment of asthma and COPD. β2 receptors mediate the stimulation of lipolysis (not inhibition).\n\nAdrenergic control of adipose tissue:\n\n• Stimulation of lipolysis - α1, β1, β2, and β3 receptors\n\n• Inhibition of lipolysis - α2\n\nAdrenergic receptor\nActions\n\nα1 receptor\n\n• Vasoconstriction of large arterioles\n\n• Genito-urinary smooth muscle - contraction\n\n• Uterine muscles contraction\n\n• Mydriasis - by contraction of radial muscles of the iris\n\n• Increase secretion of glands\n\n• Gastrointestinal relaxation\n\nα2 receptor\n\n• Autoregulatory in the sympathetic system\n\n• Vasoconstriction of precapillary arterioles in skeletal muscles\n\n• Decrease insulin synthesis\n\nβ1 receptor\n\n• Increased rhythmicity and force of contraction of the heart\n\n• Increased renin secretion - JG cells in the kidney\n\nβ2 receptor\n\n• Smooth muscle relaxation: vasodilation, bronchodilation\n\n• Uterine muscle relaxation\n\n• Stimulation of lipolysis\n\n• Increased insulin secretion\n\n• Increased hepatic gluconeogenesis\n\n• Increased muscle glycogenolysis\n\nβ3 receptor\n\n• Stimulation of lipolysis\n\n• Relaxation of detrusor","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements most appropriately describes the vasomotor reversal of Dale?","o":["Stimulation of alpha 1 followed by stimulation of beta 2","Blockade of alpha 1 followed by stimulation of beta 2","Stimulation of alpha 1 followed by blockade of beta 2","Stimulation of beta 1 followed by blockade of beta 2"],"a":1,"e":"Vasomotor reversal of Dale is a blockade of alpha (α1) followed by stimulation of beta (β2) receptors.\n\nNormally, a high dose of epinephrine injected produces an α1-mediated rise in BP followed by a β2-mediated fall in BP.\n\nAdministration of epinephrine after administration of α-blockers produces only a fall in BP. This is because the α-mediated response is abolished and hence, only the β-mediated response is seen, resulting in a fall in BP. This phenomenon is called the vasomotor reversal of Dale. This was first demonstrated by Sir H.H.Dale.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs when administered would lead to a biphasic reaction on blood pressure?","o":["Adrenaline","Noradrenaline","Dopamine","Dobutamine"],"a":0,"e":"Biphasic reaction on blood pressure is seen with the administration of adrenaline. The BP initially rises followed by a fall in BP.\n\nAdrenaline acts on α1, α2, β1, β2, and β3 receptors.\n\nα1 - present in blood vessels - causes vasoconstriction - increases BP.\n\nβ2 - present in blood vessels - causes vasodilatation - decreases BP.\n\nAt higher concentrations of adrenaline, alpha action predominates and hence a rise in BP is seen. Therefore an increase in BP is seen with IV administration of adrenaline.\n\nDue to rapid metabolism and uptake, the concentration of adrenaline decreases, and the β2 action predominates. Hence a decrease in BP is seen. This is referred to as the biphasic reaction.\n\nAdrenergic drug\n\nReceptors it acts on\n\nAdrenaline\n\nα1 + α2 +β1 + β2 + weak β3 action\n\nNoradrenaline\n\nα1 +α2 +β1 + β3 but no β2 action\n\nIsoprenaline\n\nβ1 + β2 + β3 but no α action","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not increase with slow IV infusion of adrenaline?","o":["Systolic blood pressure","Diastolic blood pressure","Mean blood pressure","Pulse pressure"],"a":1,"e":"Slow intravenous infusion of adrenaline results in a significant increase in systolic blood pressure with minimal effect on diastolic pressure.\n\nMean blood pressure and pulse pressure increase. This is because of the increased systolic pressure coupled with decreased, unchanged, or an insignificant increase in diastolic pressure.\n\nAt lower concentrations of adrenaline, vascular β2 receptors are more sensitive than α receptors, hence peripheral resistance decreases.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","o":["IV noradrenaline increases systolic and diastolic BP and causes tachycardia","IV adrenaline increases systolic, decreases diastolic BP and causes tachycardia","Dopamine increases peripheral resistance and improves renal perfusion","IV isoproterenol increases systolic and decreases diastolic BP and causes tachycardia"],"a":0,"e":"IV noradrenaline increases systolic and diastolic BP but is unlikely to cause tachycardia.\n\nIV noradrenaline acts on α1, α2, and β1 receptors:\n\n• α1- vasoconstriction - Increases DBP.\n\n• β1 - increases heart rate and contractility - Increases SBP.\n\n• This increases mean arterial pressure which causes a reflex vagal activity that decreases the heart rate leading to bradycardia.\n\nIV adrenaline acts on α1, α2, β1, β2, and β3 receptors:\n\n• β1 - increases SBP, tachycardia.\n\n• DBP - either β2 - decrease DBP, α1 - increase DBP or α1+β2 - no change.\n\nDopamine acts on β1,α1, and D1 receptors:\n\n• D1 - low concentrations - causes vasodilatation thus improving renal perfusion\n\n• β1- positive inotropic effect - increases SBP.\n\n• α1 - higher infusion rates - causes vasoconstriction and increases peripheral resistance\n\nIV isoproterenol is a non-selective β receptor agonist:\n\n• β1- increases SBP\n\n• β2- vasodilatation - decreases DBP\n\n• β1- tachycardia","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient in low output cardiogenic shock is being treated with dobutamine. Which of the following is true about the drug?","o":["Dobutamine decreases peripheral resistance","Acts on D1 and D2 receptors","Decreases renal circulation","Has no effect on coronary circulation"],"a":0,"e":"Dobutamine decreases peripheral resistance minimally.\n\nClinically, dobutamine increases cardiac output by selectively increasing myocardial contractility and promotes coronary vasodilation. This is associated with a mild decrease in total peripheral vascular resistance, which is mediated by the reflex withdrawal of sympathetic tone to the vasculature.\n\nDobutamine has a t1/2 of 2 minutes. It resembles dopamine but no action is seen on dopamine receptors.\n\nRenal circulation is variably affected depending upon the sympathetic tone of the patient.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"During a rotation in the acute medical ward, you observe a patient in cardiogenic shock being started on a low-dose dopamine infusion. Which of the following would you expect to increase in this patient?","o":["Cardiac output","Urine output","Heart rate","Blood pressure"],"a":1,"e":"At lower doses (< 2 μg/kg), dopamine only stimulates dopamine receptors in renal vasculature, causing increased urine output.\n\nAs the dose increases (2-5 μg/kg), additional cardiac stimulant action (β1) is also seen.\n\nAt very high doses (5-15 μg/kg), vasoconstriction (α1) is additionally seen.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A stress ECHO has to be done for a 56-year-old COPD patient with high suspicion of coronary artery disease. Which of the following drugs can be used to perform this test?","o":["Thalium","Noradrenaline","Adrenaline","Dobutamine"],"a":3,"e":"Dobutamine is used to perform a stress test.\n\nStress echocardiography is primarily done for the evaluation of myocardial ischemia. The basic principle is to increase cardiac stress (either with exercise or with a drug) in a controlled fashion, to aid with the identification and localization of ischemic areas (by detecting new or worsening wall motion abnormalities).\n\nPerforming stress echocardiography with a pharmacologic agent such as dobutamine allows for simulation of increased heart rate and increased myocardial physiologic demands in patients who may be unable to exercise due to musculoskeletal or pulmonary comorbidities.\n\nDobutamine causes cardiac stress by acting on β1-adrenergic receptors to increase cardiac contractility and heart rate, and on β2-adrenergic receptors to cause peripheral vasodilatation. It is preferred over vasodilators used in stress echo tests such as adenosine, dipyridamole, and regadenoson (adenosine derivative).","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You notice fenoldopam mentioned on the drug chart of an elderly male patient admitted in the ICU. What condition is he most likely being treated for?","o":["Hypertensive emergency","Congestive heart failure","Uncontrolled migraine","Atrial fibrillation"],"a":0,"e":"Fenoldopam is primarily indicated in the management of hypertensive emergencies. It is a D1 agonist at peripheral D1 receptors and acts as a rapid-acting vasodilator.\n\nDrugs used in a hypertensive emergency are:\n\n• Nitroprusside\n\n• Nicardipine\n\n• Labetalol\n\n• Hydralazine\n\n• Esmolol\n\n• Nitroglycerine\n\n• Phentolamine\n\n• Fenoldopam","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an indirectly acting sympathomimetic?","o":["Methamphetamine","Oxymetazoline","Amphetamine","Tyramine"],"a":1,"e":"Oxymetazoline is a direct-acting non-selective α1 and α2 receptor agonist.\n\nAmphetamine, methamphetamine, and tyramine are catecholamine releasing agents and are considered to be indirectly acting sympathomimetic agents.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Following spinal anesthesia, the blood pressure of a patient started dropping. Your senior asks you to administer a mixed-acting sympathomimetic. Which of the following loaded syringes will you pick?","o":["Dobutamine","Isoproterenol","Ephedrine","Epinephrine"],"a":2,"e":"Ephedrine is a mixed-acting sympathomimetic agent which can be used to manage hypotension following spinal anesthesia.\n\nDrugs that release norepinephrine and also directly activate receptors are called mixed-acting sympathomimetics.\n\nEphedrine has a dual action:\n\n• Releasing agent: releases neurotransmitters from nerve endings.\n\n• Direct action on α1, α2, β1, and β2 receptors.\n\nThe adrenergic agents are classified into three sub-groups:\n\n• Directly acting agents:\n\n• Selective agents - phenylephrine (α1), clonidine (α2), dobutamine (β1), terbutaline (β2).\n\n• Non-selective agents - oxymetazoline (α1 + α2), isoproterenol (β1 + β2), epinephrine (α1 +α2 +β1 + β2) norepinephrine (α1 +α2 + β1)\n\n• Mixed acting agents - ephedrine (α1 +α2 +β1 + β2 + catecholamine releasing agent)\n\n• Indirectly acting agents:\n\n• Catecholamine releasing agents - amphetamine and tyramine\n\n• Uptake inhibitor - cocaine\n\n• MAO inhibitor - selegeline\n\n• COMT inhibitor - entacapone","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is not true about clonidine?","o":["It acts on alpha 2 adrenergic receptors","Can result in rebound hypertension on abrupt withdrawal","Can produce CNS stimulation","Inhibits salivation"],"a":2,"e":"Clonidine is a centrally acting antihypertensive agent. It is not associated with CNS stimulation.\n\nIt treats high blood pressure by stimulating presynaptic α2 receptors in the vasomotor center in the brainstem. This binding has a sympatholytic effect, by suppressing the release of norepinephrine and other sympathomimetics.\n\nOption A - Clonidine is an alpha 2 receptor agonist that reduces central sympathetic outflow.\n\nOption B - Abrupt withdrawal after long-term therapy can result in rebound hypertension.\n\nOption D - Dry mouth and sedation are the major side effects of clonidine. It can also cause impotency and constipation.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You are administering clonidine intravenously to a patient. Which of the following effects would you observe?","o":["Prolonged rapid fall in blood pressure","Transient rise in blood pressure followed by a prolonged fall","Transient fall in blood pressure followed by a prolonged rise","Prolonged rapid rise in blood pressure"],"a":1,"e":"Intravenous administration of clonidine results in a transient rise in blood pressure followed by a prolonged fall.\n\nα2 receptors are present on the presynaptic as well as the postsynaptic ganglia. They are the autoreceptors of the sympathetic system.\n\nClonidine is a directly acting α2 agonist. When it activates the postsynaptic α2 receptors in vascular smooth muscle, there is a transient rise in the blood pressure. This is followed by activation of central α2 receptors, which results in decreases the central sympathetic tone and causes a prolonged fall in blood pressure.\n\nα2 receptors are present postsynaptically in vascular walls and activation results in the same action as α1 stimulation i.e., vasoconstriction. But the major receptor that is responsible for vasoconstriction in vascular walls is the postsynaptic α1 receptor.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is therapy with an adrenergic-agonist not useful?","o":["50-year-old woman suffering from acute decompensated heart failure","35-year-old man in septic shock with persistent hypotension","40-year-old woman with blood pressure 160/100 mmHg","55-year-old man with erectile dysfunction"],"a":3,"e":"Adrenergic agonists are not useful in the treatment of erectile dysfunction. Adrenergic antagonists (phentolamine) are used locally for vasodilation in erectile dysfunction as PIPE therapy (Phentolamine Induced Penile Erection).\n\nAdrenergic agonists are mainly sympathomimetic due to their action on peripheral vascular or post-synaptic alpha receptors. However, certain adrenergic agonists (like clonidine) act as a sympatholytic due to their predominant action on pre-synaptic α2 receptors which inhibits the release of noradrenaline.\n\nOptions A and B: In hypotensive states like shock and acute decompensated heart failure, short-term therapy with parenteral sympathomimetic drugs are indicated in treatment.\n\nOption C: Clonidine is an adrenergic agonist which acts on the pre-synaptic α2 receptor and is used in the treatment of hypertension.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 22-week pregnant woman presents to the OPD for a routine check-up. On examination, her BP is 150/100 mmHg but she does not have a history of hypertension. You prescribe methyldopa to treat her. On which of the following receptors does this drug act?","o":["α2","α1","D1","β1"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario suggests a diagnosis of gestational hypertension, for which methyldopa is used. It acts on α2 receptors.\n\nMethyldopa is a centrally acting α2-adrenergic receptor agonist that is converted into alpha-methyl-norepinephrine. It decreases central sympathetic outflow, decreasing systemic vascular resistance without decreasing cardiac output.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an alpha adrenoreceptor agonist?","o":["Clonidine","Isoproterenol","Guanabenz","Guanfacine"],"a":1,"e":"Isoproterenol is a non-selective β-receptor agonist with no action on α-receptors.\n\nClonidine, guanabenz, and guanfacine are α2 receptor agonists.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Why is salbutamol preferred over adrenaline in the treatment of an asthmatic patient?","o":["β1 selectivity","β2 selectivity","α1 selectivity","β3 selectivity"],"a":1,"e":"Salbutamol is preferred over adrenaline in an asthmatic patient due to β2 selectivity.\n\nThis β2 selectivity is relative and lost at higher concentrations. Around 40% of the beta receptors are of β2 type and their activation won't cause cardiac stimulation.\n\nMajor adverse effects of non-selective β-agonists are attributed to the stimulation of β1 receptors in the heart which result in tachyarrhythmias. Selective β2 receptor agonists are introduced to overcome this major drawback.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 56-year-old man diagnosed with chronic bronchitis was prescribed a long-acting beta-2 agonist. Identify the drug.","o":["Albuterol","Formoterol","Pirbuterol","Orciprenaline"],"a":1,"e":"Formoterol is a long-acting β2 agonist (LABA).\n\nβ2 agonists are classified as:\n\n• Short-acting β2 receptor agonists (SABA):\n\n• Albuterol (Salbutamol)\n\n• Pirbuterol\n\n• Orciprenaline\n\n• Long-acting β2 agonists (LABA):\n\n• Salmeterol\n\n• Formoterol\n\n• Ultra-long acting β2 agonists (VLABA):\n\n• Indacaterol\n\n• Olodaterol\n\n• Vilanterol\n\n• Carmoterol","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following beta 2 receptor agonists was developed specifically as a uterine relaxant?","o":["Salbutamol","Terbutaline","Ritodrine","Orciprenaline"],"a":2,"e":"Ritodrine was developed specifically as a uterine relaxant.\n\nSalbutamol, orciprenaline, and terbutaline can also be used as uterine relaxants but were initially developed as inhalational agents for asthma. β2 agonists are associated with increased mortality and are rarely used as uterine relaxants now.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A child suffering from intermittent bronchial asthma is being treated with β2 agonists. Which of the following side effects would you not expect?","o":["Tremors","Hypoglycemia","Hypokalemia","Tachycardia"],"a":1,"e":"β2 agonists are associated with hyperglycemia probably due to β2 receptor-mediated glycogenolysis and glucagon release.\n\nAdverse effects of beta 2 agonists:\n\n• Hypokalemia - direct effect on skeletal muscle uptake of K+ ions\n\n• Tachycardia - reflex sympathetic stimulation due to vasodilator action on β2 receptors and direct effect on cardiac β1 receptors (β2 selectivity is lost at high concentrations)\n\n• Muscle tremors - direct effect on β2 receptors in skeletal muscles and due to hypokalemia\n\n• Hyperglycaemia\n\n• Hyperlipidemia\n\n• Tolerance - desensitization on long-term use","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 4-year-old child on salbutamol is brought to the hospital with complaints of tremors. Which of the following effects of the drug would explain this side effect?\n\n1. Direct action on muscle spindles\n\n2. Hypokalemia\n\n3. Hyperkalemia\n\n4. Direct action at the motor end-plate\n\n5. Hypoglycemia","o":["1, 2, and 5","2 and 4","1 and 2","3 and 5"],"a":2,"e":"Tremors due to β2 agonists can be both due to direct action on muscle spindle and hypokalemia.\n\nInitially, it was thought to be due to beta receptor-mediated enhancement of discharge of muscle spindles. However, recently the tremors have also been closely correlated with hypokalemia. Hence both direct action and hypokalemia can explain the tremors associated with β2 agonist use, although the exact mechanism is still not known.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 68-year-old man with Parkinson's disease was prescribed mirabegron to control his symptoms of urinary urgency and incontinence. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["β2 agonist","β3 agonist","M3 receptor antagonist","M1 receptor antagonist"],"a":1,"e":"Mirabegron is a β3 agonist used for the treatment of overactive bladder (OAB). Stimulation of β3 receptors in detrusor muscle increases the bladder capacity and provides symptomatic relief in overactive bladder.\n\nAntimuscarinic drugs are the mainstay of oral pharmacological treatment for OAB. It acts by inhibiting muscarinic receptor subtypes, M2 and M3 in the bladder. Both subtypes are expressed in multiple tissues, resulting in anticholinergic adverse events such as dry mouth, constipation, and cognitive impairment. These adverse effects are not seen with mirabegron as it acts via adrenergic receptors.\n\nMirabegron is a moderate inhibitor of CYP2D6. Hence, caution has to be exercised when prescribing it along with CYP2D6 substrates such as metoprolol, desipramine, digoxin.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Match the following dilution of adrenaline and its use:\n\nA. 1:1000\n1. Cardiac arrest\n\nB. 1:10000\n2. Anaphylaxis\n\nC. 1:100000\n3. Local anesthesia","o":["A-1 B-2 C-3","A-2 B-1 C-3","A-3 B-2 C-1","A-1 B-3 C-2"],"a":1,"e":"Epinephrine (1:1000 solution) 0.5 ml I.M. is preferred in the treatment of anaphylactic shock.\n\nIn cardiac arrest, the 1:1000 adrenaline must be diluted to 1:10,000 for IV route, and used cautiously.\n\nAdrenaline in a concentration of 1: 50,000 to 1: 200,000 is added to local anesthetics for:\n\n• Reducing the systemic absorption\n\n• Prolonging the duration of action\n\n• Providing a more bloodless field for surgery\n\n1:1000 dilution can be used for the following routes of administration:\n\n• Intramuscular\n\n• Subcutaneous\n\n• Inhalational (nebulization)\n\nIM injection is preferred over intravenous (IV) bolus because it is faster in many situations and safer (ie, lower risk of cardiovascular complications, such as severe hypertension and ventricular arrhythmias). There is a much greater risk of causing harmful side effects by inappropriate dosage or misdiagnosis of anaphylaxis when using IV adrenaline.\n\nEpinephrine is also used in the treatment of hypotension, to induce local vasoconstriction to reduce the diffusion of local anaesthetics, to induce bronchodilation in asthma and COPD.\n\nNote: The recommended dose of epinephrine is 1mg = 10 mL of a 1:10,000 solution administered IV every 3 to 5 minutes during resuscitation as per the CPR guidelines. For nebulization, 1:1000 adrenaline is commonly used.","t":"Sympathomimetics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an anticholinergic drug?","o":["Ipratropium bromide","Dicyclomine","Atropine","Amphetamine"],"a":3,"e":"Amphetamine is not an anticholinergic (parasympatholytics) drug. It is an indirectly acting sympathomimetic drug used as a CNS stimulant.\n\nParasympatholytic drugs are classified as:\n\nClass\nDrugs\n\nMuscarinic antagonists\n\n• Atropine\n\n• Scopolamine\n\n• Homatropine\n\n• Tropicamide\n\n• Ipratropium, Tiotropium\n\n• Darifenacin, Solifenacin\n\n• Pirenzipine, Telenzipine\n\n• Glycopyrrolate\n\n• Umeclidinium\n\nNicotinic antagonists\nNn antagonists\n\n• Trimethaphan\n\n• Hexamethonium\n\n• Mecamylamine\n\nNm antagonists\nDepolarizing\n\n• Suxamethonium\n\n• Decamethonium\n\nNon-depolarizing\n\n• Pancuronium\n\n• Vecuronium\n\n• Rocuronium\n\n• d-tubocurarine\n\n• Mivacurium\n\n• Atracurium","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"What is the scientific name of this plant whose roots &amp; leaves were historically used in the treatment of asthma?","o":["Atropa belladonna","Datura stramonium","Calotropis gigantea","Digitalis purpurea"],"a":1,"e":"The above plant is Datura stramonium, also called Jamestown or Jimsonweed.\n\nThe active ingredient in Datura stramonium includes anticholinergic alkaloids such as atropine, scopolamine, and hyoscyamine. These were helpful in relieving the symptoms associated with asthma via inhibition of M3 receptors on bronchi, leading to a decrease in bronchospasm and tracheobronchial secretions.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/de446a17173745ed8be47a9b2819589c.JPEG"],"t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"On administration of atropine, which of the following effects is unlikely?","o":["Increased bowel sounds","Mydriasis","Dry mouth","Tachycardia"],"a":0,"e":"Atropine does not cause increased bowel sounds. It decreases the amplitude and frequency of peristalsis, resulting in decreased bowel sounds.\n\nPeristalsis suppression due to atropine is incomplete because of the additional control of peristalsis by local reflexes in the enteric nervous system and involvement of other neurotransmitters like 5-HT, encephalin, dopamine in peristalsis.\n\nActions of atropine:\n\n• Mydriasis - by blocking the contraction of sphincter pupillae muscle\n\n• Decreased secretions from salivary glands and lacrimal glands - by blocking M1 & M3 receptors\n\n• Tachycardia - due to its vagolytic activity at M2 receptor\n\n• Decreased sweating - by inhibiting sympathetic cholinergic supply to sweat glands which makes the skin hot and dry (atropine fever).","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"While treating a patient with acute myocardial infarction, an abnormal rhythm was noted on the ECG. The senior resident decides to administer atropine to control this. What was the likely rhythm observed in the patient?","o":["Sinus tachycardia","Sinus bradycardia","Ventricular arrhythmias","Atrial fibrillation"],"a":1,"e":"Atropine is indicated in acute myocardial infarction when it is accompanied by sinus bradycardia due to excessive vagal tone. It is seen commonly in inferior or posterior wall myocardial infarction.\n\nThe dosage of atropine has to be carefully adjusted when used in a patient with myocardial infarction. If the dose is too low it will aggravate the bradycardia by action on presynaptic M1 receptors. If the dose is too high it will cause significant tachycardia and aggravate the condition by competitive inhibition of postsynaptic M2 receptors.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You administer atropine at a dose of 0.5 mg IV to a patient with symptomatic bradycardia. What would be the effect on heart rate?","o":["Transient tachycardia followed by bradycardia","Transient bradycardia followed by tachycardia","Mild tachycardia","Worsening bradycardia"],"a":1,"e":"Atropine at average clinical doses of 0.4-0.6 mg administered parenterally results in transient bradycardia followed by tachycardia.\n\nParasympathetic postganglionic nerve terminals in the SA node contain presynaptic M1 receptors. These receptors normally inhibit the release of ACh. Therefore stimulation of these receptors decreases the release of ACh causing tachycardia. Inhibition of these receptors increases ACh causing bradycardia.\n\nAtropine initially blocks these presynaptic M1 receptors. This increases the amount of ACh released at the SA node and causes bradycardia. Then, the progressive increase in the atropine concentration blocks the postsynaptic M2 receptors on the SA node (decreasing the ACh release) decreasing the parasympathetic tone, thereby causing tachycardia.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A delirious child was rushed to the ER with a history of ingestion of an unknown plant. On examination, his pulse rate was 150 beats per minute, and his skin was warm and dry. Pupils were dilated and bowel sounds were sluggish. Which of the following toxidromes is likely to be responsible for the symptoms in this child?","o":["Anticholinergic","Sympathomimetic","Opioid","Sedative-Hypnotic"],"a":0,"e":"Mydriasis, tachycardia, dry mouth, atropine fever (warm, hot skin) and delirium are classical symptoms of anticholinergic overdose following the ingestion of plants that contain belladonna alkaloids.\n\nPhysostigmine is the drug of choice for the treatment of atropine and belladonna poisoning.\n\nOption B: Sympathomimetics can cause mydriasis and tachycardia. Hyperthermia can be seen secondary to increased agitation and aggressive movements. However, delirium is rare and bowel sounds would be normal or increased in sympathomimetic toxidromes.\n\nOption C: Opioid overdose results in decreased bowel sounds but other symptoms are not seen.\n\nOption D: In sedative-hypnotic toxidrome, CNS depression and reduced bowel sounds are seen. But it does not cause tachycardia or change in pupil diameter.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2bd3f2a02f3c43c29e64be3e6b89b38cx1280x2559.JPEG"],"t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you use in the treatment of atropine toxicity?","o":["Pralidoxime","Naloxone","Flumazenil","Physostigmine"],"a":3,"e":"Atropine toxicity is treated with physostigmine, a reversible anticholinesterase agent.\n\nPhysostigmine acts by decreasing the breakdown of acetylcholine, and it also overcomes the competitive blockade of atropine in all muscarinic receptors.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59ed946e06804f429912c8eab57c3e86x1280x2559.JPEG"],"t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You are monitoring a patient being treated with atropine for alleged consumption of an organophosphate compound. Suddenly he develops marked excitement and claims to see his late wife sitting next to him. All of the following can be used in the management of this patient except?","o":["Physostigmine","Lorazepam","Chlorpromazine","Diazepam"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features of marked excitement and hallucinations are seen in atropine toxicity. Chlorpromazine should be avoided in patients with atropine toxicity.\n\nChlorpromazine is a low potency typical antipsychotic having high anticholinergic activity. Administering phenothiazines like chlorpromazine will worsen atropine toxicity.\n\nOption A: Physostigmine is indicated as first-line therapy for atropine intoxication\n\nOption B and D: Benzodiazepines can be used when marked excitement is present to calm down the patient","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24d82682b6bf46c78807d34de23212e6x1280x2559.JPEG"],"t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following describes the mechanism of mydriasis due to the instillation of a muscarinic antagonist?","o":["Contraction of the sphincter pupillae of the iris","Paralysis of the dilator pupillae muscles of the iris","Contraction of the dilator pupillae muscles of the iris","Paralysis of the sphincter pupillae of the iris"],"a":3,"e":"Mydriatic action of a muscarinic antagonist is due to the paralysis of the sphincter pupillae (circular) muscle of the iris.\n\nIn the iris, miosis occurs due to the constriction of the pupillary sphincter muscles and mydriasis is due to the contraction of the dilator pupillae (radial) muscles. They counter-balance each other.\n\nWhen muscarinic antagonists are instilled, the pupillary sphincter muscle is paralyzed, causing mydriasis due to the unopposed contraction of the dilator pupillae muscle.\n\nAdrenergic alpha agonists also cause mydriasis, but by mediating the contraction of the dilator pupillae muscle of iris.\n\nParasympatholytic drugs are classified as:\n\nClass\nDrugs\n\nMuscarinic antagonists\n\n• Atropine\n\n• Scopolamine\n\n• Homatropine\n\n• Tropicamide\n\n• Ipratropium, Tiotropium\n\n• Darifenacin, Solifenacin\n\n• Pirenzipine, Telenzipine\n\n• Glycopyrrolate\n\n• Umeclidinium\n\nNicotinic antagonists\nNn antagonists\n\n• Trimethaphan\n\n• Hexamethonium\n\n• Mecamylamine\n\nNm antagonists\nDepolarizing\n\n• Suxamethonium\n\n• Decamethonium\n\nNon-depolarizing\n\n• Pancuronium\n\n• Vecuronium\n\n• Rocuronium\n\n• d-tubocurarine\n\n• Mivacurium\n\n• Atracurium","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"To achieve which of the following states will you use phenylephrine instead of atropine?","o":["Mydriasis without cycloplegia","Only cycloplegia","Both mydriasis and cycloplegia","Mydriasis and controlled cycloplegia"],"a":0,"e":"Atropine is substituted by phenylephrine in situations where mydriasis is required without cycloplegia, like in fundoscopy.\n\nPhenylephrine, an alpha 1 agonist, acts on the radial muscle (dilator pupillae) of the iris and causes isolated mydriasis. It has no cycloplegic action because sympathetic receptors are present only on the iris and not on the ciliary body.\n\nAtropine has both mydriatic and cycloplegic action, which is required for refraction testing.\n\nIn elderly people who have a low ciliary tone, phenylephrine is preferred as a mydriatic. This is because other anticholinergics can precipitate or aggravate glaucoma by relaxation of the ciliary body and altering the forces acting on trabecular meshwork for aqueous outflow.\n\nAtropine, cyclopentolate, and tropicamide are antimuscarinics that are available for ophthalmic use and cause cycloplegia to a variable extent. This cycloplegia/paralysis of accommodation occurs due to their parasympatholytic action, which decreases the tone of the ciliary body.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a short-acting mydriatic that you can use for performing a fundoscopy?","o":["Atropine","Homatropine","Cyclopentolate","Tropicamide"],"a":3,"e":"Tropicamide is a short-acting mydriatic used in fundoscopy.\n\nIt has a rapid onset (20-40 minutes) and a brief duration of action (3-6 hours). The cycloplegia produced by tropicamide is unreliable and insignificant. It is indicated for fundoscopy and refraction testing in adults who have a relatively low ciliary tone when compared to children.\n\nAtropine, which produces complete cycloplegia, is preferred in children as they have a higher ciliary tone. Atropine is usually preferred over other mydriatics for accurate refractory error testing because of reliable cycloplegia. However, it can also precipitate an acute attack of glaucoma and should not be used in patients with glaucoma.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old diabetic patient presents to the OPD for diabetic retinopathy screening. Why would you prefer phenylephrine over other anticholinergic drugs as a mydriatic agent before performing fundoscopy?","o":["High ciliary tone in elderly patients","Anticholinergics can precipitate glaucoma","Phenylephrine can decrease ciliary tone in elderly patients","All of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Phenylephrine is preferred in elderly people as other anticholinergics can precipitate or aggravate glaucoma.\n\nElderly people have low ciliary tone, and phenylephrine does not alter the tone of the ciliary body. Other anticholinergics cause relaxation of the ciliary body and alter the forces acting on trabecular meshwork for aqueous outflow.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A young man with a history of motion sickness presents to your OPD with upcoming plans for a road trip. What is the most appropriate treatment for this condition?","o":["Scopolamine patch one night before","Ondansetran one night before and also before trip","Domperidone on the previous night","Dimenhydrinate 1 hour before"],"a":0,"e":"Scopolamine (Hyoscine) transdermal patch is the most appropriate treatment for motion sickness before a trip.\n\nIt should be applied behind the ear about 5-6 hours before the journey, and gives protection for around 72 hours against motion sickness, after which the patch can be replaced if necessary.\n\nScopolamine is also used for narcoanalysis as it causes sedation and amnesia.\n\nDimenhydrinate is a first-generation antihistamine that can also be used in motion sickness. Although dimenhydrinate may have equal efficacy it is not preferred because of its sedating properties.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following agents shows M3 receptor selectivity?","o":["Oxybutynin","Trospium","Tolterodine","Solifenacin"],"a":3,"e":"M3 receptor selectivity is shown by solifenacin and darifenacin.\n\nOxybutynin and trospium do not show any selectivity. Tolterodine has a more specific action on the urinary bladder but subtype selectivity is absent.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Among the following antimuscarinic drugs used in the treatment of overactive bladder, which one shows direct spasmolytic activity on smooth muscles?","o":["Tolterodine","Darifenacin","Oxybutynin","Solifenacin"],"a":2,"e":"Oxybutynin is an antimuscarinic drug indicated in the overactive bladder that also exerts a direct spasmolytic action on the smooth muscles of the bladder.\n\nDirect spasmolytic activity is the direct smooth muscle relaxation effect independent of relaxation caused by antimuscarinic action.\n\nThe most important adverse effects caused by the use of muscarinic antagonists for an overactive bladder are xerostomia (most common reason for discontinuation), blurred vision, and GI side effects like constipation and dyspepsia (due to blockade of M1, M2 & M3). Drowsiness, dizziness, and confusion can also occur.\n\nTolterodine, darifenacin, and solifenacin are antimuscarinic agents but they do not have the additional direct antispasmodic effect.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true regarding the effects of muscarinic antagonists on the urinary bladder?","o":["Lowers intravesicular pressure","Lowers bladder capacity","Lowers the frequency of bladder contractions","Alters bladder sensation during filling"],"a":1,"e":"Muscarinic antagonists increase bladder capacity by blocking M3 receptors on the detrusor. This results in the relaxation of the bladder.\n\nEffects of muscarinic antagonists on the bladder:\n\n• Lowers intravesicular pressure\n\n• Increases bladder capacity\n\n• Lowers the frequency of bladder contractions\n\n• Alters bladder sensation during filling","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"After suffering from a stroke, a middle-aged patient was diagnosed with overactive bladder due to detrusor instability. Which of the following drugs is not useful in this condition?","o":["Solifenacin","Tolterodine","Flavoxate","Duloxetine"],"a":3,"e":"Duloxetine is not useful in detrusor instability. It is a selective serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor that is efficacious in stress urinary incontinence. It is also indicated in depression, fibromyalgia, and neuropathic pain.\n\nMuscarinic antagonists are useful in this case. They increase bladder capacity by blocking M3 receptors on detrusor. This results in the relaxation of the bladder.\n\nThe effects of muscarinic antagonists on the bladder are:\n\n• Lowering of intravesicular pressure\n\n• Increases bladder capacity\n\n• Lower the frequency of bladder contractions\n\n• Alter bladder sensation during filling","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"In a patient with overactive bladder, what is the most common reason for him to discontinue treatment with muscarinic antagonists?","o":["Xerostomia","Blurred vision","Constipation","Dyspepsia"],"a":0,"e":"Xerostomia is the most common reason for the discontinuation of muscarinic antagonists in overactive bladder.\n\nThe most important adverse effects caused by the use of muscarinic antagonists for an overactive bladder are xerostomia, blurred vision, and GI side effects like constipation and dyspepsia (due to blockade of M1, M2 & M3). Drowsiness, dizziness, and confusion can also occur.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following has selective action on M1 and M3 receptors?","o":["Ipratropium","Tiotropium","Atropine methonitrate","Umeclidinium"],"a":1,"e":"Tiotropium has a selective action on M1 and M3 receptors.\n\nTiotropium binds to all M1, M2 and M3 receptors. However, it dissociates slower from M1 and M3 receptors as compared to M2. This shows it has a higher affinity towards M1 and M3 receptors.\n\nIpratropium and atropine methonitrate do not have any subtype selectivity on muscarinic receptors.\n\nInhibition of mucociliary clearance by atropine results in the accumulation of lower airway secretions and the formation of mucus plugs. However, tiotropium and ipratropium have minimum interference with mucociliary clearance when compared to atropine, and the incidence of mucus plugs is also less.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is the use of trospium contraindicated?","o":["10-year-old boy with bronchial asthma","45-year-old woman with hypertension","60-year-old man with urinary retention","50-year-old woman with peptic ulcer disease"],"a":2,"e":"The use of trospium is contraindicated in urinary retention. It is an antimuscarinic agent and can worsen urinary retention.\n\nMuscarinic antagonists are contraindicated in urinary tract obstruction and in benign prostatic hyperplasia (or used with extreme caution).\n\nBronchial asthma, hypertension, and peptic ulcer disease are not the contraindications for the use of trospium.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following does not produce central anticholinergic syndrome?","o":["Glycopyrrolate","Atropine","Antihistaminics","Tricyclic antidepressants"],"a":0,"e":"Glycopyrrolate usually does not produce central anticholinergic syndrome. It is a quaternary amine compound and lipid insoluble. Therefore, glycopyrrolate does not cross the blood-brain barrier.\n\nNon-depolarising skeletal muscle relaxants are used in anesthesia. Neostigmine along with glycopyrrolate are used for reversal of this neuromuscular block. Glycopyrrolate blocks the peripheral muscarinic side effects due to the increased acetylcholine levels after the administration of neostigmine, without causing unwanted central anticholinergic effects.\n\nPeripheral and central anticholinergic syndromes are reactions due to either overdose or any unusual reaction to the usage of drugs like atropine and other muscarinic agents, phenothiazines, antihistamines, and tricyclic antidepressants\n\nOption B: Atropine is a tertiary compound that can cross the blood-brain barrier and cause central anticholinergic syndrome.\n\nOption C and D: Some of the first-generation antihistamines and tricyclic antidepressants have significant anticholinergic activity and can cause central anticholinergic syndrome.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is most commonly used for narcoanalysis?","o":["Atropine sulfate","Pentothal sodium","Phenobarbitone","Scopolamine hydrochloride"],"a":1,"e":"The most commonly used drug in narcoanalysis is pentothal sodium (thiopentone).\n\nOther drugs used in narcoanalysis are sodium amytal and secobarbital.\n\nScopolamine hydrochloride was also used in narcoanalysis during World War II. It was popularly known as the 'lie detector'. The amnesic and depressant actions of scopolamine are due to the inhibition of the brain cholinergic system. It would put the subject off guard in case of sustained interrogation and sleep deprivation, eventually forcing the truth out.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Inhibitory effect on mucociliary clearance is minimal with which of the following drugs?","o":["Atropine","Tiotropium","Ipratropium","Both b and c"],"a":3,"e":"Both ipratropium and tiotropium have a minimal inhibitory effect on mucociliary clearance when compared to atropine. Inhibition of mucociliary clearance by atropine results in accumulation of lower airway secretions and formation of mucus plugs.","t":"Parasympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a selective alpha 1 receptor antagonist?","o":["Phenoxybenzamine","Phentolamine","Prazosin","Idazoxan"],"a":2,"e":"Prazosin is a sympatholytic drug that is a selective α1 receptor antagonist.\n\nThe sympatholytics are classified into α and β-blockers.\n\nMiscellaneous antiadrenergic drugs that inhibit synthesis or release of neurotransmitters include:\n\n• Metyrosine\n\n• Reserpine\n\n• Tetrabenazine\n\n• Deutetrabenazine","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69c50cb9f3a1486b88a7be979ebdaadbx1280x1788.JPEG"],"t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding prazosin?","o":["Blocks all subtypes of alpha 1 receptors","It is only an arteriolar dilator","It has a beneficial effect on lipid profile","Causes minimal reflex tachycardia"],"a":1,"e":"Prazosin causes dilation of both arterioles and veins.\n\nAs a result, both afterload and preload decrease. However, the reflex tachycardia is less pronounced due to the absence of α2 blockade.\n\nPrazosin blocks all subtypes of the α1 receptor (α1A, α1B, and α1D). It also has a beneficial effect on lipid profile as it decreases LDL and triglycerides and increases HDL levels.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients would prazosin not be used therapeutically?","o":["45-year-old man with peripheral vascular disease","25-year-old woman with pheochromocytoma","30-year-old male with ejaculatory dysfunction","10-year-old boy stung by a scorpion"],"a":2,"e":"Prazosin is not useful to treat a patient with ejaculatory dysfunction. It is indicated in relieving symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).\n\nPrazosin is an α1 receptor antagonist and is useful in:\n\n• Relieving vasospasm in peripheral vascular diseases\n\n• Controlling the symptoms of pheochromocytoma preoperatively (as an alternative to phenoxybenzamine)\n\n• Mainstay treatment for scorpion sting along with antivenom","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with complaints of dizziness while getting up from bed. He tells you he was recently diagnosed with BPH and has been taking prazosin. Which of the following drugs would be a good replacement for him?","o":["Terazosin","Silodosin","Doxazosin","Alfuzosin"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of postural hypotension caused by prazosin. These symptoms can be avoided by using silodosin which has a selective antagonist action on α1A receptors over α1B receptors.\n\nThe prostate stroma has a maximum concentration of α1A receptors over other α1 receptor subtypes. α1B receptors are present on blood vessels which play a role in vasoconstriction. Hence, selective α1A receptor antagonists have minimal effect on blood pressure as compared to non-selective α1 antagonists like prazosin.\n\nα1 receptor antagonists can be classified as:\n\n• Selective antagonists\n\n• Silodosin (α1A)\n\n• Tamsulosin (α1A + α1D)\n\n• Non-selective\n\n• Prazosin\n\n• Terazosin\n\n• Doxazosin\n\n• Alfuzosin","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old male presents with complaints of a weak urine stream and frequent urination in small amounts for the past month. He also mentions that he often feels lightheaded and has low blood pressure. Which of the following alpha-blockers will you prefer to give him?","o":["Alfuzosin","Terazosin","Tamsulosin","Doxazosin"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) and tamsulosin should be given to this patient as it has the least effect on blood pressure.\n\nIt is an α-blocker that has more selectivity towards α1A/1D when compared to α1B receptors. The vasodilatory effects are mainly attributed to the blockade of α1B receptors present on blood vessels.\n\nThe relief of BPH symptoms is mainly due to the blockade of α1A receptors present predominantly in the prostatic stroma. It lacks the apoptotic effect on prostatic cells that is seen with terazosin and doxazosin.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You prescribe tamsulosin to a patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia. What is the main side effect that should be explained to the patient?","o":["Priapism","Postural hypotension","Retrograde ejaculation","Erectile dysfunction"],"a":2,"e":"Retrograde ejaculation is the main side effect of tamsulosin.\n\nIt is a selective α1A/1D blocker and the relief of BPH symptoms is mainly due to blockade of α1A receptors. Blockade of α1D receptors present in the bladder relaxes the urethral sphincter, which is normally closed during ejaculation. This results in the redirection of semen into the urinary bladder.\n\nSilodosin causes ejaculatory dysfunction due to absence of seminal emission.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a selective alpha 2 receptor antagonist?","o":["Terazosin","Yasmin","Yohimbine","Alfuzosin"],"a":2,"e":"Yohimbine is a competitive antagonist that is selective for α2 receptors. It was earlier used in the indication of erectile dysfunction but is rarely indicated now due to better results with PDE5 inhibitors.\n\nTerazosin and alfuzosin are selective alpha 1 receptor antagonists.\n\nYasmin (drospirenone) is a 4th generation progestin used as an oral contraceptive having mineralocorticoid antagonist action.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Phenoxybenzamine is indicated in the management of which of the following conditions?","o":["Pheochromocytoma","Essential hypertension","Erectile dysfunction","All of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Phenoxybenzamine is indicated in the preoperative preparation of patients with pheochromocytoma. It is also indicated for symptomatic control in inoperable and advanced pheochromocytoma.\n\nOption B: Phenoxybenzamine is not used in the management of essential hypertension because of the irreversible alpha blockade and increased risk of adverse effects.\n\nOption C: For erectile dysfunction, intracavernous phentolamine, a reversible non-selective alpha-blocker is indicated.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient with erectile dysfunction complained of unsatisfactory response to oral medication. He was then administered an intracavernosal injection of phentolamine following which he developed sustained painful erection. Which of the following can be used to reverse his condition?","o":["Phenoxybenzamine","Prazosin","Phenylephrine","Propranolol"],"a":2,"e":"The reversal of phentolamine induced priapism (painful erection) can be achieved by intracavernosal injection of phenylephrine.\n\nIntracavernosal phentolamine (α-blocker) is used for the management of impotency. Priapism is a serious adverse effect of this agent. Phenylephrine is an α agonist and counteracts phentolamine induced priapism by producing vasoconstriction.\n\nPhentolamine is useful in:\n\n• Hypertensive crisis due to clonidine withdrawal\n\n• Short term control of intra-operative hypertension in patients with pheochromocytoma\n\n• MAO inhibitor-induced hypertension in cheese reaction\n\nOptions A and B: Phenoxybenzamine and prazosin are α-receptor antagonists and can worsen priapism.\n\nOption D: Propranolol is a non-selective β-blocker and is not indicated in this condition.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is propanolol not indicated?","o":["Infantile hemangioma","Variant angina","Essential tremors","Portal hypertension"],"a":1,"e":"Propranolol is not indicated in variant angina. It can cause further constriction of coronary arteries by leaving alpha-mediated coronary vasoconstriction unopposed.\n\nUses of propranolol include:\n\n• Management of thyrotoxicosis\n\n• Management of hypertension (however, cardioselective beta-blockers are now preferred over non-selective)\n\n• Treatment of infantile hemangioma\n\n• Management of essential tremors and panic attacks\n\n• Prophylaxis of migraine\n\n• Prevention of variceal bleeding in portal hypertension","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man is diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus during a routine health evaluation. He mentions that he is on propranolol for essential tremors. You would caution him against continuing the drug due to what reason?","o":["Causes hypertension","Causes seizures","Masks hypoglycemic symptoms","Causes hypotension"],"a":2,"e":"Propranolol is generally avoided in diabetic patients because it masks the hypoglycaemic symptoms.\n\nNon-selective β blockers interfere with the counter-regulatory hormones secreted during hypoglycemia, resulting in prolonged hypoglycemia. It also decreases the perception of symptoms such as tremors, tachycardia, and nervousness. These symptoms are manifested due to stimulation of sympathetic beta receptors in response to hypoglycemia. Blunting of sympathetic response results in hypoglycemic unawareness.\n\nCardioselective beta-blockers have relatively lesser interference on the sympathetic response to hypoglycemia and are preferred in diabetic patients.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a cardioselective beta blocker with antioxidant property?","o":["Sotalol","Metoprolol","Nebivolol","Esmolol"],"a":2,"e":"Nebivolol is a cardioselective beta-blocker with additional antioxidant property.\n\nNebivolol:\n\n• Nebivolol is a third-generation, long-acting, and highly selective β1 adrenergic receptor antagonist\n\n• It stimulates NO-mediated vasodilation via β3 receptor agonism\n\n• Nebivolol is approved for the treatment of hypertension\n\n• It has potential utility in the treatment of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction\n\n• Nebivolol reduces oxidative stress and may have favorable effects on both carbohydrate and lipid metabolism\n\n• This can be used in metabolic syndrome.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Esmolol is indicated in which of the following conditions?\n\n1. Intraoperative tachycardia\n\n2. Postoperative hypertension\n\n3. Supraventricular tachycardia\n\n4. Pulmonary hypertension\n\n5. Hypertensive emergency","o":["1, 3, 5","3, 5","3, 4, 5","1, 2, 3, 5"],"a":3,"e":"Esmolol is a short-acting cardioselective beta-blocker that is indicated in:\n\n• Prevention and management of intraoperative tachycardia\n\n• Postoperative hypertension\n\n• Supraventricular tachycardia\n\n• Hypertensive emergency\n\nIt is contraindicated in pulmonary hypertension.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a false statement?","o":["Metoprolol prolongs survival in congestive cardiac failure","Celiprolol has vasodilatory property","Esmolol is mainly metabolised in the liver","Nebivolol releases nitric oxide"],"a":2,"e":"Esmolol contains an ester linkage and it is hydrolyzed rapidly by esterases in erythrocytes and not in the liver. This results in a short half-life of about 8-10 minutes.\n\nIt is used in the management of intraoperative and postoperative hypertension as it has a short duration of action and can be discontinued quickly.\n\nOption A: Metoprolol is approved for use in chronic congestive cardiac failure and has shown mortality benefit.\n\nOption B: Celiprolol has shown a relaxant effect on vascular smooth muscle and weak vasodilation due to partial selective β2 agonism.\n\nOption D: Nebivolol has the additional action of releasing nitric oxide.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Identify the third-generation beta-blocker that does not cause alpha 1 receptor antagonism.","o":["Labetalol","Carvedilol","Celiprolol","Bucindolol"],"a":2,"e":"Celiprolol is a third-generation beta blocker having no action on α1 receptors. It causes vasodilation by partial selective β2 agonist activity.\n\nLabetalol, carvedilol, and bucindolol are non-selective third-generation beta blockers that possess additional α1 receptor antagonistic action. This is responsible for their vasodilatory effect.\n\nNote: Third-generation β-blockers are those that possess additional vasodilating actions and include cardioselective and non-selective drugs.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You are about to add a beta-blocker to the treatment chart of a patient with heart failure. Which of the following is not true about the use of these drugs in this condition?","o":["Should be continued after discharge","Most effective in acute decompensated heart failure","Should be started at a low dose","Carvedilol is widely used in this condition"],"a":1,"e":"β-blockers are contraindicated in the treatment of acute decompensated heart failure (ADHF) as they can worsen the condition. They are indicated only in patients with chronic stable heart failure.\n\nOption A: Though contraindicated in ADHF, it is safe to start once the patient is euvolemic. Initiation of β-blocker prior to discharge is associated with improved outcomes and must be continued after discharge.\n\nOption C: β-blockers are initiated at a low dose and slowly titrated to decrease the risk of precipitating heart failure.\n\nOption D: Carvedilol has been shown to reduce mortality in patients with heart failure, and is widely used because of additional benefits like antioxidant properties and peripheral vasodilatory action.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true about beta-blockers?","o":["Atenolol is longer acting than metoprolol","Labetalol has both alpha and beta blocking action","Celiprolol undergoes hepatic elimination","Nadolol has longest half life"],"a":2,"e":"Celiprolol is excreted predominantly through the renal route. It is excreted unchanged in the urine.\n\nOption A: Atenolol is longer acting with a longer half-life than metoprolol. It is usually administered once daily.\n\nOption C: Labetalol is a non-selective β-blocker with α1 blocking action similar to carvedilol.\n\nOption D: Nadolol has the longest half-life of 20 hours and is administered once daily.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A nurse in the nephrology ward is managing the drug inventory for patients with severe renal failure. Which of the following beta-blockers is least likely to be present in the drug charts?","o":["Carvedilol","Metoprolol","Sotalol","Labetalol"],"a":2,"e":"Sotalol is least likely to be present in the treatment charts as it is contraindicated in severe renal failure. It has low lipid solubility and is excreted primarily unchanged in the urine.\n\nCarvedilol, metoprolol and labetalol are metabolised by the liver and can be used in renal failure.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Beta-blockers are not indicated in which of the following patients?","o":["65-year-old woman with hypothyroidism","35-year-old factory worker with alcohol withdrawal","40-year-old chronic alcoholic with portal hypertension","22-year-old singer with performance anxiety"],"a":0,"e":"Beta-blockers are not indicated in hypothyroidism. They are indicated in hyperthyroidism and thyrotoxicosis that manifests with increased sympathetic activity.\n\nOptions B and C: Beta-blockers are useful in the treatment of alcohol withdrawal and in reducing variceal bleeding in portal hypertension.\n\nOption D: β blockers like propranolol are effective in controlling acute panic symptoms and anxiety associated with performing in public. They reduce symptoms of increased sympathetic activity such as tachycardia and tremors.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a selective beta 2 antagonist?","o":["Betaxolol","Butoxamine","Levobunalol","Celiprolol"],"a":1,"e":"Butoxamine is a selective β2 antagonist.\n\nThere are no clinical indications currently for selective β2 antagonists and they are still being researched.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Cocaine-like effect of inhibiting neuronal uptake of noradrenaline is seen with which of the following?","o":["Propranolol","Metaprolol","Labetalol","Acebutolol"],"a":2,"e":"Cocaine-like effect of inhibiting neuronal uptake of noradrenaline (NE) is seen with labetalol.\n\nIt is a third-generation non-selective β blocker with the following actions:\n\n• Non-selective β receptor blockade\n\n• Selective α1 receptor blockade\n\n• Inhibition of neuronal uptake of NE/cocaine-like effect","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Match the following beta-blockers with their receptor selectivity and additional actions.\n\nA. Pindolol\n 1. Non-selective, antioxidant\n\nB. Carvedilol\n 2. Non-selective, intrinsic agonist\n\nC.Betaxolol\n 3. Beta 1 selective, intrinsic agonist\n\nD.Acebutolol\n 4. Beta 1 selective, membrane stabilizing","o":["A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1","A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4"],"a":1,"e":"Pindolol\n Non-selective, intrinsic agonist\n\nCarvedilol\n Non-selective, antioxidant\n\nBetaxolol\n Beta 1 selective, membrane stabilizing\n\nAcebutolol\n Beta 1 selective, intrinsic agonist\n\nPindolol is a non-selective first-generation β-blocker with significant intrinsic agonist activity, which may result in a smaller reduction in heart rate and blood pressure. This is useful in patients with diminished cardiac reserve or at risk of bradycardia.\n\nCarvedilol is a non-selective third-generation β-blocker with antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties. These properties make it useful in the treatment of congestive heart failure.\n\nBetaxolol is a β1 selective third-generation β-blocker with additional calcium entry blockade activity and a slight membrane-stabilizing property. It does not have agonist activity.\n\nAcebutolol is a β1 selective second-generation β blocker having both membrane-stabilizing and intrinsic agonist activity. It is useful in the treatment of atrial and ventricular arrhythmias.","t":"Sympatholytics","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You want to prescribe a drug that increases uveo-scleral outflow while treating a patient with open-angle glaucoma. Which of the following drugs would you choose?","o":["Prostaglandin analogues","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Rho-kinase inhibitors","Beta-blockers"],"a":0,"e":"The class of anti-glaucoma drugs which act by increasing the uveo-scleral outflow are prostaglandin analogues.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following anti-glaucoma agents has no effect on aqueous outflow?","o":["Netarsudil","Timolol","Brimonidine","Latanoprost"],"a":1,"e":"Timolol is a non-selective beta-blocker. Beta-blockers reduce intra-ocular pressure primarily by reducing aqueous humor production. Another hypothesis states that by reducing ocular blood flow, there may be a decrease in ultrafiltration responsible for aqueous production.\n\nNetarsudil is a rho-kinase inhibitor that acts by increasing trabecular outflow.\n\nBrimonidine is an alpha-agonist that has a small effect on increasing both uveoscleral and trabecular outflow.\n\nLatanoprost is a prostaglandin PGF2-alpha analog that primarily acts by releasing matrix metalloproteinases, thereby increasing trabecular and uveoscleral outflow.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Dipevefrine is a prodrug of ______.","o":["Noradrenaline","Adrenaline","Clonidine","Dopamine"],"a":1,"e":"Dipivefrine is a prodrug of adrenaline (epinephrine).\n\nAdrenaline has poor corneal penetration. Dipivefrine penetrates the cornea and is converted into adrenaline by the esterases.\n\nAdrenaline lowers intraocular pressure by:\n\n• Increasing trabecular outflow - beta-2 receptor-mediated.\n\n• Increasing both trabecular and uveoscleral outflow, and decreasing aqueous production - alpha receptor-mediated.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"What is the primary mechanism of action of topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in glaucoma?","o":["Increasing trabecular outflow","Increasing uveoscleral outflow","Reducing aqueous formation","Relaxation of trabecular meshwork"],"a":2,"e":"The primary mechanism of action of topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors in glaucoma is reducing the aqueous formation. Topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act by inhibiting type II isoenzyme of carbonic anhydrase in ciliary body epithelium. This leads to a reduction in fluid transport and decreases aqueous formation and IOP.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 40-year old man who is suffering from open-angle glaucoma reads about netarsudil being approved as a novel agent for his condition. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Carbonic anhydrase inhibition","Phosphodiesterase 5 inhibition","Rho kinase inhibition","GPCR inhibition"],"a":2,"e":"Netarsudil is a rho kinase inhibitor that acts by relaxing the trabecular meshwork. This increases the outflow of aqueous humor and reduces intraocular pressure.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient who was started on treatment for glaucoma comes to the casualty with acute onset breathlessness. On auscultation, bilateral wheeze is heard. Which of the following drugs might have led to this adverse effect?","o":["Timolol","Betaxolol","Latanaprost","Acetazolamide"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute bronchial asthma which was most likely precipitated by timolol.\n\nTimolol is a non-selective beta-blocker that reduces intraocular pressure by decreasing aqueous formation. It precipitates bronchial asthma in susceptible individuals by beta-2 blockade.\n\nBetaxolol is a beta-1 selective blocker that is used in glaucoma and has a lower propensity to precipitate bronchial asthma.\n\nLatanoprost is a prostaglandin (PGF-2 alpha) analog and does not precipitate bronchial asthma.\n\nAcetazolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor and does not precipitate bronchial asthma.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line medical therapy in a patient with open-angle glaucoma?","o":["Beta blockers","Miotics","PGF 2 alpha analogs","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"a":2,"e":"The most appropriate first-line medical therapy for open-angle glaucoma is PGF-2 alpha analogs.\n\nThey act by increasing the aqueous outflow. They have largely replaced beta-blockers and can be considered as first-line agents for open-angle glaucoma because of their following properties:\n\n• Once-daily dosing\n\n• Low incidence of systemic side effects\n\n• Potent IOP-lowering effect\n\nWhen the use of PGF-2 alpha analogs or beta-blockers is contraindicated, topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors or alpha-2 agonists are used.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"You diagnose a patient with primary open-angle glaucoma and decide to prescribe a PGF2 alpha analog. Which of the following drugs would you not choose?","o":["Bimatoprost","Travoprost","Dinoprost","Tafluprost"],"a":2,"e":"Dinoprost is a derivative of natural PGF-2 alpha. It is indicated in the termination of pregnancy and post-partum hemorrhage and not for use in glaucoma.\n\nPGF2 alpha analogs that are used topically for the treatment of glaucoma are:\n\n• Latanoprost\n\n• Bimatoprost\n\n• Travoprost\n\n• Tafluprost","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can be administered orally for the treatment of glaucoma?\n\n1. Acetazolamide\n\n2. Dorzolamide\n\n3. Brinzolamide\n\n4. Dichlorphenamide\n\n5. Methazolamide","o":["1 and 5","1, 2 and 3","2, 3 and 4","1, 2, 3, 4, 5"],"a":0,"e":"Acetazolamide and methazolamide are carbonic anhydrase inhibitors that can be used orally in the treatment of glaucoma.\n\nAcetazolamide can also be administered intravenously.\n\nDorzolamide and brinzolamide are topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.\n\nDichlorphenamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor approved for the treatment of familial periodic paralysis.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs should not be given in a patient prone to developing angle-closure glaucoma?","o":["Pilocarpine","Clozapine","Rivastigmine","Neostigmine"],"a":1,"e":"Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic having significant anticholinergic action. Drugs having significant anticholinergic action should be avoided or used with extreme caution in patients prone to developing acute angle-closure glaucoma.\n\nThe anticholinergic action results in the relaxation of the sphincter pupillae muscle. This results in the iris coming in contact with the lens and thereby obstructing the movement of the aqueous from the posterior to the anterior chamber.\n\nThis results in bulging of the iris and further leads to the obliteration of the narrow iridocorneal angle. This blocks the outflow of aqueous precipitating an acute attack of glaucoma.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"While examining a patient using a corneal esthesiometer, you find that corneal sensations are decreased. Which of the following drugs could have led to this adverse effect?","o":["Beta blockers","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Cholinomimetics","Prostaglandin analogs"],"a":0,"e":"Corneal hypoesthesia is a side effect of ocular beta-blockers.\n\nFor topical use in glaucoma, beta-blockers with no/minimal membrane-stabilizing action are used. This is the reason why propranolol is not formulated for ocular use. It has the highest membrane-stabilizing action and causes maximum corneal hypoesthesia if used.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with severe eye pain to the ophthalmology OPD. On examination, there is circumcorneal congestion and the IOP is 40 mm Hg. Which of the following drugs should not be administered immediately to this patient?","o":["Acetazolamide","Timolol","Latanoprost","Pilocarpine"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of acute angle-closure glaucoma. Pilocarpine should not be administered immediately in this condition.\n\nDuring an acute attack, the elevated pressure in the anterior chamber causes pressure-induced ischemic paralysis of the iris. Pilocarpine would be ineffective in this phase.\n\nOnce the IOP is reduced by other agents and reperfusion is achieved, pilocarpine can be used to overcome the pupillary block.\n\nPilocarpine is avoided in conditions like pseudo-exfoliation syndrome, phacomorphic glaucoma, and aqueous misdirection syndrome as it may exacerbate pupillary block in such cases.\n\nTopical beta-blockers, alpha-2 agonists and prostaglandin analogs can be used to decrease the IOP initially at presentation. Oral or intravenous acetazolamide can also be used.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient who is on treatment for glaucoma presented with the following finding during his routine follow-up. Which of the following drugs can cause this adverse effect?","o":["Timolol","Latanoprost","Brimonidine","Apraclonidine"],"a":3,"e":"The patient has presented with lid retraction which is most likely caused by the use of apraclonidine. It is a congener of clonidine that has primary α2 agonist action and a subsidiary α1 agonist action.\n\nAdverse effects like mydriasis and lid retraction are common with apraclonidine.\n\nBrimonidine is also a congener of clonidine with more α2 selectivity. Mydriasis and lid retraction are uncommon with this agent.\n\nTimolol and latanoprost do not cause lid retraction.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cf4ce6ea1d734980be8dc6aca59a7202.PNG"],"t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"The ophthalmologic examination of a patient who is on anti-glaucoma drugs shows the following finding. Identify the drug that might have resulted in this adverse effect.","o":["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Anticholinesterase agents","Muscaranic antagonists","Sympathomimetic agents"],"a":1,"e":"The ophthalmological examination shows a pearly white opacity of the lens suggestive of cataract. Anticholinesterase agents like echothiophate and cholinergic agonists like pilocarpine are known to be associated with cataract formation.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/87b025569bd448c6bd82e3b3275f638b.JPEG"],"t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient who is on anti-glaucoma therapy presents with corneal findings as shown in the image below. This finding is most commonly observed with which of the following drugs?","o":["Timolol","Apraclonidine","Netarsudil","Brimonidine"],"a":2,"e":"The bilateral whorl-like pattern of cream-colored lines on the cornea seen in the image is called cornea verticillata or vortex keratopathy. It is observed with the use of netarsudil. It is usually mild and reversible and not associated with any significant visual abnormalities.\n\nNetarsudil is approved for lowering intraocular pressure in patients with open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension.\n\nOther causes of corneal verticillata are:\n\n• Fabry’s disease\n\n• Amiodarone\n\n• Chloroquine\n\n• Hydroxychloroquine\n\n• Indomethacin\n\n• Phenothiazines.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9551b059503648af8f454cfbc8127fe6.JPEG"],"t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A patient on treatment for glaucoma presented with complaints of floaters and flashing lights. He complains of seeing a dark grey curtain move across his field of vision. Which of the following can lead to this condition?","o":["Beta-blockers","Prostaglandin analogs","Cholinergic agonists","Muscarinic antagonists"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of retinal detachment. It is associated with the use of miotics i.e., both cholinergic agonists and anticholinesterase agents.\n\nMiotics produce ciliary body contraction which can lead to altered forces at the vitreous base resulting in retinal tears. Hence, they are to be used with extreme caution in patients who are at risk of developing retinal detachment.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"Your friend who is on treatment for glaucoma tells you that she feels her eyes look prettier than before. On examination, you notice increased iris pigmentation and eyelash hypertrichosis. Which of the following drugs is associated with this effect?","o":["Pilocarpine","Acetazolamide","Brimonidine","Bimatoprost"],"a":3,"e":"Bimatoprost is a PGF-2 alpha analog. PGF-2 alpha analogs are associated with eyelash hypertrichosis and iris hyperpigmentation.\n\nBimatoprost is approved for topical application in the treatment of eyelash hypotrichosis.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"What is the most probable diagnosis in a patient with a dilated pupil not responsive to 1% pilocarpine?","o":["Diabetic 3rd nerve palsy","Adie’s tonic pupil","Uncal herniation","Pharmacological block"],"a":3,"e":"The most appropriate answer would be a pharmacological block. Antimuscarinic will occupy the receptors and results in non-responsiveness to pilocarpine.\n\nIn diabetic third nerve palsy, the dilated pupil is due to the loss of innervation to constrictor pupillae. Muscarinic receptors on constrictor pupillae will be intact resulting in miosis by pilocarpine.\n\nIn Adie's tonic pupil, there is denervation hypersensitivity. Pupil responds to even lower concentrations of pilocarpine.\n\nUncal herniation causes pressure on the third nerve leading to ipsilateral mydriasis. But muscarinic receptors are intact and thus the pupil responds to pilocarpine.","t":"Drugs For Glaucoma","c":"Autonomic Nervous System"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old male was rushed to the ER with complaints of chest pain for the past 45 minutes and was started on isosorbide mononitrate. How does this drug act?","o":["By decreasing cAMP","By increasing cAMP","By decreasing cGMP","By increasing cGMP"],"a":3,"e":"Nitrates like isosorbide mononitrate act by increasing the concentration of the second messenger cGMP.\n\nThey are used to relieve angina and cause vasodilation.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eac61aac9888456281f1d360de304834x1280x1768.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not used in angina?","o":["Fasudil","Sacubitril","Atenolol","Amlodipine"],"a":1,"e":"Sacubitril is not used in angina. Sacubitril is a neprilysin inhibitor, approved for use in the treatment of heart failure.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old patient who suffers from prinzmetal angina requires a formulation of nitroglycerine which has the most rapid onset of action. Which of the following formulations of nitroglycerin can be administered to the patient?","o":["Sublingual spray","Sublingual tablet","Transdermal patch","Topical ointment"],"a":0,"e":"The sublingual route of administration has a very rapid onset of action (1-3 mins). The onset of action is more rapid when administered as a spray when compared to a tablet.\n\nThe sublingual route is used for the termination of attacks. Oral extended-release capsule and transdermal patch are used for prophylaxis. The onset of action is approximately ≥60 min.\n\nTopical nitroglycerine ointment can be used to provide symptomatic relief in patients with anal fissures.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"In patients with angina, nitroglycerine should not be administered by which of the following routes?","o":["Oral","Sublingual","Intramuscular","Intravenous"],"a":2,"e":"Intramuscular nitroglycerin should be avoided due to severe pain in the injection area in patients with angina.\n\nSublingual and intravenous nitroglycerin can be used for acute effects. Oral extended-release capsules are available for prophylactic use in angina.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man suspected to have cyanide poisoning is brought to the ER. The medical officer stabilizes the patient and asks the nurse to bring an ampoule of amyl nitrite. By which of the following routes is this drug administered?","o":["Oral","Inhalational","Intravenous","Sublingual"],"a":1,"e":"Amyl nitrite is administered via the inhalational route for acute relief in angina and cyanide poisoning.\n\nAmyl nitrite is a highly volatile liquid available in glass ampoules. Its unpleasant odor and very short duration of action have made its use obsolete in angina.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"An 18-year-old boy was brought to the ER in an unconscious state. His coworker states he works in a factory manufacturing pesticides. Based on this, the medical officer suspects cyanide poisoning and administers sodium nitrite. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug in this case?","o":["Produces methemoglobinemia","Produces sulfhemoglobinemia","Produces carboxyhemoglobinemia","Produces deoxyhemoglobinemia"],"a":0,"e":"The mechanism of action of sodium nitrite as an antidote for cyanide poisoning is by the development of methemoglobinemia.\n\nCyanide binds to the cytochrome oxidase (complex IV) of the electron transport chain (ETC), thereby blocking it. When sodium nitrite is administered, it releases the nitrite ion. This ion binds to hemoglobin to form methemoglobin. The methemoglobin so formed, has a high affinity for the cyanide ion and binds to it, thus, detaching it from the cytochrome oxidase. This regenerates active cytochrome normalizing the function of ETC.\n\nThe cyanide ion with methemoglobin forms cyanmethemoglobin. Sodium thiosulphate is administered for the elimination of this compound. The thiosulfate forms thiocyanate ion with the cyanide and this is readily excreted.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man came to the casualty with complaints of abdominal pain. On examination, it is noted that the pain radiates from the loin to the groin of the patient. The resident wants to administer a drug to the patient for symptomatic relief. Which drug is not effective in this condition?","o":["Paracetamol","Aspirin","Morphine","Nitrates"],"a":3,"e":"Abdominal pain radiating from loin to groin is suggestive of renal colic. Nitrates are not effective in renal colic.\n\nIn renal colic, NSAIDs, and morphine are used for acute pain relief. Due to the short time of action of nitrates, their clinical efficacy as relaxants in genitourinary system is quite low.\n\nTherapeutic Uses of nitrates are:\n\n• Angina pectoris\n\n• Acute coronary syndrome\n\n• Myocardial infarction\n\n• CHF\n\n• Biliary colic\n\n• Esophageal spasm","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old male, who works in a factory that manufactures explosives, came to the OPD with a peculiar complaint. He says that he gets headaches and feels transient dizziness every Monday after his weekend off. Exposure to which compound is likely to produce his symptoms?","o":["Nitroglycerin","Benzodiazepines","Propranolol","Verapamil"],"a":0,"e":"The patient is most likely suffering from Monday disease which is associated with exposure to nitroglycerin.\n\nContinuous exposure to high levels of nitrates can occur in the chemical industry, especially where explosives are manufactured.\n\nWhen contamination of the workplace with nitrate compounds is severe, workers find that upon starting their week, they suffer headaches and transient dizziness. After a day or so back at work, tolerance develops. Over the weekend, when exposure to the chemicals is reduced, tolerance disappears, so symptoms recur each Monday.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60 year old male is on verapamil for angina. Which of the following is not an adverse effect of this medication?","o":["Gingival hyperplasia","Headache","Constipation","Tachycardia"],"a":3,"e":"Tachycardia is not an adverse effect of verapamil.\n\nVerapamil has a direct negative chronotropic effect on the heart. It is more likely to cause bradycardia because of its significant cardio depressant action. Any reflex stimulation of heart due to peripheral vasodilation is effectively countered by the direct effect.\n\nCommon side effects of verapamil include headache, gingival hyperplasia, and constipation.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Nifedipine acts by blocking ____calcium channels.","o":["T- type","R-type","N-type","L - type"],"a":3,"e":"Nifedipine acts by blocking L-type calcium channels.\n\nVerapamil, diltiazem, and dihydropyridines block L-type calcium channels by binding to alpha 1 subunit. Verapamil was the first clinically used calcium channel blocker.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is a newer dihydropyridine with additional N-type calcium channel blocking activity?","o":["Amlodipine","Nimodipine","Clevidipine","Cilnidipine"],"a":3,"e":"Cilnidipine is a novel dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that blocks both L- and N-type calcium channels.\n\nBlockage of N-type calcium channels results in the reduction of sympathetic activity. It provides additional anti-hypertensive action without inducing reflex tachycardia.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is more likely to precipitate angina?","o":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Diltiazem","Verapamil"],"a":1,"e":"Amongst the given calcium channel blockers, nifedipine is more likely to precipitate angina.\n\nNifedipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker having a rapid onset and short duration of action. It results in an abrupt decrease in blood pressure resulting in reflex tachycardia. Increased contractility leads to an increase in O2 demand which may precipitate angina.\n\nOption A: Amlodipine and extended-release preparations of nifedipine do not cause a steep fall in blood pressure and subsequent activation of sympathetic tone. Therefore, they are less likely to precipitate angina.\n\nOptions C and D: Verapamil and diltiazem have relatively less action on peripheral vascular smooth muscles. They have a direct inhibitory effect on cardiac tissue causing negative ionotropic effect (verapamil > diltiazem).","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with polycystic kidney disease came to the ER with complaints of severe headache. An NCCT brain was taken and it showed subarachnoid hemorrhage. Which of the following drugs is most appropriate for this patient?","o":["Nifedipine","Amlodipine","Nicardipine","Nimodipine"],"a":3,"e":"Nimodipine is the most appropriate drug that can be given to this patient.\n\nNimodipine is indicated in cerebral vasospasm following subarachnoid hemorrhage. It has a high affinity for cerebral blood vessels and appears to reduce mortality after subarachnoid hemorrhage.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Peripheral edema seen with the use of calcium channel blockers is primarily due to which of the following mechanisms?","o":["Reduced glomerular filtration rate and renal blood flow","Increased hydrostatic pressure in the capillaries of lower extremeties","Decreased oncotic pressure due to loss of plasma proteins in urine","Precapillary constriction and post capillary dilation"],"a":1,"e":"Calcium channel blockers induced peripheral edema is most likely due to increased hydrostatic pressure in the capillaries of the lower extremities.\n\nThe increased hydrostatic pressure occurs due to precapillary dilation and reflex postcapillary constriction.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old athlete complained of chest pain during his practice sessions. He is diagnosed to be suffering from exertional angina and is prescribed atenolol. Which of the following changes are not caused by this drug?","o":["Decrease in cardiac contractility","Decrease in heart rate","Decrease in blood pressure","Decrease in coronary collateral circulation"],"a":3,"e":"Atenolol is a beta-blocker which increases the coronary collateral circulation, thereby increasing the blood flow towards the ischaemic region. This provides relief in exertional angina.\n\nBeta-blockers decrease the heart rate, contractility, and arterial blood pressure. These effects are beneficial by decreasing the myocardial O2 demand. A decrease in heart rate increases the time available for myocardial perfusion.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can be used to balance the undesirable effects of beta-blockers in angina?","o":["Verapamil","Nitrates","Ivabradine","Diltiazem"],"a":1,"e":"Beta-blockers cause an increase in end-diastolic volume and ejection time. These effects can be balanced using nitrates by preferential venous dilatation, thus reducing preload.\n\nBeta-blockers also block the baroreceptor mediated reflex tachycardia caused by nitrates due to vasodilation.\n\nOptions A and D: Verapamil and diltiazem are not combined with beta-blockers as they can cause additive suppression of SA node and AV nodal conduction.\n\nOption C: Ivabradine is a pacemaker I(f) \"funny\" current inhibitor. Its addition does not reverse the undesirable effect but only adds to the bradycardia caused by beta blockers.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is associated with coronary steal phenomenon?","o":["Dipyridamole","Diltiazem","Propranolol","Verapamil"],"a":0,"e":"Coronary steal phenomenon is seen with dipyridamole.\n\nIn patients with partial obstruction of coronary vasculature, a region of ischemia develops in the myocardium. In the absence of drugs, arterioles in the ischemic areas are usually maximally dilated as a result of local compensatory mechanisms.\n\nWhen dipyridamole is administered, it acts as a nonselective arteriolar dilator. It can only dilate the vessels of the normal myocardium as the vessels of the ischemic myocardium are already maximally dilated. This diverts (steals) the blood away from the ischemic myocardium towards the normal myocardium. This is termed as coronary steal phenomenon.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about ranolazine is not true?","o":["It is recommended as first-line treatment for acute angina.","Hypotension is uncommon.","It improves glycemic control.","It is metabolised by CYP3A4"],"a":0,"e":"Ranolazine is not recommended as a first-line agent for acute angina. It is approved as a second-line agent for chronic angina.\n\nRanolazine acts by reducing late sodium current (INa) that facilitates calcium entry via the sodium-calcium exchanger. The reduction in intracellular calcium concentration results in decreased cardiac contractility. Additionally, it also reduces cardiac fatty acid oxidation and stimulates glucose metabolism. It results in a small decrease in HBA1c, thus improving glycemic control.\n\nIt is metabolized by CYP3A4 and hence should not be used with CYP3A4 inhibitors such as macrolides, imidazole antibiotics, and HIV protease inhibitors.\n\nThe most frequent side effects are dizziness, headache, nausea, and constipation. Hypotension is uncommon.","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a rho-kinase inhibitor?","o":["Fasudil","Ranolazine","Trimetazidine","Nicorandil"],"a":0,"e":"Fasudil is a selective RhoA /rho-kinase inhibitor.\n\nThe Rho kinases (ROCK) are a family of enzymes that inhibit vascular relaxation. Their excessive activity has been noted in coronary spasm and pulmonary hypertension. Inhibiton of these enzymes results in vascular relaxation.\n\nOption B: Ranolazine acts by reducing late sodium current (INa). This reduces calcium entry via the sodium-calcium exchanger.\n\nOption C: Trimetazidine is a partial fatty acid oxidation (pFOX) inhibitor.\n\nOption D: Nicorandil is an agonist of KATP channels","t":"Anti Anginal Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the half-life of digoxin?","o":["24 hrs","40 hrs","72 hrs","60 hrs"],"a":1,"e":"The plasma half-life of digoxin is around 40 hours (36-40 hours).\n\nDigitoxin\nDigoxin\n\nOral absorption\nVery good (90-100%)\nGood (60-80%)\n\nPlasma protein binding\n95%(t1/2 5-7 days)\n25%(t1/2 40 hrs)\n\nElimination\nHepatic metabolism\nRenal excretion (by glomerular filtration)\n\nDose adjustment in\nLiver failure\nKidney failure\n\nTherapeutic levels of digoxin in the range of 0.5-0.8 ng/ml are beneficial. An increase in levels >1.2 ng/mL is associated with increased mortality.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Digitalis inhibits Na+/ K+ ATPase pump in cardiac muscle cell membrane. How does this lead to its positive inotropic effect?","o":["By decreasing activity of Na+/Ca++ exchanger causing decreased influx of sodium and decreased efflux of Ca++","By decreasing efflux of Na+ leading to less negative resting membrane potential and opening of voltage gated Ca++ channels on the T tubules","By increasing intracellular Na+ causing increased efflux of Na+ and increased influx of Ca++ through Na+/Ca++ exchanger","By increasing intracellular Na+ decreasing the activity of Ca++ pump in the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"a":0,"e":"The positive ionotropic effect of digitalis is due to decreased activity of Na+/Ca2+ exchanger causing a decreased influx of sodium and decreased efflux of Ca2+.\n\nDigitalis selectively binds and inhibits the Na+/ K+ATPase pump of myocardial fibers which normally pushes Na+ outside and brings K+ inside. When this pump is inhibited, there is an intracellular accumulation of Na+ ions. This results in a relative decrease in the expulsion of Ca2+ ions via the Na+-Ca2+ exchanger (NCX) leading to the accumulation of calcium inside the cells resulting in increased contractility.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32226b02e4fb4015ac8795d22c1a6cf6x1280x1527.JPEG"],"t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"The action of digoxin will not be affected in which of the following patients?","o":["43-year-old man with chronic liver disease","30-year-old woman with electrolyte disturbances","82-year-old man with renal failure","60-year-old woman with myocardial infarction"],"a":0,"e":"The action of digoxin is not affected in chronic liver disease.\n\nOption B: Electrolyte disturbances like hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and hypercalcemia increase digitalis toxicity.\n\nOption C: Digoxin is excreted unchanged primarily by kidneys by glomerular filtration. Therefore, dose adjustment of digoxin has to be done in renal failure.\n\nOption D: Chances of arrhythmias increases manifold in post-MI duration. Hence, it is generally avoided post-MI as digitalis itself causes arrhythmias.\n\nDigitoxin\nDigoxin\n\nOral absorption\nVery good (90-100%)\nGood ( 60-80%) i/v also\n\nPlasma protein binding\n95%\n25%\n\nElimination\nHepatic metabolism\nRenal excretion (by glomerular filtration)\n\nDose adjustment in\nLiver failure\nKidney failure","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following does not increase the risk of digoxin toxicity?","o":["Renal impairment","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypomagnesemia"],"a":1,"e":"Digoxin toxicity is not increased by hyperkalemia.\n\nIn hyperkalemia, the ATPase alpha subunit is dephosphorylated, which is the binding site of digoxin. This reduces it's binding and effect. Therefore, hyperkalemia does not increase digoxin toxicity.\n\nFactors predisposing digoxin toxicity:\n\n• Hypokalemia (more digoxin binds to the Na+/K+ ATPase)\n\n• Hypercalcemia (increases the risk of arrhythmia)\n\n• Hypomagnesemia (Mg++ is a co-factor for Na+/K+ ATPase)\n\n• Renal failure (for digoxin)","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is to be started on digoxin therapy for heart failure. Despite already being on another drug, dose adjustment of digoxin is not necessary for this patient. Identify this drug.","o":["Amiodarone","Quinidine","Captopril","Levosimendan"],"a":3,"e":"Levosimendan does not increase digitalis toxicity and hence would not require dose adjustment of digoxin.\n\nIt is a combined Ca2+ channel sensitizer (troponin C binding) and PDE3 inhibitor.\n\nDigoxin is metabolized by P-glycoprotein and drugs that inhibit it increase the plasma levels of digoxin and may result in toxicity. The drugs that increase digoxin toxicity are:\n\n• Amiodarone\n\n• Quinidine\n\n• Propafenone\n\n• Verapamil\n\n• Captopril\n\n• Erythromycin\n\n• Gentamycin\n\n• Ranolazine","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the most appropriate treatment for a patient with severe digoxin toxicity?","o":["Potassium supplements","Propafenone","Quinidine","Fab fragments of digoxin antibodies"],"a":3,"e":"The most appropriate treatment for severe digoxin toxicity is purified Fab fragments of digitalis antibodies (Digibind).\n\nIt neutralizes the digoxin action and its dosage is based upon the total dose of digoxin ingested.\n\nOption A: KCl infusion is indicated in mild toxicity as hyperkalemia decreases the binding of digoxin by dephosphorylating Na/K ATPase pump. In acute severe poisoning, potassium is not indicated as it may already be high in plasma. Potassium is also not indicated when a higher degree of AV block is present.\n\nOptions B and C: Quinidine and propafenone are not indicated in arrhythmias caused by digoxin.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"An ECG is done for a patient suspected to have digoxin toxicity. Which of the following would you not expect to see on the ECG?","o":["Ventricular bigeminy","Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with fast ventricular rate","Regularisation of atrial fibrillation","Bidirectional ventricular tachycardia"],"a":1,"e":"Digoxin can cause either paroxysmal or non-paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with variable A-V block but not a fast ventricular rate.\n\nRegularisation of atrial fibrillation with complete A-V block and junctional or ventricular escape rhythm can occur because of the blockade of A-V conduction. Due to the blockade of AV node by digoxin, it is contraindicated in WPW syndrome.\n\nCommon arrhythmias associated with digoxin toxicity:\n\n• Ventricular bigeminy\n\n• Atrioventricular junctional rhythm\n\n• Bidirectional ventricular tachycardia\n\n• Premature ventricular depolarizations.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with heart disease presents to the emergency with severe vomiting and blurry vision and mentions that he sees a greenish-yellow tinge over all objects. He then develops ventricular arrhythmia. What would be the most appropriate drug for immediate management?","o":["Procainamide","Lignocaine","Atropine","Propranolol"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of digoxin toxicity. Xanthopsia i.e., yellowing of vision is characteristically seen in this condition. Lignocaine (and phenytoin) has the least effect on AV nodal conduction and is the most appropriate drug for the treatment of digoxin-induced arrhythmias.\n\nOption A: Procainamide and other class IA anti-arrhythmic agents are not indicated in digoxin toxicity because of their inhibitory effects on AV nodal conduction.\n\nOption C: Atropine is used for atrial bradyarrhythmias associated with digoxin toxicity.\n\nOption D: Propranolol is used for atrial tachyarrhythmias associated with digoxin toxicity.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with congestive heart failure. Which of the following is not prescribed to prevent progression and revert cardiac remodelling?","o":["Beta blocker","ACE inhibitor","Digoxin","Aldosterone antagonist"],"a":2,"e":"Digoxin has no role in preventing cardiac remodelling. It increases cardiac contractility and is indicated for symptomatic management of congestive heart failure.\n\nDrugs preventing ventricular remodelling:\n\n• ACE inhibitors/ ARBS\n\n• Beta-blockers\n\n• Aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone, eplerenone)\n\nThe activation of RAAS and the sympathetic nervous system leads to the remodelling and progressive LV dysfunction. The RAAS activation is prevented by the ACE inhibitors/ARBs and the aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone), while the overactive sympathetic system is inhibited by the beta-blockers.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e588cc951734db299c6dc05ac5ec75bx1280x1301.JPEG"],"t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are starting a treatment regimen containing an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor for a patient with congestive heart failure. Which of the following is true regarding its role in the regimen?","o":["It is the drug of choice unless contraindicated","Used as an alternative to diuretics","It is a substitute for digitalis","Used as adjuncts only in resistant cases"],"a":0,"e":"ACE inhibitors and ARBs are the first choice drugs in congestive heart failure (CHF) unless contraindicated.\n\nRenin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) activation is pivotal in the development of the symptoms and disease progression in CHF.\n\nACE inhibitors and ARBs afford symptomatic as well as disease-modifying benefits in CHF by causing vasodilation, retarding/preventing ventricular hypertrophy, myocardial cell apoptosis and fibrosis.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can overcome an increase in heart rate and renin release seen in patients with CHF?","o":["Minoxidil","Metoprolol","Metolazone","Milrinone"],"a":1,"e":"Metoprolol is a selective β1 blocker. Beta blockade reduces the sympathetic stimulation of the SA node and thus reduces the heart rate. In the same way, β1 receptors in the JG apparatus are also inhibited by metoprolol, and thus, renin release is reduced.\n\nOption A: Minoxidil is a K+ channel opener that was earlier used in hypertensive emergencies for its vasodilatory action on blood vessels. This has no effect on renin release and heart rate can increase due to reflex sympathetic stimulation.\n\nOption C: Metolazone is a thiazide diuretic that can be used even in severe renal failure.\n\nOption D: Milrinone is an inodilator. It increases cardiac contractility and causes vasodilation by inhibiting the enzyme PDE3.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"You see the cardiologist prescribe a non-selective beta-blocker for a patient with congestive heart failure. He mentions that the drug has several beneficial effects. Identify the drug.","o":["Propranolol","Pindolol","Metoprolol","Carvedilol"],"a":3,"e":"The drug most likely being discussed is carvedilol which is a non-selective beta-blocker approved for use in mild to severe CHF.\n\nAdditional actions of carvedilol:\n\n• Blockade of alpha receptors\n\n• Blockade of L-type calcium channels\n\n• Antioxidant effect\n\n• Antiproliferative effect\n\nOptions A and B: Propranolol and pindolol are non-selective beta-blockers but they are not approved for use in CHF.\n\nOption C: Metoprolol is approved for use in CHF but it is a cardioselective beta-blocker.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"β blocker therapy is to be started on a patient with congestive heart failure. Which of the following statements is false about the therapy?","o":["The drugs should be started at optimum doses","The dose should be gradually increased over weeks","Special precautions should be taken in cases of NYHA class III and IV","Carvedilol and metoprolol are the preferred drugs"],"a":0,"e":"Beta-blockers are not started with optimum doses in patients with heart failure.\n\nBeta-blocker therapy in CHF requires caution, proper patient selection, and observance of several guidelines such as:\n\n• Generally, the starting dose is less than 1/8th (low dose) of the target dose is and then titrated upwards slowly over weeks (doubling every 4 weeks)\n\n• The greatest utility of beta-blocker therapy has been seen in mild to moderate CHF (NYHA I, II)\n\n• There is no place for beta-blockers in decompensated heart disease\n\n• Carvedilol, bisoprolol, and metoprolol are commonly used beta-blockers in CHF.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are going through the treatment chart of a patient with chronic congestive heart failure. Which of the following drugs will you not expect to see?","o":["Ivabradine","Digoxin","Spironolactone","Adrenaline"],"a":3,"e":"Adrenaline is used for the treatment of acute decompensated heart failure, not chronic congestive heart failure.\n\nDrugs used in chronic congestive heart failure include :\n\n• Diuretics\n\n• ACE inhibitors\n\n• Angiotensin receptor blockers\n\n• Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor - sacubitril-valsartan\n\n• Hydralazine\n\n• Nitrates\n\n• Digoxin\n\n• Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists\n\n• Ivabradine - sinus node inhibitor","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old man is brought with complaints of severe chest pain. The ECG shows anterolateral wall MI and echocardiography shows an ejection fraction of 38%. Which of the following is the drug of choice for his condition?","o":["Dopamine","Dobutamine","Epinephrine","Norpepinephrine"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute congestive heart failure with systolic dysfunction. Dobutamine is administered as it is the beta-adrenergic agonist of choice for managing this condition.\n\nDobutamine has a well-balanced action with stimulation of cardiac output. It causes less tachycardia than epinephrine and also has an added advantage of a decrease in pulmonary artery wedge pressure.\n\nAt doses that result in a positive inotropic effect, the beta-1 action on the myocardium predominates. In the vasculature, the alpha-1 effects are counterbalanced by beta-2 effects. The net result is an increase in stroke volume, augmented by a small decrease in systemic vascular resistance, thereby resulting in a decrease in afterload.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about nesiritide?","o":["It is a BNP analogue","It can be used in decompensated CHF","It can be administered orally","It causes loss of Na+ in the urine"],"a":2,"e":"Nesiritide is available for intravenous administration only as it is a peptide and is digested by peptidases in the GIT.\n\nNesiritide is a recombinant brain natriuretic peptide (BNP). It causes natriuresis and vasodilation. It has a half life of 18 minutes. It is approved to relieve dyspnoea and other symptoms in acute refractory decompensated CHF.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which enzyme degrades BNP?","o":["Neutral endopeptidase","Elastase","Pseudocholinesterase","Carboxypeptidase"],"a":0,"e":"Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) is degraded by neutral endopeptidase.\n\nNatriuretic peptides:\n\n• Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)\n\n• Brain natriuretic peptide\n\n• C-type natriuretic peptide\n\nNesiritide is a recombinant BNP approved to relieve dyspnoea and other symptoms in acute refractory decompensated CHF.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with congestive heart failure develops hyponatremia. Which oral vasopressin receptor antagonist will you prescribe for him?","o":["Tolvaptan","Conivaptan","Both a and b","Sacubitril"],"a":0,"e":"Tolvaptan is an oral vasopressin receptor antagonist.\n\nIt preferentially binds to V2 receptors over V1a receptors. It has been used in decompensated CHF patients to restore normal sodium levels.\n\nConivaptan is also a vasopressin receptor antagonist that is administered intravenously and binds to both V2 and V1a receptors with similar affinity. It is more commonly used for hyponatremia rather than CHF. Oral conivaptan is not yet available.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the mechanism of action of milrinone?","o":["Stimulation of PDE3","Inhibition of PDE3","Inhibition of PDE5","Stimulation of PDE5"],"a":1,"e":"Milrinone acts by inhibition of the enzyme phosphodiesterase-3 (PDE 3).\n\nThe PDE-3 inhibitors decrease cellular cAMP degradation, resulting in increased levels of cAMP. This results in positive ionotropic and chronotropic action of the heart. It also dilates veins and arteries thereby decreasing the preload and afterload.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A trial is being conducted to determine the efficacy of a myofilament calcium sensitizer in patients with heart failure. Which among the following is the drug likely being studied?","o":["Levosimendan","Omapatrilat","Sacubitril","Enoximone"],"a":0,"e":"Levosimendan is a myofilament calcium senstizer.\n\nCalcium sensitizers bind and induce a conformational change in thin-filament regulatory protein troponin C. Thus increasing the sensitivity of contractile myofilaments to calcium. The result is an increased force of contraction for a given cytosolic calcium concentration.\n\nOption B: Omapatrilat is a dual ACE and neprilysin inhibitor that failed clinical trials due to increased risk of angioedema.\n\nOption C: Sacubitril is a neprilysin inhibitor approved in combination with valsartan for CHF.\n\nOption D: Enoximone is a PDE-3 inhibitor.","t":"Heart Failure Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line medication for an elderly patient with hypertension?","o":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Furosemide","Chlorthalidone","Spironolactone"],"a":2,"e":"The most appropriate first-line antihypertensive among the given drugs is chlorthalidone.\n\nThiazide diuretics are recommended as one of the first-line antihypertensive drugs for elderly patients without any comorbidities. Among them, guidelines recommend chlorthalidone over hydrochlorothiazide because of its longer duration of action.\n\nFirst-choice drugs used in the treatment of hypertension in patients without any comorbidities include:\n\n• ACE inhibitors or ARBs: No evidence of superiority of one class over the other. However, ACE inhibitors have a higher risk of side effects like dry cough and angioedema.\n\n•\n\nCalcium channel blockers: Extended-release, long-acting dihydropyridines are preferred.\n\n•\n\nThiazide diuretics: Chlorthalidone is usually preferred to hydrochlorothiazide.\n\nLoop diuretics like furosemide and torsemide are less effective antihypertensives than thiazides. They are not indicated for the routine management of hypertension.\n\nIn the presence of any associated conditions or comorbidities, the first choice drugs for the treatment of hypertension would be as follows:\n\nComorbidities\n\nFirst choice drugs for hypertension\n\nDiabetes\n\nChronic kidney disease with proteinuria\n\nACE inhibitors or ARBs\n\nAngina\n\nBeta-blockers or calcium channel blockers\n\nHeart failure\n\nACE inhibitors and alpha+beta blockers (e.g., carvedilol)\n\nBenign prostatic hyperplasia\n\nα1 blockers\n\nPregnancy\n\nLabetalol\n\nAtrial fibrillation\n\nDiltiazem or verapamil","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Chlorthalidone is being prescribed to an elderly patient for the management of hypertension. Which of the following side effects would not be expected in this patient?","o":["Hyperuricemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyperglycemia"],"a":2,"e":"Thiazide diuretics cause hypokalemia.\n\nOther side effects of thiazide diuretics include hyperuricemia, hypercalcemia, hyperglycemia and erectile dysfunction. All thiazide diuretics cross the placenta and therefore should be avoided in pregnancy.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A hypertensive patient on losartan requires an add on drug to achieve his blood pressure goal. Which of the following drugs will not increase the risk of hyperkalemia when added to his current medication?","o":["Atenolol","Amiloride","Indapamide","Aliskiren"],"a":2,"e":"Indapamide is a thiazide diuretic that increases K+ excretion decreases the risk of hyperkalemia associated with ACE inhibitors or ARBs such as losartan.\n\nOption A: Beta-blockers can contribute to K+ retention by decreasing renin release and further reducing aldosterone secretion. Non-selective beta-blockers may also interfere with the cellular uptake of K+ ions.\n\nOption B: K+ sparing diuretics like amiloride cause a decrease in K+ excretion and increase the risk of hyperkalemia.\n\nOption D: Aliskiren is a direct renin inhibitor and increases the risk of hyperkalemia when combined with losartan.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Long term reduction of blood pressure by thiazide diuretics is mainly by which of the following mechanisms?","o":["Reduction in extracellular volume","Reduction in sympathetic activity","Reduction in peripheral vascular resistance","Increase in extracellular volume"],"a":2,"e":"Long term reduction of blood pressure by thiazide diuretics is mainly due to a reduction in peripheral vascular resistance.\n\nThiazide diuretics initially decrease extracellular volume by their action on NaCl transporter in distal convoluted tubules in the nephrons. However, the compensatory response from activation of RAAS raises the extracellular volume returns almost to pre-treatment levels after a few weeks.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A diabetic patient is diagnosed with hypertension during his annual health check-up. What is the treatment of choice for him?","o":["Chlorthalidone","Carvedilol","Captopril","Nifedipine"],"a":2,"e":"ACE inhibitors such as captopril or ARBs can be considered as a treatment of choice in hypertensive patients with diabetes because of the renoprotective action. It benefits diabetic patients by reducing the risk of occurrence and progression of diabetic nephropathy.\n\nThey are also recommended in patients with CKD irrespective of diabetes status as they can delay the progression of nephropathy.\n\nOption A: Thiazide diuretics worsen glycemic control and are not preferred in diabetic patients.\n\nOption B: Beta-blockers, especially non-selective blockers, worsen glycemic control.\n\nOption D: Calcium channel blockers have a neutral effect on glycemic control and can be used in diabetes if ACE inhibitors or ARBs cannot be given.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with hypertension during routine health evaluation. Which of the following is not recommended as first-line therapy for him?","o":["Verapamil","Doxazosin","Metolazone","Quinapril"],"a":1,"e":"Alpha blockers like doxazosin are not recommended as first-line therapy for uncomplicated essential hypertension.\n\nAs per the European Society of Cardiology 2018 guidelines for hypertension, the classes of drugs recommended as first line therapy are:\n\n• ACE inhibitors\n\n• Angiotensin receptor blockers\n\n• Calcium channel blockers\n\n• Thiazide diuretics","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which class of antihypertensive drugs has a favourable effect on lipid profile?","o":["Thiazide diuretics","Beta blockers","Alpha 1 selective blockers","Calcium channel blockers"],"a":2,"e":"Alpha 1 selective blockers have a favourable effect on lipid profile. They decrease plasma concentrations of triglycerides, total LDL-cholesterol and increase HDL cholesterol.\n\nOption A: Thiazides increase plasma concentrations of triglycerides, LDL-cholesterol and total cholesterol. HDL may decrease or remain unchanged.\n\nOption B: Non-selective beta-blockers increase triglycerides and decrease HDL levels. However, total cholesterol remains unchanged.\n\nOption D: Calcium channel blockers have a neutral effect on the lipid profile.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Methyldopa is primarily used for which of the following conditions?","o":["Hypertension in pregnancy","Renovascular hypertension","Hypertension with gout","Refractory hypertension"],"a":0,"e":"Methyldopa is preferred for chronic hypertension in pregnancy based on its safety and efficacy data for both mother and fetus.\n\nAntihypertensives found safe in pregnancy:\n\n• Hydralazine\n\n• Methyldopa\n\n• Dihydropyridine CCBs like nifedipine, amlodipine - however, they should be stopped before labor as they weaken the uterine contractions\n\n• Cardioselective beta-blockers and those with intrinsic sympathomimetic activity\n\nAntihypertensives to be avoided in pregnancy:\n\n• ACE inhibitors, Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARB)\n\n• Diuretics\n\n• Nonselective beta-blockers\n\n• Nitroprusside\n\nLabetalol is considered the first-line agent for hypertensive emergency in pregnancy.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A pregnant woman with hypertension, on treatment with methyldopa, comes for routine evaluation. She is found to have a positive Coombs test but is asymptomatic. What is the next course of action for this patient?","o":["Immediately stop methyldopa","Taper the dose of methyldopa","Monitor for signs of hemolysis","Switch to labetalol"],"a":2,"e":"The best course of action for the patient is close monitoring for any signs or symptoms of hemolysis.\n\nCoombs test is done to test the presence of antibodies against Rh antigens on the red blood cells, which result in hemolysis. Positive Coombs test is seen in 20% of patients who receive methyldopa.\n\nHowever, only 1-5% of such patients develop hemolytic anemia and hence, a positive Coombs test is not a necessary indication for stopping methyldopa treatment. They must be monitored closely and any signs that indicate hemolytic anemia needs prompt discontinuation of the drug.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true about clonidine?","o":["Reduction in central sympathetic flow","Prazosin completely antagonizes its action","Sedation and xerostomia are common side effects","It can be combined with vasodilators"],"a":1,"e":"Prazosin does not antagonize the effect of clonidine. Prazosin is a selective α1 receptor antagonist whereas clonidine is an alpha 2 receptor partial agonist.\n\nOn oral administration, clonidine stimulates alpha 2A receptors in CNS and results in decreased central sympathetic flow. This causes a decrease in BP.\n\nHowever, on IV administration postsynaptic alpha 2B receptors are activated at higher doses which cause a transient rise in blood pressure. It also causes significant reductions in HDL lipids, apolipoproteins AI and AII.\n\nIt can be combined with vasodilators but has to be used with caution as potentiation of each other's actions can occur.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with complaints of multiple episodes of intermittent palpitations, throbbing headache, and sweating. Blood pressure measured during one of these episodes is 210/120 mm Hg. The results of 24-hour urinary metanephrines were inconclusive. Which of the following could be used for diagnosis?","o":["Phentolamine","Phenoxybenzamine","Clonidine","Prazosin"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical vignette is suggestive of pheochromocytoma. When first-line investigations like measurement of 24-hour urinary metanephrines are inconclusive, clonidine suppression test is used in the diagnosis of pheochromocytoma.\n\nAn oral dose of 0.3 mg clonidine is given, followed by the measurement of norepinephrine (NE) concentration at 3 hours. A concentration of >500 pg/mL of NE indicates a positive test.\n\nOption A: Phentolamine test is not recommended due to its low sensitivity and specificity.\n\nOptions B and D: Phenoxybenzamine and prazosin are used in the treatment of pheochromocytoma but have no role in its diagnosis.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a selective imidazoline receptor agonist?","o":["Clonidine","Methyldopa","Moxonidine","Brimonidine"],"a":2,"e":"Moxonidine is a selective imidazoline (I1) receptor agonist.\n\nIt is indicated as a second line agent in the long-term management of hypertension. It also has weak alpha 2 agonist action.\n\nRilmenidine is another selective imidazoline receptor agonist.\n\nOption A: Clonidine is an alpha 2 receptor partial agonist and has additional imidazoline (I1) receptor agonist action. But the main mode of action is through alpha 2 agonism.\n\nOption B: Methyldopa does not have any action on imidazoline receptors\n\nOption D: Brimonidine is a congener of clonidine.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs reduces blood pressure primarily by directly decreasing the heart rate?","o":["Propranolol","Prazosin","Alpha methyl dopa","Nitroprusside sodium"],"a":0,"e":"Propranolol reduces blood pressure by directly decreasing the heart rate. It is a nonselective beta-blocker and blocks the beta 1 receptor of the heart (SA node) thus reducing the heart rate and blood pressure.\n\nIt also reduces renin secretion by blocking beta 1 receptors in JG cells. Reduction in renin release also contributes to blood pressure reduction.\n\nOption B: Prazosin is a selective alpha 1 blocker and dilates both arteries and veins and may cause reflex tachycardia.\n\nOption C: Alpha methyldopa is a selective alpha 2 receptor agonist. Alpha 2 receptors are autoreceptors present on the presynaptic membrane. On stimulation, central sympathetic discharge is reduced. Alpha methyldopa can indirectly reduce heart rate.\n\nOption D: Nitroprusside is a vasodilator and may cause reflex tachycardia.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"The blood pressure of a patient undergoing a coronary artery bypass surgery suddenly rises to 170/110mm Hg. Which of the following can you administer intravenously for immediate management?","o":["Nifedipine","Amlodipine","Clevidipine","Felodipine"],"a":2,"e":"Clevidipine is a dihydropyridine available for intravenous administration and is indicated in the acute management of hypertension. It has a short half-life (1-2 minutes) and a rapid onset of action.\n\nOption A: Nifedipine is a short-acting dihydropyridine that is no longer indicated in the management of hypertension. The rapid reduction in blood pressure and concurrent surges in sympathetic activity are not desirable.\n\nOptions B and D: Amlodipine and felodipine are longer-acting dihydropyridines that are available in tablet forms. They are used for long-term treatment of hypertension.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly hypertensive patient is diagnosed with bilateral renal artery stenosis. He is also on treatment for diabetes mellitus. Which among the following drugs would be the most appropriate for him?","o":["Enalapril","Verapamil","Metoprolol","Metolazone"],"a":1,"e":"In the given scenario, verapamil would be the most appropriate drug for the management of hypertension in the patient as it has no adverse effect on glycemic control.\n\nOption A: Efferent arteriolar constriction maintains the glomerular filtration pressure in bilateral renal artery stenosis. ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in bilateral renal stenosis as it can precipitate acute renal failure due to dilation of efferent arterioles and fall in glomerular filtration pressure.\n\nOptions C and D: Beta-blockers and thiazides can worsen glycemic control and are not suitable in the given scenario.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a direct renin inhibitor?","o":["Captopril","Sacubitril","Aliskiren","Losartan"],"a":2,"e":"Aliskiren is a direct renin inhibitor. Though its efficacy as an antihypertensive is comparable to that of ACE inhibitors, it is not considered a first-line agent due to higher incidence of side effects.\n\nOption A: Captopril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor.\n\nOption B: Sacubitril is a neprilysin inhibitor indicated in combination with valsartan in chronic heart failure patients.\n\nOption D: Losartan is an angiotensin receptor blocker.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about diazoxide?","o":["It acts by causing prolonged opening of ATP dependent K+ channels in beta cells","It can cause severe hypoglycaemia","It can be used to treat patients with insulinoma","It is used as an antihypertensive agent"],"a":1,"e":"Diazoxide causes hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia.\n\nDiazoxide is a potent K+ channel opener and opens up the ATP-sensitive K+ channel and thus prevents depolarization of pancreatic beta cells and subsequent insulin release. It is used in some cases of insulinoma to reduce insulin secretion and prevent hypoglycemia.\n\nIt was earlier used as an intravenous agent for hypertensive emergencies due to its strong vasodilatory action.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A pregnant woman with preeclampsia and is started on hydralazine. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the drug?","o":["It decreases cerebral vascular resistance","It causes equal dilatation of both arteries and veins","Postural hypotension is not a common problem","It increases plasma renin activity"],"a":1,"e":"Hydralazine causes preferential dilatation of arteries over veins.\n\nHydralazine acts directly on vascular smooth muscles. The precise mechanism is unknown but involves a decrease in intracellular Ca2+ concentrations.\n\nOption A: It causes a selective decrease in vascular resistance in coronary, cerebral and renal circulation, with a smaller effect in skin and muscle.\n\nOption C: Because of preferential dilatation of arterioles over veins, postural hypotension is not a common problem.\n\nOption D: Hydralazine-induced vasodilation causes reflex activation of the sympathetic system through baroreceptor-mediated reflexes resulting in increased plasma renin activity.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"The efficacy of an antihypertensive agent is being studied in a clinical trial. During the course of the trial, the researchers observed hair regrowth and generalized hypertrichosis in balding patients. What is the mechanism of action of the drug being studied?","o":["Closes K+ channels in vascular smooth muscle","Activates K+ channels in vascular smooth muscle","Activates Ca2+ channels in vascular smooth muscle","Closes Ca2+ channels in vascular smooth muscle"],"a":1,"e":"The antihypertensive drug with the side effect of excess hair growth is minoxidil and it acts by activating ATP-sensitive K+ channels in smooth muscles. This causes hyperpolarization that results in the relaxation of smooth muscles of arterioles.\n\nTopical minoxidil is also used for the treatment of androgenetic alopecia.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presented to the ER with severe headache and vomiting. On examination, the blood pressure was found to be 220/130 mm Hg and papilledema was also seen. What is the mechanism of the drug of choice for the condition?","o":["Increased cAMP","Calcium channel blockage","Stimulation of guanylyl cyclase","K+ channel opener"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario of severe hypertension (blood pressure ≥180/≥120 mmHg) with fundoscopic changes (flame haemorrhages and/or papilloedema) is suggestive of malignant hypertension. The drug of choice is IV nicardipine which acts by calcium channel blockage.\n\nNicardipine blocks the influx of extracellular calcium across the vascular and myocardial smooth muscle cell membranes by blocking the voltage-sensitive calcium channels during depolarization. The decrease in intracellular calcium causes dilation of the coronary and systemic arteries.\n\nOther preferred agents for malignant hypertension are clevidipine with labetalol or esmolol.\n\nNote: Sodium nitroprusside is no longer the treatment of choice for acute hypertensive problems","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with malignant hypertension is being managed with sodium nitroprusside infusion. He is at risk of developing all of the following adverse effects except?","o":["Methhemoglobinemia","Cyanide toxicity","Drug-induced lupus syndrome","Thiocyanate toxicity"],"a":2,"e":"Drug-induced lupus is not an adverse effect of sodium nitroprusside infusion.\n\nWhen nitroprusside is administered at a high rate, it can result in the production of methemoglobin and cyanide. Cyanide is further metabolized to thiocyanate. On prolonged infusion, thiocyanate toxicity can also occur especially in patients with renal failure.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"You will counsel patients regarding first-dose phenomenon while prescribing which of the following drugs?","o":["Propranolol","Prazosin","Metoprolol","Hydralazine"],"a":1,"e":"First-dose phenomenon is characteristic of prazosin and patients must be counselled regarding the effects.\n\nWithin 30-90 minutes of the initial dose of prazosin or when the dosage is increased, there is marked orthostatic hypotension or syncope. This is known as the first-dose phenomenon. After a few initial doses, tolerance develops to this side effect. Initiating low doses at bedtime is recommended to minimize this phenomenon.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can be given safely in a patient with chronic renal failure?","o":["Aluminium hydroxide","Candesartan","Telmisartan","Sucralfate"],"a":2,"e":"Telmisartan can be given safely in a patient with chronic renal failure (CRF) because it is mainly cleared from circulation by biliary excretion.\n\nOptions A and D: Sucralfate is a basic aluminum salt of sucrose sulfate. Sucralfate, aluminum hydroxide, and magnesium hydroxide in CRF patients can cause the accumulation of Al3+ and Mg2+ ions as they are preferentially excreted via the kidney after systemic absorption. Absorbed Al3+ can contribute to osteoporosis, encephalopathy and proximal myopathy in CRF patients.\n\nOption B: Candesartan is cleared from circulation by renal and biliary excretion. Hence, plasma clearance is affected by renal insufficiency.","t":"Antihypertensive Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Choose the correct pair matching with the diagram:","o":["A: Loop diuretics, B: Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","A: Loop diuretics, B: Thiazides","A: Thiazides, B: Loop diuretics","A: Thiazides, B: Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"a":1,"e":"A is the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and loop diuretics act on this part of the nephron.\n\nB is the distal convoluted tubule and thiazides act on this part of the nephron.\n\nSite of action of other diuretics:\n\n• Proximal tubule - carbonic anhydrase inhibitors\n\n• Collecting ducts and late distal tubule - epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) inhibitors and aldosterone antagonists","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/701a33667bab4e16a48659217949ffcb.JPEG"],"t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Diuretics acting on which part of the nephron have maximum efficacy?","o":["Proximal tubule","Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","Collecting tubule","Distal tubule"],"a":1,"e":"Loop diuretics have the maximum efficacy. They act on the thick ascending loop of Henle by blocking Na+-K+-2Cl- symporter.\n\nOption A: Although 65% of Na+ is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, diuretics acting here do not have maximum efficacy. This is because the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle has a great reabsorptive capacity and it absorbs most of the filtrate from the proximal tubule.\n\nOptions C and D: Diuretics acting distal to the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle have limited efficacy because most of the reabsorption of Na+ (>90%) would have already occurred by the time the tubular fluid reaches there.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about furosemide is true?","o":["Acute pulmonary edema is an indication","Acts on the proximal convoluted tubules","Causes mild diuresis","Given only by parenteral route"],"a":0,"e":"Furosemide is indicated in acute pulmonary edema. It is a loop diuretic that acts on the thick ascending loop of Henle and inhibits Na+-K+-2Cl- symporter.\n\nLoop diuretics have maximum efficacy amongst all the diuretics and are therefore called high ceiling diuretics. They can be given by both the oral and parenteral routes.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old man with pulmonary edema is started on furosemide. Which of the following would you not expect after starting the drug?","o":["Hyperglycemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hypokalemia","Acidosis"],"a":3,"e":"Furosemide causes metabolic alkalosis.\n\nThe adverse effects of furosemide (loop diuretics) are:\n\n• Hypovolemia\n\n• Hyponatremia\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Hypocalcemia\n\n• Hypomagnesemia\n\n• Hyperuricemia\n\n• Increased LDL-C and decreased HDL-C\n\n• Glucose intolerance - hyperglycemia\n\n• Ototoxicity","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"High ceiling diuretics are used in the treatment of all of the following patients except:","o":["40-year-old man with anasarca","35-year-old woman with a head injury and cerebral edema","56-year-old woman with acute pulmonary edema","48-year-old man with pulmonary hypertension"],"a":3,"e":"High ceiling diuretics are not used in pulmonary hypertension.\n\nHigh ceiling or loop diuretics have diuretic properties as well as vasodilatory action.\n\n• Diuretic property - removing excess fluid in hepatic, renal, and cardiac diseases\n\n• Vasodilatory action - quick symptomatic relief of symptoms in LVF and acute pulmonary edema. They are also used for the treatment of chronic CHF.\n\nPulmonary artery hypertension is a condition where there is increased pulmonary vascular resistance. Since nitric oxide is an important regulator of pulmonary vascular resistance, PDE5 inhibitors like sildenafil, vardenafil or tadalafil are primarily used in the treatment of pulmonary arterial hypertension.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease was given a diuretic intravenously. Following administration of the drug, she developed tinnitus and diminished hearing. Which of the following drugs was most likely given?","o":["Thiazide","Spirinolactone","Ethacrynic acid","Triamterene"],"a":2,"e":"Ethacrynic acid is the diuretic that was most likely given as it is commonly associated with ototoxicity.\n\nOtotoxicity occurs with rapid intravenous infusion and the hearing impairment and deafness are usually reversible.\n\nEthacrynic acid should be used only in patients who are not able to tolerate other loop diuretics.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors do not cause:","o":["Increased bicarbonate excretion","Urinary alkalinization","Metabolic alkalosis","Potassium loss"],"a":2,"e":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors do not cause metabolic alkalosis. They cause metabolic acidosis due to increased bicarbonate excretion\n\nOption B: Increased bicarbonate excretion causes urinary alkalinization.\n\nOption D: Increased delivery of Na+ to distal tubules causes increased K+ excretion.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can be administered intravenously?","o":["Brinzolamide","Dorzolamide","Acetazolamide","Methazolamide"],"a":2,"e":"Acetazolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that can be administered intravenously. It is indicated in the acute management of angle-closure glaucoma for the rapid reduction of IOP before surgery. The oral formulation is given for open-angle glaucoma.\n\nOptions A and B: Brinzolamide and dorzolamide are available only as ophthalmic drops and used in open-angle glaucoma.\n\nOption D: Methazolamide is available as an oral formulation and used for open-angle glaucoma.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man joins a pilgrimage to a shrine at a mountain peak. At the summit, he develops tightness in the chest, associated with breathlessness and altered sensorium. Which of the following can be given for symptomatic relief?","o":["Mannitol","Digoxin","Diltiazem","Acetazolamide"],"a":3,"e":"The symptoms are suggestive of acute mountain sickness and acetazolamide can be given for symptomatic relief.\n\nIt can also be used for prophylaxis.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a glioblastoma multiforme is started on mannitol as palliative therapy. Which of the following statements is false regarding its diuretic action?","o":["Renal blood flow is increased","Reduction in medullary tonicity","Extracellular fluid compartment is expanded","GFR is decreased"],"a":3,"e":"Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic. These drugs do not cause much change in the GFR.\n\nOsmotic diuretics cause extraction of fluid from the intracellular compartment resulting in the expansion of the extracellular compartment. The renal blood flow increases and renin release is inhibited. An increase in renal medullary blood flow removes NaCl and urea from the renal medulla thus reducing medullary tonicity. There is not much change in the total GFR.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman presents with complaints of swelling of both feet and of breathlessness on exertion. After evaluation, she was diagnosed with congestive cardiac failure. Which of the following drugs is contraindicated in this patient?","o":["Mannitol","Spironolactone","Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"a":0,"e":"Mannitol is contraindicated in patients with cardiac failure.\n\nMannitol is an osmotic diuretic. It acts by extracting water from the intracellular compartments, which results in the expansion of the extracellular fluid. This results in volume overload and frank pulmonary edema in patients with cardiac failure.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with hypertension is prescribed chlorthalidone. What would be the effect of this drug on the excretion of electrolytes?\n\nSodium\nPotassium\nCalcium\n\na.\n↑\n↑\n↑\n\nb.\n↑\n↓\n↓\n\nc.\n↑\n↑\n↓\n\nd.\n↑\n↓\n↑","o":["A","B","C","D"],"a":2,"e":"Chlorthalidone is a thiazide diuretic which increases Na+ and K+ excretion and decreases Ca2+ excretion.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Thiazide diuretics are not used in the treatment of which of the following conditions?","o":["Idiopathic hypercalciuria with nephrocalcinosis","Hyperlipidemia","Congestive heart failure","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus"],"a":1,"e":"Thiazide diuretics are not used in the treatment of hyperlipidemia. They result in an increase in the levels of LDL, total cholesterol, and triglycerides.\n\nOption A: Thiazide reduces Ca2+ excretion and is used in the treatment of idiopathic hypercalciuria with nephrocalcinosis.\n\nOption C: They are also used in the treatment of edema associated with CHF, hepatic cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome.\n\nOption D: Thiazides are the mainstay for the treatment of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. They reduce the kidney's ability to excrete free water. They increase proximal tubular water absorption and block the ability of DCT to form dilute urine.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man with hypertension is prescribed chlorthalidone. Which of the following is unlikely to occur in this patient?","o":["Hyponatremia","Hypokalemia","Erectile dysfunction","Hypocalcemia"],"a":3,"e":"Hypocalcemia is unlikely to occur in a patient on chlorthalidone, a thiazide diuretic.\n\nThiazide diuretics do not cause hypocalcemia. They result in hypercalcemia.\n\nThiazide and loop diuretics share similar side effects except that loop diuretics cause Ca2+excretion (loop loses calcium) whereas thiazides retain Ca2+ and cause hypercalcemia.\n\nOther side effects of thiazide diuretics are:\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Hyponatremia\n\n• Hypomagnesemia\n\n• Metabolic alkalosis\n\n• Hypercalcemia\n\n• Hyperuricemia","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 42-year-old man in the nephrology ward has a GFR of less than 30mL/min, suggestive of severe renal failure. What is the best drug that can be given to him?","o":["Chlorthalidone","Metolazone","Xipamide","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"a":1,"e":"Metolazone is effective in severe renal failure (GFR<30ml/min).\n\nMetolazone works primarily in the distal convoluted tubule, rather than the proximal convoluted tubule, and has little effect on GFR and can hence be used in patients with a reduced GFR. Indapamide is another thiazide diuretic that may be effective in severe renal failure.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient has been on lisinopril since being diagnosed with hypertension last year. His blood pressure readings are quite high, and the doctor considers adding a diuretic. Which amongst the following would be the ideal add on drug?","o":["Spironolactone","Eplerenone","Hydrochlorthiazide","Amiloride"],"a":2,"e":"Hydrochlorothiazide would be the ideal add-on drug. It is a thiazide diuretic and causes hypokalemia, which can counteract the hyperkalemia caused by lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor.\n\nAldosterone causes Na+ absorption in the collecting duct and the distal tubule in exchange for H+ and K+. ACE inhibitors decrease angiotensin II levels and thereby, decrease aldosterone levels. Decreased aldosterone level causes K+ retention by reducing the exchange. Therefore, ACE inhibitors cause retention of K+.\n\nDiuretics that cause elevation of plasma K+ levels are not suitable for combining with ACE inhibitors.\n\nPotassium-sparing diuretics like spironolactone, eplerenone, and amiloride are contraindicated with ACE inhibitors.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old boy is brought with complaints of vomiting and lethargy after swallowing several pills of his father’s medication. Serum electrolytes indicate hyperkalemia. Which of the following drugs can you rule out?","o":["Triamterene","Spironolactone","Amiloride","Indapamide"],"a":3,"e":"Indapamide can be ruled out as it is a thiazide diuretic that causes hypokalemia. The other drugs are potassium-sparing diuretics that cause hyperkalemia.\n\nK+ sparing diuretics work by 2 mechanisms:\n\n• Blocking the ENaC (epithelial sodium channel)\n\n• Triamterene\n\n• Amiloride\n\n• Aldosterone receptor antagonist\n\n• Spironolactone\n\n• Eplerenone\n\n• Canrenone","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following diuretics do not need to be present in the tubular lumen for pharmacological action?","o":["Thiazide diuretics","Loop diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Aldosterone antagonists"],"a":3,"e":"Aldosterone antagonist acts from the interstitial side of the tubular cells. They do not have to be present in the tubular lumen for their action.\n\nAldosterone antagonists enter the tubular cells from the basolateral membrane. They competitively inhibit the binding of aldosterone to mineralocorticoid receptors.\n\nThiazide diuretics, loop diuretics, ENaC inhibitors, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors have to be present in the lumen for its action. They are all secreted from the proximal tubule into the tubular fluid through the organic anion/ cation transport process.\n\nOsmotic diuretics reach the lumen via glomerular filtration.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Canrenone is a metabolite of:","o":["Acetazolamide","Spironolactone","Eplerenone","Metolazone"],"a":1,"e":"Canrenone is a metabolite of spironolactone.\n\nSpironolactone is metabolized completely in the liver. It is also converted to an active metabolite, canrenone that is responsible for more than 50% of the spironolactone action. Spironolactone has a half-life of 1-2 hours while canrenone has a half-life of around 18 hours.\n\nEplerenone is an aldosterone receptor antagonist similar to spironolactone and metabolized primarily by CYP3A4, but no active metabolites have been identified.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis develops ascites. What is the drug of choice for management?","o":["Chlorothiazide","Acetazolamide","Spironolactone","Mannitol"],"a":2,"e":"The diuretic of choice in a patient with cirrhotic ascites is spironolactone.\n\nSpironolactone is a competitive inhibitor of the mineralocorticoid receptor in the late distal tubule and collecting duct of the kidneys. It causes a decrease in sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the distal tubule. As a result, spironolactone promotes sodium diuresis but maintains body potassium levels.\n\nSpironolactone is particularly helpful in edematous states caused or exacerbated by hyperaldosteronism such as in cirrhotic ascites.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a side effect seen in a patient receiving spironolactone?","o":["Gynaecomastia","Hyperkalemia","Metabolic alkalosis","Renal insufficiency"],"a":2,"e":"Aldosterone antagonists cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis and not metabolic alkalosis. They inhibit H+ secretion in parallel with K+ secretion thus resulting in acidosis.\n\nOther side effects are:\n\n• Hyperkalemia\n\n• Gynecomastia\n\n• Renal insufficiency.","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an indication for amiloride use?","o":["Lithium induced polyuria","Cystic fibrosis","Calcium nephrolithiasis","Liddle syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Amiloride is not used in the treatment of calcium nephrolithiasis. The condition is sometimes treated with thiazide diuretics but not amiloride.\n\nAmiloride is a K+ sparing diuretic that acts by inhibiting the epithelial Na+ channels. The various uses of amiloride are:\n\n• Edema/hypertension - usually in combination with thiazides\n\n• Lithium-induced polyuria - blocks lithium transport into collecting tubules\n\n• Cystic fibrosis - aerosolized amiloride enhances mucociliary clearance; acts by inhibiting Na+ absorption from epithelial surfaces and increasing hydration of secretions\n\n• Liddle syndrome - hyperactive epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) activity is reduced","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old man with asthma presents with complaints of increased urination. The doctor checks that he has been taking theophylline and counsels that it could be a side effect. What is the mechanism of action causing diuresis?","o":["Adenosine antagonism","PDE-4 inhibition","Beta-2 agonism","PDE-3 inhibition"],"a":0,"e":"The diuretic effect of theophylline is due to the blockade of adenosine (A1) receptors.\n\nTheophylline is a methylxanthine drug that acts by -\n\n• Inhibition of phosphodiesterase enzyme (PDE)\n\n• PDE3 inhibition - relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle\n\n• PDE4 inhibition - inhibition cytokines and chemokine release, which leads to a decrease in immune cell migration and activation\n\n• Blockade of adenosine receptors inhibits bronchoconstriction, however, blockade of A1 receptors in the glomerular afferent arteriole and PCT causes diuresis.\n\n• Activating enzyme histone deacetylase-2, which is responsible for its anti-inflammatory action","t":"Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with ventricular tachycardia was started on a class1B anti-arrhythmic drug. Identify the drug.","o":["Mexiletine","Quinidine","Flecainide","Propafenone"],"a":0,"e":"Mexiletine is a class 1B antiarrhythmic drug. It is a lidocaine analog.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"The additional mechanism by which class 1A drugs additionally prolong action potential duration is:","o":["Blocking Na+ channels","Opening Na+ channels","Blocking K+ channels","Opening K+ channels"],"a":2,"e":"Class IA antiarrhythmics block K+ channels leading to prolongation of action potential duration.\n\nThe main action of class IA antiarrhythmics is blocking Na + channels causing:\n\n• Slowing the upstroke of the action potential\n\n• Slowing conduction\n\n• Prolonging the QRS duration","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antiarrhythmics has the fastest rate of recovery on Na+ channel blockade?\n\n1. Procainamide\n\n2. Phenytoin\n\n3. Flecainide\n\n4. Mexiletine\n\n5. Propafenone","o":["1, 3 and 5","2 and 4","4 and 5","2 only"],"a":1,"e":"Phenytoin and mexiletine which are class IB antiarrhythmic drugs have the fastest rate of recovery on Na+ channel blockade.\n\nThey show the fastest dissociation kinetics to Na+ channel blockade (<1 sec).\n\nNa+ channel blockade\n\nPotassium channel\n\nAction potential duration\n\nClass IA\n\nIntermediate- 1-10 sec\n\nSignificant K+ channel blocker\n\nProlonged\n\nClass IB\n\nRapid- <1 sec\n\nK+ channel opener\n\nShortened or no effect\n\nClass IC\n\nSlow- >10 sec\n\nNegligible K+ channel blocker\n\nVariable or no effect","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/514a78a442434412aaa593c0eee1fa8bx1280x2735.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"An athlete diagnosed with atrial fibrillation was administered an antiarrhythmic drug. Several hours later, he complains of lower abdominal pain and not having passed urine since admission. Examination reveals a palpable bladder. Which was the most probable drug administered?","o":["Quinidine","Disopyramide","Procainamide","Quinine"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of urinary retention. Disopyramide, a class 1A antiarrhythmic is the drug most likely to cause these symptoms in this patient.\n\nIt causes prominent anticholinergic side effects like urinary retention, dry mouth, blurred vision, precipitation of glaucoma, and constipation.\n\nIt is the drug of choice for vagally mediated atrial fibrillation such as sleep-induced or atrial fibrillation in athletes.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with sinus bradycardia and bronchospasm. She was previously diagnosed with AV nodal reentrant tachycardia and had been started on an antiarrhythmic drug. Which of the following drugs is most likely being used?","o":["Flecainide","Propafenone","Mexiletine","Lidocaine"],"a":1,"e":"Propafenone is the most likely drug being used in this patient as sinus bradycardia and bronchospasm are adverse effects seen with the use of this drug.\n\nThese side effects are due to its weak beta-blocking activity.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Blurred vision in a patient being treated for paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia is most likely due to:","o":["Esmolol","Dronedarone","Dofetilide","Flecainide"],"a":3,"e":"Blurred vision is the most common dose-related non-cardiac adverse effect of flecainide therapy.\n\nFlecainide is a class 1C antiarrhythmic agent. It acts mainly by sodium channel blockade. It does not cause any change in the action potential duration.\n\nFlecainide is used in paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVTs):\n\n• Atrioventricular nodal re-entrant tachycardia (AVNRT)\n\n• AV re-entrant tachycardia (AVRT)\n\n• Atrial fibrillation/atrial flutter (without structural heart disease)","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antiarrhythmics has both class II and class III action?","o":["Propranolol","Esmolol","Sotalol","Nadolol"],"a":2,"e":"Sotalol is a class III antiarrhythmic agent with additional beta-adrenergic blocking effects. (class II antiarrhythmic action)\n\nPropranolol is a class II antiarrhythmic agent that has an additional Na+ channel blocking effect. (class I antiarrhythmic action)\n\nEsmolol is a short-acting beta-blocker with minimal/no action on cardiac Na+ and K+ channels.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presented to the ER with dizziness and palpitations. ECG is shown below. Which of the following drugs cannot be used in this condition?","o":["Digoxin","Verapamil","Quinidine","Lignocaine"],"a":3,"e":"The scenario and ECG findings (absent P waves and RR intervals that are irregularly irregular) are suggestive of atrial fibrillation. Lignocaine is not used in atrial arrhythmias.\n\nLignocaine is a class 1B anti-arrhythmic agent. It blocks Na+ channels mostly in the closed/inactivated state. It is not used in atrial arrhythmias because the atrial action potential is so short that the Na+ channels are in a closed/ inactivated state for only a brief amount of time.\n\nLignocaine is used in the acute treatment of ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation.\n\nDrugs mostly used for supraventricular arrhythmia are:\n\n• Adenosine\n\n• Verapamil\n\n• Diltiazem\n\n• Digoxin\n\nDrugs used for ventricular arrhythmia are:\n\n• Lidocaine\n\n• Mexiletine\n\nDrugs used in both conditions are:\n\n• Amiodarone\n\n• Beta-blockers\n\n• Class 1a / 1c antiarrhythmics","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/910b818d2ef64c448a6bb019951055d6.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding lignocaine?","o":["Gastrointestinal absorption is good","High volume of distribution","In heart failure patients, both loading and maintenance doses are decreased","Indicated for prophylaxis of arryhythmias in all cases of suspected MI"],"a":3,"e":"Lignocaine decreases the incidence of arrhythmias in patients with suspected MI. However, it increases mortality probably by precipitating heart block or congestive heart failure. Hence it is not routinely used for prophylaxis in MI patients but it is used only when indicated.\n\nGastrointestinal absorption of lignocaine is good but bioavailability is very less due to high first-pass hepatic metabolism. Therefore, it is not used through the oral route.\n\nLignocaine has a high volume of distribution. The loading dose is required to achieve rapid action.\n\nIn heart failure, both volumes of distribution and clearance are reduced. Both the loading dose and maintenance dose have to be reduced.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a correct statement regarding antiarrhythmics?","o":["Sotalol has additional class II action","Esmolol has additional class IV action","Propranolol has additional class I action","Both A and C"],"a":3,"e":"Sotalol has both class II (beta-blocker) and class III action (K+ channel blockade). Though it is a nonselective beta-blocker, as an antiarrhythmic, it is classified under class III.\n\nPropranolol is a non-selective beta-blocker with additional membrane-stabilizing action especially at higher doses: Na+ channel blockade/ class I action.\n\nEsmolol is a beta 1 selective blocker that does not have any additional class effect.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presents to the OPD with complaints of bluish discoloration of the skin on the sun-exposed areas. He has been on medication for monomorphic ventricular tachycardia for the past 3 years. Which drug is most likely implicated in this condition?","o":["Lidocaine","Adenosine","Amiodarone","Mexiletine"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of blue man syndrome which is most likely caused by amiodarone.\n\nOne of the side effects of amiodarone is photosensitivity. In some cases, bluish discoloration of the skin is seen, predominantly in the sun-exposed areas. This could be due to the immune response in dermal layers by amiodarone or the accumulation of amiodarone in superficial layers of skin.\n\nThe symptoms are reversible on drug discontinuation. The syndrome is related to both dose and photosensitivity.\n\nThe side effects of amiodarone include:\n\n• Pulmonary fibrosis\n\n• Hypotension, depressed ventricular function\n\n• Torsades de pointes\n\n• Corneal microdeposits\n\n• Hepatotoxicity\n\n• Neuropathies\n\n• Photosensitivity\n\n• Thyroid dysfunction\n\nOther such syndromes associated with drugs are:\n\n• Red man syndrome - Vancomycin\n\n• Gray baby syndrome - Chloramphenicol","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Chronic usage of an antiarrhythmic drug leads to optic neuritis and smurf skin or ceruloderma in a patient. Which of the following is not a mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["K+ and Na+ channel blockade","Ca2+ channel blockade","Adrenergic blockade","Cl- channel blockade"],"a":3,"e":"Optic neuritis and smurf skin (blueman syndrome or ceruloderma) are caused by amiodarone. It acts by blocking K+, Na+, and Ca2+ channels. It also blocks adrenergic effects. It does not block Cl- channels.\n\nAmiodarone is classified as a class III antiarrhythmic agent. It exerts its function through the following mechanisms:\n\n• Blockade of transient outward delayed rectifier and inward rectifier K+ currents\n\n• Blockade of inactivated Na+ channels with a rapid rate of recovery\n\n• Decreases Ca2+ current\n\n• Non-competitive adrenergic blockade\n\nIt is the drug of choice for acute treatment of VT and VF.\n\nThe side effects of amiodarone include:\n\n• Pulmonary fibrosis\n\n• Hypotension, depressed ventricular function\n\n• Torsades de pointes\n\n• Corneal microdeposits\n\n• Hepatotoxicity\n\n• Neuropathies\n\n• Photosensitivity\n\n• Thyroid dysfunction","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following adverse effects is least likely in a patient who is on chronic amiodarone therapy?","o":["Dry cough","Corneal microdeposits","Hepatic dysfunction","Nephrotoxicity"],"a":3,"e":"Nephrotoxicity is not an adverse effect of chronic amiodarone therapy. Pulmonary fibrosis associated with dry cough is the most serious side effect of chronic amiodarone therapy. It can rapidly progress and become fatal.\n\nThe side effects of amiodarone include:\n\n• Pulmonary fibrosis\n\n• Hypotension, depressed ventricular function\n\n• Torsades de pointes\n\n• Corneal microdeposits\n\n• Hepatotoxicity\n\n• Neuropathies\n\n• Photosensitivity\n\n• Thyroid dysfunction","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about dronedarone is incorrect?","o":["It does not contain iodine moiety","Has stronger antiadrenergic effects than amiodarone","Less effective antiarrhythmic when compared to amiodarone","Dose adjustment has to be done in renal failure"],"a":3,"e":"Dronedarone undergoes hepatic metabolism and hence dose adjustment is not required in renal failure as it is not excreted through the kidneys.\n\nDronedarone is a non-iodinated congener of amiodarone. Like amiodarone, it is a blocker of multiple ion channels. It has stronger antiadrenergic effects than amiodarone. It is less effective in maintaining sinus rhythm and is considered less antiarrhythmic when compared to amiodarone.\n\nUnlike amiodarone, it does not cause thyroid dysfunction or pulmonary fibrosis. However, it causes hepatotoxicity and increases the risk of stroke and heart failure.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Given below is the ECG of a patient who presented to the emergency room with sudden onset palpitations and breathlessness. Which of the following drugs can be used in this condition?","o":["Atropine","Mexiletine","Verapamil","Lidocaine"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical scenario and the ECG findings are suggestive of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT). Verapamil and diltiazem are used in both acute treatment and prophylaxis of PSVT.\n\nTheir mechanism of action is by slowing SA node automaticity and AV nodal conduction velocity. Additionally, they also decrease cardiac contractility and reduce blood pressure.\n\nDihydropyridines like nifedipine and amlodipine do not have any antiarrhythmic property.\n\nDrugs used for supraventricular arrhythmias are:\n\n• Adenosine\n\n• Verapamil\n\n• Diltiazem\n\n• Digoxin\n\nDrugs used for ventricular arrhythmias are:\n\n• Lidocaine\n\n• Mexiletine\n\nDrugs used in both conditions are:\n\n• Amiodarone\n\n• Beta-blockers\n\n• Class 1A / 1C antiarrhythmics","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b062624679194d43acf969ad9e87358a.GIF"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16c4534451f244bf9c43d74c07268356x510x812.PNG"],"t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Vernakalant is indicated in which of the following types of arrhythmias?","o":["Ventricular fibrillation","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Ventricular flutter"],"a":1,"e":"Vernakalant is a newer antiarrhythmic agent indicated in atrial fibrillation.\n\nIts mechanisms of action include:\n\n• Frequency and voltage-dependent block of the early and late components of the sodium current\n\n• The muscarinic potassium current IKACh, which is constitutively activated in atrial fibrillation is also blocked\n\nIt is indicated for rapid termination of atrial fibrillation in patients with no or minimal structural heart disease.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with palpitations and light-headedness. His ECG is shown below. Which of the following drugs is least likely to cause this condition?","o":["Quinidine","Disopyramide","Procainamide","Lidocaine"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario and the ECG findings (rapid asynchronous complexes and an undulating baseline) point towards a diagnosis of torsades de pointes. Lidocaine use is unlikely to cause this condition.\n\nLidocaine belongs to class IB antiarrhythmics. It is considered to have the least arrhythmogenic potential among antiarrhythmics.\n\nAmong the antiarrhythmics, class Ia and class III drugs prolong action potential duration and thus prolong QT interval. They are also prone to cause torsades de pointes.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/00e9fd31a507460192d5b3caf8c74708.PNG"],"t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for the immediate management of a patient with paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia?","o":["Verapamil","Diltiazem","Digoxin","Adenosine"],"a":3,"e":"The drug of choice for the termination of PSVT is adenosine.\n\nDrugs used for supraventricular arrhythmias are:\n\n• Adenosine\n\n• Verapamil\n\n• Diltiazem\n\n• Digoxin\n\nDrugs used for ventricular arrhythmias are:\n\n• Lidocaine\n\n• Mexiletine\n\nDrugs used in both conditions are:\n\n• Amiodarone\n\n• Beta-blockers\n\n• Class 1a / 1c antiarrhythmics","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/710e0226cb07491b9ae0ac0c7d3d64f3x510x812.PNG"],"t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who was recently diagnosed with a seizure disorder presents to the casualty with altered mental status. His ECG shows a prolonged QTc. There is history of sudden cardiac deaths in his family at an early age. On further evaluation, a diagnosis of congenital long QT syndrome is made. Which is the most suitable pharmacological agent to treat this condition?","o":["Metoprolol","Mexilitine","Nicorandil","Isoprenaline"],"a":0,"e":"Beta-blockers like metoprolol are the mainstay of pharmacological therapy in both asymptomatic and symptomatic patients with congenital LQTS. They reduce both syncope and sudden cardiac death (SCD).\n\nLong QT syndrome (LQTS) is a disorder of ventricular myocardial repolarization characterized by a prolonged QT interval on the electrocardiogram that can lead to symptomatic ventricular arrhythmias and an increased risk of sudden cardiac death.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly patient with heart failure and atrial fibrillation is stabilized with digoxin. By what mechanism does it control the arrhythmia in this patient?","o":["Vagolytic action","Sympatholytic action","Increasing vagal tone","Decreasing atrial repolarization by blocking Na+ channels"],"a":2,"e":"Digoxin results in an increased vagal tone which helps in controlling the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation.\n\nIncreased vagal tone increases the AV nodal refractoriness due to blockade of Ca2+ channels. It also results in hyperpolarisation and shortening of atrial action potentials due to activation of ACh-mediated K+ channels in the atria.","t":"Anti Arrythmic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 58-year-old patient with coronary artery disease has a deranged lipid profile and is started on statins. Which enzyme does this drug act on?","o":["Acyl coA synthase","Acyl coA reductase","HMG coA synthetase","HMG coA reductase"],"a":3,"e":"Statins act on the HMG-CoA reductase enzyme, thus inhibiting cholesterol synthesis.\n\nNote: Necrotizing myopathy is a newly defined form of idiopathic inflammatory myopathy. Patients who have anti-HMG Co-A reductase antibodies should not be given statins to control high cholesterol as they may develop this type of statin-induced necrotizing myopathy.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9ab5f58a5a14b499192aef0fe17276dx1280x788.JPEG"],"t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Hepatic cholesterol synthesis is maximal between midnight and 2 am. So most of the statins are preferably administered in the evening for maximal effect. Which statin can be taken irrespective of the time of the day?","o":["Simvastatin","Pravastatin","Atorvastatin","Lovastatin"],"a":2,"e":"Atorvastatin has a longer half-life (19 ± 9 hours) and may be taken at any time of the day.\n\nOther drugs that can be taken irrespective of the time of day:\n\n• Rosuvastatin - has a half-life of 20 ± 6 hours\n\n• Pitavastatin - has a half-life of 12 hours\n\nAll other statins, which have a plasma half-life of around 4 hours or less, should preferably be administered in the evening.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with hypercholesterolemia on statin therapy presents to your OPD for follow-up. You notice his cholesterol is still high and decide to add on another drug that directly inhibits the absorption of cholesterol. Which of the following will you prescribe?","o":["Ezetimibe","Orlistat","Cholestyramine","Niacin"],"a":0,"e":"Ezetimibe is the drug that prevents hypercholesterolemia by directly inhibiting the absorption of cholesterol.\n\nEzetimibe acts by interfering with a specific cholesterol transport protein NPC1L1 in the intestinal mucosa and reduces the absorption of both dietary and biliary cholesterol. It causes a compensatory increase in hepatic cholesterol synthesis but LDL cholesterol is decreased. Statins block this enhanced cholesterol synthesis (cholesterol synthesis inhibitors) and so their combination has a synergistic effect.\n\nOption B: Orlistat is an anti-obesity drug that acts by inhibiting pancreatic and gastric lipase and thus interferes with the digestion and absorption of dietary triglycerides.\n\nOption C: Cholestyramine is a bile acid sequestrant. The bile-acid sequestrants are highly positively charged and bind negatively charged bile acids. Because of their large size, the resins are not absorbed, and the bound bile acids are excreted in the stool.\n\nCholestyramine primarily reduces LDL levels by increasing hepatic bile acid synthesis; which results in increased LDL uptake and diversion of cholesterol to bile acid production.\n\nOption D: Niacin reduces lipolysis by hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL) and reduces hepatic triglyceride synthesis.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which drug decreases Lipoprotein(a)?","o":["Fenofibrate","Niacin","Gemfibrozil","Colestipol"],"a":1,"e":"The drug that decreases Lipoprotein(a) is niacin.\n\nNiacin is the only lipid-lowering agent that lowers lipoprotein(a) levels significantly. It also results in a decrease in LDL, VLDL, and triglycerides along with an increase in HDL cholesterol.\n\nThe main adverse effect of niacin is cutaneous flushing and pruritus which can be controlled by pretreatment with aspirin. Niacin can also cause hepatotoxicity, GI toxicity, and hyperuricemia.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs would you warn a patient taking statins against, due to the highest risk of myopathy?","o":["Fenofibrate","Gemfibrozil","Colestipol","Niacin"],"a":1,"e":"Myopathy risk is highest when statins are used along with gemfibrozil.\n\nGemfibrozil is a fibrate that inhibits the uptake of statins into the hepatocytes and prevents its metabolism, thereby increasing its bioavailability. Thus, the risk of toxicity of statins increases.\n\nOther drugs interacting similarly with statins include:\n\n• Cyclosporine\n\n• Digoxin\n\n• Warfarin\n\n• Macrolide antibiotics\n\n• Azole antifungals.\n\nOption A: Fenofibrate does not have much interaction with statins.\n\nOption C: Colestipol is a bile acid sequestrant and does not have much effect on statin metabolism.\n\nOption D: Niacin can also rarely increase the risk of statin-induced myopathy but the risk is much lower than that with gemfibrozil. So here gemfibrozil is the best answer.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who is on statin therapy asks about a new drug called evolocumab that his friend is taking for hyperlipidemia. What is the route of administration of this drug?","o":["Intramuscular","Intradermal","Subcutaneous","Intravenous"],"a":2,"e":"Evolocumab is a PCSK-9 inhibitor that is administered as a subcutaneous injection.\n\nPCSK-9 is a protease that binds to the LDL receptors on the surface of hepatocyte and enhances the lysosomal degradation of the LDL receptor. This results in higher plasma LDL levels. inhibition of PCSK-9 results in a reduction of plasma LDL levels.\n\nTwo approved PCSK-9 inhibitors are alirocumab and evolocumab.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is a microsomal triglyceride transfer protein inhibitor?","o":["Ticlopidine","Gugulipid","Lomitapide","Evinacumab"],"a":2,"e":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) inhibitor is lomitapide.\n\nMTP adds triglycerides to nascent VLDL in the liver and to chylomicrons in the intestine. Its inhibition decreases VLDL secretion and thus decreases the accumulation of LDL in the plasma. It is approved for use in homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.\n\nOptions A: Ticlopidine is an antiplatelet agent.\n\nOption B: Gugulipid is a plant extract that is commonly used for hyperlipidemia but has very little/no evidence to support its use.\n\nOption D: Evinacumab is a recently approved angiopoietin-like 3 (ANGPTL3) inhibitor. It is indicated as an adjunct to other LDL-cholesterol lowering therapies for homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia in adults and pediatric patients aged 12 years and older.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with hereditary hyperlipoproteinemia is prescribed a fibrate. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Increased lipolysis of triglycerides","Inhibit lipolysis in adipose tissues","Inhibit HMG CoA synthetase","Binds bile acids and bile salts in the intestine"],"a":0,"e":"Fibrates increase the lipoprotein lipase activity through PPAR-α and cause increased lipolysis of triglycerides.\n\nLipoprotein lipase (LPL) is a key enzyme in the degradation of VLDL. Fibrates bind to PPAR alpha and cause stimulation of lipoprotein lipase enzyme. Increased LPL synthesis enhances the clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins. Additionally, fibrates also stimulate fatty acid oxidation and reduce the expression of apo C-III (inhibitor of lipolysis).\n\nClass\nHMG- CoA reductase inhibitors (statins)\nFibrates\nNiacin\nBile acid-binding resins\nEzetimibe\n\nImportant examples\n\nLovastatin, atorvastatin, fluvastatin, pravastatin, simvastatin, rosuvastatin, pitavastatin\n\nGemfibrozil and fenofibrate\n\nNiacin\n\nColestipol, cholestyramine, colesevelam\n\nEzetimibe\n\nMechanism of action\n\nInhibits HMG-CoA reductase\n\nPPAR-α mediated increased lipolysis\n\nDecreases the production of VLDLs and decreases the catabolism of HDLs\nBinds to bile acids in the intestinal lumen and increases excretion. This results in increased conversion of hepatic cholesterol to bile acids, and reduction in hepatic cholesterol content.\nInhibits intestinal sterol absorption\n\nMajor effects on lipoprotein levels\n\nReduces LDL levels\n\nReduces triglyceride levels\n\nA moderate increase in HDL levels\n\nReduces triglycerides, LDL, and lipoprotein a levels\n\nSignificant increase HDL levels\n\nReduces LDL levels\n\nReduces LDL cholesterol\n\nMinimal increase in HDL level\n\nAlso decreases the absorption of phytosterols\n\nImportant side effects\nMyopathy\n\nElevations of serum aminotransferase activity\n\nElevation of creatine kinase activity\n\nRashes, gastrointestinal symptoms, myopathy, arrhythmias\n\nHypokalemia, elevated aminotransferases, or alkaline phosphatase\n\nCutaneous vasodilation\n\nElevations in aminotransferases levels\n\nConstipation\n\nBloating\n\nReversible hepatic function impairment\n\nRarely myositis","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old obese man presented to your OPD. His lipid profile showed elevated levels of cholesterol and triglycerides. On examination, you notice palmar xanthomas. What is the drug of choice for his condition?","o":["Statins","Niacin","Fibrates","Alirocumab"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario of elevated cholesterol and triglycerides along with the presence of palmar xanthomas (pathognomonic sign) is indicative of type III hyperlipoproteinemia. The drugs of choice for this condition are fibrates.\n\nFibrates are also preferred in patients having severe hypertriglyceridemia (TG >1000 mg/dl) who are at risk for pancreatitis.\n\nThe response to fibrate therapy in Type III hyperlipoproteinemia patients include:\n\n• Elevated cholesterol and triglyceride levels are dramatically lowered.\n\n• Tuberoeruptive and palmar xanthomas may regress completely.\n\n• Angina and intermittent claudication also improve.\n\nThe image below shows palmar xanthoma.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b41419073a741deb611315c1b643f21x1280x1144.JPEG"],"t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the drug of choice for type IIa and IIb hyperlipoproteinemias?","o":["Statins","Fibrates","Niacin","Bile acid sequestrants"],"a":0,"e":"The drugs of choice for type IIa and IIb hyperlipoproteinemias are statins.\n\nIn type IIa or IIb hyperlipoproteinemias, there is increased LDL cholesterol with +/- increase in TG. Statins have the maximum LDL lowering property among the hypolipidemic drugs. Therefore, they are the drugs of choice in this case.\n\nIn type III/IV hyperlipoproteinemias, there is mainly an increase in triglyceride(TG) level. Fibrates reduce the TGs contained in VLDL and increase the HDL. So they are the drugs of choice for these conditions.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has mipomersen as part of his treatment regimen. By which of the following mechanisms does this drug act?","o":["Inhibition of microsomal triglyceride transfer","Inhibition of PCSK9","Inhibition of apo B-100 synthesis","Inhibition of cholesterol absorption"],"a":2,"e":"Mipomersen is an antisense oligonucleotide that acts as an inhibitor of apo B-100 synthesis.\n\nMipomersen binds to the mRNA of apo B-100 resulting in degradation of apo B-100 mRNA and eventually reduction of apo B-100 synthesis.","t":"Hypolipidemic Drugs","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following ACE inhibitors is not a prodrug?","o":["Fosinopril","Enalapril","Ramipril","Lisinopril"],"a":3,"e":"Lisinopril is not a prodrug.\n\nAll ACE inhibitors are prodrugs except lisinopril and captopril.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with BP 225/145 presents with acute pulmonary edema and is diagnosed with hypertensive emergency. The active metabolite of which ACE inhibitor is available for intravenous use in this patient?","o":["Ramipril","Perindopril","Enalapril","Fosinopril"],"a":2,"e":"Enalapril is available in its active form as enalaprilat for intravenous use.\n\nEnalapril is a prodrug of enalaprilat. It is indicated in conditions where oral drugs cannot be used or rapid onset of action is desired like in a case of hypertensive emergency.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements regarding ACE inhibitors is true?","o":["They inhibit the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I","Omission of prior diuretic dose decreases the risk of postural hypotension","Lisinopril is shorter acting than enalapril","They are contraindicated in diabetic patients"],"a":1,"e":"The omission of prior diuretic dose decreases the risk of postural hypotension.\n\nACE inhibitors cause arteriolar dilation and increase the compliance of larger arteries. ACE inhibitors induced hypotension is thereby due to a decrease in total peripheral resistance. In diuretic-treated patients, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is more active to compensate for volume loss. Hence, ACE inhibitors increase the risk of postural hypotension in these patients.\n\nOption A: ACE inhibitors inhibit the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II.\n\nOption C: Lisinopril is longer acting than enalapril. Lisinopril is usually administered once daily in all patients. Enalapril requires twice-daily administration in some patients.\n\nOption D: ACE inhibitors and ARBs are first-line agents in diabetic patients because of their renoprotective effects.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"All of the following are indications for the use of ACE inhibitors except:","o":["Hypertensive emergency","Myocardial infarction","Left ventricular dysfunction","Pheochromocytoma"],"a":3,"e":"ACE inhibitors are usually avoided in conditions like pheochromocytoma which is associated with high renin activity. Abrupt inhibition of the RAAS system can result in severe hypotension (usually postural).\n\nThe majority of pheochromocytomas are treated surgically. Phenoxybenzamine is often used in preparing the patient for surgery and also to manage hypertension in inoperable or malignant pheochromocytoma. Phentolamine can also be used in the short-term control of hypertension in these patients.\n\nACE inhibitors are indicated in hypertensive emergency, myocardial infarction, and left ventricular dysfunction.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients are ACE inhibitors not contraindicated?\n\n1. A 23-year-old primigravid woman with preeclampsia\n\n2. A 50-year-old man with unilateral renal artery stenosis\n\n3. A 50-year-old man with bilateral renal artery stenosis\n\n4. A post-myocardial infarction patient with left ventricular systolic dysfunction\n\n5. A patient with scleroderma renal crisis","o":["1 and 3","1,2 and 3","2,4 and 5","3, 4 and 5"],"a":2,"e":"ACE inhibitors are not contraindicated in unilateral renal artery stenosis, left ventricular systolic dysfunction, and scleroderma renal crisis.\n\nPatient 1: ACE inhibitors are teratogenic and can cause hypoplasia of organs, renal failure, anuria, and growth retardation in the fetus. Therefore, they are contraindicated in pregnancy.\n\nPatient 2: ACE inhibitors can be used with caution in unilateral renal artery stenosis if the other kidney is functioning normally.\n\nPatient 3: Efferent arteriolar constriction maintains the glomerular filtration pressure in bilateral renal artery stenosis. ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in bilateral renal stenosis as they can precipitate acute renal failure due to dilation of efferent arterioles and fall in glomerular filtration pressure.\n\nPatient 4: ACE inhibition in patients with systolic dysfunction prevents or delays the progression of heart failure, decreases the incidence of sudden death and myocardial infarction, and improves the quality of life. ACE inhibition reduces afterload and systolic wall stress, and both cardiac output and cardiac index increase.\n\nPatient 5: The use of ACE inhibitors considerably improves the survival of patients with scleroderma renal crisis.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 53-year-old hypertensive patient has been on ACE inhibitors for the last 12 years. Which of the following effects would you expect to see in this patient?","o":["Rightward shift in renal pressure natriuresis curve","Reduction in filtration fraction","Significant increase in heart rate","Decrease in compliance of large arteries"],"a":1,"e":"ACE inhibitors, when used for a long time in patients with hypertension, cause a reduction in filtration fraction.\n\nFiltration fraction is the ratio of GFR to that of renal plasma flow. ACE inhibitors increase renal blood flow without altering GFR and thus result in reduction of filtration fraction.\n\nOption A: The graph plotted between urinary sodium excretion and mean blood pressure is known as the pressure natriuresis curve. For example, when dietary sodium intake is low, renin release is stimulated. Angiotensin II acts on the kidneys to shift the renal pressure – natriuresis curve to the right. This increases sodium and water retention.\n\nConversely, when dietary Na+ is high or when ACE inhibitors are used, there is the withdrawal of angiotensin II. The renal pressure natriuresis curve shifts to the left.\n\nThe following image depicts a pressure natriuresis curve:\n\nOptions C and D: ACE inhibitors do not cause a significant increase in heart rate and can have a favorable change in the compliance of large arteries on long-term use.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2612147181bf421c80443ad4c6b7cb6bx1280x1347.JPEG"],"t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who is started on lisinopril for hypertension is concerned about its adverse effects. Which among the following is not an adverse effect of this drug?","o":["Dysgeusia","Hypokalemia","Neutropenia","Skin rash"],"a":1,"e":"ACE inhibitors are associated with the development of hyperkalemia.\n\nHyperkalemia occurs due to the inhibition of angiotensin II-mediated aldosterone secretion. Decreased aldosterone levels result in decreased tubular excretion of potassium in the kidneys leading to hyperkalemia.\n\nThe adverse effects of ACE inhibitors are:\n\n• Hypotension\n\n• Hyperkalemia\n\n• Cough\n\n• Angioedema\n\n• Skin rash\n\n• Teratogenicity\n\n• Dysgeusia (alteration in or loss of taste)\n\nExtremely rare but reversible side effects include neutropenia, glycosuria in the absence of hyperglycemia, anemia, and hepatotoxicity.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to the casualty with cough, difficulty in breathing, and non-indurating edema of his face and lips. He had recently been started on ramipril for hypertension. Which among the following mediates these adverse effects?","o":["Bradykinin and renin","Angiotensin II and bradykinin","Only bradykinin","Histamine"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of ACE inhibitor-induced angioedema which occurs due to bradykinin.\n\nAngioedema is most commonly mediated by histamine. However, bradykinin-mediated angioedema, though less common, can result due to hereditary or acquired defects in C1 esterase inhibitor and as a side effect of ACE inhibitors.\n\nACE has structural similarity to another enzyme called kininase. Kininase normally metabolizes bradykinin, substance P, and prostaglandins. ACE inhibitors when administered also block kininase enzyme which results in an increase in the concentration of these substrates. An increase in the level of bradykinin/ substance P levels results in persistent brassy cough and angioedema in the patients.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"An 18-year-old boy is brought to the casualty with complaints of swelling of his face and lips as shown in the image below. On further enquiry, it is noted that he has suffered from similar episodes in the past whenever he becomes emotional. He is prescribed icatibant. What is the likely mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Bradykinin B2 receptor agonist","Bradykinin B2 receptor antagonist","Bradykinin B1 receptor agonist","Bradykinin B1 receptor antagonist"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario and image showing swelling of the face and lips is suggestive of hereditary angioedema. Icatibant is a selective bradykinin B2 receptor competitive antagonist indicated for the treatment of acute attacks of hereditary angioedema.\n\nThe other drug which is approved for the treatment of acute attacks of hereditary angioedema is ecallantide which is a synthetic plasma kallikrein inhibitor. Both these drugs are administered subcutaneously.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ec1504cc1c8408485f6e571e4ee5172.JPEG"],"t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old diabetic male is on ACE inhibitors for hypertension. Which among the following is the most serious adverse effect you would expect to see in this patient?","o":["Hypotension","Hypokalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia"],"a":0,"e":"The most significant adverse effect seen with the use of ACE inhibitors is hypotension.\n\nACE inhibitors are used in the management of hypertension and are one of the first-line drugs for this condition.\n\nOption B: ACE inhibitors may cause hyperkalemia in patients with renal insufficiency or diabetes.\n\nOptions C and D: ACE inhibitors do not affect calcium levels significantly.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man is diagnosed with hypertension and is prescribed losartan. Which of the following statements about the drug is incorrect?","o":["It is a competitive angiotensin receptor antagonist","It attenuates platelet aggregation","Associated with negligible cough","Causes hyperuricemia"],"a":3,"e":"Losartan use is not associated with hyperuricemia. In fact, losartan is uricosuric and can also be used in patients with gout.\n\nLosartan is a competitive angiotensin II receptor antagonist. It binds to the AT1 receptor with high affinity. it is also a competitive antagonist of the thromboxane A2 receptor and attenuates platelet aggregation. Losartan has no effect on ACE and it does not inhibit bradykinin breakdown. This results in negligible incidences of cough.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Combined use of ACE inhibitors and ARBs are not routinely indicated primarily due to the increased risk of which of the following?","o":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Angioedema","Heart failure"],"a":0,"e":"The combined use of ACE inhibitors and ARBs are not commonly indicated as they increase the risk of hyperkalemia.\n\nThey also increase the risk of hypotension and renal injury. Any such use should be done under the supervision of specialists with frequent monitoring.","t":"Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man was rushed to the ER with impaired consciousness following a hunger strike for a day. His wife reported, he had a head injury 1 month back and since then had complaints of polyuria. On examination, he was found to be severely dehydrated and MRI brain showed an absence of the characteristic bright spot in the pituitary. Which of the following is the drug of choice for his condition?","o":["Desmopressin","Amiloride","Thiazide diuretics","Triamterene"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario of polyuria following a head injury and an absence of a pituitary bright spot on the MRI brain is suggestive of central diabetes insipidus. The drug of choice for this condition is desmopressin, a V2 receptor agonist.\n\nMechanism of action of desmopressin is: It stimulates the V2 receptors on the basolateral membrane of the principal cells in the collecting tubules. This increases the insertion of water channels (aquaporin 2) into the apical membrane. This leads to increased water reabsorption in the collecting ducts. Activation of V2 receptors also increase the permeability of urea.\n\nOption B: Amiloride is a potassium-sparing diuretic. It is indicated for lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.\n\nOption C: Thiazides paradoxically exert an antidiuretic effect in DI but are not the drugs of choice. They are generally used in nephrogenic DI.\n\nOption D: Triamterene is also a potassium-sparing diuretic that can be used to treat nephrogenic DI.\n\nNote: Patients with DI usually do not develop overt signs of dehydration due to increased intake of water to compensate for polyuria. But when water intake is impaired they can present with dehydration.","t":"Anti Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient having polyuria and polydipsia is diagnosed with central diabetes insipidus. He is started on intranasal desmopressin. Which of the following is not a reason for choosing this over intravenous vasopressin?","o":["Higher potency","Higher selectivity for V1 receptor","Has lower vasoconstrictor activity","Longer acting"],"a":1,"e":"Desmopressin is preferred in the treatment of central diabetes insipidus as it is a selective agonist of the V2 receptor.\n\nDesmopressin has minimal or no action on V1 receptors resulting in little vasoconstrictor activity. It is more potent than vasopressin. It is longer acting than vasopressin as enzymatic degradation of desmopressin is slow.\n\nLypressin acts on both V1 and V2 receptors. Terlipressin is a synthetic vasopressin analog that has more selectivity for the V1 receptor and produces fewer adverse effects than lypressin.","t":"Anti Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman with pituitary adenoma is non-compliant with her treatment with cabergoline. She has now presented with bitemporal hemianopia and features suggestive of diabetes insipidus. Which of the following drugs cannot be used to manage her condition?","o":["Desmopressin","Furosemide","Chlorpropamide","Carbamazepine"],"a":1,"e":"Furosemide is not used for the treatment of diabetes insipidus. It is a loop diuretic that might further worsen diabetes insipidus.\n\nOption A: The drug of choice for central diabetes insipidus is desmopressin. Desmopressin is a V2 receptor agonist and synthetic analog vasopressin.\n\nOption C: Chlorpropamide enhances the action of circulating vasopressin and reduces urine formation in central DI.\n\nOption D: Carbamazepine also reduces urine formation in central DI. The exact mechanism is unknown.","t":"Anti Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Tachyphylaxis is a concern with the use of desmopressin in which of the following patients?","o":["A 50-year-old man with central DI post meningitis","A 25-year-old woman on lithium therapy with nephrogenic DI","A 17-year-old girl with von willebrand's disease","A 7-year-old child with primary nocturnal enuresis"],"a":2,"e":"Tachyphylaxis refers to the rapid development of tolerance when doses of a drug are repeated in quick succession. It is a concern when desmopressin is used in bleeding disorders because long-term use of desmopressin can cause depletion of factor VIII and von Willebrand factor stores.\n\nThe usefulness of desmopressin in bleeding disorders is limited to preoperative preparation, postoperative bleeding, excessive menstrual bleeding, and emergency situations. In patients with type I von Willebrand disease and in some with type IIn vWD, desmopressin elevates von Willebrand factor and shortens the bleeding time.\n\nOption A: It does not cause tachyphylaxis in central DI.\n\nOption B: Desmopressin is not indicated in nephrogenic DI.\n\nOption D: Bedtime administration of desmopressin for primary nocturnal enuresis provides a high response rate that is sustained with long-term use. However, intranasal desmopressin is no longer recommended for the treatment of primary nocturnal enuresis because of the increased risk of hyponatremia.","t":"Anti Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man who is on treatment for small cell carcinoma of the lung presents with acute onset confusion, nausea, and vomiting. He appears to be euvolemic. Laboratory investigations reveal serum sodium levels to be 120 mEq/l and urine osmolality is 300 mOsm/kg . Which of the following is not used as a treatment option in his condition?","o":["Demeclocycline","Desmopressin","Restriction of free water intake","3% NaCl"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH) which can occur as a paraneoplastic syndrome of small cancer of the lung. Desmopressin is not used in the treatment of SIADH.\n\nSince excess vasopressin is the cause for SIADH, giving a vasopressin analog like desmopressin would only worsen the condition.\n\nManagement of SIADH:\n\n• Fluid restriction\n\n• Hypertonic saline( 3%) + loop diuretics is the treatment of choice in severe symptomatic hyponatremia.\n\n• Tolvaptan and conivaptan (vasopressin receptor antagonists) are preferred for long-term use.\n\n• Demeclocycline (a tetracycline drug) is used as an alternative.","t":"Anti Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with liver cirrhosis presenting with ascites and pedal edema is administered conivaptan. Which of the following statements regarding the drug is true?","o":["Vasopressin receptor antagonist","V2 selective action","Given orally","Used in hypernatremia"],"a":0,"e":"Conivaptan is a nonselective vasopressin receptor antagonist.\n\nConivaptan antagonizes both V1a and V2 receptors. It is administered intravenously and is used in the treatment of euvolemic and hypervolemic hyponatremia. Vaptans have been proven to mobilize ascites and improve hyponatremia in patients with cirrhosis.\n\nVasopressin receptor blockers are classified as:\n\nClassification of receptor blockers\n Drugs\n\nNonselective (mixed V1/V2)\nConivaptan\n\nV1 selective(V1 RA)\nRelcovaptan\n\nV1b selective(V3 RA)\nNelivaptan\n\nV2 selective(V2 RA)\nLixivaptan\nMozavaptan\nSatavaptan\nTolvaptan","t":"Anti Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old alcoholic with deranged liver function tests is to be started on treatment for chronic SIADH. Which of the following drugs should not be used in his treatment regimen?","o":["Conivaptan","Tolvaptan","Demeclocycline","Lixivaptan"],"a":1,"e":"Tolvaptan should not be used in patients with liver disease as it has been associated with liver damage. Administration of tolvaptan is generally limited to 30 days to limit the risk of hepatotoxicity.","t":"Anti Diuretics","c":"Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a benzodiazepine?","o":["Flurazepam","Flumazenil","Amobarbital","Zaleplon"],"a":0,"e":"Flurazepam is a benzodiazepine.\n\nClass of drugs\nMechanism of action\n Examples\n\nBenzodiazepines\nFacilitate the action of GABA by increasing the frequency of opening of GABA mediated chloride channel\n\n• Alprazolam\n\n• Clonazepam\n\n• Lorazepam\n\n• Midazolam\n\n• Oxazepam\n\n• Temazepam\n\n• Estazolam\n\n• Flurazepam\n\nBarbiturates\nFacilitate the action of GABA by increasing the opening duration of GABA mediated chloride channel\n\n• Amobarbital\n\n• Phenobarbital\n\n• Butabarbital\n\n• Pentobarbital\n\nZ drugs\nAct like benzodiazepines\n\n• Zaleplon\n\n• Zolpidem\n\n• Zopiclone\n\n• Eszopiclone\n\nMelatonin receptor agonists\nActivates MT1 and MT2 receptors\n\n• Ramelteon\n\n• Tasimelteon\n\nOrexin antagonist\nBlocks orexins, neuropeptides that promote wakefulness\n\n• Suvorexant\n\n• Daridorexant\n\n5HT receptor agonist\nAffinity for 5HT > D2 receptor\n\n• Buspirone","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A physician prescribes clonazepam to a patient with generalized anxiety disorder. Choose the false statement regarding its mechanism of action.","o":["Bind at the α/γ interface of GABA-A receptor","Increase the affinity of the GABA-A receptor for GABA","Activate GABA-A receptors directly opening the Cl channel","Increase the frequency of opening of GABA receptor Cl channel"],"a":2,"e":"Clonazepam is a benzodiazepine. These are allosteric modulators of the GABAA receptor. They do not directly activate GABAA receptors to open the Cl- channel.\n\nGABAA receptor is a transmembrane inhibitory receptor in the CNS that functions as a chloride ion channel and has 5 subunits.\n\nBenzodiazepines are allosteric modulators of the GABAA receptor that bind at the α/γ interface of the receptor. This increases the affinity of the GABAA receptor for GABA thus increasing the frequency of the opening of the channel.\n\nBarbiturates, on the other hand, have a different binding site to the receptor and they increase the duration of the GABA gated chloride channel opening.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which is the shortest acting benzodiazepine?","o":["Remimazolam","Midazolam","Lorazepam","Temazepam"],"a":0,"e":"Remimazolam is the shortest-acting benzodiazepine. Its half-life is just around 37-53 mins.\n\nClassification of benzodiazepines:\n\n• Long-acting:\n\n• Flurazepam\n\n• Diazepam\n\n• Quazepam\n\n• Chlordiazepoxide\n\n• Intermediate-acting:\n\n• Alprazolam\n\n• Temazepam\n\n• Lorazepam\n\n• Short-acting:\n\n• Triazolam\n\n• Midazolam\n\n• Ultra-short acting\n\n• Remimazolam\n\nNote: Remimazolam was approved by FDA in 2020.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following does not act via GABA potentiation?","o":["Zopiclone","Midazolam","Thiopentone","Promethazine"],"a":3,"e":"Promethazine does not act via GABA potentiation. It is a first-generation H1 receptor antagonist.\n\nZopiclone, midazolam, and thiopentone act via GABA potentiation.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with insomnia is taking a drug belonging to the Z-compounds family. Which of the following does not belong to this family?","o":["Zopiclone","Zaleplon","Zonisamide","Eszopiclone"],"a":2,"e":"Zonisamide is a sulfonamide anticonvulsant drug, that has no activity on GABA receptors. It does not belong to the Z-compound drugs. It is an adjunct drug for Parkinson's disease, partial-onset seizures, infantile spasm, mixed seizure types of Lennox–Gastaut syndrome, myoclonic and generalized tonic-clonic seizure.\n\nZ compounds are benzodiazepine receptor agonists. They have less anticonvulsant and muscle relaxant properties when compared to benzodiazepines.\n\nThey are used for the treatment of insomnia. They include:\n\n• Zolpidem\n\n• Zaleplon\n\n• Zopiclone\n\n• Eszopiclone","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following agents does not bind to GABA receptor chloride channel complex?","o":["Ethanol","Alphaxolone","Zolpidem","Buspirone"],"a":3,"e":"Buspirone does not bind to the GABA receptor chloride channel complex. It is a partial agonist at 5-HT1A receptor used in the treatment of anxiety.\n\nOption A: Ethanol releases GABA and also has a binding site on the GABA receptor chloride channel complex.\n\nOption B: Alphaxolone is a neurosteroid and it acts through activation of GABAA receptors. These receptors are BZD insensitive.\n\nOption C: Zolpidem belongs to novel benzodiazepine receptor agonists called Z compounds. It binds to the BZD receptor present on the GABA receptor chloride channel complex.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an inverse agonist at the benzodiazepine receptor?","o":["Phenobarbitone","Beta-carboline","Flumazenil","Picrotoxin"],"a":1,"e":"The inverse agonist at the benzodiazepine receptor is beta-carboline.\n\nIt impedes the action of GABA and can cause anxiogenic, convulsive, and memory-enhancing effects.\n\nOption A: Barbiturates (phenobarbitone) are agonists at an allosteric site on the GABAA receptor that facilitate the action of GABA.\n\nOption C: Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine receptor.\n\nOption D: Picrotoxin blocks Cl- channel non-competitively.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 33-year-old woman with suspected diazepam overdose was administered the antidote for benzodiazepine poisoning. Which of the following statements is false about this antidote?","o":["It is a specific antagonist of benzodiazepines","It may be used to treat barbiturate poisoning","It is administered intravenously","It acts at the same site on GABA channel where benzodiazepines bind"],"a":1,"e":"Flumazenil is the antidote used in benzodiazepine poisoning. It is not used to treat barbiturate poisoning.\n\nFlumazenil is a benzodiazepine (BZD) receptor-specific antagonist. It has no affinity for barbiturate binding sites on the GABA receptor Cl- channel complex. Flumazenil competes with BZD agonists and inverse agonists for the BZD receptors.\n\nFlumazenil is available only for intravenous use. It is used to treat benzodiazepine overdose and for the reversal of sedative effects produced by benzodiazepines administered during general anesthesia.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a competitive antagonist of GABA at the GABA-A receptor?","o":["Flumazenil","Picrotoxin","Bicuculline","Propofol"],"a":2,"e":"Bicuculline is a competitive antagonist of GABA at the GABA-A receptor.\n\nOption A: Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the alpha/gamma interface of the benzodiazepine site. This is different from the binding site of GABA which is the alpha/beta interface.\n\nOption B: Picrotoxin blocks Cl- channel non-competitively.\n\nOption D: Propofol is an agonist at the GABA-A receptor.\n\nNote: Muscimol is an agonist at the GABA-A receptor.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfdad2a8cad94ddf9b519680d61a080dx1280x1470.JPEG"],"t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is the administration of barbiturates contraindicated?","o":["29-year-old lady with history of anxiety disorder","33-year-old man with history of acute intermittent porphyria","50-year-old man with refractory status epilepticus","11-day-old neonate with kernicterus"],"a":1,"e":"The administration of barbiturates is contraindicated in acute intermittent porphyria.\n\nThe rate-limiting enzyme in porphyria synthesis is δ-aminolaevulinic acid (ALA)-synthetase. Barbiturates are enzyme inducers and they result in the increased synthesis of porphyrins and subsequent precipitation of acute intermittent porphyria.\n\nOptions A and C: Barbiturates are used to treat anxiety and refractory status epilepticus.\n\nOption D: Phenobarbitone can be used to treat kernicterus in newborns as it increases hepatic glucuronosyltransferase (UGT) enzyme activity and the conjugation of bilirubin.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old man with insomnia is prescribed a melatonin analog by his physician. Which of the following drugs would have been prescribed?","o":["Doxepin","Suvorexant","Ramelteon","Trazodone"],"a":2,"e":"Ramelteon is a melatonin analog approved for the treatment of insomnia, particularly with difficulties of sleep onset.\n\nIt acts on both MT1 and MT2 receptors.\n\n• MT1 receptor - promotes the onset of sleep (hence used for induction of sleep)\n\n• MT2 receptor - shifts the timing of the circadian system.\n\nThe other melatonin agonists are:\n\n• Tasimelteon - used in insomnia in blind people\n\n• Agomelatine - used in major depressive disorder\n\nOption A: Doxepin is a tricyclic antidepressant and functions as a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor. It is used in depression, anxiety, and some forms of insomnia.\n\nOption B: Suvorexant is a dual orexin receptor antagonist used in the treatment of insomnia.\n\nOption D: Trazodone is a mixed agonist and antagonist at various serotonin receptors used as an antidepressant and in alcohol dependence.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young woman, who is blind, presented with complaints of night-time insomnia. What is the drug of choice for her condition?","o":["Agomelatine","Zolpidem","Tasimelteon","Ramelteon"],"a":2,"e":"The drug of choice for insomnia in blind people is tasimelteon. It is a selective agonist of the melatonin (MT1, MT2) receptors.\n\nTasimelteon is approved for the treatment of non–24-h sleep-wake syndrome (nighttime insomnia and daytime sleepiness, alternating with asymptomatic periods) in totally blind patients experiencing circadian rhythm disorder.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Your senior resident asks you to prescribe an orexin receptor inhibitor to a patient with insomnia. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe?","o":["Suvorexant","Doxepin","Ritanserin","Agomelatine"],"a":0,"e":"Suvorexant is an inhibitor of both orexin1 and orexin2 receptors. It is approved for the treatment of insomnia.\n\nOrexins are produced in the lateral hypothalamus neurons and play a major role in maintaining the sleep cycle. Orexins promote wakefulness, while antagonists at orexin receptors enhance REM and non-REM sleep.\n\nOption B: Doxepin is a tricyclic antidepressant and has H1 receptor antagonism effect. It is used for insomnia at lower doses.\n\nOption C: Ritanserin is a 5HT2A/2C receptor antagonist indicated in insomnia.\n\nOption D: Agomelatine is a melatonin receptor agonist and a 5HT2C receptor antagonist indicated in insomnia.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 68-year-old man with alcoholic liver disease is scheduled to undergo surgery. Which of the following benzodiazepines should be avoided in him?","o":["Lorazepam","Oxazepam","Triazolam","Diazepam"],"a":3,"e":"Diazepam and its long-acting metabolites can accumulate significantly in elderly patients with liver disease and therefore should not be used in such cases.\n\nDiazepam is a long-acting benzodiazepine. It is metabolized in the liver to active metabolites like nordazepam which are long-acting. Nordazepam is further converted to oxazepam which is conjugated with glucuronic acid to form inactive metabolites.\n\nLorazepam, oxazepam, and triazolam are also metabolized in the liver but do not form long-acting active metabolites. As such, they can be used in elderly patients with liver disease but with caution.\n\nOxazepam and lorazepam are intermediate-acting and undergo glucuronide conjugation directly to form inactive metabolites.\n\nTriazolam is a short acting benzodiazepine. It forms alpha-hydroxy active metabolites but they are short-acting and undergo glucuronide conjugation to form inactive metabolites.","t":"Sedatives And Hypnotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the most important mechanism of antiepileptic activity of phenytoin?","o":["Prolongation of Na+ channel inactivation","Reduction in Ca2+ influx","Facilitation of GABA response","Increasing the K+ influx"],"a":0,"e":"The most important mechanism of antiepileptic activity of phenytoin is the prolongation of Na+ ion channel inactivation. It slows down the rate of recovery of Na+ channels and thus limiting the propagation of the action potential.\n\nPhenytoin also inhibits voltage-gated Ca2+ channels. This reduces the influx of calcium inside the membrane and reduces the inflow of potassium ions during repolarization.\n\nAt higher or toxic doses, phenytoin facilitates GABA response and inhibits the action of glutamate.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A known case of seizure disorder has been on long-term phenytoin therapy. Which of the following statements about this drug would be incorrect?","o":["Extensively bound to plasma proteins","Metabolism follows only zero order kinetics","Requires therapeutic drug monitoring","Metabolized extensively by the CYP enzymes in the liver"],"a":1,"e":"Phenytoin follows first-order kinetics at lower concentrations and as the concentration increases, the elimination changes to zero-order kinetics. The rate of elimination of phenytoin varies with the serum concentration.\n\nAs the blood levels rise, the maximal capacity of the liver to metabolize phenytoin is reached. Thus even a small increase in the dosage can result in disproportionally high plasma levels. Therefore, it requires therapeutic drug monitoring.\n\nAbout 90% of phenytoin is bound to plasma proteins, mainly albumin. Phenytoin is metabolized by the CYP enzymes in the liver.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding fosphenytoin?","o":["Water soluble prodrug of phenytoin","Fosphenytoin displaces phenytoin from protein-binding sites","Causes hyperphosphatemia in renal failure patients","ECG monitoring is not required when administering IV"],"a":3,"e":"Fosphenytoin like phenytoin requires continuous ECG monitoring during IV infusion due to the risk of arrhythmias.\n\nFosphenytoin is a water-soluble prodrug of phenytoin. Fosphenytoin is also extensively protein bound, mainly albumin. This binding is saturable and fosphenytoin displaces phenytoin from protein-binding sites.\n\nHydrolysis of fosphenytoin yields phosphate, which increases the risk of hyperphosphatemia in patients with renal failure. It is contraindicated in patients with sinus bradycardia, sino-atrial block, second and third-degree A-V block.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presenting to the ER with generalized tonic-clonic seizures is started on phenytoin therapy. All of the following adverse effects can be expected in this patient except?\n\n1. Lymphadenopathy\n\n2. Ataxia\n\n3. Hypercalcemia\n\n4. Hirsutism\n\n5. Hypoglycemia","o":["1, 3 and 4","2 and 4","2 only","3 and 5"],"a":3,"e":"Phenytoin causes hypocalcemia and hyperglycemia.\n\nIt interferes with the metabolism of vitamin D and intestinal calcium absorption and results in hypocalcemia. Hyperglycemia and glycosuria can be seen due to the inhibition of insulin secretion.\n\nLong-term usage of phenytoin can also cause cutaneous pseudolymphoma which is mainly T-cell predominant.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to find in a baby born to a woman on phenytoin therapy?","o":["Digital hypoplasia","Gum hyperplasia","Microcephaly","Cleft lip"],"a":1,"e":"The newborn baby is unlikely to have gum hyperplasia. A baby born to a pregnant mother who was on phenytoin therapy will be suffering from fetal hydantoin syndrome.\n\nFeatures of fetal hydantoin syndrome are:\n\n• Microcephaly\n\n• Digital hypoplasia\n\n• Cleft lip/ cleft palate\n\nGum hyperplasia is the most common adverse effect of phenytoin in children and young adolescents on chronic use.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with recent-onset primary generalized epilepsy develops a skin reaction due to phenytoin sodium. What is the most appropriate course of action?","o":["Switch to clonazepam","Stop and restart phenytoin sodium after two weeks","Switch to sodium valproate","Switch to ethosuximide"],"a":2,"e":"Hypersensitivity reaction to phenytoin warrants discontinuation of the drug. Switching to sodium valproate is the most appropriate course of action for this patient.\n\nSodium valproate is one of the first-line agents indicated for GTCS. Clonazepam and ethosuximide are primarily used in absence seizures and are not indicated for GTCS.\n\nClonazepam is indicated as adjunctive therapy in the absence seizures when first-line agents (ethosuximide, sodium valproate, lamotrigine) fail to control the seizures adequately.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 51-year-old male complains of severe, sharp, piercing intermittent electrical shock-like pain sensation on the left side of his face. The pain was triggered by talking, washing his face, eating, and brushing his teeth. Which of the following drugs is most effective to treat this patient?","o":["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Sodium valproate","Lamotrigine"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical features in this patient are suggestive of a diagnosis of trigeminal neuralgia and carbamazepine is the most effective agent for its treatment.\n\nIf carbamazepine is ineffective, then second-line agents like lamotrigine and phenytoin are used.\n\nSodium valproate although effective for trigeminal neuralgia lacks enough evidence for use.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about carbamazepine?","o":["It is indicated in focal seizures with impaired awareness","It is an enzyme inhibitor","It causes hypernatremia","It is the drug of choice for status epilepticus"],"a":0,"e":"Carbamazepine is indicated in focal seizures with impaired awareness.\n\nCarbamazepine is an antiepileptic drug that acts by prolonging the inactivated state of the Na+ channel similar to phenytoin. It is used in:\n\n• Focal seizures with impaired awareness\n\n• Focal seizures with intact awareness\n\n• Generalized tonic-clonic seizures\n\n• Trigeminal and related neuralgias\n\n• Manic depressive psychosis.\n\nOption B: Carbamazepine is an enzyme inducer. It is a substrate as well as an inducer of CYP3A4 and CYP2C9.\n\nOption C: Carbamazepine causes hyponatremia and not hypernatremia. Other side effects of carbamazepine include agranulocytosis or aplastic anemia, hepatotoxicity, water retention and hyponatremia (by potentiating ADH action ), and dose-related neurotoxicity.\n\nOption D: It is not used in status epilepticus. Lorazepam is the drug of choice for status epilepticus.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"In patients recieving carbamazepine, which of the following idiosyncratic reactions are not expected to occur?","o":["Rash","Agranulocytosis","Peripheral neuropathy","Steven Johnson syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Peripheral neuropathy is not an idiosyncratic reaction that can occur after carbamazepine use. It is a dose-dependent CNS adverse effect. It can be minimized by gradually increasing the dose from initiation or reducing the maintenance dose.\n\nIdiosyncratic reactions are hypersensitivity reactions like:\n\n• Rash\n\n• Steven-Johnson syndrome\n\n• Eosinophilia\n\n• Lymphadenopathy.\n\n• Diplopia\n\nSerious hematological toxicity includes aplastic anemia and agranulocytosis.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old man is on carbamazepine therapy. Which of the following is expected to occur in this patient?","o":["Hypernatremia","Hyponatremia","Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia"],"a":1,"e":"Carbamazepine is expected to cause hyponatremia in this patient.\n\nCarbamazepine potentiates the action of antidiuretic hormone (ADH). It can result in water retention with decreased osmolarity and hyponatremia, especially in elderly patients with cardiac disease.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An 8-year-old child diagnosed to have focal seizures was initiated on oxcarbazepine. Which of the following would be incorrect regarding the agent being used?","o":["Keto analog of carbamazepine","Enzyme induction potency is less when compared to carbamazepine","Metabolised by glucuronide conjugation","Hyponatremia risk is less when compared to carbamazepine"],"a":3,"e":"The risk of hyponatremia with oxcarbazepine is higher when compared to carbamazepine.\n\nOption A: Oxcarbazepine is a keto analog of carbamazepine. It is a prodrug. The mechanism of action of oxcarbazepine is by blocking voltage-gated sodium channels (same as carbamazepine).\n\nOption C: Active form is metabolized by glucuronide conjugation.\n\nOption B: Oxcarbazepine does not cause auto-induction of metabolizing enzymes. It can induce CYP enzymes but less potent when compared to carbamazepine.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A neonate diagnosed with rhabdomyosarcoma was started on vincristine, cyclophosphamide, and cisplatin. A week after initiation of treatment, the baby had multiple episodes of seizures, refractory to diazepam and lidocaine. Workup revealed an elevated ADH level and euvolemic hyponatremia. Which of the following drugs can be used to treat this patient?","o":["Carbamazepine","Phenytoin","Oxcarbazepine","Phenobarbital"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of refractory seizures induced by hyponatremia caused by SIADH (due to anticancer drugs). Phenytoin is used to treat this patient. It is known to decrease ADH secretion and stop seizures by correcting severe hyponatremia.\n\nOptions A and C: Carbamazepine and oxcarbazepine increase the antidiuretic hormone (ADH) levels. This leads to increased sensitivity of renal tubules to ADH levels and hyponatremia. Carbamazepine cannot be used in SIADH because of its antidiuretic effects.\n\nOption D: Phenobarbital does not affect ADH secretion or action and thus will be ineffective in stopping the seizures.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 5th-grade student, after receiving multiple complaints from teachers at school for repeated episodes of inattention was brought to the OPD. The mother gave a history that her child had brief spells of staring associated with lip-smacking movements. Which anti-epileptic drug is the most effective for this child?","o":["Carbamazepine","Ethosuximide","Phenytoin","Gabapentin"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of absence seizures and ethosuximide is the most effective agent for this condition.\n\nFirst-line drugs in:\n\n• Typical absence seizures: Ethosuximide (drug of choice), valproate, lamotrigine\n\n• Atypical absence seizures: Sodium valproate","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A child with absence seizures improves after being treated with ethosuximide. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Inhibits L type Ca2+ channels","Inhibits N type Ca2+ channels","Inhibits T type Ca2+ channels","Inhibits R type Ca2+ channels"],"a":2,"e":"The mechanism of action of ethosuximide is by inhibition of T-type Ca2+ channels in thalamic neurons at clinically relevant concentrations.\n\nIt also affects other ion channels affected include voltage-gated sodium channels and potassium channels.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old child is brought to the OPD with a history of vacant stares lasting for about half a minute. The EEG shows a characteristic 3 Hz spike and wave pattern. Which of the following drugs can be used for this condition?\n\n1. Carbamazepine\n\n2. Valproate\n\n3. Ethosuximide\n\n4. Gabapentin\n\n5. Pregabalin","o":["1, 2 and 5","2 and 3","2 only","3, 4 and 5"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of absence seizures. Ethosuximide and valproate are the first-line drugs in this condition.\n\nAbsence seizures are characterized by sudden, brief lapses of consciousness without loss of postural control. There is no postictal confusion. The following subtle motor signs may accompany the episodes\n\n• Rapid blinking of the eyelids\n\n• Chewing movements\n\n• Small-amplitude clonic movements of the hands\n\nHyperventilation has a tendency to provoke absence seizures. First-line drugs include:\n\n• Typical absence seizures: Ethosuximide (drug of choice), valproate, lamotrigine\n\n• Atypical absence seizures: Valproate","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an enzyme inducer?","o":["Phenytoin","Phenobarbital","Carbamazepine","Sodium valproate"],"a":3,"e":"Sodium valproate is not an enzyme inducer. It is metabolized by CYP2C9/19 and is an inhibitor of CYP2C9.\n\nPhenytoin, phenobarbital, and carbamazepine are inducers of CYP and UGT enzymes.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not the mechanism of action of sodium valproate?","o":["Prolongation of Na+ channel inactivation","Inhibition of T type Ca2+ channels","Inhibition of GABA transaminase","Inhibition of GABA transporter"],"a":3,"e":"Sodium valproate has no action on the GABA transporter.\n\nMechanism of action of sodium valproate:\n\n• Prolongation of sodium channel inactivation\n\n• Inhibition of T-type Ca2+ channels\n\n• Inhibition of GABA transaminase\n\n• Increases GABA synthesis from glutamic acid.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following are not adverse effects of sodium valproate?","o":["Weight gain","Alopecia","Liver damage","Osteomalacia"],"a":3,"e":"Osteomalacia is not an adverse effect of sodium valproate.\n\nThe side effects of valproate (mnemonic: VALPROATE)\n\n• Vomiting\n\n• Alopecia\n\n• Liver toxicity\n\n• Pancreatitis\n\n• Rash\n\n• Obesity\n\n• Anorexia\n\n• Teratogenicity (most common: spina bifida), thrombocytopenia, tremors\n\n• Edema","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antiepileptic drugs does not cause fulminant hepatitis as a serious complication?","o":["Sodium valproate","Felbamate","Phenytoin","Vigabatrin"],"a":3,"e":"Vigabatrin is not associated with fulminant hepatitis. It is largely excreted unchanged in urine without undergoing any hepatic metabolism.\n\nSodium valproate, felbamate and phenytoin are associated with fulminant hepatitis.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old female patient is admitted to the neurology ward for evaluation of GTCS. She reports that she has been on oral contraceptive pills for the past 6 months. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to this patient?","o":["Carbamazepine","Phenobarbital","Valproate","Phenytoin"],"a":2,"e":"Valproate is the drug used for this patient as it does not cause contraceptive failure.\n\nValproate is an enzyme inhibitor and it inhibits the degradation of OCPs. OCPs thus have a longer duration of action and less incidence of contraceptive failure with valproate.\n\nHowever, other antiepileptic drugs like carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, primidone, oxcarbazepine, topiramate are enzyme inducers. They cause increased degradation of OCPs and hence lead to contraceptive failure.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 16-year-old patient was brought to the ER with bilateral jerking movements of arms. EEG revealed diffuse, symmetric, bilateral 4-6 Hz polyspike and wave discharges with a frontocentral predominance. What is the most appropriate drug for this patient?","o":["Lamotrigine","Levetiracetam","Topiramate","Sodium valproate"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical features and EEG findings are suggestive of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy. The most appropriate drug for management is sodium valproate.\n\nIn patients with myoclonic seizures which are refractory to valproate, clonazepam or clobazam may be added.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old man visits the neurology OPD with complaints of tremors in both his hands which improve on alcohol intake. On examination, there is a significant worsening of tremors towards the end of finger-to-nose testing and head-nodding movements. Which antiepileptic is approved for use in this condition?","o":["Phenobarbital","Phenytoin","Primidone","Valproate"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of a diagnosis of essential tremors in this patient. Primidone is considered to be the first-line therapy for the same along with propranolol.\n\nBilateral, symmetric tremors involving the hands associated with head nodding (titubation) and worsening of tremors towards the end of goal-directed movements such as drinking from a glass or finger-to-nose testing is suggestive of essential tremors.\n\nPrimidone is a barbiturate and functions as a GABA enhancer. It is an enzyme inducer. It is used in the treatment of generalized tonic-clonic seizures and partial seizures.\n\nIt has side effects like sedation, ataxia, and hyperactivity.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Pseudolymphoma may occur in patients on long-term therapy with:","o":["Carbamazepine","Phenobarbital","Phenytoin","Primidone"],"a":2,"e":"Phenytoin on long-term use can result in pseudolymphoma.\n\nThis lymphadenopathy can be difficult to distinguish from malignant lymphoma. Although some studies suggest a causal relationship between phenytoin use and Hodgkin’s disease, the data are far from conclusive. Almost all lesions are benign and termed as pseudolymphoma.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the most common adverse effect of phenytoin in children and young adolescents on chronic use?","o":["Hirsutism","Osteomalacia","Gingival hyperplasia","Megaloblastic anemia"],"a":2,"e":"Gingival hyperplasia occurs in patients on chronic phenytoin therapy. It is the most common manifestation in children and young adolescents.\n\nGingival hyperplasia due to phenytoin therapy occurs due to tissue overgrowth secondary to altered collagen metabolism. The toothless portion of gums is not involved. It does not warrant discontinuation of the drug. It can be minimized by maintaining good oral hygiene.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a carbamazepine analogue that has an advantage of once daily dosing?","o":["Oxcarbezepine","Eslicarbazepine acetate","Dacarbazine","Licarbazepine"],"a":1,"e":"Eslicarbazepine acetate is an analog of carbamazepine that has the advantage of once-daily dosing. It is a prodrug and is rapidly converted to active licarbazepine.\n\nOxcarbazepine is an analog of carbamazepine that is also converted to licarbazepine. Oxcarbazepine does not have the advantage of once-daily dosing like eslicarbazepine acetate.\n\nDacarbazine is not a carbamazepine analog like the other drugs and is a chemotherapy medication used in the treatment of melanoma and Hodgkin's lymphoma.","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of mitral stenosis presents with the given intractable finding that is refractory to conventional therapy. What is the drug of choice?","o":["Probenecid","Diazepam","Haloperidol","Sodium valproate"],"a":3,"e":"The drug used for refractory intractable rheumatic chorea is sodium valproate, as it doesn't have extrapyramidal side effects.\n\nSydenham's chorea is characterized by involuntary, purposeless, jerky movements which mainly affect the head and upper limbs. In the tongue, it is visible as the characteristic darting movements.\n\nMedications used to treat Sydenham chorea are:\n\n• Mild cases - Providing a calm environment.\n\n• Severe cases:\n\n• Dopamine antagonists - haloperidol, pimozide\n\n• Antiepileptic medications - sodium valproate, carbamazepine.\n\n• Severe, refractory cases - Steroids (thought to be immune-mediated)\n\n• IV immunoglobulins, plasmapheresis - limited studies, decreased efficacy (not currently recommended)","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b859810aab194066b796468d2d827abb.GIF"],"t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old child is brought to the OPD with a history of vacant stares lasting for about half a minute. The EEG shows &lt;3 Hz spike and wave pattern. The first-line drug for the management of this condition is:","o":["Sodium valproate","Lamotrigine","Levitiracetam","Felbamate"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario and <3 Hz spike and wave pattern on EEG is suggestive of atypical absence seizures. The first-line drug in the treatment of atypical absence seizures is sodium valproate.\n\nSymmetric 3 Hz spike and wave pattern on EEG is seen in typical absence seizures. First-line drugs in its treatment include:\n\n• Ethosuximide (drug of choice)\n\n• Valproate\n\n• Lamotrigine","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An 18-year-old girl was brought to the OPD with complaints of excessive growth of hair on her face and chest. She is a known case of epilepsy and is on treatment with phenytoin. Which of the following drugs would not cause this condition?","o":["Metoclopramide","Methyldopa","Amantadine","Phenothiazines"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical vignette of excessive hair growth secondary to treatment with phenytoin is suggestive of hirsutism. Amantadine does not cause hirsutism or hypertrichosis but its characteristic side effect is livedo reticularis (bluish discoloration of the skin).\n\nFollowing are the drugs causing hirsutism:\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Anabolic steroids\n\n• Danazol\n\n• Metoclopramide\n\n• Methyldopa\n\n• Phenothiazines\n\n• Progestins\n\n• Reserpine\n\n• Testosterone\n\nFollowing are the drugs causing hypertrichosis:\n\n• Cyclosporine\n\n• Diazoxide\n\n• Hydrocortisone\n\n• Minoxidil\n\n• Penicillamine\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Psoralens\n\n• Streptomycin","t":"Anti Epileptics I","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following anti-epileptics is an AMPA glutamate receptor antagonist?","o":["Levetiracetam","Brivaracetam","Perampanel","Rufinamide"],"a":2,"e":"Perampanel is a selective non-competitive antagonist of the AMPA-type glutamate receptor. It is approved for adjunctive therapy of focal-onset seizures.\n\nOptions A and B: Levetiracetam and brivaracetam act on SV2A proteins and modulates GABA and glutamate release.\n\nOption D: Rufinamide acts on voltage-gated sodium channels.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following mechanisms of action is observed with levetiracetam?","o":["GABA transaminase inhibition","GABA transporter inhibition","Binding to synaptic vesicular protein SV2A","Inhibition of neuronal sodium channel"],"a":2,"e":"Levetiracetam has a binding affinity to synaptic vesicular protein SV2A. SV2A plays a role in vesicle recycling following exocytosis of neurotransmitter\n\nWhen levetiracetam bind to this synaptic vesicular protein, it decreases the release of excitatory neurotransmitter glutamate thus preventing seizures.\n\nLevetiracetam also inhibits N-type Ca2+ channels and Ca2+ release from intracellular stores.Thus, it is considered a broad-spectrum antiepileptic as it has an effect on multiple channels.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Tiagabine has been initiated as an adjunctive treatment for focal seizures in a patient. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Inhibiting GABA transaminase","Increasing GABA synthesis","Inhibiting GABA transporter","Inhibiting glutamate release"],"a":2,"e":"Tiagabine acts by inhibiting the GAT1 GABA transporter, thereby reducing its reuptake into neurons. It also increases the duration of inhibitory impulses in the synapse by prolonging the action of GABA.\n\nTiagabine is approved as an add-on therapy for refractory partial seizures with or without generalization.\n\nOther antiepileptic drugs that have GABA enhancing activity are vigabatrin and stiripentol.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old patient diagnosed with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome was prescribed a drug with NMDA antagonist activity. Identify the drug.","o":["Memantine","Riluzole","Ketamine","Felbamate"],"a":3,"e":"Felbamate is the NMDA receptor antagonist prescribed to this patient. It also potentiates GABA action. It is indicated in refractory primary or secondary generalized seizures and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.\n\nFelbamate is associated with aplastic anemia and liver failure.\n\nOption A: Memantine is used in Alzheimer’s disease.\n\nOption B: Riluzole is used in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.\n\nOption C: Ketamine causes dissociative anesthesia and used in general anesthesia.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An antiepileptic agent which acts by opening neuronal potassium channels was prescribed to a patient admitted to the neurology ward. On discharge, the patient was asked to have a regular ophthalmological examination every 6 months. Which drug is most likely prescribed?","o":["Ezogabine","Rufinamide","Vigabatrin","Gabaculine"],"a":0,"e":"Ezogabine (retigabine) acts by opening neuronal potassium channels. By opening the K+ channels, stabilizes the resting membrane potential and reduces the excitability of the nerve membrane.\n\nFDA recommends that patients on chronic ezogabine therapy need visual acuity and fundus examination every 6 months due to the possibility of retinal abnormalities.\n\nOption B: Rufinamide acts by prolonging an inactive state of the Na+ channel.\n\nOption C: Vigabatrin is a GABA transaminase inhibitor.\n\nOption D: Gabaculine is an irreversible GABA reuptake inhibitor, that has only experimental value, due to high toxicity.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"All of the following statements are true about zonisamide except:","o":["Indicated in focal seizures","Nephrolithiasis is not a side effect","Inhibits T type calcium channel","Blocks low voltage gated Na+ channel"],"a":1,"e":"Nephrolithiasis is a side effect of zonisamide.\n\nZonisamide is a broad-spectrum antiepileptic, that acts by blocking low voltage Na+ channels and T type calcium channels. It also has carbonic anhydrase inhibiting activity that may result in metabolic acidosis and the formation of renal stones.\n\nIt is used in the treatment of focal seizures.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Patients on which of the following antiepileptic drugs will experience weight loss?","o":["Sodium valproate","Vigabatrin","Topiramate","Carbamazepine"],"a":2,"e":"Topiramate causes weight loss as one of its common side effects.\n\nTopiramate has mixed mechanisms of action:\n\n• Reduces voltage gate Na+ currents\n\n• Activates hyperpolarizing K+ currents\n\n• Enhances postsynaptic GABAA current\n\n• Limits the activation of AMPA subtype of glutamate receptors.\n\nTopiramate is used in focal seizures, GTCS and Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.\n\nThe side effects:\n\n• Cognitive impairment\n\n• Weight loss\n\n• Fatigue\n\n• Renal calculi\n\nSodium valproate, vigabatrin (highest incidence), and carbamazepine cause weight gain.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old patient presented to the OPD with complaints of pain over a healed dermatomal rash on his lateral chest wall. Gabapentin was prescribed to this patient. The analgesic activity of this drug is mainly exerted by which of the following?","o":["Increasing the duration of GABA Cl- channel opening","Inhibiting the GABA transaminase enzyme","Inhibiting the uptake of GABA at the presynaptic membrane","Inhibiting the Ca2+ channel current"],"a":3,"e":"Gabapentin exerts its analgesic activity by inhibiting the Ca2+ channel current in this patient who is most likely suffering from post-herpetic neuralgia.\n\nGabapentin and pregabalin have close structural similarity to GABA. Still, they do not act through the effects of GABA receptors or any other mechanism related to GABA-mediated neurotransmission.\n\nThey inhibit the calcium channel current which decreases the entry of calcium in the presynaptic neuron. When calcium entry in the neuron is reduced, there is a decrease in the release of glutamate. This lowers the excitability of the neuron, thus reducing seizure activity.\n\nTherapeutic use of these drugs:\n\nGabapentin: Focal seizures, postherpetic neuralgia pain\n\nPregabalin: Focal seizures, postherpetic neuralgia pain, diabetic neuropathy pain, spinal cord injury pain, fibromyalgia pain.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An infant presents with sudden jerky movements of the head, neck and arms. On EEG evaluation, a characteristic pattern of hypsarrhythmia is observed. Which is the GABA transaminase inhibitor approved for the treatment of this condition?","o":["Vigabatrin","Tiagabine","Progabide","Pregabalin"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with hypsarrytmia on EEG is suggestive of West syndrome. Vigabatrin is an inhibitor of the GABA transaminase that can be used in this condition.\n\nInhibition of the enzyme GABA transaminase leads to an increase in the concentration of GABA in the synaptic cleft which is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. This contributes to its anticonvulsant property of vigabatrin.\n\nWest syndrome is a rare type of epilepsy syndrome that begins in infancy. It is characterized by a triad of:\n\n• Infantile seizures (also called salaam spells)\n\n• Developmental regression\n\n• Hypsarrhythmia i.e Himalayan wave pattern on EEG\n\nTreatment of West syndrome includes:\n\n• ACTH\n\n• Vigabatrin if associated with tuberous sclerosis","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the drug of choice in the treatment of infantile spasms?","o":["ACTH","Phenobarbitone","Carbemazepine","Vigabatrin"],"a":0,"e":"The drug of choice in the treatment of infantile spasms or West syndrome is adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH.)\n\nThe mechanism of action of ACTH or glucocorticoids is unknown but may involve a reduction in inflammatory processes. Vigabatrin is used if the spasms are associated with tuberous sclerosis.\n\nOther antiseizure medications that may be helpful are valproate, topiramate, zonisamide, or benzodiazepines.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which antiepileptic drug is associated with permanent visual loss?","o":["Topiramate","Ethosuximide","Vigabatrin","Felbamate"],"a":2,"e":"Vigabatrin is associated with progressive and permanent visual loss.\n\nIt is a GABA transaminase inhibitor indicated in:\n\n• West syndrome associated with tuberous sclerosis\n\n• Refractory focal seizures with impaired awareness.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antiepileptic drugs are approved for prophylaxis in patients suffering from migraines?\n\n1. Sodium valproate\n\n2. Carbamazepine\n\n3. Topiramate\n\n4. Rufinamide\n\n5. Phenytoin","o":["2, 3 and 4","3 and 5","1, 4 and 5","1 and 3"],"a":3,"e":"Sodium valproate, topiramate and gabapentin are approved anti-epileptics for use in prophylaxis of migraine.\n\nCarbamazepine, phenytoin and rufinamide are not used for prophylaxis of a migraine.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 22-year-old female is brought to the casualty with seizures for the past 10 minutes. History from relatives suggests no seizure-free intervals in the past 10 minutes. After stabilizing the vitals of the patient, which is the first drug you administer to the patient?","o":["Lorazepam","Diazepam","Phenobarbitone","Thiopentone sodium"],"a":0,"e":"This scenario described above is of status epilepticus. The first drug used in the management is lorazepam.\n\nStatus epilepticus refers to continuous seizures or repetitive, discrete seizures with impaired consciousness in the inter-ictal period. For practical purposes, it is defined as any one of the following:\n\n• Continuing seizures >5 minutes or\n\n• No regaining of consciousness between 2 seizures.\n\nManagement of status epilepticus:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c41b7774f474be49e4a076917ec371cx1096x1496.PNG"],"t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old newly married female comes to the OPD with a positive urine pregnancy test. Her history is suggestive of antiepileptic use since 8 years. Which of the following statements is incorrect about further management in this patient?","o":["Anti epileptic agents has to be continued at stabilised doses","Immediately stop antiepileptics once pregnancy is detected","Folate supplementation is a must","No antiepiletic is deemed safe in pregnancy"],"a":1,"e":"Stopping antiepileptics abruptly once pregnancy is detected in this patient is not recommended. If this is done, then there is a lot of risk to the fetus by uncontrolled seizures and can result in anoxia and intellectual disability. Antiepileptics have to be continued and then the dose can be tapered slowly if there are no episodes of seizures.\n\nManagement of seizures in pregnancy:\n\n• If the patient is a known case of seizure and is on any antiepileptic drug: The same prescribed drug is continued\n\n• If the first episode occurs during pregnancy: The drug preferred is either lamotrigine or levetiracetam\n\n• Folic acid (1-4 mg/day) should be given to all\n\nNo antiepileptic is deemed safe in pregnancy. So, it is important to have the patient on monotherapy at the lowest effective dose, especially during the first trimester.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An infant is born with a birth defect as shown in the image below. It is the most common teratogenic effect of:","o":["Phenytoin","Topiramate","Sodium valproate","Phenobarbital"],"a":2,"e":"The above illustration is suggestive of an infant with spina bifida of the open type which is the most common teratogenic effect of sodium valproate.\n\nOption A: Phenytoin is usually associated with fetal hydantoin syndrome.\n\nOption B: Topiramate has shown growth defects in animal studies and hypospadias have been shown in the male fetus but the definite relationship is lacking.\n\nOption D: Phenobarbital has teratogenic effects similar to phenytoin.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ba16aff3a87f412ab13b9cea93dcd207.PNG"],"t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which is the treatment of choice for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy in pregnancy?","o":["Levetiracetam","Carbamazepine","Vigabatrin","Phenytoin"],"a":0,"e":"The drug of choice for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME) in pregnancy is levetiracetam. Lamotrigine or levetiracetam are alternative first-line in pregnancy as they have fewer teratogenic effects.\n\nThe drug of choice for JME is valproate. However, in pregnant women or women of childbearing age, valproate should be avoided because of the increased risks of fetal malformations and neurodevelopmental delay.\n\nCarbamazepine, vigabatrin, and phenytoin are not indicated in juvenile myoclonic epilepsy as they may worsen myoclonic jerks and absence seizures.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 29-year-old primigravida, at 4-months period of gestation and history of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, visits your clinic. She has been regularly taking sodium valproate and is now concerned about the medication possibly affecting her child. What would you suggest to this patient?","o":["Immediately taper valproate and start lamotrigine","Continue valproate with serial drug monitoring","Switch to carbamazepine","Add lamotrigine to valproate"],"a":1,"e":"In the given scenario, the patient should be advised to continue valproate, and serial drug monitoring should be done.\n\nValproate has a higher teratogenic potential. It is usually avoided before conception if other antiepileptics with less teratogenicity (lamotrigine. levetiracetam) can be used. But for a woman who is pregnant and is already taking valproate, changing the drug is not recommended.\n\nOption A - The patient is presenting in the second trimester when organogenesis would have been complete. Therefore, tapering the drug and introducing lamotrigine is not an option as precipitating seizures has a greater risk of fetal harm at this stage.\n\nOption C: Carbamazepine is not indicated in JME as it may worsen the myoclonic jerks and absence seizures.\n\nOption D- Adding additional drugs to stabilized doses may increase teratogenicity. So adding lamotrigine to valproate is also not a viable option.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is recommended for prophylaxis in a pregnant woman who is on anti-epileptic drugs with an enzyme-inducing property?","o":["Vitamin K","Anti D immunoglobulin","Indomethacin","Prostacyclin"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamin K is recommended for prophylaxis in pregnant women who are on antiepileptic drugs having enzyme-inducing properties.\n\nAntiepileptic drugs with enzyme inducing properties:\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Carbamazepine\n\n• Oxcarbazepine\n\n• Topiramate\n\n• Phenobarbital\n\n• Primidone\n\nThey induce CYP enzymes that interfere with the metabolism of vitamin K. Deficiency of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors can occur in the newborn leading to coagulopathy and intracerebral hemorrhage.\n\nAlthough hemorrhage is uncommon, the mother should be treated with oral vitamin K, 20 mg/day in the last two weeks of pregnancy. The infant should receive an intramuscular injection of vitamin K (1 mg) at birth.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antiepileptics is effective in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, but shows the highest propensity to cause rashes in young children warranting its withdrawal?","o":["Sodium valproate","Levetiracetam","Lamotrigine","Vigabatrin"],"a":2,"e":"Lamotrigine is effective in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome and has the highest propensity to cause rashes in young children.\n\nLamotrigine is also associated with toxic epidermal necrosis and Stevens-Johnson Syndrome in some patients. The incidence of serious rash and other serious skin conditions is higher with lamotrigine than other anti-epileptics provided in the options. The occurrence of rash warrants the stoppage of the drug.\n\nOption A: Sodium valproate is the drug of choice in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. It can cause fulminant hepatitis especially in children < 2 years.\n\nOption B: Levetiracetam is also effective in most of the seizures present in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. It can increase blood pressure in children < 4 years.\n\nOption D: Vigabatrin is also effective in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, especially as an add-on therapy. It can cause permanent visual field defects.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Patients receiving which of the following anti-microbial drugs are least likely to develop seizures?","o":["Beta lactam compounds","Quinolones","Macrolides","Isoniazid"],"a":2,"e":"Macrolides are the least likely anti-microbial out of the given options to cause seizures.\n\nAntimicrobials associated with causing seizures:\n\n• Beta-lactam and related compounds\n\n• Quinolones\n\n• Acyclovir\n\n• Isoniazid\n\n• Ganciclovir\n\nOther groups of drugs that are implicated in causing epilepsy:\n\n• Alkylating agents - busulfan, chlorambucil\n\n• Antimalarials - chloroquine, mefloquine\n\n• Anesthetics/ analgesics - meperidine, fentanyl, tramadol, local anesthetics\n\n• Immunomodulatory drugs - cyclosporine, tacrolimus, interferons\n\n• Psychotropics:\n\n• Antidepressants - bupropion\n\n• Antipsychotics - clozapine\n\n• Anti-manic drugs - lithium\n\n• Drugs of abuse - amphetamine, cocaine, phencyclidine, methylphenidate\n\n• Miscellaneous - Theophylline, memantine.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs of abuse is least likely to be associated with seizures?","o":["Amphetamine","Cocaine","Cannabinoids","Phencyclidine"],"a":2,"e":"Cannabinoids are being investigated for the treatment of various types of epilepsy and are least likely to cause seizures.\n\nDrugs of abuse that are implicated in causing seizures are:\n\n• Amphetamine\n\n• Cocaine\n\n• Phencyclidine\n\n• Methylphenidate","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presented to the OPD with a skin rash for 3 days. He gives a history of antiepileptic drug use for 2 months and is ataxic on examination. He is diagnosed to have Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Which of the following drugs are implicated in these adverse effects?","o":["Phenobarbital","Lamotrigine","Topiramate","Ethosuximide"],"a":1,"e":"Lamotrigine is known to cause such adverse effects in patients.\n\nAdverse effects of lamotrigine:\n\n• Skin rash\n\n• Stevens-Johnson syndrome\n\n• Dizziness\n\n• Diplopia\n\n• Sedation\n\n• Ataxia\n\n• Headache","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs if used can lead to behavioral effects such as aggression?","o":["Phenytoin","Phenobarbitone","Levetiracetam","Pregabalin"],"a":2,"e":"Children who are on levetiracetam have a higher risk of developing several behavioral side-effects such as aggression, hostility, and nervousness compared to children who do not use levetiracetam.\n\nThese side effects are seen in less frequency with brivaracetam.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drug is approved for use in fibromyalgia?","o":["Gabapentin","Pregabalin","Tiagabine","Topiramate"],"a":1,"e":"Pregabalin is approved for use in fibromyalgia. It is also approved for use in diabetic peripheral neuropathy.\n\nMechanism of analgesic action:\n\n• Interacts at the binding site of the alpha, delta subunit of voltage-dependent Ca2+ channels\n\n• Correlates with decreased Ca2+ channel function and release of neurotransmitters\n\n• Decreased neurotransmitter release is associated with reduced neuronal hyperexcitability.\n\nOptions A and C: Gabapentin and tiagabine are also useful in fibromyalgia but are not approved for this indication and are used off-label.\n\nOption D: Topiramate's efficacy is not encouraging in fibromyalgia and neuropathic pain.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the first-line treatment for infantile spasms associated with tuberous sclerosis?","o":["ACTH","Vigabatrin","Glucocorticoids","Sodium valproate"],"a":1,"e":"Vigabatrin is particularly effective in infantile spasms associated with tuberous sclerosis and is considered the first-line treatment in this condition.\n\nACTH is considered to be the drug of choice for infantile spasms that are not associated with tuberous sclerosis.","t":"Anti Epileptics Ii","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the primary mechanism of action of typical antipsychotics?","o":["D2 receptor antagonism","5HT2 receptor antagonism","D2 receptor agonism","5HT2 receptor agonism"],"a":0,"e":"Typical or first-generation antipsychotics primarily act by postsynaptic D2 receptor blockade.\n\nThe second-generation or atypical antipsychotics mainly act by blocking 5HT2A receptors but they also have weak D2 blocking effect.\n\nAntipsychotics\nDrugs\n\nFirst-generation\nD2 antagonists\n\n• Chlorpromazine\n\n• Haloperidol\n\n• Fluphenazine, Trifluoperazine\n\n• Thiothixene\n\nSecond-generation\n\n5HT2A and D2 antagonists\n\n• Clozapine\n\n• Iloperidone\n\n• Olanzapine\n\n• Quetiapine\n\n• Risperidone\n\n• Ziprasidone\n\nD2 partial agonists\n\n• Aripiprazole\n\n• Cariprazine\n\nD2 and D3 antagonist\n\n• Amisulpride\n\n5HT2A inverse agonist without D2 binding\n\n• Pimavanserin\n\nHistaminic H1 and muscarinic M1 receptors are blocked by most of the first and second generation agents. This contributes to sedation and anticholinergic effects of antipsychotics.\n\nOption C: Ropinirole and pramipexole are D2 agonists used in restless leg syndrome and Parkinson's disease.\n\nOption D: Lorcaserin is a 5HT2C agonist that was used to treat obesity. It was withdrawn in 2020 as it was linked to increased cancer risk.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with psychosis was prescribed an antipsychotic medication. Which one of the following side effects cannot be explained by the antagonistic action of the drug on D2 receptors?","o":["Constipation","Hyperprolactinemia","Affective flattening","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Constipation and dry mouth are side effects of antipsychotics that have potent antimuscarinic action.\n\nHyperprolactinaemia, affective flattening, and rigidity in neuroleptic malignant syndrome occur due to D2 receptor blockade.\n\nApart from their action on D2 and 5-HT receptors, antipsychotic drugs also exert the following effects:\n\nAction of drug\nExample\nSide effect\n\nAnti-muscarinic\n\nChlorpromazine\n\nThioridazine\n\nOlanzapine\n\nDry mouth, constipation, dilated pupil, urinary retention\n\nAlpha blocker\n\nChlorpromazine\n\nClozapine\n\nHypotension, sedation\n\nH1 blocker\nRisperidone\nAntiemetic, sedation","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A woman presented with sudden onset high-grade fever, muscle rigidity, and altered sensorium. She had been on antipsychotic therapy with chlorpromazine. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Malignant hyperthermia","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Anticholinergic syndrome","Serotonin syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The features are suggestive of neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) that is associated with the use of typical antipsychotics like chlorpromazine.\n\nMalignant neuroleptic syndrome is characterized by extreme muscle rigidity, fever, unstable BP and myoglobinemia. It can even lead to death. It can occur weeks to months after initiation of antipsychotic therapy and can persist for up to a week after discontinuation of the drug.\n\nThe probable mechanism of this condition is dopamine antagonism.\n\nIt is treated by stopping the offending antipsychotic immediately, supportive care, dantrolene, and bromocriptine.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old patient presents to the OPD with complaints of a brownish hue to her vision since 2 weeks. She has a history of being on antipsychotic medication for the past 10 weeks. Complete workup reveals pigmentary retinopathy in this patient. Which of the following drugs is implicated in this condition?","o":["Ziprasidone","Thioridazine","Quetiapine","Chlorpromazine"],"a":1,"e":"The pigmentary retinopathy seen in this patient is an adverse effect seen with the use of thioridazine which is a typical antipsychotic.\n\nIn advanced cases, the deposits may resemble retinitis pigmentosa and the deposits are associated with 'browning of vision'. The drug is rarely used as it causes prolongation of QTc interval.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient having both positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia was prescribed an atypical antipsychotic with partial D2 agonism. Identify the drug.","o":["Clozapine","Fluphenazine","Aripiprazole","Risperidone"],"a":2,"e":"Aripiprazole is an atypical antipsychotic showing D2 partial agonism. It has a higher affinity for D2 receptors than for 5HT2A receptors.\n\nAs a partial agonist, aripiprazole has a dual-action which makes it useful in patients with both positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia.\n\nOptions A & D: Clozapine and risperidone are atypical antipsychotics with predominant 5HT2A antagonistic activity over D2 antagonism.\n\nOption B: Fluphenazine is a typical antipsychotic. They have predominant D2 antagonism property and such action on the mesocortical pathway may increase the negative symptoms of schizophrenia.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following atypical antipsychotics is a preferential D3 receptor partial agonist?","o":["Aripiprazole","Cariprazine","Clozapine","Amisulpride"],"a":1,"e":"Cariprazine is an atypical antipsychotic which is also a preferential D3 receptor partial agonist. Like other atypical antipsychotics, it also blocks D2 and 5-HT2 receptors.\n\nOption A: Aripiprazole is a dual D2/D3 partial agonist but it has no preference for the D3 receptor.\n\nOption C: Clozapine is a D2 receptor antagonist.\n\nOption D: Amisulpride is a D2 and D3 receptor antagonist.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antipsychotics should be given sublingually?","o":["Quetiapine","Asenapine","Olanzapine","Amisulpiride"],"a":1,"e":"Asenapine is only available as an orally disintegrating tablet preparation that has to be administered sublingually.\n\nIt undergoes very high first-pass metabolism and so bioavailability is very low via the oral route. Hence, sublingual administration is preferred.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A schizophrenic patient was initiated on haloperidol therapy. 2 days later he presented to the OPD with deviation of his eyes, asymmetric neck posture and inability to open his mouth completely. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Acute dystonia","Tardive dyskinesia","Parkinsonism","Akathisia"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario of deviation of the eyes (oculogyric crisis), asymmetric position of the head or neck (torticollis) and inability to open the mouth (trismus) are pointing towards a diagnosis of haloperidol-induced acute dystonia.\n\nThese extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) occur due to the antagonism of dopamine D2 receptors.\n\nAcute dystonia is more commonly associated with high potency typical antipsychotics like haloperidol and occurs within 1 to 5 days of initiation of therapy. Benztropine or diphenhydramine may be used for treatment.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man complains of feeling restless and irritable with an irresistible urge to move. He had recently been started on antipsychotic therapy with haloperidol. He appears agitated and paces up and down the room. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Ekbom disease","Magnan syndrome","Restless leg syndrome","Akathisia"],"a":3,"e":"The features mentioned are suggestive of akathisia that is commonly associated with the use of high potency typical antipsychotics like haloperidol.\n\nAkathisia is described as subjective complaints of restlessness accompanied by observed movements such as movement of the legs, rocking from foot to foot, pacing, or inability to sit or stand still. It can occur at any time after 5-60 days from starting the drug.\n\nTreatment of akathisia:\n\n• Following a slower dose titration schedule, dose reduction, or switching to a less potent antipsychotic\n\n• First-line drugs - beta-blockers\n\n• Second line drugs - benzodiazepines like lorazepam\n\n• Third line drugs - anticholinergic agents like trihexyphenidyl, benztropine","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs does not cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome?","o":["Amantadine","Domperidone","Quetiapine","Metoclopramide"],"a":0,"e":"Amantadine does not cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome.\n\nNeuroleptic malignant syndrome resembles very severe parkinsonism and is caused by D2 receptor antagonists. Amantadine increases the presynaptic release of dopamine and has agonist and modulatory action at postsynaptic D2 receptors.\n\nOption C: Quetiapine is an atypical antipsychotic. Although this class of antipsychotics has a lower chance of causing NMS compared to typical antipsychotics, but cases of fatal NMS following the administration of quetiapine have been recorded.\n\nOptions B and D: Metoclopramide and domperidone are gastro kinetic agents with D2 antagonism. Although domperidone has restricted entry across blood-brain barrier when compared to metoclopramide, there are reports of NMS with this agent.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following effects of chlorpromazine is not due to its antidopaminergic activity?","o":["Hyperprolactinemia","Extrapyramidal side effects","Antiemetic","Orthostatic hypotension"],"a":3,"e":"Orthostatic hypotension is due to the alpha-receptor blocking the action of chlorpromazine.\n\nChlorpromazine is a typical or first-generation antipsychotic with D2 blocking and additional alpha-receptor-blocking and anticholinergic activity.\n\nOption A: Dopamine inhibits prolactin release causing hypoprolactinemia and hence antidopaminergic action of chlorpromazine causes hyperprolactinemia.\n\nOption B: Extrapyramidal side effects occur due to D2 antagonism in the nigrostriatal region.\n\nOption C: The antiemetic action is due to D2 antagonism in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ).","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08cf0bfc78c94c4eb266fd9cb414178cx1280x2075.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old with schizophrenia has not responded to adequate treatment with two different antipsychotics. Which of the following would be the drug of choice for his condition?","o":["Olanzapine","Clozapine","Risperidone","Paliperidone"],"a":1,"e":"The drug of choice for treatment-resistant schizophrenia is clozapine.\n\nTreatment-resistant schizophrenia is the lack of response to two different antipsychotics given at adequate dosages for a minimum duration of 4-6 weeks. If patients demonstrate even a mild amount of improvement during this period, it may be reasonable to wait before changing the medication, since patients may improve at a steady rate for 3-6 months.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man is brought to the casualty following an episode of seizure. He says he was recently switched to a higher dose of his antipsychotic medication. Which drug is he most likely on?","o":["Olanzapine","Clozapine","Aripirazole","Risperidone"],"a":1,"e":"The drug most likely implicated here is clozapine as it has the highest risk of precipitating seizures. It causes dose-dependent lowering of the seizure threshold.\n\nClozapine-associated seizures can be treated with sodium valproate.\n\nClozapine side effects: (Mnemonic - “Some say Clozapine has too many nasty adverse effects”)\n\n• Seizures\n\n• Sedation\n\n• Constipation\n\n• Hypotension/hypersalivation\n\n• Tachycardia\n\n• Mass increase: weight gain and increased appetite\n\n• Nausea and vomiting\n\n• Agranulocytosis (neutropenia)\n\n• Enuresis and polyuria","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true regarding clozapine?","o":["More potently blocks D2 as compared to D1 receptors","Drug concentration is kept below 350ng/ml to avoid agranulocytosis","Should not be used along with carbamazepine","Should be discontinued if the neutrophil count is below 500/microL"],"a":1,"e":"Agranulocytosis is an immune-mediated adverse effect and unlike seizures, it is not dependent on dose. Thus, agranulocytosis can happen at any plasma concentration of clozapine and concentration < 350 ng/mL does not lower the risk.\n\nOption A: Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic, which acts by blocking the 5HT2, α2 and D4 receptors. The potency of block is D4 > D2 >D1.\n\nOption C: Concomitant administration of carbamazepine with clozapine is contraindicated because of the increased risk of leukopenia. In addition, carbamazepine is an enzyme inducer and reduces the half-life of clozapine.\n\nOption D: Administration of clozapine requires regular blood count monitoring due to the risk of agranulocytosis. Monitoring is performed weekly for the first 6 months and once in two weeks for the next 6 months. Patients must be asked to report for evaluation in case of symptoms such as fever or sore throat. Clozapine should be discontinued if the neutrophil count drops below 500 cells/μL.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 34-year-old woman presented to the ophthalmology OPD with complaints of diminution of vision. She has bipolar disorder and has been on antipsychotic therapy for the past 8 months. Ophthalmic examination revealed the finding seen in the below image . What drug was she most likely on?","o":["Clozapine","Quetiapine","Aripiprazole","Risperidone"],"a":1,"e":"The image shows a whitish opacity in the pupillary region and this in combination with the presenting features are suggestive of cataract. This is an adverse effect seen with the use of quetiapine which is an atypical antipsychotic.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/91264c7aba114dc3b26e20d57e557e24.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antipsychotics cause QT prolongation at therapeutic doses?\n\n1. Haloperidol\n\n2. Fluphenazine\n\n3. Quetiapine\n\n4. Ziprasidone\n\n5. Clozapine","o":["1, 2, 5","3, 4","1, 3, 4","1, 2"],"a":2,"e":"Haloperidol, quetiapine and ziprasidone cause QT prolongation at therapeutic doses.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Patient X and patient Y are on different antipsychotics. X has gained a lot of weight and has been feeling dizzy when she stands. Y has not gained weight, but is very restless and keeps pacing around the room. The drugs they are most likely taking are:","o":["X- Haloperidol, Y- Chlorpromazine","X- Haloperidol, Y- Risperidone","X- Clozapine, Y- Quetiapine","X- Olanzapine, Y- Haloperidol"],"a":3,"e":"The most likely option amongst the given options is:\n\nPatient X is taking olanzapine which has side effects of weight gain and hypotension.\n\nOlanzapine and clozapine account for most cases of weight gain and drug-induced diabetes mellitus.\n\nPatient Y is taking haloperidol and has developed the side effect of akathisia. This is an extrapyramidal effect in which the patient is restless, irritable, and feels an urge to keep moving.\n\nHaloperidol has a high risk of developing extrapyramidal side effects such as akathisia, dystonia, and tardive dyskinesia.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a side effect of ziprasidone?","o":["Sialorrhea","Lenticular deposits","Prolongation of QT interval","Agranulocytosis"],"a":2,"e":"Ziprasidone causes prolongation of QT interval. It should be avoided in patients with a history of cardiac arrhythmias and should not be given in combination with other drugs known to prolong the QT interval.\n\nThe other options mentioned are side effects of clozapine.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman suffering from Huntington's chorea is on haloperidol. Which of the following adverse drug effects is best attributed to the supersensitivity of dopamine receptors?","o":["Muscle rigidity, labile blood pressure, hyperreflexia, hyperthermia, tachycardia, tremors","Lip smacking, facial dyskinesia, protrusion and rolling of the tongue, involuntary movements of the extremities","Facial grimacing, involuntary upward eye movement, laryngeal spasm and arching of the trunk forward","Stooped posture, rigidity, shuffling gait, bradykinesia, and pill rolling tremors"],"a":1,"e":"Lip-smacking, facial dyskinesia, protrusion and rolling of the tongue, and involuntary movements of the extremities are characteristic of tardive dyskinesia. This is caused by supersensitivity of dopamine receptors.\n\nIt occurs as a consequence of chronic postsynaptic D2 receptor blockade by antipsychotics, which results in upregulation and supersensitivity of postsynaptic dopamine receptors.\n\nTardive dyskinesia is usually seen in patients treated with typical antipsychotics (first generation) for more than a year. This may be reversible with early recognition and drug discontinuation. Treatment is with valbenazine, deutetrabenazine (VMAT2 inhibitors).","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old man is brought by his family with complaints of mental instability. On questioning, he says that he sees his grandparents in the room and talks to them often but the family confirms that it's been 10 years since his grandparents passed away. He was then prescribed an antipsychotic drug following which he developed parkinsonism. Which of the following antiviral drugs can be used for treating his condition?","o":["Foscarnet","Acyclovir","Amantadine","Interferons"],"a":2,"e":"Amantadine is an antiviral agent that is the most commonly used medication for antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism.\n\nIt increases the concentration of dopamine (DA) at the synapse and appears to act by certain mechanisms like:\n\n• presynaptic DA reuptake blockade\n\n• facilitation of DA release\n\n• postsynaptic DA agonism\n\n• receptor modulation","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statement is false with respect to antipsychotics and pregnancy?","o":["Olanzapine crosses the placenta","Olanzapine is unlikely to increase the risk of birth defects","There is a high risk of gestational diabetes and neonatal seizures with clozapine","Guidelines recommend switching from clozapine to another drug during pregnancy"],"a":3,"e":"The guidelines recommend continuing the same antipsychotic, as switching the drug during pregnancy would cause a higher rate of relapse.\n\nAntipsychotic drugs and pregnancy:\n\n• Crossing the placenta: Olanzapine > Haloperidol > Risperidone > Quetiapine.\n\n• Olanzapine is unlikely to increase the risk of birth defects above the background population risk. However, it can cause low birth weight and increased NICU admissions.\n\n• Clozapine is associated with gestational diabetes and neonatal seizures.\n\nNote: Although antipsychotic drugs appear to be relatively safe in pregnancy, the available data does not provide adequate guidance on the choice of agent. Because of limited experience in humans, olanzapine should be used in pregnancy only when the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.","t":"Anti Psychotics","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old man is brought to the psychiatry OPD by his mother with complaints that he has been behaving aggressively for the last 10 days. He believes he owns the biggest automobile company in the world and talks about how his made-up company will put satellites into space someday. She also says that he sleeps for only 3 hours a day. Which of the following drugs cannot be used to treat this patient?","o":["Sodium valproate","Lamotrigine","Carbamazepine","Lithium"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical features given are suggestive of acute mania in this patient and lamotrigine is not indicated in this condition.\n\nIt requires a slow, extended titration to minimize the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome and is used for maintenance therapy in bipolar disorder.\n\nLithium, sodium valproate, carbamazepine, and atypical antipsychotics are indicated in acute mania.\n\nLithium is indicated in both acute mania and maintenance therapy.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is being treated with lithium for manic-depressive psychosis. Which of the following is true about the use of this drug?","o":["Can be given alone for acute episodes","Serum concentration monitoring is not used for dose adjustment","Cause benign and reversible flattening of T wave on ECG","Causes significant weight loss"],"a":2,"e":"Prolonged use of lithium causes a benign and reversible flattening of T-wave.\n\nOption A: Lithium is usually not given alone in acute episodes as the onset of antimanic action is slow. Benzodiazepines, anticonvulsants, or atypical antipsychotics are administered along with lithium in the early phases of treatment.\n\nOption B: Lithium has a very narrow therapeutic index and monitoring of serum concentration is used for guiding dose adjustment.\n\nOption D: Lithium causes significant weight gain.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on lithium therapy is diagnosed to be hypertensive. Which of the following antihypertensive drugs is contraindicated here?","o":["Clonidine","Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Thiazide diuretics"],"a":3,"e":"Thiazides are contraindicated in patients who are on lithium therapy.\n\nThiazide diuretics cause significant reductions in Li+ clearance that result in toxic levels.\n\nThe K+ sparing diuretics have modest effects on lithium excretion, with a mild increase in serum levels.\n\nLoop diuretics have a limited impact on Li+ serum levels.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of mania, on lithium therapy, is brought with complaints of vomiting and multiple episodes of diarrhea. On examination, coarse tremors and an ataxic gait are noted. What is the expected level of serum lithium in this patient?","o":["&gt; 1.2 mEq/L","&gt; 0.8 mEq/L","&gt; 1.5 mEq/L","&gt; 0.5 mEq/L"],"a":2,"e":"Clinical features are suggestive of lithium toxicity, where serum levels >1.5 mEq/L are considered toxic.\n\nLithium has a narrow therapeutic index (0.8–1.2 mEq/L) and therefore requires monitoring to suspect toxicity. The toxic effects of lithium usually start manifesting when the serum level crosses 1.5 mEq/L.\n\nAcute intoxication is characterized by:\n\n• Vomiting\n\n• Profuse diarrhea\n\n• Coarse tremors\n\n• Ataxia\n\n• Coma\n\n• Convulsions.\n\nA fine action tremor is common in patients taking lithium at therapeutic doses and can develop at any time during treatment. Therapeutic lithium tremor is generally symmetric, limited to the arms, and nonprogressive.Tremor from lithium toxicity is coarser and more disabling, may also affect the legs, and occurs with other symptoms of toxicity.\n\nSerious effects of lithium toxicity will involve confusion, hyperreflexia, and seizures. Motor and cognitive side effects can become irreversible with persistent cerebellar tremor being the most common.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"At what serum lithium levels would you consider dialysis in acute lithium toxicity?","o":["&gt; 2.0 mEq/L","&gt; 4.0 mEq/L","&gt; 3.0 mEq/L","&gt; 1.5 mEq/L"],"a":1,"e":"In acute overdoses, hemodialysis is indicated in:\n\n• Serum lithium level is > 4.0 mEq/L\n\n• Patients exhibiting symptoms of toxicity.\n\nNote: While hemodialysis is considered when serum lithium level is 4.0 mEq/L and above, signs of lithium toxicity usually start showing when the serum level is > 1.5 mEq/L. One should not get confused between the two.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions do not warrant lithium therapy?","o":["Chemotherapy induced neutropenia","Major depression","Vasculogenic headache","Generalised anxiety disorder"],"a":3,"e":"Lithium is not indicated in generalized anxiety disorder.\n\nBipolar disorder is the primary indication for lithium.\n\nOption A: Effect of leukocytosis may hasten recovery from cancer chemotherapy-induced leukopenia and agranulocytosis.\n\nOption B: Unipolar depression can be treated with lithium as an adjuvant in resistant cases.\n\nOption C: Vasculogenic headaches like cluster headaches.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following must be administered initially in patients with acute mania with psychotic features?","o":["Lithium","Olanzapine","Carbamazepine","Sodium valproate"],"a":1,"e":"In acute mania, even in patients without overt psychotic manifestations, atypical antipsychotics like olanzapine is used initially for rapid control of psychosis. Atypical antipsychotics are also effective as monotherapy.\n\nThe therapeutic effects of lithium, carbamazepine, and valproic acid are seen only after a few days. Therefore they are effective only with daily dosing with adequate serum monitoring.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with mania has been initiated on lithium therapy. While counseling the patient about the medication, which of the following effects do you mention as the most common CNS adverse effect?","o":["Ataxia","Slurred speech","Fine tremors","Coarse tremors"],"a":2,"e":"The most common CNS adverse effect of lithium in the therapeutic dose range is fine postural hand tremors. Therapeutic lithium tremor is generally symmetric, limited to the arms, and nonprogressive.\n\nOptions A and B: Ataxia, slurred speech, and/or incoordination are adverse effects seen during peak CNS/serum concentrations. Single bedtime dosing can avoid these adverse effects.\n\nOption D: Coarse tremors are seen in case of lithium toxicity and is more disabling and may also affect the legs.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on chronic lithium therapy is diagnosed to have hypothyroidism. What should be the next course of action?","o":["Stop lithium immediately and start exogenous thyroid replacement","Stop lithium immediately and wait for normalisation of thyroid status","Continue lithium along with exogenous thyroid replacement","Decrease the dose of lithium and wait for normalisation of thyroid status"],"a":2,"e":"Hypothyroidism developed in a patient on chronic lithium therapy is treated by exogenous replacement of thyroid hormones.\n\nIt is not a reason for the discontinuation of lithium therapy.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 34-year-old woman presents with complaints of increased frequency of urination and fatigue. She gives a history of lithium intake for bipolar mood disorder for the past 6 years. On investigations, her serum osmolality was 300 mOsm/L and her urine osmolality was 250 mOsm/L. Which of the following drugs is used to treat this patient?","o":["Amiloride","Demeclocycline","Thiazide diuretics","Indomethacin"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario with a history of lithium intake and investigations showing ↑serum osmolality and ↓urine osmolality is suggestive of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus secondary to lithium toxicity. Amiloride is the drug of choice for this condition.\n\nAmiloride is a potassium-sparing diuretic. It inhibits the renal epithelial Na+ channel (ENac channel) in collecting duct cells. It blocks the entry of Li+ ions through the ENac channels and thus mitigates diabetes insipidus induced by lithium.\n\n Thiazide diuretics cause significant reductions in Li+ clearance that result in toxic levels. Thiazides are contraindicated in patients who are on lithium therapy.","t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old woman on lithium therapy now presents with a positive urine pregnancy test. Which of the following teratogenic effects can be expected in the fetus?","o":["Cardiac defects","Facial defects","CNS defects","Urogenital defects"],"a":0,"e":"Lithium use in pregnancy can lead to cardiac defects.\n\nEbstein's anomaly is the specific cardiac defect with lithium use in pregnancy.\n\nIn Ebstein’s anomaly, the radiological sign seen is a box-shaped heart.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4eda5b58e2f64337b1330f34da988cbax510x487.GIF"],"t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which drug, when given to a mother in her third trimester of pregnancy, would result in the following finding in her newborn?","o":["Venlafaxine","Diazepam","Promethazine","Clozapine"],"a":1,"e":"Benzodiazepines, when given during the third trimester of pregnancy can lead to floppy baby syndrome.\n\nBenzodiazepines in pregnancy:\n\n• First trimester - cleft palate, GI anomalies\n\n• Third trimester - floppy baby syndrome with neonatal hypotonia, as a withdrawal effect.\n\nNote: Fetal lithium exposure is also associated with floppy baby syndrome.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/989df78e2cda415ebb5241aa72ffb54f.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a50a926d8c414f2680622a248acb29dax1280x2559.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Manic Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs crosses the blood-brain barrier?","o":["Dopamine","Levodopa","Both A and B","Carbidopa"],"a":1,"e":"Levodopa crosses the blood-brain barrier.\n\nIt is a naturally occurring aromatic amino acid and an immediate precursor of dopamine that can cross the blood-brain barrier. Dopamine does not cross the blood-brain barrier because of the polar nature of the molecule. Levodopa is also a polar compound but it can cross the blood-brain barrier through an aromatic amino acid transporter.\n\nTherefore, levodopa is used therapeutically when dopamine is required for central action. Levodopa is converted to dopamine by the decarboxylase enzyme.\n\nCarbidopa is a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor and does not penetrate the blood-brain barrier.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old is diagnosed with Parkinson's disease and is started on levodopa therapy. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the drug?","o":["In parkinsonism, phenothiazines reduce its efficacy","It is a prodrug","Pyridoxine reduces effect of levodopa in parkinsonism","Domperidone reduces levodopa induced emesis and its therapeutic potential"],"a":3,"e":"Domperidone is an antidopaminergic antiemetic that can reverse levodopa-induced emesis without affecting the therapeutic potential as it cannot cross the blood-brain barrier.\n\nThe antiemetic effect is through its action on CTZ which is outside the blood-brain barrier.\n\nOption A: Typical antipsychotics like phenothiazines can reduce the efficacy of levodopa because of its central D2 blocking property.\n\nOption B: Levodopa is a prodrug. It is converted into dopamine by the decarboxylase enzyme in CNS.\n\nOption C: Pyridoxine can reduce the effect of levodopa by increasing its peripheral conversion to dopamine by potentiating peripheral dopa decarboxylase enzyme.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"After initiation of levodopa therapy, a 55-year-old patient starts experiencing adverse effects. Which of these side effects is not decreased by the addition of carbidopa?","o":["Nausea and vomiting","On and off effect","Cardiac arrhythmias","Hallucinations"],"a":3,"e":"Behavioral adverse effects like confusion, hallucinations, nightmares, delusions, postural hypotension are not reduced after the addition of carbidopa.\n\nIn fact, they are increased in patients who are taking levodopa with peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors. This is because higher levels of dopamine are formed in CNS.\n\nThe combination of levodopa with peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors like carbidopa inhibits peripheral adverse effects of dopamine. This decreases the adverse effects like:\n\n• Anorexia, nausea, and vomiting\n\n• Cardiac arrhythmias\n\n• On and off effect of motor performance","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old male is brought to the casualty in a confused state. On examination, he has severe muscle rigidity. His son says that the patient is on treatment for Parkinson's disease but has forgotten to take his medication as they went on a holiday trip. What is the likely diagnosis in this patient?","o":["Wearing off phenomenon","On / Off phenomenon","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Abrupt withdrawal of levodopa or dopaminergic agonists can precipitate neuroleptic malignant syndrome. It is characterized by extreme rigidity, confusion, and hyperthermia.\n\nIt is an emergency condition that can turn fatal if immediate intervention is not done. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is commonly associated with the use of typical antipsychotics.\n\nOption A: Wearing off phenomenon - Each dose of levodopa improves mobility for a period of 1-2 hours. After that, the effect of the drug wears off, and rigidity and akinesia return. This can be surpassed by increasing the dosing and frequency.\n\nOption B: On/off phenomenon - Rapid fluctuation between on and off periods.\n\n• On period: When the patient has a beneficial effect of the medications but with disabling dyskinesias\n\n• Off period: No beneficial effects from anti-Parkinsonism medications.\n\n'Wearing off' and 'On / Off' phenomenon occur with long-term treatment of levodopa.\n\nOption D: Serotonin syndrome occurs with co-prescription of MAO-inhibitors and SSRIs.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on long-term treatment with levodopa and carbidopa for Parkinson's disease is diagnosed with on/off phenomenon. Which of the following drugs is used to treat this condition?","o":["Safinamide","Amantadine","Benztropine","Domperidone"],"a":0,"e":"Safinamide is used for the treatment of the on/off phenomenon.\n\nManagement of On/Off phenomenon:\n\n• To prolong the 'On' phase:\n\n• COMT inhibitor - entacapone, tolcapone\n\n• Irreversible MAO-B inhibitor - selegiline, rasagiline\n\n• Reversible MAO-B inhibitor - safinamide.\n\n• Rescue from 'Off' phase: injection apomorphine\n\nDopaminergic agonists like pramipexole, ropinirole, and rotigotine are also effective in the treatment of the on/off phenomenon.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who is on levodopa therapy presents with confusion, delusions of grandeur, and hallucinations. What is the most appropriate treatment for his condition?","o":["Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Trifluperazine","Quetiapine"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of levodopa-induced psychosis for which quetiapine is the most appropriate treatment.\n\nQuetiapine and clozapine are the two atypical antipsychotics that are most effective and best tolerated in levodopa-induced psychosis.\n\nTypical antipsychotics are effective against levodopa-induced psychosis but may cause marked worsening of parkinsonism. Hence, atypical antipsychotics are deemed safer.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"While discharging a patient with Parkinson's disease, the treating physician writes a prescription of levodopa along with another drug that can increase the bioavailability of the former. Identify the drug.","o":["Entacapone","Ropinirole","Amantadine","Apomorphine"],"a":0,"e":"Entacapone is a peripheral COMT inhibitor that increases the bioavailability of levodopa.\n\nIt inhibits the peripheral metabolism of levodopa thereby increasing the bioavailability of levodopa.\n\nOptions B and D: Ropinirole and apomorphine are dopamine receptor agonists and do not have any significant effect on levodopa bioavailability.\n\nOption C: Amantadine which is an antiviral drug is also anti-Parkinsonian due to its varied actions including increased release and decreased uptake of dopamine. It has no significant effect on the bioavailability of levodopa.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following anti-Parkinsonism drugs is correctly matched with its mechanism of action?","o":["Tolcapone - COMT inhibition","Rasagiline - Muscarinic antagonism","Amantidine - MAO-B inhibition","Apomorphine - Inhibition of dopa"],"a":0,"e":"Tolcapone and entacapone are selective, potent, and reversible COMT (catechol-O-methyl transferase) inhibitors.\n\nCOMT metabolizes levodopa and blockade of this pathway prolongs the half-life of levodopa and allows a larger fraction of the administered dose to reach the brain.\n\nOption B: Rasagiline is a selective MAO-B inhibitor.\n\nOption C: Amantadine acts by antagonism of the NMDA type of glutamate receptors.\n\nOption D: Apomorphine is a dopaminergic agonist that is FDA-approved as a 'rescue therapy' for the acute intermittent treatment of 'off' episodes.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the use of selegiline in Parkinsonism?","o":["It acts as a selective MAO A inhibitor","Incidence of cheese reaction is minimal","May be used for on / off phenomenon","It forms amphetamines as metabolites"],"a":0,"e":"Selegiline acts as a selective MAO-B inhibitor.\n\nAt higher doses, MAO-A can also be inhibited. Higher doses are used for depression and administered in the form of the transdermal patch.\n\nOption B: Selective MAO-B inhibitors do not interfere significantly with the peripheral metabolism of dietary amines and do not cause cheese reaction.\n\nOption C: It has lower efficacy than levodopa but when added with levodopa it prolongs its action and also decreases the wearing-off effect of levodopa.\n\nOption D: Selegiline forms metabolites like amphetamine and methamphetamine that can result in anxiety, insomnia, and other undesirable effects. Rasagiline, unlike selegiline, does not form amphetamines as metabolites.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following dopamine agonists is an ergot derivative?\n\n1. Bromocriptine\n\n2. Pergolide\n\n3. Ropinirole\n\n4. Rotigotine\n\n5. Pramipexole","o":["3, 4 and 5","1, 2 and 3","1 and 2","4 and 5"],"a":2,"e":"Bromocriptine and pergolide are ergot derivatives that act as potent agonists at the D2 receptor.\n\nPramipexole and ropinirole are non-ergot derivatives having D2/D3 receptor agonistic action. They are better tolerated and have fewer GI symptoms than bromocriptine.\n\nDopamine agonists can be classified as follows:\n\n• Ergot derivatives - Bromocriptine, pergolide, cabergoline\n\n• Non-ergot derivatives - Pramipexole, ropinirole, apomorphine, rotigotine\n\nDopaminergic agonists exert subtype-selective activation of dopaminergic receptors involved in the Parkinsonism circuit of the striatum.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old female patient visits the OPD with complaints of burning sensation associated with an irresistible urge to move her legs since 2 weeks. It occurs especially at night and the discomfort gets relieved on moving her legs. Which is the first-line drug that can be prescribed for this patient?","o":["Benztropine","Gabapentin","Riluzole","Clonazepam"],"a":1,"e":"The symptoms in this patient are suggestive of restless leg syndrome and gabapentin is the first-line choice of treatment for this condition.\n\nRestless leg syndrome is a typical sensory-motor disorder that generally affects the legs during rest periods, especially sleep. There is an irresistible urge to shake the legs associated with burning sensation, itching, and cramps of the legs. In idiopathic cases, dopaminergic hypofunction is postulated.\n\nIn patients with restless legs syndrome, iron stores are initially repleted, and the aggravating factors are eliminated. If RLS still persists, initial therapy with a gabapentinoid (pregabalin, gabapentin, gabapentin enacarbil) are preferred, rather than a dopamine agonist (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine). This is due to the high frequency of intermediate and long-term augmentation with dopamine agonists, as well as their risk of impulse control disorders.\n\nFDA-approved drugs for the treatment of restless leg syndrome:\n\n• Gabapentin\n\n• Ropinirole\n\n• Pramipexole\n\n• Rotigotine\n\nA dopamine agonist is used as an alternative in patients with increased risk for side effects from gabapentinoids, such as those with obesity and its complications, a past or present history of moderate to severe depression, a disorder causing gait instability or respiratory failure, and a history of substance use disorder.\n\nOption A: Benztropine is an anticholinergic that can be used for drug-induced parkinsonism.\n\nOption C: Riluzole is a benzothiazole derivative that is used in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.\n\nOption D: Benzodiazepines like clonazepam can be used as co-adjuvant drugs in the treatment of restless leg syndrome but should not be used as first-line therapy.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following dopamine receptor agonists has antioxidant properties?","o":["Bromocriptine","Pergolide","Pramipexole","Ropinirole"],"a":2,"e":"Pramipexole is a dopamine receptor agonist that has an additional antioxidant effect.\n\nPramipexole has a possible neuroprotective effect by its ability to scavenge hydrogen peroxide and enhance neurotrophic activity.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs would you prescribe as a transdermal patch to patients with Parkinson’s disease?","o":["Selegiline","Rotigotine","Entacapone","Apomorphine"],"a":1,"e":"Rotigotine is a dopamine agonist used in Parkinson’s disease as a transdermal patch.\n\nA transdermal patch can lead to various local reactions at the application site. Rotigotine was withdrawn from some markets due to crystal formation on the patch but later reintroduced with a newer formulation.\n\nSelegiline transdermal patch is approved for depression and not for Parkinson’s disease.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old female is on treatment with ropinirole for restless leg syndrome. Which of the following adverse effects are not expected to be seen in this patient?","o":["Drowsiness","Postural hypotension","Cardiac fibrosis","Hallucination"],"a":2,"e":"Cardiac fibrosis is a side effect of pergolide, not ropinirole.\n\nThe adverse effects of ropinirole include:\n\n• Nausea\n\n• Drowsiness\n\n• Postural hypotension\n\n• Hallucinations\n\nCardiac and pleural fibrosis are seen with pergolide which is also a dopamine agonist. Pergolide was withdrawn from the market due to reports of serious valve damage.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which anti-Parkinsonism drug is an NMDA-glutamate receptor antagonist?","o":["Amantadine","Rotigotine","Benzhexol","Trihexyphenidyl"],"a":0,"e":"Amantadine which is an antiviral agent, also used in parkinsonism has NMDA glutamate receptor antagonist action.\n\nThis action is responsible for its antidyskinetic properties. It also promotes presynaptic synthesis and release of dopamine.\n\nOption B: Rotigotine is a dopamine agonist available as a transdermal patch.\n\nOptions C and D: Benzhexol and trihexyphenidyl are central anticholinergics.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old patient presents to the dermatology OPD with findings as shown in the image below. Which of the following drugs can result in this adverse effect?","o":["Pergolide","Memantine","Amantadine","Trihexyphenidyl"],"a":2,"e":"The above presentation is of livedo reticularis, which is an adverse effect of amantadine.\n\nThe patient presents with a common skin finding consisting of a mottled vesicular pattern that appears as a lace-like purplish discoloration of the skin which is due to the local release of catecholamines resulting in post-capillary vasoconstriction.\n\nAdverse effects of amantadine are:\n\n• Insomnia, restlessness, confusion\n\n• Nightmares\n\n• Anticholinergic effects\n\n• Hallucinations\n\n• Livedo reticularis\n\n• Edema of ankles","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4889023d4b04e79ad939c5bed8f8810.JPEG"],"t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which is the 5HT2A inverse agonist approved for use in psychosis associated with Parkinsonism?","o":["Lorcaserin","Pimavanserin","Pitolisant","Mianserin"],"a":1,"e":"Pimavanserin is a selective 5-HT2A receptor inverse agonist that is approved for the management of delirium and hallucinations associated with Parkinson's disease.\n\nOption A: Lorcaserin is a 5-HT2C agonist indicated as an anti-obesity agent.\n\nOption C: Pitolisant is an H3 receptor inverse agonist approved for the treatment of narcolepsy.\n\nOption D: Mianserin is an anti-depressant.","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old male patient presented to the casualty department with bradykinesia, rigidity, and tremors. His attender reported that he had been diagnosed with schizophrenia and had recently started treatment. What is the drug of choice to treat the patient's presenting symptoms?","o":["Levodopa","Benzhexol","Amantadine","Selegiline"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of drug-induced parkinsonism, for which the drug of choice is benzhexol, also known as trihexiphenidyl.\n\nDrug-induced parkinsonism is caused by certain drugs that have D2 antagonistic action, such as typical antipsychotics. Due to the decrease in dopaminergic activity, they result in an unbalanced cholinergic activity. Benzhexol is a centrally acting antimuscarinic agent. It acts by restoring the cholinergic balance in the striatal interneurons.\n\nThe following drugs are implicated in drug-induced parkinsonism:\n\nDrug class\n\nExamples\n\nTypical antipsychotics\n\nPhenothiazines, haloperidol\n\nAtypical antipsychotics\nRisperidone, olanzapine\n\nDopamine depleters\nReserpine, tetrabenazine\n\nAntiemetics\nMetoclopramide, levosulpiride\n\nCalcium channel blockers\nFlunarizine, cinnarizine","t":"Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of antidepressants?","o":["Change in neurotransmitter receptor sensitivity","Decrease in level of neurotransmitters","Change in efficacy of neurotransmitters","Increase the reuptake of neurotransmitters"],"a":0,"e":"Antidepressants increase levels of neurotransmitters at the synapse by preventing their reuptake. Due to a prolonged increase in the levels of neurotransmitters, there is a change in neurotransmitter receptor sensitivity which is thought to be the major mechanism responsible for the treatment of depression.\n\nChronic treatment with a number of antidepressants desensitizes presynaptic autoreceptors and heteroreceptors, producing long-lasting changes in monoaminergic neurotransmission. Postreceptor effects of antidepressant treatment, including modulation of GPCR signaling and activation of protein kinases and ion channels, are involved in the mediation of the long-term effects of antidepressant drugs.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Moclobemide belongs to which class of drugs?","o":["SSRI","SNRI","MAO inhibitor","Tricyclic antidepressant"],"a":2,"e":"Moclobemide is a reversible and selective inhibitor of the MAO-A enzyme.\n\nMonoamine hypothesis states that in depression, there is a decrease in monoamines like serotonin (5HT) and norepinephrine. These monoamines are metabolized by the enzyme monoamine oxidase (MAO). MAO inhibitors, therefore, increase these monoamines by inhibiting the enzyme.\n\nThe various classes of drugs used in depression and anxiety are listed below:\n\nClass of drugs\nExamples\n\nSelective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRI)\n\n• Fluoxetine\n\n• Fluvoxamine\n\n• Paroxetine\n\n• Sertraline\n\n• Citalopram\n\nSerotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors\n\n• Venlafaxine\n\n• Desvenlafaxine\n\n• Duloxetine\n\n• Milnacipran\n\nTricyclic antidepressants\n\n• Amitriptyline\n\n• Imipramine\n\n• Nortriptyline\n\n• Desipramine\n\n• Amoxapine\n\nAtypical antipsychotics\n\n• Aripiprazole\n\n• Olanzapine\n\n• Quetiapine\n\n• Risperidone\n\nMonoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAO)\n\n• Isocarboxazid\n\n• Selegiline\n\n• Phenelzine\n\n• Moclobemide\n\nAtypical antidepressants\n\n• Bupropion\n\n• Trazodone\n\n• Mirtazapine\n\n• Mianserin","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a selective MAO-B inhibitor?","o":["Moclobemide","Isocarboxazid","Phenelzine","Selegiline"],"a":3,"e":"Selegiline is a selective MAO-B inhibitor.\n\nNonselective MAO inhibitors\n\n Selective MAO inhibitors\n\n• Phenelzine\n\n• Isocarboxazid\n\n• Tranylcypromine\n\n• MAO-A inhibitors - Moclobemide\n\n• MAO-B inhibitors - Selegiline, Rasagiline","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is transdermal selegiline approved for use?","o":["30-year-old man with obsessive compulsive disorder","64-year-old man with Parkinson's disease","29-year-old woman with depression","33-year-old woman with generalized anxiety disorder"],"a":2,"e":"Selegiline transdermal patch is approved for depression. The transdermal patch provides the high concentration required for antidepressant action. At higher doses, MAO-A is also inhibited and is highly effective in depression.\n\nSelegiline is a selective MAO-B inhibitor at low doses. It is used in Parkinson's disease at low doses, administered orally.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A college student on MAO inhibitor therapy for depression consumed two bottles of beer during a party. The next day he started experiencing severe headache, nausea, and vomiting. He was taken to the casualty by his friends where his BP was recorded to be 180/110mmHg. Which of the following compounds would be responsible for this complication?","o":["Tyramine","Tyrptophan","Threonine","Thiamine"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical presentation of a hypertensive crisis in a patient on MAO inhibitor therapy is suggestive of cheese reaction, which is seen after consumption of foods rich in tyramine (beer).\n\nTyramine is an indirect sympathomimetic. It induces the release of catecholamines from the neurons. Tyramine can be produced in high concentrations in protein-rich foods by decarboxylation of tyrosine during fermentation.\n\nNormally tyramine is inactivated when taken orally because it is readily metabolized by MAO in the liver. In patients who are on MAO inhibitor therapy, the tyramine taken in the diet is not inactivated and results in its increased sympathomimetic effect resulting in elevated blood pressure, termed as hypertensive crisis or cheese reaction. Drug of choice for cheese reaction is IV phentolamine.\n\nWhen MAO inhibitors are taken along with SSRI or TCA, it results in serotonin syndrome due to the overstimulation of 5HT receptors. The clinical features are a triad of:\n\n1. Cognitive effect - delirium, coma\n\n2. Autonomic effects- hypertension, tachycardia\n\n3. Somatic effects- myoclonus, hyperreflexia, tremors","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e161466fb10d429f81c37cb0bbc1f784x1280x1437.JPEG"],"t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to the medicine OPD with hypertension. He is already on imipramine for depression. Which of the following antihypertensive drugs will not be effective in him?","o":["Enalapril","Atenolol","Diltiazem","Clonidine"],"a":3,"e":"Clonidine will not be effective in this patient.\n\nImipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant would abolish the antihypertensive effect of clonidine, by inhibiting its transport into the adrenergic neurons.\n\nSide effects of tricyclic antidepressants:\n\nReceptor blocked\nSide effects\n\nMuscarinic receptor\nAnticholinergic - Dry mouth, dilated pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention\n\nAlpha 1 adrenergic receptor\nDrowsiness, postural hypotension, sexual dysfunction\n\nH1 receptor\nWeight gain, drowsiness","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which tricyclic antidepressant has an additional dopaminergic receptor antagonist action?","o":["Clomipramine","Doxepin","Imipramine","Amoxapine"],"a":3,"e":"The tricyclic antidepressant having an additional dopaminergic receptor antagonist action is amoxapine.\n\nTricyclic antidepressants block the transporter of norepinephrine (NET) and serotonin (SERT) thereby preventing their uptake. Some of the TCA additionally block other receptors like alpha 1, H1, and M1.\n\n• TCA blocking NET selectively - desipramine, nortriptyline, amoxapine.\n\n• TCA blocking both NET and SERT equally - imipramine, amitriptyline.\n\nClomipramine, doxepin, and imipramine are tertiary amines and do not possess any dopaminergic antagonist effect.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antidepressants can be used for the treatment of a 6-year-old child diagnosed with nocturnal enuresis?","o":["Imipramine","Amitriptyline","Desipramine","Clomipramine"],"a":0,"e":"Imipramine is a tricyclic antidepressant that can be used for the treatment of nocturnal enuresis.\n\nIn children above 5 years, imipramine 25 mg at bedtime is effective for nocturnal enuresis.\n\nIntranasal desmopressin is also effective in the treatment of nocturnal enuresis.\n\nOption B: Amitriptyline is used in postherpetic neuralgia. It is also used in neuropathic pain.\n\nOption C: Desipramine is used in the treatment of cocaine dependence.\n\nOption D: Clomipramine is the best TCA for OCD.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following side effects of tricyclic antidepressants are correctly matched to the receptor involved in their pathogenesis?\n\n1. H1 receptor - sedation\n\n2. Muscarinic receptor - sialorrhea\n\n3. 5HT receptor- weight loss\n\n4. Alpha1 receptor - postural hypotension\n\n5. Muscarinic receptor - constipation","o":["1, 4 and 5","1, 2 and 3","3, 4 and 5","4 and 5 only"],"a":0,"e":"The blockade of H1, alpha1, and muscarinic receptors causes sedation, postural hypotension, and constipation respectively.\n\nTricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) are drugs that act by inhibiting the reuptake of noradrenaline and serotonin from the synaptic clefts.\n\nThe side effects of TCAs are due to blockade of other receptors:\n\n• Muscarinic (M) receptors: constipation, dry mouth (not sialorrhea), arrhythmia\n\n• Histamine (H1) receptors: sedation\n\n• 5HT receptors: weight gain due to increased appetite\n\n• Alpha1 receptors: postural hypotension","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman having depression took multiple tablets of amitriptyline in an attempt to commit suicide. Which of the following statements regarding the management is incorrect?","o":["Physostigmine is not recommended","Sodium bicarbonate should be administered to treat acidosis","Atropine sulphate should be administered as an antidote","Diazepam should be injected to control seizures"],"a":2,"e":"Tricyclic antidepressants like amitriptyline are competitive antagonists of muscarinic receptors. Atropine being an antimuscarinic drug should not be administered.\n\nTricyclic antidepressant overdose presents with anticholinergic effects. Hypotension occurs because of alpha1 receptor blockade.\n\nTCAs act on Na+ channels and cause slowed conduction and depressed cardiac contractility resulting in serious arrhythmias. Seizures and agitation may occur followed by depression.\n\nManagement of TCA overdose:\n\n• Initial resuscitation by securing airway, breathing, and circulation.\n\n• Hypotension - IV fluids and norepinephrine infusion.\n\n• Cardiac-depressant effects - bolus dose of sodium bicarbonate helps overcome Na+ blockade by increasing extracellular Na+. It also addresses metabolic acidosis which is often present.\n\n• Arrhythmias - lignocaine for ventricular arrhythmias.\n\n• Seizures - benzodiazepines like diazepam/lorazepam.\n\nPhysostigmine can reverse anticholinergic effects but can aggravate cardiac depression and cause seizures. Therefore physostigmine is not recommended.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A psychiatrist plans to prescribe an SSRI to a patient diagnosed with generalized anxiety disorder. Which of the following was not likely prescribed?","o":["Fluoxetine","Citalopram","Paroxetine","Duloxetine"],"a":3,"e":"Duloxetine is not an SSRI but a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI). It blocks the uptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine.\n\nThe selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are:\n\n• Fluoxetine\n\n• Fluvoxamine\n\n• Citalopram\n\n• Escitalopram\n\n• Sertraline\n\n• Dapoxetine\n\n• Paroxetine","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"In a middle-aged man with a history of closed-angle glaucoma, which of the following SNRIs should be avoided?","o":["Venlafaxine","Duloxetine","Desvenlafaxine","Fluoxetine"],"a":1,"e":"Duloxetine should be avoided as it is contraindicated in patients with closed-angle glaucoma. SNRIs like duloxetine and milnacipran have anticholinergic side effects like mydriasis which can precipitate closed-angle glaucoma attacks.\n\nDuloxetine is the longest-acting SNRI with no withdrawal symptoms. In addition to the treatment of depression and anxiety, it is also used in fibromyalgia, peripheral neuropathy, and stress incontinence.\n\nOption A: Venlafaxine is the shortest acting SNRI with the maximum risk of withdrawal symptoms. It is approved for use in PTSD, panic disorder, and depression associated with hypotension. It is contraindicated in pregnancy.\n\nOption C: Desvenlafaxine is a longer-acting SNRI and hence has lesser risk of withdrawal symptoms.\n\nOption D: Fluoxetine is an SSRI, not an SNRI. It is avoided as it can cause mydriasis and narrow the angle.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old man on antidepressant therapy with TCAs for the past five years is being switched to SSRIs. Which of the following would not be an advantage of this change?","o":["Minimal lowering of seizure threshold","Minimal anticholinergic side effects","Minimal sexual dysfunction","Minimal sedation"],"a":2,"e":"SSRIs have a higher incidence of sexual dysfunction when compared to TCAs.\n\nSSRI-induced sexual dysfunction can result in loss of libido, delayed ejaculation, and interference with orgasm.\n\nOption A: The potential of SSRIs to lower the seizure threshold is less than that of TCAs.\n\nOptions B and D: SSRIs have a selective action on serotonin reuptake with no effect on cholinergic or histaminergic receptors.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors are not the drug of choice in which of the following patients?","o":["A college student with an acute panic attack","An adolescent girl with social phobia","A young lady with post traumatic stress disorder","A middle-aged-man with generalized anxiety disorder"],"a":0,"e":"In acute panic attacks, benzodiazepines or propranolol are the preferred anxiolytics. SSRIs are not the drug of choice as they have a long latent period before beneficial effects are seen.\n\nSSRIs are considered as the drug of choice for:\n\n• Social anxiety disorder\n\n• Post-traumatic stress disorder\n\n• Generalized anxiety disorder\n\n• Obsessive-compulsive disorder","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with depression has improved symptomatically after initiating SSRIs. On discontinuing the drug, he became irritable and complained of insomnia, nausea, and flu-like symptoms. Which of the following drugs has the least risk of causing these symptoms?","o":["Paroxetine","Fluoxetine","Sertraline","Fluvoxamine"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical presentation is suggestive of drug discontinuation or withdrawal symptoms which are seen after sudden discontinuation of SSRIs. Fluoxetine has the least risk of causing drug-discontinuation symptoms.\n\nDrug discontinuation symptoms of SSRIs are:\n\n• Nausea\n\n• Paresthesia\n\n• Flu-like symptoms\n\n• Insomnia\n\n• Irritability\n\n• Fatigue.\n\nWithdrawal is least with fluoxetine as its metabolite norfluoxetine has the longest half-life. Thus the plasma level of norfluoxetine is maintained for a long time even after stopping the drug, minimizing withdrawal symptoms. Withdrawal is seen maximum with paroxetine.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young man with obsessive-compulsive disorder is taking SSRIs. Which of the following adverse effects would you not expect in him?","o":["Diarrhea","Nausea","Weight loss","Loss of libido"],"a":2,"e":"Weight loss is not an adverse effect of SSRIs.\n\nSide effects of SSRIs:\n\n• Nausea and diarrhea (early)\n\n• Loss of libido\n\n• Erectile dysfunction\n\n• Headache\n\n• Sleep disturbances - insomnia or hypersomnia.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is a serotonin receptor antagonist?","o":["Milnacipran","Trazodone","Citalopram","Fluoxetine"],"a":1,"e":"Trazodone is a serotonin receptor antagonist.\n\nTrazodone blocks postsynaptic 5HT2A receptors and is considered an atypical antidepressant. It has weak serotonin reuptake inhibitory effect.\n\nNefazodone is another serotonin receptor antagonist.\n\nOption A: Milnacipran is a serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor.\n\nOptions C and D: Citalopram and fluoxetine are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs inhibit dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake?","o":["Clozapine","Bupropion","Zolpidem","Mirtazapine"],"a":1,"e":"Bupropion is an atypical antidepressant that inhibits the reuptake of noradrenaline and dopamine.\n\nIndications of bupropion are:\n\n• Prevention of a seasonal depressive disorder\n\n• Smoking cessation\n\n• ADHD\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy\n\nOption A : Clozapine is an atypical antipsychotic that selectively blocks dopamine (D4) receptors in the brain.\n\nOption C : Zolpidem - It is a nonbenzodiazepine hypnotic. It acts as a selective agonist in benzodiazepine receptor.\n\nOption D : Mirtazapine - block histamine (H1) receptors.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young woman diagnosed with major depressive disorder is refractory to first-line antidepressant therapy. The psychiatrist now decides to prescribe a recently approved NMDA antagonist. Which of the following was most likely prescribed?","o":["Brexanolone","Esketamine","Lurasidone","Lemborexant"],"a":1,"e":"Esketamine is an NMDA antagonist recently approved for the treatment of treatment-resistant depression.\n\nEsketamine is a non-selective, non-competitive antagonist of the NMDA receptor, an ionotropic glutamate receptor. Though the exact mechanism by which it acts as an antidepressant is unknown, a single dose of intranasal Esketamine has shown to have short-term antidepressant efficacy suggesting a possible use in suicidality.\n\nOption A: Brexanolone has GABAA modulatory action. It is given via intravenous route for post-partum depression.\n\nOption C: Lurasidone is a second-generation antipsychotic that is an antagonist at dopamine (D2) and serotonin (5-HT2A) receptors. It is approved for use in depressive episodes in bipolar disorder.\n\nOption D: Lemborexant is an orexin receptor antagonist indicated for the treatment of adult patients with insomnia.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old married man complains of low energy and lack of motivation. He mentions that he feels useless and has no hope for the future. He is willing to take medication but is apprehensive about its side effects on his sexual life. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to him?","o":["Fluoxetine","Venlafaxine","Mirtazapine","Imipramine"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical presentation is suggestive of depression. Mirtazapine can be prescribed to this patient as sexual dysfunction is not a side effect associated with its use.\n\nMirtazapine and mianserin block histamine (H1) receptors, and also have high antagonistic activity against 5HT2A, 5HT2C, and 5HT3 receptors.\n\nDue to its sedative effect, mirtazapine is also used in depression with insomnia.\n\nOption A: Fluoxetine is an SSRI that can cause side effects like GI disturbances, risk of suicidal behavior, sexual dysfunction, and weight gain. Vilazodone is an SSRI that is not associated with sexual dysfunction or weight gain.\n\nOption B: SNRIs like venlafaxine have side effects that include nausea, dizziness, sexual dysfunction, and risk of suicidal thoughts or behavior.\n\nOption D: TCAs like imipramine have side effects like orthostatic hypotension, weight gain, GI disturbances, sexual dysfunction, seizures, and irregular heartbeats.\n\nThe various classes of drugs used in depression and anxiety are listed below\n\nClass of drugs\nExamples\n\nSelective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRI)\n\n• Fluoxetine\n\n• Fluvoxamine\n\n• Paroxetine\n\n• Sertraline\n\n• Citalopram\n\nSerotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors\n\n• Venlafaxine\n\n• Desvenlafaxine\n\n• Duloxetine\n\n• Milnacipran\n\nTricyclic antidepressants\n\n• Amitriptyline\n\n• Imipramine\n\n• Nortriptyline\n\n• Desipramine\n\n• Amoxapine\n\nAtypical antipsychotics\n\n• Aripiprazole\n\n• Olanzapine\n\n• Quetiapine\n\n• Risperidone\n\nMonoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAO)\n\n• Isocarboxazid\n\n• Selegiline\n\n• Phenelzine\n\nAtypical antidepressants\n\n• Bupropion\n\n• Trazodone\n\n• Mirtazapine\n\n• Mianserin","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which group does the antianxiety drug buspirone belong to?","o":["Partial antagonist of 5-HT 1A","Partial agonist of 5-HT 1A","Partial antagonist of 5-HT 2A","Partial agonist of 5-HT 2A"],"a":1,"e":"The antianxiety drug buspirone is a partial agonist of 5-HT1A receptors. The most important locations of the 5-HT1A receptor are raphe nuclei of brainstem and hippocampus.\n\nIt mimics the antianxiety properties of benzodiazepines but does not interact with GABAA receptors or display the sedative and anticonvulsant properties of benzodiazepines. Buspirone is primarily used in the treatment of generalized anxiety disorder.\n\n5-HT1A partial agonists are:\n\n• Buspirone\n\n• Gepirone\n\n• Ipsapirone","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A psychiatry resident should not prescribe a tricyclic antidepressant to a patient having which of the following co-morbidities?","o":["Essential hypertension","Asthma","Narrow angle glaucoma","Increased intracranial tension"],"a":2,"e":"Tricyclic antidepressants produce anticholinergic effects of pupillary dilatation and are thus, contraindicated in narrow-angle glaucoma.\n\nSide effects of tricyclic antidepressants:\n\nReceptor blocked\nSide effect\n\nMuscarinic receptor\nAnticholinergic- dry mouth, dilated pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention\n\nAlpha 1 adrenergic receptor\nDrowsiness, postural hypotension, sexual dysfunction\n\nH1 receptor\nWeight gain, drowsiness","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old man was admitted to the emergency ward with priapism lasting for about 7 hours. A detailed history revealed the initiation of a drug for a mood disorder by a psychiatrist 3 weeks ago. Which of the following drugs is responsible for his condition?","o":["Trazodone","Venlafaxine","Bupropion","Mirtazapine"],"a":0,"e":"Trazadone causes priapism. Intracavernous injection of phenylephrine can be used to treat priapism.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A man feels frustrated because of his wife's decreased sexual desire. She was recently diagnosed with depression and started on a drug for the same. Which of the following drugs was most likely prescribed?","o":["Fluoxetine","Mirtazapine","Bupropion","Trazodone"],"a":0,"e":"Fluoxetine is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) that is associated with the side effect of sexual dysfunction.\n\nFluoxetine is used in the treatment of major depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder, post-traumatic stress disorder, bulimia and anorexia nervosa, generalized anxiety disorders, and premenstrual dysphoric disorder.\n\nOther adverse effects include dry mouth, urinary retention, insomnia, GI disturbances, risk of suicidal behavior, and weight gain.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an adverse effect of escitalopram?","o":["Nausea","Vivid dreams","Anorgasmia","Sialorrhoea"],"a":3,"e":"Sialorrhoea is not an adverse effect of escitalopram.\n\nEscitalopram is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) mainly used to treat major depressive disorder or generalized anxiety disorder.\n\nNausea is the commonest side effect of SSRIs as they target the 5HT3 receptor.\n\nVivid dreams may occur because the drug suppresses REM sleep (the stage of dream), hence when patients eventually fall into REM sleep it may be more intense.\n\nAnorgasmia is the persistent inability to achieve orgasm despite responding to sexual stimulation. A proposed mechanism of escitalopram causing sexual dysfunction involves stimulation of the 5HT2C receptors.","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"In children and adolescents treated with an SSRI, there appears to be an increased risk of particular ideations. Clinicians are therefore advised to remain vigilant of which of the following?","o":["Nihilistic ideations","Schizotypal ideations","Suicidal ideations","Homicidal ideations"],"a":2,"e":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) should be used carefully in the young for the management of depression due to increased suicidal ideation. In children and adolescents, there appears to be a slightly increased risk of suicidal ideations and attempts, but not of completed suicide.\n\nSomatic symptoms of depression improve faster than psychological symptoms when a patient is started on SSRIs and hence patients have the energy to act on their thoughts of deliberate self-harm and suicide (paradoxical suicide).","t":"Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman with resistant intractable back pain is planned to receive neuraxial opioids for analgesia. Which of the following is the site of action of this drug in the spinal cord?","o":["Ventral horn of spinal cord","Dorsal horn of spinal cord","Lateral horn of spinal cord","No spinal component is seen with opioid analgesia"],"a":1,"e":"The spinal component of opioid analgesia is seen due to the action in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord.\n\nOpioid analgesia:\n\n• Spinal component: opioids act in the substantia gelatinosa of the dorsal horn to inhibit the release of excitatory transmitters like substance P from primary afferents carrying pain impulses. This action is through interneurons that are involved in the gating of pain impulses.\n\n• Supraspinal component: action at the medulla, periaqueductal gray matter, limbic, and cortical areas alter the processing and interpretation of pain impulses. It also sends inhibitory impulses through descending pathways to the spinal cord.\n\n• Peripheral action: acts on small primary afferent terminals in the skin or deeper structures, decreasing their sensitization following tissue injury.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A primigravida in labor is administered opioids for epidural analgesia. Which of the following statements is false regarding the drug administered?","o":["Act on dorsal horn substantia gelatinosa","Can cause itching","Function of the GIT is not affected","Can cause respiratory depression"],"a":2,"e":"Gastrointestinal motility is decreased by epidural opioids.\n\nOption A: The epidural opioids provide more direct access to the first pain-processing synapse i.e., substantia gelatinosa by acting on the dorsal horn of the spinal cord.\n\nOption B: Symptoms such as pruritus, nausea, and vomiting are also common with the epidural route.\n\nOption D: Respiratory depression is seen with epidural opioids but the incidence is lesser than the parenteral route.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is receiving morphine for pain management. Which of the following findings would you not expect?","o":["Urinary retention","Vomiting","Pruritus","Mydriasis"],"a":3,"e":"Miosis (not mydriasis) would be expected in a patient receiving morphine.\n\nOpioids stimulate the Edinger-Westphal nucleus of the III cranial nerve resulting in miosis.\n\nUrinary retention, vomiting, and pruritus are actions of opiates.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An experimental drug that acts only as an agonist at the mu-opioid receptor is being developed. Which of the following effects would be seen with this drug?\n\n1. Miosis\n\n2. Tachycardia\n\n3. Dysphoria\n\n4. Nausea and vomiting\n\n5. Diarrhea","o":["1 &amp; 4 only","1, &amp; 3 only","1, 2 &amp; 3 only","2 &amp; 5 only"],"a":0,"e":"Mu opioid receptor activation results in miosis, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nMu receptor actions (mnemonic- MUSCARINE):\n\n• Miosis\n\n• Urine retention\n\n• Sedation\n\n• Constipation\n\n• Analgesia\n\n• Respiratory depression\n\n• Increased muscular rigidity\n\n• Negative bile flow (contraction of the sphincter of Oddi)\n\n• Euphoria","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Dysphoria is mediated by which opioid receptor?","o":["Mu","Kappa","Delta","Zeta"],"a":1,"e":"Dysphoria is mediated by kappa opioid receptor.\n\nKappa receptor actions:\n\n• Dysphoria, psychotomimetic\n\n• Analgesia (Spinal and supraspinal)\n\n• Miosis\n\n• Sedation\n\n• Physical dependence\n\n• Reduced GI motility","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is administered intravenous opioids for postoperative pain management. This drug elicits its analgesic action through which of the following receptors?\n\n1. µ receptor\n\n2. k receptor\n\n3. δ receptor\n\n4. G protein-coupled receptor\n\n5. Nicotinic receptor","o":["1, 2 &amp; 3","1 &amp; 3 only","1, 2, 4 &amp; 5","1, 2, 3 &amp; 4"],"a":3,"e":"Analgesia produced by opioids is mediated by µ (mu), k (kappa), and δ (delta) receptors. Opioid receptors are part of the G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) family.\n\nSupraspinal analgesia is predominantly mediated by the µ1 receptor and spinal analgesia by the k and δ receptor.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Following surgery, a woman received multiple doses of intravenous opioids for pain management. Later, her respiratory rate became 8 per minute. Which receptor mediates this adverse effect?","o":["µ","k","δ","Both µ &amp; k"],"a":0,"e":"The given features suggest opioid-induced respiratory depression, which is mediated by the action of opioids on µ receptors (µ2) in the brainstem respiratory centers.\n\nDrug of choice for opioid-induced respiratory depression is naloxone.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs does not aggravate opioid-induced respiratory depression?","o":["Alcohol","Barbiturate","Diazepam","Naloxone"],"a":3,"e":"Naloxone is an opioid antagonist, which can reverse the respiratory depression induced by opioids.\n\nRespiratory depression can be prolonged when opioids are taken with CNS depressant substances like alcohol, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, inhalational anesthetics, and other sedative hypnotics.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an opiate?","o":["Pentazocine","Heroin","Fentanyl","Morphine"],"a":3,"e":"Morphine is an opiate.\n\nThe term opiate specifically describes the naturally occurring alkaloids: morphine, codeine, thebaine, and papaverine.\n\nOptions A and C: Pentazocine and fentanyl are synthetic opioids.\n\nOption B: Heroin is a semisynthetic opioid.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the other name for diacetylmorphine?","o":["Codeine","Heroin","Methadone","Meperidine"],"a":1,"e":"Heroin is structurally diacetylmorphine.\n\nIt is more potent than morphine and causes more euphoric effects. It is banned in some countries.\n\nDiacetylmorphine is converted into monoacetylmorphine and morphine, to which the pharmacological actions of heroin are mainly attributed. Monoacetylmorphine is more lipid-soluble than morphine.\n\nCodeine is also known as methyl morphine.\n\nMeperidine is also known as pethidine.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who has a history of substance abuse with a semisynthetic opioid is now referred to a rehabilitation center. Which of the following drugs is he likely addicted to?","o":["Morphine","Heroin","Codeine","Thebaine"],"a":1,"e":"He is likely addicted to heroin as it is the only semisynthetic opioid among the given options.\n\nMorphine, codeine, and thebaine are natural opioids derived from poppy Papaver somniferum.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old pregnant woman is in labor. Your senior administered her a synthetic opioid to provide labor analgesia. Which of the following would be the drug administered to her?","o":["Heroin","Dihydromorphone","Butorphanol","Buprenorphine"],"a":2,"e":"Butorphanol would be the drug administered to her as it is the only synthetic opioid among the given options.\n\nHeroin, dihydromorphone, and buprenorphine are semisynthetic opioids.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old woman is receiving acupuncture treatment for her back pain. Which of the following opioid peptides does not mediate analgesia in her?","o":["Endorphin","Nociceptin","Enkephalin","Morphine"],"a":3,"e":"Morphine does not mediate analgesia in acupuncture as it is not an endogenous opioid. It is a naturally occurring opioid derived from poppy Papaver somniferum.\n\nOne of the most accepted mechanisms of action of acupuncture is that it works by releasing endogenous opioid peptides resulting in analgesia. Endogenous opioid peptides are produced physiologically in the body and act through an opioid receptor.\n\nEndogenous opioid peptides:\n\n• Enkephalins (more potent at δ receptor)\n\n• β-endorphins (more potent at μ receptor)\n\n• Dynorphins (more potent at κ receptor)\n\n• Orphanin FQ or Nociceptin\n\n• Neoendorphins\n\n• Endomorphins","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"You decide to administer morphine for analgesia in your patients. In which of the following patients would you avoid morphine?","o":["A 50 year old man with hypotension","A 32 year old woman with head injury","A 44 year old woman with hypothyroidism","A 21 year old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus"],"a":1,"e":"Morphine should be avoided in a patient with head injury because:\n\n• Morphine retains CO2 that increases intracranial pressure (ICP) and adds to the already increased ICP.\n\n• Therapeutic doses can result in marked respiratory depression.\n\n• Interferes with the assessment of progress by causing miosis, vomiting, and altered mentation.\n\nAmong the above options, head injury seems to be the most appropriate answer and some authorities consider head injury as a contraindication.\n\nOption A: Hypotension can increase the susceptibility to vasodilating effects of morphine. Caution needs to be exercised.\n\nOption C: Sensitivity to opioids is increased in hypothyroidism. Caution needs to be exercised.\n\nOption D: Diabetes is not a contraindication for morphine use.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Morphine is used in pulmonary edema\n\n2. Pethidine can be used in biliary colic\n\n3. Indomethacin is used for headache\n\n4. Aspirin can be used in peptic ulcer disease\n\n5. Pethidine is used in post-anesthetic shivering","o":["1, 3, 4 &amp; 5","1, 2, 3 &amp; 5","1, 3 &amp; 5","3 &amp; 5 only"],"a":1,"e":"Statements 1, 2, 3 & 5 are true.\n\nOption 1: Morphine is used in pulmonary edema due to its vasodilatory and anxiolytic actions.\n\nOption 2: Morphine should be avoided as it causes constriction of the sphincter of Oddi causing an increase in fluid pressure in the common bile duct and gall bladder. Pethidine causes lesser spasm of the sphincter of Oddi and can be used in biliary colic.\n\nOption 3: Indomethacin is an NSAID used in headache.\n\nOption 4: The use of aspirin can worsen peptic ulcer disease.\n\nOption 5: Single dose of pethidine (meperidine) can be effective in the treatment of postanesthetic shivering.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a contraindication to the use of morphine?","o":["Bronchial asthma","Renal failure","Emphysema","Acute myocardial infarction"],"a":3,"e":"Acute myocardial infarction is not a contraindication for morphine use.\n\nMorphine is the opioid of choice for myocardial infarction as it relieves anxiety, chest pain, and myocardial oxygen demand.\n\nContraindications to the use of Morphine:\n\n• Bronchial asthma: Histamine release can cause bronchoconstriction and precipitate attacks of asthma.\n\n• Renal failure: Morphine-6-glucuronide (metabolite) can accumulate and lead to prolonged respiratory depression.\n\n• Emphysema: Respiratory depression and decreased response to hypoxia can cause hypercapnia in patients with COPD\n\n• Head injury: Hypercapnia due to respiratory depression can increase ICP. Miosis, vomiting, and effect on wakefulness make interpreting the signs of head injury difficult.\n\n• Biliary colic: Constriction of sphincter of Oddi can worsen biliary spasm\n\n• Hypotension and shock","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about the WHO analgesic ladder for chronic pain in adults?","o":["Analgesics are given 'on demand'","Adjuvants are indicated only for mild pain","Step 3 includes use of Morphine","Intravenous route is preferred"],"a":2,"e":"The true statement is the use of morphine falls under step 3 of WHO analgesic ladder for chronic pain in adults.\n\nWHO has developed 3 step ladder for chronic/cancer pain relief in adults.\n\nThe 3 main principles are as follows:\n\n• By the clock: Drugs should be given “by the clock” or “around the clock” rather than “on-demand”.\n\n• By the mouth: The oral route is preferred. If not feasible, the least invasive route should be considered.\n\n• By the ladder: Drugs are given in the following order, Non-opioids → Mild opioids → Strong opioids. Adjuvants are added at any stage of the WHO ladder to relieve anxiety.\n\nNote: This 3-step ladder approach is inexpensive and 80-90% effective when utilized optimally.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abc7295dd85742b29f929ec9861f2703x1280x1125.JPEG"],"t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"You prescribe a cough syrup to a patient that has an opioid cough suppressant as one of the constituents. Which would be the opioid present in the syrup?","o":["Guaifenesin","Diphenoxylate","Codeine","Naltrindole"],"a":2,"e":"Codeine (methyl morphine) is an opioid used in the treatment of cough.\n\nIt is a more selective cough suppressant than morphine and widely used as an antitussive agent. It has antitussive actions at subanalgesic doses.\n\nThe analgesic action of codeine is mainly due to its conversion to morphine.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"How does dextromethorphan differ from codeine?","o":["Its antitussive action can be more easily blocked by naloxone","It depresses mucociliary function of the airway mucosa","Addiction is more common","It causes less constipation"],"a":3,"e":"Dextromethorphan causes fewer gastrointestinal side effects like constipation when compared with codeine.\n\nIt is a synthetic central NMDA (N-methyl D-aspartate) receptor antagonist.\n\nIt is used as an antitussive agent and has no analgesic property.\n\nOption A: The antitussive action of dextromethorphan is not blocked by naloxone.\n\nOption B: Dextromethorphan does not depress the mucociliary function of the airway mucosa.\n\nOption C: It has no addictive property.","t":"Opioids   Functions And Classification","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following opioids has maximum plasma protein binding capacity?","o":["Morphine","Sufentanil","Fentanyl","Pethidine"],"a":1,"e":"Sufentanil has maximum plasma protein binding capacity.\n\nMore than 90% of sufentanil is bound to alpha acid glycoprotein in plasma.\n\nPlasma protein binding capacity of opioids:\n\n• Sufentanil: 90%\n\n• Fentanyl: 80-85%\n\n• Pethidine (meperidine): 65-75%\n\n• Morphine: 36%","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is the most potent opioid analgesic?","o":["Fentanyl","Sufentanil","Remifentanil","Morphine"],"a":1,"e":"Sufentanil is the most potent opioid. It is 1000 times more potent than morphine.\n\nOn the other hand, fentanyl is around 100 times more potent than morphine.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following has the most rapid analgesic action?","o":["Fentanyl","Remifentanil","Sufentanil","Meperidine"],"a":1,"e":"The most rapid analgesic action is achieved by remifentanil.\n\nAnalgesic action (peak) is achieved within 1 to 1.5 min following intravenous administration.\n\nAnalgesic action- duration for peak action:\n\n• Remifentanil: 1 to 1.5 min\n\n• Fentanyl: 5 min\n\n• Sufentanil: 5 min\n\n• Meperidine: 15 min\n\nThe rapid onset of action and the rapid recovery (within 15 min) owing to metabolism by plasma esterases of remifentanil make it an ideal analgesic for short painful procedures.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man with liver metastasis and altered LFT is posted for a surgery. What would be the opioid of choice in him?","o":["Fentanyl","Alfentanil","Sufentanil","Remifentanil"],"a":3,"e":"Opioid of choice in patients with compromised hepatic function is remifentanil.\n\nRemifentanil is hydrolyzed by plasma and tissue esterases and hence its metabolism is independent of hepatic function.\n\nOther opioids like fentanyl, alfentanil, and sufentanil are metabolized by the liver.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old woman with CKD is scheduled for a surgery. Her serum creatinine is 2.4mg/dL and eGFR is 13. Which of the following opioids can be safely used in her?","o":["Pethidine","Remifentanil","Alfentanil","Morphine"],"a":1,"e":"The given lab values in a CKD patient are suggestive of renal failure and remifentanil can be safely used in renal failure patients.\n\nRemifentanil is hydrolyzed by plasma and tissue esterases. The pharmacokinetics and the pharmacodynamics of remifentanil are not altered by renal dysfunction.\n\nOption A: Pethidine is metabolized to norpethidine, accumulation of which can lead to CNS excitatory effects (seizures).\n\nOption C: High levels of alfentanil can lead to prolonged action.\n\nOption D: Morphine is metabolized to morphine-6-glucuronide which can accumulate leading to respiratory depression.\n\nNote: Fentanyl clearance is not altered by renal failure but its plasma protein binding is decreased which can alter the concentration of the free drug. Hence, fentanyl is the second drug of choice after remifentanil.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who underwent an intra-abdominal surgery is about to receive intraspinal opioid injection for analgesia. Which of the following drugs should not be used for this purpose?","o":["Sufentanyl","Alfentanyl","Fentanyl","Remifentanil"],"a":3,"e":"Remifentanil should not be used for intraspinal injection.\n\nRemifentanil is formulated in combination with glycine. Glycine acts as an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal dorsal horn that causes a reversible motor weakness when it is injected intrathecally.\n\nFor this reason, remifentanil is not approved for spinal or epidural use.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the shortest acting opioid?","o":["Fentanyl","Remifentanil","Alfentanil","Sufentanil"],"a":1,"e":"The shortest acting opioid is remifentanil with t1/2 of 8-20 minutes.\n\nThe rapid metabolism by tissue esterases and rapid clearance results in its ultra short-acting nature.\n\nRemifentanil has the fastest onset of action and recovery, making it the opioid of choice in daycare surgery.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not included in neuroleptic anesthesia?","o":["Nitrous oxide","Propofol","Droperidol","Fentanyl"],"a":1,"e":"Propofol is not included in neuroleptic anesthesia.\n\nNeuroleptic anesthesia includes fentanyl, droperidol, and nitrous oxide.\n\nNeuroleptic analgesia includes fentanyl and droperidol.\n\nNeuroleptic anesthesia is characterized by analgesia, the absence of clinically apparent motor activity, suppression of autonomic reflexes, maintenance of cardiovascular stability, and amnesia.\n\nQT interval prolongation and torsades de pointes due to droperidol have made this technique obsolete.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are planning to use a transdermal patch for one of your patients who require around-the-clock opioids for his cancer pain. Which of the following drugs will you prescribe?","o":["Fentanyl","Remifentanyl","Sufentanyl","Alfentanyl"],"a":0,"e":"Fentanyl is available as a transdermal patch and can be prescribed to this patient. It is mainly used for chronic pain.\n\nVarious routes of administration of fentanyl are: intravenous, epidural, spinal, transdermal therapeutic system (transdermal patch and transdermal iontophoresis), oral (buccal tablets, films, lozenges).","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for rescue analgesia?","o":["Morphine","Codeine","Oxycodone","Fentanyl"],"a":3,"e":"Fentanyl is the drug of choice for providing rescue analgesia.\n\nRescue analgesia involves the use of medications to minimize post-operative pain. Mild pain can be controlled with oral analgesics but severe pain requires opioids like fentanyl.\n\nFentanyl is preferred over morphine or oxycodone due to its rapid onset of action, lesser incidence of postoperative nausea, vomiting, and other adverse effects.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man was posted for bronchoscopy under procedural sedation. After the administration of anesthetic drugs, he developed chest wall rigidity and ineffective spontaneous ventilation. Attempts at manual ventilation were unsuccessful due to the non-compliant chest wall. The opioid that is most commonly associated with this condition is?","o":["Morphine","Fentanyl","Pethidine","Tramadol"],"a":1,"e":"Chest wall rigidity and ineffective ventilation is a classic presentation of 'wooden chest syndrome'. This opioid-induced muscle rigidity is most commonly associated with the use of lipophilic opioids such as fentanyl and its congeners (alfentanil, sufentanil, and remifentanil).\n\nRisk factors for wooden chest syndrome:\n\n• Rapid administration of a large dose of fentanyl\n\n• Concomitant use of nitrous oxide\n\n• Old age\n\nThe clinical presentation can vary from mild hoarseness to chest wall rigidity severe enough to prevent adequate bag-and-mask ventilation.\n\nManagement of wooden chest syndrome:\n\n• Discontinuation of the opioid\n\n• Replacement of the opioid with a non-lipophilic version (such as hydromorphone or morphine) for analgesia\n\n• Consider naloxone for reversal (you do not want to reverse analgesia on an intubated patient unless absolutely necessary)\n\n• Addition of a non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocker for paralysis\n\n• Supportive ventilatory care","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Upon recovering from anesthesia, a patient started feeling cold accompanied by shivering. Which is the opioid indicated to treat this condition?","o":["Morphine","Meperidine","Methylmorphine","Methadone"],"a":1,"e":"Meperidine is commonly indicated for post-anesthetic shivering.\n\nIt prevents post-anesthetic shivering by lowering the temperature threshold at which shivering is triggered by the hypothalamus.\n\nA single dose of 12.5 to 35mg meperidine (pethidine) is effective in the treatment of postanesthetic shivering.\n\nInfusion-related chills and rigors associated with amphotericin B, aldesleukin, trastuzumab, and alemtuzumab are also managed with meperidine in combination with other agents.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old housewife with diabetic nephropathy and receiving dialysis weekly comes to your clinic with an accidental burn on her left hand. On examination, 2nd-degree burns are seen. Which of the following opioids should not be prescribed to her for analgesia?","o":["Pethidine","Methadone","Fentanyl","Alfentanil"],"a":0,"e":"Pethidine (meperidine) should not be prescribed to her as it is contraindicated in patients with renal failure.\n\nIn renal failure, there is an accumulation of its metabolite norpethidine which causes excitatory syndrome consisting of hallucinations, tremors, muscle twitches, and convulsions. Therefore, pethidine is avoided in renal failure patients.\n\nMethadone, alfentanil, and fentanyl primarily undergo hepatic metabolism.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A woman was administered an intravenous opioid for managing postoperative pain. Within 5 minutes, she started developing severe redness and pruritus along the distribution of the vein into which the drug was injected. Which of the following drugs would be responsible for this?","o":["Meperidine","Alfentanil","Remifentanil","Fentanyl"],"a":0,"e":"The clinical findings are suggestive of histamine release, which is common with the use of meperidine (pethidine) due to mast cell activation.\n\nCodeine, morphine, and hydromorphone are also associated with histamine release.\n\nFentanyl, alfentanil, and remifentanil do not lead to histamine release.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 16-year-old boy received an intravenous opioid for analgesia. A few minutes later, his heart rate decreased from 72/min to 58/min. Which among the following drugs is least likely to be associated with this finding?","o":["Fentanyl","Sufentanyl","Alfentanyl","Meperidine"],"a":3,"e":"Meperidine (pethidine) is least likely to be associated with bradycardia.\n\nIntravenous meperidine produces tachycardia as it has a structure similar to atropine. Unlike other opioids, meperidine causes mydriasis due to its anticholinergic effect.\n\nPentazocine, which is an opioid agonist-antagonist, also produces tachycardia, hypertension, and mydriasis.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following opioids has local anesthetic property?","o":["Morphine","Methadone","Pethidine","Codeine"],"a":2,"e":"The opioid with local anesthetic property is pethidine (meperidine), which is especially seen after intrathecal administration.\n\nIntrathecal pethidine can provide spinal anesthesia for endoscopic urological procedures and surgery of the lower limb, perineum, and lower abdomen including cesarean section.\n\nHowever, due to low potency and systemic opioid side effects, it is usually not used for this purpose.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding tramadol?","o":["Safe in epileptics","Less potent than morphine","Less respiratory depression","Synthetic analogue of codeine"],"a":0,"e":"Tramadol is contraindicated in epileptics.\n\nTramadol is epileptogenic and can cause seizures in predisposed individuals and in those taking medications like monoamine oxidase inhibitors and neuroleptic drugs.\n\nIt is a synthetic analogue of codeine and a weak opioid agonist with 1/6000 affinity for µ receptors as that of morphine.\n\nApart from opioid action, it also inhibits the reuptake of 5HT and NE. Due to non-opioid receptor-mediated actions, it produces less respiratory depression and less constipation.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old woman requires an intranasal opioid spray for her migraine. Which of the following would you prescribe her?","o":["Morphine","Buprenorphine","Butorphanol","Methadone"],"a":2,"e":"Butorphanol is a synthetic opioid available as a nasal spray for use in migraine headaches and painful conditions.\n\nIt is used parenterally as an analgesic for moderate to severe pain or as an adjunct to general anesthesia.\n\nOpioids available for intranasal administration are butorphanol and fentanyl.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man, on treatment with fluoxetine for depression, was started on an opioid for severe neuropathic pain. A few days later he had an episode of syncope and was brought to the casualty. ECG taken is given below, with QTc = 477 ms. Which of the following drugs is most likely the cause?","o":["Morphine","Meperidine","Methadone","Propoxyphene"],"a":2,"e":"The given ECG findings show QT prolongation which is associated primarily with the use of methadone.\n\nIn the ECG QTc(corrected QT interval= QT/√RR) is 477ms. Normal QTc is about 440ms. Values of more than 440ms in males and 460ms in females are considered to be prolonged. QT prolongation can present as syncope or cardiac arrest.\n\nMethadone has additive action with agents known to cause QT prolongation. Serious cardiac arrhythmias including torsades de pointes have been noted with methadone use.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20ff55bf95d84c80806011b3296ed557.PNG"],"t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding methadone?","o":["Long acting mu receptor agonist","Used for chronic pain relief","Rapidly absorbed from GIT and appears in plasma within 30 min of oral administration","Onset of analgesia is 30-60 min after parenteral and 1-2 h after oral administration"],"a":3,"e":"Methadone’s onset of analgesia is 10-20 minutes after the parenteral route, and 30-60 minutes after the oral route of administration.\n\nOption A: Methadone is a long-acting mu receptor agonist with a plasma half-life of 15-40 hours.\n\nOption B: It is an analgesic and used for chronic pain relief.\n\nOption C: Absorption from GIT is rapid and appears in plasma within 30 minutes and reaches peak concentrations at around 4 hours.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young man is scheduled for laparoscopic cholecystectomy. His medical history is significant for depression and he is on monoamine oxidase inhibitors for the same. Which of the following opioids can be safely administered to him?","o":["Tramadol","Meperidine","Dextromethorphan","Morphine"],"a":3,"e":"Morphine can be safely administered as it does not inhibit the neuronal reuptake of serotonin and hence does not trigger serotonin syndrome in patients on MAO inhibitors.\n\nPhenylpiperidine series of opioids such as meperidine, tramadol, dextromethorphan, and methadone inhibit the neuronal reuptake of serotonin leading to serotonergic overactivity. This can cause serotonin syndrome in patients receiving monoamine oxidase inhibitors.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A woman sustained multiple fractures in a motorcycle accident. Her physician prescribed an oral opioid for analgesia which is a partial agonist at mu and a full agonist at kappa receptors. Identify the drug given.","o":["Pentazocine","Buprenorphine","Tramadol","Fentanyl"],"a":0,"e":"Pentazocine is a partial agonist at mu and a full agonist at kappa receptors.\n\nAnalgesia produced by pentazocine is predominantly mediated by the κ receptor.\n\nPentazocine is a mixed agonist-antagonist opioid agent. It is a full agonist at the κ receptor and a partial agonist or weak antagonist at the µ receptor.\n\nBuprenorphine is a partial agonist at mu receptors but an antagonist at kappa receptors.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A cancer patient is dependent on morphine for pain management. His physician decides to switch him to buprenorphine. Due to which property he chooses this new drug?","o":["Partial agonism at mu receptor","Partial agonism at kappa receptor","Partial antagonism at mu receptor","Partial antagonism at kappa receptor"],"a":0,"e":"Buprenorphine is a partial agonist at the mu receptor with a lower intrinsic activity compared to morphine and hence does not produce withdrawal symptoms in patients with morphine dependence.\n\nBuprenorphine is a synthetic thebaine congener and also acts as an antagonist at kappa receptor.\n\nIt is more potent than morphine at mu receptor but with lower intrinsic activity and ceiling effect. Due to lower intrinsic activity, it has no withdrawal symptoms and can be used in the treatment of morphine dependence.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly man with bone metastasis is receiving intravenous opioids for pain management. Which of the following drugs can be safely administered to him without a risk of developing respiratory depression?","o":["Morphine","Buprenorphine","Fentanyl","Alfentanyl"],"a":1,"e":"Buprenorphine can be safely administered to this patient because of its ceiling effect on respiratory depression.\n\nCeiling effect is defined as no further increase in the effect of the drug with a further increment in dose, once the ceiling level is reached.\n\nOpioid agonist-antagonist compounds produce respiratory depression (and analgesia) with a ceiling effect which means increasing the dose further does not produce any further respiratory depression and may actually result in increased ventilation (due to the predominance of antagonist actions).\n\nOther drugs that show the ceiling effect are:\n\n• Butorphanol\n\n• Nalbuphine\n\n• Pentazocine.\n\nRespiratory depression with morphine, fentanyl & alfentanyl does not have a ceiling effect because they are pure opioid agonists. As the dose increases, respiratory depression also increases progressing to respiratory arrest.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a7fd0f1f0f942db96ce078817067e02x1280x1195.JPEG"],"t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false?","o":["Naltrexone is an antagonist at µ receptor","Meperidine is a pure agonist at µ receptor","Nalbuphine is an agonist at k receptor","Butorphanol is an antagonist at k receptor"],"a":3,"e":"Butorphanol is an agonist at κ receptor. It may be a partial agonist or antagonist at µ receptor.\n\nPentazocine, butorphanol, buprenorphine, meptazinol, nalorphine, nalbuphine, and dezocine are mixed agonist-antagonist opioids.\n\nAll opioid agonist-antagonists are µ antagonists and k agonists, except buprenorphine, pentazocine, and dezocine. Buprenorphine, pentazocine, and dezocine are partial agonists at the μ receptor.\n\nOption A: Naltrexone is a pure antagonist at µ, k, and δ receptors.\n\nOption B: Meperidine is a pure agonist of both μ and κ receptors.","t":"Synthetic Opioids","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a pure opioid antagonist?","o":["Naloxone","Naltrexone","Nalorphine","Both a and b"],"a":3,"e":"Both naloxone and naltrexone are pure opioid antagonists.\n\nNalorphine is an agonist-antagonist. It has antagonistic activity at the mu receptor and additional agonistic activity at the kappa receptor.\n\nNote: Nalmefene is sometimes classified as a pure antagonist but it has additional kappa receptor partial agonist activity.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about opioid antagonists?","o":["Naloxone is a short acting drug","Naltrexone lowers craving in alcoholics","Nalmefene can be used for treatment of opioid overdose","Nalmefene is shorter acting than naloxone"],"a":3,"e":"Nalmefene is longer-acting (not shorter-acting) than naloxone.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about naltrexone?","o":["Is orally active","Is used in the treatment of alcohol dependence","Is less potent than naloxone","Is a pure opioid antagonist"],"a":2,"e":"Naltrexone is more potent than naloxone.\n\nNaltrexone is a pure opioid antagonist which is orally active.\n\nAlcohol craving is reduced by naltrexone. It is also approved for the prevention of relapse in opioid addiction treatment.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is on chronic opioid therapy for pain management. He would not develop tolerance against which of the following actions of opioids?","o":["Miosis","Analgesia","Euphoria","Nausea and vomiting"],"a":0,"e":"Tolerance is developed to all the above actions of opioids except for miosis.\n\nExceptions to opioid tolerance (3C's):\n\n• Constricted pupil (miosis)\n\n• Constipation\n\n• Convulsions","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly man, who is on chronic morphine therapy for pain relief, visits your clinic with complaints of not passing stools for the last 4 days. Which of the following drugs is best suited to treat his condition?","o":["Naltrexone","Methylnaltrexone","Dextromethorphan","Nalmefene"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical picture points towards opioid-induced constipation. Methylnaltrexone, an opioid antagonist with predominant peripheral action is best suited for treating opioid-induced constipation.\n\nMethylnaltrexone is a peripheral µ receptor antagonist. It was introduced in the treatment of opioid-induced constipation refractory to other measures.\n\nIt is a quaternary ammonium compound and hence cannot cross the blood-brain barrier. It can reverse the effects mediated by peripheral opioid receptors and spares CNS effects like analgesia.\n\nThe peripherally acting opioid antagonists include:\n\n• Methylnaltrexone\n\n• Naloxegol\n\n• Naldemedine\n\n• Alvimopan","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an antagonist at µ receptor?","o":["Naltrindole","Naloxone","Nalbuphine","Naltrexone"],"a":0,"e":"Naltrindole is a new non-peptide drug which has a selective antagonistic activity on the δ receptor. This drug is still in the trial phase and is not yet in use.\n\nOption B: Naloxone is considered a pure opioid receptor antagonist but at high doses, it is found to have partial agonistic activity on the μ- and κ-opioid receptors.\n\nOption C: Nalbuphine is an agonist-antagonist opioid which acts as an antagonist at the μ receptor and an agonist at the κ receptor.\n\nOption D: Naltrexone is a μ-, δ- and κ-opioid receptor antagonist.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old man presented to the casualty with slurred speech and shallow breathing. On examination, you find he has bradycardia, hypotension, and pin-point pupils. Which drug will you choose to treat him?","o":["Nalorphine","Naltrexone","Naloxone","Nalmefene"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical features are suggestive of opioid overdose. Naloxone is the drug of choice for opioid overdose.\n\nOpioid overdose symptoms:\n\n• Triad of coma, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils (miosis)\n\n• Hypotension, bradycardia\n\n• Shallow breathing\n\n• Hypothermia\n\nNaloxone is also used for reversing neonatal asphyxia due to opioid usage in labor. Naloxone is a pure opioid antagonist which reverses all effects except sedation.\n\nNote: Naloxone is contraindicated in infants born to mothers with opiate addiction as it may precipitate acute neonatal withdrawal with seizures.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A college student who is addicted to intravenous heroin abruptly stopped abusing the drug during exams. Which of the following would you not expect as a part of withdrawal?","o":["Lacrimation","Constipation","Increased salivation and running nose","Yawning"],"a":1,"e":"Diarrhea (not constipation) is seen in opioid withdrawal.\n\nOpioid withdrawal symptoms (mnemonic- O MY WITHDRAWALS):\n\n• Mydriasis\n\n• Yawning\n\n• Weakness\n\n• Insomnia, irritability\n\n• Tremors, tachycardia\n\n• Hypertension, hyperactive\n\n• Diarrhea\n\n• Rhinorrhoea\n\n• Apnoeic attacks\n\n• Weight loss\n\n• Anxiety\n\n• Lacrimation\n\n• Sweating","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly woman who was dependent on morphine for her palliative care recently got switched to another opioid medication. A few days later she started developing withdrawal symptoms. Which of the following opioids would have been prescribed to her?","o":["Pethidine","Pentazocine","Morphine","Methadone"],"a":1,"e":"Pentazocine can precipitate withdrawal symptoms in opioid-dependent patients.\n\nIt acts as a weak antagonist or partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors which may precipitate withdrawal symptoms.\n\nOptions A, C, and D: Pethidine, morphine, and methadone are pure agonists at mu-receptors and do not precipitate withdrawal symptoms.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 33-year-old man with a history of heroin addiction is referred to a rehabilitation center. Which of the following would you use as a substitute drug for maintenance therapy?","o":["Methylmorphine","Methadone","Meperidine","Methylphenidate"],"a":1,"e":"Methadone is used as a substitute drug for the maintenance therapy of opioid (heroin) dependence. This is because of its good oral bioavailability and long half-life.\n\nMethadone is substituted for opioids during detoxification to avoid withdrawal symptoms. Once stabilized, the dose is gradually reduced and the drug is stopped slowly over days.\n\nDue to its long half-life and accumulation in tissues, discontinuing the drug still results in low concentrations of the drug in plasma resulting in protracted withdrawal symptoms.\n\nMaintainance therapy for opioid dependence:\n\n• Long-acting opioid agonist - a safer substitute like methadone is given in a supervised manner to prevent withdrawal symptoms\n\n• Partial agonist - buprenorphine can also be used\n\n• Clonidine, lofexidine - alpha-2 agonists act as sympatholytic and reduce the withdrawal symptoms\n\n• LAAM(L-Alpha-Acetyl-Methadol) - opioid agonist which reduces craving\n\n• Naltrexone - is an opioid antagonist used in maintenance therapy to prevent relapses","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A physician prescribes naltrexone to a patient undergoing treatment for opioid addiction. What is the function of this drug in opioid addiction?","o":["Prevention of relapse","Treatment of withdrawal","Treatment of overdose","Prevention of withdrawal"],"a":0,"e":"Naltrexone is used for the prevention of relapse in opioid addiction.\n\nIt is given only after initial opioid detoxification. The patient should be opioid-free for 7-10 days and have a negative naloxone challenge (no withdrawal after naloxone administration).\n\nNaltrexone is a pure opioid antagonist which is more potent than naloxone. It is orally active and has a long duration of action which makes it suitable for opioid blockade therapy in post-addicts.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about the use of naltrexone in the treatment of opioid addiction?","o":["Discontinuation of naltrexone does not cause withdrawal symptoms","It satisfies craving in opioid addicts","It blocks the effects of opioids if they are used","It is administered only when the patient has completed detoxification from opioids"],"a":1,"e":"Naltrexone does not satisfy the craving of opioid addicts. It is an opioid antagonist with a high affinity for μ opioid receptors.\n\nNaltrexone does not suppress craving in opioid addiction but it suppresses craving in chronic alcoholics.\n\nOpioid agonists such as methadone and buprenorphine suppress craving and withdrawal symptoms and are therefore used in the first stage of detoxification in opioid addiction.\n\nOption A: Discontinuation of naltrexone ingestion has no negative physical consequences, as opposed to discontinuation of agonist maintenance therapies, which results in the emergence of opioid withdrawal symptoms. This is a major reason for noncompliance and treatment failure.\n\nOption C: It blocks the effects of opioids and prevents the user from experiencing subjective symptoms of opioid intoxication such as euphoria on subsequent use. As the user does not experience these symptoms, he does not develop a craving for the substance. Thus, naltrexone prevents relapse.\n\nOption D: It is only administered when the patient has completed a medically supervised detoxification from opioids because it can cause immediate withdrawal symptoms.","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are treating a 31-year-old man with heroin dependence. Which of the following drugs would you not prescribe to him?","o":["Disulfiram","Buprenorphine","Clonidine","Lofexidine"],"a":0,"e":"Disulfiram cannot be used to treat heroin dependence.\n\nIt is an aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor used in the maintenance therapy of alcohol abstinence.\n\nMaintainance therapy for opioid dependence:\n\n• Long-acting opioid agonist - a safer substitute like methadone is given in a supervised manner to prevent withdrawal symptoms\n\n• Partial agonist - buprenorphine can also be used\n\n• Clonidine, lofexidine - alpha-2 agonists act as sympatholytic and reduce the withdrawal symptoms\n\n• LAAM(L-Alpha-Acetyl-Methadol) - opioid agonist which reduces craving\n\n• Naltrexone - is an opioid antagonist used in maintenance therapy to prevent relapses","t":"Opioid Antagonists","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Effect of acute ethanol consumption is inhibitory on which of the following ligand-gated ion channels?","o":["GABA-A receptor","Glycine receptor","NMDA receptor","Nicotinic ACh receptor"],"a":2,"e":"The effect of acute ethanol consumption is inhibitory on NMDA receptor-operated channels.\n\nEffects of acute ethanol on ion channels:\n\nEnhancement\n\n• GABA-A receptor-operated channels\n\n• Glycine receptor-operated channels\n\n• Nicotinic ACh receptor-operated channels\n\n• 5HT3 receptor-operated channels\n\n• G protein-coupled inwardly rectifying K+ channels\n\n• Large conductance, Ca2+/ voltage-activated K+ channels\n\nInhibition\n\n• NMDA receptor-operated channels\n\n• Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"The black-outs associated with high levels of ethanol can be attributed to its action on which of the following receptors?","o":["GABA-A receptor","GABA-B receptor","NMDA receptor","Glycine receptor"],"a":2,"e":"Blackouts are periods of memory loss that occur with high ethanol consumption and may result from the ability of ethanol to inhibit the action of glutamate on NMDA receptors.\n\nNMDA receptor is implicated in many aspects of cognitive function including learning and memory.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Ethanol ingestion results in enhanced diuresis. What is the most probable mechanism causing this?","o":["Inhibition of ADH action in collecting tubules","Stimulation of ADH action in collecting tubules","Stimulation of ADH release from pituitary gland","Inhibition of ADH release from pituitary gland"],"a":3,"e":"Ethanol inhibits the release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH)/ vasopressin from the posterior pituitary gland. This results in enhanced diuresis observed with ethanol ingestion.\n\nInhibition of ADH action in collecting tubules is not the mechanism by which alcohol induces diuresis. Stimulation of ADH release or action will have an antidiuretic action.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"What effect does acute consumption of ethanol have on cytochrome enzymes?","o":["Induction","Inhibition","Stimulation","No effect"],"a":1,"e":"Acute consumption of ethanol inhibits the function of several cytochrome enzymes that are involved in the metabolism of drugs. CYP2E1 is one of the isoenzymes that is specifically inhibited.\n\nChronic consumption of alcohol causes induction of cytochrome enzymes, particularly CYP2E1, increasing the rate of metabolism of drugs dependent on CYP2E1 for metabolism.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor that you would prescribe for the pharmacological management of a patient addicted to alcohol?\n\n1. Fomepizole\n\n2. Disulfiram\n\n3. Acamprosate\n\n4. Naltrexone\n\n5. Chlordiazepoxide","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1, 2 and 5","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"a":1,"e":"Disulfiram is an irreversible inhibitor of both cytosolic and mitochondrial forms of aldehyde dehydrogenase.\n\nEthanol consumption during disulfiram therapy can lead to increased concentrations of acetaldehyde. Increased acetaldehyde levels can cause flushing, pulsating headache, throbbing pain in the head and neck, and other aversive reactions.\n\nDisulfiram is indicated in alcohol deaddiction maintenance therapy.\n\nOption 1: Fomepizole is an inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase.\n\nOption 3: Acamprosate is an NMDA receptor antagonist with modest GABA-A receptor agonistic activity.\n\nOption 4: Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist that suppresses craving in chronic alcoholics.\n\nOption 5: Chlordiazepoxide is a benzodiazepine that can be used to treat acute alcohol withdrawal and anxiety.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 51-year-old diabetic patient presents to your OPD with severe restlessness, palpitations, flushing, and sweating. His medication history includes OHAs and an antibiotic that was started 3 days back. On enquiring, he admits that he consumed two shots of whiskey after taking his medications. Which of the following drugs is not likely to cause similar effects?","o":["Metronidazole","Chlorpropamide","Cefoperazone","Metformin"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical presentation is suggestive of a disulfiram-like reaction. Metformin is not associated with a disulfiram-like reaction following alcohol ingestion.\n\nDisulfiram-like reaction with alcohol ingestion is seen with the following drugs:\n\n• Metronidazole\n\n• 1st generation sulfonylureas like chlorpropamide\n\n• Cephalosporins like cefoperazone, cefamandole\n\n• Griseofulvin\n\n• Procarbazine","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man with alcohol dependence is referred to a rehabilitation center for deaddiction. Which of the following drugs would you not prescribe for maintenance of deaddiction?","o":["Morphine","Naltrexone","Acamprosate","Disulfiram"],"a":0,"e":"Morphine, an opioid receptor agonist, is not indicated for alcohol deaddiction maintenance. Opioid receptor antagonist is indicated for this condition.\n\nDrugs approved for the maintenance therapy and to prevent relapse:\n\n• Naltrexone: It is an orally active opioid receptor antagonist.\n\n• Acamprosate: An NMDA receptor antagonist with modest GABA-A receptor agonistic activity.\n\n• Disulfiram: A deterrent drug that works by inhibiting aldehyde dehydrogenase enzyme.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following anti-epileptics is effective in maintaining abstinence in a patient undergoing treatment for alcohol deaddiction?","o":["Vigabatrin","Topiramate","Phenytoin","Zonisamide"],"a":1,"e":"Topiramate has shown efficacy in maintaining abstinence and reducing craving in chronic alcoholism.\n\nThough effective, topiramate is not yet approved for use in alcohol deaddiction maintenance. It is used off-label for this indication.\n\nOndansetron and baclofen are other drugs that are found to be effective but not yet approved for this use.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young man presented to the ER with complaints of headache, vomiting, and blurring of vision after he consumed a bottle of illicit liquor. His ABG shows a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Which of the following statements would be incorrect regarding his management?","o":["Fomepizole is used intravenously","Intravenous ethanol is an alternative to fomepizole","Hemodialysis is not effective","Folinic acid is also used in treatment"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical picture and labs are suggestive of accidental methanol toxicity. Hemodialysis is used in severe cases of methanol toxicity.\n\nA serum methanol concentration greater than 50 mg/dl is serious enough to consider hemodialysis. It can be used to remove both methanol and its toxic metabolite formic acid from the blood.\n\nOption A: Fomepizole, an alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, is administered intravenously in methanol toxicity. It acts by inhibiting the formation of formic acid.\n\nOption B: Intravenous ethanol is an alternative to fomepizole. It has a higher affinity to alcohol dehydrogenase than methanol. Thus, saturation of ADH enzymes results in less formation of toxic formic acid.\n\nOption D: Folate-dependent systems are responsible for the oxidation of formic acid to CO2. Both folinic and folic acid are often administered during methanol toxicity but the exact benefit is yet to be determined.\n\nNote: Ethanol is given intravenously in the management of methanol and ethylene glycol poisoning.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"How is the administration of ethanol going to help in the management of methanol poisoning?","o":["Ethanol accelerates metabolism of methanol","Ethanol slows down metabolism of methanol","Ethanol reacts with methanol to form an inactive metabolite","Ethanol reverses the actions of methanol"],"a":1,"e":"Administration of ethanol slows down the metabolism of methanol.\n\nEthanol has a higher affinity for alcohol dehydrogenase than methanol.\n\nFormic acid is the metabolite of methanol which is highly toxic. Therefore, slowing down the metabolism of methanol reduces its toxic effects.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man undergoing treatment for alcohol deaddiction was started on disulfiram. Which of the following statements is true regarding this drug?","o":["When used with alcohol it causes respiratory difficulties","It is an anticraving drug","It can be initiated even in minimally motivated persons","Its action persists up to 2-3 days after stopping the drug"],"a":0,"e":"Alcohol consumption during disulfiram therapy can lead to increased concentrations of acetaldehyde. This can cause aversive reactions resulting in flushing, pulsating headache, throbbing pain in the head and neck, nausea, vomiting, rashes, respiratory difficulties, chest pain, and autonomic manifestations.\n\nDisulfiram is an irreversible inhibitor of both cytosolic and mitochondrial forms of aldehyde dehydrogenase. It is used in behavioral modification for alcohol de-addiction.\n\nOption B: The principle of action is aversive conditioning (not anti-craving).\n\nOption C: Disulfiram can be given only to highly motivated individuals. It should not be administered until the person has abstained from alcohol for at least 12 hours.\n\nOption D: The disulfiram-alcohol reaction may persist as long as 14 days after the last dose of disulfiram.","t":"Alcohols","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old male patient is brought to the OPD by his son, with complaints of increasing forgetfulness. Neuroimaging shows marked cortical atrophy of temporal and parietal lobes. What is the most striking neurochemical disturbance in this condition?","o":["Deficiency of acetylcholine","Deficiency of dopamine","Deficiency of serotonin","Deficiency of glutamate"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Alzheimer's disease (AD). The most striking neurochemical disturbance in AD is the deficiency of acetylcholine in the subcortical area (especially in the nucleus basalis of Meynert).\n\nNeurotransmitters like glutamate and 5-HT and other areas of the brain like the hippocampus are also involved in the pathogenesis of this disease.\n\nThe drugs used in Alzheimer's disease are:\n\n• Central acetylcholine esterase inhibitors targeting acetylcholine deficiency\n\n• NMDA antagonists targeting glutamate excitotoxicity","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a reversible cholinesterase inhibitor indicated in a patient with Alzheimer's disease?","o":["Donepezil","Rivastigmine","Galantamine","Memantine"],"a":3,"e":"Memantine is an NMDA receptor antagonist used to treat moderate to severe dementia in Alzheimer's.\n\nDonepezil, rivastigmine, and galantamine are reversible cholinesterase inhibitors indicated in Alzheimer's disease. Irreversible antagonists are not indicated in Alzheimer's disease.","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A neurology resident changed the current oral medication of a patient with Alzheimer's disease to a transdermal patch of a cholinesterase inhibitor. What must be the new drug that was prescribed?","o":["Tacrine","Donepezil","Rivastigmine","Galantamine"],"a":2,"e":"Rivastigmine is a cholinesterase inhibitor that is available as a transdermal patch for use in Alzheimer's disease. It can be applied for over 24 hours. Transdermal delivery reduces the fluctuation and avoids direct gastric irritation effects associated with oral formulations.\n\nOption A: Tacrine is an anticholinesterase that is obsolete because of its association with hepatotoxicity.\n\nOptions B and D: Donepezil and galantamine transdermal formulations are under development and yet to be available in the market.","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following NMDA receptor antagonist would you prescribe to a patient with severe Alzheimer's disease?","o":["Memantine","Ketamine","Piracetam","Amantadine"],"a":0,"e":"Memantine is a noncompetitive N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor antagonist indicated in Alzheimer's disease. It slows the deterioration of patients with moderate and severe Alzheimer's disease. It is claimed to reduce the excitotoxicity of glutamate.\n\nOption B - Ketamine is an NMDA receptor antagonist indicated as an anesthetic agent.\n\nOption C - Piracetam is a nootropic agent (a drug that enhances cognitive function) that is used off-label to enhance memory.\n\nOption D - Amantadine is an antiviral agent with NMDA receptor antagonistic action which is also indicated in Parkinson's disease due to its action on dopamine release and reuptake.","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the management of Alzheimer's disease?","o":["Monoclonal antibodies can be used in the treatment","Cholinesterase inhibitors are commonly used","Memantine is approved only in moderate to severe disease","Cholinesterase inhibitors and memantine have no effect on behavioral and psychiatric symptoms"],"a":3,"e":"Cholinesterase inhibitors and memantine reduce some of the behavioral and psychiatric symptoms in Alzheimer's disease.\n\nSSRIs like citalopram or benzodiazepines for agitation and atypical antipsychotics for psychiatric symptoms may be considered in severe cases. But atypical antipsychotics in dementia-related psychosis have shown higher mortality.\n\nOption A - Amyloid beta-directed monoclonal antibodies approved for the treatment of Alzheimer's disease are - lecanemab, donanemab, aducanumab\n\nOption B - Cholinesterase inhibitors are commonly used and are considered as first-line in early or mild to moderate stages of Alzheimer's disease.\n\nOption C - Memantine is approved only in moderate-severe Alzheimer's disease. There is not enough data to support its use in mild to moderate stages.\n\nNote- Aducanumab has been discontinued in the treatment of Alzheimer's","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old lady came to the OPD with complaints of occasional shock-like sensations down her spine and muscle spasms. T2 weighted MRI shows typical Dawson fingers and oligoclonal bands were detected in the CSF study. Which of the following is approved for the treatment of her condition?","o":["Interferon alpha","Interferon beta","Interferon gamma","Infliximab"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of multiple sclerosis. Interferon beta is approved for use in this condition. It reduces the frequency of attacks in relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis.\n\nThe probable mechanisms of action of interferon-beta are:\n\n• Immune modulation and a decrease in T cell proliferation.\n\n• Limiting the entry of T cells into CNS.\n\n• Reducing pro-inflammatory and increasing regulatory cytokines.","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"You plan to start disease-modifying therapy in a 40-year-old patient with relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis. Which of the following drugs would you not include in the treatment regimen?","o":["Glatiramer acetate","Methylprednisolone","Fingolimod","Cladribine"],"a":1,"e":"The disease-modifying therapy of relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (MS) does not include methylprednisolone. It is used in acute attacks and exacerbations for MS symptom control. The long-term value or disease-modifying potential of glucocorticoid therapy is unknown.\n\nFDA approved disease-modifying therapy of relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis are:\n\n• Interferon-beta 1a\n\n• Interferon-beta 1b\n\n• Glatiramer\n\n• Dimethyl fumarate\n\n• Teriflunomide\n\n• Alemtuzumab\n\n• Natalizumab\n\n• Fingolimod\n\n• Mitoxantrone\n\n• Cladribine","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"John Cunningham (JC) virus antibody titer is being measured in a patient with multiple sclerosis. During treatment with which of the following drugs are the titres mandatory?","o":["Alemtuzumab","Natalizumab","Glatiramer","Interferon"],"a":1,"e":"John Cunningham (JC) virus antibody titre measurement is mandatory during treatment with monoclonal antibodies like natalizumab. Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is a life-threatening disease caused by the JC virus in patients with multiple sclerosis.\n\nIt is more common when the patient is immunocompromised during their treatment with natalizumab. The current recommendation is to start natalizumab only in a patient who tests negative for JC antibodies.\n\nSeroconversion can occur anytime during treatment. Regular monitoring for JC antibody every 6 months is recommended in JC antibody-negative patients on natalizumab.","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old lady presents with complaints of sudden, involuntary, and uncontrollable dance-like movements. Her mother had also developed similar symptoms at the age of 40. Which of the following drugs is indicated for the treatment of the involuntary movements associated with her condition?","o":["Benzhexol","Tetrabenazine","Tetrahydrazoline","Benztropine"],"a":1,"e":"This patient is most likely suffering from chorea associated with Huntington's disease, for which tetrabenazine is indicated. It is a reversible inhibitor of vesicular monoamine transporter-2 (VMAT-2).\n\nOther drugs used in Huntington's disease are:\n\n• Reserpine, an irreversible inhibitor of VMAT-2 and a longer-acting agent as compared to tetrabenazine.\n\n• Deutetrabenazine refers to deuterium attached to carbon in place of hydrogen, in tetrabenazine (deuterated tetrabenazine). It is a newer form of tetrabenazine approved for the treatment of chorea associated with Huntington's disease. It slows down its metabolism, hence it is longer acting than tetrabenazine. It is associated with better control of chorea.\n\nOption A - Benzhexol is a central anticholinergic indicated in parkinsonism.\n\nOption C - Tetrahydrozoline is a topical alpha-adrenergic agonist indicated for the relief of conjunctival congestion.\n\nOption D - Benztropine is a muscarinic receptor antagonist that can be used for the management of acute dystonia.","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a mechanism of action of Riluzole?","o":["Inhibition of glutamate release","Inhibition of GABA reuptake","NMDA glutamate receptor antagonism","Kainate glutamate receptor antagonism"],"a":1,"e":"Riluzole does not act by causing inhibition of GABA reuptake.\n\nRiluzole is the only drug known to improve survival in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), also known as Lou Gehrig's disease. The mechanism of action involves the reduction of glutamate-induced excitotoxicity by:\n\n• Inhibition of glutamate release\n\n• Antagonism of NMDA and kainate-type glutamate receptor\n\n• Direct action on the voltage-dependent sodium channels.","t":"Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","c":"CNS Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young man who collapsed at a concert was suspected to have bacterial meningitis. The other attendees of the concert were administered ciprofloxacin by the medical staff on duty. What is this kind of antimicrobial therapy called?","o":["Preemptive therapy","Prophylactic therapy","Empirical therapy","Suppressive therapy"],"a":1,"e":"The antimicrobial treatment initiated in a patient who is not yet infected is called prophylactic therapy. The goal is to prevent infection in some patients and to prevent dangerous manifestations of infections in other patients.\n\nOption A: Preemptive therapy is therapy in an asymptomatic individual with evidence of infection (high-risk).\n\nOption C: Empirical therapy is broad-spectrum therapy in a patient with symptoms of infection but no microbiological confirmation of the causative agent.\n\nOption D: Suppressive therapy is given to patients when the infection is controlled but not completely eradicated by the initial round of antimicrobial treatment. This is usually indicated for immunosuppressed patients.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The total microbial population was found to be susceptible to antimicrobials on testing but a subset of the same population was resistant which was undetected. What is this phenomenon called?","o":["Cross resistance","Heteroresistance","Idioresistance","Alloresistance"],"a":1,"e":"Heteroresistance occurs when a subset of the total microbial population is resistant, despite the total microbial population being susceptible on testing. The resistant subpopulation is usually well below the assay-detection limits.\n\nHeteroresistant Staphylococci and M. tuberculosis have led to therapeutic failures and mortality. C. albicans and C. neoformans with heteroresistance to fluconazole have resulted in increased treatment failures.\n\nNote: For viruses, the resistant viral species occurring due to the above phenomenon is termed as 'quasi-species'. Quasi-species resistant to antiretroviral agents have resulted in the failure of antiretroviral therapy.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs shows concentration-dependent killing?","o":["Penicillins","5-fluorocytosine","Aminoglycosides","Vancomycin"],"a":2,"e":"Aminoglycosides exhibit concentration-dependent killing and are preferably dosed once daily for maximum effect.\n\nPeak concentration above MIC (minimum inhibitory concentration) matters for certain classes of drugs for their antimicrobial action. It is referred to as concentration-dependent killing. The period during which the concentration remains above MIC is not relevant for such drugs. Aminoglycosides and quinolones have this property.\n\nTime-dependent killing: The antimicrobial action is dependent on the period for which the concentration of drug remains above the MIC. Penicillins, 5-fluorocytosine and vancomycin have this property.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient having pneumococcal pneumonia could not be treated using penicillin G as the microbe was found to be resistant to it. How is this resistance mainly acquired?","o":["Conjugation","Transduction","Transformation","Lysogenic conversion"],"a":2,"e":"Transformation is the molecular basis of penicillin G resistance in pneumococci and meningococci.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the extrachromosomal DNA present in bacteria that transmits drug resistance called?","o":["Cytochrome","Plasmid","Introns","Exons"],"a":1,"e":"Drug resistance transmitting extrachromosomal DNA present in bacteria is a plasmid.\n\nThey are usually involved in the horizontal transfer of drug resistance gene in bacteria. Chromosomal DNA can also be involved in the transfer of resistance.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"How is multidrug resistance transferred among bacteria?","o":["Conjugation","Transformation","Transduction","Transfection"],"a":0,"e":"Multidrug resistance is transferred through conjugation.\n\nMultiple genes, each encoding for resistance to a drug can be present in a single bacterial cell. Typically such genes are present on resistance (R) plasmids. The main mode of transfer of resistant gene-containing plasmids is through conjugation.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"You want to prescribe a bactericidal drug to a patient having an infection. Which of the following drugs will you not choose?","o":["Isoniazid","Tigecycline","Daptomycin","Ciprofloxacin"],"a":1,"e":"Tigecycline is not a bactericidal drug. Tigecycline belongs to a new class of glycylcyclines which is a congener of teteracycline . It is a protein synthesis inhibitor and generally considered as a bacteriostatic.\n\nIsoniazid, daptomycin and ciprofloxacin are all bactericidal agents.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient developed a surgical site infection with Klebsiella, which is found to be sensitive to a cell wall synthesis inhibitor. Which of the following antibiotics is this likely to be?","o":["Aminoglycosides","Cefepime","Doxycycline","Erythromycin"],"a":1,"e":"Cefepime is an antibiotic that acts by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.\n\nCefepime is a fourth-generation cephalosporin. Cephalosporins act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.\n\nAminoglycosides, doxycycline, and erythromycin are protein synthesis inhibitors.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient has presented to your OPD complaining of vomiting and rice water stools. You decide to treat him with a protein synthesis inhibitor. Which antibiotic do you choose?","o":["Cefotetan","Doxycycline","Ciprofloxacin","Oxacillin"],"a":1,"e":"Doxycycline is a tetracycline that acts by inhibiting protein synthesis. It is the drug of choice for cholera.\n\nOptions A & D: Cefotetan and oxacillin are beta-lactam antibiotics that act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.\n\nOption C: Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone that acts by inhibiting DNA gyrase.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antibacterial agents does not act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis?","o":["Carbapenems","Monobactams","Cephalosporins","Nitrofurantoin"],"a":3,"e":"Nitrofurantoin is an antibacterial drug that does not act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.\n\nThe antibacterial activity of nitrofurantoin appears to correlate with the rapid intracellular conversion of nitrofurantoin to highly reactive intermediates by bacterial reductases. These intermediates react nonspecifically with many ribosomal proteins and disrupt the synthesis of proteins, RNA, DNA, and metabolic processes.\n\nCarbapenems, monobactams, and cephalosporins are beta-lactam antibiotics that act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are studying the susceptibility of Streptococci to bacitracin in the lab. What is the site of action of this drug?","o":["Cell wall","Cell membrane","Nucleic acid","Ribosome"],"a":0,"e":"Bacitracin acts on the cell wall by inhibiting the synthesis of the cell wall.\n\nIt interferes with the dephosphorylation of bactoprenol (lipid carrier) which carries peptidoglycan subunits outside the cell membrane to the growing cell wall. It can also alter the integrity of the cell membrane.\n\nBacitracin is mainly active against gram-positive cocci and bacilli.","t":"General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antimalarial drugs is a slow acting schizonticide?","o":["Artemether","Mefloquine","Pyrimethamine","Quinine"],"a":2,"e":"Pyrimethamine is a slow-acting schizonticide.\n\nFemale Anopheles mosquito inoculates Plasmodium species in the form of sporozoites into the human host during blood meal. Sporozoites migrate to the liver and mature into hepatic schizonts. Hepatic schizonts are released into the blood in the form of merozoites. Merozoites in the erythrocyte transform into ring form and erythrocytic schizonts. Some merozoites in the blood transform into gametocytes.\n\nAntimalarial drugs are classified based on their onset of action of schizonticidal function as below:\n\n• Fast onset of action\n\n• Artemisinin derivatives like artemether , arteether and artesunate are fastest acting\n\n• Chloroquine\n\n• Intermediate onset of action\n\n• Mefloquine\n\n• Quinine\n\n• Proguanil\n\n• Lumefantrine\n\n• Slow onset of action\n\n• Pyrimethamine\n\n• Sulfonamides\n\n• Tetracyclines\n\n• Clindamycin","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/698602ccedb94870a59715ab1e30d06fx1280x1621.JPEG"],"t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A researcher who was studying plasmodium species decided to visit a malaria-endemic place in Odisha to do his clinical study for 5 weeks. He is planning to leave after 2 days. Which of the following drugs can be taken as prophylaxis before visiting the malaria-endemic place?","o":["Artesunate","Mefloquine","Doxycycline","Chloramphenicol"],"a":2,"e":"Doxycycline can be taken as prophylaxis by the researcher before going to malaria-endemic places.\n\nChemoprophylaxis against malaria for travelers:\n\n• If the duration of travel is less than 6 weeks then doxycycline 100mg once a day is to be started 2 days before travel and continued atleast 4 weeks after travel.\n\n• If the duration of travel is more than 6 weeks then mefloquine 250mg once a week is to be started 2 weeks before travel and continued atleast 4 weeks after travel.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old woman presented with fever and chills. Peripheral blood smear revealed findings suggestive of P. vivax malaria. Which among the following is the most appropriate drug for this patient?","o":["Chloroquine","Primaquine","Sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine","Artesunate"],"a":0,"e":"Most approproate drug for the treatment of malaria due to P. vivax is chloroquine.\n\nP. vivax treatment recommendations are:\n\n• Chloroquine-susceptible infections: Chloroquine or artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT).\n\n• Chloroquine-resistant infections: ACT is the drug of choice but quinine is used during 1st trimester of pregnancy.\n\nIn the absence of resistance data in the question, chloroquine appears to be the most appropriate answer.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Radical cure is required for malaria caused by which of the following species?","o":["P. falciparum and P. vivax","P. falciparum and P. malariae","P. vivax and P. malariae","P. vivax and P. ovale"],"a":3,"e":"Radical cure is required for malaria caused by P. vivax and P. ovale.\n\nIn case of Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale, malarial parasites have an exoerythrocytic stage (hypnozoites). In the exoerythrocytic phase malarial parasites are found in the liver that can cause relapse of malaria after clinical cure.\n\nTherefore, they require radical cure by drugs acting on the exoerythrocytic stage. Primaquine is effective against the exoerythrocytic stages of Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale in the liver. Primaquine is administered for 14 days for radical cure.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Chloroquine is not useful in the treatment of which of the following conditions?","o":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Porphyria cutanea tarda","Giardiasis","Sjogren's Syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Chloroquine is used in the treatment of systemic lupus erythematosus, porphyria cutanea tarda and giardiasis, due to its anti-inflammatory effect; but it is not used in the treatment of Sjogren's Syndrome.\n\nChloroquine and hydroxychloroquine concentrate in lysosomes and have an anti-inflammatory effect. It also has antihistaminic and antiarrhythmic properties.\n\nNon-antimalarial uses of choloroquine are:\n\n• Hepatic amoebiasis\n\n• Giardiasis\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Discoid lupus erythematosus\n\n• Porphyria cutanea tarda\n\n• Severe polymorphous light eruption","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 26-year-old woman who is 10 weeks pregnant, comes with history of fever and chills. She is diagnosed with falciparum malaria. Which of the following drugs will you choose for her treatment?","o":["Quinine plus clindamycin","Quinine plus doxycycline","Artemether plus lumefantrine","Artesunate plus pyrimethamine-sulfadoxine"],"a":0,"e":"The woman is in the 1st trimester of pregnancy and falciparum malaria during 1st trimester of pregnancy is treated with quinine plus clindamycin.\n\nWorldwide, most species of Falciparum malaria are resistant to chloroquine. In all trimesters, pregnant women diagnosed with uncomplicated malaria caused by chloroquine-resistant P. falciparum infection can be treated with a combination of quinine and clindamycin.\n\nQuinine is considered the drug of choice in the first trimester. In the second and third trimesters artemether-lumefantrine can be used, but not in the first trimester.\n\nVivax malaria during pregnancy is treated with chloroquine as chloroquine is safe in pregnancy and children.\n\nClindamycin can be used in children for chloroquine-resistant cases. It is safe to use in children and pregnant women.\n\nOption B: Doxycycline is useful in the treatment of malaria but not used in pregnancy.\n\nOptions C and D: Artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT) is not recommended during the 1st trimester of pregnancy because of a lack of safety data.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old man presents with fever with chills and seizures. On examination, his blood pressure was 70/50 mm of Hg and labored breathing was present. Routine blood investigations revealed Hb of 4.8 g/dl. ABG was done and the pH was 7.21. Findings in peripheral blood smear are shown below. Which of the following is the drug of choice in this condition?","o":["Chloroquine","Quinine","Artesunate","Artemether"],"a":2,"e":"This scenario depicts a case of severe complicated falciparum malaria, and the drug of choice in this condition is artesunate. The image shows ring forms/early trophozoites in erythrocyte, which are binucleate (double chromatin) and this is characteristic of P. falciparum malaria.\n\nSevere complicated malaria is caused by P. falciparum and is characterized by hyperparasitemia, hyperpyrexia, acidosis, respiratory distress, bleeding manifestation, cardiovascular collapse, renal failure and cerebral malaria.\n\nAs per WHO guidelines, all patients with severe malaria should be treated with intravenous or intramuscular artesunate for at least 24 hours. Once the patient recovers and can tolerate oral medications, oral ACT for 3 days is recommended.\n\nArtemether is used only if parenteral artesunate is not available.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d429b8f0575b4110a136e8742db2c027.JPEG"],"t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a WHO recommended artemisinin-based combination therapy?","o":["Artesunate plus amodiaquine","Artesunate plus quinine","Artesunate plus pyrimethamine-sulfadoxine","Artesunate plus mefloquine"],"a":1,"e":"WHO recommended artemisinin-based combination therapies are:\n\n• Artemether plus lumefantrine\n\n• Artesunate plus pyrimethamine-sulfadoxine\n\n• Artesunate plus mefloquine\n\n• Artesunate plus amodiaquine\n\n• Dihydroartemisinin plus piperaquine","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are posted in a health centre in Nagaland as part of your rural service and a patient is diagnosed with uncomplicated falciparum malaria. Which of the following artemisinin-based combination therapy is recommended by NVBDCP for this patient?","o":["Artesunate - sulfadoxine+pyrimethamine","Artesunate - chloroquine","Artesunate - mefloquine","Artemether - lumefantrine"],"a":3,"e":"The artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT) recommended by NVBDCP in the north-eastern states is a combination of artemether and lumefantrine.\n\nNVBDCP guidelines for the treatment of uncomplicated falciparum cases:\n\n• North-eastern states: Artemether-lumefantrine based ACT\n\n• Rest of the country: Artesunate-sulfadoxine+pyrimethamine\n\nIn the north-eastern states (Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, and Tripura), the presently recommended ACT for P. falciparum malaria is a fixed-dose combination of artemether-lumefantrine due to the recent reports of late treatment failures with the current combination of AS+SP.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Loading dose is essential for chloroquine because of which of the following properties?","o":["Undergoes fast hofmann degradation","Rapid hepatic elimination","High volume of distribution","High first-pass metabolism"],"a":2,"e":"Loading dose is essential for chloroquine because of its property of high volume of distribution.\n\nChloroquine is distributed extensively in the liver, spleen, kidney, lungs, skin and, to a lesser extent, brain and spinal cord. Because of extensive tissue binding, a loading dose is required for achieving effective plasma concentrations earlier in the therapy.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"While writing a prescription for a patient diagnosed with malaria, the medical officer instructed the patient to take a particular drug along with a high fatty meal. Identify the drug.","o":["Mefloquine","Lumefantrine","Chloroquine","Amodiaquine"],"a":1,"e":"The drug described here is lumefantrine and its absorption increases when it is administered along with fatty food or milk because it is highly lipophilic.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who was diagnosed with malaria was initiated on treatment about 3 days ago. Now she presents with seizures and confusion. Which of the following drugs is most likely to have caused this condition?","o":["Artesunate","Arteether","Lumefantrine","Mefloquine"],"a":3,"e":"The case depicts neuropsychiatric adverse effects which are commonly associated with the use of mefloquine. The US FDA has put a \"black box warning\" for this drug due to permanent and serious neuropsychiatric side effects.\n\nThe CNS toxicity caused by mefloquine includes:\n\n• Seizures\n\n• Acute psychosis\n\n• Severe vertigo\n\n• Confusion or decreased sensorium","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"The finding shown in the image below is seen as a side effect of which of the following drugs?\n\n1. Chloroquine\n\n2. Quinine\n\n3. Hydroxychloroquine\n\n4. Artesunate\n\n5. Lumefantrine","o":["1, 4 and 5 only","1, 3 and 5 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only"],"a":2,"e":"The finding shown in the image is bulls-eye maculopathy, and chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine use are associated with this side effect.\n\nBull's-eye maculopathy refers to the ophthalmoscopic appearance of a central area of retinal pigment epithelial depigmentation surrounded by relatively normal retinal pigment epithelium, giving a bull's-eye appearance to the macula.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05e0376fc11340daa9d74b749ff969d7.PNG"],"t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A pregnant patient with severe malaria is undergoing treatment with quinine. Which of the following adverse effects is not associated with this drug?","o":["Tinnitus","Hypoglycemia","Hypertension","Blackwater fever"],"a":2,"e":"Quinine is not associated with hypertension. It is associated with hypotension.\n\nQuinine is used in the treatment of drug-resistant and severe P. falciparum malaria. It acts against asexual erythrocytic forms but has no significant effect on hepatic forms of malarial parasites.\n\nOption A: Quinine use is associated with cinchonism which manifests as tinnitus, visual and auditory disturbances, headache, nausea, and vomiting.\n\nOption B: Quinine has a stimulatory effect on pancreatic beta cells and can cause hypoglycemia.\n\nOption D: Blackwater fever is a rare type of hypersensitivity reaction to quinine. It presents with massive hemolysis, hemoglobinemia, and hemoglobinuria that may lead to anuria, renal failure, and death.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who was initiated on treatment for malaria two days ago, now presents with complaints of passage of dark-colored urine. Hemolysis due to G6PD deficiency is suspected. With which of the following drugs is this condition more common?","o":["Quinine","Chloroquine","Hydroxychloroquine","Primaquine"],"a":3,"e":"Hemolysis in a G6PD deficient patient is more common with primaquine.\n\nG6PD deficient patients are highly sensitive to oxidative stress. They are at increased risk of hemolysis due to the oxidant property of primaquine. Therefore, before initiating primaquine therapy, the G6PD status of the patient has to be determined. Passage of dark color urine is an indication of hemolysis and the drug has to be stopped if it occurs.\n\nQuinine, chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine can also cause hemolysis in G6PD deficient patients, but the incidence is less compared to primaquine.","t":"Antimalarial Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Sulfonamides are structural analogs of which of the following compounds?","o":["Para-amino benzoic acid","Pteridine","Dihydropteroic acid","Dihydrofolic acid"],"a":0,"e":"Sulfonamides are structural analogs of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) that interfere in the synthesis of folic acid.\n\nSulfonamides competitively inhibit dihydropteroate synthase, the enzyme that is involved in the incorporation of PABA into pteridine (dihyropteroate diphosphate) to form dihydropteroic acid, which is an immediate precursor of folic acid.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c065e998ec714ee8922d61cb501aa8b6x1280x1214.JPEG"],"t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a topically used sulfonamide?","o":["Sulfacetamide","Sulfasalazine","Silver sulfadiazine","Mafenide"],"a":1,"e":"Sulfasalazine is a sulfonamide which is not used topically. It is used orally in the management of ulcerative colitis, rheumatoid arthritis, and regional enteritis.\n\nSulfacetamide is used topically as eye drops for ophthalmic infections.\n\nSilver sulfadiazine and mafenide are used topically for the prevention of burn infections.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Trimethoprim is an inhibitor of which of the following enzymes?","o":["Dihydropteroate synthase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Dihydropteroate reductase","Dihydrofolate synthase"],"a":1,"e":"Trimethoprim is a selective inhibitor of microbial dihydrofolate reductase. Dihydrofolate reductase is involved in the reduction of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c47919543efc471385d7bbc2125b6eeax1280x1214.JPEG"],"t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following combinations is indicated for the prophylaxis of malaria?","o":["Sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim","Sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine","Sulfadiazine-pyrimethamine","Sulfamethoxazole-pyrimethamine"],"a":1,"e":"Sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine combination is used for prophylaxis and treatment of malaria caused by mefloquine-resistant strains of Plasmodium falciparum.\n\nSulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim is used in the treatment of Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia.\n\nSulfadiazine-pyrimethamine is used in the treatment of toxoplasmosis.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following options correctly describes the dose ratio of trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole in co-trimoxazole?","o":["1 : 20 (trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole)","1 : 5 (trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole)","20 : 1 (trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole)","5 : 1 (trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole)"],"a":1,"e":"Co-trimoxazole is a combination of trimethoprim with sulfamethoxazole in the dose ratio of 1:5 (trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole).\n\nThe antimicrobial activity of this combination is because of its actions on sequential steps in the pathway of synthesis of tetrahydrofolic acid as shown in the image.\n\nEach agent alone usually exerts bacteriostatic activity. But when an organism is sensitive to both agents, bactericidal activity may be achieved.\n\nBecause trimethoprim is more lipid-soluble than sulfamethoxazole, it has a larger volume of distribution than the latter drug.\n\nTherefore, when 1 part of trimethoprim is given with 5 parts of sulfamethoxazole (dose ratio is 1:5; trimethoprim:sulfamethoxazole), the peak plasma concentrations are in the ratio of 1:20, which is optimal for the combined effects of these drugs in vitro.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6225c62f015743ea81667d2d573dab97x1280x1214.JPEG"],"t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia prophylaxis in a patient who has undergone organ transplantation?","o":["Aerolized pentamidine","Cotrimoxazole","Dapsone","Dapsone plus pyrimethamine"],"a":1,"e":"Cotrimoxazole is considered the drug of choice for Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia prophylaxis in immunocompromised patients like patients who have undergone organ transplantation and HIV-positive patients.\n\nOther drugs that can be used for prophylaxis are:\n\n• Aerosolized pentamidine\n\n• Dapsone\n\n• Dapsone plus pyrimethamine\n\n• Atovoquone","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Co-trimoxazole is not prescribed in which of the following conditions?","o":["Chancroid","Lower urinary tract infection","Prostatitis","Nocardiosis"],"a":0,"e":"Chancroid was earlier treated with cotrimoxazole but it is not used anymore because better alternatives like ceftriaxone, azithromycin, erythromycin, and ciprofloxacin exist.\n\nOptions B & C: Cotrimoxazole is indicated in both upper and lower urinary tract infections and also in prostatitis.\n\nOption D: Patients with severe nocardiosis are initially treated with a combination including cotrimoxazole, amikacin, and ceftriaxone or imipenem.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A girl presents to the emergency room with severe abdominal pain, dark-colored urine, and seizures. You find out that she was recently prescribed co-trimoxazole for a UTI. What is the probable diagnosis?","o":["Acute intermittent porphyria","Porphyria cutanea tarda","Congenital erythropoietic porphyria","Hepatoerythropoietic porphyria"],"a":0,"e":"The given neurovisceral symptoms following the use of sulfonamides point towards a diagnosis of acute intermittent porphyria (AIP).\n\nAIP is a rare heme biosynthesis disorder due to a partial deficiency of the enzyme porphobilinogen deaminase (PBGD). Diagnosis is made based on elevated urine PBG levels. Alcohol, infections, pregnancy, and certain medications can trigger attacks by inducing heme synthesis. This results in the accumulation of porphyrin precursors, causing symptoms.\n\nSide effects of sulfonamides are:\n\n• Acute intermittent porphyria - sulphonamides precipitate attacks\n\n• Hypersensitivity reaction - urticaria, erythema nodosum, erythema multiforme of the Stevens-Johnson type, exfoliative dermatitis, and photosensitivity\n\n• Crystalluria - due to low solubility. It is treated with good hydration and alkalization of urine\n\n• Hemolysis in G-6-PD deficiency (sulphonamides are oxidizers)\n\n• Kernicterus in the newborn (displace bilirubin)","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the mechanism of action of fluoroquinolones in gram-negative bacteria?","o":["Inhibits bacterial DNA helicase enzyme","Inhibits bacterial primase enzyme","Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase enzyme","Inhibits bacterial topoisomerase IV"],"a":2,"e":"Fluoroquinolones inhibit two essential type II topoisomerases, DNA gyrase (in gram-negative bacteria) and topoisomerase IV (in gram-positive bacteria).\n\nDNA gyrase introduces negative supercoils and thereby reduces the torsional strain that occurs when double-stranded DNA is being unwound for replication or translation. Topoisomerases IV is responsible for unlinking daughter chromosomes after replication. Quinolone antibiotics form a complex with the topoisomerase enzyme and the DNA, and thereby block DNA replication and bacterial cell division.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who is a known case of cystic fibrosis presents to the OPD with complaints of fever and increased cough with expectoration. Sputum culture is positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Which of the following fluoroquinolones can be used in this condition?\n\n1. Gemifloxacin\n\n2. Ciprofloxacin\n\n3. Moxifloxacin\n\n4. Levofloxacin\n\n5. Gatifloxacin","o":["1 and 5 only","1, 3 and 5 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only"],"a":3,"e":"Ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin have substantial activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and can be used in this patient.\n\nMild to moderate respiratory exacerbations owing to Pseudomonas aeruginosa in patients with cystic fibrosis respond to oral ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin therapy.\n\nActivity against streptococci is significantly greater with newer quinolones like levofloxacin, gemifloxacin, and moxifloxacin.\n\nMoxifloxacin has clinically significant activity against anaerobes including B. fragilis. It is reported to have similar efficacy to piperacillin-tazobactam for complicated intra-abdominal infection.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following quinolones is not approved for use in a patient diagnosed with urinary tract infection?","o":["Nalidixic acid","Ciprofloxacin","Moxifloxacin","Norfloxacin"],"a":2,"e":"Moxifloxacin does not accumulate significantly in urine and is not approved for the treatment of urinary tract infections (UTIs).\n\nUTIs are the only indication for the use of nalidixic acid. Ciprofloxacin and norfloxacin can be used in UTIs caused by susceptible organisms.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with a wound on his leg with purulent discharge. Pus culture was positive for MRSA. Which of the following drugs will you prescribe?","o":["Ciprofloxacin","Sparfloxacin","Delafloxacin","Levofloxacin"],"a":2,"e":"Delafloxacin is a newer fluoroquinolone approved for use in acute bacterial skin and skin structural infections (ABSSSI) caused by susceptible bacteria including MRSA.\n\nCompared to other fluoroquinolones which are zwitterionic in nature, delafloxacin is anionic. This enables the molecule to achieve increased intracellular penetration and concentration, especially under acidic conditions (skin wounds). This results in increased antimicrobial activity and a decreased adverse event profile at clinically potent doses.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following fluoroquinolones can be used in a patient with rifampicin-resistant leprosy?","o":["Ciprofloxacin","Norfloxacin","Ofloxacin","Pefloxacin"],"a":2,"e":"Ofloxacin can be used in this patient as it is the fluoroquinolone that has high activity against M. leprae and is indicated in alternative multidrug therapy regimens for leprosy. It also has high activity against Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to the OPD with sudden onset abdominal cramps, fever, and diarrhea. He is a foreigner who is on a tour in India and is a known asthmatic on theophylline. You decide to start antibiotics. However, you would avoid ciprofloxacin in this patient because _______.","o":["Theophylline inhibits ciprofloxacin metabolism","Ciprofloxacin inhibits theophylline metabolism","Ciprofloxacin decreases the effect of theophylline","Theophylline induces metabolism of ciprofloxacin"],"a":1,"e":"Ciprofloxacin should not be given to this patient because it is a cytochrome P450 inhibitor and inhibits the metabolism of theophylline and can potentially lead to theophylline toxicity.\n\nPefloxacin is another drug that inhibits the metabolism of theophylline and results in its toxicity.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man diagnosed with multi-drug resistant tuberculosis requires second-line agents for his therapy. However, he is found to have a creatinine clearance of 50 ml/min. Which of the following fluoroquinolones can be started in this patient without dosage adjustment?","o":["Ciprofloxacin","Levofloxacin","Moxifloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"a":2,"e":"Moxifloxacin is metabolized predominantly by the liver and hence can be used safely in patients with decreased renal function without dose adjustment.\n\nFluoroquinolones, especially moxifloxacin, may be used as part of multiple-drug regimens for the treatment of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis.\n\nMost of the available fluoroquinolones are cleared predominantly by kidneys and dosage needs to be adjusted in patients with renal failure. But fluoroquinolones such as moxifloxacin and pefloxacin undergo hepatic elimination and so should be used carefully in patients with liver disease.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old man presents with symptoms suggestive of Achilles tendon rupture. He had undergone renal transplantation and is now being treated with an antimicrobial agent. Which of the following antimicrobials might he be on?","o":["Cotrimoxazole","Ciprofloxacin","Capreomycin","Clotrimazole"],"a":1,"e":"Among the following options, the most likely drug to have caused this condition is ciprofloxacin, since Achilles tendon rupture and tendinitis are side effects of quinolones. Elderly patients (>60 years), patients on corticosteroids and solid organ transplant recipients are at higher risk.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman undergoing treatment with moxifloxacin was brought to the casualty with delirium and seizures. According to her son, she had been taking diclofenac tablets over the counter to relieve her ankle pain, which recently started. Which of the following best describes the reason for her current ER admission?","o":["Inhibition of GABA metabolism","Enhanced GABA binding to its receptor","Displacement of GABA from receptor","Inhibition of glycine metabolism"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a fluoroquinolones drug interaction, where NSAIDs augmented the displacement of GABA from its receptors, leading to CNS excitation symptoms.\n\nFluoroquinolones act as selective antagonists of GABAA receptors and thereby have the potential to cause CNS excitation. NSAIDs such as diclofenac cause the displacement of GABA from its receptors, further enhancing the neuropsychiatric adverse effects caused by fluoroquinolones.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The ECG of a patient undergoing treatment with a quinolone is given below and you notice that QTc = 477 ms. Which of the following drugs has the highest risk of causing this condition?","o":["Moxifloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Gemifloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario describes QT interval prolongation (Normal QTc = 440 ms). Among the given options, moxifloxacin has the highest risk for causing this condition.\n\nGemifloxacin, ofloxacin, and ciprofloxacin have a relatively lower risk than moxifloxacin. Ciprofloxacin has the least risk for QT interval prolongation and torsades de pointes. Sparfloxacin has the highest overall risk of QT prolongation and has been withdrawn from many countries due to its propensity to cause torsades de pointes or polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.\n\nWithdrawn Fluoroquinolones\nAssociated Adverse Events\n\nSparfloxacin and Gatifloxacin\nPhototoxicity, QTc prolongation\n\nTrovafloxacin\nHepatotoxicity\n\nGatifloxacin\nHypoglycemia\n\nGrepafloxacin\nCardiotoxicity\n\nTemafloxacin\nImmune hemolytic anemia\n\nClinafloxacin\nPhototoxicity","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28e1d2a7a2a54dd4a9e5da3fb00c5446.JPEG"],"t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a urinary antiseptic?","o":["Galantamine","Methenamine","Mecamylamine","Pyrimethamine"],"a":1,"e":"Methenamine is a urinary antiseptic and is indicated in urinary tract infections. At pH<5.5, methenamine releases formaldehyde that inhibits bacteria.\n\nOption A: Galantamine is a central anticholinesterase approved for the treatment of Alzheimer's disease.\n\nOption C: Mecamylamine is a ganglionic blocker that can be used as an antihypertensive agent.\n\nOption D: Pyrimethamine is an antimalarial agent.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to a patient in order to relieve pain due to urinary tract infection?","o":["Methenamine","Nitrofurantoin","Phenazopyridine","Phenacetin"],"a":2,"e":"Phenazopyridine can be prescribed to this patient since it is a urinary anesthetic. It is used as a local anesthetic in urinary tract disorders to relieve the pain of lower urinary tract irritation, as in cystitis, urethritis, or prostatitis.\n\nIt does not have any antibacterial action but affords symptomatic relief of burning sensation, dysuria and urgency due to cystitis.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to the casualty with fever with chills and chest pain. He also gives history of passing brown-colored urine. He was prescribed an antimicrobial drug for urinary tract infection three days ago and his symptoms began after he had started this medication. Which of the following antimicrobial agents had most likely been prescribed?","o":["Methenamine","Nitrofurantoin","Norfloxacin","Gatifloxacin"],"a":1,"e":"The patient had most likely been prescribed nitrofurantoin as the scenario depicts side effects of this drug.\n\nNitrofurantoin is a urinary antiseptic used against lower UTI caused by E.coli and enterococci. Its use results in brown urine. It is also associated with acute pneumonitis with fever, chills, cough, dyspnea, chest pain, pulmonary infiltration, and eosinophilia within hours to days of initiation of therapy. The symptoms resolve quickly after discontinuation of the drug.\n\nThe antibacterial activity of nitrofurantoin is due to its intracellular conversion to highly reactive intermediates by bacterial reductases. These intermediates react nonspecifically with ribosomal proteins and disrupt the synthesis of proteins, RNA, DNA, and other metabolic processes.","t":"Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with tuberculosis is undergoing treatment with streptomycin. Which of the following is the primary intracellular site of action of this drug?","o":["30S ribosomal subunit","DNA helicase enzyme","50S ribosomal subunit","Topoisomerase IV enzyme"],"a":0,"e":"Streptomycin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic and the primary intracellular site of action of aminoglycosides is the 30S ribosomal subunit.\n\nMechanism of action of aminoglycosides:\n\n• Aminoglycosides are transported actively across the cell membrane into the cytoplasm (oxygen-dependent process)\n\n• Binding of aminoglycosides to 30S ribosomes cause misreading of mRNA template and result in incorporation of incorrect amino acids in the growing polypeptide chain\n\n• The resulting aberrant proteins may be inserted into the cell membrane leading to altered permeability of the cell membrane of bacteria causing bacterial death (bactericidal)","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about aminoglycosides is false?","o":["They are teratogenic","They are excreted unchanged in urine","They are bacteriostatic","They are distributed only extracellularly"],"a":2,"e":"Aminoglycosides are rapid bactericidal agents.\n\nAminoglycosides like streptomycin and tobramycin cause hearing loss in a newborn child when taken during pregnancy. They are polar compounds and are excreted usually unchanged in the urine. They are distributed mainly in the extracellular compartment.\n\nPrimarily bacteriostatic drugs are:\n\n• Sulfonamides\n\n• Erythromycin\n\n• Tetracyclines\n\n• Clindamycin\n\n• Chloramphenicol\n\n• Linezolid\n\n• Ethambutol\n\nPrimarily bactericidal drugs are:\n\n• Penicillins\n\n• Cephalosporins\n\n• Aminoglycosides\n\n• Vancomycin\n\n• Polypeptides\n\n• Ciprofloxacin\n\n• Rifampin\n\n• Metronidazole\n\n• Isoniazid\n\n• Cotrimoxazole\n\n• Pyrazinamide","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young man working in a rabbit farm presents with fever, lymphadenopathy and an ulcer on the hand and is diagnosed with tularemia. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","o":["Amikacin","Neomycin","Kanamycin","Streptomycin"],"a":3,"e":"Streptomycin (if not available, gentamicin) is considered the drug of choice for the treatment of tularemia.\n\nTularemia is caused by Francisella tularensis, an aerobic gram-negative bacteria.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A child presented with recurrent episodes of cough with expectoration. Investigations revealed a positive sweat chloride test. Inhalational formulation of which of the following aminoglycosides is approved for use in this condition?","o":["Amikacin","Gentamicin","Tobramycin","Streptomycin"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of cystic fibrosis. Inhalational formulation of tobramycin is approved for use in cystic fibrosis patients with Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections.\n\nOption A: Injectable formulation of amikacin is used in cystic fibrosis.\n\nOptions B & D: Inhalational formulations of gentamicin and streptomycin are available but they are usually not used.\n\nNote: The question is about approved inhalational formulation.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The organisms detected in culture and sensitivity reports of various specimens obtained from the patients from a medical ward are given below. In which of the following infections would amikacin not be indicated?\n\n1. Pus culture positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n\n2. Sputum culture positive for Klebsiella pneumoniae\n\n3. Urine culture positive for Proteus mirabilis\n\n4. Blood culture positive for Serratia marcescens\n\n5. Urine culture positive for Enterococcus faecalis","o":["4 only","1 and 4 only","5 only","2 and 3 only"],"a":2,"e":"Amikacin has less activity against enterococci and hence is not indicated in infections caused by this organism. In enterococcal infections, gentamicin along with penicillin (or ampicillin) is given.\n\nAmikacin is used against serious nosocomial gram-negative bacillary infections which are resistant to gentamicin and tobramycin. It has good activity against most strains of the following organisms:\n\n• Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n\n• Serratia\n\n• Proteus\n\n• Klebsiella\n\n• Enterobacter\n\n• Escherichia coli","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Oral neomycin will not be effective for treating which of the following patients?","o":["A patient requiring pre-operative antisepsis for bowel surgery","A elderly man with hepatic encephalopathy","A child with enteropathogenic E. coli associated diarrhea","A woman with a cut wound on the finger"],"a":3,"e":"Topical neomycin is used widely for a variety of infections of the skin and mucous membranes and to prevent infection of minor cuts and wounds.\n\nOral neomycin can be used for the eradication of bacteria confined to GIT in the following conditions:\n\n• Pre-operative antisepsis for bowel surgery\n\n• Hepatic encephalopathy: To reduce deleterious ammonia producing bacteria\n\n• Enteropathogenic E. coli associated diarrhea\n\nOrally administered neomycin is poorly absorbed from the GI tract and is eliminated unchanged in the feces.\n\nNeomycin is highly nephrotoxic in human beings and hence should not be administered systemically.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with hospital-acquired pneumonia is being treated with an aminoglycoside antibiotic by a single daily dose administration method. This method is preferred over the multiple daily dosage method because of which of the following properties of aminoglycosides?","o":["Decreased CYP450 mediated metabolism","Long post antibiotic effect","Increased binding to plasma proteins","Time dependent killing"],"a":1,"e":"Single daily dose aminoglycoside administration is more preferable to multiple daily dose administration because of the long post-antibiotic effect.\n\nFor certain classes of drugs such as aminoglycosides and quinolones, its antimicrobial action is dependent on the peak concentration above MIC (Minimum Inhibitory Concentration). It is referred to as concentration-dependent killing.\n\nHigh-dose, extended-interval dosing schemes take advantage of the concentration-dependent activity of aminoglycosides to achieve maximal initial bacterial killing. And then because of the postantibiotic effect of aminoglycosides, good therapeutic response can be attained even when concentrations fall below MIC for a substantial fraction of the dosing interval.\n\nTherefore, a single daily dose would be equal to or more effective than multiple 8 hourly doses. This dosing strategy may also reduce the ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity of these drugs.\n\nTime-dependent killing is an effect seen when the antimicrobial action is dependent on the duration, the drug concentration remains above the MIC. Beta-lactams, macrolides, and vancomycin have this property.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who was diagnosed with urinary tract infection receives a single intramuscular dose of gentamicin. Within how many minutes of administration is the peak concentration of this drug achieved?","o":["0-30 minutes","30-90 minutes","90-120 minutes","120-180 minutes"],"a":1,"e":"Gentamicin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic and the peak concentration of aminoglycosides following intramuscular administration is achieved after 30-90 minutes.\n\nAminoglycosides need therapeutic dose monitoring because of their low therapeutic index especially in patients in whom renal function is compromised. Both trough and peak levels are calculated for aminoglycosides.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The urine culture and sensitivity report of a patient with urinary tract infection is positive for aminoglycoside-resistant Enterococcus faecalis. What is the most common mode of this resistance?","o":["Inhibition of transport across bacterial membranes","Production of inactivating enzymes","Low affinity of bacterial ribosome","Drug efflux mechanism through the membrane"],"a":1,"e":"Clinically, the most common cause of aminoglycoside resistance is drug inactivation by enzymes.\n\nThe inactivating enzymes are commonly acquired through conjugation and transfer of plasmids containing resistance genes.\n\nCauses of aminoglycoside resistance are:\n\n• Drug inactivation by enzymes\n\n• Increased drug efflux\n\n• Decreased permeability to the drug\n\n• Modifications of the 30S ribosomal subunit that interferes with binding of the aminoglycosides","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients will you prescribe aminoglycosides at a lower dose?","o":["A urosepsis patient with associated AKI","A cystic fibrosis patient with acute pulmonary exacerbation","A diabetic patient with infected burn wounds","A CLD patient with osteomyelitis"],"a":0,"e":"The dose of aminoglycosides is to be reduced in patients with renal failure (AKI) because clearance of the drug is reduced.\n\nAminoglycosides undergo minimum metabolism and are primarily excreted almost entirely by glomerular filtration. Therefore, renal failure decreases the clearance of the drug. Because the incidence of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity is likely related to the overall exposure to aminoglycosides, it is critical to reduce the maintenance dose and dosing interval of these drugs in patients with impaired renal function.\n\nOption B: Renal clearances are increased and the half-life is reduced in cystic fibrosis patients necessitating higher doses.\n\nOption C: Burn patients have a more rapid drug clearance probably due to increased drug loss through burnt tissue and require higher doses in them.\n\nOption D: Aminoglycoside dose adjustment is not required in liver disease","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who was on aminoglycoside therapy presents with nausea and vomiting. Investigations revealed rising serum creatinine levels, and granular casts were seen on urine microscopy. Which of the following aminoglycosides is least likely to cause this condition?","o":["Amikacin","Gentamicin","Streptomycin","Tobramycin"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario depicts a case of aminoglycoside-induced nephrotoxicity and the least nephrotoxic among aminoglycosides is streptomycin because it does not concentrate in the renal cortex.\n\nAmong aminoglycosides, neomycin has the highest and streptomycin has the least nephrotoxicity.\n\nAdverse effects of aminoglycosides are:\n\n• Nephorotoxicity\n\n• Ototoxicity\n\n• Acute neuromuscular blockade and apnea\n\n• Hypersensitivity reactions (rare)","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Vestibular toxicity is predominantly seen with which of the following aminoglycosides?","o":["Amikacin","Kanamycin","Streptomycin","Neomycin"],"a":2,"e":"Vestibular toxicity is predominantly seen with streptomycin and gentamicin.\n\nOtotoxicity seen with aminoglycosides:\n\n• Cochlear toxicity (high-pitched tinnitus) - amikacin, kanamycin, neomycin\n\n• Vestibular toxicity (moderately intense headache and vertigo) - streptomycin > gentamicin\n\n• Both vestibular toxicity and cochlear toxicity - tobramycin","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who was recently started on i.v. gentamicin for enterococcal endocarditis presented to the OPD with complaints of headache and dizziness. By which of the following mechanisms does the drug cause this adverse effect?","o":["Direct hair cell toxicity","Inhibition of hair cell Na+/K+ ATPase","Non-cumulative toxicity","Interference with Ca2+ channels"],"a":0,"e":"The scenario depicts a case of gentamicin-induced ototoxicity which is caused due to direct hair cell toxicity. Degeneration of vestibular hair cells and neurons leads to vertigo and associated headache.\n\nStreptomycin and gentamicin produce predominantly vestibular effects. Amikacin, kanamycin, and neomycin primarily affect auditory function. Tobramycin affects both equally.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly patient who was taken up for an emergency laparotomy showed prolonged respiratory depression which did not resolve with neostigmine. On inquiry, the attendant confirmed that the patient was on aminoglycoside therapy. Which of the following is least likely to cause this effect?","o":["Amikacin","Kanamycin","Gentamicin","Tobramycin"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario depicts the neuromuscular blockade effect of aminoglycosides, and tobramycin has the least potency for blockade in this group.\n\nDecreasing order of potency for the neuromuscular blockade is:\n\nNeomycin > Kanamycin > Amikacin > Gentamicin > Tobramycin\n\nIt is a recognized clinical side effect of aminoglycoside antibiotics, and this effect becomes significant (prolonged respiratory depression) when the drug is used in conjunction with neuromuscular blocking agents or calcium channel blockers. They are avoided in patients with myasthenia gravis.\n\nAminoglycosides cause neuromuscular blockade by interfering with calcium channels and thereby inhibiting acetylcholine's pre-junctional release and post-junctional sensitivity. The neuromuscular blockade produced by these antibiotics is not reversed by neostigmine but is reversed by IV calcium gluconate.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"By which of the following mechanisms do tetracyclines inhibit protein synthesis?","o":["Inhibition of initiation of translation and misreading of mRNA","Binding to 30S subunit and inhibiting the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to A site","Binding to 30S subunit and inhibiting peptide bond formation","Inhibits binding of aminoacyl-t-RNA to 50s subunit of ribosome"],"a":1,"e":"Tetracyclines inhibit protein synthesis by binding to 30s subunit and inhibiting the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A (acceptor) site of the mRNA – ribosome complex.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following organisms is resistant to tetracyclines?","o":["MRSA","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Rickettsia","Mycoplasma"],"a":1,"e":"All strains of Pseudomonas aeruginosa are resistant to the action of tetracyclines.\n\nTetracyclines are active against MRSA and MSSA. Doxycycline and minocycline can be used in cutaneous MRSA infections.\n\nRickettsia and chlamydia are highly susceptible to tetracyclines. Tetracyclines are considered first line agents for chlamydial infections like non-specific urethritis/ endocervicitis and rickettsial infections like typhus, rocky mountain spotted fever, Q fever.\n\nThey are also second-line drugs in patients with syphilis who are allergic to penicillin.\n\nTetracyclines are bacteriostatic drugs which are more active against gram-positive than gram-negative. They are effective against spirochetes, Haemophilus ducreyi, Vibrio cholera, Mycoplasma pneumoniae.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"In patients with which of the following conditions is doxycycline not used?","o":["Leptospirosis","Lymphogranuloma veneruum","Borrelliosis","Typhoid fever"],"a":3,"e":"Doxycycline is not used in typhoid infection. Enterobacteriaceae and gram-negative anaerobes are resistant to tetracyclines, hence doxycycline is not used in intraabdominal infections. Third-generation cephalosporins and quinolones are used in treating typhoid fever.\n\nDoxycycline treatment for 21 days is the first-line therapy for lymphogranuloma venereum. It is also indicated as one of the first-line treatments for borreliosis.\n\nDoxycycline is the alternative to penicillin for the treatment of leptospirosis.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Tetracycline is used for the prophylaxis of which of the following conditions?","o":["Cholera","Brucellosis","Leptospirosis","Meningitis"],"a":0,"e":"Tetracycline is used for the prophylaxis of cholera. It is recommended for close contacts of a case of cholera. It is supposed to be effective in preventing spread.\n\nDoxycycline in combination with rifampicin is used for prophylaxis of brucellosis. It is also used in the prophylaxis of leptospirosis.\n\nNote: The question is about the drug tetracycline and not about the class of tetracyclines as a whole.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The urine culture and sensitivity report of a patient is positive for tetracycline-resistant Enterococcus faecalis. Which among the following is a minocycline derivative that can be used in this condition?","o":["Methacycline","Lymecycline","Doxycycline","Tigecycline"],"a":3,"e":"Tigecycline is a glycylcycline and a derivative of minocycline that is active against most bacteria that are resistant to classical tetracyclines, and hence can be used in this condition.\n\nThe mechanism of action is similar to tetracyclines. It is used against enterobacteriaceae, gram-negative anaerobes, and enterococcus which are resistant to tetracyclines.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with the syndrome of inappropriate secretion of antidiuretic hormone is prescribed a drug known to cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Identify this drug.","o":["Minocycline","Demeclocycline","Tetracycline","Doxycycline"],"a":1,"e":"The drug described here is demeclocycline. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is associated with the use of this drug. This side effect of the drug is utilized in the treatment of SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate secretion of antidiuretic hormone).","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient develops dizziness, ataxia, and nausea after he was started on an antimicrobial drug. Which of the following drugs can be implicated?","o":["Tetracycline","Oxytetracycline","Doxycycline","Minocycline"],"a":3,"e":"The drug described here is minocycline as it is associated with vestibular toxicity which manifests as dizziness, ataxia, nausea, and vomiting. Symptoms usually occur soon after the initial dose is given and disappears 24-48 hours after drug administration is stopped.\n\nUses of minocycline are:\n\n• Cutaneous MRSA infections\n\n• Along with sulfonamides for nocardiosis (alternative therapy)\n\n• Subgingival administration in dentistry (extended-release preparations)","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"An infant on antibiotics becomes very irritable. Investigations reveal increased intracranial pressure. However, the condition resolves on discontinuation of the drug. Which drug had the baby likely been on?","o":["Aminoglycosides","Tetracyclines","Chloramphenicol","Macrolides"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario depicts a case of pseudotumor cerebri and tetracyclines are associated with this condition in infants, even when given in normal doses. Only elevated intracranial pressure is seen without any abnormality in the spinal fluid. Increased pressure returns to normal after therapy is discontinued.\n\nOther side effects of tetracyclines are:\n\n• Teratogenic\n\n• Deposit in developing teeth and bones, cause brownish discoloration of teeth (chelating property)\n\n• Marked suppression of normal flora of intestine causing superinfection. Pseudomembranous enterocolitis is a rare side effect","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Gray baby syndrome is associated with the use of which of the following drugs?","o":["Clindamycin","Chloramphenicol","Tetracycline","Cotrimoxazole"],"a":1,"e":"Gray baby syndrome is associated with the use of chloramphenicol, especially in premature infants.\n\nIt develops due to the developmental deficiency of the enzyme glucoronyl transferase. This enzyme metabolizes chloramphenicol in neonates. Its deficiency leads to an inability to excrete unconjugated drugs adequately through renal excretion.\n\nThe initial symptoms are:\n\n• Vomiting\n\n• Refusal to suck\n\n• Irregularly rapid respiration\n\n• Periods of cyanosis.\n\nAfter 1-2 days:\n\n• Flaccid baby\n\n• Hypothermia\n\n• Ashen-gray color skin discoloration\n\nIf there is a recovery, usually there are no sequelae.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Chloramphenicol acts by binding to:","o":["50s ribosomal subunit","Topoisomerase IV enzyme","30s ribosomal subunit","DNA helicase enzyme"],"a":0,"e":"Chloramphenicol acts by binding to the 50s ribosomal subunit.\n\nIt inhibits the binding of amino acid containing tRNA to acceptor site on 50s subunit of the ribosome. This prevents peptide bond formation by inhibiting the interaction between peptidyltransferase enzyme and the amino acid.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who was diagnosed with meningitis is on chloramphenicol therapy. Which of the following blood dyscrasias as a side effect of this drug?","o":["Hypoplastic anemia","Agranulocytosis","Aplastic anemia","Thrombocytopenia"],"a":2,"e":"The patient is most likely to have aplastic anemia, as it is the most common blood dyscrasia associated with the use of chloramphenicol. It can lead to fatal pancytopenia that has a high rate of mortality.\n\nHematological toxicities of chloramphenicol include:\n\n• Dose-related toxicity - pancytopenia (anemia, thrombocytopenia, leukopenia)\n\n• Idiosyncratic response - aplastic anemia\n\nBlood dyscrasias occurring with chloramphenicol:\n\n• Aplastic anemia (70%)\n\n• Hypoplastic anemia\n\n• Agranulocytosis\n\n• Thrombocytopenia\n\nChloramphenicol remains an alternative drug for the treatment of meningitis caused by H. influenzae, N. meningitidis, and S. pneumoniae in developing countries and also in patients who have a severe allergy to β-lactams.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antibiotics structurally resembles aminoacyl t-RNA and inhibits the protein synthesis by premature termination?","o":["Tetracycline","Chloramphenicol","Puromycin","Erythromycin"],"a":2,"e":"Puromycin structurally resembles aminoacyl t-RNA and inhibits the protein synthesis by premature termination. It is used as an antibiotic in cell cultures.\n\nOption A: Tetracycline binds to the 30s ribosome and prevents attachment of aminoacyl t-RNA to the acceptor site.\n\nOption B: Chloramphenicol binds to the 50s subunit of the ribosome and inhibits the binding of amino acid containing tRNA to the acceptor site on 50s subunit of the ribosome.\n\nOption D: Erythromycin acts by interfering with translocation at the 50s ribosome.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following groups of antibiotics has additional anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory activities?","o":["Tetracyclines","Polypeptides","Fluoroquinolones","Macrolides"],"a":3,"e":"Macrolides are a group of antibiotics that have additional anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory activities.\n\nThey are being investigated and used in pulmonary diseases for their immunomodulatory properties.\n\nExamples of macrolide antibiotics:\n\n• Erythromycin\n\n• Azithromycin\n\n• Clarithromycin","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"An unvaccinated child diagnosed with diphtheria is initiated erythromycin therapy. This drug acts by interfering with:","o":["Translocation at 30s ribosome","Translocation at 50s ribosome","Transcription at 50s ribosome","Signal transduction of 50s ribosome"],"a":1,"e":"Erythromycin acts by interfering with translocation at the 50s ribosome.\n\nIt does not inhibit the peptide bond formation but the translocation step is inhibited where the peptidyl t-RNA molecule moves from the acceptor site on the ribosome to the peptidyl donor site.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the rationale for the use of erythromycin in a patient with diabetic gastroparesis?","o":["It increases cAMP and increases intestinal motility","It decreases bacterial count in intestinal hypomotility","It binds to adenyl cyclase and increases intestinal motility","It binds to motilin receptors and stimulates intestinal motility"],"a":3,"e":"Erythromycin can be used in diabetic gastroparesis as it binds to motilin receptors and stimulates intestinal motility.\n\nIt has been used in conditions with hypomotility of the gastrointestinal tract like in postoperative ileus to promote peristalsis and to increase gastric emptying.\n\nErythromycin is used off-label as a prokinetic agent in the intensive care setting and in patients with diabetic gastroparesis.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The chest x-ray of a patient who presented with fever and cough with expectoration is shown below. Following discussion, a lincosamide antibiotic is prescribed. Which of the following will you choose?","o":["Telithromycin","Spectinomycin","Clindamycin","Streptomycin"],"a":2,"e":"Clindamycin, a lincosamide is used in the management of lung abscess and anaerobic lung and pleural space infections.\n\nClindamycin is a congener of lincomycin and binds to the 50S subunit of bacterial ribosomes and inhibits protein synthesis.\n\nOption A: Telithromycin is a ketolide antibiotic.\n\nOption B: Spectinomycin is an aminocyclitol antibiotic that binds to the 30S subunit of ribosomes and inhibits protein synthesis.\n\nOption D: Streptomycin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/653b55b4bf464e6ea6e66052cb0912d7.PNG"],"t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a serious adverse effect you would consider as a possibility in a patient receiving clindamycin?","o":["Nephrotoxicity","Pseudotumor cerebri","Pseudomembranous colitis","Aplastic anemia"],"a":2,"e":"Clindamycin use is to be limited because of the risk of pseudomembranous colitis.\n\nPseudomembranous colitis is a lethal syndrome caused by Clostridium difficile due to alteration in normal intestinal microbiota. It is characterized by fever, watery diarrhea and increased peripheral white blood cell count. Metronidazole and oral vancomycin are used in the treatment of this condition.\n\nDrugs causing pseudomembranous colitis are:\n\n• Penicillins\n\n• Clindamycin\n\n• Rifaximin\n\n• Tetracycline (rare side effect)\n\n• Erythromycin","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about streptogramins is true?","o":["Quinupristin-dalfopristin combination is not active against gram-positive cocci","Synergistic activity of quinupristine and dalfopristine has bactericidal action","Quinupristin binds to the 30s subunit of ribosomes","Dalfopristin binds to the 30s subunit of ribosomes"],"a":1,"e":"Quinupristin and dalfopristin are a combination of streptogramins and the synergistic activity of these drugs has bactericidal action.\n\nQuinupristin binds to the 50s subunit of ribosomes and acts similar to macrolides by interfering in translocation and inhibiting protein synthesis.\n\nDalfopristin binds to a site nearby, resulting in conformational changes in the 50S ribosome thereby increasing the activity of quinupristin. The synergic activity of these drugs can have bactericidal action in certain susceptible bacteria.\n\nQuinupristin/dalfopristin is active against gram-positive cocci and organisms responsible for atypical pneumonia (e.g., M. pneumoniae, Legionella spp., and C. pneumoniae).\n\nQuinupristin is a streptogramin B molecule whereas dalfopristin is a streptogramin A molecule.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following binds to the P site of 50s ribosome and inhibits bacterial protein synthesis?","o":["Daptomycin","Tetracycline","Linezolid","Meropenem"],"a":2,"e":"Linezolid binds to the P site of the 50s ribosomal subunit and inhibits bacterial protein synthesis. It is an oxazolidinone that is active against various resistant organisms including MRSA.\n\nLinezolid is well absorbed orally and the bioavailability approaches 100%, hence intravenous and oral doses are the same for this drug.\n\nLinezolid is associated with the side effect of myelosuppression (anemia, leukopenia, pancytopenia, thrombocytopenia). Thrombocytopenia is the most common side effect. Platelet count monitoring is done in a patient with linezolid who is at risk of myelosuppression and blood dyscrasias. Linezolid also causes serotonin syndrome due to monoamine oxidase(MAO) inhibition.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Linezolid is approved for use in which of the following patients?\n\n1. A patient with urinary tract infection caused by vancomycin-resistant E. faecium\n\n2. A patient with nosocomial pneumonia due to S. aureus\n\n3. A patient with community-acquired pneumonia due to S. pneumoniae\n\n4. A patient with methicillin-resistant S. aureus wound infection\n\n5. A patient with Pseudomonas aeruginosa wound infection","o":["1 and 5 only","1, 2 and 4 only","1, 3 and 5 only","1, 2, 3 and 4 only"],"a":3,"e":"Linezolid is approved for use in the following conditions:\n\n• Vancomycin-resistant E. faecium infections\n\n• Methicillin resistance/susceptible S. aureus infections\n\n• Community-acquired pneumonia due to S.pneumoniae\n\n• Nosocomial pneumonia due to S. aureus.","t":"Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which class of antiretroviral drugs does nevirapine belong to?","o":["Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors","Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors","Nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors","Protease inhibitors"],"a":1,"e":"Nevirapine is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI)\n\nUS FDA has approved doravirine, a new non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor(NNRTI) for the treatment of adults with HIV-1 infection given in combination with other ARTs.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Resistance to zidovudine develops due to:","o":["Increased efflux of the drug from nucleus","Mutations of reverse transcriptase","Increased efflux of the drug from inside the cell","Decreased zidovudine triphosphate formation"],"a":1,"e":"Resistance to zidovudine occurs due to mutations in the reverse transcriptase enzyme.\n\nThe mutations are referred to as thymidine analog mutations (TAM) because of their ability to confer cross-resistance to other thymidine analogs such as stavudine.\n\nM184V mutation, which is associated with the use of lamivudine or emtricitabine, restores zidovudine susceptibility in zidovudine-resistant HIV.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with AIDS is planned to be started on a lamivudine-based-antiretroviral therapy. Which of the following drugs will you not add to her regimen?","o":["Zidovudine","Stavudine","Emtricitabine","Abacavir"],"a":2,"e":"Lamivudine and emtricitabine are not combined because of similar actions and no added benefits. Emtricitabine is a cytidine analog. It is chemically related to lamivudine and shares most of its pharmacodynamic characteristics.\n\nThe same holds good for the zidovudine and stavudine combination. Zidovudine and stavudine are thymidine analogues. In fact, efficacy may even decrease because of competition for a similar activation process.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about lamivudine is false?","o":["It is an effective agent for patients with chronic hepatitis B","Dose is lower for blocking HIV replication than HBV replication","Should not be used as monotherapy in HBV/HIV infected patients","Anti-HBe seroconversion occurs in a minority of the patients"],"a":1,"e":"The daily dose of lamivudine indicated for HIV (300 mg) is higher than that of the dose indicated for HBV (100 mg).\n\nLamivudine has both anti-HIV and anti-HBV activity and is an effective agent for patients with chronic hepatitis B. It should not be used alone because resistance can rapidly emerge in HIV viruses.\n\nAnti-HBe seroconversion occurs in only a minority of the patients taking lamivudine.\n\nDrugs that cause anti-HBe seroconversion:\n\n• Lamivudine\n\n• Entecavir\n\n• Adefovir\n\n• Tenofovir\n\n• Telbivudine\n\n• Pegylated interferon","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on antiretroviral therapy presents with severe tingling and numbness in the legs. On examination, you find that his tendon reflexes are reduced. Which of the following drugs has the highest propensity to cause this condition?","o":["Zidovudine","Lamivudine","Stavudine","Didanosine"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of peripheral neuropathy. The drug causing maximum peripheral neuropathy is stavudine.\n\nThe incidence of peripheral neuropathy is very rare with both zidovudine and lamivudine. Lamivudine is considered one of the least toxic antiretroviral agents.\n\nDidanosine is frequently associated with peripheral neuropathy, but stavudine is associated with the highest risk of peripheral neuropathy.\n\nNote: Zalcitabine was associated with a higher incidence of peripheral neuropathy. It is no longer marketed.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs are not protease inhibitors?\n\n1. Nelfinavir\n\n2. Saquinavir\n\n3. Abacavir\n\n4. Ritonavir\n\n5. Raltegravir","o":["1 and 3","2 and 4","3 and 5","1 and 5"],"a":2,"e":"Abacavir is a guanosine analogue and is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor.\n\nRaltegravir is an integrase inhibitor.\n\nNelfinavir, saquinavir and ritonavir are protease inhibitors.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"During a clinical trial, it was noted that the bioavailability of some drugs was substantially improved on ritonavir administration. Which of the following drugs does not exhibit this property?","o":["Saquinavir","Lopinavir","Nelfinavir","Darunavir"],"a":2,"e":"The bioavailability of nelfinavir is not substantially improved with ritonavir administration because it undergoes metabolism primarily by CYP2C19 and ritonavir is a weak inhibitor of CYP2C19.\n\nAll other protease inhibitors undergo hepatic oxidative metabolism predominantly by the CYP3A4 enzyme. Ritonavir, being a potent inhibitor of CYP3A4 acts as a pharmacological booster in combination with other protease inhibitors.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a pharmacological booster with no antiretroviral activity?","o":["Ritonavir","Vorinostat","Belinostat","Cobicistat"],"a":3,"e":"Cobicistat is a pharmacological booster with no antiretroviral activity.\n\nCobicistat is a ritonavir analog developed as an exclusive pharmacological booster that inhibits the CYP3A4 enzyme and has no antiviral activity. It is available only in combination with atazanavir or darunavir or elvitegravir for HIV infections.\n\nRitonavir is also used as a pharmacological booster but it has antiretroviral activity.\n\nCobicistat\nRitonavir\n\nSpecific CYP3A4 inhibition\n\nPotent inhibitor of CYP3A4 and weak inhibitor of CYP2D6\n\nBetter tolerated\nLess tolerated\n\nReduced impact on plasma lipds\nCauses dose-dependent elevation in serum total cholesterol and\ntriglycerides\n\nLacks P450 enzyme induction\nModerate inducer of P450 enzymes\n\n Vorinostat and belinostat are histone deacetylase inhibitors. Vorinostat is approved for the treatment of patients with cutaneous T-cell lymphoma with persistent or recurrent disease after two systemic therapies.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a subcutaneously administered drug, that will be helpful in a patient showing high viral load despite good compliance with ART?","o":["Maraviroc","Enfuvirtide","Raltegravir","Emtricitabine"],"a":1,"e":"The drug described in this case is enfuvirtide, which is an anti-HIV drug that is administered subcutaneously.\n\nIt is used only in the treatment of those who have evidence of HIV replication (high viral load) despite ongoing antiretroviral therapy.\n\nIt binds to the gp41 subunit of the viral envelope and prevents the fusion of the virus to the cellular membranes thereby preventing entry of the virus.\n\nEnfuvirtide is active against HIV-1 only.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient showing treatment failure with multiple antiretroviral drugs is now started on maraviroc. This drug acts against:","o":["CCR5","CXCR4","CD4","CCR5 and CD4"],"a":0,"e":"Maraviroc is a chemokine receptor antagonist acting against the CCR5 receptor.\n\nIt binds to the host cell CCR-5 receptor and prevents the binding of viral GP-120 to host cells. It is indicated in patients with evidence of infection with predominantly CCR-5 tropic virus.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"The husband of an AIDS patient noticed that his wife had become forgetful and confused lately. She complained of dizziness and lack of sleep. Eventually, she started behaving bizarrely, paranoid that everybody is trying to kill her. Yesterday she was found in her room talking to somebody who did not exist in reality. Which of the following drugs is most commonly associated with this side effect?","o":["Nevirapine","Efavirenz","Etravirine","Delavirdine"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario describes psychiatric or CNS adverse effects, which are commonly seen with efavirenz.\n\nThe CNS/ psychiatric adverse effects seen with the use of efavirenz are:\n\n• Frank psychosis (depression, hallucinations, and/or mania)\n\n• Impaired concentration\n\n• Dysphoria\n\n• Dizziness\n\n• Vivid dreams\n\n• Insomnia","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presenting with low-grade fever and weight loss of 3 months is diagnosed to have HIV-2 infection. Which of the following drugs is not indicated here?","o":["Ritonavir","Tenofovir","Efavirenz","Zalcitabine"],"a":2,"e":"Efavirenz is not indicated for HIV-2 infection. It is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI).\n\nNNRTIs are HIV-1 specific and have no activity against HIV-2. The binding site for NNRTI is virus strain-specific which only inhibits the HIV-1 reverse transcriptase. It is approved only for HIV-1 infections and should not be used for HIV-2 infections.\n\nRitonavir, tenofovir, and zalcitabine are indicated for HIV-2 infections.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man who has been on ART for the past two months presents with abdominal pain. On evaluation, he was found to have acute pancreatitis. Which of the following drugs is associated with the highest risk for causing this condition?","o":["Didanosine","Lamivudine","Zidovudine","Abacavir"],"a":0,"e":"The maximum risk of pancreatitis is seen with didanosine.\n\nIt should, therefore not be combined with hydroxyurea, alcohol, or pentamidine.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with AIDS is initiated on antiretroviral therapy with zidovudine. Which of the following side effects is he most likely to develop?","o":["Anemia","Peripheral neuropathy","Lactic acidosis","Hepatotoxicity"],"a":0,"e":"This patient is most likely to develop anemia since it is the most common side effect of zidovudine.\n\nHigher dosages of zidovudine lead to bone marrow suppression resulting in anemia and granulocytopenia.\n\nPeripheral neuropathy, hepatotoxicity, and lactic acidosis are rare side effects of zidovudine.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Of all the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, the use of which of the following is most strongly associated with fat wasting?","o":["Zidovudine","Lamivudine","Stavudine","Abacavir"],"a":2,"e":"Of all the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, the use of stavudine is most strongly associated with fat wasting (lipoatrophy).","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on antiretroviral therapy comes to you for follow-up. His lipid profile reveals high cholesterol levels. Which of the following drugs is not associated with this condition?","o":["Atazanavir","Saquinavir","Amprenavir","Nelfinavir"],"a":0,"e":"Atazanavir is not associated with dyslipidemia, fat redistribution, or metabolic syndrome. This is in contrast to all the other protease inhibitors, which causes side effects like hypertriglyceridemia, hypercholesterolemia and other signs of lipodystrophy.\n\n Patients treated with atazanavir have significantly lower fasting triglyceride and cholesterol concentrations than patients treated with nelfinavir and lopinavir. Clinically, when abacavir (Nucleoside Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors) is substituted for protease inhibitors in patients having dyslipidemia, it leads to an improvement in lipid profile.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old known hemophiliac on antiretroviral therapy suddenly developed headache, vomiting and altered consciousness and was rushed to a hospital. Which of the following protease inhibitors is implicated here?","o":["Saquinavir","Ritonavir","Tipranavir","Indinavir"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of intracranial hemorrhage and the protease inhibitor that is associated with this condition is tipranavir. It is a non-peptidic protease inhibitor\n\nTipranavir use has been associated rarely with intracranial hemorrhage (including fatalities) and bleeding episodes in patients with hemophilia.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on antiretroviral therapy comes for regular follow-up. You note nail changes as shown below. Which of the following drugs causes this condition?","o":["Abacavir","Ritonavir","Zidovudine","Didanosine"],"a":2,"e":"The image shows nail hyperpigmentation and it is commonly encountered with the use of zidovudine. This is seen with chronic zidovudine administration.\n\nThe adverse effects of zidovudine are:\n\n• Fatigue, malaise, myalgia, and anorexia\n\n• Headache and insomnia\n\n• Erythrocytic macrocytosis\n\n• Nail hyperpigmentation\n\n• Myopathy\n\n• Bone marrow suppression - anemia and granulocytopenia","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76b1fcce32a14af98378f43320c5ca9e.PNG"],"t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which is the only antiretroviral drug that may have teratogenic properties?","o":["Didanosine","Emtricitabine","Efavirenz","Nevirapine"],"a":2,"e":"The only antiretroviral drug that may have teratogenic properties is efavirenz.\n\nIt has shown teratogenicity in primates. However, clinical trials have suggested that there is no significantly increased risk of teratogenicity in humans as compared to other antiretroviral drugs.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing treatment for HIV complains of dizziness. On examination, you note his supine BP to be 140/80 mmHg and standing BP to be 110/70 mmHg. Which of the following drugs would have caused this condition?","o":["Maraviroc","Raltegravir","Enfuvirtide","Nelfinavir"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario depicts a case of orthostatic hypotension and maraviroc is associated with dose-dependent and concentration-dependent orthostatic hypotension.\n\nMaraviroc is a chemokine receptor antagonist acting against the CCR5 receptor. It binds to the host cell CCR-5 receptor and prevents the binding of viral GP-120 to host cells. It is indicated in patients with evidence of infection with predominantly CCR-5 tropic virus.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"The ECG of a patient on antiretroviral therapy is shown below. You observe that the QTc = 477 ms. This can be attributed to:","o":["Raltegravir","Rilpivirine","Etravirine","Efavirenz"],"a":1,"e":"The given ECG indicates QT interval prolongation (normal QTc = 440ms) and this is observed with the use of rilpivirine. It is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI).\n\nQtc interval prolongation is observed with higher concentrations of the drug. Caution has to be exercised when rilpivirine is administered along with CYP3A4 inhibitors that can increase the concentration of rilpivirine.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ef1e9f9f002544b5a2a453e8aa910ce3.JPEG"],"t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive man was started on HAART. On follow-up testing, his CD4 cell count had decreased below 200/mm³. Subsequently, he developed diarrhea and redistribution of body fat. What is false about the causative drug class?","o":["Atazanavir has the lowest propensity to increase total cholesterol levels.","Tipranavir is used in sub-therapeutic doses as a pharmacodynamic booster in cART.","Indinavir can cause nephrolithiasis when adequate hydration is not maintained.","Ritonavir is a potent inhibitor of the CYP3A4 isoform of P450 &amp; may alter levels of drugs."],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of adverse effects of gastrointestinal tolerance and fat redistribution due to protease inhibitors. HAART therapy includes this class of drugs along with 2 nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors +/- NNRTIs. Ritonavir, not tipranavir, is used in sub-therapeutic doses as a pharmacodynamic booster of other protease inhibitors in cART.\n\nThis is because ritonavir is a potent inhibitor of the CYP3A4 isoform of P450. It inhibits their CYP3A-mediated metabolism and permits the use of lower doses of other protease inhibitors. Nelfinavir is the only protease inhibitor not boosted by ritonavir.\n\nIndinavir is notable for its tendency to precipitate in the urinary tract, causing nephrolithiasis, unless the patient maintains a high state of hydration.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"An AIDS patient on treatment with AZT, lamivudine, indinavir, ketoconazole, and cotrimoxazole, develops breast hypertrophy, central adiposity, hyperlipidemia, insulin resistance, and nephrolithiasis. Which of the following drugs could have caused these?","o":["Azidothymidine","Indinavir","Ketoconazole","Lamivudine"],"a":1,"e":"Indinavir, a protease inhibitor, is the most likely cause for these adverse effects.\n\nPatients being treated with protease inhibitors (e.g., indinavir) develop a syndrome involving the derangement of lipid metabolism. This causes changes in body fat distribution. Type 2 diabetes mellitus has also been reported. This is called HIV/HAART-associated lipodystrophy.\n\nIndinavir can also precipitate in the urinary tract, causing nephrolithiasis, unless the patient is maintained in a high state of hydration.\n\nIndinavir binds to the site of HIV-1 protease activity and inhibits cleavage of viral gag-pol polyprotein precursors into individual functional proteins required for infectious HIV. This results in the formation of immature, non-infectious viral particles.\n\nOption A: Azidothymidine or zidovudine, is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor. Chronic zidovudine administration has been associated with nail hyperpigmentation.\n\nOption C: Ketoconazole is an antifungal agent that inhibits fungal ergosterol synthesis. Its adverse effects include liver dysfunction and testosterone synthesis inhibition leading to gynecomastia.\n\nOption D: Lamivudine is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor. It is one of the least toxic antiretroviral drugs. Neutropenia, headache, and nausea have been reported at higher-than-recommended doses.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a monoclonal antibody approved for use in the treatment of HIV?","o":["Bevacizumab","Ibalizumab","Panitumumab","Pembrolizumab"],"a":1,"e":"Ibalizumab is an anti-CD4 monoclonal antibody approved for use in the treatment of multidrug-resistant HIV-1. It is administered intravenously.\n\nIt acts as an entry inhibitor. It blocks HIV-1 from infecting CD4+ T cells by binding to domain 2 of CD4 cells. It also interferes with post-attachment steps required for the entry of HIV-1 virus particles into host cells and thus prevents the viral transmission that occurs via cell-cell fusion.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an integrase inhibitor indicated in the treatment of patients with HIV?","o":["Lopinavir","Abacavir","Raltegravir","Ritonavir"],"a":2,"e":"The integrase inhibitor indicated in the treatment of HIV is raltegravir.\n\nIt inhibits the catalytic activity of the HIV-encoded integrase and prevents the integration of virus DNA into the host chromosome. It is eliminated primarily by glucuronidation by UGT1A1.\n\nThe other two FDA-approved integrase inhibitors are elvitegravir (EVG) and dolutegravir (DTG).\n\nOptions A and D: Lopinavir and ritonavir are protease inhibitors.\n\nOption B: Abacavir is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor.\n\nNote: Dolutegravir (DTG) is recommended as the preferred third drug for HIV post-exposure prophylaxis as per the recent MOHFW Jan 2020 guideline, in line with WHO.\n\nThe FDA approved dolutegravir tablets and suspension for the treatment of HIV-1 infection in pediatric patients at least four weeks old and weighing at least 3 kg who have never been treated for HIV or who have been treated, but not with an integrase inhibitor class of drug.\n\nWHO recently recommended long-acting Cabotegravir, an HIV integrase strand transfer inhibitor may be offered as a HIV prevention option for people at substantial risk of HIV infection. It is an IM injectable form of pre-exposure prophylaxis that has been shown to be highly effective at reducing the risk of HIV acquisition. The first 2 injections were administered 4 weeks apart, followed thereafter by an injection every 8 weeks.","t":"Antiretroviral Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following organisms is most sensitive to penicillin?","o":["Staphylococcus","Streptococcus","Pneumococcus","Treponema pallidum"],"a":3,"e":"Among the given organisms, Treponema pallidum is most sensitive to penicillin. Penicillin G remains the drug of choice for the treatment of syphilis.\n\nStaphylococcus, Streptococcus, and Pneumococcus have developed variable resistance to penicillin G and penicillin G is no longer considered the drug of choice for most of the infections caused by them.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about penicillin-binding proteins is false?","o":["Present within cell membrane of bacteria","Mutation in the PBPs gives rise to resistance","PBPs are involved in protein synthesis","These are targeted by imipenem"],"a":2,"e":"The targets for the action of β-lactam antibiotics are collectively termed penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) and they are not involved in protein synthesis.\n\nTranspeptidase enzyme and related proteins constitute the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) which are present in the bacterial cell membrane. Transpepetidase is involved in the synthesis of peptidoglycan of the bacterial cell wall. All β-lactam groups of antibiotics bind to PBPs and inhibit the last step in peptidoglycan synthesis.\n\nMutation in the different PBPs confers resistance to the bacteria against the beta-lactams. Methicillin resistance in Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) occurs due to alteration in the PBPs.\n\nImipenem targets the PBPs, disrupting cell wall synthesis and causing death of the micro-organism.\n\nBeta-lactam group of antibiotics include:\n\n• Penicillins\n\n• Cephalosporins\n\n• Carbapenems\n\n• Monobactam","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is penicillin G is not used as treatment?","o":["38-year-old man with bacterial meningitis","23-year-old trekker with scrub typhus","28-year-old pregnant lady with syphilis","30-year-old police officer infected with anthrax"],"a":1,"e":"Penicillin G is not used against rickettsial infections like scrub typhus, and tetracyclines are the drug of choice in this condition.\n\nPenicillins are ineffective against amebae, plasmodia, rickettsiae, fungi or viruses.\n\nUses of penicillin are:\n\n• Prophylaxis of rheumatic carditis (benzathine penicillin)\n\n• Syphilis\n\n• Leptospirosis\n\n• Cervicofacial actinomycosis\n\n• Rat-bite fever\n\n• Meningococcal meningitis\n\n• Anthrax\n\n• Listeria","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is Penicillin G not the drug of choice?","o":["Actinomycosis","Syphilis","Lyme disease","Rat-bite fever"],"a":2,"e":"Penicillin G is not the drug of choice for Lyme disease, but is an alternative to doxycycline, amoxixilline and cefuroxime in the treatment of Lyme disease.\n\nPenicillin G is the drug of choice for all forms of actinomycosis, syphilis and rat-bite fever.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about penicillins?","o":["Penicillin V is absorbed orally","Benzathine penicillin is short acting penicillin","Cloxacillin is beta lactamase and acid resistant","Ampicillin is not resistant to beta lactamases"],"a":1,"e":"Benzathine penicillin G and procaine penicillin G are repository preparations of penicillin G and are long-acting.\n\nThey are given as deep I.M. injection. Benzathine penicillin has the longest half-life and is administered every 2-4 weeks.\n\nPenicillin V is an acid-resistant penicillin preparation that is administered orally.\n\nAmpicillin has an extended spectrum but is not resistant to beta-lactamases.\n\nPenicillinase-resistant penicillins are:\n\n• Cloxacillin\n\n• Dicloxacillin\n\n• Flucloxacillin\n\n• Methicillin\n\n• Nafcillin","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is prescribed probenecid along with penicillin. What is the rationale behind this?","o":["It is a beta lactamase inhibitor","It increases the duration of action of penicillins","It increases the excretion of penicillins in kidney","It decreases the metabolism of penicllins in liver"],"a":1,"e":"Probenecid is used along with penicillin because it increases the duration of action of penicillin.\n\nPenicillin is excreted by tubular secretion in the kidney. Probenecid is a uricosuric drug used in gout that competes with the same transporter (OATP: organic anion transporter) used by penicillin for tubular secretion. Therefore, it decreases the rate of excretion and increases the duration of action of penicillin.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The culture and sensitivity report from a wound swab is positive for penicillinase-producing Staphylococcus aureus. Which of the following antimicrobials can be administered?","o":["Amoxicillin","Penicillin G","Piperacillin","Cloxacillin"],"a":3,"e":"The drug that can be used here is cloxacillin since it is not penicillinase susceptible. It is penicillinase-resistant penicillin.\n\nBacteria can inactivate β-lactam antibiotics by the action of the β-lactamase enzyme. The β-lactamases enzyme activity can be narrow or broad spectrum. In general, gram-positive bacteria produce and secrete a large amount of β-lactamase. Most of these enzymes are penicillinases.\n\nPenicillinase-resistant penicillins are:\n\n• Cloxacillin\n\n• Dicloxacillin\n\n• Flucloxacillin\n\n• Methicillin\n\n• Nafcillin\n\nOption B: Penicillin G encompasses the natural preparations and salts of penicillin. They have a narrow spectrum and are susceptible to penicillinase.\n\nOptions A and C: Amoxicillin and piperacillin are extended-spectrum penicillins but are susceptible to penicillinase.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an extended spectrum penicillin?","o":["Ampicillin","Cloxacillin","Carbenicillin","Piperacillin"],"a":1,"e":"Cloxacillin is not extended-spectrum penicillin. It is penicillinase-resistant penicillin.\n\nExtended-spectrum penicillins have greater activity than penicillin against gram-negative bacteria because of their enhanced ability to penetrate the Gram-negative outer membrane.\n\nExtended-spectrum penicillins are:\n\n• Aminopenicillins - ampicillin, amoxicillin\n\n• Carboxypenicillins - carbenicillin, ticarcillin\n\n• Ureidopenicillins - piperacillin, mezlocillin","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 22-year-old woman presented with dysuria and high-grade fever. Urine culture sensitivity shows the growth of carbenicillin-sensitive E. coli. Which of the following is true about the antibiotic?","o":["Is effective in Pseudomonas infection","Has no effect in Proteus infection","Is a ureidopenicillins antibiotic","Is administered orally"],"a":0,"e":"Carbenicillin is effective in Pseudomonas infection.\n\nIt is extended-spectrum penicillin belonging to the carboxypenicillins group. It has activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and indole-positive Proteus. It is acid labile and is not administered orally.\n\nAntipseudomonal penicillins are:\n\n• Carboxypenicillins - carbenicillin, ticarcillin\n\n• Ureidopenicillins - mezlocillin, piperacillin","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not a beta-lactamase enzyme inhibitor?","o":["Tazobactam","Clavulanic acid","Piperacillin","Avibactam"],"a":2,"e":"Piperacillin is not a beta-lactamase enzyme inhibitor. It is an extended-spectrum beta-lactam (ureidopenicillin) drug having significant antipseudomonal action.\n\nBeta-lactamases are a family of enzymes produced by many gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria that inactivate beta-lactam antibiotics by opening the beta-lactam ring.\n\nBeta-lactamase inhibitors available for clinical use are:\n\n• Clavulanic acid\n\n• Sulbactam\n\n• Tazobactam\n\n• Avibactam","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an acid-susceptible penicillin?","o":["Methicillin","Ampicillin","Amoxicillin","Cloxacillin"],"a":0,"e":"Methicillin and nafcillin are penicillinase-resistant penicillins that are acid susceptible and are administered parenterally.\n\nMethicillin use is associated with higher incidence of interstitial nephritis.\n\nAmpicillin, amoxicillin and cloxacillin are acid-resistant penicillins.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who is being treated with an antimicrobial agent presents with neutropenia. Which of the following drugs could be the most likely cause?","o":["Nafcillin","Methicillin","Carbenicillin","Ampicllin"],"a":0,"e":"Neutropenia is seen with nafcillin. It is penicillinase-resistant parenteral penicillin.\n\nIt is associated with side effects like neutropenia and interstitial nephritis. In patients with renal failure, it is associated with seizures.\n\nMethicillin is also a penicillinase-resistant penicillin. Its use is associated with a higher incidence of interstitial nephritis than other penicillins, hence it is no longer used.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old man undergoing treatment with penicillin presents with fever, myalgia, and exacerbation of pre-existing cutaneous lesions. Which of the following diseases is he most probably being treated for?","o":["Rheumatic endocarditis","Gonorrhoea","Syphilis","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction and it is associated with the use of penicillin in syphilis.\n\nJarisch-Herxheimer reaction is a host immune reaction due to the release of spirochetal antigen by the dying T.pallidum in patients on treatment with penicillin. This occurs in primary and most cases of secondary syphilis and is characterized by fever, shivering, myalgia, exacerbation of lesions and vascular collapse. Symptomatic treatment suffices and penicillin treatment need not be interrupted.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who is undergoing treatment with antibiotics presents with diarrhea, fever, and leukocytosis. Clostridium difficile associated diarrhea is suspected. This condition is most commonly associated with the use of which of the following group of drugs?","o":["Aminopenicillins","Tetracyclines","Carbapenems","Macrolide"],"a":0,"e":"Clostridium difficile associated diarrhea is mostly due to aminopenicillins.\n\nAntibiotic-associated colitis is caused by Clostridium difficile infection due to the destruction of the normal gut microbial flora by the use of antibiotics.\n\nAlthough all antibiotics can cause the condition, the most commonly implicated are:\n\n• Ampicillin (aminopenicillin)\n\n• Clindamycin\n\n• Third-generation cephalosporins\n\n• Fluoroquinolones.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient being treated with ampicillin presents with diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever. C. difficile infection is suspected. Which of the following monoclonal antibodies is approved for use in this patient?","o":["Actoxumab","Bezlotoxumab","Bevacizumab","Adalimumab"],"a":1,"e":"Bezlotoxumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds to Clostridium difficile toxin B, indicated to reduce the recurrence of Clostridium difficile infection (CDI).\n\nActoxumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds to Clostridium difficile toxin A but it is not approved because of lack of efficacy.\n\nAdalimumab is an anti-TNF alpha antibody used in rheumatoid arthritis and various other conditions.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Systemic use of bacitracin is restricted due to:","o":["Ototoxicity","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Pulmonary toxicity"],"a":1,"e":"The systemic use of bacitracin is restricted due to nephrotoxicity.\n\nBacitracin is a polypeptide antibiotic and it inhibits the synthesis of the bacterial cell wall.\n\nCurrently, it is used topically for dermal infections and for ophthalmic infections. Parenteral use of this antibiotic leads to nephrotoxicity.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Daptomycin is not indicated in which of the following conditions?","o":["Complicated skin and soft-tissue infections","Complicated bacteremia","Community acquired pneumonia","Right-sided endocarditis"],"a":2,"e":"Daptomycin is not indicated in community-acquired pneumonia because even though it penetrates adequately into the lung, it is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant and thus is not useful.\n\nDaptomycin is a cyclic lipopeptide antibiotic. It acts by depolarising bacterial membranes resulting in loss of membrane potential and cell death.\n\nDaptomycin is indicated in the following infections:\n\n• Complicated skin and soft-tissue infections\n\n• Complicated bacteremia caused by staphylococcus and streptococcus\n\n• Right-sided endocarditis\n\n• Vancomycin-resistant enterococcal infections\n\nIts efficacy in the above-mentioned conditions is comparable to that of vancomycin.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Polymyxins are effective against:","o":["Gram-positive bacteria","Gram-negative bacteria","Acid-fast bacilli","Anaerobic bacteria"],"a":1,"e":"Polymyxins are effective against gram-negative bacteria.\n\nPolymyxin group of antibiotics includes polymyxin B and colistin which have their antimicrobial spectrum limited primarily to gram-negative aerobic bacteria. They interact with the phospholipid of the bacterial cell membranes and disrupt it.\n\nPolymyxins are used in various topical formulations. Parenteral formulations are used only in serious systemic infections that are resistant to other antimicrobials. Nephrotoxicity limits widespread systemic use.","t":"Penicillins","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a first-generation cephalosporin?","o":["Cefadroxil","Cefazolin","Cephalexin","Cefaclor"],"a":3,"e":"Cefaclor is not a first-generation cephalosporin. It is a second-generation oral cephalosporin.\n\nFirst-generation cephalosporins are :\n\n• Oral formulation\n\n• Cefadroxil\n\n• Cephalexin\n\n• Cephradine\n\n• Parenteral formulation - cefazolin","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is undergoing orthopedic surgery. Which of the following first-generation cephalosporins can you use for surgical prophylaxis?","o":["Cephalexin","Cefadroxil","Cephradine","Cefazolin"],"a":3,"e":"The first-generation cephalosporin indicated for surgical prophylaxis is Cefazolin. It is the preferred agent for surgical prophylaxis when skin flora is the likely pathogen.\n\nCephalexin, cefadroxil, and cephradine are first-generation oral cephalosporins.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"You want to prescribe an oral second-generation cephalosporin to a patient with pneumonia. Which of the following drugs will you choose?","o":["Cefepime","Cephradine","Cefaclor","Cefadroxil"],"a":2,"e":"Cefaclor is a second-generation oral cephalosporin.\n\nOption A: Cefepime is a fourth-generation cephalosporin.\n\nOptions B and D: Cefadroxil and Cephradine are oral first-generation cephalosporins.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"In comparison with first-generation cephalosporins, second-generation cephalosporins have which of the following properties?","o":["Greater activity against gram positive bacteria","Lesser activity against gram positive bacteria","Lesser activity against anaerobes","Lesser hepatotoxicity"],"a":1,"e":"Second-generation cephalosporins have lesser activity against gram-positive bacteria when compared with first-generation cephalosporins.\n\nProperties of second-generation cephalosporins, when compared to first-generation cephalosporins, are:\n\n• Lesser activity against gram-positive bacteria\n\n• Broader gram-negative activity against Hemophilus species, indole-positive Proteus, and Klebsiella species\n\n• Anaerobes like B.fragilis are susceptible to second-generation cephalosporins like cefoxitin and cefotetan but not first-generation cephalosporins","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a third-generation cephalosporin that can be given orally?","o":["Cefixime","Cefotaxime","Ceftriaxone","Cefoperazone"],"a":0,"e":"Cefixime is a third-generation oral cephalosporin.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Cephalosporins are not useful in which of the following infections?","o":["Gonorrhea","Lyme disease","Listeriosis","Pseudomonas infections"],"a":2,"e":"Ampicillin is the drug of choice for listeriosis, which is caused by Listeria monocytogenes, and cephalosporins are not useful in treating this condition.\n\nCeftriaxone is a third-generation cephalosporin, which is considered as a treatment of choice for gonorrhea and severe forms of Lyme disease.\n\nCefoperazone and ceftazidime are third-generation cephalosporins that have clinically significant activity against Pseudomonas.\n\nCephalosporins\nTherapeutic uses\n\nFirst-generation\n\n• Skin and soft tissue infections\n\n• Serious infections due to MSSA\n\n• Perioperative surgical prophylaxis\n\nSecond generation\n\n• Upper respiratory tract infections (sinusitis, otitis media)\n\n• Cefoxitin/cefotetan - gynecologic infections, perioperative surgical prophylaxis\n\nThird generation\n\n• Community-acquired pneumonia, meningitis, urinary tract infections\n\n• Streptococcal endocarditis\n\n• Gonorrhea\n\n• Severe Lyme disease","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"The pus culture and sensitivity report of a hospitalized patient with diabetic ulcer is positive for pseudomonas. Which of the following cephalosporins can be used in this patient?","o":["Cefpodoxime proxetil","Cephradine","Cefotetan","Ceftazidime"],"a":3,"e":"The drug that can be used in this patient is Ceftazidime, which is a third-generation parenteral cephalosporin having significant anti-pseudomonal action.\n\nCefpodoxime proxetil is a third-generation oral cephalosporin. It does not have significant antipseudomonal action.\n\nCephradine and cefotetan are first and second-generation cephalosporins with no antipseudomonal action.\n\nAntipseudomonal cell wall synthesis inhibitors are:\n\n• Cephalosporins:\n\n• Third generation - Ceftazidime, Cefoperazone\n\n• Fourth generation - Cefepime and cefpirome. They are also used against enterobacter\n\n• Fifth generation - Ceftolozane\n\n• Extended-spectrum penicillins:\n\n• Carboxypenicillins - carbenicillin, ticarcillin\n\n• Ureidopenicillins - piperacillin, mezlocillin","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about cefepime is false?","o":["Fourth generation cephalosporin","Dose should be reduced in renal failure","Possesses antipseudomonal activity","Has good activity against MRSA"],"a":3,"e":"Cefepime, which is a fourth-generation cephalosporin, has good activity against Pseudomonas, Enterobacter, and S. aureus, but not against MRSA. It is effective in serious infections like hospital-acquired pneumonia, septicemia and febrile neutropenia.\n\nCefepime is excreted primarily through kidneys and dose reduction is necessary for renal failure. Encephalopathy and non-convulsive status epilepticus have been reported with the use of cefepime.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with a discharging wound post-operatively. Cultures reveal infection with MRSA. Which of the following cephalosporins can be used for this patient?","o":["Cefepime","Cefpirome","Ceftolozane","Ceftaroline"],"a":3,"e":"Ceftaroline is the cephalosporin that is indicated for MRSA infections and hence can be used in this patient. It is considered as anti-MRSA cephalosporin and along with ceftobiprole is grouped as fifth-generation cephalosporins.\n\nCeftaroline has gram-negative and also enhanced gram-positive coverage. It is effective against MRSA and penicillin-resistant S. pneumoniae.\n\nCefepime and cefpirome are fourth-generation cephalosporins with no significant action on MRSA.\n\nCeftolozane is a fifth-generation antipseudomonal cephalosporin with no action on MRSA.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is to be started on a cephalosporin antibiotic. His serum creatinine levels yesterday at the time of admission was 1.1 mg/dl and now it is 1.5 mg/dl. Which of the following cephalosporins can be started without dose reduction in this patient?","o":["Cefuroxime","Cefoperazone","Ceftazidime","Cefotaxime"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of acute kidney injury and the cephalosporin that does not require dose reduction in this patient is cefoperazone.\n\nAll cephalosporins are usually excreted by glomerular filtration and tubular secretion. They need a dose reduction in renal failure, except cefpiramide and cefoperazone that have predominant biliary excretion.\n\nDose reduction is necessary for cefoperazone and cefpiramide in patients with hepatic impairment.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the drug of choice for a patient who presents with an initial episode of Clostridium difficile infection?","o":["Metronidazole","Vancomycin","Fidaxomicin","Loracarbef"],"a":2,"e":"The drug of choice for the treatment of initial episode of Clostridium difficile infection (CDI) is fidaxomicin. It is a macrocyclic antibiotic.\n\nFidaxomicin is preferred because of its safety profile and higher beneficial effects. But in resource-limited settings where it is not available, vancomycin can be used for the treatment of this condition.\n\nTreatment of Clostridium difficile infection:\n\n• Fidaxomicin\n\n• Vancomycin\n\n• Metronidazole\n\nVancomycin and fidaxomicin are more effective than metronidazole. All of the above drugs are given orally.\n\nLoracarbef is a second-generation cephalosporin and is not indicated in pseudomembranous colitis caused by Clostridium difficile.\n\nNOTE:\n\nAs per the Infectious Disease Society of America (IDSA) management guidelines 2021, Fidaxomicin is now preferred over Vancomycin for the treatment of initial episode of Clostridium difficile infection. Vancomycin can be used as an alternative drug.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Oral vancomycin is used in the treatment of :\n\n1. C. difficile associated diarrhea\n\n2. Pneumococcal pneumonia\n\n3. Staphylococcal enterocolitis\n\n4. Wound infection caused by MRSA\n\n5. Staphylococcal endocarditis","o":["1, 4 and 5","1, 2 and 4","1 and 3","3 and 5"],"a":2,"e":"Oral vancomycin is used in the treatment of Clostridium difficile associated diarrhea and staphylococcal enterocolitis.\n\nIts oral formulation is not absorbed systemically and hence is used for local action in the gastrointestinal tract.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on intravenous vancomycin develops flushing and erythematous lesions. On examination, tachycardia, and hypotension are noted. By what mechanism does vancomycin cause these effects?","o":["Allergic reaction","Direct toxic effect on mast cells","Histamine antagonist action","Immune-complex deposition"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario depicts red man syndrome associated with vancomycin. It occurs due to a direct toxic effect on mast cells resulting in the release of histamine.\n\nRed man or red neck syndrome is a non-allergic reaction due to rapid intravenous infusion of vancomycin characterized by:\n\n• Flushing\n\n• Erythematous, urticarial reactions\n\n• Tachycardia\n\n• Hypotension\n\nSlow infusion, prophylactic use of antihistamines can reduce the incidence.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Match the following bacteria with the drug of choice for each.\n\nBacteria\nDrug\n\nA. Bacteroides\n1. Vancomycin\n\nB. MRSA\n2. Metronidazole\n\nC. Hemophilus\n3. Meropenem\n\nD. Klebsiella\n4. Penicillin","o":["A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2","A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3"],"a":1,"e":"Vancomycin is the drug of choice for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA). It should be used only if the organism is resistant to other drugs.\n\nMetronidazole is the drug of choice for anaerobic infections caused by Bacteroides.\n\nMetronidazole is used in the treatment of the following conditions:\n\n• Giardiasis\n\n• Trichomoniasis\n\n• Amoebiasis\n\n• Bacterial vaginosis\n\n• Pseudomembranous colitis\n\nMeropenem is used in infections caused by ESBL producing bacteria like Klebsiella.\n\nCarbapenems are not hydrolyzed by ESBL (extended-spectrum beta-lactamases). Therefore, carbapenems like imipenem, meropenem and ertapenem are resistant to hydrolysis by ESBLs. They are indicated in the treatment of infections caused by ESBL producing bacteria, like Klebsiella and E. coli.\n\nPenicillins like amoxicillin, ampicillin can be used in H. influenzae infections.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient allergic to penicillin requires antibiotic therapy. Which of the following can you safely prescribe?","o":["Aztreonam","Cefepime","Imipenem","Ceftriaxone"],"a":0,"e":"Aztreonam can be safely given to a patient with a history of allergy to penicillin. It is a monobactam group of β-Lactam antibiotics resistant to narrow-spectrum beta-lactamase produced by bacteria.\n\nIt lacks cross-sensitivity with the other beta-lactams, except for ceftazidime. It has activity only against gram-negative bacteria. It has no activity against gram-positive bacteria and anaerobes.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false about extended-spectrum beta-lactamases?","o":["They can hydrolyse penicillins, cephalosporins as well as monobactam","Carbapenems are hydrolysed by ESBL","Ambler classification of ESBL is based on amino acid sequence","Third and fourth generation cephalosporins are used for detection of ESBL"],"a":1,"e":"Carbapenems like imipenem, meropenem, ertapenem are resistant to hydrolysis by extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBL) and hence are indicated in the treatment of infections caused by ESBL producing bacteria.\n\nBeta-lactamases are enzymes produced by bacteria that are able to hydrolyze antibiotics and make them ineffective, thus making the bacteria resistant to these antibiotics. They confer resistance against:\n\n• Penicillins\n\n• Cephalosporins\n\n• Monobactams\n\nBeta lactamases hydrolyze first and second-generation cephalosporins but are ineffective against third and fourth-generation.\n\nBeta lactamases are classified into four categories based on their amino acid sequences, by the Ambler classification.\n\nESBLs also hydrolyze third and fourth-generation cephalosporins - cefotaxime, ceftazidime, ceftriaxone and cefepime.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a complicated urinary tract infection is put on a newly approved combination of meropenem and a non-suicidal beta-lactamase inhibitor. Identify this drug.","o":["Sulbactam","Tazobactam","Vaborbactam","Clavulanic acid"],"a":2,"e":"Vaborbactam is a recently approved non-suicidal beta-lactamase inhibitor. It is indicated in combination with meropenem for complicated urinary tract infections.\n\nSulbactam, tazobactam and clavulanic acid are suicidal inhibitors of beta-lactamase enzyme.\n\nSuicidal inhibitors are irreversible inhibitors that can permanently inactivate the β-lactamase through secondary chemical reactions in the enzyme active site.","t":"Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following best describes pyronaridine?","o":["Antimalarial","Antiretroviral","Antifungal","Antibacterial"],"a":0,"e":"Pyronaridine is an antimalarial agent. It is an erythrocyte schizonticide with high efficacy and mechanism of action similar to chloroquine.\n\nIt is used in combination with artesunate.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs gets deposited in the retina?","o":["Chlortetracycline","Chloramphenicol","Chloroquine","Chlordiazepoxide"],"a":2,"e":"Chloroquine has a large volume of distribution and is deposited in the retina.\n\nLong-term use of chloroquine results in retinopathy. The classical finding is 'bull’s-eye retinopathy'","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman presented with itching and malodorous discharge in the vagina. Whiff's test was positive and microscopy of the discharge is shown below. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","o":["Ampicillin","Ciprofloxacin","Metronidazole","Clotrimazole"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical scenario, a positive Whiff test and clue cells on microscopy point towards a diagnosis of bacterial vaginosis. Metronidazole (oral or intravaginal) or intravaginal clindamycin are considered the drugs of choice.\n\nThe male partner should be treated in case of recurrent infections of bacterial vaginosis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4449bab169741b798f3407876a157bf.JPEG"],"t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is best associated with lumefantrine?","o":["Antimycobacterial","Antifungal","Antimalarial","Antiameobic"],"a":2,"e":"Lumefantrine is an antimalarial agent.\n\nLumefantrine is an orally active, high efficacy; long-acting erythrocytic schizonticide related structurally and acts similar to halofantrine.\n\nLumefantrine is used only in combination with artemether.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Suramin is indicated in which of the following conditions?","o":["African trypanosomiasis","American trypanosomiasis","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","HIV infection"],"a":0,"e":"Suramin is primarily indicated in the treatment of African trypanosomiasis. It is indicated only in the early stages of African trypanosomiasis because of its limited entry across the blood-brain barrier.\n\nIt has no clinical utility in American trypanosomiasis. Suramin has in vitro activity against retroviral reverse transcriptase but is ineffective in HIV infection.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with watery diarrhea, fever and abdominal pain. Investigations reveal a WBC count of 16,000 cells/microL. The patient has been on treatment with ampicillin for a few days. Which of the following antibiotics is approved for use in this condition?","o":["Azithromycin","Roxithromycin","Fidaxomicin","Clindamycin"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of pseudomembranous colitis caused by Clostridium difficile and fidaxomicin is a macrocyclic antibiotic that is indicated as an alternative to vancomycin in severe and recurring Clostridium difficile infections.\n\nIt acts by inhibiting protein synthesis through the blockade of RNA polymerase.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following best describes tolnaftate?","o":["Antifungal agent","Antiviral agent","Antimalarial agent","Antileprotic agent"],"a":0,"e":"Tolnaftate is a topical synthetic antifungal agent.\n\nIt distorts the mycelial growth of yeast-like fungi and molds. It is effective in the treatment of dermatomycosis and tinea.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A class of antibiotics that is clinically indicated in combination with penicillins is inactivated if combined with penicillins in the same solution before administration. Identify this class of drugs.","o":["Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides","Macrolides","Glycopeptides"],"a":1,"e":"Aminoglycosides can be inactivated by various penicillins in vitro and thus should not be admixed in solution before administration.\n\nAminoglycosides and penicillin combination is indicated in several infections and they should not be admixed in the same solution before administration.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antibiotics is not excreted through bile?","o":["Erythromycin","Cefoperazone","Rifampicin","Gentamicin"],"a":3,"e":"Gentamicin is not excreted through bile. It is an aminoglycoside antibiotic and aminoglycosides undergo minimum metabolism and are excreted almost entirely by the renal route.\n\nErythromycin, cefoperazone, and rifampicin are excreted predominantly in bile.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Rifampicin can be used as first-line therapy in which of the following patients?\n\n1. 23-year old with pulmonary tuberculosis\n\n2. 55-year-old with paucibacillary leprosy\n\n3. 19-year-old with chlamydia urethreitis\n\n4. 40-year-old milkmaid with brucellosis\n\n5. 45-year-old with visceral leishmaniasis","o":["1, 3 and 5","1, 2 , 3 and 4","1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4"],"a":3,"e":"Rifampicin is the first-line therapy and is included in the regimens for both tuberculosis and leprosy.\n\nDoxycycline combined with rifampicin is considered a first-line agent for brucellosis.","t":"Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","c":"Antibacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old woman presents with malodorous vaginal discharge and dysuria. The examination finding is shown below. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","o":["Tinidazole","Mebendazole","Metronidazole","Secnidazole"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario and image showing strawberry vagina and cervix are suggestive of trichomoniasis. The drug of choice is metronidazole. It is given as a single dose of 2g.\n\nBoth metronidazole and tinidazole are effective in the management of trichomonas vaginitis. Tinidazole has been reported to have fewer GI-related side effects and less resistance compared to metronidazole. However, it is much more expensive and is preferred in cases refractory to metronidazole.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c523b61be2394126be693562e756acb1.PNG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Metronidazole is selectively active against anaerobic and microaerophilic organisms because:","o":["Aerobes have oxygen gradient inside the cell which deactivates metronidazole moiety","Only anaerobes and microaerophilic organisms reduce it to generate reactive nitro radicals","Aerobes have efflux drug pumping mechanism for metronidazole","Metronidazole inserts pore in the cell membrane of anaerobes and microaerophilic bacteria"],"a":1,"e":"Metronidazole is selectively active against anaerobic and microaerophilic organisms because only these organisms reduce it to generate reactive nitro radicals.\n\nThey contain electron transport components that donate an electron to metronidazole. This results in the formation of a highly reactive nitro radical anion that targets DNA and kills the organism.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A child presents with diarrhea, weight loss, and abdominal pain. His mother reports stools to be greasy and foul-smelling. A wet preparation of the stool sample is shown below. What is first-line therapy for this condition?","o":["Metronidazole","Tinidazole","Albendazole","Mebendazole"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical scenario and the presence of cysts of Giardia lamblia in stool point towards a diagnosis of giardiasis, and tinidazole is the treatment of choice for this condition.\n\nBoth metronidazole and tinidazole are considered first-line drugs however, tinidazole is considered superior in its efficacy in comparison to metronidazole.\n\nDrug dosage:\n\n• Metronidazole: 250- 500mg TID orally for 7 days\n\n• Tinidazole: 2g single dose orally\n\nThe cyst of Giardia is small and oval, and is surrounded by a hyaline cyst wall. Its internal structure includes two pairs of nuclei grouped at one end. A young cyst contains one pair of nuclei. The axostyle lies diagonally, forming a dividing line within the cyst wall.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb3b13512bb34b10ab981d21bb9b7b5e.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Metronidazole is least likely to be effective in the treatment of:","o":["Hepatic amebiasis","B. fragilis infection","Pseudomembranous colitis","Pneumocystosis"],"a":3,"e":"Metronidazole is not effective in the management of pneumocystosis caused by Pneumocystis jirovecii. Co-trimoxazole is used in the management of this condition.\n\nMetronidazole is used in the following conditions:\n\n• Giardiasis\n\n• Trichomoniasis\n\n• Amoebiasis\n\n• Bacterial vaginosis\n\n• Anaerobic infections - bacteroides and clostridium.\n\n• Pseudomembranous colitis (alternative to vancomycin & fidaxomicin)","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 6-year-old boy presents with lower abdominal pain and diarrhea. The stool examination finding is shown below. Which of the following drugs cannot be used for the eradication of intestinal cysts in this condition?","o":["Diloxanide furoate","Nitazoxanide","Metronidazole","Iodoquinol"],"a":2,"e":"The image shows hematophagous trophozoite of Entamoeba histolytica and metronidazole is not an effective agent against cysts of amoeba in the colon.\n\nMetronidazole is used in the treatment of amoebiasis (amoebic colitis, amoebic liver abscess). It is well absorbed from the small intestine and only a small amount of the unabsorbed drug reaches the colon, which is insufficient to kill the cysts.\n\nHence, luminal agents like diloxanide furoate, nitazoxanide or iodoquinol are required for the eradication of cysts and trophozoites in the intestine.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5cef49282af74bb78d5fb089d463a184.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is used in the treatment of a patient with asymptomatic intestinal amoebiasis?","o":["Metronidazole","Tinidazole","Paromomycin","Tetracycline"],"a":2,"e":"The drug of choice for the treatment of asymptomatic intestinal luminal amoebiasis due to E. histolytica is paromomycin.\n\nParomomycin (aminosidine) is an aminoglycoside that has poor oral absorption and hence it acts only in the intestinal lumen as a luminal amebicide. It has no effect against extraintestinal organisms.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 21-year-old male presented to the OPD complaining of blood-streaked stools. Sigmoidoscopy was done and it showed ulcers in the sigmoid. Biopsy was taken from this area and the histopathological image is given below. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Intravenous ceftriaxone","Intravenous metronidazole","Intravenous paromomycin","Intravenous chloroquine"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical picture and the histopathological image showing flask-shaped ulcers are suggestive of intestinal amoebiasis. The most appropriate treatment is intravenous metronidazole.\n\nThe amoebic ulcer which is flask-shaped is the typical lesion seen in intestinal amoebiasis. The ulcers are multiple and are confined to the colon. Sometimes, in secondary intestinal lesions, the ulcers are numerous in the cecum, followed by the sigmoido-rectal region.\n\nThree classes of drugs are used in the treatment of amebiasis:\n\n• Luminal amoebicides- diloxanide furoate, iodoquinol, paromomycin, and tetracycline. They act in the intestinal lumen but not in tissues.\n\n• Tissue amoebicides- emetine and chloroquine. They are effective in systemic infection but less effective in the intestine.\n\n• Both luminal and tissue amebicides- Metronidazole, tinidazole and imidazole. They act on both sites and are the drug of choice for treating amebic colitis and amebic liver abscess.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f1cab2058f1b48d1a3c05ba381c0b080.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with sudden onset fever with chills and malaise. On examination splenomegaly was present. Bone marrow picture is shown in the image. Which of the following drugs is not used in the treatment of this condition?","o":["Paromomycin","Liposomal amphotericin B","Hydroxychloroquine","Miltefosine"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario and the bone marrow picture showing macrophages infected with intracellular Leishman-Donovan bodies point towards a diagnosis of visceral leishmaniasis. Hydroxychloroquine is not indicated in the treatment of this condition.\n\nThe drugs effective in visceral leishmaniasis are:\n\n• Liposomal amphotericin B (preferred)\n\n• Sodium stibogluconate (not in India)\n\n• Miltefosine\n\n• Paromomycin\n\n• Pentamidine (rarely used)\n\nHydroxychloroquine is an antimalarial agent and is also used in the treatment of dermatological conditions like:\n\n• Cutaneous lupus erythematosus\n\n• Cutaneous dermatomyositis\n\n• Polymorphous light eruption\n\n• Porphyria cutanea tarda\n\n• Sarcoidosis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1bb4ee6b8c0d45ee9eb3fbb5e401becb.PNG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old woman presents with the finding as shown in the image. She gives history of travel to South America and was bitten by a reduvid bug. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this condition?","o":["Nifurtimox","Albendazole","Benznidazole","Metronidazole"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario and the image showing Romaña sign point towards a diagnosis of Chagas disease (American trypanosomiasis) and the drug of choice in this condition is benznidazole.\n\nAmerican trypanosomiasis is caused by Trypanosoma cruzi and can be managed by either benznidazole or nifurtimox. Due to its favorable safety profile, benznidazole is considered the drug of choice.\n\nRomaña sign is the unilateral painless periorbital swelling associated with the acute stage of Chagas disease.\n\nAlbendazole and metronidazole are not indicated for American trypanosomiasis. Albendazole is used in infections with Ascaris lumbricoides, Trichuris trichiura and hookworms. Metronidazole is used in the treatment of trichomoniasis and amebiasis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72c232f192204c96995fdc21466c7342.PNG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents in the late stage of West African sleeping sickness. What is the drug of choice in this condition?","o":["Nifurtimox","Nifurtimox - eflornithine combination","Nifurtimox - melarsoprol combination","Melarsoprol"],"a":1,"e":"Nifurtimox in combination with eflornithine is the drug of choice for late-stage West African sleeping sickness. It is caused by Trypanosoma brucei gambiense.\n\nNifurtimox alone is indicated in American trypanosomiasis.\n\nMelarsoprol is the drug of choice for late-stage East African (Rhodesian) trypanosomiasis. It is not indicated in combination with nifurtimox.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can be used as first-line treatment in patients with early-stage of East African trypanosomiasis?","o":["Suramin","Pentamidine","Melarsoprol","Eflornithine"],"a":0,"e":"Suramin is the first-line therapy for early-stage East African trypanosomiasis caused by Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense.\n\nIt is also active against Trypanosoma brucei gambiense but only indicated as a second-line agent when pentamidine fails or is contraindicated.\n\nMelarsoprol is the only effective drug available for the treatment of the late encephalopathic stage of East African sleeping sickness. It is caused by Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense. Melarsoprol is effective in earlier stages also but is avoided because of its toxicity.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"The microscopy of fecal sample of a patient showed presence of Ascaris lumbricoides eggs. This condition can most effectively be treated with which of the following drugs?","o":["Metronidazole","Mebendazole","Albendazole","Pyrantel pamoate"],"a":2,"e":"Roundworm infestation is most effectively treated with albendazole. It is considered as the drug of choice for the treatment of roundworm (Ascaris lumbricoides) infestation.\n\nAlbendazole is considered as the drug of choice for treatment of infestation by:\n\n• Whipworm - Trichuris trichura causing trichuriasis\n\n• Hookworm - Ancylostoma duodenale\n\n• Roundworm - Ascaris lumbricoides\n\n• Dog tapeworm - Echinococcus granulosus causing hydatid disease\n\nAlbendazole is administered as a single oral 400-mg dose to adults and children more than 2 years of age. Safety is not established for children less than 2 years. However, according to WHO, if the benefit outweighs the risk, albendazole at 200 mg can be given to children between the ages of 1-2years.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the primary mechanism of action of benzimidazole?","o":["Inhibition of microtubule polymerization","Stabilisation of microtubule polymers","Inhibition of protein synthesis","Paralysis of respiratory muscles"],"a":0,"e":"The primary mechanism of action of benzimidazoles is by inhibition of microtubule polymerization. Benzimidazoles bind to beta-tubulin and cause inhibition of microtubule polymerization.\n\nDrugs belonging to the benzimidazole group are:\n\n• Albendazole\n\n• Mebendazole\n\n• Triclabendazole\n\n• Thiabendazole.\n\nMechanisms of action:\n\n• Inhibition of mitochondrial fumarate reductase\n\n• Reduced glucose transport\n\n• Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young man working at a dairy farm presents with fever and abdominal pain. Examination of his stool samples revealed the finding shown in the image. Which of the following benzimidazoles is indicated in this condition?","o":["Albendazole","Mebendazole","Thiabendazole","Triclabendazole"],"a":3,"e":"The given image showing operculated egg (egg having a lid-like structure on its end) is suggestive of Fasciola hepatica and the benzimidazole indicated for infection with this organism is triclabendazole.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2618d24fe954bd2adf091cfa926b239.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"The MRI of a patient who presented with new-onset seizures is shown below. What is the drug of choice?","o":["Praziquantel","Albendazole","Levamisole","Pentamidine"],"a":1,"e":"The MRI findings (multiple cysticerci within the brain) are suggestive of neurocysticercosis and albendazole is the drug of choice in this condition.\n\nGlucocorticoid therapy is usually begun before initiating albendazole and continued for several days after the commencement of therapy. It reduces the inflammatory reaction to the dead and dying cysticerci of Taenia solium.\n\nNote: Even though Albendazole and Praziquantel are used as a combination, the single best answer choice to this question is Albendazole.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4d49b6d768c46c5a37a35ad696cc559.GIF"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old man hospitalized with a hydatid cyst refused to undergo surgical removal of the cyst. He was treated with high dose albendazole for 3 months. This patient should be monitored for toxicity to the:","o":["Liver","Kidney","Pancreas","Peripheral nerves"],"a":0,"e":"The patient described above should be monitored for toxicity to the liver. Liver dysfunction is one of the common adverse effects of albendazole therapy and liver function tests should be routinely conducted when long-term therapy is initiated.\n\nLong-term albendazole therapy is also associated with bone marrow toxicity. Therefore, blood counts should also be regularly monitored.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the drug of choice in patients with onchocerciasis?","o":["Albendazole","Praziquentel","Ivermectin","Diethylcarbamazine"],"a":2,"e":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for onchocerciasis caused by Oncocerca volvulus in adults and children aged more than 5 years.\n\nIvermectin is the drug of choice for:\n\n• Strongyloidiasis\n\n• Onchocerciasis\n\nIt is associated with Mazzotti reaction (fever, pruritic rash, lymphadenopathy) as a side effect when given for filariasis due to immune system activation by the dying parasite. This reaction is also seen with the use of diethylcarbamazine.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with itchy, serpiginous eruptions as shown in the image. He says that these lesions advance rapidly to different sites. What is the primary mechanism of action of the drug preferred for this disease?","o":["Activation of glutamate gated chloride channels","Inhibition of glutamate gated chloride channels","Inhibition of nicotinic acetycholine receptor","Activation of nicotinic acetylcholine receptor"],"a":0,"e":"The clinical scenario and the image showing larva currens, points towards a diagnosis of strongyloidiasis, and the drug of choice in this condition is ivermectin. The mechanism of action of ivermectin primarily involves activation of glutamate-gated chloride channels found only in invertebrates.\n\nIvermectin activates glutamate-activated chloride channels in the nerves and muscles of nematode and results in the influx of chloride into the cell. This causes hyperpolarization of cells and results in paralysis of nematodes.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/277577b869374f28a256f6013e592df9.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the first-line treatment for a patient presenting with the finding shown below?","o":["DEC","Pyrantel pamoate","Mebendazole","Ivermectin"],"a":3,"e":"The above image shows cutaneous larva migrans. Ivermectin is the first-line drug for the treatment of cutaneous larva migrans.\n\nThe first-line drugs used in cutaneous larva migrans include:\n\n• Ivermectin - 200μg/kg orally single dose\n\n• Albendazole - 400mg orally for 3 days","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f69f719296204d8db7f6b932a78271ef.PNG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 17-year-old boy presents with painless hematuria. He has a history of travel to Kenya. Urine microscopy revealed the finding shown below. What is the drug of choice in this condition?","o":["Albendazole","Mebendazole","Praziquantel","Triclabendazole"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical scenario and the image showing the egg of Schistosoma hematobium (egg with terminal spine), point towards the diagnosis of schistosomiasis. Praziquantel is the drug of choice for schistosomiasis caused by all Schistosoma species that can infect humans.\n\nA single oral dose of 40 mg/kg or three doses of 20 mg/kg each, given 4–6 h apart, generally produce cure rates of 70%–95% and consistently high reductions (>85%) in egg counts.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5b1873018b6449e5850fa0ec858dce74.PNG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs causes flaccid paralysis of Ascaris lumbricoides?","o":["Metrifonate","Levamisole","Piperazine","Tribendimidine"],"a":2,"e":"Piperazine causes flaccid paralysis of Ascaris lumbricoides.\n\nIt acts as a GABA receptor agonist and increases chloride ion conductance across the Ascaris muscle membrane. This results in hyperpolarisation and reduced excitability, which leads to muscle relaxation and flaccid paralysis.\n\nMetrifonate, levamisole and tribendimidine are anthelminthic drugs that cause spastic paralysis.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Diethylcarbamazine is used for the treatment of:","o":["Filariasis","Dracunculiasis","Schistosomiasis","Ascariasis"],"a":0,"e":"Diethylcarbamazine (DEC) is used for the control and treatment of lymphatic filariasis caused by W. bancrofti, B. malayi and B. timori.\n\nOther indications for the use of DEC are:\n\n• Loiasis\n\n• Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with fever, fatigue, and malaise. Investigations reveal anemia and thrombocytopenia. Thin blood smear is shown below. What is the recommended therapy for this condition?","o":["Clindamycin plus atovaquine","Azithromycin plus atovaquone","Azithromycin plus clindamycin","Clindamycin plus quinine"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario and the microscopic image showing tetrads or Maltese cross, point towards a diagnosis of babesiosis and the recommended therapy for this condition is azithromycin plus atovaquone.\n\nBabesiosis is a zoonotic infection that resembles malaria. It presents with febrile illness, hemolysis and hemoglobinuria.\n\nFor mild or moderate disease: Azithromycin plus atovaquone.\n\nFor severe disease:\n\n• Recommended regimen - azithromycin plus atovaquone.\n\n• Alternative regimen - clindamycin plus quinine.\n\nSevere babesiosis typically occurs in patients with one or more of the following characteristics: age >50 years, neonatal prematurity, asplenia/hyposplenism, HIV/AIDS, malignancy, and immunosuppressive therapy.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d496256e6f843c38b8f2d162befefcf.PNG"],"t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with fever and cervical lymphadenopathy. Investigations revealed IgM toxoplasma antibodies. Which of the following agents is most effective in treating this condition?","o":["Trimethoprim","Pyrimethamine","Pentamidine","Pyridoxine"],"a":1,"e":"Pyrimethamine is the most effective agent against toxoplasmosis and is present in most of the regimens. It is combined with sulfadiazine or clindamycin for toxoplasmosis.\n\nTrimethoprim plus sulfamethoxazole (cotrimoxazole) is an alternative.","t":"Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a polyene antifungal agent?","o":["Capsofungin","Griseofulvin","Amphotericin B","Terbinafine"],"a":2,"e":"Amphotericin B is a polyene antifungal.\n\nOther polyene antifungals are:\n\n• Amphotericin B\n\n• Nystatin\n\n• Natamycin\n\n• Hamycin\n\nAmphotericin B is the least toxic among polyenes and is used systemically for fungal infections. Nystatin and hamycin have high systemic toxicity and are used only for local and topical actions.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which formulation of amphotericin B has the highest risk of nephrotoxicity?","o":["Liposomal amphotericin B","Conventional amphotericin B","Amphotericin B lipid complex","Amphotericin B colloidal dispersion"],"a":1,"e":"Nephrotoxicity risk is highest with conventional amphotericin B.\n\nConventional amphotericin B (C-AMB) is not a lipid-based formulation of amphotericin B. Amphotericin B is insoluble in water, hence C-AMB is formulated by complexing with bile salt deoxycholate to make it suitable for intravenous infusion.\n\nNephrotoxicity risk with amphotericin B formulations in decreasing order:\n\n• C-AMB > Amphotericin B lipid complex (ABLC) > Liposomal amphotericin B (L-AMB)\n\n• All lipid formulations of amphotericin B (ABLC, L-AMB, ABCD) are less nephrotoxic than conventional amphotericin B\n\nNote: Amphotericin B colloidal dispersion (ABCD) has not been compared with other lipid-based formulations","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A diabetic patient presents with periorbital swelling as shown below. She complains of significant visual impairment and a fungal etiology is suspected. What is the most appropriate drug for this patient?","o":["Itraconazole","Liposomal amphotericin B","Ketoconazole","Broad spectrum antibiotics"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of invasive mucormycosis in the form of orbital cellulitis. Intravenous administration of amphotericin B is the treatment of choice for invasive mucormycosis.\n\nMucormycosis is an opportunistic infection seen in patients with diabetes mellitus, leukaemia, lymphoma and other immunocompromised states. The most prevalent species is Rhizopus Oryza.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/734701004545428db8eab1f84887b2ba.JPEG"],"t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with mucormycosis is being treated with intravenous amphotericin B. Following administration of the drug, he starts experiencing chills, fever and dyspnea. These symptoms occur most frequently with which formulation?","o":["Liposomal amphotericin B","Conventional amphotericin B","Amphotericin B lipid complex","Amphotericin B colloidal dispersion"],"a":3,"e":"Development of fever and chills following intravenous administration is suggestive of infusion-related reactions which occur most frequently with amphotericin B colloidal dispersions.\n\nInfusion-related adverse events are commonly seen with amphotericin B and it has been nicknamed 'shake and bake syndrome' due to the associated chills and fever.\n\nDecreasing order of frequency of infusion-related reactions with amphotericin B formulations:\nAmphotericin B colloidal dispersions (ABCD) > conventional amphotericin B (C-AMB) > amphotericin B lipid complex (ABLC) > liposomal amphotericin B (L-AMB).","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"An immunocompromised patient with invasive mucormycosis is administered intravenous amphotericin B. Which frequently associated electrolyte disturbance would you be concerned about?","o":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypermagnesemia","Hyponatremia"],"a":0,"e":"Amphotericin B causes hypokalemia and hence potassium levels should be monitored in this patient.\n\nAmphotericin B can cause renal tubular acidosis and renal wasting of K+ and Mg2+ leading to hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia during and several weeks after therapy. Potassium supplements are required in around one-third of patients on prolonged amphotericin B therapy.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive patient presents with headache and nuchal stiffness. The CD4 count is 90 and CSF sample with India-ink staining shows the presence of encapsulated yeast cells. Which of the following is the treatment of choice for the patient?","o":["Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Flucytosine","Amphotericin B plus flucytosine"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario and the presence of encapsulated yeast cells in CSF with India ink staining are suggestive of cryptococcal meningitis. Amphotericin B plus flucytosine is the treatment of choice for this patient.\n\nCryptococcal meningitis is an AIDS-defining opportunistic fungal CNS infection that generally occurs in untreated patients, with CD4+ T cell counts <100/μL.\n\nIn cryptococcal meningitis, amphotericin B combined with flucytosine is the current gold standard treatment in immunocompromised patients, especially in AIDS patients.\n\nIn non-immunocompetent patients, the role of additional flucytosine along with amphotericin B is less clear.\n\nOral fluconazole is used for consolidation and maintenance therapy after two weeks of induction therapy with amphotericin B.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b8477287c1214e30adcbebab64a8d212.JPEG"],"t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A washerman presents with itchy, annular scaly plaques in the groin. He is prescribed with an antifungal that acts by interfering with mitosis. Identify the drug.","o":["Caspofungin","Ciclopirox","Natamycin","Griseofulvin"],"a":3,"e":"Itchy annular plaques in the groin are suggestive of tinea cruris and the drug most likely given was griseofulvin which acts by interfering with fungal mitosis.\n\nMicrotubule function is necessary for the formation of the mitotic spindle during cell division. Griseofulvin inhibits microtubule function and thereby disrupts mitotic spindle formation and inhibits cell division.\n\nTinea cruris is a dermatophytosis involving the groin and is also known as jock itch or dhobi's itch.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"To which of the following patients will you not prescribe griseofulvin?","o":["A middle aged man with tinea capitis","A student with tinea cruris","A child with tinea versicolor","An athlete with tinea pedis"],"a":2,"e":"Griseofulvin is not useful in the treatment of tinea versicolor. It is treated with topical azoles.\n\nTinea capitis, tinea cruris and tinea pedis are dermatophytoses. Griseofulvin is indicated only for dermatophytoses caused by species of Microsporum, Epidermophyton, and Trichophyton. It is considered the drug of choice for tinea capitis in children. It is reserved only for large topical tinea infections when topical therapy is inadequate.\n\nTinea versicolor is not a dermatophytosis. It is a superficial mycosis caused by lipophilic yeast Malassezia.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can cause contraceptive failure in a lady on oral contraceptive pills?","o":["Ketoconazole","Fluconazole","Griseofulvin","Flucytosine"],"a":2,"e":"Griseofulvin is a cytochrome enzyme inducer and can cause contraceptive failure when taken with oral contraceptive pills.\n\nIt induces hepatic CYP enzymes and can increase the metabolism of drugs. Warfarin metabolism is increased and the dose has to be adjusted according to the change in response. Estrogen metabolism is also increased thereby reducing the efficacy of low-dose estrogen-based oral contraceptives.\n\nNote: Ketoconazole is a cytochrome enzyme inhibitor.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Fungal cell wall synthesis is altered by which of the following drugs?","o":["Caspofungin","Amphotericin B","Nystatin","Flucytosine"],"a":0,"e":"Fungal cell wall synthesis is altered by caspofungin.\n\n1,3-β-D glucan is an essential component of the fungal cell wall which maintains cell integrity. Echinocandin group of antifungals like caspofungin, anidulafungin, and micafungin acts by inhibiting the synthesis of 1,3-β-D-glucan and thereby weakens the fungal cell wall and increases the osmotic susceptibility.\n\nEchinocandins are fungicidal against Candida species. They show fungistatic activity against Aspergillus species.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient was diagnosed with coccidioidal meningitis. What is the drug of choice for the condition?","o":["Itraconazole","Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Griseofulvin"],"a":1,"e":"Fluconazole is the drug of choice for coccidioidal meningitis caused by Coccidioides immitis because of better CSF penetration.\n\nItraconazole is also effective in this condition but it is not the drug of choice. Amphotericin B is indicated in patients in whom triazoles have failed.\n\nNote: Amphotericin B is the drug of choice for initial treatment of cryptococcal meningitis caused by Cryptococcus neoformans and fluconazole is used for maintenance therapy.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle aged man presents with the condition shown in the image. Which of the following drugs can cause this condition?","o":["Fluconazole","Itraconazole","Ketoconazole","Voriconazole"],"a":2,"e":"The image above depicts gynecomastia and can be caused by ketoconazole due to its antiandrogenic action.\n\nKetoconazole use results in various antiandrogenic effects. It decreases testosterone production and displaces testosterone from protein binding sites in plasma. It also decreases hydrocortisone production, especially at higher doses. Adverse effects that can occur are gynecomastia, loss of hair, libido, and oligozoospermia.\n\nFluconazole, itraconazole and voriconazole do not have antiandrogenic action.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0142136944d48b79c501a822e03ca57.PNG"],"t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an indication for ketoconazole use?","o":["Cushing’s syndrome","Dermal leishmaniasis","Addison’s disease","Kala azar"],"a":2,"e":"Ketoconazole is not indicated for use in Addison’s disease.\n\nIt is a potent nonselective inhibitor of adrenal and gonadal steroidogenesis by inhibiting 17 α-hydroxylase enzymes which are involved in steroid hormone synthesis and it can hence worsen Addison's disease. However, this property also makes it useful in the treatment of Cushing’s syndrome.\n\nAzoles interfere with leishmanial cell membrane biosynthesis and are therefore used in dermal leishmaniasis and kala-azar.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"While administering which of the following drugs will you counsel patients regarding transient visual and auditory hallucinations?","o":["Oral voriconazole","Intravenous voriconazole","Oral itraconazole","Intravenous itraconazole"],"a":1,"e":"Transient visual or auditory hallucinations are commonly seen with the use of intravenous voriconazole. These usually occur at night.\n\nOther side effects of voriconazole are:\n\n• Hepatotoxicity\n\n• Prolonged QTc interval and increased risk of torsades de pointes\n\n• Anaphylactoid reaction when given intravenously","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Omeprazole dose is reduced when given along with voriconazole because of what action of the latter drug?","o":["CYP3A4 inhibition","CYP3A4 induction","CYP2C19 inhibiton","CYP2C19 induction"],"a":2,"e":"Voriconazole is a strong inhibitor and substrate of CYP2C19. Concomitant omeprazole administration can result in increased omeprazole levels because of inhibition of metabolism.\n\nIn a patient on omeprazole 40 mg/day, the dose of omeprazole should be reduced to half when voriconazole is initiated. Additionally, higher levels of omeprazole can also interfere in voriconazole metabolism as omeprazole also inhibits CYP2C19.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"In a patient with athlete's foot, which keratolytic ointment would you prescribe to enhance treatment outcome?","o":["Benzoic acid","Salicylic acid","Undecylenic acid","Ciclopirox olamine"],"a":1,"e":"Salicylic acid is a keratolytic agent that can be prescribed to enhance the treatment outcome in patients with athlete's foot (i.e. tinea pedis).\n\nSalicylic acid accelerates the desquamation of the stratum corneum. It is used in combination with a fungistatic drug called benzoic acid because eradication of infection is possible only if the infected stratum corneum is shed. This combination is called Whitfield's ointment and is used in the treatment of tinea pedis.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with multiple annular itchy skin lesions with central clearing over his back. You may prescribe any of the following except?","o":["Itraconazole","Terbinafine","Amphotericin B","Griseofulvin"],"a":2,"e":"Pruritic annular skin lesions with central clearing are suggestive of dermatophytosis for which amphotericin B must not be prescribed. Azoles, terbinafine, and griseofulvin are indicated for the treatment of dermatophytosis.\n\nDermatophytes are inhibited by amphotericin B but drug concentrations attained in the skin are very low and ineffective for the treatment of dermatophytosis.\n\nThe uses of amphotericin B are:\n\n• Treatment of choice in invasive mucormycosis\n\n• Candida albicans infection\n\n• Cryptococcus neoformans infection\n\n• Endemic mycosis","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not used to treat candida infections?","o":["Ketoconazole","Nystatin","Amphotericin B","Griseofulvin"],"a":3,"e":"Griseofulvin is not used to treat candida infection. It is active against most dermatophytes but not against candida and other fungi causing deep mycosis.\n\nKetoconazole, nystatin, and amphotericin B are used in the treatment of candida infections.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true about antifungal drugs?","o":["Amphotericin B is more effective parenterally","Griseofulvin is effective orally","Ciclopirox olamine is effective in systemic mycoses","Fluconazole is effective orally as well as intravenously"],"a":2,"e":"Ciclopirox olamine is a topical antifungal agent and is not effective in systemic mycoses.\n\nCiclopirox olamine is a broad spectrum fungicidal agent that acts by chelating trivalent metal ions which leads to inhibition of the metal-dependent enzyme that degrades free radicals. It is available as a nail lacquer formulation in the treatment of onychomycosis.\n\nOption A: Amphotericin B is more effective parenterally for systemic mycoses. However, local or topical administration of amphotericin B drops is used in mycotic corneal ulcers.\n\nOption B: The absorption of griseofulvin is less when administered orally but microfining the drug particles increases the absorption.\n\nOption D: Fluconazole can be administered orally as well as intravenously.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Topical antifungal agents have no role in the treatment of patients with which of the following conditions?","o":["Dermatophytosis","Tinea versicolor","Tinea nigra","Chromoblastomycosis"],"a":3,"e":"Topical antifungal agents have no role in the treatment of subcutaneous mycoses like chromoblastomycosis. Itraconazole is the first-line antifungal indicated in this condition.\n\nTopical antifungal agents are used in the treatment of:\n\n• Dermatophytosis\n\n• Candidiasis\n\n• Piedra\n\n• Tinea versicolor\n\n• Tinea nigra\n\n• Fungal keratitis","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antifungal drugs are used topically?\n\n1. Tolnaftate\n\n2. Flucytosine\n\n3. Butenafine\n\n4. Naftifine\n\n5. Clotrimazole","o":["1, 2, 3","1, 3, 5","1, 2, 3, 4, 5","1, 3, 4, 5"],"a":3,"e":"Tolnaftate, butenafine, naftifine, and clotrimazole are used topically.\n\n Allylamine antifungals available for topical application are:\n\n• Terbinafine\n\n• Naftifine\n\n• Butenafine\n\nAzoles that are used topically are:\n\n• Clotrimazole\n\n• Miconazole\n\n• Econazole\n\nTolnaftate is a topical synthetic antifungal agent. It distorts the mycelial growth of yeast-like fungi and molds. It is effective in the treatment of dermatomycosis and tinea.","t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old woman presented with yellowish discolouration of toenails. The discolouration spread proximally as a streak in the nail and later, the entire nail separated from the nailbed. What is the mechanism of action of the drug of choice for her?","o":["Inhibition of microtubule formation","Binding to ergosterol and altering membrane functions","Formation of pore in cell membrane","Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of tinea unguium or onychomycosis. The drug of choice is oral terbinafine which acts by inhibition of ergosterol synthesis.\n\nTerbinafine acts as a non-competitive inhibitor of squalene epoxidase, an early step enzyme in the synthesis of fungal ergosterol. This results in the accumulation of sterol squalene which is toxic to the organism.\n\nThe efficacy of terbinafine in onychomycosis is improved when combined with amorolfine 5% nail lacquer.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d4109c637db46e79c4c5ef0ad192a82.JPEG"],"t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A farmer presented with complaints of redness and excessive watering from the right eye. Slit-lamp examination reveals the following finding. Microscopy of the corneal ulcer scraping shows branched septate hyphae. Which of the following drugs can be used in the treatment of this patient?","o":["Nystatin 3.5%","Fluconazole 0.2%","Natamycin 5%","Oral voriconazole"],"a":2,"e":"Slit-lamp examination showing a corneal ulcer with satellite lesions and a thick hypopyon are suggestive of mycotic keratitis (fungal corneal ulcer) while branched septate hyphae are seen with Aspergillus and Fusarium. Natamycin 5% suspension is the drug of choice in this condition.\n\nTreatment of fungal corneal ulcer:\n\n• Topical antifungals: Aspergillus and Fusarium infections are treated with natamycin, amphotericin B and voriconazole while Candida infections are treated with nystatin.\n\n• Systemic antifungals: Ketoconazole or voriconazole may be needed if there is a severe corneal ulcer, perforated ulcer, or suspicion of endophthalmitis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ddf897619c5458780880273d5ab0cca.PNG"],"t":"Antifungal Agents","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old lady presents with pulmonary TB is started on anti-tubercular therapy. What is the rationale for administering multi-drug therapy to her?","o":["To delay development of resistance","To reduce toxicity","To broaden antimicrobial spectrum","To prevent toxin release from the organism"],"a":0,"e":"The reason for administering multi-drug therapy in tuberculosis is to prevent the development of resistance.\n\nResistance to individual drugs in Mycobacterium tuberculosis occurs by spontaneous point mutations that occur at a low but predictable rate (10-7 to 10-10 ). Development of this drug resistance is almost invariably the result of monotherapy, i.e., the failure of the health care provider to prescribe at least 2 drugs to which the tubercle bacilli are susceptible. Hence, multi-drug therapy is recommended for the treatment of tuberculosis.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which is the most effective drug against extracellular mycobacteria?","o":["Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol"],"a":1,"e":"The most effective drug against extracellular mycobacteria is Rifampicin.\n\nOption A: Isoniazid and Rifampicin act against both intracellular and extracellular mycobacterium. But, Rifampicin inhibits the extracellular mycobacteria at much lower drug concentrations than isoniazid.\n\nOption B: Pyrazinamide acts only against intracellular mycobacterium.\n\nOption C: Ethambutol acts against only extracellular mycobacteria but is not as effective as rifampicin. It is a bacteriostatic drug. Fast-multiplying bacteria are highly susceptible to its action.\n\nNote: Streptomycin is primarily active against only the extracellular tubercle bacilli as its penetration into the cells is very poor.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A study was conducted to genotype NAT2 gene polymorphisms in patients receiving ATT. Among the 300 patients genotyped, 200 were slow acetylators (group A), 70 were intermediate (group B) and 30 were fast acetylators (group C). Patients in group A are prone to develop which of the following?","o":["Failure of therapy","Peripheral neuropathy","Hepatotoxicity","Allergic reactions"],"a":1,"e":"Isoniazid, a key agent in the ATT, is metabolized primarily through N-acetylation by the genetically polymorphic N-acetyltransferase-2 (NAT2) enzyme. Slow acetylators (group A) of isoniazid are prone to develop peripheral neuropathy.\n\nThe rate of metabolism of isoniazid is determined by the genetic polymorphism of NAT2 enzyme and classified broadly into two groups - slow and fast acetylators.\n\nIn slow acetylators, the drug accumulates due to slow metabolism resulting in an increased risk of side effects, and hence they should undergo periodic monitoring. Peripheral neuropathy is a more frequent side effect of INH when compared to hepatotoxicity.\n\nFast acetylators metabolize the drug rapidly and show less response to standard doses and the therapy may be less effective.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Line probe assay done on sputum sample of a patient with TB revealed over-expression of InhA gene. This can lead to cross-resistance of isoniazid with which of the following drugs?","o":["Rifampicin","Ethionamide","Cycloserine","Ethambutol"],"a":1,"e":"Over-expression of the gene for InhA leads to cross-resistance of isoniazid (INH) with ethionamide.\n\nMechanisms of INH resistance\n\nMutation of KatG\n\n• most common mechanism\n\n• confers a high level of resistance\n\nOverexpression of InhA\n\n• confers a low level of resistance\n\n• cross-resistance with ethionamide\n\nOverexpression of ahpC\n\n• increases the bacterial survival under oxidative stress\n\nInduction of efflux pumps\n\n• cross-resistance with ethambutol","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 4-month-old boy was brought to the ER with recurrent seizures. His mother informs you that he is receiving isoniazid prophylaxis as she was diagnosed with TB during pregnancy. ABG revealed a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. What would be the mainstay of treatment?","o":["Benzodiazepines","Barbiturates","Pyridoxine","Thiamine"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of isoniazid overdose for which the mainstay of treatment is intravenous pyridoxine.\n\nIsoniazid overdose results in decreased pyridoxine availability to neurons. This decreases the levels of GABA, which in turn lowers the seizure threshold. Benzodiazepines can be used to control seizures.\n\nProlonged treatment with isoniazid leads to the deficiency of pyridoxine which can lead to peripheral neuropathy and other neurological manifestations like ataxia, paresthesia, and numbness.\n\nClinical triad of isoniazid overdose:\n\n• Seizures refractory to treatment with phenytoin and barbiturates\n\n• High anion gap metabolic acidosis (HAGMA) resistant to treatment with sodium bicarbonate\n\n• Coma","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man undergoing treatment for TB visits your OPD for follow-up. His wife states that recently he is forgetting the names of his colleagues and neighbors, but later recollects them correctly. Which drug is implicated to cause this condition?","o":["Ethionamide","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"a":1,"e":"Isoniazid is the antitubercular drug that is implicated in the causation of transient memory loss.\n\nNeurological toxicities like peripheral neuropathy, optic neuritis, and seizures can occur with isoniazid therapy. It is also associated with abnormalities like:\n\n• Euphoria\n\n• Transient memory loss\n\n• Separation of ideas and reality\n\n• Loss of self-control\n\n• Florid psychosis\n\nPyridoxine therapy can prevent and reverse most of the neurological toxicity and mental abnormalities.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old woman, on anti-tubercular therapy, presented with pain in the right hand and shoulder for the past 1 month. On physical examination, her wrist was swollen and her shoulder was extremely painful with a limited range of motion. Which drug is most likely associated with this condition?","o":["Levofloxacin","Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical presentation is suggestive of shoulder-hand syndrome which is associated with the use of isoniazid.\n\nThis syndrome is characterized by painful arthropathy in the upper extremity joints. It can also present as generalized arthropathy.\n\nOther drugs that can cause shoulder-hand syndrome are phenobarbitone and phenytoin.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Before initiating treatment for a sputum-positive TB patient, the physician orders for Cartridge Based Nucleic Acid Amplification Test in order to test for drug resistance. Which anti-tubercular drug resistance is he checking for?","o":["Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol"],"a":1,"e":"The Cartridge Based-Nucleic Acid Amplification Test (CBNAAT) is a molecular test for tuberculosis that detects the DNA of tubercle bacilli within 2 hours and mutations in the rpoB gene responsible for rifampicin resistance.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following rifamycin drugs would you advise a patient to take on an empty stomach?","o":["Rifabutin and rifapentine","Rifabutin and rifampicin","Rifampicin only","Rifapentine only"],"a":2,"e":"Rifampicin should be advised to be taken on an empty stomach because food decreases its absorption.\n\nRifabutin absorption is not altered by food.\n\nRifapentine absorption is enhanced by fatty food and hence it is recommended to be taken with food.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive patient on ART with zidovudine, lamivudine, and indinavir develops pulmonary tuberculosis. Which of the following anti-tubercular drugs should be avoided in him?","o":["Isoniazid","Rifampicin","Streptomycin","Ethambutol"],"a":1,"e":"Rifampicin should be avoided as it is a potent inducer of CYP enzymes including CYPs 1A2, 3A4, 2C9, and 2C19, which in turn can induce the metabolism of indinavir and results in therapeutic failure of ART.\n\nRifabutin causes the least induction of cytochrome enzymes and is therefore preferred in HIV patients who are on protease inhibitors/NNRTIs.\n\nCytochrome enzyme-inducing potency of rifamycins is: rifampicin > rifapentine > rifabutin.\n\nNote:\n\nCytochrome p-450 inducers\nCytochrome p-450 inhibitors\n\n• Barbiturates\n\n• Carbamazepine\n\n• Rifampin\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Aminoglutethimide\n\n• Cigarette smoking\n\n• Chronic alcoholism\n\n• Macrolide antibiotics\n\n• Azole antifungals\n\n• Chloramphenicol\n\n• Omeprazole\n\n• Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)\n\n• HIV protease inhibitors\n\n• Cimetidine\n\n• Ciprofloxacin\n\n• Metronidazole","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following rifamycins is not used for mycobacterial infections?","o":["Rifampicin","Rifaximin","Rifapentine","Rifabutin"],"a":1,"e":"Rifaximin is not used for mycobacterial infections. It is a synthetic rifamycin that is poorly absorbed from the GIT and is indicated in the management of GI conditions like:\n\n• Traveler’s diarrhea\n\n• Irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea\n\n• Hepatic encephalopathy\n\nRifamycins indicated in mycobacterial infections are rifampicin, rifapentine, and rifabutin.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A Gene Xpert test was conducted on the sputum sample of a patient with suspected TB. The results were positive and showed resistance to rifampicin. What is the primary mechanism of resistance against this drug?","o":["Alteration in target enzyme","Induction of efflux pumps","Alteration in activation pathway","Drug inactivating enzymes"],"a":0,"e":"Rifampicin resistance is primarily due to alteration in the target enzyme.\n\nRifampicin acts by binding to the β subunit of DNA-dependent RNA polymerase (rpoB) and inhibits chain formation in RNA synthesis. Rifampicin is a potent inducer of cytochrome enzymes CYP3A4, CYP1A2, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19. Alteration of the target enzyme (rpoB) by a mutation in the gene coding for rpoB is the primary mechanism of resistance to rifampicin. Gene Xpert identifies mutations in the rpoB gene to detect rifampicin resistance.\n\nEfflux pump induction has also been demonstrated as a mechanism of rifampicin resistance.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions can rifampicin be used?\n\n1. Prophylaxis of meningococcal meningitis\n\n2. Treatment of brucellosis\n\n3. Eradication of staphylococcal nasal carrier state\n\n4. Treatment of pleuropulmonary nocardiosis\n\n5. Treatment of paucibacillary leprosy","o":["1, 3 and 5","2 and 4 only","1, 3, 4 and 5","1, 2, 3 and 5"],"a":3,"e":"Besides its anti-tubercular action, rifampicin also has certain non-tubercular uses which include:\n\n• Prophylaxis of meningococcal and H. influenzae meningitis\n\n• Treatment of MRSA, diphtheroid, and legionella infections\n\n• Eradication of staphylococcal nasal carrier state in patients with chronic furunculosis\n\n• A combination of doxycycline and rifampicin is used as the first-line therapy for brucellosis\n\n• A part of the WHO regimen for standard therapy of both paucibacillary and multibacillary leprosy\n\nRifampicin is not used in the treatment of Nocardia species. Cotrimoxazole is considered the drug of choice for pleuropulmonary nocardiosis.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are prescribing first-line anti-tubercular therapy to a patient newly diagnosed with TB. Which of the following is incorrect about his therapy?","o":["Red green color impairment is an early sign of ethambutol induced optic neuritis","Ethambutol accumulates in renal failure","Flu-like syndrome is usually seen if rifampicin is taken on a daily basis","Hyperuricemia is a recognised side effect of pyrazinamide"],"a":2,"e":"Flu-like syndrome is usually seen when rifampicin is administered less than twice weekly (high dose) and it is uncommon when administered daily (lower dose).\n\nFlu-like syndrome, characterized by fevers, chills, malaise, and arthralgia is a known dose-dependent adverse effect of rifampicin therapy and tends to occur late into the patient's treatment course. It resolves with either discontinuation of rifampin or switching to a daily-dose regimen. It can often be confused with occult infections or treatment failure.\n\nOptions A and B: Red-green colour impairment is one of the early signs of ethambutol-induced optic neuritis. Ethambutol is primarily excreted unchanged by kidneys and accumulation occurs in renal failure. Ethambutol does not undergo hepatic metabolism and can be safely prescribed for the treatment of TB in patients with hepatitis.\n\nOption D: Pyrazinamide interferes with urate excretion and can result in hyperuricemia.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young woman undergoing treatment for tuberculosis visits your OPD for a routine checkup. She informs you that recently she has noticed an orange-colored discoloration of her contact lenses. Which of the following drugs is most likely to be responsible for this?","o":["Isoniazid","Pyrazinamide","Ethambutol","Rifampicin"],"a":3,"e":"Rifampicin is the drug most likely to cause orange-colored discoloration of contact lenses in this case.\n\nDrugs like rifampicin and rifabutin are known to cause reddish-orange discoloration of the skin, urine, feces, saliva, sweat, and tears (which can stain the patient's contact lenses).","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are planning to start treatment in a middle-aged man diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis. He has a history of chronic kidney disease and his current creatinine clearance is 25 mL/min. Which of the following drugs is least likely to require dose modification?","o":["Ethambutol","Streptomycin","Rifampicin","Isoniazid"],"a":2,"e":"Rifampicin is the least likely drug to require dose modification in renal failure patients as it is metabolized in the liver and primarily excreted in bile.\n\nIn patients with renal failure, the half-life of isoniazid will be prolonged, but its adverse effects are not significantly increased. Hence dose modification is not usually done for isoniazid in clinical practice as a reduction in dose may render the regimen ineffective and increase the chances of resistance.\n\nEthambutol and streptomycin are primarily excreted unchanged through the renal system and need dose modification in renal failure.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Pseudojaundice is an adverse effect of which of the following drugs?","o":["Phenytoin","Rifabutin","Pyrazinamide","Isoniazid"],"a":1,"e":"Pseudojaundice is an adverse effect of rifabutin. It resolves on discontinuation of the drug.\n\nUnique adverse effects of rifabutin are:\n\n• Polymyalgia/arthralgia\n\n• Pseudojaundice\n\n• Anterior uveitis","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis nicotinamidase is involved in the activation of which of the following antitubercular drug?","o":["Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide","Cycloserine"],"a":2,"e":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis nicotinamidase is involved in the activation of pyrazinamide.\n\nNicotinamidase which is produced by M. tuberculosis is also called pyrazinamidase. It is encoded by the pncA gene and its mutations are associated with pyrazinamide resistance. It converts pyrazinamide to active pyrazinoic acid.\n\nPyrazinoic acid has the following actions:\n\n• Inhibition of fatty acid synthetase type 1 leads to interference in mycolic acid synthesis\n\n• Reduction of intracellular pH\n\n• Disruption of membrane transport","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old man on treatment for TB presents with complaints of severe pain and swelling in his left great toe since today morning, as shown in the image below. Which of the following drugs is most likely to cause this?\n\n1. Rifampicin\n\n2. Pyrazinamide\n\n3. Isoniazid\n\n4. Ethambutol\n\n5. Streptomycin","o":["1 and 2","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4","1, 3 and 5"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical history and findings of first metatarsophalangeal joint swelling are suggestive of an acute episode of gout. Antitubercular drugs that are most likely to cause hyperuricemia and precipitate gout are pyrazinamide and ethambutol.\n\nBoth pyrazinamide and ethambutol inhibit uric acid excretion in the kidney causing hyperuricemia.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9889e56dc3f147bb8d75b6bca6f81bc5.JPEG"],"t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 33-year-old woman receiving treatment for TB presents to your OPD with complaints of blurry vision and pain on movement of the left eye for the past 2 days. Examination showed Marcus Gunn pupil. Which anti-tubercular drug is most likely associated with this condition?","o":["Ethambutol","Isoniazid","Ethionamide","Cycloserine"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical features along with Marcus Gunn pupil are suggestive of optic neuritis, which is a dose-dependent adverse effect associated with ethambutol.\n\nOptic neuritis resulting in decreased visual acuity and loss of red-green discrimination is the most serious adverse effect of ethambutol. It can be unilateral or bilateral and usually recovers when the drug is stopped.\n\nTests of visual acuity and red-green discrimination before the start of therapy and periodically thereafter are recommended.","t":"First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not hepatotoxic?","o":["Pyrazinamide","Isoniazid","Cycloserine","Ethionamide"],"a":2,"e":"Cycloserine is not a hepatotoxic drug. The adverse effects of cycloserine are mainly neurological.\n\nHalf of the patients on cycloserine (1g/day) experience neuropsychiatric symptoms. It ranges from headaches to psychosis, seizures, and suicidal ideas. Therefore, cycloserine is contraindicated in patients with epilepsy.\n\nEthionamide, rifampicin, isoniazid and pyrazinamide are hepatotoxic agents. They are either contraindicated or used with extreme caution in hepatitis.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old woman who was started on treatment for MDR-TB 6 months back, presented with complaints of fatigue, amenorrhea, and constipation since the past 1 month. On examination, a swelling was noticed on the anterior aspect of her neck. Her lab reports show raised TSH levels with low free T3 and T4 levels. Which anti-tubercular drug is likely to cause this adverse effect?","o":["Streptomycin","Pyrazinamide","Cycloserine","Ethionamide"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical features and lab reports are suggestive of hypothyroidism with goiter, which is a known adverse effect of ethionamide.\n\nMDR-TB is treated with 2nd line agents that include ethionamide (ETH). ETH is not a first-line agent due to its gastrointestinal side effects. It is structurally related to thionamides (propylthiouracil and methimazole) and therefore causes hypothyroidism by preventing organification of iodine in thyroid hormone synthesis. Another antitubercular drug that can cause hypothyroidism is Para-amino salicylic acid (PAS).\n\nETH/PAS-induced hypothyroidism should be treated with thyroxine replacement therapy, instead of changing the regimen. Thyroid function usually optimizes within 2 months after completing MDR therapy.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the mechanism of anti-mycobacterial action of bedaquiline?","o":["Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis","Inhibition of protein synthesis","Inhibition of ATP synthase","Inhibition of RNA synthesis"],"a":2,"e":"The mechanism of anti-mycobacterial action of bedaquiline is by inhibition of ATP synthase.\n\nBedaquiline is a diarylquinoline that targets the subunit c of the ATP synthase of M. tuberculosis. This leads to inhibition of the proton pump activity of the ATP synthase and causes a failure in the bacillary energy metabolism.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 68-year-old man with hypertension was diagnosed with drug-resistant pulmonary TB. One week after starting ATT he presented to the casualty following an episode of syncope. ECG showed a corrected QT interval of 477ms, which is shown below. Which drug used in his anti-tubercular therapy is responsible for this adverse effect?","o":["Clofazimine","Isoniazid","Bedaquiline","Para-amino salicylic acid"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with a corrected QT (QTc) interval of 477ms is suggestive of QTc prolongation which is an adverse effect associated with bedaquiline.\n\nIn the ECG QTc (corrected QT interval= QT/√RR) is 477ms. Normal QTc is about 440ms. Values of more than 440ms in males and 460ms in females are considered to be prolonged. QTc prolongation usually presents as syncope or cardiac arrest.\n\nAlthough QT prolongation can cause Torsades de pointes, no cases have been reported with bedaquiline. A 5-fold higher rate of death has been reported in patients on bedaquiline versus those on placebo. Therefore, the World Health Organization (WHO) advises caution when administering this medication. Bedaquiline is contraindicated in the age group of <18 years.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c145c79aefc41c1b540e6ad23c1ebb6.PNG"],"t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the mechanism of action of delamanid?","o":["Inhibition of ATP synthase","Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis","Inhibition of DNA gyrase","Inhibition of alanine racemase"],"a":1,"e":"Delamanid acts by inhibiting mycolic acid synthesis.\n\nPretomanid and delamanid are bicyclic nitroimidazoles that act by inhibiting mycolic acid (a component of the mycobacterial cell wall) and protein synthesis in the mycobacteria.\n\nPretomanid in combination with bedaquiline and linezolid was approved by FDA in 2019 for the treatment of adult patients with extensively drug-resistant (XDR) TB and for patients who are treatment-intolerant or nonresponsive to multidrug-resistant pulmonary (MDR) TB treatment.\n\nIn India, delamanid is only approved for the management of MDR-TB.\n\nIsoniazid also acts by inhibiting mycolic acid synthesis.\n\nOther options:\n\nOption A: Bedaquiline acts by inhibition of ATP synthase. It is a diarylquinoline that targets the subunit c of the ATP synthase of M. tuberculosis. This leads to inhibition of the proton pump activity of the ATP synthase and causes a failure in the bacillary energy metabolism.\n\nOption C: Fluoroquinolones act by inhibition of DNA gyrase. They are used as second-line agents in the treatment of tuberculosis.\n\nOption D: Cycloserine is a D-alanine analogue used as a second-line drug for MDR-TB that acts by inhibition of alanine racemase. Alanine racemase converts L-alanine to D-alanine which is needed for the mycobacterial cell wall.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman was diagnosed with tuberculosis. Which of the following antitubercular drugs should not be used in her?","o":["Isoniazid","Streptomycin","Rifampicin","Ethambutol"],"a":1,"e":"Streptomycin should not be used for the treatment of TB in a pregnant patient as it can cause hearing loss in the newborn.\n\nFirst-line treatment for tuberculosis in pregnancy is done with isoniazid(along with pyridoxine), rifampicin, ethambutol, and pyrazinamide.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive patient with a CD4 count of 40/mm3 requires prophylaxis against M. avium complex bacteremia. Which of the following drugs would you prefer?","o":["Azithromycin","Dapsone","Isoniazid","Rifabutin"],"a":0,"e":"Azithromycin 1200 mg given once a week, is preferred for prophylactic therapy against bacteremia caused by Mycobacterium-avium complex (MAC) in people living with AIDS.\n\nClarithromycin 500 mg twice daily, may also be administered for prophylaxis. Rifabutin 300 mg once daily is used for patients who are intolerant to macrolides.\n\nProphylaxis is indicated when CD4 counts are less than 50/mm3 and should be discontinued once the CD4 count is more than 100/mm3.\n\nDapsone is used as an alternative agent for prophylaxis of pneumocystis jirovecii infection, whereas isoniazid is used for prophylaxis of mycobacterium tuberculosis infection.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly woman on ART with a CD4 count of 35/μL and a high viral load presents with a 2 week history of cough, fever, and night sweats. She was diagnosed with pulmonary M. avium complex infection. Which of the following is not included in the triple-drug therapy for the management of her condition?","o":["Rifabutin","Isoniazid","Ethambutol","Clarithromycin"],"a":1,"e":"Triple drug therapy for pulmonary Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infections does not include isoniazid.\n\nTreatment for pulmonary MAC infection is triple-drug therapy with:\n\n• A rifamycin, like rifampicin or rifabutin (In HIV patients on ART, rifabutin is preferred)\n\n• Ethambutol\n\n• A macrolide, like clarithromycin or azithromycin\n\nMAC infection is an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection, occurring predominantly at CD4+ T cell counts of <50/μL and high viral load. The average CD4+ T cell count at the time of diagnosis is 10/μL. The most common presentation is disseminated disease with fever, weight loss, and night sweats.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing treatment for TB developed resistance to rifampicin and isoniazid after 3 months. He can be treated with all the following drugs except __________.","o":["Tobramycin","Amikacin","Moxifloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"a":0,"e":"Tobramycin cannot be used in the treatment of MDR-TB (rifampicin and isoniazid resistant) as it is not considered a second-line antitubercular drug.\n\nInjectable aminoglycosides like amikacin, kanamycin, capreomycin, and fluoroquinolones like moxifloxacin and ofloxacin are second-line drugs used for treating MDR-TB.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following beta lactam class has shown activity against MDR-TB?","o":["Aminopenicillins","Monobactams","Carboxypenicllins","Carbapenems"],"a":3,"e":"Carbapenems have shown activity against Mycobacterium tuberculosis.\n\nNote: Until recently beta-lactams were assumed to lack action against Mycobacterium tuberculosis. However, carbapenems in conjunction with clavulanate have demosntrated efficacy against Mycobacterium tuberculosis.","t":"Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Dapsone would not be helpful in which of the following patients?","o":["HIV positive patient with CD4&lt;100 and IgG positive for Toxoplasma","An elderly man with pemphigoid","HIV positive patient with Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia","A child with schistosomiasis"],"a":3,"e":"Dapsone is not used in the treatment of schistosomiasis. Praziquantel is the drug recommended for this condition.\n\nDapsone with pyrimethamine and leucovorin can be given for prophylaxis of Toxoplasma encephalitis in HIV patients with CD4<100. Along with trimethoprim, it can be given for prophylaxis and treatment of Pneumocystis pneumonia. However, cotrimoxazole remains the drug of choice in both conditions.\n\nThe uses of dapsone are:\n\nInfections\n\n• Leprosy\n\n• Pneumocystis jerovecii infections (prophylaxis and treatment)\n\n• Malaria\n\n• Toxoplasma gondi infections (prophylaxis)\n\nInflammatory conditions\n\n• Dermatitis herpetiformis\n\n• Pemphigoid\n\n• Relapsing chondritis\n\n• Linear IgA bullous disease","t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the treatment of choice for the patient's condition depicted below?","o":["Rifampicin + dapsone + cycloserine, for 12 months","Rifampicin + dapsone + clofazimine, for 6 months","Rifampicin + ofloxacin + minocycline, for 6 months","Rifampicin + dapsone + clofazimine, for 12 months"],"a":3,"e":"The given image of a man with nodular lesions on his face depicts lepromatous leprosy and is treated by standard multidrug regimen (MDR-WHO recommended) for 12 months.\n\nIt consists of:\n\n• Rifampicin: 600 mg once a month\n\n• Dapsone: 100 mg daily\n\n• Clofazimine: 300 mg once a month + 50 mg once daily","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64763838666743d5aad6d65bd9304aac.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"All of the following are administered under supervision in India except?","o":["Clofazimine","Pyrazinamide","Rifampicin","Dapsone"],"a":3,"e":"Dapsone is administered daily in both multibacillary and paucibacillary leprosy without supervision.\n\nClofazimine is indicated as a once-monthly supervised dose (300 mg) and daily unsupervised doses (50 mg) in multibacillary leprosy and paucibacillary leprosy.\n\nPyrizanamide is an ATT drug which is administered under direct supervision.\n\nRifampicin is indicated as a once-monthly supervised dose for both multibacillary and paucibacillary leprosy.","t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with multiple hypopigmented skin lesions and loss of sensation over the lesions. What is the most bactericidal drug for the condition?","o":["Clofazimine","Dapsone","Rifampicin","Ethionamide"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of leprosy and rifampicin is the most bactericidal anti-leprotic drug.\n\nClofazimine and dapsone are bacteriostatic agents, while ethionamide is not indicated in leprosy.","t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Thalidomide is useful in the treatment of which of the following conditions?","o":["Downgrading reactions","Reversal reactions","Erythema nodosum leprosum","All of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Thalidomide is useful in the treatment of erythema nodosum leprosum (ENL) or type 2 lepra reaction. It is indicated in recurring and persistent ENL lesions despite the completion of at least two courses of glucocorticoid therapy.\n\nThalidomide is not effective in type 1 lepra reaction. If type 1 reactions occur after initiation of antimycobacterial therapy, they are termed reversal reactions and it is histologically more tuberculoid. When they occur before the initiation, they are termed downgrading reactions and it is histologically more lepromatous.","t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with borderline leprosy develops severe type 1 lepra reaction during the course of his multidrug therapy. What would be the most appropriate treatment for him?","o":["Glucocorticoids","Clofazimine","Thalidomide","Plasmapheresis"],"a":0,"e":"The most appropriate therapy for severe type 1 lepra reactions is glucocorticoids.\n\nClofazimine, though effective in type 1 lepra reaction, is less efficacious than glucocorticoids.\n\nThalidomide and plasmapheresis are not indicated in the treatment of type 1 lepra reaction.","t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with leprosy, who is on multidrug therapy, presents with painful raised lesions on his forehead as shown in the image. Which of the following must be avoided in his condition?","o":["Clofazimine","Corticosteroids","Thalidomide","Rifampicin"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of tender, erythematous, subcutaneous nodules as seen in the image is suggestive of Type II lepra reaction in which rifampicin must be avoided.\n\nRifampicin is highly bactericidal to M. leprae and results in high kill rates and massive release of bacterial antigens and may aggravate type 2 lepra reactions (also called erythema nodosum leprosum).\n\nOptions A and B: Clofazimine and corticosteroids are indicated in both type 1 and type 2 lepra reactions.\n\nOption C: Thalidomide is indicated in type 2 lepra reactions.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9069f1e296cf4b30a5d9119bd035a4a9.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with complaints of reddish-black discolouration of skin and body secretions. Which anti-mycobacterial agent can cause this condition?","o":["Clofazimine","Cycloserine","Capreomycin","Ciprofloxacin"],"a":0,"e":"Reddish-black discolouration of skin and body secretions is observed with the use of clofazimine.\n\nClofazimine has good penetration into many tissues and results in reddish-black discolouration of skin and body secretions. This may take a long time to resolve.","t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"Rifampin is not active against which of the following Mycobacterial species?","o":["Mycobacterium avium complex","Mycobacterium leprae","Mycobacterium abscessus","Mycobacterium kansasii"],"a":2,"e":"Rifampin is not active against Mycobacterium abscessus. It is innately resistant to rifampicin.\n\nFirst-line therapy for Mycobacterium abscessus includes cefoxitin (or imipenem) + amikacin + clarithromycin.\n\nRifampicin is effective against Mycobacterium avium complex, Mycobacterium leprae, and Mycobacterium kansasii. (Options A, B, D)","t":"Anti Leprosy Drugs","c":"Antimycobacterial Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old man was brought to the casualty with fever, headache, focal seizures, and right hemiparesis. MRI brain showed hyperintensity in the left frontotemporal region. PCR of his CSF sample was positive for HSV DNA. What would be the drug of choice for this condition?","o":["Valacyclovir","Acyclovir","Gancyclovir","Foscarnet"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario and investigations are suggestive of HSV encephalitis for which the drug of choice is intravenous acyclovir. The treatment is effective only if it is started early.\n\nThe clinical hallmark of HSV encephalitis is the acute onset of fever and focal neurologic signs with hemorrhagic necrosis of especially the temporal lobe. The most sensitive investigation is HSV DNA demonstration in CSF by PCR.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with shingles was prescribed acyclovir. Which of the following statements is false regarding this drug?","o":["Acyclovir is a nucleoside congener","Nephrotoxicity is a dose limiting side effect of acyclovir","Valacyclovir has a higher bioavailability than acyclovir","Acyclovir does not enter the CSF"],"a":3,"e":"Acyclovir can enter CSF as it is widely distributed into all body fluid compartments, including vesicular fluid and aqueous humor. It can be used for treating shingles caused by HZV.\n\nOption A: Acyclovir is a guanosine nucleoside congener. The other nucleoside congeners are cidofovir, ganciclovir, and famciclovir.\n\nOption B: Nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity, though rare and reversible, are dose-limiting side effects of acyclovir.\n\nOption C: Acyclovir has a low bioavailability which is overcome by administering valacyclovir, a prodrug with higher bioavailability.\n\nMechanism of action of anti-viral drugs:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90a29df15d924ea084eb85bb747cb5c4x1280x1406.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of resistance to acyclovir in HSV-1?","o":["Induction of efflux pumps","Mutation of viral DNA polymerase","Deficient viral thymidine kinase activity","Expression of inactivating enzymes"],"a":2,"e":"The most common cause of resistance to acyclovir in HSV-1 is deficient or absent viral thymidine kinase activity.\n\nViral thymidine kinase is needed in the conversion of acyclovir to acyclovir triphosphate which competitively inhibits the viral DNA polymerase activity.\n\nMutation of viral DNA polymerase is rare.\n\nMechanism of action of anti-viral drugs:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed5c8c53b9274a568dc382eba1a70f66x1280x1406.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients can valacyclovir be used as the drug of choice?","o":["An elderly woman with avian influenza","An infant with RSV bronchiolitis","An adolescent with chickenpox","An immunocompromised patient with CMV retinitis"],"a":2,"e":"Oral valacyclovir 1g three times daily for 5-7 days is considered the drug of choice for chickenpox (VZV) in an immunocompetent patient. Treatment should be started ideally within 24 hours of rash onset.\n\nOral acyclovir (800 mg five times daily) is an alternative drug, but valacyclovir is preferred due to its higher bioavailability and less frequent dosing. In immunocompromised patients, varicella infection is treated with IV acyclovir.\n\nOption A: Oseltamivir is used for the treatment of avian influenza.\n\nOption B: Ribavirin is used for treating RSV bronchiolitis.\n\nOption D: Ganciclovir, foscarnet, and cidofovir are approved for CMV retinitis.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient showed persistence of genital herpes lesions even after 7 days of high-dose oral acyclovir therapy. Which of the following antivirals can you use to manage this case?","o":["Ganciclovir","Abacavir","Foscarnet","Valacyclovir"],"a":2,"e":"Intravenous foscarnet is used to treat acyclovir-resistant HSV (genital herpes) and VZV infections.\n\nIt inhibits viral nucleic acid synthesis by directly inhibiting HSV DNA polymerase (or HIV reverse transcriptase). It is active against herpes simplex, CMV, VZV, and HIV.\n\nMetabolic abnormalities are very common with foscarnet. Symptomatic hypocalcemia and nephrotoxicity are major dose-limiting toxicities of foscarnet.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a true statement about amantadine?","o":["The most common side effect is CNS toxicity","It is effective only against Influenza B","It is routinely used for the treatment of influenza","It is largely metabolised by the liver"],"a":0,"e":"The most common side effect of amantadine is minor CNS toxicity which includes nervousness, difficulty concentrating, and insomnia. It is effective only against Influenza A.\n\nMost strains of influenza are now resistant to amantadine and hence, it is not routinely used for the treatment or prophylaxis of influenza infections. Amantadine is largely excreted unmetabolized in the urine.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young woman with a recent travel history presented with fever and respiratory difficulty. She was diagnosed with Influenza A and was started on oseltamivir. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["DNA polymerase inhibition","Protein synthesis inhibition","Nucleoside analogue","Neuraminidase inhibition"],"a":3,"e":"Oseltamivir is a neuraminidase inhibitor.\n\nDrugs like oseltamivir and zanamivir competitively and reversibly interact with the enzyme sites to inhibit viral neuraminidase activity. This interferes with the release of the progeny from infected host cells, halting the spread of infection.\n\nEarly administration hastens the resolution of symptoms if given within 48 h of infection. Oseltamivir is given at a dose of 75 mg, twice a day for 5 days for the treatment of influenza A and B. For prophylaxis, it is given at a dose of 75mg, once per day, and continued for the duration of exposure.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Zanamivir is to be given for a patient with an influenza B infection. How will you administer this drug?","o":["Oral route","Inhalational route","Intravenous route","Intramuscular route"],"a":1,"e":"Zanamivir is delivered by oral inhalation of dry powder in a lactose carrier. Its oral bioavailability is very low (<5%).\n\nZanamivir is a neuraminidase inhibitor similar to oseltamivir and is active against both influenza A and B.\n\nThe different routes of administration of neuraminidase inhibitors are as follows:\n\n• Zanamivir: Inhalational\n\n• Oseltamivir: Oral\n\n• Peramivir: Intravenous","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 22-year-old man is admitted with acute H1N1 influenza pneumonia. He is having severe nausea and vomiting and is not able to take any oral medications. Which of the following drugs can be administered intravenously to him?","o":["Oseltamivir","Zanamivir","Peramivir","Entecavir"],"a":2,"e":"Peramivir is a neuraminidase inhibitor available for intravenous administration for acute influenza (H1N1).\n\nIt is indicated in acute influenza infections where the patient is not able to take drugs via the oral route or if inhalational (zanamivir) formulation is unavailable.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man, who has history of contact with a known case of influenza, is having fever, cough, and runny nose since today morning. Rapid antigen test turns out to be positive. The endonuclease inhibitor that can be used for his treatment is ___________.","o":["Oseltamivir","Baloxavir","Zanamivir","Favipravir"],"a":1,"e":"The endonuclease inhibitor which can be used for the treatment of acute uncomplicated influenza in adults is baloxavir marboxil.\n\nIt is a novel drug that was approved by the FDA in 2018. It is used to treat acute uncomplicated influenza in patients 12 years of age and older who have been symptomatic for less than 48 hours.\n\nIt is a selective inhibitor of influenza cap-dependent endonuclease which blocks proliferation by inhibiting mRNA synthesis.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is approved for prophylaxis against respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection?","o":["Omalizumab","Palivizumab","Denosumab","Ribavirin"],"a":1,"e":"Palivizumab is a monoclonal antibody approved for prophylaxis against respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection.\n\nIt is a humanized monoclonal antibody against F-glycoprotein present on the surface of RSV. It is used in high-risk infants (prematurity and immunosuppressed conditions) and children (with cardiovascular and pulmonary diseases).\n\nThe treatment of RSV infections is done with inhaled ribavirin.\n\nOption A: Omalizumab is a monoclonal antibody against IgE indicated in asthma.\n\nOption C: Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL indicated in osteoporosis.\n\nOption D: Ribavirin is used for treatment of RSV infections but is not approved for prophylaxis.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A hepatitis B infected patient awaiting liver transplantation is admitted with ascites and encephalopathy. Which of the following is the drug of choice for him?","o":["Interferon alpha","Lamivudine","Tenofovir","Telbivudine"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario of ascites and encephalopathy in a hepatitis B infected patient is suggestive of decompensated liver disease. Tenofovir is the drug of choice for decompensated cirrhosis due to hepatitis B infection.\n\nTenofovir is one of the first-line drugs for chronic hepatitis B infections. It is available as two prodrug formulations tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and tenofovir alafenamide (TAF).\n\nOption A: Interferon-alpha is one of the first-line agents in individuals with ongoing HBV-DNA replication and liver inflammation. But it is contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis with HBV infection as they can precipitate clinical deterioration and increase the risk of bacterial infections.\n\nOptions B and D: Lamivudine and Telbivudine are not considered first-line agents for HBV infections because of the increased frequency of resistance associated with their use.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Sofosbuvir is indicated in the treatment of which of the following patients?\n\n1. A 33-year-old man with post-transfusion hepatitis B\n\n2. A 47-year-old man with symptomatic chronic hepatitis C\n\n3. A 24-year-old man with hepatitis B and D coinfection\n\n4. A 52-year-old woman with hepatitis B and D superinfection\n\n5. A 42-year-old woman with asymptomatic chronic hepatitis C","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 5 only","2, 3 and 4 only"],"a":2,"e":"Sofosbuvir is indicated in the treatment of hepatitis C infection only. It is a directly acting antiviral, NS5B nucleotide polymerase inhibitor.\n\nThe goal of treatment in hepatitis C is a sustained virologic response (SVR). It is defined as the absence of a virus in the blood (undetectable HCV RNA), 12 weeks after completion of treatment. This can be achieved by the use of pan-genotypic directly acting antiviral drugs, irrespective of the disease stage.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a direct-acting agent (DAAs) used in the treatment of HCV infections?","o":["NS5B polymerase inhibitors","NS5A inhibitors","NS3/4A protease inhibitors","NS4B inhibitors"],"a":3,"e":"NS4B inhibitors are not direct-acting agents (DAAs).\n\nDirect-acting agents (DAAs) are drugs that target specific viral proteins which are vital to the replication and lifecycle of HCV.\n\nThe currently available DAAs inhibit 3 specific non-structural (NS) proteins:\n\n•\n\nNS3/4A protease inhibitors\n\n•\n\nNS5A inhibitors\n\n•\n\nNS5B polymerase inhibitors","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old woman with history of hepatitis B infection presents with fatigue, vomiting, and weight loss. Her serological markers are given in the table below. What is the treatment of choice for her condition?\n\nDiagnostic markers\nResult\n\nHBsAg\n+\n\nIgM Anti-HBc\n-\n\nHDAg\n-\n\nHDV RNA\n+\n\nIgG Anti-HDV\n+","o":["Paritaprevir booster with ritonavir","Pegylated interferon-alpha","Velpatasvir + Voxilaprevir + Sofosbuvir","Tenofovir + Entecavir"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario and serology are suggestive of chronic hepatitis D infection in a hepatitis B carrier for which the treatment of choice is pegylated interferon-alpha (pegIFN-α).\n\nIt is the only drug currently available to treat chronic hepatitis D infection.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young woman presents with painless warty lesions on the vulva as shown in the image below. Which of the following is a topical agent that can be prescribed for her condition?","o":["Docosanol","Nystatin","Imiquimod","Acyclovir"],"a":2,"e":"Warty lesions on the vulva as seen in the given image is suggestive of condyloma acuminata caused by HPV (6, 11). Imiquimod is a topical agent with antiviral and immunomodulatory properties indicated in the treatment of external genital and perianal warts.\n\nOther topical agents that can be used include podophyllotoxin and trichloroacetic acid.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c8066293e7f1477da782821d1de724de.PNG"],"t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is currently approved for the treatment of small pox?","o":["Tecovirimat","Lumacaftor","Pegvaliase","Apalutamide"],"a":0,"e":"Tecovirimat has been recently approved as a treatment of smallpox.\n\nIt was approved under the FDA's animal rule, which allows findings from animal studies to support an FDA approval when it is not feasible or ethical to conduct efficacy trials in humans. Cidofovir has also been approved by the FDA for smallpox under this rule.\n\nBoth these drugs are currently stockpiled by CDC’s strategic national stockpile, which has the medical supplies required to protect the public in case of a public health emergency.\n\nTecovirimat could also be used under an investigational new drug (IND) protocol to treat adverse reactions from vaccinia vaccination.\n\nOption B: Lumacaftor is a drug approved for cystic fibrosis.\n\nOption C: Pegvaliase has been approved for the treatment of phenylketonuria in adults.\n\nOption D: Apalutamide is a nonsteroidal antiandrogen medication which is used in the treatment of prostate cancer. It is specifically indicated for use in conjunction with castration in the treatment of non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.","t":"Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","c":"Antiviral, Antifungal and Antiparasitic Drugs"},
 {"q":"You decide to start recombinant growth hormone therapy in a child with idiopathic short stature. The treatment primarily works by the activation of which of the following?","o":["G protein coupled receptors","JAK/STAT pathway","Nuclear receptors","PI3/Akt pathway"],"a":1,"e":"Growth hormone primarily acts through activation of the JAK-STAT signaling pathway.\n\nCytokines such as interferon-gamma and prolactin hormone also act by the JAK STAT pathway.","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is growth hormone therapy not indicated?","o":["Turner syndrome","Prader Willi syndrome","Sotos syndrome","Noonan syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Growth hormone therapy is not indicated in Sotos syndrome.\n\nIndications for GH therapy are:\n\n• GH Deficiency in children & adults\n\n• AIDS-associated wasting\n\n• Malabsorption associated with the short-bowel syndrome (As GH stimulates the adaptation of GI epithelial cells).\n\n• Children with other conditions associated with short stature despite adequate GH production:\n\n• Turner syndrome\n\n• Noonan syndrome\n\n• Prader-Willi syndrome\n\n• Chronic renal insufficiency\n\n• Small for gestational age babies\n\n• Idiopathic short stature\n\nSotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) is characterized by excessive physical growth during the first few years of life resulting in a disproportionately large and long head with a slightly protrusive forehead, large hands and feet, hypertelorism, and down-slanting eyes. It is accompanied by mild cognitive impairment, developmental abnormalities, hypotonia, and speech abnormalities.\n\nThe image given below shows a large head, large hands, and characteristic eye features in a child with Sotos syndrome.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5abbb04bf06249f0a95cb0f7ff0a978ax440x704.PNG"],"t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"All of the following are sound advice to the respective patients except:\n\n1. A 5-year-old child is advised to undergo fundoscopic examination before the initiation of GH therapy\n\n2. A 7-year-old child on GH therapy is advised periodic fundoscopic examination\n\n3. An 8-year-old child on GH therapy is advised periodic fasting glucose monitoring\n\n4. A 17-year-old female who was receiving GH therapy and is now starting OCPs is prescribed an increased GH dose\n\n5. A 7-year-old child with leukemia who has recently gone into remission is advised to defer GH therapy for 3 years","o":["1, 2 and 5","only 5","2 and 4","3, 4 and 5"],"a":1,"e":"GH deferral is only recommended for the first year after treatment of leukemia.\n\nBecause of the anti-insulin action of GH, there is an increased incidence of type 2 diabetes mellitus and therefore, fasting glucose levels should be monitored periodically during GH therapy. Due to the rare potential for developing intracranial hypertension, fundoscopic examination is recommended at the initiation of therapy and at periodic intervals thereafter. Rapid growth induced by GH can cause slipped epiphyses or scoliosis in children.\n\nEstrogens (OCPs and estrogen supplements) inhibit GH action so that a larger dose is needed to maintain the same IGF-1 level.\n\nThe adverse effects of growth hormone are:\n\nChildren\nAdults\n\n• Intracranial hypertension\n\n• Increased risk of type 2 diabetes\n\n• Slipped epiphysis\n\n• Scoliosis\n\n• Peripheral edema\n\n• Carpal tunnel syndrome\n\n• Arthralgias\n\n• Myalgia","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man with tall stature, frontal bossing, prognathism, large hands, and feet is started on a growth hormone receptor antagonist. Identify the drug.","o":["Octreotide","Lanreotide","Pegvisomant","Cabergoline"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario depicts acromegaly. Pegvisomant is a growth hormone receptor antagonist that is indicated for the treatment of acromegaly.\n\nOctreotide and lanreotide are synthetic somatostatin analogs that can be used in the management of acromegaly.\n\nCabergoline is a dopamine agonist used primarily to treat hyperprolactinemia also has an indication in the management of acromegaly.","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old lady presented to the gynecology OPD with complaints of amenorrhea and milky discharge from her breasts. Her urine pregnancy test is negative and her serum prolactin level is 175 µg/L. The resident prescribes a long-acting D2 agonist. Which of the following drugs is she likely to have been prescribed?","o":["Bromocriptine","Lisuride","Cabergoline","Apomorphine"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario depicts hyperprolactinemia. Cabergoline is a long-acting D2 agonist.\n\nIts t1/2 is >60 hours. It is indicated for the treatment of hyperprolactinemia and acromegaly.\n\nBromocriptine, lisuride, and apomorphine are short-acting when compared with cabergoline.\n\nBromocriptine is an ergot alkaloid derivative used in the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus, hyperprolactinemia, acromegaly, and Parkinson's disease. Cabergoline is preferred over bromocriptine for hyperprolactinemia due to lower adverse effects such as nausea and also due to dosing advantage (twice a week instead of daily dosing).\n\nLisuride is indicated in early Parkinson's disease but is also known to decrease prolactin levels and prevent migraine attacks. Apomorphine is a morphine derivative used to treat hypomobile \"off\" episodes of advanced Parkinson's disease.","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is octreotide not used?","o":["Glucagonoma","Insulinoma","Short bowel syndrome","Glioma"],"a":3,"e":"Octreotide is not used in glioma.\n\nUses of octreotide are :\n\n1. Secretory diarrhea in carcinoid syndrome and VIPoma\n\n2. Short bowel syndrome, AIDS\n\n3. Pituitary tumors causing acromegaly\n\n4. Islet cell tumors like glucagonoma, insulinoma\n\n5. Acute bleeding in esophageal varices\n\n6. Thyrotrope adenoma\n\nAdverse effects include gall stones, sinus bradycardia, and conduction abnormalities. Vit B12 deficiency can occur on long term use.","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Valvular heart disease is not associated with the use of which of the following drugs?","o":["Bromocriptine","Quinagolide","Cabergoline","Pergolide"],"a":1,"e":"Quinagolide is not associated with valvular heart disease.\n\nQuinagolide is a non-ergot D2 receptor agonist.\n\nCabergoline, Bromocriptine, and Pergolide are ergot derivatives and are associated with valvular heart disease. The risk of valvular heart disease from ergot therapy is associated with higher doses of the drug (necessary for Parkinson's disease treatment) but not with the lower doses used in treating hyperprolactinemia.\n\nNote: Pergolide was withdrawn from the market due to reports of serious valve damage.","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is bromocriptine not indicated?","o":["A 35-year-old lady with galactorrhea-amenorrhea complex","A 30-year-old male patient with acromegaly","A 23-year old lady with endogenous depression","A 45-year old parkinson's patient with type 2 diabetes mellitus"],"a":2,"e":"Bromocriptine is not indicated in endogenous depression.\n\nIt is a semisynthetic ergot D2 receptor agonist.\n\nUses of bromocriptine:\n\n1. Hyperprolactinemia (causing galactorrhoea, amenorrhea, or infertility in women and gynecomastia or infertility in men)\n\n2. Parkinsonism\n\n3. Acromegaly\n\n4. Type 2 diabetes mellitus\n\n5. Hepatic coma\n\n6. Supression of lactation","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old chronic alcoholic patient with a history of coronary bypass surgery presents with haematemesis to the casualty. An upper GI endoscopy shows the following finding. Which of the following drugs will you administer to this patient?","o":["Desmopressin","Propranolol","Octreotide","Terlipressin"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical history of the patient and the endoscopic image showing prominent cherry-red spots are suggestive of bleeding esophageal varices.\n\nOctreotide is indicated in the treatment of acute variceal bleeding. It is a somatostatin analog that reduces azygous blood flow significantly and decreases variceal bleeding.\n\nOther drugs that can be used for the management of bleeding esophageal varices:\n\n• Terlipressin\n\n• Vasopressin\n\n• Propranolol\n\nOption A: Desmopressin is a selective V2 receptor agonist used for central diabetes insipidus and nocturnal enuresis.\n\nOption B: Propranolol (non-selective beta-blocker) is used in the prophylaxis of variceal bleeding and not in its acute management.\n\nOption D: Terlipressin is a vasoconstrictor used for bleeding esophageal varices but may compromise cardiac function and induce ischemia and hence can't be used in this case.\n\nNote: Although terlipressin is a vasoconstrictor and is be used for bleeding oesophagal varices, the patient in this scenario has a history of MI (undergone coronary bypass surgery). Terlipressin can compromise cardiac function (reduces cardiac output and ejection fraction) and induce ischemia. Hence, it is not preferred in this case.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8a0f088f40a64d359eb049aa6ff287c7.PNG"],"t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Pulsatile dosing of GnRH agonist is used for the treatment of __________.","o":["Male infertility","Endometriosis","Precocious puberty","Prostate cancer"],"a":0,"e":"Pulsatile administration of GnRH agonists results in increased FSH and LH secretion and is therefore used to treat male and female infertility.\n\nNon-pulsatile or continuous dose of GnRH agonist results in suppression and decreased concentrations of gonadotropins LH and FSH. They are used in:\n\n1. Endometriosis\n\n2. Uterine fibroids\n\n3. Central precocious puberty\n\n4. Ca prostate\n\n5. Ca ovary and breast\n\n6. Controlled ovary stimulation","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old lady who is suffering from dysmenorrhea and infertility is diagnosed with endometriosis. She has been prescribed an intranasal spray containing a GnRH agonist. What is the likely drug?","o":["Leuprolide","Goserelin","Histrelin","Nafarelin"],"a":3,"e":"Nafarelin is a GnRH agonist administered via nasal spray.\n\nIt is approved for use in endometriosis and central precocious puberty.\n\nRoutes of administration of other GnRH agonists:\n\n• Intranasal and subcutaneous - Buserelin\n\n• Intramuscular and subcutaneous - Leuprolide\n\n• Subcutaneous implants - Histrelin and Goserelin\n\n Some GnRH antagonists are:\n\n• Degarelix\n\n• Ganirelix\n\n• Cetrorelix (Synthetic)\n\n All GnRH antagonists are administered subcutaneously.","t":"Hypothalamus And Pituitary","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In comparison to thyroxine, liothyronine has ___________.","o":["Longer half life","Better absorption","Slower onset of action","Less potency"],"a":1,"e":"Liothyronine has better absorption compared to levothyroxine. It is almost completely absorbed (100%) when compared to thyroxine (80%).\n\n Liothyronine(T3)\n Levothyroxine(T4)\n\nOnset of action\nRapid\nSlow\n\nPotency\nMore potent\nLess potent\n\nHalf-life\nT1/2 -18-24 hr\nT1/2 - 7-8 days\n\nSource\n25% from gland; 75% from peripheral conversion\nSolely produced by the gland\n\nPlasma protein binding\n0.2% unbound\n0.02% unbound\n\nNote: Liotrix is a 4:1 mixture of Levothyroxine sodium (LT4): Liothyronine sodium (LT3)","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old hypothyroid patient taking levothyroxine regularly has now become pregnant. What would your advice be regarding levothyroxine?","o":["Switch to liothyronine as levothyroxine is teratogenic","Increase the dose as pregnancy causes an increased demand for levothyroxine","Continue the same dose but monitor the serum levels on a fortnightly basis","Stop levothyroxine till the end of the first trimester as it has teratogenic potential"],"a":1,"e":"The dose of levothyroxine in patients who become pregnant should usually be increased. This is because demand for levothyroxine increases in pregnancy and low thyroxine levels are associated with adverse fetal outcomes such as mental retardation.\n\nThe dose of levothyroxine is increased by 30-50% by asking the patient to take 2 extra pills per week. Increased demand for levothyroxine in pregnancy is due to:\n\n• The increased serum concentration of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) induced by estrogen\n\n• Increased expression of type 3 deiodinase in the placenta\n\n• A small amount of passage of levothyroxine to the fetus\n\nThyroid hormones are not teratogenic. Liothyronine is not preferred in pregnancy because T3 has a short half-life and requires frequent dosing to limit large fluctuations in levels. In addition, excess T3 often acts to normalize TSH while lowering T4, thereby risking inadequate T4 availability to the fetus.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on levothyroxine supplementation would need to increase her dose if she is taking _______.","o":["Methylprednisolone","Aspirin","Estradiol","Furosemide"],"a":2,"e":"Patients who start estrogen therapy will need to increase their thyroid supplementation doses.\n\nEstrogens raise the circulating levels of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG), thereby increasing the bound fraction and decreasing the free (bioactive) fraction of circulating thyroxine (T4). As a consequence, estrogen therapy increases the T4 supplementation requirements of women being treated for primary hypothyroidism.\n\nDrugs increasing TBG levels:\n\n• Estrogens\n\n• Tamoxifen\n\n• Methadone\n\n• 5-fluorouracil\n\nDrugs decreasing TBG levels:\n\n• Androgens\n\n• Corticosteroids\n\n• Niacin\n\nDrugs decreasing binding of T4 to TBG (by displacement):\n\n• Salicylates (aspirin, mefenamic acid)\n\n• Heparin\n\n• Furosemide","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old patient suffering from Graves disease has been on treatment for the past three weeks. She has now developed fever and sore throat with an absolute neutrophil count of 80 neutrophils per microlitre of blood. The use of which of the following drugs can result in such a clinical picture?\n\n1. Methimazole\n\n2. Propylthiouracil\n\n3. Lugol's iodine\n\n4. Corticosteroids\n\n5. Propranolol","o":["Only 1","1, 2 or 3","1 or 2","3, 4 or 5"],"a":2,"e":"This patient is suffering from agranulocytosis which is a serious adverse effect that can be seen with the use of either methimazole or propylthiouracil.\n\nAgranulocytosis usually occurs in the initial weeks or months of therapy. It occurs rapidly and periodic monitoring of granulocyte levels is generally ineffective.\n\nHowever, it is reversible on discontinuation of the drug. Therefore, patients who are on these drugs should be instructed to report immediately if they develop fever or sore throat.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient developed thyroid storm in the post-operative ward and was immediately started on IV propranolol and a loading dose of propylthiouracil. The antithyroid drug acts by inhibiting which of the following deiodinases?","o":["D1 only","D2 only","D1 and D2","D3"],"a":0,"e":"Propylthiouracil inhibits only type I deiodinase (D1).\n\nDeiodinases are the enzymes that are involved in the peripheral conversion of T4 to either T3 or reverse T3. Type I deiodinase (D1) and type II deiodinase (D2) convert T4 to T3. Type III (D3) is involved in the conversion of T4 to reverse T3.\n\nNote: Amiodarone inhibits both types I and type II deiodinases.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/686777488e554c3a874bb81252c8993cx1280x1660.JPEG"],"t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man presented with complaints of increased sweating and weight loss over the past two months. On examination, he is tachycardic, has exophthalmos, and a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland. Which of the following is the drug of choice for the management of this condition?","o":["Methimazole","Propylthiouracil","Levothyroxine","Lugol's iodine"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Graves disease and the drug of choice is methimazole.\n\nOption A: Methimazole is preferred over propylthiouracil due to fewer side effects and single-daily dosing (compared to PTU which is given TID).\n\nOption C: Levothyroxine is used in hypothyroidism.\n\nOption D: Lugol's iodine is a thyroid hormone-release inhibitor but not used as a first-line drug.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old primiparous woman presented with features suggestive of thyrotoxicosis, at 7 weeks of gestation. Which of the following is the drug of choice for the treatment of her condition?","o":["Methimazole","Carbimazole","Propylthiouracil","Radioactive iodine"],"a":2,"e":"The drug of choice for the treatment of thyrotoxicosis during the first trimester of pregnancy is propylthiouracil (PTU). It is because it has lower teratogenic effects than carbimazole or methimazole.\n\nCarbimazole and methimazole are associated with teratogenic effects such as:\n\n• Aplasia cutis congenita\n\n• Choanal atresia\n\n• Treacheosopageal fistula\n\n• Fetal goiter\n\nHowever, propylthiouracil use is limited to the first trimester due to the risk of hepatotoxicity. It is replaced with carbimazole or methimazole in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters of the pregnancy.\n\nThe lowest effective antithyroid drug dose should be used throughout gestation. This is to maintain the maternal serum-free T4 level in the upper limit of the non-pregnant normal reference range.\n\nRadioiodine therapy is contraindicated during pregnancy and lactation.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements best describes the Wolff-Chaikoff effect?","o":["Decrease in thyroid hormone release due to low doses of iodine","Increase in thyroid hormone release due to high doses of iodine","Decrease in thyroid hormone synthesis due to high doses of iodine","Increase in thyroid hormone synthesis due to low iodine intake"],"a":2,"e":"The Wolff-Chaikoff effect is best described as iodine-induced hypothyroidism.\n\nIt is a normal, protective downregulation of thyroid hormone synthesis and release via transient inhibition of iodide organification in the presence of an iodine load (exogenous supplementation, iodinated contrast dye, drugs like amiodarone). This occurs in the thyroid gland which is under pituitary control.\n\nWith persistent high iodine load, an escape phenomenon occurs in normal individuals after around 10 days as the iodide levels fall inside the follicle. In autoimmune diseases (Hashimoto’s or Grave’s), however, the suppression may persist.\n\nThe Jod-Basedow phenomenon is iodine-induced hyperthyroidism. It is also seen after an iodine load. However, it is seen in thyroid glands where autoregulation and pituitary control have failed. When exposed to an iodine load in excess, instead of blocking the organification process, the thyroid gland (areas of the autonomously functioning gland) continues to uptake the iodine, producing more thyroid hormone independent of normal regulatory mechanisms. It is seen in endemic goiter, toxic multinodular goiter, thyroid adenoma, and sometimes in Grave’s disease.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient suffering from Graves disease is posted for thyroidectomy. He is administered iodides preoperatively. What is the primary benefit of administrating iodides in this patient?","o":["To render the patient euthyroid","To make the gland firm and less vascular","To prevent hypothyroidism","To augment radioactive iodine uptake"],"a":1,"e":"The primary benefit of the preoperative use of iodides in Grave’s disease is to make the gland firm and less vascular. This makes manipulation and surgical resection easier.\n\nAntithyroid drugs like methimazole or carbimazole are indicated to render the patient euthyroid.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient suffering from hyperthyroidism is administered a drug that acts by inhibiting the release of thyroid hormone. Identify the drug.","o":["Propylthiouracil","Radioactive iodine","Thiocyanate","Potassium iodide"],"a":3,"e":"Potassium iodide inhibits the release of the thyroid hormone.\n\nIodides of sodium and potassium and Lugol's iodine act by inhibiting thyroid hormone release. Hence, they are also called thyroid constipating agents. They are the fastest-acting agents.\n\nThe beneficial effect of iodides peaks over 10-15 days and gradually disappears. Tolerance is hence an issue with the use of iodides.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 43-year-old lady with papillary thyroid cancer is being treated with radioactive iodine 131. What is the half-life of this isotope?","o":["8 hours","8 days","8 weeks","8 years"],"a":1,"e":"The half-life of radioactive I 131 is 8 days\n\nI131 emits both beta and gamma rays. It is used both for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes for thyroid disorders.\n\nI127 is the stable isotope of iodine.\n\nI123 is the radioactive isotope of iodine that emits only gamma rays. The action is short-lived with a half-life of around 13 hours. It is indicated for diagnostic indications only.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about Lugol's iodine is incorrect?","o":["Used to make thyroid gland less vascular","Used to block thyroid hormone release rapidly","Tolerance is an issue","Augments radioactive iodine uptake into thyroid gland"],"a":3,"e":"Lugol's iodine does not augment the uptake of radioactive iodine in the thyroid gland.\n\nRadioactive iodine uptake into the thyroid gland is reduced by Lugol's iodine as the uptake is inversely proportional to the serum concentration of stable iodine.\n\nLugol's iodine makes the thyroid gland less vascular and firmer. It blocks the release of thyroid hormone. The beneficial effect peaks over 10-15 days and gradually disappears. Therefore tolerance is an issue.","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old lady who is a known case of Graves disease was posted for thyroidectomy. During the surgery, she developed hyperthermia, agitation and the monitors showed an arrhythmia. She was managed with aggressive IV fluid therapy, ice packs, and multiple drugs. Which of the following drugs that were used, do not inhibit the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3?","o":["Propranolol","Glucocorticoids","Iopanoic acid","Potassium iodide"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical picture is suggestive of a thyrotoxic crisis/thyroid storm. Potassium iodide does not inhibit the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3.\n\nThe major action of potassium iodide is the inhibition of hormone release. It is usually given 1 hour after PTU administration to avoid iodine stimulating thyroid hormone synthesis.\n\nDrugs inhibiting the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 are:\n\n• Propranolol\n\n• Glucocorticoids\n\n• Propylthiouracil\n\n• Amiodarone\n\n• Iopanoic acid","t":"Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Glucocorticoids act on which of the following?","o":["G protein couple receptor","Intracellular cytoplasmic receptor","Ligand gated ion channel","JAK-STAT pathway"],"a":1,"e":"Glucocorticoids act on the intracellular cytoplasmic receptors.\n\nThey diffuse into cytosol and bind to the cytoplasmic receptors and influence nuclear changes by activation of the receptor.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Glucocorticoids do not have permissive action on which of the following?","o":["Angiotensin","Adrenaline","Noradrenaline","Bradykinin"],"a":3,"e":"Glucocorticoids do not have permissive action on bradykinin. They inhibit inflammatory mediators such as histamine, bradykinin, and eicosanoids.\n\nGlucocorticoids possess permissive action on the vasoconstrictor effect of angiotensin and lipolytic action of adrenaline and noradrenaline. In the absence of glucocorticoids, these hormones have low activity but the presence enhances their activity several folds.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A pregnant woman diagnosed with Cushing's syndrome does not want surgery and requests medical treatment. Which of the following drugs can be recommended?","o":["Mifepristone","Flutamide","Finasteride","Metyrapone"],"a":3,"e":"Metyrapone can be recommended as it is approved in the treatment of pregnant women with Cushing's syndrome. It inhibits the synthesis of cortisol by inhibiting steroid 11 β-hydroxylase reversibly.\n\nCushing’s syndrome (CS) is a manifestation of symptoms that are precipitated by excessive cortisol production. Transsphenoidal surgery, radiation, and pharmacotherapy constitute the standard treatment for tumor-related causes of CS (e.g. pituitary adenoma).\n\nThe three main pharmacological approaches are:\n\n• Cortisol synthesis inhibitors: Ketoconazole, metyrapone, mitotane, etomidate, aminoglutethimide\n\n• ACTH secretion inhibitors: Cyproheptadine, pasireotide, octreotide, cabergoline\n\n• Glucocorticoid receptor antagonist: Mifepristone\n\nNote: Metyrapone is also used to test the hypothalamic-pituitary feedback pathway in the diagnosis of Cushing syndrome.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"The enzyme A in the following image corresponds to?","o":["17β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase1","11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase2"],"a":3,"e":"\"A\" here corresponds to 11-beta-HSD2 (11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase2). It converts active cortisol to inactive cortisone.\n\nCortisol is active and can bind to both glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoid receptors whereas cortisone is inactive and does not bind to either. The selective inactivation of cortisol is required in certain sites such as the distal nephron so that the specific autoregulatory responses to aldosterone can be achieved.\n\nTherefore, cortisol (active) is metabolized to cortisone (inactive) by 11-beta-HSD2 in the kidney, so as to protect renal mineralocorticoid receptors from cortisol binding. Some tissues, like the liver, can convert inactive cortisone back to active cortisol through an enzyme called 11-beta-HSD1 (\"B\" in the image). This completes the “cortisone–cortisol shuttle” because the kidney can inactivate cortisol where it is not needed and the liver can reactivate it afterward.\n\nHowever, when circulating cortisol levels are very high, as in severe Cushing syndrome, they can overwhelm this protective mechanism, allowing cortisol to bind to and activate the mineralocorticoid receptor. This leads to volume expansion, hypertension, and hypokalemia often observed in hypercortisolism. A mutation of 11-beta-HSD2 also causes a similar clinical picture and is the reason for the syndrome of apparent mineralocorticoid excess.\n\n17β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase and 3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase are involved in the metabolism of sex steroids.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce85a543b090472f885a51539c8d76cc.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb2fd6d0efba4d66b6788326ecc331d2x1280x1437.JPEG"],"t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a prodrug?","o":["Prednisolone","Methylprednisolone","Hydrocortisone","Prednisone"],"a":3,"e":"Prednisone is a prodrug.\n\nIt is a synthetic glucocorticoid that is biologically inert until converted via hepatic metabolism (11-beta HSD1) to the active component, prednisolone.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with moderate COVID-19 was started on a long-acting glucocorticoid. Which of the following drugs could it be?","o":["Triamcinolone","Prednisolone","Dexamethasone","Hydrocortisone"],"a":2,"e":"Dexamethasone is the longest acting glucocorticoid. Its t1/2 is >36 hours.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Highest mineralocorticoid activity is seen with which of the following?","o":["Aldosterone","DOCA","Fludrocortisone","Triamcinolone"],"a":0,"e":"The highest mineralocorticoid activity is seen with aldosterone.\n\nHowever, it cannot be used clinically. The highest mineralocorticoid activity in a drug that is used clinically is fludrocortisone.\n\nGlucocorticoid activity\nMineralocorticoid activity\n\nShort-acting glucocorticoids\n\nCortisone\n\nHydrocortisone\n\n0.8\n\n1\n\n0.8\n\n1\n\nIntermediate-acting glucocorticoids\n\nPrednisone-prednisolone\n\nMethylprednisolone\n\n4\n\n5\n\n0.8\n\n0.5\n\nLong-acting glucocorticoids\n\nDexamethasone\n\nBetamethasone\n\n30\n\n30\n\n0\n\n0\n\nMineralocorticoids\n\nDeoxycorticosterone (DOCA)\n\nFludrocortisone\n\nAldosterone\n\n0.2\n\n15\n\n0\n\n100\n\n150\n\n200-500","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist?","o":["Spironolactone","Eplerenone","Drospirenone","Metyrapone"],"a":3,"e":"Metyrapone is not a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist.\n\nIt is a selective inhibitor of 11β-hydroxylase and thereby reduces cortisol and corticosterone synthesis.\n\nOptions A and B - Spironolactone and eplerenone are aldosterone antagonist that is used in hypertension and chronic CHF for reversing the effects of aldosterone\n\nOption C - Drospirenone is a 4th generation progestin used as an oral contraceptive having mineralocorticoid antagonist action","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A woman diagnosed with Sheehan's syndrome is planned for the initiation of hormone replacement. Glucocorticoids should be administered prior to thyroxine supplementation due to what concern?","o":["Interference in absorption of glucocorticoids","Precipitation acute adrenal insufficiency","Decrease in the metabolism of glucocorticoids","Suppression the HPA axis"],"a":1,"e":"Glucocorticoids are administered before initiating treatment with thyroid hormones as administration of thyroxine can precipitate acute adrenal insufficiency.\n\nIn the case of combined adrenal insufficiency and hypothyroidism, as in panhypopituitarism (due to surgery or Sheehan's syndrome), adrenal insufficiency is generally treated first. This is because giving thyroxine first can enhance cortisol clearance and precipitate an adrenal crisis. Also, levothyroxine increases the basal metabolic rate (BMR) leading to an increased requirement of cortisol, which if not met can also precipitate an adrenal crisis.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A pregnant woman came for routine antenatal screening. During prenatal testing, the fetus was suspected to have congenital adrenal hyperplasia. What is the drug of choice for treating the fetus in-utero?","o":["Dexamethasone","Betamethasone","Hydrocortisone","Prednisolone"],"a":0,"e":"The drug of choice for treating the fetus in-utero when suspected of having congenital adrenal hyperplasia is dexamethasone.\n\nDexamethasone readily crosses the placenta and suppresses the secretion of steroids and androgens by fetal adrenal glands. If started by 6 weeks of gestation, it reduces the virilization of external genitalia in affected females.\n\nChorionic villus biopsy is then performed to determine the sex and the genotype of the fetus and therapy is only continued if the fetus is an affected female.\n\nThis is not widely accepted currently due to insufficient data on long-term risks especially in unaffected females and males who derive no benefit from the treatment.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is started on hormone replacement for chronic adrenal insufficiency. What is the replacement dose of hydrocortisone that has to be given (in mg/day)?","o":["10","15","20","5"],"a":2,"e":"The replacement dose of hydrocortisone for chronic adrenal insufficiency is 20 mg/day.\n\nThe treatment of chronic adrenal insufficiency (not in crisis) requires glucocorticoid replacement at a dose that replaces the physiologic daily cortisol production. This is achieved by administering an oral replacement dose of 15-25mg Hydrocortisone.\nA midpoint dose of 20mg is given in 2-3 divided doses: 10mg + 5mg + 5mg.\nA higher morning dose is given at 8:30 am (usually 1/2 to 2/3 of the total dose). The next dose is given at 1:30 pm and in the evening (4:30-5:00 pm). This is to follow the normal circadian rhythm.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A pregnant woman at 33 weeks of gestation presents to your antenatal clinic with preterm labour. Which corticosteroid will you administer for stimulation of fetal lung maturation?","o":["Hydrocortisone","Triamcinolone","Methylprednisolone","Betamethasone"],"a":3,"e":"Betamethasone is indicated for the stimulation of fetal lung maturation in preterm labor.\n\nAs compared to cortisol, betamethasone binds to maternal proteins less and has less placental metabolism. This allows increased transfer to the fetus. Additionally, infants exposed to betamethasone are less likely to require postnatal resuscitation. 2 doses of 12 mg each are administered 18-24 hours apart when the delivery is anticipated before 34 weeks of gestation.\n\nDexamethasone is another long-acting glucocorticoid that can be given. It has benefits such as less risk of intraventricular hemorrhage and a shorter length of stay in the NICU.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with chronic adrenal insufficiency is on long term corticosteroid therapy. Which of the following side effects is he unlikely to experience?","o":["Muscular hypertrophy","Peptic ulceration","Psychosis","Hypertension"],"a":0,"e":"Corticosteroid therapy does not cause muscular hypertrophy and the patient is unlikely to experience it as a side effect.\n\nExcess glucocorticoid activity results in muscle wasting and myopathy while excess mineralocorticoid activity results in hypokalemia and causes muscle weakness.\n\nOptions B and C: Peptic ulceration and psychosis are known adverse effects of corticosteroids.\n\nOption D: Glucocorticoids with some mineralocorticoid activity will result in sodium and water retention and may cause hypertension.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are planning to add oral steroids to manage a case of severe asthma. Presence of which of the following comorbidities is not a relative contraindication for starting the medication?","o":["Diabetes mellitus","Hypertension","Eczematous skin disease","Peptic ulcer disease"],"a":2,"e":"Eczematous skin diseases are not a contraindication for the use of oral corticosteroids. In fact, they are highly effective and are indicated in this condition.\n\nAll the contraindications to steroids are relative because of the life-saving nature of steroids.\n\nRisk-benefit should be assessed cautiously when initiating steroids in the following conditions:\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Peptic ulcer\n\n• Viral and fungal infections\n\n• Tuberculosis and other infections\n\n• Osteoporosis\n\n• Herpes simplex keratitis\n\n• Epilepsy\n\n• CHF\n\n• Renal failure","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old woman is being evaluated for chronic back pain. She mentions that she had been on corticosteroids for asthma since childhood. Which of the following actions of the drug would not cause her condition?","o":["Inhibition of gonadal steroidal hormones","Reduced gastrointestinal absorption of calcium","Suppressive action on osteoblasts","Inhibition of action of osteoclasts"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic back pain following long term corticosteroid therapy is suggestive of osteoporosis. Glucocorticoids do not inhibit of the action of osteoclasts.\n\nIt causes stimulation of osteoclasts and increases bone resorption.\n\nMechanism of glucocorticoids induced osteoporosis:\n\n• Inhibition of gonadal steroidal hormones\n\n• Reduced gastrointestinal absorption of calcium\n\n• Secondary effect of decreased calcium absorption: increased parathyroid hormone leading to further resorption\n\n• Suppressive action on osteoblasts\n\n• Stimulation of osteoclasts","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Osteonecrosis following glucocorticoid therapy is most commonly associated with which site?","o":["Femoral head","Distal femur","Jaw bone","Humeral head"],"a":0,"e":"The femoral head is the most frequently affected site by osteonecrosis due to glucocorticoid therapy.\n\nThe earliest symptoms are joint pain and stiffness. When patients on steroid therapy develop hip, shoulder or knee pain, osteonecrosis must be considered and ruled out.\n\nOptions B and D - It can also affect the distal femur and humeral head\n\nOption C - Osteonecrosis of the jaw is associated with the use of bisphosphonates","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with Cushing’s syndrome has very high levels of ACTH. Which of the following is a CRH preparation that can be used to determine if it is a pituitary source or an ectopic source?","o":["Gonadorelin","Corticorelin","Ganirelix","Cosyntropin"],"a":1,"e":"Corticorelin is a corticotrophin-releasing hormone (CRH) preparation used in CRH stimulation test which helps to differentiate between a pituitary source and an ectopic source for ACTH-dependent hypercortisolism.\n\nPituitary tumors tend to be sensitive to CRH stimulation and responds with a rise in cortisol while ectopic tumors do not usually respond. Ectopic sources of ACTH include carcinoid, medullary thyroid carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma.\n\nIt can also be used to test the HPA axis.\n\nOption A - Gonadorelin is a recombinant GnRH.\n\nOption C - Ganirelix is a GnRH antagonist.\n\nOption D - Cosyntropin is a synthetic preparation of ACTH.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a steroid receptor antagonist that can be given to a patient with Cushing’s syndrome due to ectopic ACTH?","o":["Ketoconazole","Metyrapone","Mifepristone","Aminoglutethimide"],"a":2,"e":"Mifepristone is a steroid receptor antagonist that can be used in Cushing’s syndrome.\n\nIt is used in Cushing’s syndrome especially in inoperable cases with ectopic ACTH secretion or adrenal carcinoma.\n\nOptions A, B, and D - Ketoconazole, metyrapone, and aminoglutethimide reduce steroid synthesis by enzyme inhibition.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following intravenous anaesthetics can be used for acute control of hypercortisolism?","o":["Thiopentone","Propofol","Ketamine","Etomidate"],"a":3,"e":"The intravenous anesthetic that can be used for acute control of hypercortisolism is etomidate.\n\nEtomidate at sub-hypnotic doses inhibits cortisol secretion by selectively inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase activity.\n\nIt is used off-label to treat hypercortisolism when rapid control is needed and the patient is not able to take oral medication.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with inoperable adrenocortical carcinoma and needs medical adrenalectomy. Which of the following drugs could be used for him?","o":["Mitotane","Methotrexate","Doxorubicin","5-Fluorouracil"],"a":0,"e":"Mitotane is used for medical adrenalectomy in this patient.\n\nIt has a non-selective toxic action on the adrenal cortex. It causes suppression of adrenal cortex hormone secretion comparable to that of adrenalectomy.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antifungal drugs can be used in the treatment of a patient with Cushing syndrome?","o":["Ketoconazole","Fluconazole","Itraconazole","Miconazole"],"a":0,"e":"Ketoconazole can be used in the treatment of Cushing syndrome for symptomatic control.\n\nIt is a non-selective inhibitor of adrenal and gonadal steroid synthesis. These actions are more apparent at higher doses than those used for fungal infections.","t":"Corticosteroids","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following decreases both serum calcium and phosphate levels?","o":["Vitamin D","Parathyroid hormone","Calcitonin","Vitamin A"],"a":2,"e":"Calcitonin decreases both serum calcium and phosphate levels.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81cacc6be93e4ea4994da307a1fd6057x1280x850.JPEG"],"t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following interferes with the absorption of calcium?\n\n1. Glucocorticoids\n\n2. Phenytoin\n\n3. Tetracycline\n\n4. Phytates\n\n5. Oxalates","o":["1,2 and 3","4 and 5","1,2,3 and 4","1,2,3,4 and 5"],"a":3,"e":"Glucocorticoids, phenytoin, tetracyclines, phytates, and oxalates all interfere with the absorption of calcium.\n\nGlucocorticoids and phenytoin depress intestinal calcium transport whereas, tetracyclines, phytates, phosphates, and oxalates form insoluble complexes with calcium and thereby interfere with calcium absorption.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Following a road traffic accident, a patient underwent transfusion with 5 units of packed red blood cells. Soon after, he developed tetany. His serum calcium level was 7.5 mg/dl on lab reports. Which of the following is preferred as the treatment of choice for the given condition?","o":["Calcium chloride","Calcium gluconate","Calcium carbonate","Calcium lactate"],"a":1,"e":"This is a case of hypocalcemia following a massive blood transfusion and IV calcium gluconate is the preferred treatment.\n\nThe hypocalcemia results from Ca2+ chelation by citrate (added to the blood product as preservative and anticoagulant). Calcium gluconate is the preferred over calcium chloride for the emergency treatment of hypocalcemia because it is less irritating and better tolerated.\n\nHowever, calcium chloride may be preferable to calcium gluconate in the presence of abnormal liver function, because decreased citrate metabolism results in a slower release of ionized calcium. It is generally reserved for severe symptomatic hypocalcemia, (<4 mg/dL) and when given, should be administered via a central venous access device to avoid extravasation and tissue necrosis.\n\nCalcium carbonate and calcium lactate are oral preparations of calcium.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true regarding plasma calcium?","o":["Ca2+ sensing receptor is a voltage gated ion channel","Albumin bound calcium is physiologically most important","Calcium is mainly absorbed from large intestine","Acidosis favours ionization of calcium"],"a":3,"e":"Acidosis favors the ionization of calcium.\n\nHyperventilation by causing alkalosis favors the deionization of calcium. Alkalosis result in precipitation of tetany and laryngospasm in calcium-deficient patients\n\nOption A - Ca2+ sensing receptor is a type of G protein coupled receptor.\n\nOption B - 50% of the Ca2+ is in ionized form and is physiologically most important.\n\nOption C - Calcium is mainly absorbed from the small intestine.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 57-year-old post menopausal lady has been on alendronate for the past two years. By which of the following mechanisms does this drug act?","o":["Increasing the osteoid formation","Increasing the mineralization of osteoid","Decreasing the osteoclast mediated resorption of bone","Decreasing the activity of osteoprotegerin"],"a":2,"e":"Alendronate is a bisphosphonate which acts by decreasing osteoclast mediated bone resorption.\n\nOsteoclasts internalize bisphosphonates as they are released along with calcium from the bone surface and then reduce osteoclast activity by:\n\n• Promoting apoptosis of osteoclasts\n\n• Disrupting the cytoskeleton and ruffled border of osteoclasts\n\n• Inhibiting the differentiation of osteoclasts","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an indication to use bisphosphonates?","o":["Paget’s disease","Vitamin D excess","Postmenopausal osteoporosis","Hypercalcemia of malignancy"],"a":1,"e":"Bisphosphonates are not indicated in the treatment of vitamin D excess.\n\nIndications for the use of bisphosphonates:\n\n• Osteoporosis\n\n• Paget’s disease\n\n• Hypercalcemia of malignancy\n\n• Osteolytic bone metastasis","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old lady has been taking zoledronate for the treatment of osteoporosis. She is at a high risk of developing which of the following side effects?","o":["Acute renal failure","Ventricular fibrillation","Peptic ulcer","Anterior uveitis"],"a":0,"e":"Zoledronate use has been associated with acute renal failure.\n\nIt is nephrotoxic. So baseline renal function and monitoring of renal function during therapy with zoledronate is recommended.\n\nLong term complications seen with bisphosphonate therapy are osteonecrosis of jaw and subtrochanteric fracture of femur.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"You start a patient on alendronate for osteoporosis. The patient enquires about the side effects and their management. Which of the following measures would you not recommend to decrease oesophageal irritation?","o":["Remaining upright for 30 minutes","Use of proton pump inhibitor at bedtime","Shifting to intravenous formulation","Administration with food"],"a":3,"e":"Bisphosphonates are not recommended to be administered with food to minimize esophageal irritation.\n\nOral bisphosphonates have very limited bioavailability (<1-10%). Bisphosphonates are recommended to be administered after an overnight fast with a full glass of water at least 30 minutes before breakfast.\n\nOptions A and B- Remaining upright for 30 minutes after dosing and taking proton pump inhibitors at bedtime can decrease the symptoms.\n\nOption C- As esophageal irritation is due to the local direct effect of the oral formulation, shifting to intravenous formulations reduces the symptoms.\n\nNote- The prescribing information of all the oral bisphosphonates states that it has to be taken along with tap or filtered water, and not mineral water as it contains higher amounts of calcium which can interfere with the absorption of bisphosphonates. It is prudent to inform patients to not have the medications with water having higher calcium levels.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is teriparatide indicated?","o":["Severe osteoporosis","Paget disease","Hypercalcemia","Osteosarcoma"],"a":0,"e":"Teriparatide is indicated in severe osteoporosis.\n\nTeriparatide is a recombinant human parathyroid preparation. It increases bone formation especially when given in low doses and intermittently.\n\nAlthough it may be more effective than bisphosphonates and estrogen, it has to be administered subcutaneously every day. Hence it indicated mostly in severe osteoporosis.\n\nTeriparatide increases the risk of osteosarcoma and it is contraindicated in conditions that causes high risk of osteosarcoma such as:\n\n• Paget disease\n\n• Prior h/o of radiotherapy\n\n• Young adults with open epiphysis\n\n• Unexplained elevated ALP\n\n• Hypercalcemia\n\nAbaloparatide is also a PTHrP preparation available.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old patient with severe backache has a T-score of -3. He is started on a monoclonal antibody against RANKL. Identify the drug.","o":["Romosozumab","Denosumab","Odanacatib","Blosozumab"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical history depicts a case of osteoporosis. Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL that is indicated in the treatment of osteoporosis.\n\nIt inhibits the binding of RANKL to RANK receptors on osteoclasts and thereby inhibiting the activation of osteoclast and also promotes their apoptosis.\n\nOptions A and D: Romosozumab and blosozumab are monoclonal antibodies against sclerostin (sclerostin blocks osteoblast proliferation).\n\nOption C: Odanacatib is an inhibitor of cathepsin K which is an enzyme in osteoclast that facilitates bone resorption.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A post-menopausal lady who is taking estrogen as part of her hormone replacement therapy reads about raloxifene on the internet and wants to change her current treatment. Which of the following is not an advantage of using raloxifene over estrogen?","o":["Reduces fracture rates","Avoids endometrial hyperplasia","Reduces incidence of venous thrombosis","No increase in incidence of breast carcinoma"],"a":2,"e":"Raloxifene causes a 3-fold increase in deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.\n\nIt is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) with the following actions:\n\n• Agonist actions:\n\n• Bone - increases bone mineral density and reduces the risk of vertebral fracture\n\n• Lipids - increases the risk of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.\n\n• Antagonist actions:\n\n• Breast - incidence of breast carcinoma is reduced\n\n• Uterus - endometrial hyperplasia is avoided","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old patient with chronic kidney disease developed secondary hyperparathyroidism for which he was treated appropriately, and was started on cinacalcet. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Calcitonin preparation","PTH preparation","Calcimimetic","Vitamin D3 analog"],"a":2,"e":"Cinacalcet is a calcimimetic agent.\n\nIt activates the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) in the parathyroids and blocks PTH secretion.\n\nCinacalcet is a type II calcimimetic agent , it means that it needs either Ca2+ or some other agonist to activate CaSR.\n\nIt is indicated for secondary hyperparathyroidism due to chronic kidney disease and tumor of parathyroid gland.\n\nType I calcimimetic agents are those which can activate CaSR directly such as aminoglycoside and polylysine.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old male patient who requires weekly hemodialysis is started on sevelamer. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Vitamin D analog","Calcium-sensing receptor mimetic","Phosphate binder","Potassium binder"],"a":2,"e":"Sevelamer is a phosphate binder indicated in chronic kidney disease.\n\nSevelamer is a nonabsorbable polymer that acts as a nonselective anion exchanger that lowers serum phosphate concentration.\n\nIt is indicated to lower phosphate levels in patients with chronic kidney disease who are on dialysis.\n\nNote: Patiromer is a potassium binder approved for use in hyperkalemia.","t":"Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following does not cause insulin release?","o":["Rosiglitazone","Nateglinide","Glimepiride","Tolbutamide"],"a":0,"e":"Rosiglitazone does not increase insulin release from the beta cells of the pancreas.\n\nRosiglitazone is a thiazolidinedione that acts by activating PPAR-gamma (nuclear receptor) which induces several genes involved in lipid and glucose metabolism and thereby, decreases insulin resistance.\n\nNateglinide (a meglitinide) and glimepiride, tolbutamide (both sulfonylureas) are insulin secretagogues that act by inhibiting the ATP-sensitive K+ channels of the beta cells of the pancreas to cause insulin release.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Insulin release due to the closure of K+ channels is seen with?","o":["Repaglinide","Metformin","Exenatide","Sitagliptin"],"a":0,"e":"Repaglinide acts by increasing insulin secretion by blockage of ATP-sensitive K+ channels of the beta cells of the pancreas.\n\nWhen ATP-sensitive K+ channels are blocked, it will lead to depolarization of beta cells and the release of insulin.\n\nSulfonylureas also act by this similar mechanism.\n\nOption B - Metformin is an insulin sensitizer and also inhibits gluconeogenesis and increases hepatic uptake of glucose.\n\nOption C - Exenatide is a GLP-1 analog.\n\nOption D - Sitagliptin is a DPP-4 inhibitor.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/263f29c8c67841b594bb001f8af7a1b8x808x550.PNG"],"t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A diabetic patient being treated with an oral hypoglycemic agent develops dilutional hyponatremia. Which one of the following could be responsible for the effect?","o":["Tolbutamide","Chlorpropamide","Glyburide","Glimepiride"],"a":1,"e":"Chlorpropamide use is associated with dilutional hyponatremia.\n\nIt is a first generation sulfonylurea and can lead to SIADH.\n\nAlong with alcohol use,it can also lead to disulfiram-like reaction.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with complaints of fatigue. She is a known case of type 2 diabetes mellitus on treatment. On examination, there is pallor and knuckle pigmentation. Which of the following drugs could have caused her condition?","o":["Gliclazide","Acarbose","Metformin","Rosiglitazone"],"a":2,"e":"Metformin can cause vitamin B12 deficiency which manifests as megaloblastic anemia (pallor, fatigue) with knuckle pigmentation.\n\nThe most common side effect of metformin is GI intolerance as nausea, bloating, abdominal cramps and diarrhoea.\n\nNote: The exact reason why metformin causes vitamin B12 deficiency is not known.\n\nThe proposed mechanisms include:\n\n• Alterations in small bowel motility, which stimulates bacterial overgrowth and consequential vitamin B12 deficiency\n\n• Competitive inhibition or inactivation of vitamin B12 absorption\n\n• Alterations in intrinsic factor (IF) levels and interaction with the cubilin endocytic receptor.\n\n• Inhibition of the calcium-dependent absorption of the vitamin B12-IF complex at the terminal ileum.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding metformin?","o":["Causes little or no hypoglycemia in non-diabetic patients","Acts by increased insulin secretion","Increases peripheral utilization of glucose and decreases absorption of glucose from the intestine","Increase intake of calcium may prevent metformin induced B12 malabsorption"],"a":1,"e":"Metformin does not act by increasing insulin secretion.\n\nIt decreases insulin resistance and increases the peripheral utilization of glucose.\n\nMetformin is a biguanide that acts by activation of AMP-dependent protein kinase activation and thereby mediates a series of actions that helps in reducing hepatic glucose production.\n\nOption A - Metformin has minimal to nil effect on insulin secretion and as a result, has minimal or no hypoglycemic effect in non-diabetic patients.\n\nOption C - Metformin reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis, increases the insulin-mediated peripheral glucose uptake and also decreases the absorption of glucose from the gut.\n\nOption D - Increase intake of calcium will decrease the B12 malabsorption because metformin interferes with Ca2+ dependent B12 absorption.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the central pharmacological action of metformin in type 2 diabetes?","o":["Reduction in glucose absorption","Reduction in insulin resistance","Reduction in hepatic glucose production","Reduction in effects of glucagon"],"a":2,"e":"The central pharmacological action of metformin is to reduce hepatic glucose production by inhibiting gluconeogenesis. Metformin effect is mainly mediated through activation of AMPK and altering the energy dynamics at mitochondrial respiration.\n\nOption A - The reduction in glucose absorption can also occur but the contribution to the antihyperglycemic action is not significant\n\nOption B - Metformin increases peripheral glucose utilization by both insulin-dependent (reducing insulin resistance) and independent ways. But the current evidence does not support the reduction in insulin resistance as the primary mechanism of metformin's action.\n\nOption D - Recent evidence shows blunting of glucagon effects as one of the mechanisms of metformin's action\n\nNote: All are mechanisms of metformin, however, the key action is Option C and it is the most appropriate answer.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 58-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease with recently diagnosed Type 2 DM was started on metformin therapy. Her repeat eGFR was 40/min/1.73 m2. Which of the following is not true regarding the further management of this lady?","o":["Metformin dose has to be decreased.","Metformin should be discontinued and replaced with insulin.","eGFR should be measured at least annually in her.","eGFR is preferred over serum creatinine concentration to assess renal function for metformin therapy."],"a":1,"e":"According to current guidelines (KDIGO 2020), metformin should be discontinued only in patients with eGFR less than 30 ml/min/1.73 m2 or kidney failure. Metformin dose should be reduced when the eGFR is less than 45 ml/min/1.73 m2.\n\nMetformin is excreted by the kidneys, and accumulation with reduced kidney function may increase the risk of lactic acidosis (due to inhibition of hepatic gluconeogenesis). It is contraindicated below eGFR<30.\n\neGFR is obtained at baseline and at least annually in all patients taking metformin. It is recommended that the eligibility of CKD patients for metformin administration be changed from one based on a single laboratory parameter (blood creatinine concentration) to one that provides a better estimate of kidney function in patients with kidney disease i.e., glomerular filtration rate estimating equation (eGFR).","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 13-year-old boy is diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Which of the following drugs can be prescribed?","o":["Glimepiride","Metformin","Sitagliptin","Pioglitazone"],"a":1,"e":"Metformin can be prescribed as it is FDA approved for medical treatment of type 2 diabetes in pediatric population as young as 10 years of age.\n\nIt is available as a liquid oral formulation (100mg/ml) for pediatric use.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding pioglitazone?","o":["Metabolised in the liver by CYP3A4","Selective agonist for the nuclear peroxisome proliferator activated receptor","It can modulate the transcription of genes for carbohydrate and fat metabolism in the absence of insulin","It should be avoided in any patient with cardiovascular disease"],"a":3,"e":"Pioglitazone need not be avoided in all patients with cardiovascular disease.\n\nPioglitazone is known to cause fluid retention and edema and is therefore avoided in patients who have heart failure. It is not avoided in all cardiovascular diseases.\n\nOption A - Pioglitazone is metabolized by CYP3A4 and CYP2C8.\n\nOption B - Pioglitazone is a selective agonist of nuclear peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPAR). It modulates the expression of genes involved in lipid and glucose metabolism, insulin signal transduction and adipocyte differentiation. These effects increase the sensitivity of the tissues to insulin.\n\nOption C - Pioglitazone does not increase insulin levels in the body. It sensitizes the tissues to the action of insulin. Hence, insulin is needed for the action of pioglitazone. However, it does not need insulin to modulate the expression of genes.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old diabetic man on treatment presents with complaints of difficulty reading. Fundoscopy shows the following findings. Which antidiabetic drug class is likely to cause this?","o":["Thiazolidinediones","DPP-4 inhibtors","SGLT-2 inhibitors","Sulfonylureas"],"a":0,"e":"The image shows diabetic macular edema, with hard exudates surrounding the blood vessels. It is a rare adverse effect seen with thiazolidinediones, and it resolves when the drug is discontinued.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20bee22a7c7d4df1b8fc065782163abd.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly man was diagnosed with hypertension 2 years ago and has been on losartan. During a routine health checkup, his HbA1c was found to be 7.3%. Which of the following is a better alternative to losartan for this patient?","o":["Irbesartan","Telmisartan","Candesartan","Olmesartan"],"a":1,"e":"This patient has hypertension and diabetes mellitus (HbA1c >6.5% is diagnostic for diabetes). Telmisartan would be a better alternative to losartan in patients with both hypertension and diabetes, due to its additional PPAR gamma agonist action.\n\nPPAR gamma agonists act by inducing several insulins sensitizing genes and thereby reducing the overall insulin resistance.\n\nWhile other angiotensin receptor blockers have been found to have PPAR gamma action, telmisartan has the highest and has been extensively studied in diabetes.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman with a known history of hypertension and dyslipidemia was admitted for evaluation of recurrent TIA. On workup, she was diagnosed with diabetes mellitus. Which of the following drugs should be avoided in the management of this patient?","o":["Pioglitazone","Rosiglitazone","Metformin","Troglitazone"],"a":1,"e":"Rosiglitazone should be avoided as it increases the risk of cardiovascular events like myocardial infarction and stroke.\n\nIt remains banned in Europe, India, and other countries. It has not been withdrawn in the US and is marketed with a black box warning of increased risk of myocardial infarction and stroke.\n\nOption A: Several studies have shown relative cardiovascular safety of pioglitazone over rosiglitazone.\n\nOption C: Metformin use is associated with decreased risk of microvascular complications in diabetes.\n\nOption D: Troglitazone was withdrawn due to increased hepatotoxicity.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A professional boxer is diagnosed with diabetes mellitus. He is concerned about weight changes as a side effect of anti-diabetic drugs and explains that he has to maintain his current weight category. Which of the following will you prescribe?","o":["Pioglitazone","Saxagliptin","Liraglutide","Gliclazide"],"a":1,"e":"Saxagliptin, a DPP-4 inhibitor, can be prescribed for this patient as it has a weight-neutral effect.\n\nEFFECT\nDRUGS\n\nWeight gain\nSulfonylureas, Insulin, Thiazolidinediones\n\nWeight loss\nGLP-1 analogues, Pramlintide, SGLT-2 inhibitors, Metformin*\n\nWeight neutral\nAlpha-glucosidase inhibitors, DPP-4 inhibitors\n\nNote: *Metformin classification is a bit difficult. It can cause mild weight loss in some patients but few authorities do consider metformin as weight neutral.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true about dapagliflozin?","o":["It is indicated in heart failure","It causes weight gain","It is effective orally","It will increase the risk of fractures"],"a":1,"e":"Dapagliflozin does not cause weight gain. It results in weight loss.\n\nIt acts by inhibiting the SGLT2 (sodium glucose-2) transporter, which is present on the proximal segment of the renal tubule and responsible for the reabsorption of 80-90% of filtered glucose. It inhibits glucose reabsorption resulting in glycosuria and a decrease in blood glucose level. It is effective orally.\n\nThe common adverse effects include urinary tract infections and genital mycotic infections due to the high glucose content of the urine and also the high risk of fractures.\n\nOther SGLT2 inhibitors include empagliflozin, canagliflozin, and ertugliflozin.\n\nNote: Empagliflozin has been found to decrease the risk of mortality from cardiovascular causes in diabetic patients. Dapagliflozin has been recently approved by the FDA for the treatment of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction(HFrEF) in both diabetic and non-diabetic patients.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with diabetes mellitus came to the OPD with a history suggestive of recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia. She was recently put on combination therapy. Her symptoms are least likely to be caused by which of the following drugs?","o":["Acarbose","Insulin","Gliclazide","Repaglinide"],"a":0,"e":"Acarbose is least likely to cause hypoglycemia among the given options.\n\nIt is an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor. It inhibits the intestinal degradation and absorption of starch and disaccharides and thereby lowers the postprandial rise in glucose levels.\n\nIt has no role in stimulating insulin secretion and is therefore, least likely to cause hypoglycemia during monotherapy.\n\nInsulin and insulin secretagogues like sulfonylureas (gliclazide) and meglitinides (repaglinide) can result in hypoglycemia.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"While at a bakery, a man complains of giddiness and palpitations. As the only doctor there, you're asked for help. The man says he had just taken his diabetic medications and shows a prescription listing insulin and acarbose. What would you give for immediate management?","o":["Honey","Bread","Sugar","Cake"],"a":0,"e":"Giddiness and palpitations after taking insulin and acarbose are suggestive of hypoglycemia. Honey is the most suitable agent among the given options for the management of hypoglycemia in a patient who is on acarbose therapy.\n\nAcarbose is an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor. It inhibits the intestinal degradation of starch and disaccharides into glucose. Honey contains monosaccharides (fructose and glucose) that do not need to be broken down and are directly absorbed and would be beneficial in hypoglycemia caused by alpha-glucosidase inhibitors. However, glucose or dextrose tablets is recommended when available.\n\nWhile alpha-glucosidase inhibitors themselves do not cause hypoglycemia, the risk of hypoglycemia is increased when they are taken along with insulin or insulin secretagogues.\n\nDisaccharides and complex carbohydrates will not be completely broken down and absorbed in a patient who is on alpha-glucosidase inhibitors.\n\nOption B - Bread predominantly contains complex carbohydrates like starch\n\nOption C - Sugar contains sucrose, a disaccharide\n\nOption D - Cake contains sugar (disaccharide) and other complex carbohydrates","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old diabetic patient presented to the ED with complaints of chest pain on exertion. Laboratory tests showed dyslipidemia and features of metabolic syndrome. Which of the following drugs was recently approved for this condition?","o":["Aleglitazar","Saroglitazar","Muraglitazar","Rosiglitazone"],"a":1,"e":"Saroglitazar is a dual PPAR alpha/gamma agonist and is approved for diabetic dyslipidemia only in India.\n\nPeroxisome proliferator-activated receptors (PPARs) are a superfamily of nuclear receptors involved in the regulation of energy homeostasis and metabolic function. It has three subtypes:\n\n• PPARα - reduces triglyceride level\n\n• PPARγ - causes insulin sensitization\n\n• PPARδ - enhances fatty acids metabolism\n\nOf the three PPAR isoforms, synthetic pharmaceutical ligands that activate PPARalpha (the antidyslipidemic fibrates) and PPARgamma (the antidiabetic thiazolidinediones) have been studied extensively. Recently developed dual PPARalpha/gamma agonists combine the therapeutic effects of these drugs to be used in type 2 diabetes with dyslipidemia.\n\nOptions A and C - Aleglitazar and muraglitazar are also dual PPAR alpha/gamma agonists but their development was halted due to increased risk of cardiovascular mortality.\n\nOption D - Rosiglitazone is a PPAR gamma agonist indicated in type 2 diabetes mellitus. It has been withdrawn from many countries including India due to increased cardiovascular mortality.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is approved as an adjunctive treatment for type 2 diabetes mellitus?","o":["Bromocriptine","Phenytoin","Ropinirole","Metoclopromide"],"a":0,"e":"Bromocriptine is a dopamine agonist that has been approved as an adjunctive treatment for diabetes mellitus.\n\nThe exact mechanism is unknown but may involve alteration in circadian (hypothalamic) control of blood glucose levels. A fast-release, low-dose formulation of bromocriptine is approved for type 2 diabetes that has to be administered within 3 hours of awakening.\n\nOption B - Phenytoin is a Na+ channel blocker and is an antiepileptic drug\n\nOption C - Ropinirole is an antiparkinsonian drug that is a dopamine agonist and is also approved for treatment of restless leg syndrome\n\nOption D - Metoclopramide is a 5HT4 agonist and D2 blocker and used as an antiemetic agent","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old woman comes to the diabetic clinic for follow up. Her lipid profile shows high LDL cholesterol. Which bile acid sequestrant would you prescribe for her?","o":["Colesevelam","Colestipol","Cholestyramine","Ezetimibe"],"a":0,"e":"Colesevelam can be prescribed for her as it is the only bile acid sequestrant that is specifically approved for the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nIt is used to reduce LDL cholesterol and improve glycemic control in diabetics.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the half-life of endogenously secreted insulin?","o":["1-2 minutes","5-6 minutes","20-30 minutes","1-2 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Endogenously secreted insulin has a half-life of only 5-6 minutes due to its extensive hepatic clearance.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old nurse was brought to the casualty with seizures and altered mental status. Her plasma glucose was 35 mg/dl. Which test would help to rule out the possibility of exogenous insulin administration?","o":["Glucagon","C-peptide","Pancreatic polypeptide","Preproinsulin"],"a":1,"e":"C-peptide can be used to rule out the possibility of factitious hypoglycemia resulting from exogenously administered insulin.\n\nEndogenous insulin is initially synthesized as preproinsulin that is processed to proinsulin inside beta cells. Proinsulin is released by secretory granules after processing it to form insulin and C-peptide.\n\nSynthetic insulin usually lacks C-peptide, so in factitious hypoglycemia secondary to exogenous insulin administration, plasma insulin will be elevated, but there will be suppression of C-peptide and proinsulin, and insulin to C-peptide ratio will be greater than one.\n\nOn the other hand, insulinomas and insulin secretagogues like sulfonylureas stimulate endogenous insulin production. As a result, plasma insulin, C-peptide, and proinsulin levels will be all elevated. The only way to differentiate between insulinoma and insulin secretagogues-induced hypoglycemia is by detecting the drug, e.g., sulfonylurea, in the blood or urine.\n\nC-peptide has no known physiological function or receptor. It has a half-life of about 30 min.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient requires the shortest acting formulation of insulin to prevent postprandial hyperglycemia. Which of the following formulations would you choose?","o":["Insulin lispro","Insulin aspart","Regular insulin","Inhaled insulin"],"a":3,"e":"Inhaled insulin is the shortest acting formulation of insulin.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on basal-bolus regimen for type 1 diabetes wants an alternative to multiple daily injections. She read about the new drug Afrezza and wants to know more about it. Which of the following is not true about this formulation?","o":["Inhaled insulin","Can be used as single or multiple pre meal doses","If multiple doses used then no need of basal insulin","Avoided in active smokers"],"a":2,"e":"Afrezza should be used in combination with basal long-acting insulin to supplement it.\n\nOption A - Afrezza is an inhaled insulin that is available in dry powder form. It is the shortest acting insulin (2-3 hours) and it reduces the use of short-acting injectable insulin. Adverse events include cough and throat irritation.\n\nOption B - It is taken pre-meal to reduce postprandial hyperglycemia.\n\nOption D - Afrezza is contraindicated in active smokers, asthmatics and COPD patients.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following preparations of insulin is not rapid-acting?","o":["Glargine","Lispro","Glulisine","Aspart"],"a":0,"e":"All of the following preparations of insulin are rapid-acting except glargine.\n\nInsulin preparations and duration of action\n\nShort-acting:\n\n• Insulin lispro, Insulin aspart, Insulin glulisine: 3-4 hours\n\n• Regular insulin: 4-6 hours\n\nLong-acting:\n\n• Insulin NPH: 10-16 hours\n\n• Insulin detemir: 12-24 hours\n\n• Insulin glargine: 12-24 hours\n\n• Insulin degludec: 24-42 hours","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Identify the correct insulin combination option.","o":["1 - Glargine, 2 - Detemir","1 - Glargine, 2 - Degludec","1 - Detemir, 2 - Aspart","1 - Glulisine, 2 - Glargine"],"a":2,"e":"Detemir is a long-acting insulin and aspart is a rapid-acting insulin and matches with 1 and 2 respectively in the image showing basal-bolus regimen.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/947efd6318f64d5aa2658e117e9d83b7.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old patient with type 1 diabetes presented with complaints of vomiting and abdominal pain. On examination, she had fast, deep breathing with a fruity odor. Her capillary blood glucose was 450mg/dL. What is the clinical concern associated with administering insulin alone?","o":["Hypokalemia","Hypernatremia","Hyperkalemia","Hyponatremia"],"a":0,"e":"Deep breathing (Kussmaul respiration) and fruity breath (acetone odor) in a symptomatic patient with type 1 diabetes are suggestive of diabetic ketoacidosis. Insulin therapy alone in a patient with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) may result in hypokalemia.\n\nDKA is usually associated with a major K+ deficit. Potassium loss is caused by an extracellular shift of potassium (in exchange with hydrogen ions) due to acidosis. Much of the shifted extracellular potassium is lost due to emesis and osmotic diuresis.\n\nWhen insulin therapy is initiated for DKA, the serum K+ is shifted intracellularly. Dangerous hypokalemia might occur if K+ supplementation is not initiated subsequently. Insulin should be withheld if the serum level of potassium is below 3.3 mmol/L.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"You see a nurse administering an insulin preparation that appears cloudy white. Which formulation of insulin could it be?","o":["Insulin glargine","Insulin glulisine","Insulin detemir","Insulin NPH"],"a":3,"e":"The formulation is most likely NPH insulin as it produces a cloudy or whitish solution in contrast to the clear appearance of other insulin solutions.\n\nNPH or isophane insulin is a suspension of native insulin complexed with zinc and protamine in phosphate buffer.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient was recently diagnosed with diabetes. She was prescribed the longest-acting insulin as it has the least risk of hypoglycemia. Which insulin preparation is she on?","o":["Insulin NPH","Insulin glargine","Insulin detemir","Insulin degludec"],"a":3,"e":"Insulin degludec has the maximum duration of action (24-42 hours) among long-acting insulins. It is sometimes considered an ultra long-acting insulin.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A diabetic patient is in the postoperative area following cardiac surgery. He is to be given intravenous insulin for optimal glycemic control and to avoid sternal wound infection. Which of the following should not be administered?","o":["Insulin glulisine","Insulin detemir","Regular insulin","Insulin aspart"],"a":1,"e":"Insulin detemir is a long-acting insulin and should not be given intravenously or intramuscularly. It is only given via subcutaneous route.\n\nOptions A, C, and D: Regular insulin and short-acting insulins (glulisine and aspart) can be given intravenously. While short-acting insulins are approved for intravenous administration under supervision, regular insulin is preferred in perioperative glycemic control due to the lesser risk of hypoglycemia.\n\nIntravenous insulin is used in diabetic ketoacidosis and other conditions where insulin requirements change drastically like perioperative period, labor, and in intensive care situations.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following insulin precipitates into a cloudy white precipitate when mixed with short acting insulins?","o":["Insulin detemir","Insulin degludec","Insulin glargine","Insulin glulisine"],"a":2,"e":"Insulin glargine forms a cloudy white precipitate when mixed with short-acting insulins.\n\nDue to the acidic pH (4.0) of insulin glargine, it cannot be mixed with short-acting insulin preparations that are formulated at a neutral pH as it aggregates at neutral pH into a cloudy white precipitate. This reduces the effectiveness of the insulin dose.\n\nInsulin degludec and detemir have a pH of 7.4 that is very near to neutral pH and they do not form such aggregates when mixed with short-acting insulins.\n\nBut the manufacturers do not advise manual mixing with short-acting insulin as it may alter the activity profile of the combination that can turn unpredictable. Degludec is available as an approved fixed-dose combination with aspart that can be used with predictable effects.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are counseling a diabetic patient and teaching her the technique to self-administer insulin at home every day. Which of the following sites would you not suggest as the preferred site for insulin administration?","o":["Anterior part of thigh","Lateral part of thigh","Buttocks","Medial part of thigh"],"a":3,"e":"The medial part of the thigh is not a preferred site for insulin administration in patients. This is because of the relatively higher density of blood vessels and nerves in this area. Another consideration is that the inner thighs rub together while walking and may cause soreness at the injection sites.\n\nThe preferred sites for insulin administration are:\n\n• Upper arm\n\n• Anterior and lateral aspects of the thigh\n\n• Buttocks\n\n• Abdomen (with the exception of a circle with a 2-inch radius around the navel)\n\nRotation of the injection site is important to prevent lipodystrophy. However, rotating within one area is recommended (e.g., rotating injections systematically within the abdomen) rather than rotating to a different area with each injection. This helps decrease variability in absorption from day to day.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/336c015a430f474d9d6818f4ee6c4336x472x449.PNG"],"t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding exenatide?","o":["It is a GLP- 1 analogue","It can be used for the treatment of type 1 diabetes mellitus","It is given subcutaneously","It decreases glucagon"],"a":1,"e":"Exenatide is not indicated in type 1 diabetes mellitus.\n\nExenatide is a GLP-1 analog that acts as an insulin secretagogue. As the beta cells are already destroyed and do not secrete insulin in type 1 diabetes, there is no role of drugs that enhance insulin secretion.\n\nOption A - Exenatide is a GLP-1 analog and it is synthesized from exendin-4\n\nOption C - It is a peptide and hence has to be administered subcutaneously\n\nOption D - It increases insulin secretion, decreases glucagon secretion, delays gastric emptying, and reduces food intake","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a GLP-1 receptor agonist?","o":["Liraglutide","Semaglutide","Pramlintide","Exenatide"],"a":2,"e":"Pramlintide is an islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP) or amylin analogue.\n\nAmylin analogues reduce glucagon secretion, slows gastric emptying, and centrally decreases appetite.\n\nOptions A, B and D - Liraglutide, semaglutide and exenatide are GLP-1 agonists.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is not true about pramlintide?","o":["It can be used in both type 1 and type 2 diabetes mellitus","It has got weight reducing property by causing a central anorectic effect","It should not be administered with insulin in the same syringe","It is given orally"],"a":3,"e":"Pramlintide is administered as a subcutaneous injection.\n\nOption A - It is a synthetic analogue of amylin and is approved for both type 1 and type 2 diabetes mellitus\n\nOption B - It has weight reducing property by causing a central anorectic effect. It acts by reducing glucagon secretion, delaying gastric emptying and decreasing appetite.\n\nOption C - It should not be administered with insulin in the same syringe because of a difference in pH","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old diabetic woman comes for follow up. Her BMI is 32 kg/m2 despite several months of diet and exercise. Which of the following is the drug preferred for her condition?","o":["Liraglutide","Linagliptin","Insulin lispro","Pioglitazone"],"a":0,"e":"Liraglutide is approved for the management of obesity.\n\nGLP-1 is a physiological regulator of appetite and calorie intake, and the GLP-1 receptor is present in several areas of the brain involved in appetite regulation. Liraglutide is a GLP-1 analog and lowers body weight by decreasing calorie intake.\n\nNote: FDA has recently approved semaglutide subcutaneous injection once weekly for chronic weight management. This is for use in patients with obesity and overweight individuals with at least one weight-related condition such as type 2 diabetes, hypertension or dyslipidemia.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man comes for a routine health check-up. He has a family history of medullary thyroid cancer. His thyroid examination is normal but his HbA1c is found to be 7.5%. In view of his history, which of the following should not be prescribed for him?","o":["SGLT 2 inhibitors","DPP4 inhibitors","GLP 1 agonists","Sulfonylureas"],"a":2,"e":"GLP-1 analogues are contraindicated in patients with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer and in patients with MEN 2 syndrome.\n\nGLP-1 analogues cause thyroid C-cell tumours at clinically relevant exposures in rodents. However, studies have not yet shown if they cause thyroid C-cell tumours like medullary thyroid carcinoma in humans.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man with superior mesenteric artery occlusion underwent extensive bowel resection. Following surgery, he developed short bowel syndrome and has been on parenteral nutrition. Which of the following drugs would be beneficial for his condition?","o":["Liraglutide","Lixisenatide","Teduglutide","Semaglutide"],"a":2,"e":"Teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog and is approved for the treatment of short bowel syndrome.\n\nGLP-2 binds to enteric neurons and endocrine cells, stimulating the release of a number of hormones such as insulin-like growth factor. This stimulates mucosal epithelial growth and enhances fluid absorption.\n\nGLP-1 analogs:\n\n• Exenatide\n\n• Liraglutide\n\n• Albiglutide\n\n• Dulaglutide\n\n• Lixisenatide\n\n• Semaglutide\n\nNote: GLP-1 analogs are used for the treatment of Type II diabetes mellitus.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Identify the true statement regarding teduglutide.","o":["It is not recommended for patients with colorectal cancer","It’s half life is 6 - 8 hours","It can be administered orally","It promotes gastric acid secretion and gastric emptying"],"a":0,"e":"Teduglutide can potentially cause bowel cancer, therefore it is not recommended for patients with active malignancies.\n\nIt is a GLP-2 analog that has been recently approved for the treatment of short bowel syndrome. It inhibits gastric acid secretion and gastric emptying, stimulates mucosal epithelial growth and intestinal blood flow, and enhances nutrient and fluid absorption\n\nThe drug has a t1/2 of 1–2 hours and is administered subcutaneously once daily.\n\nNote: GLP-1 analogs are used for the treatment of Type II diabetes mellitus.","t":"Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a natural estrogen?","o":["Estradiol","Ethinylestradiol","Estriol","Estrone"],"a":1,"e":"Ethinylestradiol is a synthetic estrogen.\n\nNatural estrogens are:\n\n• Estradiol\n\n• Estriol\n\n• Estrone","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old postmenopausal lady is on estrogen as part of her hormone replacement therapy. Which of the following statements about estrogen is not true?","o":["Decreases HDL","Increases triglycerides","Increases turnover of LDL receptors","Increases apolipoprotein level"],"a":0,"e":"Estrogen causes an increase in plasma HDL levels.\n\nEstrogen also causes an increase in triglycerides, apolipoprotein levels, and an increase in the turnover of LDL receptors.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A post-menopausal lady who is on estrogen therapy for hot flashes is concerned about its adverse effects. She is not at an increased risk of developing which of the following cancers?","o":["Breast cancer","Endometrial cancer","Carcinoma of the gall bladder","Hepatocellular carcinoma"],"a":2,"e":"Women receiving estrogen therapy do not have an increased risk of developing gall bladder carcinoma.\n\nLong-term estrogen therapy can increase the risk of gall stones. Although gall stones can be associated with the development of gall bladder carcinoma, there is no direct correlation between long term estrogen therapy and gall bladder carcinoma.\n\nEstrogen therapy has been associated with breast, endometrial and hepatocellular carcinoma.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is hormone replacement therapy not helpful?","o":["Vaginal atrophy","Flushing","Coronary heart disease","Osteoporosis"],"a":2,"e":"Hormone replacement therapy(HRT) is not helpful in coronary heart disease.\n\nProgesterone used in the HRT is associated with an increased risk of coronary artery disease, thromboembolism, and related mortality. Estrogens promote coagulation and thromboembolic episodes.\n\nOptions A and B: Estrogen is helpful in flushing and local preparations are preferred for vaginal atrophy.\n\nOption D: Estrogen is beneficial for osteoporosis if it is started before the onset of significant osteoporosis.\n\nNote: The use of HRT for cardiovascular benefits is quite controversial. The current consensus is that the HRT should be started only after assessing the benefit/risk profile individually and is not indicated for the prevention of cardiovascular events in all menopausal women as it was previously advocated.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Progestin dose remains constant throughout the cycle in which of the following type of combined contraceptive pills?","o":["Monophasic pills","Biphasic pills","Triphasic pills","Multiphasic pills"],"a":0,"e":"Monophasic pills contain constant amounts of progesterone and estrogen that is taken daily for 21 days, followed by a 7-day pill-free period.\n\nBiphasic and triphasic pills have variable amounts of active ingredients (especially progesterone) that mimic the natural changes in the hormonal levels in the menstrual cycle. These pills reduce the total amount of steroids that needs to be administered.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A young lady who is on oral contraceptive pills refrains from taking any drug even for minor ailments due to the fear of contraceptive failure. Which of the following drugs can be taken safely?","o":["Aspirin","Tetracycline","Phenytoin","Rifampicin"],"a":0,"e":"Among the given drugs, aspirin use is least likely to cause contraceptive failure.\n\nOption B: Broad-spectrum antibiotics like tetracycline suppress the intestinal flora and interfere with the enterohepatic circulation of estrogen. This leads to lowering of plasma estrogen levels and can result in contraceptive failure.\n\nOptions C and D: Enzyme inducers like phenytoin and rifampicin increase the metabolism of oral contraceptives and can cause contraceptive failure.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old lady has been on anti-epileptic therapy since childhood and now comes to the OPD seeking advice on a suitable method of contraception. Which contraceptive method should be avoided in her?","o":["Oral contraceptives","Condoms","Intrauterine contraceptive devices","Post-coital pills"],"a":0,"e":"Oral contraceptives must be avoided or used cautiously in epilepsy.\n\nThe most important aspect to consider is the drug interactions between anti-epileptic drugs and contraceptives. There is no conclusive evidence on whether estrogen is beneficial or harmful for seizure control.\n\nNote: Convulsive disorder is not an absolute contraindication; it is safe to avoid but can be used with caution.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about oral contraceptive pills is true?","o":["Progesterone is resposible for thromboembolism","It is protective against ovarian cancer","OCP are used in treatment mastalgia","Rifamipicin decreases the metabolism of OCP"],"a":1,"e":"Oral contraceptive pills (OCP) protect against ovarian cancer.\n\nThis benefit is thought to be due to the prolonged suppression of gonadotropin stimulation of the ovary on long-term use of OCPs.\n\nOther benefits of oral contraceptive pills:\n\n• Lesser incidence of endometrial carcinoma due to progestin component in OCP\n\n• Regularisation of menstrual cycles results in relief of dysmenorrhea and menorrhagia\n\n• Reduced incidence of fibrocystic breast disease and ovarian cysts\n\n• Endometriosis and pelvic inflammatory disease are improved\n\nOption A: The estrogen component of combined oral contraceptive pills is mainly responsible for venous thromboembolism.\n\nOption C: OCP is not used in the treatment of mastalgia. Mastalgia, chloasma, and breakthrough bleeding are known side effects of oral contraceptive pills.\n\nOption D: OCPs are metabolized by CYP3A4 enzymes. Rifampicin is a strong inducer of CYP3A4. Hence, if administered together, rifampicin can cause enhanced metabolism of OCPs and may lead to contraceptive failure.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old lady on oral combined contraceptive pills misses a single dose and reports to you in the OPD the next morning. What will be the most appropriate advice to this patient?","o":["Continue taking the next dose as scheduled","Double the next dose to make up for the missed dose","Interrupt the course and start taking the medications again on 5th day of bleeding","Start a fresh course of OCP again"],"a":1,"e":"The most appropriate advice for this patient who has missed a single dose of the oral contraceptive pill (OCP) is to double the next dose to make up for the missed dose.\n\nMissed dose considerations for oral combined contraceptive pills:\n\n• If a single dose is missed - a double dose can be taken on the next day to make up for the missed dose.\n\n• If two or more doses are missed on subsequent days - the last missed pill has to be taken along with the present pill and alternative methods of contraceptive must be used for the next 7 days.\n\n• If the pills were missed on the last week (days 15-21) - omit the hormone-free interval by finishing the hormonal pills in the current pack and starting the new pack from the next day.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is combined oral contraceptive pills not absolutely contraindicated?","o":["A perimenopausal woman with coronary heart disease","A 23-year-old lady with a past history of hepatic adenoma","A multiparous lady with abnormal undiagnosed vaginal bleeding","A 40-year-old woman with migraine headache"],"a":3,"e":"A migraine headache is a relative contraindication for the use of combined oral contraceptives. Migraine with focal neurological symptoms is an absolute contraindication to combined oral contraceptive use.\n\nAbsolute contraindications to combined oral contraceptive use:\n\n• Presence or history of thromboembolic disease, cerebrovascular disease, myocardial infarction, coronary artery disease\n\n• Congenital hyperlipidemia\n\n• Known or suspected carcinoma of the breast, female reproductive tract\n\n• Known or suspected pregnancy\n\n• Past or present liver tumors or impaired liver function\n\n• Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding.\n\n• Migraine with focal neurological symptoms\n\nRelative contraindications to combined oral contraceptive use:\n\n• Migraine headaches in >35 yrs female\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• Obstructive jaundice of pregnancy\n\n• Prior oral contraceptive use\n\n• Gallbladder disease","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Progestin-only pills do not act by which of the following mechanisms?","o":["Inhibition of ovulation","Decreasing sperm penetration","Impairing implantation","Eliciting an inflammatory action in the endometrium"],"a":3,"e":"Copper intrauterine devices elicit an inflammatory reaction within the endometrium. This impairs sperm viability, motility, and fertilization.\n\nAll the following actions contribute to the contraceptive action of progestin-only pills:\n\n• The effectiveness of this drug is largely due to the thickening of cervical mucus which in turn impair sperm penetration.\n\n• Inhibits ovulation via exerting negative feedback on the hypothalamus (GnRH). This leads to the absence of the FSH and LH surge, which is essential to mature the dominant follicle and trigger ovulation.\n\n• Interfere with the implantation of a zygote by altering the endometrial environment.\n\nThe major reason women discontinue the use of progestin-only contraceptives is breakthrough bleeding.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old lady, P2L2, has been taking depot medroxy progesterone acetate as a contraceptive. Which of the following best describes the formulation?","o":["Long acting progesterone preparation","Long acting estrogen preparation","Long acting anti-progestin preparation","Short acting anti-progestin preparation"],"a":0,"e":"Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA) is a long-acting progestin-only injection formulation.\n\nDMPA is administered every 3 months intramuscularly as a contraceptive.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following progesterone is used in emergency contraception?","o":["Levonorgestrel","Micronised progesterone","Norgesterone","Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate"],"a":0,"e":"Levonorgestrel is the progesterone that is used in emergency contraception. Levonorgestrel-containing regimens are considered the standard regimen now.\n\nOption B: Micronised progesterone is indicated for amenorrhea and to counteract estrogen effects on the uterus.\n\nOption C: Norgesterone is usually used along with ethinyl estradiol for regular contraception.\n\nOption D: Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate is a long-acting depot preparation indicated for long-term contraception.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A newly married couple came to the OPD saying that they had unprotected intercourse two days back but are not desiring a baby at present. Which of the following drugs can be prescribed in this case?","o":["Nomegestrel","Tamoxifen","Ulipristal","Toremifene"],"a":2,"e":"Ulipristal is a selective progesterone receptor modulator (SPRM) that can be given as an emergency contraceptive in this case.\n\nIt should be administered within 5 days of unprotected intercourse.\n\nIt inhibits ovulation by suppressing LH surge as well as by direct effect on follicular rupture. In addition, its action on the endometrium can interfere with implantation.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"The standard emergency contraceptive pill regimen, levonorgestrel can be taken up to how many hours after unprotected intercourse?","o":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","120 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Levonorgestrel regimen can be taken within 72 hours after unprotected intercourse but is most effective when taken as early as possible.\n\nLevonorgestrel for emergency contraception can be taken as either of the following:\n\n• 2 tablets of 0.75 mg 12 hours apart\n\n• 1.5 mg single-dose","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 20-year old lady came to the OPD with complaints of missed period. You diagnose her to be 5 weeks pregnant but she is not willing to continue the pregnancy. You decide to prescribe her a drug that has anti-progestational activity. Which of the following drugs would you choose?","o":["Levonorgestrel","Desogestrel","Misoprostol","Mifepristone"],"a":3,"e":"Mifepristone (RU-486) has potent anti-progestational (abortive activity) and significant anti-glucocorticoid and antiandrogenic activity.\n\nOptions A and B: Levonorgestrel and desogestrel are progesterone preparations.\n\nOption C: Misoprostol is a synthetic prostaglandin (PGE1) preparation.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In the treatment of which of the following patients will you not consider clomiphene citrate?","o":["A 24-year-old lady with PCOS","A 48-year old perimenopausal woman with hot flashes","A 32-year-old man with oligospermia","A 30-year old patient with SUNCT"],"a":1,"e":"Clomiphene citrate is not used in the treatment of hot flashes.\n\nOption A- Clomiphene increases gonadotropin secretion and stimulates ovulation. It mainly acts by blocking the inhibitory actions of estrogen on gonadotropin release from the pituitary gland. It is primarily indicated in the management of infertility.\n\nOption C - In men also, clomiphene increases gonadotropin secretion and promotes spermatogenesis. However, success rates are low.\n\nOption D - Studies have shown clomiphene citrate to be effective in treating and preventing short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks (SUNCT). It has not yet been FDA-approved for the treatment of SUNCT.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old lady, diagnosed with breast cancer, is started on a selective estrogen receptor modulator. Identify the drug.","o":["Raloxifene","Mifepristone","Danazol","Anastrozole"],"a":0,"e":"Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator.\n\nIt has agonist action on bone and antagonist action on the breast and uterus.\n\nIt is used in the treatment and prevention of osteoporosis and in breast cancer prevention in high-risk postmenopausal women.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old lady who is on tamoxifen, for the treatment of breast cancer is worried about its side effects. Which of the following side effects of tamoxifen is least likely to occur in this patient?","o":["Increased risk of pulmonary embolism","Endometrial carcinoma","Cataract","Cancer in opposite breast"],"a":3,"e":"Tamoxifen acts as an estrogen receptor antagonist in breast tissues. The use of tamoxifen in carcinoma of breast patients decreases the incidence of carcinoma in opposite breasts.\n\nThe estrogen agonistic action of tamoxifen and its consequences are:\n\n• Uterine endometrium - endometrial hypertrophy, vaginal bleeding, and endometrial cancer\n\n• Coagulation system - thromboembolism leading to pulmonary embolism (tamoxifen decreases the level of antithrombin III and protein C in the blood)\n\n• Bone metabolism - increase in bone mineral density\n\n• Liver - alterations of blood lipid profile\n\n• Cataract","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a selective estrogen receptor down regulator?","o":["Tamoxifen","Toremifene","Fulvestrant","Raloxifene"],"a":2,"e":"Fulvestrant is a selective estrogen receptor down-regulator. It does not have estrogen agonistic activity for any tissue and is regarded as a pure estrogen antagonist.\n\nTamoxifen, toremifene, and raloxifene are selective estrogen receptor modulators with the agonist or antagonist action at different tissues.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an aromatase inhibitor?","o":["Tamoxifen","Danazol","Ospemifene","Exemestane"],"a":3,"e":"Exemestane is an aromatase inhibitor.\n\nEstrogen is locally produced from androgen by the action of the aromatase enzyme. Locally synthesized estrogens play an important role in the development of breast cancer because they are estrogen-dependent for proliferation.\n\nAromatase inhibitors decrease estrogen synthesis and are widely used now in the treatment of breast cancer in postmenopausal women.\n\nAromatase inhibitors are:\n\n• Letrozole\n\n• Anastrozole\n\n• Exemestane (Irreversible inhibitor)","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"You decide to change the chemotherapeutic regimen of a patient with breast cancer from tamoxifen to an aromatase inhibitor. Which of the following is not an advantage of the latter in the treatment of breast cancer?","o":["No increase in risk of venous thromboembolism","No increase in risk of endometrial cancer","Can be used in advanced cases which are tamoxifen-resistant","They maintain bone mineral density"],"a":3,"e":"Aromatase inhibitors lack the property of maintaining bone mineral density when compared to tamoxifen.\n\nAromatase inhibitors dramatically reduce estrogen levels that result in a decrease in the risk of venous thromboembolism and also decreases the risk of endometrial hyperplasia/cancer. It is also indicated in tamoxifen-resistant advanced breast cancer.","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Yuzpe's regimen of emergency contraception consists of which of the following?","o":["Etonogestrel + ethinylestradiol","Levonorgestrel + ethinylestradiol","Levonorgestrel + desogestrel","Norethisterone enanthate + estradiol valerate"],"a":1,"e":"Yuzpe's regimen of emergency contraception consists of a combination of levonorgestrel and ethinylestradiol.\n\nThe regimen includes two doses at 12 hours intervals within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse. It has a higher incidence of nausea and vomiting and is less efficacious than progesterone only standard regimen.\n\nMethods of Emergency contraception:\n\nDrug\nDose\n\nLevonorgestrel\n0.75mg stat and after 12 hours\n\nEthinyl estradiol 50 μg + Norgestrel 0.25 mg\n2 tab stat and 2 tab after 12 hours\n\nConjugated estrogen\n15 mg BD x 5 days\n\nEthinyl Estradiol\n2.5 mg BD X 5 days\n\nCopper IUDs\nInsertion within 5-7 days after accidental unprotected exposure.Not suitable for rape victims\n\nCentchroman\n2 tablets(60 mg) to be taken twice at an interval of 12 hours within 24 hours of intercourse.\n\nUlipristal\n30 mg within 120 hours","t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are an intern posted in the medicine ward and you notice the given dermatological finding in a patient with abdominal distension. Which of the following hormones is implicated in this finding?","o":["Testosterone","Estrogen","Hepatotoxins","FSH"],"a":1,"e":"The image shown is of spider nevus and the hormone implicated in its development is estrogen.\n\nIt is commonly seen in hyperestrogenic states such as pregnancy and in those taking estrogen-containing contraceptives. It can also be seen in male patients with cirrhosis due to the decreased ability of the liver to metabolize circulating estrogens, specifically estrone, which derives from the androgen androstenedione. Estrogens cause blood vessels to enlarge and dilate.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ff2147f686ce41ef8505b13f47e5e59c.PNG"],"t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old man presents to the OPD with the following condition. Which of the following drugs can cause this adverse effect?\n\n1. Clomiphene citrate\n2. Tamoxifen\n3. Spironolactone\n4. Ketoconazole\n5. Chlorpromazine","o":["1 and 2","1,2 and 3","3,4 and 5","Only 3"],"a":2,"e":"This is a case of gynecomastia which can occur as an adverse effect of spironolactone or ketoconazole or chlorpromazine.\n\nSpironolactone - decreases testosterone production, increases peripheral conversion of testosterone to estradiol, and displaces estradiol from their plasma-protein-binding sites\n\nKetoconazole - decreases testosterone production and displaces testosterone from their binding sites\n\nChlorpromazine (neuroleptics) - increase prolactin release by blocking the inhibitory action of dopamine on prolactin\n\nClomiphene and tamoxifen are used mainly in reducing male gynecomastia.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3698c533a5b7418bba718cf536bf5e52.JPEG"],"t":"Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most potent androgen?","o":["Dehydroepiandrostenidione","Dihydrotestosterone","Androstenedione","Testosterone"],"a":1,"e":"Dihydrotestosterone is the most potent androgen.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Aromatase converts testosterone into ____________","o":["Dihydrotestosterone","Estradiol","Epiandrosterone","Androstanedione"],"a":1,"e":"Aromatase (CYP19) converts testosterone to estradiol. Certain effects of testosterone are mediated by estradiol.\n\nIn men with deficient CYP19 or ER receptors, the epiphyses do not fuse, the long bones grow indefinitely and such patients are osteoporotic. Administration of estradiol can correct the bone abnormalities in CYP19 deficiency. However, this cannot be used in those with ER receptor defects.\n\nTestosterone is converted to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) by 5-alpha-reductase.\n\nAndrostanedione and epiandrosterone are metabolites of DHT due to the action of 17-beta-HSD.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 59-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer is started on flutamide therapy. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["5-alpha reductase inhibitor","Non-competitive androgen receptor inhibitor","Competitive androgen receptor inhibitor","Selective Androgen Receptor Modulator (SARM)"],"a":2,"e":"Flutamide acts by competitive inhibition of androgen receptors and is used to treat prostate cancer.\n\nFlutamide is a non-steroidal antiandrogen that competes with androgens such as testosterone and dihydrotestosterone for binding to the androgen receptors in the prostate gland. By doing so, it prevents them from stimulating the prostate cancer cells to grow.\n\nIn addition to its use in prostate cancer, flutamide has been used to treat hyperandrogenism (excess androgen levels) in women, such as in those with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) and Hirsutism.\n\nOther anti-androgens are:\n\n• Bicalutamide, apalutamide, and nilutamide\n\n• Spironolactone (aldosterone antagonist with antiandrogen activity)\n\n• Cyproterone acetate (progestin with antiandrogen activity)\n\n• Cimetidine (H2 blocker with anti-androgen activity)\n\nSelective Androgen Receptor Modulators (SARMs) are a class of androgen receptor ligands that have similar anabolic properties to anabolic steroids, but with reduced androgenic side effects. There are no FDA-approved SARMs at present.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old medical student is worried about his receding hairline and is thinking about taking finasteride. The drug enhances hair growth by acting on which of the following?","o":["Aromatase enzyme","5 alpha reductase enzyme","Androgen receptors","PDE-5 enzyme"],"a":1,"e":"Finasteride is a competitive inhibitor of 5 α-reductase enzyme and thereby inhibits the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone.\n\nFinasteride selectively inhibits the Type 2 isoenzyme of 5α-reductase. It is indicated for use in:\n\n• Benign prostatic hyperplasia\n\n• Male pattern baldness\n\n• Hirsutism.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following inhibits spermatogenesis?","o":["Gemfibrosil","Gemcadiol","Gestodine","Gossypol"],"a":3,"e":"Gossypol is a non-steroidal agent that inhibits spermatogenesis.\n\nGossypol is obtained from cottonseed. It inhibits spermatogenesis by exerting toxic effects on seminiferous tubules but has low efficacy. Libido and potency are not significantly affected and fertility usually returns after a few months of discontinuation of the drug. But, around 10% of the patients may remain infertile. Hypokalemia is a major adverse effect and may even lead to transient paralysis.\n\nLow efficacy and significant toxicity have caused gossypol to be abandoned as a candidate male contraceptive.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old male patient presented to the urology OPD with complaints of erectile dysfunction and was prescribed a PDE-5 inhibitor. Identify the drug.","o":["Podofilox","Finasteride","Apalutamide","Tadalafil"],"a":3,"e":"Tadalafil is a PDE-5 inhibitor used in the treatment of patients with erectile dysfunction.\n\nPenile erection is mediated by NO and cGMP. During arousal, the corpus cavernosum produces nitric oxide which in turn stimulates the formation of cGMP. This leads to relaxation of the smooth muscle of corpus cavernosum and penile arteries, causing engorgement of corpus cavernosum producing an erection. The biological actions of cGMP are terminated by phosphodiesterase enzymes and PDE-5 is the major cGMP metabolizing enzyme in the penile tissue.\n\nPhosphodiesterase-5-inhibitors include:\n\n• Sildenafil\n\n• Tadalafil - Longest acting (24-36 hrs), bioavailability is not affected by food\n\n• Vardenafil\n\n• Avanafil - Most Rapid-acting (within 15 -30 mins)\n\nOption A: Podofilox topical cream is used in the treatment of genital warts and molluscum contagiosum lesions.\n\nOption B: Finasteride is a 5 α-reductase inhibitor.\n\nOption C: Apalutamide is a androgen receptor antagonist.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old diabetic patient was prescribed sildenafil for his erectile dysfunction. Following this, he develops a blurring of vision and complains that things seem to appear with a bluish-green hue. Inhibition of which of the following enzymes is likely to have caused his visual side effects?","o":["Phosphodiesterase 3","Phosphodiesterase 5","Phosphodiesterase 6","Phosphodiesterase 2"],"a":2,"e":"Visual disturbance seen with sildenafil is due to PDE-6 inhibition.\n\nSildenafil is used for the treatment of erectile dysfunction principally due to its inhibitory actions on PDE-5 (Phosphodiesterase 5) in the vascular tissues of the corpus cavernosa. However, it also exerts minor inhibitory action against PDE-6, which is present exclusively in rod and cone photoreceptors and results in blurring and blue-green tinting of vision.\n\nTadalafil and avanafil have better PDE-5/PDE-6 selectivity, compared to sildenafil and vardenafil and hence have lesser visual side effects.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a prostaglandin that can be administered as an intracavernosal injection for erectile dysfunction?","o":["Dinoprost","Dinoprostone","Alprostadil","Misoprostol"],"a":2,"e":"Alprostadil can be given as a direct intracavernosal injection for erectile dysfunction.\n\nAlprostadil is a natural PGE1 preparation. It is usually used in patients who are not responding to PDE-5 inhibitors. It is also available as an intraurethral suppository.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old physician presented to the ED with a prolonged painful erection induced by intracorporeal injection of an alprostadil+phentolamine combination. He had been using this technique to overcome occasional performance anxiety. Corporeal aspiration reveals dark coloured blood and based on further workup, a diagnosis of low flow priapism is made. Which of the following is the drug of choice to treat his condition?","o":["Finasteride","Phenoxybenzamine","Phenylephrine","Prazosin"],"a":2,"e":"Phenylephrine is the drug of choice for the treatment of low flow priapism.\n\nCharacteristics of low-flow priapism include painful rigid erection and ischemic corpora, indicated by dark blood upon corporeal aspiration.\n\nPrazosin, and, phenoxybenzamine are alpha-blockers that cause vasodilation and can worsen priapism.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old male athlete seeks your opinion on the usage of anabolic steroids to improve his performance. What would be your advice?","o":["Reassure the patient that all anabolic steroids are safe to use","Explain clearly that only newer anabolic steroids are safe to use as they have negligible androgenic action","Advice against use and sensitise the patient that all anabolic steroids have significant androgenic action","Explain that older testosterone preparations are more appropriate for use in male athletes"],"a":2,"e":"All anabolic steroids have significant androgenic action and the use of anabolic steroids by athletes is not recommended.\n\nTestosterone and its derivatives are used for their anabolic effects but they have significant androgenic action. Some of them (17 methyl derivatives) are also hepatotoxic. The current consensus is to advise against the use of such anabolic steroids.","t":"Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is administered oxytocin as part of the active management of the third stage of labor (AMTSL) protocol. Which of the following statements is correct regarding this drug?","o":["It is responsible for galactokinesis","It can be given by oral route","It is secreted by anterior pituitary","At higher doses, it exerts diuretic like action"],"a":0,"e":"Oxytocin is responsible for galactokinesis (milk ejection reflex).\n\nOption B: It is a peptide and inactive orally.\n\nOption C: It is secreted by posterior pituitary along with vasopressin.\n\nOption D: At higher doses, it can exert vasopressin like action and cause water retention.","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an oxytocic?","o":["Ethacridine","Ergometrine","Pitocin","Orciprenaline"],"a":3,"e":"Orciprenaline is a beta 2 receptor agonist that has uterine relaxing property.\n\nOxytocics are uterine stimulants that increase uterine motility, especially at term.\n\nUterine stimulants are:\n\n• Posterior pituitary hormones - oxytocin/pitocin\n\n• Ergot derivatives - ergometrine, methylergometrine\n\n• Prostaglandins - PGE2, PGF2 alpha, and others\n\n• Others - quinine, ethacridine\n\nEthacridine is used as an abortifacient.","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old multipara at 40 weeks gestation developed water intoxication and hyponatremia following prolonged IV infusion with oxytocin by an inexperienced junior resident. This adverse effect is due to oxytocin's action on which of the following sites?","o":["Vasopressin V2 receptors on serosal surface of renal tubular cells","Vasopressin V2 receptors on luminal surface of renal tubular cells","Vasopressin V1 receptors on serosal surface of renal tubular cells","Vasopressin V1 receptors on luminal surface of renal tubular cells"],"a":0,"e":"Oxytocin can bind to V2 receptors on the serosal surface of renal tubular cells resulting in reduced free water clearance.\n\nThis is because oxytocin and vasopressin have structural similarities. Oxytocin can have vasopressin (ADH) like activity at V2 receptors. The increase in water permeability occurs through the insertion of aquaporins (water channels) in collecting tubules.\n\nProlonged i.v. administration at high doses in conjunction with large volumes of hypotonic fluid may cause water intoxication associated with hyponatremia.\n\nTo avoid this complication, whenever high doses of oxytocin are administered over a long time:\n\n• An electrolyte-containing diluent must be used (not dextrose)\n\n• The volume of infused fluid should be kept low (by infusing oxytocin at a higher concentration)\n\n• Fluid intake must be restricted, fluid balance chart should be kept and serum electrolytes should be monitored","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is oxytocin not indicated?","o":["A primiparous woman with spontaneous premature labor","A multiparous woman with postpartum haemorrhage","A primiparous woman with uterine inertia","A patient with breast engorgement due to inefficient milk ejection reflex"],"a":0,"e":"Spontaneous premature labor is not an indication for oxytocics. Infact, it is an indication for tocolytics.\n\nUses of oxytocin are:\n\n• Induction of labor\n\n• Postpartum hemorrhage\n\n• Uterine inertia\n\n• Breast engorgement due to insufficient milk ejection reflex","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 33-year old lady with an obstetric score of G4P3L3 delivered a baby by cesarean section. She is at risk of developing uterine atony and the resident asks you to bring a long-acting analog of oxytocin. Which drug will you get?","o":["Desamino-oxytocin","Carbetocin","Solifenacin","Darifenacin"],"a":1,"e":"Carbetocin is a long-acting analog of oxytocin.\n\nIt is indicated for the prevention of uterine atony following caesarian section and for prevention of PPH.\n\nOption A: Desamino-oxytocin is a buccal formulation of oxytocin that is similar to oxytocin injection.\n\nOptions C and D: Solifenacin and darifenacin are synthetic tertiary amine antimuscarinic agents that are indicated in overactive bladder.","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is/are the primary indication(s) of ergot alkaloids in obstetrics?\n\n1. Initiation of labor\n\n2. Uterine inertia\n\n3. Postpartum hemorrhage\n\n4. Medical termination of pregnancy\n\n5. Preterm labor","o":["2,3","1,2,3","Only 3","1,2,3,4,5"],"a":2,"e":"Ergot derivatives are indicated for the prevention and/or treatment of postpartum hemorrhage.\n\nErgot alkaloids markedly increase the motor activity of the uterus. The capacity to induce sustained uterine tetany and fetal distress exclude their use in the initiation or facilitation of labor.\n\nDue to the high incidence of postoperative vomiting and lack of evidence in reducing bleeding, routine or prophylactic use of uterotonic medications at the time of MTP is not recommended.\n\nErgot alkaloids used in PPH include ergometrine and methylergometrine.","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"You administer methyl ergometrine to a patient with postpartum hemorrhage in the labor ward. Which of the following adverse effects will you not expect to occur in this patient?","o":["Nausea and vomiting","Hypotension","Decrease in pain threshold","Decrease milk secretion postpartum"],"a":1,"e":"Administration of Ergot alkaloids (such as methyl ergotamine) result in hypertension.\n\nThe use of ergot alkaloids is preferably avoided in hypertensive patients.\n\nAdverse effects of ergometrine/methylergometrine are:\n\n• Nausea and vomiting\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Decrease in pain threshold but analgesia might be required\n\n• Decrease in milk secretion postpartum (due to dopaminergic action especially at higher doses)","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year old woman, G2P1L1 at 33 weeks of gestation is diagnosed with spontaneous preterm labor. The senior resident instructed you to give the patient antenatal steroids and a tocolytic. Which of the following drugs will you not choose?","o":["Atosiban","Dopamine","Nifedipine","Isoxsuprine"],"a":1,"e":"Dopamine does not have any significant activity on the tone of the uterus, since uterine relaxation is mediated by beta-2 receptors.\n\nSpecific tocolytic agents are:\n\n• Beta 2 agonists - ritodrine, isoxsuprine, terbutaline\n\n• Calcium channel blockers - nifedipine\n\n• Oxytocin receptor antagonist - atosiban\n\n• Magnesium sulfate\n\n• Indomethacin","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman has an obstetric history of preterm labor and 2 spontaneous abortions. She is very anxious about her current pregnancy and enquires if she can take any medication to prevent preterm labor. Which of the following drugs can be given to her?","o":["Ritodrine","Progesterone","Terbutaline","Nifedipine"],"a":1,"e":"Progesterone can be given prophylactically to reduce the risk of preterm labor.\n\nIt inhibits the secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines and delays cervical ripening. Therefore, progesterone and its derivatives have been advocated to diminish the onset of preterm labor in women at high risk due to previous preterm delivery.\n\nIntramuscular hydroxyprogesterone administered weekly as well as vaginal administration has both been shown to reduce the incidence of preterm delivery.\n\nRitodrine, terbutaline, and nifedipine are tocolytics that are indicated for inhibition of uterine contractions of preterm labor and not for prophylaxis against preterm labor.","t":"Drugs Acting On Uterus","c":"Endocrine and Reproductive Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["NSAIDs act by inhibiting lipooxygenase.","NSAIDs act by inhibiting the phospholipases.","Cyclooxygenase-1 is a housekeeping enzyme.","Phospholipase-A2 is a housekeeping enzyme."],"a":2,"e":"Cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) is primarily a housekeeping enzyme.\n\nNSAIDs act by inhibiting both COX-1 and COX-2.\n\n• COX-1 is constitutively expressed in most cells. It performs housekeeping functions like gastrointestinal cytoprotection, fluid and electrolyte balance in the kidney.\n\n• COX-2 is normally present in insignificant amounts in most of the cells. It is induced by cytokines, growth factors and other stimuli during inflammation.\n\nPhospholipase-A2 is the initial enzyme that acts on the membrane phospholipids to generate arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid produces prostaglandins by the action of the cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX).","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of myocardial infarction asks you why he is being prescribed aspirin. You tell him it prevents the formation of clots which reduces the risk of MI. Formation of which of the following eicosanoids is blocked by aspirin for this purpose?","o":["PGI2","PGD2","Thromboxane A2","All of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Thromboxane A2 is a pro-aggregatory eicosanoid whose formation requires the activity of COX enzyme. Aspirin blocks this step, thus reducing the chances of clot formation.\n\nThromboxane A2 is an inducer of platelet aggregation and amplifies the signal of more potent platelet agonists, such as thrombin and ADP.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient of CREST syndrome has developed pulmonary hypertension. Which of the following drugs will you not prescribe to treat this?","o":["Epoprostenol","Iloprost","Treprostinil","Rioprostil"],"a":3,"e":"Rioprostil is a PGE1 analog and is not indicated in the treatment of pulmonary hypertension.\n\nLong term therapy with PGI2 analogs can delay or preclude the need for lung or heart-lung transplantation. PGI2 analogs used for the treatment of pulmonary hypertension are:\n\n• Epoprostenol (IV)\n\n• Iloprost (IV)\n\n• Treprostinil (IV or SC)\n\nPGI2 (Epoprostenol) can also be used to prevent platelet aggregation and damage during haemodialysis or cardiopulmonary bypass. It also improves the harvest of platelets for transfusion.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old man has presented to the OPD with a complaint of erectile dysfunction not responding to oral medication. The issue is determined to be of vascular origin. Which prostaglandin preparation can be given as a local injection to treat this?","o":["Dinoprost","Epoprostenol","Enprosnil","Alprostadil"],"a":3,"e":"Alprostadil (PGE1) is given as an intracavernosal injection or urethral suppository for erectile dysfunction.\n\nIt is considered as a second-line therapy. Its use has been largely replaced by phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors like sildenafil and tadalafil which are the drugs of choice.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false about aspirin?","o":["It is an irreversible inhibitor of the cyclooxygenase enzyme.","Its clearance is independent of plasma concentration at low dose.","Alkalinization of urine increases its rate of excretion.","Its antiplatelet effect at low dose is related to presystemic COX inhibition."],"a":1,"e":"Aspirin clearance is dependent on plasma concentration at a lower dose.\n\nAspirin shows saturation kinetics. This means that at lower plasma concentrations it has first-order kinetics where the rate of elimination is proportional to the plasma concentration but at higher plasma concentrations it has zero-order kinetics where the rate of elimination is independent of its plasma concentration.\n\nAround 80-90% of aspirin (salicylates) are bound to plasma proteins, especially albumin.\n\nLow dose aspirin acetylates the COX enzyme of platelets in the portal circulation (pre-systemic) and deactivates it which is responsible for antiplatelet action.\n\nAspirin and salicylates are acidic in nature. Alkalinization of the urine increases the rate of excretion of aspirin, as ionised form of aspirin increases which is not reabsorbed from the tubular fluid in kidney.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 12-year-old child developed fever during an epidemic of influenza. Which of the following drugs is contraindicated in this child?","o":["Acetaminophen","Aspirin","Oseltamivir","Baloxavir"],"a":1,"e":"Aspirin is contraindicated in this child due to the risk of development of Reye syndrome.\n\nChildren and young adults less than 20 years of age with viral fever have a risk of developing Reye's syndrome on administration of aspirin or other salicylates.\n\nFeatures of Reye syndrome include:\n\n• Acute onset of encephalopathy\n\n• Liver dysfunction\n\n• Fatty infiltration of the liver\n\nOption A - Acetaminophen is considered the antipyretic of choice in children with viral infections.\n\nOptions C and D - Oseltamivir and baloxavir are antiviral drugs used in influenza.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old pilot with low back pain requires a painkiller that is effective at once-daily dosing. You decide to prescribe the NSAID with the longest plasma half-life. Which drug will you choose?","o":["Aspirin","Phenylbutazone","Piroxicam","Ibuprofen"],"a":2,"e":"Piroxicam is chosen as it has the longest plasma half-life among NSAIDs.\n\nPiroxicam is absorbed completely and undergoes enterohepatic circulation. Due to the long half-life, once-daily administration of the drug is sufficient for the therapeutic response.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of gastritis complains of joint pain. You diagnose it to be a case of osteoarthritis and prescribe celecoxib. Which of the following statements is true regarding this drug?","o":["It causes renal toxicity","It is more likely to cause gastric ulcers compared to ibuprofen","It is not associated with cardiotoxicity","It has antiplatelet action"],"a":0,"e":"Celecoxib is a selective COX-2 inhibitor. As COX-2 enzyme is constitutively active within the kidney, recommended doses of selective COX-2 inhibitors cause renal toxicities similar to those associated with traditional NSAIDs.\n\nOption B - It is less likely to cause gastric ulcers. Celecoxib has analgesic, antipyretic, and anti-inflammatory effects similar to those of non-selective NSAIDs but with better gastrointestinal tolerability.\n\nOption C - It inhibits COX-2-mediated prostacyclin synthesis in the vascular endothelium. As a result, use of celecoxib is associated with increased cardiovascular risk.\n\nOption D - Celecoxib at usual doses does not inhibit platelet aggregation. This is because platelet aggregation is mediated by thromboxane produced by the COX-1.\n\nThese points are also true for other selective COX-2 inhibitors.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs has the least COX-2 selectivity?","o":["Lumiracoxib","Etoricoxib","Valdecoxib","Celecoxib"],"a":3,"e":"Celecoxib has the least COX-2 selectivity among the following.\n\nSome selective COX-2 inhibitors are withdrawn or have restricted use because of an increase in the risk of cardiovascular events like myocardial infarction, serious skin reactions (valdecoxib), fatal hepatic failure (lumiracoxib).\n\nNowadays, dual inhibitors inhibiting 5-LOX and COX like licofelone and tepoxalin are also used for their anti-inflammatory property.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Your senior asks you to administer a selective COX-2 inhibitor intravenously to a patient for post-op analgesia. Which of the following will you choose?","o":["Celecoxib","Etoricoxib","Rofecoxib","Parecoxib"],"a":3,"e":"Parecoxib is chosen as it is the only injectable COX-2 selective drug among the given options.\n\nIt is a water-soluble prodrug of valdecoxib. It is effective in the perioperative period when oral medications are not indicated.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a true statement about acetaminophen?","o":["It has significant analgesic, antiinflammatory and antipyretic action.","Acetaminophen toxicity causes periportal necrosis.","It does not antagonize effects of uricosuric drugs.","It has significant anti-platelet effect."],"a":2,"e":"Acetaminophen does not antagonize effects of uricosuric agents. It may be used concomitantly with probenecid in the treatment of gout as it does not have any effect on uric acid levels.\n\nOption A - Acetaminophen has analgesic and antipyretic action equivalent to aspirin but lacks a significant anti-inflammatory effect.\n\nOption B - Acetaminophen toxicity causes centrilobular necrosis.\n\nOption D - It is a weak inhibitor of both COX-1 and COX-2 and has minimal or no effect on platelet activity.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old patient of major depression presented to the ER after having consumed 20 tablets of acetaminophen (500 mg each) 1 hour ago in a self-harm attempt. She does not have any symptoms at the moment. Which of the following should you administer to her?","o":["Flumazenil","N-acetylcysteine","Methylene blue","Nothing as she is asymptomatic"],"a":1,"e":"The patient in the given clinical scenario has ingested a potentially toxic dose of acetaminophen. N-acetylcysteine is considered as a definitive antidote for acetaminophen poisoning to prevent hepatic injury.\n\nN-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI) is a toxic metabolite of acetaminophen which causes hepatotoxicity. In adults, hepatotoxicity may occur after ingestion of a single dose of 10–15 g (150–250 mg/kg) of acetaminophen; doses of 20–25 g or more are potentially fatal.\n\nIn acetaminophen poisoning, due to increased NAPQI, there is utilization of glutathione (GSH). This in turn leads to depletion of GSH leading to oxidative cellular damage of the hepatocytes. N-acetylcysteine replenishes GSH stores and also conjugates with NAPQI and prevents oxidative damage to the cell. It also protects against extrahepatic injury due to its antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties.\n\nOption A: Flumazenil is an antidote for benzodiazepine toxicity.\n\nOption C: Methylene blue is used in methemoglobinemia.\n\nOption D: Symptoms may take upto 2 days to develop in a case of acetaminophen poisoning by which time the hepatic damage may become irreversible. Thus, early treatment is initiated in all suspected cases.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not considered a hit and run drug?","o":["Phenelzine","Omeprazole","Piroxicam","Aspirin"],"a":2,"e":"Piroxicam is a reversible inhibitor of cyclooxygenase enzyme and cannot be considered as a hit and run drug.\n\nHit and run drugs are a group of drugs whose half-life is short, but cause irreversible inhibition of a particular enzyme. So, the effect of the drug is present even after the drug is cleared from the system. Examples include:\n\n• MAO inhibitors ( e.g.: Phenelzine)\n\n• PPI (e.g.: omeprazole)\n\n• Guanethidine\n\n• Reserpine\n\n• Aspirin","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients should be prescribed NSAIDs for pain relief?","o":["A hypertensive patient on chlorthalidone having osteoarthritis","Patient suffering from primary dysmenorrhea","Patient complaining of low back pain who is a known case of CKD","Patient having painful diabetic neuropathy"],"a":1,"e":"NSAIDs can be used in the treatment of primary dysmenorrhea. Prostaglandin release from endometrium causes cramps and other symptoms. NSAIDs inhibit the synthesis of prostaglandin and cause symptomatic relief.\n\nOption A: NSAIDs interfere with the antihypertensive effect of diuretics. The exact mechanism is not yet known. So it should be avoided in such cases.\n\nOption C: NSAIDs are generally avoided in renal diseases as inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis worsens renal function.\n\nOption D: NSAIDs generally lack efficacy in cases of neuropathic pain like diabetic neuropathy.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient of chronic low back pain has presented to your OPD with deranged LFT reports. He tells you he has been on long term treatment with a painkiller for his condition. Which NSAID has he most likely been taking?","o":["Acetaminophen","Diclofenac","Piroxicam","Indomethacin"],"a":1,"e":"Diclofenac is the NSAID that has the highest risk of hepatotoxicity within the therapeutic dose.\n\nLiver injury occurs in acetaminophen overdose but it is extremely rare in therapeutic dose. NSAIDs are not recommended in advanced hepatic and renal failure.","t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old male on multiple drugs presents with a round macule on his right leg for the last 7 days. The patient gives a history of similar episodes in the past which resolved spontaneously. Which of the following drugs is the most likely cause?","o":["Ibuprofen","Phenytoin","Captopril","Carbamazepine"],"a":0,"e":"This is a case of fixed drug eruption and the most likely cause is ibuprofen.\n\nFixed drug eruptions are characterized by sharply demarcated dull red to brown lesions. They occur in lips, hands, legs, face, genitalia, and oral mucosa, and cause a burning sensation. The lesion recurs in the same locations upon re-exposure to the offending drug.\n\nSome of the drugs commonly known to cause fixed drug eruptions are Sulphonamides, NSAIDs, Analgesics, and Hypnotics.\n\nDiagnosis is based on history and lesion morphology. Discontinuation of the offending drug is the first step in management.\n\nPhenytoin and carbamazepine can lead to Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)/toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) and drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS syndrome).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99b112fb42c241358b7fcf4110b4e9a9.JPEG"],"t":"Nsaids","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following receptors is a ligand-gated ion channel?","o":["5-HT 1 receptor","5-HT 2 receptor","5-HT 3 receptor","5-HT 4-7 receptors"],"a":2,"e":"The 5-HT3 receptor is a ligand-gated ion channel receptor. It rapidly depolarizes nerve endings by opening the cation channels located within the receptor.\n\nThe 5-HT3 receptors are located in the GI tract (vagal & splanchnic afferents) and the brain (nucleus tractus solitarius & area postrema) and are involved in the emetic response. Hence 5-HT3 receptor antagonists like ondansetron, dolasetron etc are used as antiemetics.\n\nAll other 5-HT receptors are G-protein-coupled receptors.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding triptans?","o":["They are 5HT 1B/1D agonists","No action on adrenergic or cholinergic or dopaminergic receptors","Sumatriptan is safe in pregnancy","They are used for prophylaxis of migraine"],"a":3,"e":"Triptans are not used in prophylaxis of migraine. They are indicated in the management of acute attacks of migraine.\n\nDrugs used in prophylaxis of migraine are :\n\n• Beta-blocker - Propranolol, metoprolol\n\n• Antidepressant - Amitriptyline, nortriptyline\n\n• Anticonvulsant - Topiramate, valproate\n\n• Serotonergic drug - Pizotifen\n\n• Antihistamine - Flunarizine\n\n• Angiotensin receptor blocker - candesartan","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old patient presented to the ER with an acute attack of migraine. You decide to administer a long-acting triptan. Which among the following do you choose?","o":["Sumatriptan","Rizatriptan","Frovatriptan","Zolmitriptan"],"a":2,"e":"Frovatriptan is a long-acting triptan among other triptans.\n\nHeadache recurrences in an attack are less with longer-acting triptans like frovatriptan and naratriptan but onset of action may be slow.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient of migraine has come to the OPD for follow up. She tells you that she gets very nauseous during the attacks and is not being able to take the oral drug. You decide to prescribe an intranasal formulation instead. Which of the following antimigraine agents will you not choose?","o":["Dihydroergotamine","Sumatriptan","Zolmitriptan","Naratriptan"],"a":3,"e":"Naratriptan is not available for intranasal administration. It is available only for oral administration.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"After the administration of a drug for an acute attack of migraine, a patient developed nausea and vomiting. She also developed tingling and numbness at the tips of her fingers that eventually turned blue. Which of the following is the most likely drug implicated?","o":["Dihydroergotamine","Sumatriptan","Butorphanol","Aspirin"],"a":0,"e":"Nausea and vomiting, as well as tingling and numbness (paresthesia), are common adverse effects of dihydroergotamine.\n\nThe numbness progressing to bluish discolouration of the fingers are the initial signs of occlusion. It can eventually lead to gangrene if left untreated. This is a rare adverse effect of dihydroergotamine.\n\nContraindications for ergotamine:\n\n• Peripheral vascular disease\n\n• Coronary artery disease\n\n• Uncontrolled hypertension\n\n• Fever and/or sepsis\n\n• Pregnancy\n\n• Within 24 hours of using triptans","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient has just been diagnosed with migraine. Which of the following drugs can you prescribe for prophylaxis?","o":["Sumatriptan","Ergotamine","Domperidone","Topiramate"],"a":3,"e":"Topiramate is used in the prophylaxis of migraine. Long-term use of topiramate can cause weight loss.\n\nThe drugs that have been approved by the FDA for the preventive treatment of migraine include:\n\n• Propranolol\n\n• Timolol\n\n• Sodium valproate\n\n• Topiramate\n\nOptions A and B - Sumatriptan, a 5HT1B/1D agonist is a first-line agent for the termination of acute attacks. Ergotamines are also used in the termination of acute attacks.\n\nOption C - Domperidone is indicated as an adjuvant in acute attacks. Acute attacks are associated with gastric stasis and an increased incidence of nausea and vomiting. It is helpful in reducing gastrointestinal effects. It can also increase gastrointestinal absorption of other antimigraine agents that are not absorbed due to gastric stasis.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with chronic tophaceous gout was prescribed sulfinpyrazone. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Inhibits formation of uric acid","Promotes excretion of uric acid","Inhibits xanthine oxidase enzyme","Inhibits cyclooxygenase enzyme"],"a":1,"e":"Sulfinpyrazone is a uricosuric agent that promotes excretion of uric acid. It is used to decrease the body pool of urate in patients with gout. They act by inhibiting the organic anion transporter that is involved in uric acid reabsorption in renal tubules.\n\nUricosuric agents used in gout are:\n\n• Probenecid\n\n• Sulfinpyrazone\n\n• Benzbromarone\n\n• Lesinurad\n\nUricosuric therapy should be initiated in gouty patients with underexcretion of uric acid and when allopurinol or febuxostat is contraindicated or when tophi are present. Therapy should not be started until 2–3 weeks after an acute attack.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged obese man has presented to the OPD with acute pain around his left first metatarsophalangeal joint. He has a history of similar episodes in the past. X-ray is given below. Which drug is commonly used as initial treatment for this condition?","o":["Aspirin","Indomethacin","Allopurinol","Colchicine"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario and the X-ray showing Martel's sign point towards a diagnosis of acute on chronic presentation of gout. Indomethacin is commonly used in the initial treatment of gout.\n\nIn addition to inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis, indomethacin and other NSAIDs also inhibit urate crystal phagocytosis.\n\nOption A - Aspirin is not used because it causes renal retention of uric acid at low doses. It is uricosuric only at higher doses that are poorly tolerated.\n\nOption C - Allopurinol is used for prophylaxis of gout.\n\nOption D - NSAIDs are selected over colchicine because of troublesome diarrhea associated with colchicine.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a063ae8e64e4e7bb4bc1d04c6362f44.PNG"],"t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing chemotherapy was diagnosed with tumour lysis syndrome. She was administered rasburicase. Which group does this drug belong to?","o":["Recombinant xanthine oxidase","Recombinant urate oxidase","Inhibitor of xanthine oxidase","Inhibitor of uric acid oxidase"],"a":1,"e":"Rasburicase is a recombinant urate oxidase which converts uric acid into soluble and inactive allantoin. It is more effective in reducing the urate level than allopurinol.\n\nRecombinant uric acid oxidase is available as rasburicase and pegloticase. It is produced by genetically modified Saccharomyces cerevisiae strain. They are indicated in gout only for chronic refractory gout. It is primarily indicated for hyperuricemia associated with tumor lysis syndrome.\n\nPegloticase is a recombinant pegylated uric acid oxidase that is indicated for treatment of severe, treatment-refractory, chronic gout and also prophylaxis of gout.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"You have prescribed the first-line urate-lowering drug to a patient with chronic gout. Which of the following is true about this drug?","o":["Pyrimidine antimetabolite","Inhibitor of neutrophil recruitment","Uricosuric drug","Xanthine oxidase inhibitor"],"a":3,"e":"Allopurinol is recommended as the first-line urate-lowering agent in all patients with gout. It is a purine antimetabolite and an inhibitor of xanthine oxidase enzyme.\n\nIt prevents the conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid reducing plasma uric acid levels. Allopurinol is also a substrate for xanthine oxidase. The metabolite of allopurinol called oxypurinol is a non-competitive inhibitor of xanthine oxidase.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient was on treatment with indomethacin for gout. You decide to add a non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor to his treatment plan. Which of the following will you prescribe?","o":["Allopurinol","Febuxostat","Either a or b","Sulfinpyrazone"],"a":1,"e":"Febuxostat is a non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor that is indicated for reducing hyperuricemia in chronic gout. Both allopurinol and febuxostat can be used as first line agents but since the question mentions a non-purine inhibitor is chosen, febuxostat is the answer.\n\nOption A - Allopurinol is a purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor.\n\nOption D - Sulfinpyrazone is a uricosuric agent.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient, who is a known case of gout on treatment with allopurinol, comes to the OPD for follow up. His serum uric acid level is 11 mg/dl. You add lesinurad to his treatment regime for adequate disease control. Which group does this drug belong to?","o":["Uric acid synthesis inhibitor","Uricosuric agent","Xanthine oxidase inhibitor","Recombinant xanthine oxidase"],"a":1,"e":"Lesinurad is a uricosuric agent.\n\nIt is a URAT1 inhibitor indicated in combination with a xanthine oxidase inhibitor for the treatment of hyperuricemia associated with gout. By inhibiting URAT1, it increases the urinary excretion of uric acid.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is correct about the drugs used in gout?\n\n1. Aspirin is not used as it decreases the uric acid secretion\n\n2. Diclofenac is used as it concentrates in the joint\n\n3. Indomethacin is used as it acts by multiple pathways\n\n4. Aspirin is used as it is most powerful NSAID\n\n5. Indomethacin is not used as it is not very effective","o":["1, 2 &amp; 3","2, 3 &amp; 4","1, 2 &amp; 5","3 &amp; 4"],"a":0,"e":"Gout is a metabolic disease associated with hyperuricemia.\n\nThe drug of choice in an acute attack of gout is NSAIDs (non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) like indomethacin or the drug colchicine. Colchicine is poorly tolerated and should not be used in elderly patients with chronic kidney disease.\n\nIndomethacin is commonly used in the initial treatment of gout. In addition to inhibiting prostaglandin synthase, indomethacin and other NSAIDs also inhibit urate crystal phagocytosis\n\nIntraarticular steroids like triamcinolone or methylprednisolone can also be given.\n\nAspirin is not used because it causes renal retention of uric acid at low doses. It is uricosuric only at higher doses that are poorly tolerated.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient was prescribed erenumab-aooe for the prophylaxis of migraine. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Parathyroid hormone related protein (PTHrP) receptor agonist","Parathyroid hormone related protein (PTHrP) receptor antagonist","Calcitonin gene related protein (CGRP) receptor agonist","Calcitonin gene related protein (CGRP) receptor antagonist"],"a":3,"e":"Erenumab-aooe is a calcitonin gene related protein (CGRP) receptor antagonist approved for the prophylaxis of migraine.\n\nThe pathology of migraine is not completely understood but it includes activation of trigeminal ganglion nociceptive neurons and the subsequent release of CGRP from fibers associated with meningeal vessels. This is believed to mediate vasodilation and mast cell degranulation. Thus, CGRP is involved in the development and maintenance of persistent pain, central sensitization and allodynia, which are events characteristic of migraine pathology.\n\nBlockage of CGRP receptors by CGRP receptor antagonists, causes inhibition of the inflammatory and nociceptive effects of CGRP at multiple sites within the peripheral and central nervous systems that are implicated in the underlying pathology of migraine.\n\nMonoclonal antibodies that directly inhibit CGRP have also been developed. These include:\n\n• Fremanezumab\n\n• Galcanezumab\n\nNote:\n\nThe FDA has recently approved Atogepant, a calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) receptor antagonist for the preventive treatment of episodic migraine in adults. The recommended dosage is 10 mg, 30 mg, or 60 mg orally once daily. In severe renal impairment or end-stage renal disease it is 10 mg once daily.","t":"Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has been on treatment for the past 15 years. During an annual eye check-up, her fundus appeared as shown in the picture given. Which of the following anti-rheumatoid drugs likely led to this side effect?","o":["Cyclophosphamide","Methotrexate","Hydroxychloroquine","Leflunomide"],"a":2,"e":"The image shows bull’s eye maculopathy.\n\nHydroxychloroquine use is associated with this side effect.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/53a773fdfa0041548e5b82487376e5de.PNG"],"t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman with rheumatoid arthritis was on DMARD therapy with a drug that increases extracellular adenosine. Which drug was she likely on?","o":["Methotrexate","Lefunamide","Sulphasalazine","Hydroxychloroquine"],"a":0,"e":"Methotrexate is the DMARD that increases extracellular adenosine.\n\nIt is a dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) inhibitor that leads to purine synthesis inhibition and this is cytotoxic to lymphocytes.\n\nIt also inhibits amino-imidazole carboxamide ribonucleotide (AICAR) transformylase and thymidylate synthetase. This leads to increased extracellular adenosine which is anti-inflammatory.\n\nThe increased extracellular adenosine is hepatotoxic and hence monitoring with LFT is needed every 2-3 months.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c28e6d580919489d97a36f1f7b2db64ax1280x1432.JPEG"],"t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis on treatment with methotrexate came for a follow-up visit. Her peripheral blood smear shows the following finding. What drug can be administered to alleviate this side effect?","o":["Ascorbic acid","Cyanocobalamin","Thiamine","Folinic acid"],"a":3,"e":"The peripheral smear findings point to a diagnosis of megaloblastic anemia. Folinic acid is given with methotrexate to decrease its side effects.\n\nMethotrexate is a dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) inhibitor and thus can lead to a deficiency of folic acid which may present as megaloblastic anemia.\n\nFolinic acid/leucovorin calcium/citrovorum factor is the 5-formyl derivative of tetrahydrofolic acid. Thus, it decreases the side effects caused due to folate deficiency.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/650badba96cf4b7cb1b3c15c2f5a15a6.PNG"],"t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can be used for the treatment of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Sulfacetamide","Sulfasalazine","Silver sulfadiazine","Mafenide"],"a":1,"e":"Sulfasalazine can be used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis, as a conventional DMARD. It is also used in the management of ulcerative colitis and regional enteritis.\n\nSulfasalazine is metabolized into the following:\n\n• 5-amino salicylic acid (5-ASA) - it is not absorbed and is used to treat ulcerative colitis.\n\n• Sulfapyridine - it is absorbed and is used to treat rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nIt is used as monotherapy in mild cases and as an add-on drug in moderate to severe cases.","t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"How does etanercept act?","o":["Interleukin 1 inhibition","Interleukin 6 inhibition","TNF alpha inhibition","Janus kinase inhibition"],"a":2,"e":"Etanercept acts by TNF alpha inhibition. It is a biological DMARD (disease-modifying anti-rheumatoid drug).","t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are using TNF alpha inhibitors in the treatment of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis. Which of the following is not true regarding these drugs?","o":["They can also be used in the treatment of plaque psoriasis","They can be used in the treatment of psoriatic arthritis","They can be used in the treatment of lymphomas","They can be used in the treatment of ulcerative colitis"],"a":2,"e":"TNF-α inhibitors are not used in the treatment of lymphomas.\n\nDespite their benefits, multiple potential adverse effects following the use of TNF-alpha inhibitors have been reported. These include:\n\n• Malignancy (lymphoma)\n\n• Injection site reactions\n\n• Infusion reactions\n\n• Opportunistic infections.\n\nTNF-α inhibitors can be used in the treatment of:\n\n1. Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n2. Ankylosing spondylitis\n\n3. Crohn's disease\n\n4. Ulcerative colitis\n\n5. Plaque psoriasis\n\n6. Psoriatic arthritis.","t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man with rheumatoid arthritis on treatment with methotrexate presents to your OPD complaining of no improvement in his condition. Which of the following is an IL-1 receptor antagonist that can you prescribe for him?","o":["Anakinra","Rituximab","Tofacitinib","Golimumab"],"a":0,"e":"The IL-1 receptor antagonist that is used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis is anakinra.\n\nAnakinra is a biological DMARD (Disease Modifying Anti-Rheumatoid Drug) used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis. All biological DMARDs downregulate the immune system and can lead to adverse effects like opportunistic infections and flaring of latent TB.","t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which is the monoclonal antibody approved for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Durvalumab","Sarilumab","Nivolumab","Pembrolizumab"],"a":1,"e":"The monoclonal antibody approved for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis is sarilumab.\n\nIt is an IL-6 receptor antagonist indicated for the treatment of adults with moderate to severe rheumatoid arthritis who show inadequate response or intolerance to one or more disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs).\n\nOption A: Durvalumab is an anti PD-L1 antibody approved for metastatic urothelial cancer.\n\nOption C: Nivolumab is a fully human monoclonal IgG4 antibody that blocks the interaction between PD-1 (Programmed cell Death-1) receptor and its ligands.\n\nOption D: Pembrolizumab (formerly called lambrolizumab or MK-3475) is a humanized monoclonal IgG4-κ isotype antibody that blocks the interaction between PD-1 and its ligands. Nivolumab and pembrolizumab are used to treat advanced melanoma.","t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a Janus kinase inhibitor approved for use in rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Ruxolitinib","Baricitinib","Denosumab","Dasatinib"],"a":1,"e":"Baricitinib was approved for use in patients with rheumatoid arthritis. It is a Janus kinase inhibitor.\n\nOral Janus kinase inhibitors for rheumatoid arthritis and upadacitinib and tofacitinib.\n\nOption A: Ruxolitinib is a Janus kinase inhibitor, approved for use in various forms of myelofibrosis, polycythemia vera, and acute graft vs host disease.\n\nOption C: Denosumab is a RANK-L inhibitor that is approved for the treatment of osteoporosis.\n\nOption D: Dasatinib and ponatinib are BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors indicated for the treatment of CML.","t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not an indication for the use of corticosteroids in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Management of acute disease flares while on therapy with DMARDs","As bridging therapy initially, till DMARDs take effect","Chronic therapy in cases that cannot be controlled with DMARDs","As monotherapy for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis"],"a":3,"e":"Glucocorticoids are not used as monotherapy for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis (RA).\n\nHigh doses of glucocorticoids may be needed to treat severe extraarticular manifestations of RA.\n\nIntraarticular injection of triamcinolone can be considered for one/few actively inflamed joints.\n\nOption A: A burst of glucocorticoids for 1-2 weeks may be prescribed to manage acute disease flares.\n\nOption B: They may be administered as bridging therapy initially to achieve rapid disease control till DMARDs take effect, which may take several weeks or even months.\n\nOption C: Chronic therapy with low doses may be needed to control the disease in patients with an inadequate response to DMARD therapy.","t":"Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following histamine receptors are presynaptic autoreceptors?","o":["H1","H2","H3","H4"],"a":2,"e":"H3 histamine receptor is a presynaptic autoreceptor.\n\nH3 receptors are predominantly presynaptic and are present in basal ganglia, hippocampus, and cerebral cortex. They function as autoreceptors inhibiting histamine release from the neuron. H3 receptors are also found postsynaptically but their function is not known.\n\nHistamine receptor\n\n G-protein coupling\n\nDistribution\n\nH1\n\nGq\n\nSmooth muscle, endothelial cells, CNS\n\nH2\nGs\n\nGastric parietal cells, cardiac muscle, mast cells, CNS\n\nH3\n\nGi/o\n\nCNS: predominantly presynaptic but also found postsynaptically\n\nH4\n\nGi/o\n\nCells of hematopoietic origin","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following will not be seen on the administration of an H3 receptor agonist?","o":["Inhibition of H1 receptor induced wakefulness","Increase in H2 mediated gastric secretion","Inhibition of H1 mediated bronchoconstriction","Negative chronotropic effect on atria"],"a":1,"e":"H3 receptor agonist decreases H2 mediated gastric secretion.\n\nH3 receptors are presynaptic inhibitory autoreceptors on histaminergic neurons. H3 receptor agonists decrease the release of histamine and have actions similar to that of antihistamines.\n\nNormally, histamine increases gastric secretion by acting through the H2 receptor in the stomach. H3 receptor agonist decreases histamine release and hence, decreases H2 receptor-mediated gastric secretion.\n\nOptions A and C: H1 receptor stimulation by histamine in the CNS causes wakefulness, and at the bronchus causes bronchoconstriction. Therefore H3 receptor agonists by decreasing histamine release inhibit H1 receptor-induced wakefulness and bronchoconstriction.\n\nOption D: H3 receptors are also presynaptic heteroreceptors on various neurons and their activation can decrease noradrenergic, serotoninergic, cholinergic and glutaminergic transmission. A decrease in noradrenergic transmission in atria can result in a negative chronotropic effect.","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are about to prescribe an antihistamine to a patient with allergic rhinitis. You wish to prescribe a second-generation drug as they cause fewer side effects. Which of the following will you not choose?","o":["Olopatidine","Loratidine","Cetrizine","Clemastine"],"a":3,"e":"Clemastine is a first-generation antihistamine.\n\nH1 antagonists are classified into 2 generations:\n\nFirst-generation antihistamines - They have a strong sedative property and also have an antimuscarinic function. They include:\n\n• Doxepin\n\n• Clemastine, Diphenhydramine, Dimenhydrinate\n\n• Promethazine\n\n• Pheniramine, Chlorpheniramine, Dexchlorpheniramine\n\n• Cyproheptadine\n\n• Hydroxyzine, Cyclizine, Meclizine, Buclizine, Cinnarizine\n\nSecond-generation antihistamines - They are less sedative because of less central nervous system penetration and do not block autonomic receptors. They include:\n\n• Olopatadine\n\n• Fexofenadine, Loratadine, Desloratadine\n\n• Cetirizine, Levocetirizine\n\n• Azelastine\n\n• Mizolastine\n\n• Ebastine\n\n• Rupatadine","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A highway truck driver has presented with profuse rhinorrhea and sneezing. Which of the following drugs will you prefer to prescribe to him?","o":["Pheniramine","Promethazine","Hydroxyzine","Fexofenadine"],"a":3,"e":"The truck driver is suffering from allergic rhinitis and fexofenadine can be prescribed to him. This is preferred as it is a second-generation antihistamine having minimal/no sedative effects as it cannot cross the blood-brain barrier easily.\n\nPheniramine, promethazine, and hydroxyzine are first-generation antihistamines which should not be prescribed to drivers and heavy machine workers because they are lipophilic and cross the blood-brain barrier easily and cause sedation.\n\nHighly sedative first-generation antihistamines:\n\n• Diphenhydramine\n\n• Dimenhydrinate\n\n• Promethazine\n\n• Hydroxyzine","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to you with difficulty in writing and performing fine movements. On examination, he has a pill-rolling resting tremor. He has been on treatment with haloperidol for the past 12 years. Which of the following antihistamines would you prefer to prescribe to him?","o":["Chlorpheniramine","Pheniramine","Promethazine","Hydoxyzine"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario points towards a diagnosis of drug-induced Parkinsonism due to long-term intake of haloperidol. Promethazine is the preferred antihistamine as it has the strongest anticholinergic (muscarinic blocking) activity among the given options.\n\nDrug-induced Parkinsonism is caused by drugs that have D2 dopamine antagonistic action like typical antipsychotics (e.g. haloperidol). The decrease in dopaminergic activity results in an unbalanced cholinergic activity. Anticholinergics act by restoring balance.\n\nFirst-generation H1 antagonists have anticholinergic properties, while second-generation H1 antagonists do not have anticholinergic properties.\n\nH1 antagonists having high anticholinergic activity are as follows:\n\n• Promethazine\n\n• Diphenhydramine\n\n• Dimenhydrinate\n\n• Pheniramine","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient having persistent allergic rhinitis is on treatment with intranasal steroids. You wish to add an antihistamine to his treatment regimen. He requests an intranasal preparation. Which of the following will you prescribe?","o":["Azelastine","Ebastine","Fexofenadine","Mizolastine"],"a":0,"e":"Azelastine is an antihistamine which has an intranasal preparation for allergic rhinitis.\n\nOlopatadine and azelastine are the second generations antihistaminics available as intranasal (nasal spray) preparations for allergic rhinitis. They have good topical activity.\n\nIntranasal corticosteroids (INCS) remain the preferred monotherapy for persistent allergic rhinitis (AR). However, additional studies support the additive benefit of combination treatment with INCS and intranasal antihistamines in AR. For patients receiving INCS, an intranasal decongestant may be added to the treatment for upto 4 weeks.","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following antihistaminic drugs has additional platelet-activating factor antagonistic property?","o":["Fexofenadine","Cetirizine","Ebastine","Rupatadine"],"a":3,"e":"Rupatadine is an antihistaminic drug that has additional platelet activating factor antagonistic property and is indicated in allergic rhinitis.\n\nPlatelet-activating factor (PAF) is involved in the pathophysiology of allergic rhinitis, asthma, and anaphylaxis.","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with cystic fibrosis was found to be underweight during his regular check-up. He complains of low appetite and you decide to prescribe an appetite-stimulating agent. Which antihistaminic drug can you prescribe to him?","o":["Cyclizine","Azelastine","Olapatidine","Cyproheptadine"],"a":3,"e":"Cyproheptadine has an appetite-stimulating effect and has been used to promote weight gain in poor eaters.\n\nThe appetite-stimulating effect may be due to the H1 antihistaminic effect and an effect on growth hormone secretion.","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient has presented to your OPD complaining of hearing loss following an attack of vertigo. He says he has had similar episodes in the past. You decide to prescribe him betahistine. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["H1 agonist","H1 antagonist","H2 agonist","H2 antagonist"],"a":0,"e":"The scenario given in the question likely points to a diagnosis of Meniere's disease. Betahistine is a selective H1 agonist used in this condition.\n\nIt causes vasodilation in the inner ear which improves circulation. Hence, it is used to treat vertigo in Meniere's disease.","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with narcolepsy has been prescribed tiprolisant. How does this drug act?","o":["H1 receptor agonism","H1 receptor inverse agonism","H3 receptor agonism","H3 receptor inverse agonism"],"a":3,"e":"Tiprolisant is a selective H3 receptor - inverse agonist.\n\nTiprolisant promotes wakefulness, improves cognitive functions (e.g., enhances memory, learning, and attention), and reduces food intake. It is also known as pitolisant, and it reduces excessive daytime sleepiness and cataplexy in adults with narcolepsy.","t":"Anti Histamines","c":"Autacoids and Anti-inflammatory Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of myocardial infarction has been on 75 mg aspirin OD for two years. The drug primarily acts by inhibiting which enzyme?","o":["Cyclooxygenase 1","Cyclooxygenase 2","Both a and b","Lipoxygenase"],"a":0,"e":"Aspirin at low doses (75mg) primarily acts by inhibiting cyclooxygenase 1 (COX-1).\n\nLow-dose aspirin, used for antiplatelet action, results in selective inhibition of platelet COX-1 (thromboxane synthase) inhibiting its ability to form thromboxane A2 (needed for platelet aggregation).\n\nAs platelets lack nuclei, new platelets have to be formed (life span of 7-10 days) for the return of COX-1 activity.\n\nAt higher doses (~1g/day), aspirin also inhibits COX-2 and results in decreased production of prostacyclin from endothelium. This may nullify the beneficial anti-aggregatory effect of aspirin.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86e4d0d5598943c3b1b9608e6621be8dx1279x1740.JPEG"],"t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old underwent coronary stent implantation. He was prescribed dual antiplatelet therapy for 6 months in which clopidogrel is one of the components. Which of the following is not true about this drug?","o":["It inhibits Gp IIb/IIIa receptors","The onset of action is slow","It is activated by the hepatic CYP2C19","It has a long duration of action"],"a":0,"e":"Clopidogrel acts by irreversible inhibition of P2Y12 receptors of the platelets.\n\nOptions B and C: The onset of action is slow (starts after 4 hours) as it requires metabolic activation by the hepatic CYP2C19 enzyme. Patients with a loss of function mutation of the CYP2C19 allele exhibit reduced platelet inhibition and experience a higher rate of cardiovascular events.\n\nOption D: Due to irreversible inhibition of platelet P2Y12 receptors the duration of action is long and lasts for 5-7 days.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d700b764308341ee86adcd820bd63332x1279x1740.JPEG"],"t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"The efficacy of which of the following antiplatelet drugs will be reduced, if omeprazole is used concurrently?","o":["Clopidogrel","Prasugrel","Ticagrelor","Vorapaxar"],"a":0,"e":"Clopidogrel is a prodrug that is converted into an active metabolite by the CYP2C19 enzyme and omeprazole can reduce the efficacy of clopidogrel due to its CYP2C19 inhibitory activity.\n\nOption B: Prasugrel is converted to its active metabolite mainly by other CYP enzymes and therefore omeprazole does not interfere in its activation.\n\nOption C: Ticagrelor does not require activation and is metabolized by the CYP3A4 enzyme.\n\nOption D: Vorapaxar is a thrombin receptor antagonist that is also metabolized by the CYP3A4 enzyme.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a prodrug?","o":["Clopidogrel","Prasugrel","Ticagrelor","Ticlopidine"],"a":2,"e":"Ticagrelor is not a prodrug and does not require metabolic activation to exert its antiplatelet action.\n\nIt is an orally active, reversible antagonist of P2Y12 subtype of ADP receptors. Since the drug does not require hepatic activation, it can be used in patients with CYP2C19 mutations. Ticagrelor is metabolized into another active metabolite by CYP3A4 and is finally excreted in the urine.\n\nClopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticlopidine are irreversible antagonists of P2Y12 and are prodrugs requiring metabolic activation.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about prasugrel?","o":["It is a reversible inhibitor of ADP receptor","Has a slower onset of action than clopidogrel","It is contraindicated in cerebrovascular accidents","Dose adjustment is needed in renal impairment"],"a":2,"e":"Prasugrel is contraindicated in cerebrovascular accidents as it is associated with higher rates of fatal and life-threatening bleeding.\n\nOption A: It is an irreversible inhibitor of the ADP (P2Y12) receptor of platelets.\n\nOption B: It has a faster onset of action and produces greater inhibition of platelets than clopidogrel.\n\nOption D: Dose adjustment is not needed in moderate hepatic and renal impairment.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs does not act by blocking Gp IIb/ IIIa receptors?","o":["Abciximab","Eptifibatide","Tirofiban","Vorapaxar"],"a":3,"e":"Vorapaxar is a competitive antagonist of PAR-1 (thrombin receptor) and inhibits thrombin-induced platelet aggregation.\n\nAbciximab, tirofiban, and eptifibatide act by blocking Gp IIb/IIIa receptors. They are used in patients undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) for acute coronary syndrome. They carry a high risk of bleeding and can cause thrombocytopenia.\n\nEptifibatide is contraindicated in renal failure and tirofiban needs dose adjustment in cases of renal impairment.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a294fc581a94b0dbb3ebd8b07b09fcax1279x1740.JPEG"],"t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a urine output of 0.4ml/Kg per hour in the last 6 hours can be safely administered which of the following GP IIb/IIIa antagonists?","o":["Abciximab","Eptifibatide","Tirofiban","All of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Abciximab can safely be administered to this patient who is likely suffering from acute kidney injury (urine volume less than 0.5 mL/kg per hour for 6 hours).\n\nAbciximab, eptifibatide, and tirofiban are parenteral GpIIb/IIIa receptor antagonists used in the treatment of acute coronary syndromes.\n\nEptifibatide is contraindicated in renal failure and tirofiban needs dose adjustment in cases of renal impairment. Both these drugs undergo renal clearance and used cautiously in renal impairment.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old male patient requires percutaneous coronary intervention. He was given an IV infusion of a reversible ADP receptor inhibitor. Which of the following drugs was used?","o":["Abciximab","Eptifibatide","Cangrelor","Tirofiban"],"a":2,"e":"Cangrelor is a parenteral, reversible ADP receptor (P2Y12 receptor) inhibitor, administered intravenously.\n\nAbciximab, eptifibatide, and tirofiban are parenteral agents too but, they are GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"After suffering from a myocardial infarction, a patient was started on two drugs X and Y. The drug X inhibits the COX-1 enzyme and drug Y inhibits thrombin-induced platelet aggregation. Identify the drugs.","o":["X- Aspirin, Y- Clopidogrel","X- Clopidogrel, Y- Vorapaxar","X- Aspirin, Y- Vorapaxar","X- Aspirin, Y- Cangrelor"],"a":2,"e":"X- Aspirin and Y- Vorapaxar are the drugs administered to the patient.\n\nAspirin reduces thromboxane A2 production by inhibiting the COX-1 enzyme.\n\nVorapaxar is a competitive antagonist of PAR-1 and inhibits thrombin-induced platelet aggregation. Vorapaxar is indicated in combination with aspirin and/or clopidogrel to reduce thromboembolic events in patients with MI or peripheral vascular disease.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"How is alteplase differentiated from streptokinase?","o":["It is longer acting","It is cheap","It is derived from human kidney","It has more affinity towards plasminogen bound to fibrin"],"a":3,"e":"Alteplase differs from streptokinase as it has more affinity towards plasminogen bound to fibrin.\n\nIt activates fibrin-bound plasminogen more rapidly than it activates plasminogen in the circulation. Streptokinase activates both free and fibrin bound plasminogen.\n\nOption A: Alteplase is rapidly cleared by the liver and inactivated by plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 making it shorter-acting than streptokinase.\n\nOption B: It is also more expensive than streptokinase\n\nOption C: Alteplase is the recombinant tissue-type plasminogen activator derived from human tissue (endothelial cells) culture.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"In patients with which of the following conditions would fibrinolytic therapy be an absolute contraindication?\n\n1. Active peptic ulcer\n\n2. Ischaemic stroke in past 3 months\n\n3. Menstrual bleeding\n\n4. Suspected aortic dissection\n\n5. Uncontrolled hypertension","o":["1, 3 and 5","2 and 4","3 and 4","1 and 2"],"a":1,"e":"Among the given options, ischaemic stroke in the past 3 months and suspected aortic dissection are absolute contraindications to fibrinolytic therapy.\n\nActive peptic ulcer and uncontrolled hypertension are relative contraindications for fibrinolytic therapy. Menstruation is not a contraindication to fibrinolytic therapy.\n\nNote: History of a hemorrhagic stroke at any time is an absolute contraindication for thrombolytic therapy.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a recombinant tissue-type plasminogen activator?","o":["Alteplase","Anistreplase","Tenecteplase","Reteplase"],"a":1,"e":"Anistreplase is not a recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA).\n\nAnistreplase (anisoylated plasminogen streptokinase activator complex) is a preformed complex of human plasminogen and bacterial streptokinase which is used as a thrombolytic agent.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following plasminogen activator can be administered as a single bolus dose for a patient who requires coronary thrombolysis?","o":["Alteplase","Tenecteplase","Reteplase","Anistreplase"],"a":1,"e":"Tenecteplase can be administered as a single bolus dose for patients requiring coronary thrombolysis.\n\nOptions A and D: Alteplase and anistreplase are administered as intravenous infusion.\n\nOption C: Reteplase can also be given as two IV boluses, given 30 minutes apart.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old woman came to the gynecology OPD with a history of heavy menstrual bleeding. She complained of having to change 4-5 pads in a day and an increase in bleeding to more than 7 days since the last few months. Which of the following is the preferred drug that can be given to this patient?","o":["Mefenamic acid","Tranexamic acid","Epsilon amino caproic acid","Acetylsalicylic acid"],"a":1,"e":"Tranexamic acid is the drug approved for treating heavy menstrual bleeding.\n\nIt is a lysine analogue that binds to the active site of plasmin and prevents the interaction of fibrin with plasmin. This prevents the dissolution of clots and decreases bleeding.\n\nOption A: Mefenamic acid is an NSAID and indicated in primary dysmenorrhea.\n\nOption C: ε-Aminocaproic acid is also an antifibrinolytic agent like tranexamic acid. However, it is not approved for heavy menstrual bleeding.\n\nOption D: Acetylsalicylic acid is aspirin, an anti-platelet drug.","t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old man admitted with ischemic stroke was given IV alteplase. After 8 hours, the ICU staff noticed decreasing mental status and acute hypertension in the patient. A repeat CT revealed the following, Which of the following drugs can be used to manage this patient?","o":["Protamine","Streptokinase","Heparin","ε-Aminocaproic acid"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of post-thrombolytic hemorrhagic transformation of an ischemic stroke. ε-Aminocaproic acid can be used as an antidote for fibrinolytic therapy.\n\nIt is a lysine analog that binds competitively to plasminogen, blocking plasminogen from binding to fibrin and the subsequent conversion to plasmin. This activity subsequently results in the inhibition of fibrinolysis.\n\nIt should not be given unless hemorrhage is life-threatening because it inhibits intrinsic fibrinolytic activity and can precipitate DIC. Fresh frozen plasma and cryoprecipitate may also be used to replenish fibrin and clotting factors.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb08e9bb3c00434293e681b7169a94d4.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5434f9f9b644b03994bd2a670bccd16x1280x1847.JPEG"],"t":"Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A newly married couple planning for pregnancy has come to your clinic for preconception counselling. Which of the following drugs will you prescribe to the patient to decrease the chances of having neural tube defects in the baby?","o":["Vitamin B9","Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Vitamin B6"],"a":0,"e":"Preconceptional intake of vitamin B9 (folic acid) results in decreased incidence of neural tube defects like spina bifida, encephalocele, and anencephaly.\n\nAll women of reproductive age should be given 400 µg of folic acid daily. Women, who had fetal neural tube defects in the previous pregnancy, should be given 4 mg of folic acid daily beginning 1 month before conception to about 12 weeks of pregnancy.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old patient has subclinical folate deficiency. Which of the following drugs is least likely to precipitate megaloblastic anaemia in this patient?","o":["Alcohol","Phenytoin","Chloroquine","Sulfasalazine"],"a":2,"e":"Among the following, chloroquine is the least likely drug to precipitate folic acid deficiency.\n\nThe drugs that can result in the deficiency of folic acid include anticonvulsants (phenytoin, primidone), oral contraceptives, and dihydrofolate reductase inhibitors (trimethoprim, sulfasalazine, methotrexate). Megaloblastic anemia can be caused by a deficiency of folic acid or vitamin B12.\n\nA mnemonic for the causes of megaloblastic anemia is: Prime BATSMAN\n\n• Prime – phenytoin, primidone\n\n• B – B12 and folate deficiency\n\n• A – alcohol\n\n• T – Trimethoprim\n\n• S – Sulfasalazine\n\n• M – Metformin\n\n• A – Antifolate (methotrexate)\n\n• N – N2O","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old patient presented with complaints of burning sensations of the palms and soles. History revealed a predominantly vegetarian diet and workup showed low levels of serum B12. Treatment for this patient must not be administered through which of the following routes?","o":["Oral route","Deep subcutaneous route","Intravenous route","Intramuscular route"],"a":2,"e":"Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) should never be administered through the intravenous route. This is because IV administration will result in almost all of the vitamin being lost in the urine.\n\nCyanocobalamin is usually administered by the intramuscular or deep subcutaneous route. In patients with a previous history of sensitivity to vitamin B12 injections, an intradermal skin test of cyanocobalamine should be done.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Absorption of iron is impeded by which of the following?","o":["Meat based foods","Ascorbic acid","Amino acids containing –SH groups","Egg based foods"],"a":3,"e":"The absorption of iron is impeded by egg-based foods. They contain a high amount of phosphates (egg yolk) that form complexes with iron and impede absorption.\n\nHeme-based iron, present in meat products, is easily absorbed without the aid of a carrier and is not much affected by other foods. Inorganic iron is affected by several factors.\n\nFactors increasing iron absorption are:\n\n• Reducing substances such as ascorbic acid and amino acids containing –SH groups - reduce ferric iron to the soluble ferrous form\n\n• Meat-based foods - enhance heme absorption and increase acid secretion.\n\nFactors decreasing iron absorption are:\n\n• Phosphates, phytates, and tetracyclines - by forming complexes\n\n• Alkalies - prevents the reduction of ferric iron to the soluble ferrous form\n\n• Presence of other food in the stomach - by increasing pH","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"When treating an anemic patient with a history of inflammatory bowel disease, which of the following iron formulations will you prefer?","o":["Ferrous sulphate","Ferrous gluconate","Ferrous sucrose","Ferrous fumarate"],"a":2,"e":"In an anemic patient with IBD, parenteral iron is preferred over oral formulations. The parenteral formulation of iron among the given options is ferrous sucrose.\n\nOn intravenous administration, it is taken up by reticuloendothelial cells where it dissociates and iron is utilized.\n\nThe formulations used for parenteral iron therapy are:\n\n• Iron dextran\n\n• Iron - sorbitol - citric acid\n\n• Ferrous sucrose\n\n• Ferric carboxy maltose\n\nFerrous sulfate, ferrous gluconate, and ferrous fumarate are oral iron preparations.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements regarding erythropoietin is false?\n\n1. Used to treat anaemia due to chronic renal failure\n\n2. Causes functional iron deficiency anaemia\n\n3. Increases requirement for blood transfusions\n\n4. Can aggravate hypertension\n\n5. Decreases the reticulocyte count","o":["3, 4 and 5","3 and 5","1, 2 and 5","2 only"],"a":1,"e":"Erythropoietin decreases the need for blood transfusions and increases reticulocyte counts.\n\nTherapeutic uses and effects of erythropoietin:\n\n• The primary indication of erythropoietin is anaemia due to chronic renal failure\n\n• It causes functional iron deficiency due to the inability to mobilize iron stores rapidly enough to support the increased erythropoiesis (low transferrin saturation)\n\n• It increases the hematocrit, haemoglobin and reduces the need for transfusions\n\n• It aggravates hypertension due to an increase in hematocrit and leads to thrombus formation\n\n• Release of reticulocyte into circulation from the marrow and results in an increase in reticulocyte count.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"In a patient with anaemia due to chronic renal failure, which of the following is the long-acting derivative of erythropoietin suitable for treatment?","o":["Oprelvekin","Darbepoetin alfa","Dornase alfa","Filgastrim"],"a":1,"e":"Darbepoetin alpha is the recombinant derivative of erythropoietin that is long-acting and approved for anaemia due to chronic renal failure.\n\nOption A: Oprelvekin is an IL-11 analog that stimulates megakaryocyte maturation.\n\nOption C: Dornase alfa is a recombinant human DNase 1 that is used in cystic fibrosis to reduce the viscosity of mucosal secretions.\n\nOption D: Filgrastim is a recombinant human G-CSF, indicated in the treatment of severe neutropenia.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Sargramostim is a recombinantly engineered analogue of which of the following?","o":["G-CSF","GM-CSF","Erythropoietin","IL-11"],"a":1,"e":"Sargramostim is a recombinant human GM-CSF (Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor).\n\nIndications for use:\n\n• Autologous bone marrow transplant - reduces the duration of neutropenia\n\n• Cancer chemotherapy - reduces the duration of neutropenia\n\n• Peripheral blood stem cell collection - to mobilize CD34 positive progenitor cells\n\nIt is administered subcutaneously or by slow intravenous infusion.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a thrombopoietin receptor agonist?","o":["Lusutrombopag","Romiplostim","Eltrombopag","Oprelvekin"],"a":3,"e":"Oprelvekin is an IL-11 analogue that stimulates megakaryocyte maturation and is not a thrombopoietin receptor agonist.\n\nRomiplostim, eltrombopag and lusutrombopag are thrombopoietin receptor agonists. They are indicated in patients with immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) who have failed to respond to the conventional treatments.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following agents is used in the treatment of patients who develop severe thrombocytopenia following anti-cancer therapy?","o":["Filgrastim","Oprelvekin","Erythropoietin","Iron dextran"],"a":1,"e":"Oprelvekin is used in the treatment of thrombocytopenia secondary to anti-cancer therapy.\n\nIt is a recombinant human interleukin-11. Its predominant hemopoietic activity is the stimulation of megakaryocytopoiesis. Oprelvekin is indicated for the prevention and treatment of severe thrombocytopenia and the reduction of platelet transfusion requirements following myelosuppressive chemotherapy in patients with non-myeloid malignancies.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient requires autologous stem cell transplantation due to multiple myeloma. A CXCR-4 antagonist which mobilizes stem cells to the peripheral blood is administered few weeks before the procedure. Identify the drug.","o":["Maraviroc","Sargramostim","Filgrastim","Plerixafor"],"a":3,"e":"Plerixafor is the CXCR-4 antagonist indicated for peripheral blood stem cell mobilization before autologous stem cell transplantation.\n\nIt is indicated for peripheral blood stem cell mobilization in patients with Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma and multiple myeloma having inadequate response to G-CSF. It mobilizes the release and entry of HSCs from the bone marrow into circulation. Peripheral blood stem cell mobilization/collection is followed by autologous transplantation.\n\nOption A: Maraviroc is a CCR-5 receptor inhibitor indicated in HIV infections.\n\nOptions B and C: Sargramostim (GM-CSF) and filgrastim (G-CSF) are used for peripheral blood stem cell mobilization.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient having chronic liver disease is set to undergo a tooth extraction. During pre-op evaluation, his platelet count is found to be 60 x 10^9/ L. Which thrombopoietin receptor agonist can you prescribe to treat this?","o":["Eltrombopag","Romiplostim","Oprelvekin","Avatrombopag"],"a":3,"e":"Avatrombopag is a thrombopoietin receptor agonist approved for the treatment of thrombocytopenia with chronic liver disease in patients who are scheduled to undergo a medical or dental procedure.\n\nThrombopoietin receptor agonist stimulates proliferation and differentiation of megakaryocytes from bone marrow progenitor cells resulting in increased production of platelets. Lusutrombopag is a drug with similar action and function.\n\nOptions A & B: Eltrombopag and romiplostim are thrombopoietin receptor agonists indicated in chronic immune thrombocytopenia.\n\nOption C: Oprelvekin is a recombinant IL-11 that is used in the prophylaxis of thrombocytopenia during cancer chemotherapy.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 26-year-old woman presented to the OPD with multiple purpuric lesions. Further workup revealed isolated thrombocytopenia with a normal peripheral smear. Which tyrosine kinase inhibitor is indicated in her treatment?","o":["Romiplostim","Bosutinib","Fostamatinib","Oprelvekin"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario in the question depicts a case of chronic immune thrombocytopenia which can be treated with a tyrosine kinase inhibitor called fostamatinib.\n\nFostamatinib has activity against spleen tyrosine kinase (SYK). The major metabolite of fostamatinib, R406, inhibits signal transduction of Fc-activating receptors and B-cell receptors. The fostamatinib metabolite R406 reduces antibody-mediated destruction of platelets.\n\nOption A: Romiplostim is a TPO receptor agonist indicated in chronic immune thrombocytopenia.\n\nOption B: Bosutinib is a BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitor indicated in CML.\n\nOption D: Oprelvekin is a recombinant IL-11 that is used in the prophylaxis of thrombocytopenia during cancer chemotherapy.","t":"Hematinics","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with sustained atrial fibrillation was given heparin cover. What is the primary mechanism of action of the drug?","o":["Activation of thrombin","Activation of antithrombin","Inhibition of antithrombin","Direct inhibition of thrombin"],"a":1,"e":"The primary mechanism of action of heparin is by activation of antithrombin.\n\nIn patients with atrial fibrillation that has persisted for more than 48 hours, heparin can be used to reduce the risk of thrombus formation and embolization until the warfarin level is therapeutic or cardioversion is performed.\n\nHeparin, low molecular weight heparin (LMWHs), and fondaparinux have no intrinsic anticoagulant activity. They bind to antithrombin and accelerates the rate at which it inhibits various coagulation proteases, especially thrombin (factor IIa) and factor Xa.\n\nLMWH has some action against thrombin. However, fondaparinux has no action against thrombin.\n\nAntithrombin inhibits these factors by serving as a “suicide substrate”.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true about heparin?","o":["It is the weakest acid found in the body","It is commonly obtained from pig intestine","It acts by activation of antithrombin","It can lead to thrombocytopenia"],"a":0,"e":"Heparin is the strongest organic acid present in the body.\n\nOption B: Most commercial preparations of heparin are extracted from the porcine intestinal mucosa.\n\nOption C: Heparin acts indirectly through the activation of antithrombin and inhibition of factors IIa and Xa.\n\nOption D: Heparin use can result in thrombocytopenia due to the development of antibodies to heparin-platelet factor 4 complex.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is receiving low dose heparin infusions after hip replacement surgery to prevent thromboembolism. Which of the following abnormalities are least likely to be expected in this patient?","o":["Prolonged aPTT","Alopecia","Hypokalemia","Thrombocytopenia"],"a":2,"e":"Hypokalemia is least likely seen in this patient because heparin-induced hypoaldosteronism causes hyperkalemia.\n\naPTT is prolonged in heparin therapy and is used to monitor its anticoagulant effect.\n\nThe other side-effects of heparin are (OATH):\n\nO - Osteoporosis\n\nA - Alopecia (reversible)\n\nT - Thrombocytopenia (Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia - HIT)\n\nH - Hemorrhage, Hyperkalemia","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient scheduled for a CABG procedure receives preoperative heparin for anticoagulation. During the surgery, the patient starts bleeding massively and there is a drop in BP. Which of the following would you use to manage this patient?","o":["Protamine sulfate","Prothrombin complex concentrate","Aminocaproic acid","Tranexamic acid"],"a":0,"e":"Protamine sulfate is the drug used to treat life-threatening, heparin-induced bleeding.\n\nIt is a mixture of basic polypeptides isolated from salmon sperm. It binds to acidic heparin with high affinity and neutralizes anticoagulant activity. The resultant protamine-heparin complexes are cleared. Protamine binds only to long heparin molecules (UFH). Therefore, protamine only partially reverses the activity of LMWH and has no effect on fondaparinux.\n\nIt is most commonly used to neutralize heparin-induced anticoagulation after separation from cardiopulmonary bypass. It is also utilized in the setting of dialysis, invasive vascular procedures, and acute ischemic strokes.\n\nNote: Protamine sulfate has anticoagulant activity at higher doses and hence, only small amounts of protamine (1mg/100 units of heparin) are used for heparin-induced bleeding.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Life-threatening bleeding in a patient receiving heparin is treated immediately with a drug X that reverses its effects. Identify the type of antagonism between X and heparin.","o":["Chemical","Physical","Physiological","Competitive"],"a":0,"e":"The drug antagonism between heparin and protamine sulfate (Drug X) is an example of chemical antagonism.\n\nProtamine sulfate is a mixture of basic polypeptides that are positively charged. They bind to the highly acidic heparin with a strong negative charge and form protamine-heparin complexes that are cleared.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Predominant activity of low-molecular-weight heparin is seen against which clotting factor?","o":["IIa","IXa","XIIa","Xa"],"a":3,"e":"Low-molecular-weight heparin(LMWH) has predominant activity against factor Xa.\n\nLMWH has an anti-factor activity against both Xa and IIa with a greater effect on Xa.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"As compared to unfractionated heparin, which of the following is true about enoxaparin?","o":["Is absorbed more uniformly when given subcutaneously","Requires more frequent laboratory monitoring","Can be given to patients with heparin induced thrombocytopenia","Predisposes to a higher risk of osteopenia"],"a":0,"e":"Enoxaparin (LMWH) has a very high bioavailability and is absorbed more uniformly when compared to unfractionated heparin. Hence, it does not require frequent monitoring.\n\nLMWH can also lead to heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) and hence, it cannot be given in patients with HIT.\n\nLMWH preparations:\n\n• Enoxaparin\n\n• Dalteparin\n\n• Tinzaparin.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Fondaparinux can be best described as which of the following?","o":["Low molecular weight heparin","Antithrombin activator","Direct thrombin inhibitor","Direct factor Xa inhibitor"],"a":1,"e":"Fondaparinux is an antithrombin activator.\n\nIt is a synthetic analog of the pentasaccharide unit of heparin that binds and activates antithrombin. Activation of antithrombin causes the inactivation of factor Xa. So it is also described as an indirect factor Xa inhibitor.\n\nIt is not a direct factor Xa inhibitor. Direct factor Xa inhibitors include rivaroxaban, apixaban, and edoxaban.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old patient has been scheduled for cardiopulmonary bypass surgery. Which is the anticoagulant of choice for this patient?","o":["Unfractionated heparin","LMWH","Fondaparinux","Warfarin"],"a":0,"e":"Unfractionated heparin is the anticoagulant of choice for patients requiring cardiopulmonary bypass.\n\nCardiopulmonary bypass circuits activate factor XII which can lead to clotting in the oxygenator. This process is called catheter-induced thrombosis.\n\nUnfractionated heparin is better than LMWH or fondaparinux for blocking it. Also, reversal of anticoagulation using protamine sulfate is easier for UFH compared to the other heparins.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Lab reports of a 45-year-old female patient, who was recently started on heparin, showed rapidly falling platelet counts. She was suspected to have heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. What is the most common presentation seen in this condition?","o":["Bleeding","Venous thrombosis","Skin necrosis","Arterial thrombosis"],"a":1,"e":"The most common presentation of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is venous thrombosis.\n\nHIT is an immune complication of heparin therapy caused by IgG antibodies against the heparin-platelet factor 4 complex. This results in the activation of platelets to propagate a hypercoagulable state culminating in life-threatening thrombosis.\n\nIt is characterized by thrombocytopenia (Platelet count <1,00,000/μL or reduced by over 50% from pre-treatment value) and clinically presents as venous thrombosis (in the form of DVT or pulmonary embolism)\n\nIt occurs around 5-10 days after starting heparin. It is more common with UFH when compared to LMWH. It is also more common in women surgical patients. Arterial thrombosis (resulting in MI or stroke), as well as bilateral adrenal hemorrhage, can rarely occur.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f8147bb533c46348feec27df0631f5ex1280x1355.JPEG"],"t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 64-year-old man was started on heparin postoperatively for thromboprophylaxis. After a week he developed sudden difficulty in breathing. His platelet count was found to be 70,000/microliter. Which of the following is incorrect regarding the management of this patient?","o":["Heparin should be discontinued immediately","Alternative anticoagulant such as argatroban should be administered","Low molecular weight heparins should be avoided","Heparin should be replaced with warfarin"],"a":3,"e":"The patient in the above scenario has heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). Direct thrombin inhibitors, such as argatroban is the anticoagulant of choice in HIT.\n\nManagement of HIT:\n\n• Stop heparin/LMWH\n\n• Give an alternative anticoagulant, such as lepirudin, argatroban, bivalirudin, fondaparinux or rivaroxaban\n\n• Do not give platelet transfusions (it only worsens the consumptive coagulopathy)\n\n• Do not give warfarin until the platelet count returns to its baseline level. If warfarin was administered, give vitamin K to restore the INR to normal.\n\nIncreased thrombin generation associated with HIT can cause consumption of protein C. Warfarin administration can further decrease protein C levels and cause skin necrosis. Therefore warfarin is avoided in HIT.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on anticoagulant therapy presented to the casualty with an uncontrolled bleeding episode and was given IV protamine sulfate. However, the patient did not respond to the treatment. Which of the following anticoagulant agents was most likely being used by the patient?","o":["Unfractionated heparin","Enoxaparin","Dalteparin","Fondaparinux"],"a":3,"e":"Since no effect was noted with protamine sulfate, the likely anticoagulant in use must be fondaparinux. This is because protamine sulfate fails to bind the drug.\n\nProtamine sulfate binds tightly to heparin and neutralizes its anticoagulant effect. It partially reverses the anticoagulant activity of LMWH also. But it has no effect on fondaparinux.\n\nThere is no known specific antidote approved for use in fondaparinux overdose. Fondaparinux-associated bleeding complications are managed by treatment discontinuation and search for the primary cause. Surgical hemostasis, blood replacements, fresh plasma transfusion, plasmapheresis should be considered.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A clinical trial is done to compare the safety profile of a certain direct thrombin inhibitor (drug X) with that of heparin in patients undergoing coronary angioplasty. Identify drug X.","o":["Enoxaparin","Bivalirudin","Fondaparinux","Dalteparin"],"a":1,"e":"Among the given options, the direct thrombin inhibitor is bivalirudin.\n\nBivalirudin is a parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor (DTI) frequently used for anticoagulation in invasive cardiology, particularly percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Unlike other DTIs, it undergoes predominant non-organ elimination (proteolysis) and has the shortest half-life (approximately 25 min).\n\nIt can be used in patients with, or at risk for, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.\n\nOther options:\n\nOptions A and D: Enoxaparin and dalteparin are low-molecular-weight-heparin (LMWH) preparations.\n\nOption C: Fondaparinux is a synthetic analog of the pentasaccharide unit of heparin.\n\nHeparins and their analogs produce anticoagulant effects indirectly by activating antithrombin-III.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"The oral thrombin inhibitor which can be used for prevention of stroke in patients with non-valvular atrial fibrillation is?","o":["Dabigatran","Ximelgatran","Lepirudin","Saxagliptin"],"a":0,"e":"The oral thrombin inhibitor which can be used for the prevention of stroke in patients with non-valvular atrial fibrillation is dabigatran. However, it is contraindicated in patients with mechanical heart valves.\n\nOption B: Ximelagatran is also an oral thrombin inhibitor, but it has been discontinued due to the risk of hepatotoxicity.\n\nOption C: Lepirudin is a parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor used mainly in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.\n\nOption D: Saxagliptin is a DPP-4 inhibitor used in the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor?","o":["Apixaban","Argatroban","Dabigatran","Enoxaparin"],"a":1,"e":"The parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor is argatroban.\n\nArgatroban binds reversibly to thrombin and inhibits it. It is metabolized in the liver and excreted in bile, so it can be safely used in patients with renal impairment.\n\nIt can be used for prophylaxis and treatment of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT).\n\nOther parenteral direct thrombin inhibitors are their indications:\n\n• Lepirudin - was indicated in HIT but is discontinued now\n\n• Desirudin - indicated for thromboprophylaxis in hip replacement surgery\n\n• Bivalirudin - used in procedures like coronary angioplasty or cardiopulmonary bypass surgery and HIT.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient on apixaban for nonvalvular atrial fibrillation requires urgent aortic surgery for a pseudoaneurysm. Identify the decoy factor Xa used for reversal of apixaban action before the surgery.","o":["Dornase alfa","Andexanet alfa","Idarucizumab","Protamine sulfate"],"a":1,"e":"Andexanet alfa is the decoy factor Xa approved for reversal of oral Xa inhibitors. It was approved in May 2018.\n\nOption A: Dornase alfa is a recombinant human DNase 1 that is used in cystic fibrosis to reduce the viscosity of mucosal secretions.\n\nOption C: Idarucizumab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody against dabigatran. It has a higher affinity towards dabigatran than thrombin and is approved as a reversal agent for dabigatran.\n\nOption D: Protamine sulfate is an antidote for heparin overdose.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of regular oral anticoagulant therapy suffers a traumatic injury. He receives idarucizumab before damage control surgery to reverse the anticoagulant effect. Identify the oral anticoagulant that was being used by the patient.","o":["Heparin","Warfarin","Dabigatran","Rivaroxaban"],"a":2,"e":"Idarucizumab is a reversal agent for dabigatran. It is a humanized mouse monoclonal antibody fragment.\n\nIt binds irreversibly to dabigatran and forms a complex that is cleared by the kidneys.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is under clinical trials and shows a reversal in the action of all direct anticoagulants?","o":["Protamine","Idarucizumab","Andexanet alfa","Ciraparantag"],"a":3,"e":"Ciraparantag is a synthetic cationic molecule that binds to all direct anticoagulants (dabigatran and other oral factor Xa inhibitors) as well as heparin and LMWH and reverses their actions. It is still under trial.\n\nOption A: Protamine sulfate reverses the action of heparin and LMWH.\n\nOption B: Idarucizumab is a specific antidote for dabigatran overdose.\n\nOption C: Andexanet alfa is a decoy for oral factor Xa inhibitors.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following anticoagulants can be safely used in a pregnant patient?","o":["Heparin","Warfarin","Dicumarol","Phenindione"],"a":0,"e":"Heparin is the anticoagulant that can be safely used in pregnancy.\n\nHeparin, LMWH, and fondaparinux do not cross the blood-brain barrier or placenta and hence they are the anticoagulants preferred in pregnancy.\n\nWarfarin and other vitamin K antagonists are contraindicated during pregnancy due to the risk of teratogenicity.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient is on DVT prophylaxis with warfarin. It has an inhibitory action on which of the following enzymes?","o":["Gamma glutamyl carboxylase","Vitamin K epoxide reductase","Thrombin","HMG-CoA reductase"],"a":1,"e":"Warfarin directly inhibits the enzyme Vitamin K epoxide reductase.\n\nIt inhibits the conversion of vitamin K epoxide to its reduced form - vitamin K hydroquinone which is a co-factor for the enzyme gamma-glutamyl carboxylase. This enzyme catalyzes the γ-carboxylation process of vitamin K dependent clotting factors.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17a7c94a393c40bc84db00bbcafd63f4x1280x1234.JPEG"],"t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is vitamin K dependent clotting factor?","o":["Factor I","Factor VII","Factor XI","Factor XII"],"a":1,"e":"Factor VII is a vitamin K dependant clotting factor.\n\nThe vitamin K dependent factors:\n\n• Clotting factors: Factors II, VII, IX, X\n\n• Anticoagulant factors: Protein C and Protein S","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient suffering from non-valvular atrial fibrillation is prescribed warfarin tablets. Which of the following clotting factors would you expect to decline the earliest in this patient after drug administration?","o":["Factor II","Factor VII","Factor IX","Factor X"],"a":1,"e":"Factor VII activity declines most rapidly when warfarin is administered. It has the shortest plasma half-life among the vitamin K dependent clotting factors.\n\nClotting factors\n\nPlasma half-life (hours)\n\nII\n\n50\n\nVII\n\n6\n\nIX\n\n24\n\nX\n\n30\n\nClinical importance: Because of the long half-life of the other clotting factors, especially factor II, the full anticoagulant effect of warfarin is not seen for several days. Initially, parenteral anticoagulants are used along with warfarin until the full effect of warfarin is achieved.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"In a patient receiving warfarin tablets, which enzyme primarily metabolizes the drug?","o":["CYP2C19","CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP1A2"],"a":1,"e":"Warfarin is primarily metabolized by CYP2C9.\n\nGenetic polymorphisms of CYP2C9 and drugs inhibiting or inducing CYP2C9 can result in variable response to warfarin administration.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"While prescribing warfarin, a decreased effect of the drug is expected in patients with which of the following conditions?","o":["Nephrotic syndrome","Hepatic impairment","Gastroenteritis on treatment with metronidazole","Congestive heart failure"],"a":0,"e":"Decreased effect of warfarin is seen in patients with nephrotic syndrome.\n\nWarfarin is highly bound to plasma protein and in nephrotic syndrome there is proteinuria. Drugs that are bound to plasma proteins are lost in the urine. Similarly, warfarin is lost in urine in nephrotic syndrome, resulting in a decreased effect.\n\nOption C: Warfarin is mainly metabolized by the CYP2C9 enzyme. In patients being treated with metronidazole (a CYP2C9 inhibitor), the metabolism of warfarin is decreased. Hence, the effect of warfarin is increased.\n\nOptions B and D: Hepatic impairment, congestive heart failure, and hyperthyroidism lead to low concentrations of clotting factors and thus the effect of warfarin is enhanced in these conditions.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with splanchnic vein thrombosis has been started on warfarin therapy. Which of the following can be used to monitor the treatment in this patient?","o":["Prothrombin time","Activated partial thromboplastin time","Bleeding time","Clotting time"],"a":0,"e":"Warfarin therapy is usually monitored by measuring prothrombin time.\n\nProthrombin time is sensitive to the reduction in levels of prothrombin, factor VII, and factor X. The test is performed by adding thromboplastin, which is a reagent containing tissue factor, phospholipid and calcium to citrated plasma and determining the time to clot formation.\n\nDue to the variable sensitivity of thromboplastin, the International normalized ratio (INR) was developed to standardize the reporting of prothrombin time. In INR, the patient’s prothrombin time is divided by mean normal prothrombin time and this ratio is multiplied by the International Sensitivity Index (ISI) of the particular thromboplastin reagent.\n\nWarfarin therapy should be monitored with INR every 3–4 weeks. The target INR is 2.0–3.0. In patients with mechanical heart valves, the target INR is 2.5–3.5.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old patient admitted with atrial fibrillation was started on bisoprolol and warfarin. After 4 days of admission, she started experiencing painful ecchymoses on her right lower limb. It progressed to the condition shown in the image over the next few days. Deficiency in which of the following can lead to this condition?","o":["Protein C","Protein S","Either a or b","Thrombin"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features and the image given are suggestive of warfarin-induced skin necrosis seen due to a congenital or acquired deficiency in either protein C or protein S.\n\nWarfarin therapy in such patients leads to a rapid fall in protein C or protein S, which are vitamin K dependent anticoagulants. This occurs before warfarin can exert an antithrombotic effect.\n\nTreatment: Warfarin should be discontinued and vitamin K (the antidote for warfarin) should be administered.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09f2a88d8c1f4e55ade74cc116e7b99d.PNG"],"t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old male patient on anticoagulant therapy for his DVT started coughing up blood. He has a history of irregular warfarin intake. Workup revealed elevated INR and diffuse alveolar hemorrhage. Which of the following agents would be the best choice for immediate reversal of bleeding?","o":["Cryoprecipitate","Fresh frozen plasma","Prothrombin complex concentrates","Vitamin K1"],"a":2,"e":"Immediate or urgent reversal of warfarin-induced bleeding is best achieved by the administration of prothrombin complex concentrates (PCC).\n\nThese are highly purified concentrates prepared from pooled plasma and contain factors II, IX, and X. Factor VII may be included or produced separately.\n\nOption D: Vitamin K1 is the specific antidote for warfarin-induced bleeding, but it takes 6-24 hours for the clotting factors to be resynthesized and released in blood after Vitamin K administration and hence its action is delayed for several hours.\n\nOption A: Cryoprecipitate mainly contains factor VIII and fibrinogen and therefore is not suitable for warfarin-induced bleeding, as warfarin inhibits factors II, VII, IX, X.\n\nOption B: Fresh frozen plasma can also be used but prothrombin complex concentrate normalizes the INR more rapidly.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not a fetal abnormality expected after warfarin administration to a pregnant patient?","o":["Nasal hypoplasia","Fetal haemorrhage","Stippled epiphyses","Macrocephaly"],"a":3,"e":"Macrocephaly is not a fetal abnormality caused by warfarin administered during pregnancy.\n\nWarfarin crosses the placenta and can cause fetal abnormalities which include nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses leading to abnormal bone formation. Fetal or neonatal hemorrhage is also seen.\n\nThe risk of embryopathy is highest with exposure in the first trimester of pregnancy. CNS abnormalities have also been reported following exposure during the second and third trimesters.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"In patients with indications for warfarin usage, what is the recommended range of INR necessary for safe administration of the drug?","o":["1.0 – 1.5","2.0 – 3.0","2.5 – 3.5","3.5 – 4.5"],"a":1,"e":"The INR ratio recommended for the administration of warfarin in most of the indications is in the range of 2.0-3.0.\n\nOption A: The range of 1.0-1.5 is too low for beneficial effects.\n\nOption C: In selected patients with mechanical heart valves, the INR target is around 2.5-3.5.\n\nOption D: INR >3.5 can result in increased adverse effects and is not recommended for any condition.","t":"Anticoagulants","c":"Blood Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a long-acting β-2 agonist that can be prescribed in a patient with moderately severe bronchial asthma?","o":["Salbutamol","Indacaterol","Terbutaline","Formoterol"],"a":3,"e":"Formoterol is a long-acting β-2 agonist (LABA).\n\nBeta 2 agonists are classified based on their duration of action into:\n\n• Short-acting β-2 agonists (SABA):\n\n• Salbutamol\n\n• Terbutaline\n\n• Pirbuterol\n\n• Long-acting β-2 agonists (LABA):\n\n• Salmeterol\n\n• Formoterol\n\n• Very long-acting β-2 agonists (V-LABA):\n\n• Indacaterol\n\n• Olodaterol\n\n• Vilanterol","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the current GINA recommendation for the prescription of long-acting β-2 agonists in patients suffering from bronchial asthma?","o":["As monotherapy","In combination with an inhaled anticholinergic","In combination with an inhaled corticosteroid","In combination with a short acting β-2 agonist"],"a":2,"e":"LABA is used in combination with inhalational corticosteroids (ICS), preferably in the form of a combination inhaler.\n\nLABA is never taken alone as it does not treat the underlying chronic inflammation of asthma. Corticosteroids will decrease the underlying inflammation and halt the progression of the disease.\n\nShort-acting β-2 agonists (SABA) can be given as an add-on therapy to the LABA-ICS combination (as-needed therapy).","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is the most appropriate therapy for bronchodilation in acute severe asthma?","o":["Intravenous aminophylline","Inhalational anticholinergics","Nebulized SABA","Intravenous SABA"],"a":2,"e":"Nebulized short-acting beta-2 agonists (SABAs) are the most appropriate therapy in acute severe asthma among the given options.\n\nNebulized route is preferred over the intravenous route as it is easier and safer with equivalent efficacy.\n\nIntravenous aminophylline is less effective than SABA and is reserved for those who are resistant or intolerant to beta-2 agonists with acute severe asthma.\n\nInhalational anticholinergics are not indicated in acute severe asthma as they are less effective than SABAs.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"An asthmatic patient complains of wheezing and shortness of breath most days of the week. Which of the following therapies is preferred in him?","o":["Inhaled SABA","Inhaled LABA","Inhaled LABA + inhaled corticosteroids","Inhaled SABA + inhaled corticosteroids"],"a":2,"e":"This patient has persistent asthma for which the preferred therapy is LABA with ICS available as a combination inhaler.\n\nICS should be started in any patient who needs to use a short-acting beta-2 agonist inhaler for symptomatic control more than twice a week (persistent asthma).","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 5-year-old child requires bronchodilator therapy. You attach the device shown below to the pressurized metered-dose inhaler for ease of use. Which of the following is false about this device?","o":["Decreases the deposition of drug on the oropharynx","Increases the delivery of drug to lower airways","Increases the incidence of oropharyngeal candidiasis","Systemic absorption of drugs is unaffected"],"a":2,"e":"The given image is of a spacer device. It decreases the incidence of oropharyngeal candidiasis and other propellant-mediated oropharyngeal adverse effects like dysphonia that occur with the use of a pMDI.\n\nSpacers reduce the velocity and size of particles entering the upper airways. This reduces the amount of drug that impinges on the oropharynx and increases the proportion of drug inhaled into lower airways. The systemic absorption is unchanged by the use of a spacer.\n\nSpacer devices are useful in delivering inhaled drugs to children who are not able to use a pMDI alone but can also be used by adults for better drug delivery. The image below shows the use of a spacer device with a face mask which is preferred in children younger than 3 years of age.\n\nThe various devices used for the delivery of inhalational drugs are:\n\n• Pressurized metered-dose inhalers - Drugs are propelled from a canister in the pMDI with the aid of a propellant. These devices are convenient, portable, and typically deliver 50–200 doses of a drug\n\n• Dry powder inhalers - these devices scatter the drug into a fine powder that is dispersed by air turbulence on inhalation. DPIs have been developed to deliver drugs such as insulin, but the dosing is inconsistent. Children <7 years find it difficult to use a DPI.\n\n• Nebulizers: Two types of nebulizer are available -\n\n• Jet nebulizers that are driven by a stream of gas (air or oxygen)\n\n• Ultrasonic nebulizers that use a rapidly vibrating piezoelectric crystal and thus do not require a source of compressed gas.\n\nThe nebulizers deliver much higher drug doses as compared pMDIs. These are useful in:\n\n• Treating acute exacerbations of asthma and COPD\n\n• Extreme airway obstruction (severe COPD)\n\n• Delivering inhaled drugs to infants and small children who cannot use the other inhalation devices\n\n• Administering drugs such as antibiotics in relatively high doses","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c79df03efd6d4a87baad5dad87dd05de.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e51d99cce336454585713400e71a010cx720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf3ec9228e96415da2ec3c3932577099x596x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fe2da00a2b7445d8a99ec027efeab5ax799x533.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2687fee5df024653908a0728befd85afx1024x683.JPEG"],"t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"An asthmatic patient has been taking theophylline tablets for symptomatic relief. On which of the following receptors does this drug have an antagonistic action?","o":["Histamine receptors","Bradykinin receptors","Adenosine receptors","Kallikrein receptors"],"a":2,"e":"Theophylline has an antagonistic action on adenosine receptors.\n\nTheophylline is a methylxanthine. It acts as a non-selective PDE inhibitor. Its other actions include:\n\n• Increased IL-10 release\n\n• Decrease in gene transcription of pro-inflammatory cytokines\n\n• Promotes apoptosis of granulocytes\n\n• Histone deacetylase activation. The recruitment of histone deacetylase-2 by glucocorticoid receptors switches off inflammatory genes. Through this mechanism, theophylline enhances the anti-inflammatory effects of corticosteroids.\n\nTheophylline can be used as a bronchodilator in an asthmatic patient on ICS as an add-on drug. LABAs are more effective but theophylline is a cheaper option.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about theophylline is not correct?","o":["Increase in dose is required in cardiopulmonary disease","Decreases the breakdown of cAMP in smooth muscles","Increase in dose is required in smokers","Is a non-selective phosphodiesterase inhibitor"],"a":0,"e":"A decrease in dose of theophylline is required in cardiopulmonary disease. This is because reduced clearance of theophylline is seen in liver disease, pneumonia, and heart failure.\n\nIn smokers, clearance of theophylline is increased due to the induction of CYP1A2 enzyme.\n\nTheophylline is a non-selective phosphodiesterase inhibitor that decreases the breakdown of cAMP levels in smooth muscles resulting in bronchodilation.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an indication for theophylline?","o":["Supraventricular tachycardia","Epilepsy","Acute severe asthma","Apnea of prematurity"],"a":3,"e":"Theophylline is indicated in the treatment of apnea of prematurity.\n\nIn apnea of prematurity, methylxanthines such as theophylline and caffeine increase the central respiratory drive by lowering the threshold of response to hypercapnia. They also enhance the contractility of the diaphragm and prevent diaphragmatic fatigue.\n\nOptions A & B - The adenosine receptor antagonism property of theophylline precipitates cardiac arrhythmias and epileptic seizures.\n\nOption C - Theophylline is no longer recommended as a treatment for acute severe asthma by the latest guidelines (GINA 2021) because of its poor efficacy and safety profile.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with severe persistent asthma continues to have symptoms despite being on high-dose ICS-LABA therapy. Which of the following is a leukotriene receptor antagonist, appropriate as an add-on therapy for this patient?","o":["Dupilumab","Roflumilast","Zafirlukast","Zileuton"],"a":2,"e":"Zafirlukast and montelukast are leukotriene receptor antagonists. They are competitive antagonists of the cysLT1 receptors. These drugs act by:\n\n• Decreasing bronchoconstriction\n\n• Reducing airway mucus secretion\n\n• Decreasing vascular permeability and\n\n• Reducing eosinophil chemotaxis\n\nOption A: Dupilumab is an interleukin-4 receptor antagonist used in severe asthma.\n\nOption B: Roflumilast is a PDE-4 selective inhibitor used in the treatment of COPD.\n\nOption D: Zileuton is a lipoxygenase inhibitor that can be used in the treatment of asthma.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"The use of which of the following therapies for acute asthma requires serial monitoring of liver function tests?\n\n1. Zileuton\n\n2. Salbutamol\n\n3. Salmeterol\n\n4. Zafirlukast\n\n5. Montelukast","o":["3 and 4","2 and 3","1, 2 and 5","1, 4 and 5"],"a":3,"e":"Zileuton, montelukast, and zafirlukast are all associated with rare instances of hepatic dysfunction. Liver function tests should be monitored during the course of their therapy.\n\nTherapy with selective β-2 agonists (salbutamol, salmeterol) does not require serial monitoring of LFTs.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly asthmatic on treatment presented with fever, malaise, and lower limb sensory deficit. Significant investigations are given below. Which of the following drugs is associated with these findings?\n\n• CBC: eosinophilia\n\n• HRCT chest: bilateral ground-glass attenuation\n\n• Bronchoalveolar lavage: 14.6% eosinophils\n\n• Nasal mucosa biopsy: granulomas\n\n• Serum p-ANCA: positive","o":["Montelukast","Cromolyn sodium","Theophylline","Ketotifen"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario points towards a diagnosis of Churg-Strauss syndrome. It is an eosinophilic vasculitis associated with the use of montelukast and zafirlukast. This is a very rarely reported side effect of these drugs.\n\nThese agents block the effects of the cysLTs (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) but they do not block the effect of LTB4, which is a chemoattractant for eosinophils and neutrophils. This can lead to eosinophilia.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"An asthmatic patient was brought to the ER in an agitated state. His respiratory rate was 32/min and pulse was 124/min. Pulse oximetry revealed a SpO2 of 88% on room air. Which of the following drugs is not useful in this scenario?","o":["Salbutamol","Hydrocortisone","Cromolyn sodium","Ipratropium bromide"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute severe asthma and cromolyn sodium is not useful in its treatment. It is indicated only for chronic prophylaxis.\n\nShort-acting beta 2 agonists (SABA) and intravenous hydrocortisone are used in the treatment of acute severe asthma.\n\nIpratropium bromide can also be given in severe asthma exacerbations. It should be given after beta 2-agonists as they have a slower onset of bronchodilatory action.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Omalizumab is added as a step-up therapy in a child with severe asthma, refractory to treatment. Which of the following is true about this drug?","o":["Is administered intravenously","Has anti interleukin-5 action","Is indicated in acute severe asthma","Is a monoclonal antibody against IgE"],"a":3,"e":"Omalizumab is a monoclonal antibody against IgE.\n\nIt is used only in patients with very severe asthma (not acute severe asthma) who show poor symptom control even with step 4/5 measures as per GINA 2021 guidelines.\n\nIt can only be given to patients aged ≥6 years. It neutralizes circulating IgE and inhibits mast cell degranulation caused by IgE. It is administered subcutaneously.\n\nAnother drug used in severe asthma is mepolizumab. It is a monoclonal antibody acting against IL-5.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with Churg-Strauss syndrome is started on Mepolizumab. This drug acts against:","o":["IgE","IgA","IL-5","IL-12"],"a":2,"e":"Mepolizumab is a monoclonal antibody against IL-5. It is indicated in:\n\n• Selected (eosinophilic phenotype) patients with severe asthma\n\n• Churg-Strauss syndrome\n\n• Hypereosinophilic syndrome\n\nIL-5 plays a pivotal role in eosinophilic inflammation and is also involved in eosinophil survival and priming.\n\nMonoclonal antibodies against IL-5 approved for asthma are:\n\n• Mepolizumab\n\n• Reslizumab\n\n• Benralizumab","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the thromboxane receptor antagonist used in the treatment of asthma?","o":["Seratrodast","Rimanobant","Sacubitril","Roflumilast"],"a":0,"e":"Seratrodast is a thromboxane receptor antagonist indicated for use in long term prophylaxis of asthma. It is available and approved in India.\n\nOption B- Rimonabant is a cannabinoid receptor antagonist that was investigated for weight gain but now withdrawn due to adverse psychological effects\n\nOption C- Sacubitril is a neprilysin inhibitor approved in fixed-dose combination with valsartan for heart failure patients.\n\nOption D- Roflumilast is a phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor which reduces eosinophil infiltration and inhibits inflammatory cell ,effective against smoke induced inflammation and emphysema.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient has had 2 acute exacerbations of asthma over the span of 1-month despite being on therapy. Which of the following is a systemic steroid that can be used to treat her?","o":["Ciclesonide","Beclomethasone","Prednisolone","Budesonide"],"a":2,"e":"Prednisolone is a systemic steroid used for maintenance therapy after an acute exacerbation of asthma.\n\nIntravenous corticosteroids such as hydrocortisone are indicated if the lung function is predicted to be <30% or if there's poor response to nebulized β2 agonists. After the exacerbation, oral predisolone can be substituted with maximal beneficial effect at 30-40mg daily.\n\nInhaled corticosteroids commonly used in asthma are:\n\n• Beclomethasone dipropionate\n\n• Budesonide\n\n• Ciclesonide\n\n• Fluticasone\n\n• Mometasone","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"An asthmatic patient presented to the ER with dyspnea and wheezing. Intake of which of the following is unlikely to have precipitated this?","o":["Phenylbutazone","Naproxen","Caffeine","Aspirin"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an acute exacerbation of asthma. Caffeine does not precipitate this.\n\nCaffeine is a dietary xanthine similar in structure to methylxanthines (theophylline) which are used as adjuvants in the treatment of asthma.\n\nNSAIDs (like phenylbutazone, naproxen, and aspirin) inhibit the cyclooxygenase pathway thereby diverting arachidonic acid towards the lipoxygenase pathway. This increases the level of pro-inflammatory leukotrienes and can precipitate an acute asthma attack.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with mild episodic asthma is prescribed daily low-dose inhaled budesonide therapy. Which of the following conditions is he not at increased risk of developing?","o":["Dysphonia","Pulmonary TB","Oropharyngeal candidiasis","Carcinoma of lung"],"a":3,"e":"Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) like budesonide do not increase the risk of developing carcinoma of the lung.\n\nCommon problems with the use of ICS:\n\n• Hoarseness of voice\n\n• Weakness of voice (dysphonia)\n\n• Increased risk of bacterial and mycobacterial lung infection\n\n• Oropharyngeal candidiasis","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"An elderly man with a 30-year history of smoking complains of breathing difficulty. A diagnosis of COPD is made, and treatment with a long-acting muscarinic antagonist is initiated. Which of the following will you not prescribe?","o":["Glycopyrronium","Umeclidinium","Ipratropium","Tiotropium"],"a":2,"e":"Ipratropium is a short-acting muscarinic antagonist which is not used for the long-term management of COPD. It can be used to treat acute exacerbations.\n\nThe long-acting muscarinic antagonists (LAMAs) are:\n\n• Tiotropium\n\n• Glycopyrronium\n\n• Umeclidinium\n\n• Aclidinium","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Inhalational anticholinergics the mainstay of treatment in:","o":["Asthma","COPD","Both a and b","Status asthmaticus"],"a":1,"e":"Inhalational anticholinergics are the mainstay for the treatment of COPD.\n\nIpratropium is as effective as albuterol in patients with COPD. Longer-acting antimuscarinic agents, including tiotropium, aclidinium, and umeclidinium, are approved for maintenance therapy of COPD.\n\nAnticholinergics are less effective than β-2 agonists as bronchodilators in asthma and are not the mainstay in the treatment of asthma.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) patient requires a step-up in therapy due to symptom persistence. Which PDE-4 inhibitor can you prescribe as an add-on drug to his treatment regimen?","o":["Seratrodast","Roflumilast","Zileuton","Nedocromil"],"a":1,"e":"Roflumilast is a PDE-4 selective inhibitor approved for use in COPD.\n\nPDE-4 is the major isoform present in inflammatory cells and its inhibition results in higher anti-inflammatory effects.\n\nOption A: Seratrodast is a thromboxane receptor antagonist used in the prophylaxis of asthma. It decreases the bronchial hypersensitivity caused by thromboxane.\n\nOption C: Zileuton is a lipoxygenase inhibitor that can be used in the treatment of asthma.\n\nOption D: Nedocromil is a mast cell stabilizer indicated in the prophylaxis of asthma.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs are antitussives?\n\n1. Dextromethorphan\n\n2. Morphine\n\n3. Guaifenesin\n\n4. Carbocysteine\n\n5. Gabapentin","o":["1, 2 and 4","1, 2 and 5","3 and 5","3 and 4"],"a":1,"e":"Dextromethorphan, morphine, and gabapentin are all antitussives.\n\nDextromethorphan is an NMDA receptor antagonist that suppresses the cough center.\n\nOpiates like morphine have a central mechanism of action on the medullary cough center and are indicated for intractable cough associated with bronchial carcinoma.\n\nGabapentin and pregabalin are GABA analogs that have been shown to benefit chronic idiopathic cough involving neural hypersensitivity.\n\nGuaifenesin is an expectorant that is taken orally.\n\nCarbocysteine is a mucolytic that reduces the disulfide bridges that bind glycoproteins to other proteins, such as albumin and secretory IgA. It is also taken orally and helps reduce the viscosity of sputum.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A known case of CREST syndrome presents with dyspnea. On auscultation, a Graham-Steele murmur is heard. On further investigation, her pulmonary artery pressure is found to be 28 mm Hg. Which drug will you use to manage her condition?","o":["Non selective PDE inhibitors","Lipoxygenase inhibitors","Selective PDE-5 inhibitors","Cyclo-oxygenase inhibitors"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of pulmonary artery hypertension (pulmonary artery pressure > 20mmHg) which can be managed using selective PDE-5 inhibitors.\n\nInhibition of PDE-5 in the pulmonary bed leads to a reduction in the hydrolysis of cGMP which causes pulmonary vasodilation.\n\nSildenafil and tadalafil are the PDE-5 inhibitors approved for use in PAH.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following groups does riociguat belong to?","o":["Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator","Soluble guanylate cyclase inhibitor","Soluble adenylate cyclase stimulator","Soluble adenylate cyclase inhibitor"],"a":0,"e":"Riociguat is a soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator (sGC), indicated in the treatment of pulmonary artery hypertension.\n\nRiociguat increases the sensitivity of nitric oxide to sGC and also directly stimulates the sGC enzyme resulting in increased formation of cGMP. This leads to relaxation in the pulmonary vasculature.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is a prostacyclin analog that can be added to the treatment regimen of a patient with pulmonary artery hypertension?","o":["Sildenafil","Misoprostol","Dinoprost","Iloprost"],"a":3,"e":"Iloprost is a prostacyclin analog indicated in the management of pulmonary artery hypertension. It falls under the category of IP receptor (prostacyclin receptor) agonists.\n\nOther drugs falling under the category of IP receptor agonists are:\n\n•\n\nEpoprostenol\n\n•\n\nTreprostinil\n\n•\n\nIloprost\n\n•\n\nSelexipag\n\nOption A: Sildenafil is a PDE-5 inhibitor.\n\nOption B: Misoprostol is a synthetic PGE1 analog.\n\nOption C: Dinoprost is a natural PGF2-alpha preparation. It should not be confused with dinoprostone which is a synthetic analogue of PGE2.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which is a selective endothelin receptor A antagonist used in the management of pulmonary artery hypertension?","o":["Bosentan","Ambrisentan","Tezosentan","Macitentan"],"a":1,"e":"Ambrisentan is a selective endothelin receptor A (ET-A) antagonist which is used in the management of pulmonary artery hypertension (PAH).\n\nBosentan, tezosentan, and macitantan are non-selective (ET-A and ET-B) endothelin receptor antagonists.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Dornase alfa is indicated in which of the following patients?\n\n1. A 30-year-old man with bronchial asthma\n\n2. A 72-year-old man with COPD\n\n3. A 19-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis\n\n4. A 25-year-old woman with pulmonary artery hypertension\n\n5. A newborn with refractory atelectasis","o":["Only 3","2, 4 and 5","3 and 5","1, 2 and 3"],"a":2,"e":"Dornase alfa is indicated in patients with cystic fibrosis and as a rescue treatment for refractory atelectasis in newborn and pediatric populations.\n\nDornase alfa is a recombinant DNase 1 that digests neutrophil DNA deposited in the pulmonary mucus thereby reducing mucosal viscoelasticity.","t":"Respiratory System","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"The final step in gastric acid production is inhibited by:","o":["H2 blockers","Proton pump inhibitors","Octreotide","Antimuscarinics"],"a":1,"e":"The final step in gastric acid production is inhibited by the proton pump inhibitors (PPIs).\n\nThe secretion of protons by the activated H+/K+- ATPase (proton pump) in the parietal cells into the gastric lumen is the final step involved in the production of gastric acid.\n\nPPIs cause maximum suppression of gastric acid and also reduces basal acid secretion. Suppression of gastric acid occurs regardless of the stimulus present.","ei":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/79f8d7a356254942ac77ef98dbd915b6x912x630.PNG"],"t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Despite their short half-lives, how do proton-pump inhibitors cause prolonged suppression of acid secretion for up to 48 hours?","o":["Prodrugs and undergo activation gradually","Exit from plasma and enter acid secretory canaliculi, blocking the secretion of acid for a long time","Irreversibly inhibit proton pump molecule, acid secretion requires synthesis of new proton pumps","Available as enteric coated capsules from which drug is gradually released"],"a":2,"e":"PPIs irreversibly inhibit the proton pump molecule and hence, acid secretion requires the synthesis of new proton pumps.\n\nPPIs are prodrugs, when ingested, get absorbed into the systemic circulation. It then diffuses into the parietal cells of the stomach and accumulates in the acidic secretory canaliculi. Acidic pH in the parietal cell canaliculi causes activation of the PPIs. These activated PPIs irreversibly inhibit H+/K+ ATPase (proton pump) in the parietal cell.\n\nThe maximum proton pump activation corresponds to the peak concentration of PPI resulting in maximum efficacy.\n\nThe secretion resumes gradually over 3-4 days of stopping the drug when new proton pumps are synthesized.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with gastroesophageal reflux disease was prescribed an oral PPI preparation that causes the fastest acid suppression. Identify the drug.","o":["Dexlansoprazole","Esomeprazole","Pantoprazole","Rabeprazole"],"a":3,"e":"Rabeprazole is the oral PPI preparation that causes the fastest acid suppression. It has a higher pKa that causes more rapid conversion to active species in acidic canaliculi.\n\nThe efficacy and potency are similar to that of omeprazole.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A neighbor approaches you for advice on the best time to take pantoprazole for heartburn. What would you recommend?","o":["After meals","Shortly before meals","Along with milk","During meals"],"a":1,"e":"Proton-pump inhibitors (pantoprazole) should be ideally given shortly (20-30 minutes) before food.\n\nPPIs are usually given before breakfast. Food hinders the absorption of PPIs and decreases their bioavailability.\n\nPPIs are prodrugs, when ingested, get absorbed into the systemic circulation. It then diffuses into the parietal cells of the stomach and accumulates in the acidic secretory canaliculi. Acidic pH in the parietal cell canaliculi causes activation of the PPIs. The maximum proton pump activation corresponds to the peak concentration of PPI resulting in maximum efficacy.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing evaluation for multiple gastroduodenal ulcers was diagnosed with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. What would be the drug of choice for this patient?","o":["Proton pump inhibitors","H2 blockers","Sucralfate","Prostaglandin analogs"],"a":0,"e":"Proton-pump inhibitors are the drug of choice in Zollinger–Ellison syndrome.\n\nZollinger Ellison syndrome includes pancreatic or duodenal gastrinoma which leads to the secretion of large amounts of gastrin. This, in turn, increases gastric acid leading to severe gastroduodenal ulceration and other effects of hyperchlorhydria.\n\nNote: Netazepide is a gastrin/cholecystokinin-2 receptor antagonist used in the treatment of gastrinoma. It is also an antisecretory agent.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman with osteoarthritis developed gastric ulcers following long-term use of NSAIDs. Of the following, which drug is FDA-approved for her condition?","o":["PGE-1 analogue","PGD-2 antagonist","PGD-2 agonist","PGF-2 analogue"],"a":0,"e":"PGE1 analog (misoprostol) is approved by the FDA for NSAID-induced gastric ulcers. It reduces gastric acid secretion in a dose-dependent manner.\n\nProstaglandins inhibit gastric acid secretion. Misoprostol also increases mucosal blood flow and stimulates bicarbonate and mucin secretion which contribute to its cytoprotective action. However, the reduction in acid secretion is less than that of H2 blockers.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the most effective therapy for NSAID-induced ulcers?","o":["Antacids","H2 blockers","Misoprostol","Proton pump inhibitors"],"a":3,"e":"The most effective therapy for NSAID-induced ulcers is proton pump inhibitors.\n\nThey are proven to be more effective than misoprostol and H2 receptor antagonists.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which proton pump inhibitor should be avoided in a patient on warfarin anticoagulation?","o":["Rabeprazole","Pantoprazole","Lansoprazole","Omeprazole"],"a":3,"e":"Omeprazole should not be used in a patient on warfarin therapy as it decreases the metabolism of warfarin.\n\nAll PPIs are metabolized by hepatic P450 cytochromes, including CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, and may interfere with the metabolism of the other drugs. It inhibits the metabolism of clopidogrel, diazepam, and phenytoin.\n\nNote: There is disagreement among different authors regarding the PPIs and their interactions with the metabolism of other drugs.\n\n• Goodman and Gilman mention that lansoprazole and rabeprazole along with omeprazole and esomeprazole interact with the metabolism of warfarin.\n\n• Katzung and various online sources agree that lansoprazole and rabeprazole do not or minimally interact with warfarin metabolism.\n\n• All of them agree that pantoprazole does not or minimally interacts with warfarin metabolism.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"You are counseling a patient with gastroesophageal reflux disease about the need for long-term therapy with proton-pump inhibitors. All of the following are complications of this therapy, except:","o":["Decreased vitamin B12 absorption","Increased susceptibility to infections","Hypogastrinemia","Increased risk of bone fracture"],"a":2,"e":"Long-term PPI therapy is not associated with hypogastrinemia but is associated with hypergastrinemia. This hypergastrinemia contributes to rebound gastric acid hypersecretion if PPI therapy is discontinued.\n\nChronic PPI therapy is associated with:\n\n• Decreased vitamin B12 absorption\n\n• Increased susceptibility to infections- hospital-acquired pneumonia, pseudomembranous enterocolitis\n\n• Reduced calcium absorption increases the risk of fractures","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Gynecomastia can occur as a side effect of:","o":["Aluminium hydroxide","Cimetidine","Pirenzepine","Lansoprazole"],"a":1,"e":"Gynecomastia can occur as a side effect of cimetidine. It inhibits the binding of dihydrotestosterone to androgen receptors, increases the serum prolactin level and inhibits the metabolism of estradiol by the liver. Cimetidine causes maximum hepatic inhibition.\n\nThe specific side effect of high doses of cimetidine given for a long time is gynecomastia or impotence in males and galactorrhea in females. This property is not shared by other H2 blockers.\n\nDrugs causing gynecomastia:\n\n• Alcohol, marijuana, heroin\n\n• Antiretroviral therapy\n\n• Anabolic steroids\n\n• Digoxin\n\n• Risperidone\n\n• Ketoconazole\n\n• Spironolactone\n\n• α-methyldopa\n\n• Bromocriptine\n\n• Cimetidine\n\n• Phenytoin therapy.\n\n• Androgens- testosterone and its esters (except dihydrotestosterone).","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with peptic ulcer disease was prescribed ranitidine and sucralfate to be taken together. Why is this combination incorrect?","o":["Ranitidine combines with sucralfate and forms a toxic metabolite","Sucralfate competes with ranitidine for H2 receptor","Ranitidine increases the gastric pH which reduces sucralfate efficacy","Sucralfate inhibits the absorption of ranitidine"],"a":2,"e":"Ranitidine increases the gastric pH (i.e. less acidic) which reduces sucralfate efficacy; hence the combination is incorrect.\n\nPolymerization of sucralfate occurs only at an acidic pH by crosslinking of the molecules which forms a sticky gel-like consistency. This adheres to the ulcer base predominantly and remains there for almost 6 hours, preventing the ulcer base from coming in contact with the acid.\n\nConcurrent use of H2 blockers like ranitidine increases the gastric pH and thus hinders the polymerization and reduces the efficacy of sucralfate.\n\nSucralfate should be taken at least 2 hours after the administration of any other drug.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Gastric bezoars are commonly associated with __________.","o":["Proton pump inhibitors","H2 blockers","Misoprostol","Sucralfate"],"a":3,"e":"Gastric bezoars are commonly associated with sucralfate.\n\nSucralfate produces a viscous gel that has a ‘sticky nature’. The sticky nature may be responsible for the formation of gastric bezoars, particularly in those with gastroparesis.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of milk-alkali syndrome?","o":["Alkalosis","Renal insufficiency","Hypercalcemia","Hepatotoxicity"],"a":3,"e":"Milk alkali syndrome is not characterized by hepatotoxicity.\n\nCharacteristics of the milk-alkali syndrome include:\n\n• Alkalosis\n\n• Hypercalcemia\n\n• Renal insufficiency\n\nMilk alkali syndrome used to occur in patients who were administered high doses of NaHCO3 and CaCO3 along with milk or cream for the management of peptic ulcers.\n\nThe syndrome is rare now and generally occurs with a large intake of calcium supplements along with milk.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can cause diarrhea?","o":["Sodium bicarbonate","Magnesium hydroxide","Calcium bicarbonate","Aluminium hydroxide"],"a":1,"e":"Magnesium salts have a laxative property and can cause diarrhoea.\n\nAluminium salts can cause constipation by relaxing smooth muscles.\n\nThe combination of aluminium and magnesium salts can neutralize each other’s action, thus the combination is preferred.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antacids is more likely to induce rebound acid secretion?","o":["Calcium carbonate","Magnesium hydroxide","Aluminium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"],"a":0,"e":"Calcium carbonate is an antacid that is more likely to induce rebound acid secretion.\n\nCalcium containing antacids like calcium carbonate rapidly and effectively neutralizes gastric acid. Calcium in antacids causes rebound acid secretion, hence it is given at frequent intervals to reduce the incidence of rebound acid secretion.\n\nMagnesium, aluminium and sodium-containing antacids are less likely to cause this phenomenon.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with complaints of black stools and black coated tongue as shown in the image. Which of the following drugs is implicated?","o":["Sucralfate","Rasburicase","Colloidal bismuth subsalicylate","Simethicone"],"a":2,"e":"Colloidal bismuth subsalicylate is known to cause blackening of the tongue, dentures and stools.\n\nIt combines with trace amounts of sulfur in saliva and colon to form bismuth sulfide. Bismuth sulfide is a highly insoluble black salt. The adverse effect is claimed to be temporary and harmless.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9206133a89a423db3a72bcca6e8937b.JPEG"],"t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an antifoaming agent?","o":["Sucralfate","Simethicone","Colloidal bismuth subcitrate","Rebamipide"],"a":1,"e":"Simethicone is a surfactant that decreases foaming. Due to this antifoaming action, it is included in many antacid preparations to reduce gastroesophageal reflux.\n\nOption A: Sucralfate acts as an ulcer protective agent by forming a viscous sticky polymer that binds to the ulcer base.\n\nOption D: Rebamipide is also a cytoprotective agent that acts by increasing the production of prostaglandin in gastric mucosa and by scavenging reactive oxygen species.\n\nOption C: Bismuth compounds bind to the base of the ulcer, promote mucin and bicarbonate production. They have significant antibacterial action and are part of many anti-H.pylori regimens.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of peptic ulcer underwent endoscopic evaluation. The biopsy image is shown below. Which of the following drugs is not used in treating this condition?","o":["Metronidazole","Proton pump inhibitors","Sucralfate","Bismuth compounds"],"a":2,"e":"The image shows a Warthin silver starry stained biopsy specimen, diagnostic for Helicobacter pylori. Sucralfate does not have any anti-H. pylori action and is not used in the treatment of H. pylori gastritis.\n\nOption A: Metronidazole is used in the triple, quadruple therapy, and sequential therapy regimens.\n\nOption B: Proton pump inhibitors significantly enhance the effectiveness of regimens containing amoxicillin or clarithromycin.\n\nOption D: Bismuth compounds have significant anti-bacterial action and are given in quadruple therapy.\n\nTwo or three antimicrobials are combined in regimens that range from 10-14 days. Monotherapy is not recommended due to the development of resistance.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e5c85441ff74237bf75eabd85d8128d.JPEG"],"t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presented with bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain for the past week. Pseudopolyps were noted on colonoscopy. Which of the following can be used in managing his condition?","o":["Sulfasalazine","Sulfamethoxazole","Sulfinpyrazone","Sulphadoxine"],"a":0,"e":"Bloody diarrhea and colonoscopy findings of pseudopolyps are suggestive of ulcerative colitis. The sulfa drug indicated is sulfasalazine.\n\nIt is indicated mainly in mild-moderate ulcerative colitis and as an adjunctive in severe cases. Efficacy in Crohn’s disease is less.\n\nSulfasalazine consists of 5-ASA (mesalamine or 5-amino salicylic acid) linked to sulfapyridine with an azo bond. Individually, 5-ASA and sulfapyridine would be absorbed in the upper gastrointestinal tract, the azo bond prevents such absorption. Colonic bacteria break the azo bond to liberate sulfapyridine and 5-ASA. 5-ASA is considered to be the active moiety.","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a prodrug?","o":["Sulfasalazine","Mesalamine","Balsalazide","Olsalazine"],"a":1,"e":"Mesalamine is not a prodrug. Mesalamine or 5-ASA is the active component that is responsible for the beneficial effects of inflammatory bowel disease.\n\nCleavage of the bond of prodrugs that deliver mesalamine to the colon occurs by colonic bacteria.\n\nOption A: Sulfasalazine - 5-ASA + sulfapyridine\n\nOption C: Balsalazide - 5-ASA + an inert compound\n\nOption D: Olsalazine - 5-ASA + 5-ASA","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient with Crohn's disease, refractory to steroid therapy is planned to be started on an anti-TNF alpha monoclonal antibody. Which of the following drugs does not belong in this class?","o":["Infliximab","Adalimumab","Certolizumab pegol","Vedolizumab"],"a":3,"e":"Vedolizumab is a humanized anti-alpha-4-beta-7 integrin monoclonal antibody that prevents lymphocyte recruitment to the intestinal mucosa.\n\nIt is approved for use in moderate-severe Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis.\n\nAnti-TNF alpha monoclonal antibodies indicated in IBD are:\n\n• Infliximab\n\n• Adalimumab\n\n• Certolizumab pegol","t":"Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old man came to the OPD with complaints of vomiting for which he was prescribed metoclopramide. What is the primary mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["D2 antagonism","Sensitization of muscarinic receptors on smooth muscles","5HT3 antagonism","5HT3 agonism"],"a":0,"e":"The mechanism of action of metoclopramide is primarily D2 antagonism in the chemoreceptor trigger zone.\n\nMetoclopramide has the following actions:\n\n• 5HT4 receptor agonism - prokinetic action\n\n• 5HT3 receptor antagonism - anti-emetic action at higher doses\n\n• Peripheral D2 antagonism - prokinetic action\n\n• Sensitization of muscarinic receptors on smooth muscles","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following actions is exhibited by metoclopramide?","o":["Inhibits cholinergic smooth muscle stimulation in the gastrointestinal tract","Decreases the lower esophageal sphincter pressure","Stimulates D2 receptor","Enhances gastrointestinal motility"],"a":3,"e":"Metoclopramide enhances gastrointestinal motility.\n\nAcetylcholine increases gastrointestinal motility and lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone. Dopamine which is an inhibitory neurotransmitter suppresses ACh release. This decreases gastrointestinal motility and LES tone. Therefore, D2 receptor inhibition augments ACh release and enhances GI motility and LES tone.\n\nThe action of metoclopramide is generally confined to the upper digestive tract and an increase in colonic motility is clinically insignificant.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient developed abnormal posturing and grimacing following the administration of metoclopramide. Which drug will you administer to manage this patient?","o":["Phenelzine","Promethazine","Chlorpromazine","Prochlorperazine"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of dystonia associated with D2 blocker (metoclopramide) use. Promethazine is the drug of choice.\n\nIt is a first-generation antihistaminic having significant central anticholinergic activity.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is implicated in prolonging QT interval and causing serious arrhythmias?","o":["Domperidone","Metoclopramide","Cisapride","Ondansetron"],"a":2,"e":"Cisapride is associated with prolongation of QT interval and causation of serious ventricular arrhythmias.\n\nProlongation of QT interval is due to blockade of rapid delayed rectifier potassium channels. It is banned or has restricted use in most of the countries.\n\nOndansetron is associated with asymptomatic electrocardiogram changes, including prolongation of PT and QTc intervals.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs do not act as a prokinetic agent by 5HT4 agonism?","o":["Renzapride","Metoclopramide","Domperidone","Cisapride"],"a":2,"e":"Domperidone does not have any 5HT4 agonist action. It mainly acts by D2 antagonist action.\n\n5HT4 agonism and D2 antagonism can result in prokinetic action by increasing the cholinergic activity of the gut.\n\nThe 5HT4 agonist action is shown by:\n\n• Metoclopramide\n\n• Cisapride\n\n• Mosapride\n\n• Itopride\n\n• Renzapride","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old male with unhealthy eating practices now complains of constipation. He took bisacodyl, following which he had symptomatic relief. Which group of drugs does this belong to?","o":["Bulk forming laxatives","Stool softeners","Stimulant laxatives","Chloride secretory agents"],"a":2,"e":"Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative.\n\nStimulant laxatives act by irritation of the intestinal mucosa and direct action on myenteric plexus neurons stimulating motility and secretion.\n\nExamples of stimulant/irritant laxatives are:\n\n• Bisacodyl\n\n• Sodium picosulfate\n\n• Anthraquinones like senna, cascara sagrada\n\n• Castor oil","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"You want to prescribe a bulk-forming laxative to an elderly patient with constipation. Which among the following would you not prescribe?","o":["Bran","Psyllium","Liquid paraffin","Methyl cellulose"],"a":2,"e":"Liquid paraffin is not a bulk-forming laxative. It is a mineral oil and acts as a stool softener.\n\nIt is rarely used in clinical practice today because of its drawbacks, such as:\n\n• Bland and unpleasant taste\n\n• Risk of lipoid pneumonia\n\n• Anal leakage\n\nBulk-forming laxatives are:\n\n• Bran\n\n• Psyllium\n\n• Methylcellulose\n\n• Polycarbophil","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 3-year-old child is brought to the pediatric OPD by his mother with complaints of acute watery diarrhea for 2 days. Oral rehydration solution, zinc supplement, and a peripheral enkephalinase inhibitor were prescribed. Which of the following drugs did he prescribe?","o":["Racecadotril","Diphenoxylate","Loperamide","Linaclotide"],"a":0,"e":"Racecadotril is a peripheral enkephalinase inhibitor.\n\nEnkephalins are endogenous delta-opioid receptor agonists that decrease intestinal secretions without affecting motility. Enkephalinase is the enzyme that degrades enkephalin.\n\nRacecadotril, by inhibiting the enkephalinase enzyme increases enkephalin level and acts as an antisecretory agent in diarrhea. It has minimal/no effect on motility. Racecadotril is indicated for symptomatic treatment of diarrhea in infants and children.\n\nOptions B and C: Diphenoxylate and loperamide are μ-opioid receptor agonists.\n\nOption D: Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase agonist.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old male who is on anti-retroviral therapy presents with complaints of diarrhea for three days. An infectious etiology is ruled out on work-up. Which of the following drugs is preferred to manage his diarrhea?","o":["Racecadotril","Crofelemer","Ondansetron","Cisapride"],"a":1,"e":"This is a case of non-infectious diarrhea associated with antiretroviral therapy (ART). Crofelemer is indicated for non-infectious diarrhea associated with ART in HIV/AIDS.\n\nCrofelemer has minimal systemic absorption. It acts by inhibiting the cyclic AMP-stimulated CFTR chloride channel and calcium-activated chloride channels on the luminal side of the intestine.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old woman complains of recurrent episodes of constipation and crampy abdominal pain for the last 4 months. The pain is relieved on defecation. After investigations, a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome with constipation is considered. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe?","o":["Lubiprostone","Loperamide","Alosetron","Eluxadoline"],"a":0,"e":"The drug used in irritable bowel syndrome with constipation is lubiprostone.\n\nIt is approved for chronic idiopathic constipation and constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome (in female patients >18 years). It acts by stimulating Cl-channels in the intestine and increasing fluid secretion in the gut. This decreases the transit time.\n\nEluxadoline is an FDA-approved opioid drug for the treatment of diarrhea-predominant IBS in adults.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"The use of which of the following drugs is associated with ischemic colitis?","o":["Ondansetron","Alosetron","Palonosetron","Granisetron"],"a":1,"e":"Ischemic colitis is associated with the use of alosetron. It is a 5HT3 antagonist that may suppress intestinal relaxation leading to ischemic colitis in susceptible individuals.\n\nIt is approved for resistant diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome in female patients.\n\nIt is either withdrawn/ has restricted use because of the serious side effect.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A patient who is suffering from chronic constipation is prescribed a guanylcycline C agonist. Identify the drug.","o":["Lubiprostone","Loperamide","Linaclotide","Tegaserod"],"a":2,"e":"The guanylcycline-C (GC-C) agonist indicated in chronic constipation is linaclotide.\n\nLinaclotide is a peptide agonist that activates GC-C which leads to increased synthesis of cyclic-GMP. This results in enhanced chloride and bicarbonate secretion into the intestinal lumen leading to increased water secretion and enhanced motility.\n\nIt is indicated in constipation-predominant IBS in females and in chronic idiopathic constipation in adults.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old woman suffering from erratic bowel habits for the past few months is diagnosed with diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome. Which antibiotic is specifically approved for the treatment of her condition?","o":["Rifabutin","Rifampicin","Rifaximin","Rifapentine"],"a":2,"e":"The antibiotic that is specifically approved for diarrhea-predominant IBS is rifaximin. It is an RNA synthesis inhibitor. It may provide benefit by altering the gastrointestinal flora favorably.\n\nRifabutin, rifampicin and rifapentine are antitubercular agents.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"A 20-year-old primiparous woman came to the OPD with complaints of nausea and vomiting at 8 weeks of gestation. Which of the following is specifically indicated to provide symptomatic relief in this patient?","o":["Domperidone with doxylamine","Doxylamine with pyridoxine","Dolasetron with pyridoxine","Dolasetron with metoclopramide"],"a":1,"e":"Doxylamine is indicated with pyridoxine to reduce mild to moderate nausea and vomiting in the early stages of pregnancy.\n\nIt is a histamine H1 antagonist and its efficacy is improved when combined with pyridoxine.\n\nCombinations of domperidone, dolasetron and metoclopramide with pyridoxine are not specifically indicated in pregnancy.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"What is the drug of choice in a patient complaining of nausea and vomiting, 2 days after commencement of a cisplatin-based chemotherapeutic regimen?","o":["5HT3 antagonist + NK-1 antagonist","NK-1 antagonist + 5HT3 antagonist + Corticosteroid","NK-1 antagonist + Corticosteroid","5HT3 antagonist + Corticosteroid"],"a":1,"e":"This patient is most likely suffering from delayed emesis associated with cancer chemotherapy.\n\nCombined therapy with aprepitant (NK-1/ neurokinin-1 receptor antagonist), a 5-HT3-receptor antagonist, and dexamethasone is the best treatment in delayed emesis associated with cancer chemotherapy.\n\nDelayed chemotherapy-induced vomiting is any emesis occurring >24 hours after chemotherapy. If NK-1 receptor antagonists are not used in the combination, delayed emesis is prevented only in 30% of the patients. Usage of 5HT3 antagonists alone has no role in the treatment of delayed emesis.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"An AIDS patient presents with anorexia and weight loss. Which of the following is a naturally extracted cannabinoid that is approved for his condition?","o":["Dronabinol","Nabilone","Tibolone","Cannabidiol"],"a":0,"e":"Dronabinol or delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol is a cannabinoid that can be extracted naturally.\n\nIt is the only cannabinoid that is approved for anorexia and weight loss associated with AIDS. It is obtained from the marijuana plant or can be synthesized chemically.\n\nOption B: Nabilone is a synthetic cannabinoid.\n\nDronabinol and nabilone are approved for the treatment of refractory cases of chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.\n\nOption C: Tibolone is a 19-norsteroid derivative specifically to be used for hormone replacement therapy.\n\nOption D: Cannabidiol is a cannabinoid approved for the treatment of seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, Dravet syndrome, or tuberous sclerosis complex.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about aprepitant is false?","o":["Agonist at NK1 receptor","Crosses blood brain barrier","Ameliorate nausea and vomiting induced by chemotherapy","Metabolised by the CYP450 enzymes"],"a":0,"e":"Aprepitant is a high-affinity NK1 receptor antagonist that blocks the emetic action of substance P.\n\nAprepitant is well absorbed orally and crosses the blood-brain barrier.\n\nCo-administration of oral aprepitant with dexamethasone and ondansetron significantly enhances the antiemetic efficacy against the highly emetogenic side effects of chemotherapeutic drugs like cisplatin and cyclophosphamide.\n\nIt is metabolized in the liver by the CYP450 enzyme.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antiemetic drugs also decreases acid secretion due to its action on H1 receptors?","o":["Promethazine","Domperidone","Palanosetron","Ondansetron"],"a":0,"e":"An antiemetic drug that also decreases acid secretion due to its action on H1 histamine receptors is promethazine.\n\nThe central anticholinergic activity of promethazine is responsible for antiemetic action.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"The 5HT3 antagonist having maximum potency in chemotherapy-induced vomiting is:","o":["Ondansetron","Granisetron","Cilansetron","Palonosetron"],"a":3,"e":"The 5HT3 antagonist having maximum potency in chemotherapy-induced vomiting is palonosetron.\n\nIt has a high affinity for 5HT3 and is also the longest-acting 5HT3 antagonist. This makes it more effective in delayed emesis associated with chemotherapy.\n\nDuring chemotherapy, there is cell lysis and 5HT (serotonin) is released from the gut mucosa. The released serotonin acts on the 5HT3 receptors, activating the vagal afferents in the gut that carry the emetogenic impulses to the nucleus tractus solitarius and chemoreceptor trigger zone. The selective blockade of 5HT3 receptors reduces the emetogenic impulses.\n\nGranisetron, dolasetron, and ondansetron are also effective for chemotherapy-induced vomiting.\n\nCilansetron is a new 5HT3 antagonist indicated for irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"QT interval prolongation is minimal with which of the following 5-HT3 antagonists?","o":["Ondansetron","Palonosetron","Dolasetron","Granisetron"],"a":1,"e":"Palonosetron is a second-generation 5-HT3 antagonist and has minimal/no propensity to cause QT prolongation.\n\nFirst-generation 5-HT3 antagonists have an increased risk of QT interval prolongation.\n\nIntravenous dolasetron is contraindicated for prophylaxis of chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting for the same reason.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following NK1 receptor antagonists has the longest half-life?","o":["Netupitant","Rolapitant","Aprepitant","Fosaprepitant"],"a":1,"e":"Rolapitant has the longest plasma half-life of 180 hours. It is a highly selective NK1 receptor antagonist.","t":"Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","c":"Respiratory and GI Drugs"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a cell-cycle specific agent?","o":["Ifosfamide","Melphalan","Vinblastine","Cyclophosphamide"],"a":2,"e":"Vinblastine is a cell cycle-specific agent and a vinca alkaloid. It arrests mitosis and acts specifically on M-phase.\n\nCyclophosphamide, melphalan, and ifosfamide are nitrogen mustards and are cell-cycle non-specific agents.\n\nCell cycle specific agents:\n\n• Antimetabolites: S phase\n\n• Camptothecin analogs: S Phase\n\n• Taxanes, Vinca alkaloids: M phase\n\n• Epipodophyllotoxin: G1-S phase\n\n• Bleomycin: G2-M phase\n\nCell cycle non-specific agents:\n\n• Alkylating agents\n\n• Platinum analogs\n\n• Anthracyclines\n\n• Mitomycin, dactinomycin","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an antimetabolite?","o":["Cyclosporine","Methotrexate","Etoposide","Vinblastine"],"a":1,"e":"Methotrexate is an antimetabolite. It is a folic acid analog that inhibits the enzyme dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR). Methotrexate and its polyglutamate also inhibit the enzymes involved in denovo purine and thymidylate synthesis.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is methotrexate not used?","o":["Giant cell arteritis","Psoriasis","Sickle cell anemia","Organ transplantation"],"a":2,"e":"Indications for methotrexate (inflammatory conditions) include:\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Psoriasis\n\n• Psoriatic arthritis\n\n• Ankylosing spondylitis\n\n• Polymyositis\n\n• Dermatomyositis\n\n• Wegener’s granulomatosis\n\n• Giant cell arteritis\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Other systemic vasculitis\n\n• Graft versus host disease associated with organ transplantation.\n\nSickle cell anemia appears to be the most appropriate answer among the options. Though small trials have shown positive results in sickle cell disease, it is neither extensively used nor approved for this indication.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions is high dose methotrexate indicated in?","o":["Osteosarcoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Retinoblastoma","Ewing’s sarcoma"],"a":0,"e":"High-dose methotrexate with leucovorin rescue (HDM-L) is a standard agent for adjuvant therapy of osteosarcoma. HDM is critical in the management of childhood acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL). HDM-L also produces a high response rate in CNS lymphomas.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old lady was diagnosed with gestational trophoblastic neoplasia and started on methotrexate. However the hCG levels of the patient kept rising. What could be the cause of this phenomenon?","o":["Depletion of folate","Overproduction of DHFR","Overproduction of thymidylate kinase","Decreased DHFR"],"a":1,"e":"The scenario depicts a case of Methotrexate resistance. This happens due to the overproduction of DHFR (dihydrofolate reductase).\n\nMechanism of resistance to methotrexate includes:\n\n• Impaired transport of methotrexate into cells\n\n• Production of altered forms of DHFR that have decreased affinity for methotrexate\n\n• The increased concentration of intracellular DHFR through gene amplification or gene regulation\n\n• Decreased ability to form methotrexate polyglutamates\n\n• Increased expression of drug efflux transporter","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with impaired renal function, undergoing chemotherapy, develops methotrexate toxicity. Which of the following drugs is a methotrexate cleaving enzyme that can be used to manage this condition?","o":["Adenyl cyclase","Glucarpidase","Rasburicase","Serine protease"],"a":1,"e":"Glucarpidase, which is a methotrexate cleaving enzyme, can be used in this condition. Glucarpidase is an FDA approved intravenous drug for the treatment of methotrexate toxicity in the treatment of cancer patients who have impaired kidney function.\n\nGlucarpidase is a recombinant bacterial enzyme that breaks down methotrexate into inactive metabolites rapidly after injection. These inactive metabolites are then eliminated by the liver.\n\nNote: Leucovorin is also a substrate of glucarpidase. Leucovorin is avoided 2 hours before and after the administration of glucarpidase to avoid its breakdown.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about methotrexate is false?","o":["Folinic acid enhances its action","It inhibits dihydrofolate reductase","Non-proliferative cells are resistant to it","It targets the S-phase of the cell cycle"],"a":0,"e":"Folinic acid, also known as leucovorin, the active form of folic acid, is administered with methotrexate to reduce the toxicity associated with higher doses. It does not enhance the action of methotrexate.\n\nMethotrexate is an antimetabolite that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase. It is a cell cycle (S-phase) specific antineoplastic agent and non-proliferative cells are relatively resistant to it.\n\nBone marrow suppression and gastrointestinal mucosa inflammation (mucositis) are the common side effects seen due to inhibition of DNA synthesis.\n\nMethotrexate toxicity:\n\n• Prevention - administration of leucovorin/folinic acid.\n\n• Treatment - maintaining adequate hydration and forced alkaline diuresis in patients with normal renal function.\n\n• Glucarpidase is an intravenous drug administered in patients who have impaired renal function.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient is on methotrexate therapy as part of his chemotherapy regimen. Which of the following tests is not indicated for monitoring in this patient?","o":["Liver function test","Lung function test","Fundoscopy","Hemogram"],"a":2,"e":"Fundoscopy is not monitored in a patient on methotrexate therapy. This is because no significant eye-related adverse effect is associated with either low, normal or high-dose methotrexate use, mandating frequent monitoring.\n\nOption A - Hepatic fibrosis or cirrhosis is a significant side effect of long-term therapy of methotrexate that requires regular hepatic function monitoring.\n\nOption B - Allergic interstitial pneumonitis and other pulmonary-related adverse effects are associated with methotrexate.\n\nOption D - Myelosuppression is a common and a serious adverse effect that mandates routine monitoring of hemogram.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"During rounds, one of your colleagues suggested alkalization of urine with sodium bicarbonate for a patient, to reduce the toxicity of a chemotherapeutic agent. Which is the chemotherapeutic drug being discussed here?","o":["Arabinoside-cytosine","Ifosfamide","Cisplatin","Methotrexate"],"a":3,"e":"The drug being discussed here is methotrexate. Alkalization of urine ameliorates the toxicity of methotrexate. Methotrexate is an acidic drug and the excretion is increased by alkalization of urine.\n\nHigh-dose methotrexate with leucovorin (HDM-L) used in some conditions has a higher incidence of nephrotoxicity. It is attributed to the precipitation of methotrexate in the acidic tubular fluid. Vigorous hydration and alkalinization of urine prior to HDM-L administration is required to minimize the chance of nephrotoxicity.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"What should be done to the dose of intravenous 6-mercaptopurine when it is administered in a patient taking allopurinol?","o":["Reduce by 75%","Reduce by 50%","Increase by 50%","No dose adjustment necessary"],"a":3,"e":"Intravenous dosing of 6-mercaptopurine (6MP) does not require any dosing adjustment when administered with allopurinol.\n\n6MP undergoes the first-pass metabolism by xanthine oxidase in the liver and intestine. Since allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, it causes elevated levels of 6-MP. Hence, the oral dose of 6MP needs to be reduced by 75 % if allopurinol is given.\n\nIn the case of intravenous administration, the dosage need not be changed as it escapes the first-pass metabolism.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient developed the following lesions after 10 days of initiation of chemotherapy. The patient also complains of similar lesions over soles and they are sensitive to touch. Which is the most likely agent to cause this adverse effect?","o":["Cisplatin","Vincristine","Capecitabine","Mitomycin-C"],"a":2,"e":"The given image depicts classical findings of hand-foot syndrome and capecitabine is commonly associated with this prominent side effect.\n\nHand foot syndrome or palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia consists of erythema, desquamation, pain and sensitivity to touch of the palms and soles.\n\nDrugs commonly implicated are:\n\n• Capecitabine\n\n• 5-Fluorouracil\n\n• Cytarabine\n\n• Pemetrexed\n\n• Doxorubicin","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9fe119a45cdc49f8adf56e21ec3b07a3.PNG"],"t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients is pemetrexed the drug of choice?","o":["A patient with malignant mesothelioma","A patient with small cell carcinoma","A patient with stage II non-small cell lung carcinoma","A patient with inflammatory breast cancer"],"a":0,"e":"Pemetrexed is the drug of choice for the treatment of malignant mesothelioma. It is an antifolate drug that belongs to the antimetabolite group.\n\nIt acts by inhibiting thymidylate synthase and dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) and prevents the formation of DNA and RNA, which are required for the growth and survival of both normal cells and cancer cells.\n\nPemetrexed is used for the treatment of stage IV non-small cell carcinoma along with cisplatin.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient who is undergoing chemotherapy is to be started on allopurinol. Which of these drugs might require dose reduction?","o":["6-mercaptopurine","Cyclophosphamide","6-thioguanine","Methotrexate"],"a":0,"e":"A patient receiving allopurinol requires a dose reduction of 6-mercaptopurine.\n\nAllopurinol is a potent xanthine oxidase inhibitor that is often used in acute leukemias as supportive care to prevent the development of hyperuricemia due to tumor lysis syndrome.\n\n6-mercaptopurine is converted to an inactive metabolite (6-thiouric acid) by xanthine oxidase in the liver and intestine. Therefore, the oral bioavailability of the 6-mercaptopurine is reduced significantly due to high first-pass metabolism.\n\nConcomitant allopurinol use increases 6-mercaptopurine availability which leads to toxicity, thereby mandating oral dose reduction.\n\nNote: Intravenous dosing of 6-mercaptopurine need not require any dosing adjustment when administered with allopurinol.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following enzymes is inhibited by pentostatin?","o":["Adenosine deaminase","Aldolase","Adenosine aminase","Adenylyl cyclase"],"a":0,"e":"Pentostatin mimics adenosine and inhibits the enzyme adenosine deaminase. Adenosine deaminase inhibition leads to the accumulation of adenosine and deoxyadenosine nucleotides that blocks DNA synthesis by inhibiting RNR (ribonucleotide reductase).\n\nPentostatin also inhibits RNA synthesis, its triphosphate derivative is incorporated into DNA causing strand breakage.\n\nIt is indicated in the treatment of hairy cell leukemia.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old patient presented with weakness, fatigue, and abdominal discomfort. On examination, splenomegaly was present. Investigations revealed pancytopenia, and his peripheral blood smear is shown below. Immunophenotype testing revealed CD11c, CD25, and CD103 positive. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","o":["Pentastatin","Cladribine","Fludarabine","Clofarabine"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical scenario, characteristic immunophenotype, and the presence of hairy cells on the Wright-stained peripheral blood smear point to a diagnosis of hairy cell leukaemia. Cladribine is considered the drug of choice for hairy cell leukemia.\n\nIt is administered daily by continuous infusion for 7 days. A single course of therapy achieves a complete response in about 80% of the patients.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3d647ef16074c1594341da1fd62d3af.JPEG"],"t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is a topoisomerase I inhibitor?","o":["Doxorubicin","Irinotecan","Etoposide","Vincristine"],"a":1,"e":"Irinotecan is a topoisomerase I inhibitor.\n\nDoxorubicin and etoposide inhibit topoisomerase II.\n\nVincristine is a vinca alkaloid derivative that inhibits microtubule polymerization.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a mitotic polymerization inhibitor?","o":["Vinblastine","Paclitaxel","Dactinomycin","Gemcitabine"],"a":0,"e":"Vinca alkaloids like vinblastine and vincristine bind to beta-tubulin and inhibit its polymerization with alpha-tubulin into microtubules.\n\nPaclitaxel does not inhibit polymerization, rather it stabilizes polymerization and inhibits the breakdown of microtubules.\n\nGemcitabine is an antimetabolite and a pyrimidine analog.\n\nDactinomycin or actinomycin D is an actinomycin.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient who was diagnosed with myocardial infarction underwent PCI and stenting with paclitaxel coated drug-eluting stent. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["Topoisomerase inhibition","Increases the polymerisation of tubulin","Inhibits protein synthesis","Alkylation of DNA"],"a":1,"e":"Paclitaxel binds specifically to beta-tubulin and promotes the polymerization and inhibits the disassembly of this cytoskeletal protein, resulting in the arrest of the cell cycle in M-phase.\n\nThe first generation drug eluting stents used in PCI were coated with paclitaxel or sirolimus as an anti-proliferative agent. Second-generation drug-eluting stents use newer agents such as everolimus, biolimus, and zotarolimus.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Bone marrow suppression is not seen with which of the following drugs?","o":["Vincristine","Vinblastine","Vinorelbine","Methotrexate"],"a":0,"e":"Bone marrow suppression is not seen with vincristine.\n\nVinblastine and vinorelbine have prominent bone marrow depression.\n\nBone marrow sparing cytotoxic drugs are:\n\n• Vincristine\n\n• Bleomycin\n\n• L-asparaginase","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man was diagnosed with AML and initiated on chemotherapy. During the course of his treatment, he develops ataxia and slurred speech. Which of the following drugs could have caused this?","o":["Cisplatin","Cytarabine","Bleomycin","Actinomycin D"],"a":1,"e":"This scenario describes a case of cerebellar toxicity and cytarabine is associated with this condition.\n\nIt is seen especially with intrathecal administration or high dose systemic use. Cerebellar toxicity manifests as ataxia and slurred speech. Cerebral toxicity (seizures, dementia, and coma) is also seen.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding vincristine?","o":["It is an alkaloid","Its use is associated with neurotoxicity","It does not cause alopecia","It is a useful drug for induction of remission in ALL"],"a":2,"e":"Vincristine use is associated with alopecia. It is usually reversible frequently without discontinuation of the drug.\n\nVincristine is a vinca alkaloid derivative. It is mainly indicated for induction of remission in acute leukemias including ALL. Neurotoxicity is one of the main side effects.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"In which of the following conditions can all-trans-retinoic acid be used for treatment?","o":["Acute promyelocytic leukemia","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"],"a":0,"e":"All-trans-retinoic acid (tretinoin) is used in the treatment of acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). RAR-alpha and retinoid X receptor dimer binds tretinoin tightly and causes differentiation of promyelocytes resulting in decreased proliferation.\n\nAs monotherapy, tretinoin induces a high rate of complete remission and in combination with anthracyclines cures most of the patients.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antineoplastic agents is extracted from the plant shown below?","o":["Epothilones","Camptothecin analogs","Vinca alkaloids","Taxanes"],"a":2,"e":"The image shown is of the periwinkle plant (Catharanthus roseus). Vinca alkaloids like vinblastine and vincristine are extracted from this plant.\n\nSources of few other drugs are:\n\n• Epothilones - Myxobacterium Sorangium cellulosum\n\n• Camptothecin analogs - Camptotheca acuminata tree\n\n• Taxanes - bark of the western yew tree.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8cfcd125db234466ab771d63e1ead398.JPEG"],"t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman is diagnosed with melanoma. On examination, a palpable lymph node was present in the posterior triangle. Biopsy revealed presence of tumour cells. Which of the following drugs used to treat this condition is a MEK inhibitor?","o":["Dabrafenib","Vemurafenib","Trametinib","Ipilimumab"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of metastatic melanoma and trametinib is used in this condition. It is a reversible inhibitor of mitogen-activated extracellular signal regulated kinase (MEK). It inhibits the serine-threonine kinase domain of BRAF V600E mutant of MEK.\n\nIt is widely used in the treatment of metastatic or unresectable melanoma. It can be used as a single agent but it is more effective when used with BRAF inhibitors like dabrafenib.\n\nAdverse effects of this drug include rash, diarrhea, increased ALT, abdominal pain, stomatitis, increased AST, anemia, etc.\n\nDabrafenib and vemurafenib are BRAF inhibitors used in metastatic melanoma.\n\nIpilimumab is a human cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen 4 (CTLA-4) blocking antibody used in metastatic or unresectable melanoma.\n\nRecent update: The FDA has approved the combination of the BRAF inhibitor encorafenib and the MEK inhibitor binimetinib for the treatment of patients with BRAF-mutant unresectable or metastatic melanoma.","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old woman had undergone modified radical mastectomy followed by chemotherapy with trastuzumab. She now presents with liver metastases. Which of the following drugs could be used in this condition?","o":["Lapatinib","Panitumumab","Erlotinib","Imatinib"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario depicts a case of trastuzumab-refractory breast cancer and lapatinib is used in this condition.\n\nDrug\nUse\n\nLapatinib\nRefractory and metastatic breast carcinoma\n\nPanitumumab\nColorectal carcinoma\n\nErlotinib\n\n• Non small cell lung carcinoma\n\n• Pancreatic carcinoma\n\nImatinib\n\n• Chronic myeloid leukemia\n\n• Gastrointestinal stromal tumour","t":"Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is more likely to be susceptible to alkylation with a bifunctional alkylating agent?","o":["N3 of guanine","N1 of adenosine","N3 of adenosine","N7 of guanine"],"a":3,"e":"The N7 atom of guanine is particularly susceptible to alkylation with bifunctional alkylating agents. It leads to the formation of a covalent bond with bifunctional alkylating agents and represents the key target in nucleic acids for alkylating agents. Alkylation results in cross-linking/abnormal base pairing of DNA which inhibits DNA replication.\n\nOther sites that can be involved are:\n\n• N1 and N3 of adenosine\n\n• N3 of cytosine\n\n• O6 of guanine","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"During a study on the cytotoxic effects of alkylating agents, you observe that cyclophosphamide has a higher cytotoxic effect compared to procarbazine. Which property of cyclophosphamide is the reason for this?","o":["Cross-linking of DNA","Methylation of DNA","Additional inhibition of topoisomerase I","Additional inhibition of topoisomerase II"],"a":0,"e":"Cyclophosphamide (nitrogen mustard) is a bifunctional alkylating agent and it results in cross-linking of DNA that causes a major disruption in nucleic acid function and increased cytotoxicity.\n\nProcarbazine is a monofunctional alkylating agent.\n\nDifferences between bifunctional and monofunctional alkylating agents are:\n\nBifunctional alkylating agents\n\nMonofunctional alkylating agents\n\nUsually transfers ethyl group to DNA resulting in cross-linking\nUsually transfers a methyl group resulting in simple methylation. No cross-linking of DNA\n\nHigher cytotoxicity because of cross-linking results in a greater threat to cellular survival\n\nHigher carcinogenesis risk as simple methylation can be bypassed by DNA polymerases resulting in permanent modification of DNA sequence","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not an alkylating agent?","o":["Carmustine","Melphalan","Chlorambucil","5-Flourouracil"],"a":3,"e":"5-Flourouracil is a pyrimidine analogue and is an antimetabolite.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a nitrogen mustard?","o":["Mechlorethamine","Melphalan","Cyclophosphamide","Carmustine"],"a":3,"e":"Carmustine belongs to the nitrosourea class of alkylating agents.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Temozolomide is used as a first-line agent for the management of which of the following conditions?","o":["Anaplastic astrocytoma","Glioblastoma multiforme","Acoustic neuroma","Craniopharyngioma"],"a":1,"e":"Temozolomide is used as a first-line agent for the management of glioblastoma multiforme. It is used for newly diagnosed cases along with radiotherapy and also used for maintenance therapy.\n\nTemozolomide belongs to the triazene group of alkylating agents and is administered orally. The oral bioavailability of temozolomide is nearly 100%.\n\nIn anaplastic astrocytoma, refractory cases with disease progression are treated with a regimen containing nitrosourea and procarbazine.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with astrocytoma. Which of the following drugs crosses the blood brain barrier and hence can be used for the treatment of this condition?","o":["Cisplatin","Lomustine","Vincristine","Vinblastine"],"a":1,"e":"Lomustine can cross the blood-brain barrier and is used in the management of brain tumours like astrocytoma.\n\nNitrosoureas like lomustine and carmustine are highly lipophilic drugs and can easily cross the blood-brain barrier. Hence, they are indicated in the management of brain tumors.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not an anthracycline antibiotic?","o":["Doxorubicin","Dactinomycin","Mitoxantrone","Epirubicin"],"a":1,"e":"Dactinomycin or actinomycin D is an actinomycin and not an anthracycline antibiotic.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Topical mitomycin C is used in which of the following patients?","o":["A patient with Sturge-Weber syndrome","A patient with laryngotracheal stenosis","A patient undergoing endoscopic treatment of angiofibroma","A patient with hand-foot syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Topical mitomycin C is used in laryngotracheal stenosis.\n\nMitomycin C is an antitumor antibiotic that inhibits cell division, protein synthesis and fibroblast proliferation. Topical mitomycin C has been shown to cause symptomatic improvement when applied to patients with laryngotracheal stenosis.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with superficial bladder carcinoma is being treated with a drug by intravesical therapy. Identify this drug.","o":["Actinomycin","Mitomycin","Cisplatin","Vincristine"],"a":1,"e":"Mitomycin C is used in the treatment of superficial transitional cell carcinoma by intravesical instillation. It is also used in upper gastrointestinal cancers (e.g. esophageal carcinoma), anal cancers, and breast cancers.\n\nTopical mitomycin C is used in:\n\n• Superficial bladder cancer\n\n• Intraperitoneal tumors\n\n• Strabismus surgery to reduce scarring\n\n• To prevent tracheal and esophageal stenosis\n\n• Post nasal surgery synechae formation","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient was diagnosed with acute promyelocytic leukemia and chemotherapy was initiated. The patient achieved complete remission. However, after a few months molecular relapse was detected. Which of the following drugs would be highly effective in this case?","o":["Tretinoin","Arsenic trioxide","Idarubicin","Cytarabine"],"a":1,"e":"Arsenic trioxide (ATO) is highly effective in relapsed or refractory acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). It acts by enhancing reactive oxygen free radical generation in APL cells.\n\nTretinoin and idarubicin (anthracycline) are usually first-line agents for acute promyelocytic leukemia.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with osteosarcoma was initiated on chemotherapy. After a few days, he developed dysuria and hematuria. Which group of drugs among the following is most likely to have caused this complication?","o":["Alkylating agents","Antimetabolites","Mitotic inhibitors","Topoisomerase inhibitors"],"a":0,"e":"This scenario depicts haemorrhagic cystitis and the possible cause could be alkylating agents like ifosfamide.\n\nChemotherapeutic agents used for the treatment of osteosarcoma include ifosfamide, which is one of the commonest drugs causing haemorrhagic cystitis.\n\nIfosfamide is a nitrogen mustard and belongs to the alkylating agents.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Hemorrhagic cystitis is a characteristic side effect of which of the following drugs?","o":["Melphalan","Busalfan","Cyclophosphamide","Procarbazine"],"a":2,"e":"Hemorrhagic cystitis is a characteristic side effect of cyclophosphamide. The metabolite responsible for hemorrhagic cystitis is acrolein. This can be decreased by vigorous hydration and by the use of mercapto ethane sulfonic acid (mesna).","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old woman with breast carcinoma is started on cyclophosphamide. What is the rationale behind using sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate along with this drug?","o":["Increase absorption","Decrease excretion","Ameliorate hemorrhagic cystitis","Decrease metabolism"],"a":2,"e":"Sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate (MESNA) is given along with cyclophosphamide to reduce the incidence of hemorrhagic cystitis.\n\nMESNA concentrates in the bladder where acrolein accumulates after administration of cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide. It forms a conjugate with acrolein and other urotoxic metabolites. This conjugation reaction inactivates the urotoxic compounds to harmless metabolites which are excreted in the urine.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about ifosfamide is false?","o":["Metabolised by cytochrome P450 enzymes","Less neurotoxic than cyclophosphamide","Chloroacetaldehyde is the metabolite of ifosfamide","It is a nitrogen mustard"],"a":1,"e":"Ifosfamide is more neurotoxic than cyclophosphamide. It causes greater platelet suppression, nephrotoxicity, and urothelial damage when compared to cyclophosphamide.\n\nIfosfamide is a nitrogen mustard (alkylating agent). It is metabolized by hydroxylation by cytochrome P450 enzymes. Chloroacetaldehyde is a metabolite of ifosfamide that is responsible for severe neurological adverse effects.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 67 year old woman with a malignant lump in the breast who is undergoing chemotherapy, presents with shortness of breath, tachycardia and hypertension. ECG shows ST and T wave changes. Which among the following drugs could have led to this?","o":["Doxorubicin","Mitomycin-C","Bleomycin","Actinomycin-D"],"a":0,"e":"This scenario depicts Doxorubicin induced cardiotoxicity. It is a unique adverse effect of doxorubicin. It is an anthracycline group of anticancer antibiotics.\n\nDoxorubicin-induced cardiomyopathy takes two forms:\n\n• Acute: pericarditis-myocarditis syndrome, arrhythmias, ST and T wave changes in ECG.\n\n• Chronic: dilated cardiomyopathy.\n\nNote: Dexrazoxane is a cardioprotective iron-chelating agent that is indicated for use along with high dose doxorubicin to avoid cardiotoxicity.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old man is diagnosed with Kaposi sarcoma. You are planning to start chemotherapy with doxorubicin, which can cause cardiotoxicity. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to prevent this?","o":["Leucovorin","Mesna","Dexrazoxane","Dexamethasone"],"a":2,"e":"Dexrazoxane is a cardioprotective iron-chelating agent that is indicated for use along with high dose doxorubicin to avoid cardiotoxicity. The exact cardioprotective mechanism of dexrazoxane is unknown and it appears not to compromise with anticancer activity of doxorubicin.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Least cardiotoxicity is seen with which of the following anthracycline antibiotic?","o":["Doxorubicin","Daunorubicin","Idarubicin","Epirubicin"],"a":2,"e":"Idarubicin has least cardiotoxicity when compared to other anthracyclines.\n\nIt is approved for use in acute myeloid leukemia along with cytarabine. Doxorubicin, daunorubicin and epirubicin share similar cardiotoxicity profile.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"While reviewing the drug chart of a patient undergoing radiotherapy, which of the following drugs would require a dose adjustment to prevent radiation toxicity?","o":["Vincristine","Doxorubicin","Cyclophosphamide","6-mercaptopurine"],"a":1,"e":"Doxorubicin requires dose adjustment during or after radiotherapy in order to prevent radiation toxicity.\n\nAnthracyclines like doxorubicin can result in a radiation recall reaction, where erythema and desquamation of skin are observed at sites of previous radiation injury. The risk of cardiotoxicity also increases with previous irradiation. Therefore, careful monitoring and dosage adjustments are required for doxorubicin.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient is undergoing chemotherapy for glioblastoma multiforme. You notice that his serial blood investigations reveal sustained neutropenia. Which of the following drugs could have caused this?","o":["Carmustine","Cisplatin","Vinblastine","Cyclophosphamide"],"a":0,"e":"Sustained neutropenia is seen with nitrosoureas. Nitrosoureas like carmustine and lomustine cause profound and delayed myelosuppression with recovery 4-6 weeks after a single dose.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with Hodgkin’s disease on chemotherapy with an alkylating agent develops behavioral disturbances and experiences sedation. He presented to the emergency room with a disulfiram-like reaction. What is the drug being described here?","o":["Dacarbazine","Procarbazine","Carmustine","Lomustine"],"a":1,"e":"The most likely alkylating agent being described here is procarbazine. It is associated with behavioural disturbances and sedation. Concomitant CNS depressants should be avoided.\n\nProcarbazine is a weak MAO inhibitor and can potentiate tyramine and catecholamines action. It also has disulfiram-like actions and alcohol should be avoided.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 50 year old man who presented with dysphagia was diagnosed with esophageal carcinoma and underwent multiple cycles of chemotherapy. During the cycles, he had developed tingling and numbness in the lower limbs and the symptoms progressed even after cessation of the cycles. Which drug could have led to this?","o":["Cisplatin","Paclitaxel","Mitomycin","Vincristine"],"a":0,"e":"The scenario depicts coasting effect neuropathy, which is seen with cisplatin.\n\nThe coasting effect means the progression of symptoms even after the cessation of the drug. Neurotoxicity is usually dependent on the cumulative dose of anticancer drugs. The severity of neuropathy increases with the duration of treatment and progression stops once drug treatment is completed.\n\nPlatinum compounds are an exception, where sensory loss progresses for several months after cessation of treatment. The coasting effect is more common with cisplatin and oxaliplatin than with carboplatin.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 65 year old man who was diagnosed with small cell carcinoma of the lung had undergone chemotherapy about 5 years ago. Now he presents with features suggestive of AML. Which of the following drugs could have caused this condition?","o":["Vinblastine","Paclitaxel","Cisplatin","Bleomycin"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of secondary leukemia which is associated with cisplatin. Cisplatin is a platinum-containing anticancer agent that forms DNA adduct. It is associated with the development of AML usually more than 4 years after treatment.\n\nProcarbazine, a methylhydralazine (alkylating agent), is most commonly associated with the development of leukemias with a 5-10% risk of acute leukemia in patients treated with the MOPP regimen.\n\nCisplatin produces responses in cancers of the bladder, head and neck, cervix, and endometrium; all forms of carcinoma of the lung; anal and rectal carcinomas; and neoplasms of childhood.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient who was diagnosed with gastric carcinoma is undergoing chemotherapy. His serum creatinine levels are rising and you suspect renal tubular damage caused by one of his chemotherapeutic drugs. Which drug could it be?","o":["Cisplatin","Streptozocin","Methysergide","Cyclophosphamide"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggesstive of nephrotoxicity and it is one of the dose-limiting toxicity of cisplatin therapy. Cisplatin achieves a high concentration in nephrons. Both direct toxicity and inflammation are responsible for nephrotoxicity. A high concentration of chloride stabilizes cisplatin and reduces nephrotoxicity. Therefore, chloride diuresis is used to reduce nephrotoxicity.\n\nOption B - Streptozotocin use can result in diabetes mellitus due to the destruction of beta cells of the pancreas.\n\nOption C - Methysergide use is associated with retroperitoneal fibrosis.\n\nOption D - Cyclophosphamide causes hemorrhagic cystitis as one of its characteristic side effect.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is a highly emetogenic anticancer drug?","o":["Paclitaxel","Vincristine","Cisplatin","5-Fluorouracil"],"a":2,"e":"Cisplatin is a highly emetogenic anticancer drug. Cisplatin causes marked nausea and vomiting in almost all cases and is usually administered along with anti-emetic regimens.\n\nThis can usually be controlled with 5HT3 receptor antagonists, neurokinin (NK1) receptor antagonists (e.g., aprepitant), and high-dose corticosteroids.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient who is undergoing chemotherapy for testicular cancer, now presents with dyspnea and dry cough. Investigations suggest pulmonary fibrosis. This condition is commonly associated with which of the following drugs?","o":["Bleomycin","Cisplatin","Methotrexate","Actinomycin D"],"a":0,"e":"Pulmonary fibrosis is commonly seen with bleomycin. The main abnormality in bleomycin toxicity occurs in endothelial and epithelial cells. This leads to the destruction and desquamation of type I pneumocytes and the resultant hyperplasia of type II pneumocytes.\n\nPulmonary toxicity begins with a dry cough, fine rales, and diffuse basal infiltrates on X-ray and may progress to life-threatening pulmonary fibrosis. No specific treatment is available to ameliorate the toxicity and managed by general symptomatic care.\n\nNote: Few case reports of methotrexate causing pulmonary fibrosis are reported. The association is still uncertain as the underlying disease process like RA can also contribute to fibrosis.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient who is undergoing chemotherapy, now presents with weight gain, ascites, hepatomegaly and hyperbilirubinemia. You suspect sinusoidal obstruction syndrome. Which drug is the most common cause of this condition?","o":["Ifosfamide","Cyclophosphamide","Bleomycin","Busulfan"],"a":3,"e":"Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome is most commonly associated with high-dose busulfan. Incidence increases when coadministered with CYP inhibitors.\n\nSinusoidal obstruction syndrome, previously known as veno-occlusive hepatic disease (VOD), involves primary hepatic sinusoidal injury that progresses to diffuse hepatocyte involvement and panvasculitis. This leads to multi-organ failure, and can be life-threatening.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing chemotherapy was brought to the ER with seizures. He is diagnosed with cerebrovascular accident which you suspect to be due to a drug-induced hypercoagulable state. Which of the following drugs is implicated?","o":["5-FU","L-asparaginase","Melphalan","Carmustine"],"a":1,"e":"L-asparaginase causes a hypercoagulable state. It is an enzyme and toxicity results due to the inhibition of protein synthesis in normal tissues.\n\nSide effects of L-asparaginase:\n\n• Thrombosis and increased coagulation - physiological anticlotting factors are impaired\n\n• Hemorrhage and bleeding - clotting factors are impaired\n\n• Hyperglycemia - insulin deficiency.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs cause carcinoma bladder?","o":["Cyclophosphamide","Cisplatin","Taxane","Tamoxifen"],"a":0,"e":"The only chemotherapeutic agent that has been proven to cause bladder cancer is cyclophosphamide.\n\nThe risk of the bladder cancer formation is linearly related to the duration and intensity of cyclophosphamide treatment, supporting a causative role. Phosphoramide mustard is the primary mutagenic metabolite that causes bladder cancer in patients exposed to cyclophosphamide.","t":"Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Cetuximab is approved for use in which of the following conditions?","o":["Palliation in head and neck cancer","Anal canal carcinoma","Gastric carcinoma","Lung carcinoma"],"a":0,"e":"Cetuximab is used for palliation in head and neck cancer.\n\nIndications for use of cetuximab are:\n\n• Advanced squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck in combination with radiotherapy for locally or regionally\n\n• Metastatic colorectal cancer\n\nCetuximab is a recombinant chimeric monoclonal antibody against the external domain of the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR).","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with metastatic colorectal carcinoma is being treated with panitumumab. This drug acts against which of the following receptors?","o":["PDGFR","EGFR","HER2/neu receptor","VEGF receptor"],"a":1,"e":"Panitumumab is a recombinant fully-humanized monoclonal antibody that binds specifically to the external domain of the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR).\n\nIt is approved for use in patients with metastatic colorectal carcinoma.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 57-year-old woman is diagnosed with HER 2/neu positive breast cancer. Which of the following monoclonal antibodies is indicated for the management of this patient?","o":["Panitumumab","Cetuximab","Trastuzumab","Bevacizumab"],"a":2,"e":"Trastuzumab is indicated for adjuvant therapy in early and metastatic HER2 positive breast cancer.\n\nTrastuzumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that binds to the extracellular domain of HER2/Neu.\n\nHuman epidermal growth factor receptor 2 tyrosine kinase receptor (HER-2-TK) inhibitors are:\n\n• Lapatinib\n\n• Neratinib\n\nAnti-Her-2 monoclonal antibodies are:\n\n• Trastuzumab\n\n• Pertuzumab","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 57-year-old woman diagnosed with breast carcinoma was prescribed trastuzumab. Which of the following statements regarding this drug is true?","o":["It is an antibody produced entirely from mouse containing no human component","It is a monoclonal antibody produced by injecting HER-2 antigen","It is a polyclonal antibody","It is a monoclonal antibody containing only human component"],"a":1,"e":"Trastuzumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody against the external domain of HER2/neu. In humanized antibodies, all components are identical to human antibodies (95% human component), except the complementary region of the variable chain that contains sequences from other species.\n\nIt is indicated in the management of adjuvant therapy in early and metastatic HER-2 positive breast cancer.\n\nHuman epidermal growth factor receptor 2 tyrosine kinase receptor (HER-2-TK) inhibitors are:\n\n• Lapatinib\n\n• Neratinib\n\nAnti-Her-2 monoclonal antibodies are:\n\n• Trastuzumab\n\n• Pertuzumab","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient was diagnosed with HER2/neu positive breast cancer and was started on adjuvant therapy with trastuzumab. Which of the following is the most serious and dose-limiting toxicity of this drug?","o":["Nephrotoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Ototoxicity"],"a":2,"e":"The most serious toxicity of trastuzumab is cardiac failure. Cardiac failure is a potentially disabling or fatal side effect unless recognized early and the drug is discontinued.\n\nCardiotoxicity is caused by the interruption of HER2/4 heterodimer signaling in cardiomyocytes, which is a type of signaling that is essential for the maintenance of contractile function.\n\nClinical correlation:\n\n• Baseline ECG and cardiac ejection fraction should be monitored to rule out underlying heart disease\n\n• Careful clinical follow-up for signs and symptoms of CHF\n\nOther side effects include fever, chills, nausea, dyspnea, and rashes.\n\nDose-limiting toxicities are side effects of a drug or other treatment that are serious enough to prevent an increase in dose or level of that treatment.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with wet age-related macular degeneration is being administered ranibizumab intravitreally. All of the following are known side effects of the drug, except _______.","o":["Eye pain","Fall in IOP","Conjunctival hemorrhage","Floaters"],"a":1,"e":"Ranibizumab is associated with a raise in IOP, not a fall.\n\nRanibizumab is a monoclonal antibody directed against VEGF-A (vascular endothelial growth factor-A). Ranibizumab is indicated in wet age-related macular degeneration and is used intravitreally where it inhibits VEGF-A induced ocular neovascularization.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec6b8af55d8d4214adecf78d326084bex1280x1399.JPEG"],"t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is the monoclonal antibody that is targeted specifically against VEGFR?","o":["Bevacizumab","Ranibizumab","Ramucirumab","Aflibercept"],"a":2,"e":"Ramucirumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds to VEGFR-2 and inhibits the binding of VEGFR ligands. It is indicated in NSCLC, gastric cancer and metastatic colorectal cancer.\n\nBevacizumab, aflibercept and ranibizumab are monoclonal antibodies against VEGF ligands and not the receptor.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Rituximab is not used in which of the following conditions?","o":["Non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Rheumatoid arthritis","Systemic lupus ertythematosis"],"a":1,"e":"Rituximab is used in all conditions, except paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.\n\nIndications for rituximab include: (mnemonic- CANT MIS Ritu):\n\n• CLL\n\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia\n\n• Non-Hodgkin lymphoma\n\n• TTP\n\n• Marginal zone lymphomas, mantle cell lymphoma, myasthenia gravis\n\n• ITP\n\n• SLE\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\nIt is a chimeric monoclonal antibody targeted against the CD20 receptor that is present on the surface of B lymphocytes.\n\nOfatumumab and obinutuzumab are the other monoclonal antibodies directed against CD20 receptors. They are also approved for use in CLL.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old man is found to have leukocytosis on his yearly health check-up. Further evaluation led to a diagnosis of chronic lymphocytic leukemia. Immunophenotyping was positive for CD52. Which is the monoclonal antibody indicated in this condition?","o":["Rituximab","Alemtuzumab","Cetuximab","Obinutuzumab"],"a":1,"e":"Alemtuzumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody against CD52 that is indicated in the management of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).\n\nRituximab and obinutuzumab are monoclonal antibodies against CD20 that are also indicated in the treatment of chronic lymphocytic leukemia.\n\nCetuximab is a chimeric human-mouse anti-epidermal growth factor receptor monoclonal antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following monoclonal antibodies is not a HER1 inhibitor?","o":["Pertuzumab","Cetuximab","Necitumumab","Panitumumab"],"a":0,"e":"Pertuzumab is a monoclonal antibody against the HER2 receptor.\n\nPertuzumab is indicated in HER2-positive breast cancer in combination with trastuzumab and docetaxel.\n\nMonoclonal antibodies against EGFR (HER1) are:\n\n• Panitumumab\n\n• Cetuximab\n\n• Necitumumab","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a monoclonal antibody directed against PDGFR-alpha?","o":["Panitumumab","Pertuzumab","Cetuximab","Olaratumab"],"a":3,"e":"Olaratumab is a monoclonal antibody against PDGFR-alpha and blocks ligand-mediated receptor activation. It is indicated along with doxorubicin for adult patients with soft tissue sarcoma.\n\nPanitumumab and cetuximab are monoclonal antibodies against EGFR (HER1).\n\nPertuzumab is a monoclonal antibody against HER2.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a bispecific monoclonal antibody?","o":["Daratumumab","Alemtuzumab","Blinatumomab","Panitumumab"],"a":2,"e":"Blinatumomab is a bispecific monoclonal antibody.\n\nBispecific monoclonal antibody simultaneously binds to two different types of antigen. It binds simultaneously to CD3 on T cells and to CD19 on B cells. This links the two cells and activates T cells to exert cytotoxic activity against CD19 B cells.\n\nBlinatumomab is approved for the treatment of Philadelphia chromosome-negative relapsed or refractory B-cell precursor ALL.\n\nOption A: Daratumumab is a monoclonal antibody against CD-38.\n\nOption B: Alemtuzumab is a monoclonal antibody against CD-52.\n\nOption D: Panitumumab is a monoclonal antibody against EGFR.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man who presented with fever, night sweats and weight loss was evaluated and diagnosed with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Which of the following is a radioimmunoconjugate that can be used in this condition?","o":["Gemtuzumab ozogamicin","Brentuximab vedotin","Denileukin deftitox","Ibritumomab tiuxetan"],"a":3,"e":"Ibritumomab tiuxetan is a radioimmunoconjugate that is approved for use in non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is a subtype of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.\n\nIbritumomab is a monoclonal antibody against CD20 which is attached to the radioactive isotope yttrium-90 using the chelator tiuxetan. Beta emission from Y-90 destroys the surrounding tissues.\n\nRadioimmunoconjugates provide targeted delivery of radionuclides to tumor cells. It is made by attaching a radioactive molecule to a monoclonal antibody.\n\nOption A: Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is a monoclonal antibody against CD33 covalently linked to a semisynthetic derivative of calicheamicin which is a potent antitumor antibiotic.\n\nOption B: Brentuximab vedotin is a monoclonal antibody against CD30 linked with microtubule disrupting agent MMAE.\n\nOption C: Denileukin diftitox is an immunotoxin produced by genetic recombination of IL-2 and the catalytically active fragment of diphtheria toxin.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 2-year-old girl was brought to the hospital by her mother with complaints of abdominal pain and diarrhea. Examination revealed a palpable abdominal mass. Investigations showed elevated levels of urinary homovanillic acid and vanillyl mandelic acid. The lesion could not be completely resected during surgery and histopathology findings are shown in the image. Which of the following is a monoclonal antibody approved for use in this condition?","o":["Alirocumab","Alemtuzumab","Dinutuximab","Denosumab"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario and the histopathology (image) showing small round blue tumor cells with Homer-Wright pseudorosettes, point towards a diagnosis of paediatric neuroblastoma. Dinutuximab is the monoclonal antibody against glycolipid GD2 which is indicated in this condition.\n\nGD2 glycolipid is expressed on neuroblastoma cells and on normal cells of neuroectodermal origin, including the central nervous system and peripheral nerves.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da6232ea8f694e2382067f8c07e0ef17.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/865333df5e6d4bc6944f21720fcf027cx1280x1096.JPEG"],"t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is recently approved for the treatment of colorectal cancer arising through Lynch Syndrome?","o":["Ipilimumab","Nivolumab","Pembrolizumab","Atezolimumab"],"a":2,"e":"Pembrolizumab is the drug recently approved for the treatment of colorectal cancer arising through Lynch syndrome. The increased risk for colorectal cancer in Lynch Syndrome is due to inherited mutations that impair DNA mismatch repair.\n\nImmune checkpoint inhibitors inhibit proteins that help to keep immune responses to cancer cells in check. When these proteins are blocked, the brake on the immune system is released and T cells are able to kill cancer cells more efficiently.\n\nPembrolizumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor. It is a humanized monoclonal IgG4-κ isotype antibody that blocks the interaction between programmed cell death protein (PD-1) and its ligands.\n\nThe FDA has approved pembrolizumab for patients with recurrent, locally advanced or metastatic, squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus and head and neck squamous cell cancer in combination with platin agents and 5-FU for the first-line treatment.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Pembrolizumab is not approved for use in which of the following malignancies?","o":["Non-metastatic small cell lung cancer","Metastatic squamous cell lung cancer","Refractory classical hodgkin’s lymphoma","Urothelial cancer"],"a":0,"e":"Pembrolizumab is not approved for use in non-metastatic small-cell lung cancer. It is approved for metastatic small cell lung cancer (SCLC).\n\nPembrolizumab is a monoclonal antibody (IgG4) against PD-1 and blocks interaction between PD-1 and its ligands PD-L1 and PD-L2. It is used in the following conditions:\n\n• Metastatic SCLC\n\n• Hodgkin’s lymphoma\n\n• Metastatic urothelial cancer\n\n• Metastatic non SCLC\n\n• Merkel cell carcinoma\n\n• Chemotherapy-refractory head and neck cancers\n\n• Refractory melanoma\n\n• Triple-Negative Breast Cancer\n\n• Esophageal Cancer\n\n• Colorectal cancer due to Lynch syndrome","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is the Interleukin-5 antagonist indicated in the management of asthma?","o":["Omalizumab","Lebrikizumab","Reslizumab","Atezolimumab"],"a":2,"e":"Reslizumab is a monoclonal antibody against interleukin-5 that is approved for use in severe asthma.\n\nOption A & B: Omalizumab is an anti-IgE antibody and lebrikizumab is an antibody against interleukin 13, which are also indicated in the management of asthma.\n\nOption D: Atezolizumab is an immune checkpoint modulator that inhibits the interaction of PD-L1 with PD-1 and B7-H1. It is approved for use in metastatic urothelial cancer and for NSCLC.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the monoclonal antibody developed against Bacillus anthracis?","o":["Alemtuzumab","Omalizumab","Obiltoxaximab","Abciximab"],"a":2,"e":"Obiltoxaximab is a monoclonal antibody against the protective antigen of Bacillus anthracis.\n\nIt is indicated in the treatment of inhalational anthrax due to B. anthracis in combination with appropriate antibacterial drugs. It is also indicated for prophylaxis of inhalational anthrax.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an immune checkpoint inhibitor?","o":["Alemtuzumab","Denosumab","Durvalumab","Abciximab"],"a":2,"e":"Durvalumab is an immune checkpoint inhibitor. It is an antagonist of PD-L1 which is a protein-ligand that is present on antigen-presenting cells, T cells, B cells, and some cancer cells.\n\nImmune checkpoint inhibitors inhibit proteins that help the cancer cells evade the immune response. When these proteins are blocked, the brake on the immune system is released and T cells are able to kill cancer cells more efficiently.\n\nDurvalumab is approved for use in metastatic urothelial and Merkel cell cancer. The FDA approved Durvalumab in combination with etoposide and either carboplatin or cisplatin as first-line treatment of patients with extensive-stage small-cell lung cancer.\n\nOther PD-L1 inhibitors include:\n\n• Atezolizumab\n\n• Avelumab\n\n• Durvalumab","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has inadequate disease control with methotrexate. Which Janus kinase inhibitor can you add to his treatment regimen?","o":["Ruxolitinib","Baricitinib","Dasatinib","Ponatinib"],"a":1,"e":"Baricitinib is a Janus kinase inhibitor approved for use in rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nOral Janus kinase inhibitors for rheumatoid arthritis are:\n\n• Baricitinib\n\n• Tofacitinib\n\nOption A: Ruxolitinib is a Janus kinase inhibitor approved for use in various forms of myelofibrosis, polycythemia vera and acute graft vs host disease.\n\nOptions C and D: Dasatinib and ponatinib are BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors indicated for CML.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient has presented to the OPD with raised erythematous plaques on his arms and torso covered in silvery scales. You decide to prescribe a monoclonal antibody targeting interleukin-23 for treatment. Identify this drug.","o":["Secukinumab","Ixekizumab","Brodalumab","Tildrakizumab"],"a":3,"e":"The scenario described in the question points towards a diagnosis of chronic plaque psoriasis. Tildrakizumab is a monoclonal antibody directed against IL-23 used in the treatment of this condition.\n\nIL-23 is a naturally occurring cytokine that is involved in inflammatory and immune responses. Tildrakizumab prevents the interaction of IL-23 with its receptors. It is indicated in moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis.\n\nSecukinumab, ixekizumab, brodalumab are other monoclonal antibodies directed against IL-17A and indicated in moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis.\n\nNote: Ustekinumab is a monoclonal antibody against both IL-12 and IL-23. It is used for the treatment of Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis, plaque psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Dupilumab is indicated in the management of _______.","o":["Resistant osteoporosis","Moderate atopic dermatitis","Guttate psoriasis","Severe rheumatoid arthritis"],"a":1,"e":"Dupilumab is indicated in the treatment of moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis.\n\nDupilumab inhibits interleukins IL-4 and IL-13. When these interleukins are inhibited, they are unable to form complexes with their receptors. This results in a decrease in the immune response including the release of proinflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and IgE.","t":"Monoclonal Antibodies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"What is the mechanism of action of imatinib mesylate?","o":["Increase in metabolism of P glycoprotein","Blocking the action of P glycoprotein","Blocks the action of chimeric fusion protein of BCR-ABL","Non-competitive inhibition of ATP binding site"],"a":2,"e":"Imatinib mesylate blocks the action of the chimeric fusion protein of BCR-ABL. It targets the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase and inhibits the activity of the fusion protein.\n\nA single molecular event, 9:22 translocation, leads to the expression of the Abelson proto-oncogene kinase (ABL) fused to BCR (breakpoint cluster region) yielding a constitutively activated protein kinase in chronic myelogenous leukemia. Imatinib causes remission in >90% of patients in the chronic phase of CML.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitor?","o":["Bosutinib","Dasatinib","Erlotinib","Nilotinib"],"a":2,"e":"Erlotinib is an epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) inhibitor.\n\nClassification of BCR-ABL kinase inhibitors:\n\n• First-generation: most likely to develop resistance.\n\n• Imatinib\n\n• Second generation: active against imatinib-resistant CML cases but not against T315I mutant cases.\n\n• Nilotinib\n\n• Dasatinib\n\n• Bosutinib\n\n• Third generation: active against T315I mutant CML cases and other known mutant cases.\n\n• Ponatinib","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with abdominal pain. Imaging revealed a mass in the small intestine and a lesion in the liver. A diagnosis of a c-KIT positive gastrointestinal stromal tumor with liver metastasis was made on further evaluation. What is the drug of choice?","o":["Imatinib","Bevacizumab","Denosumab","Rituximab"],"a":0,"e":"The drug of choice for the management of a patient with a metastatic gastrointestinal stromal tumor is imatinib.\n\nIt is an oral anticancer drug that acts by inhibiting tyrosine kinase activated due to BCR-ABL fusion. It also inhibits other related tyrosine kinases and hence is used in the treatment of conditions like gastrointestinal stromal tumor (driven by a c-KIT mutation).\n\nImatinib is the agent of choice for GIST patients with metastatic disease and as adjuvant therapy for c-kit positive GIST.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with chronic myeloid leukemia was switched to nilotinib as he showed only partial response to imatinib therapy. What is the mechanism resulting in the loss of response?","o":["Amplification of wild-type kinase gene","Increased efflux","Point mutations in kinase domain","Overexpression of MDR gene"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of the development of resistance to imatinib, which is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor. This condition arises due to point mutations in three separate regions of the kinase domain.\n\nThe contact points between imatinib and the enzyme become sites of mutations in drug-resistant leukemic cells and hence these mutations prevent tight binding of the drug and lock the enzyme in its open configuration (the configuration in which it has access to the substrate and is enzymatically active).\n\nMechanisms other than BCR-ABL kinase mutations play a minor role in resistance to imatinib. They include:\n\n• Amplification of the wild-type kinase gene has been identified in some cases.\n\n• Overexpression of MDR-gene leading to increased drug efflux transporter has been shown experimentally but not implicated in clinical resistance.\n\nNilotinib retains inhibitory activity in the presence of most point mutations that confer resistance to imatinib.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Imatinib is indicated in which of the following patients?\n\n1. 53-year-old with gastrointestinal stromal tumor\n\n2. 43-year-old with pyoderma gangrenosum\n\n3. 38-year-old with dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans\n\n4. 60-year-old with autoimmune hepatitis\n\n5. 35-year-old with hypereosinophilia syndrome","o":["2 and 4 only","1, 2 and 4","1 and 5 only","1, 3 and 5"],"a":3,"e":"Imatinib is the agent of choice in patients with metastatic GIST and as adjuvant therapy for c-kit positive GIST. Imatinib also shows therapeutic benefits in patients with hypereosinophilia syndrome and dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans (driven by activating mutations in the PDGFR).\n\nImatinib is an oral anticancer drug that acts by inhibiting tyrosine kinase activated due to ABL-BCR fusion. Imatinib also inhibits other kinases and hence is used in the above-mentioned conditions.\n\nIndications for imatinib:\n\n• BCR-ABL kinase mutations:\n\n• Chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML)\n\n• Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)\n\n• C-KIT mutations - gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GIST)\n\n• PDGFR mutations:\n\n• Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans\n\n• Hypereosinophilia syndrome\n\n• Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CML)","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a third generation BCR-ABL kinase inhibitor?","o":["Nilotinib","Bosutinib","Ponatinib","Dasatinib"],"a":2,"e":"Ponatinib is a third-generation BCR-ABL kinase inhibitor.\n\nClassification of BCR-ABL kinase inhibitors:\n\n• First-generation: most likely to develop resitance.\n\n• Imatinib\n\n• Second-generation: active against imatinib-resistant CML cases but not against T315I mutant cases.\n\n• Nilotinib\n\n• Dasatinib\n\n• Bosutinib\n\n• Third-generation: active against T315I mutant CML cases and other known mutant cases.\n\n• Ponatinib","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with trastuzumab-refractory breast cancer is started on lapatinib. Which of the following statements is true regarding this drug?","o":["Dual inhibitor of VEGFR and HER 2 tyrosine kinase","Dual inhibitor of MET and HER 2 tyrosine kinase","Dual inhibitor of VEGFR and MET tyrosine kinase","Dual inhibitor of EGFR and HER 2 tyrosine kinase"],"a":3,"e":"Lapatinib is a dual inhibitor of epidermal growth factor (EGFR) and human epidermal growth factor 2 (HER2) tyrosine kinase.\n\nLapatinib in combination with capecitabine is approved for the treatment of patients with metastatic HER2-positive and trastuzumab-refractory breast cancer.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is an irreversible inhibitor of EGFR tyrosine kinase?","o":["Gefitinib","Erlotinib","Trametinib","Afatinib"],"a":3,"e":"Afatinib binds covalently with epidermal growth factor receptor tyrosine kinase (EGFR-TK) also known as Her-1 receptor and inhibits it irreversibly. It is approved for the use of metastatic non-small cell lung cancer.\n\nGefitinib and erlotinib compete with ATP binding to EGFR and results in competitive inhibition, which is reversible.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e5de2d60b9f4b5cb196af855a64327fx1280x1399.JPEG"],"t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing treatment for colon cancer develops hypertension. Which of the following drugs is most commonly implicated?","o":["Trastuzumab","Bevacizumab","Panitumumab","Cetuximab"],"a":1,"e":"Hypertension is one of the characteristic side effects of angiogenesis inhibitors like bevacizumab.\n\nThe mechanism is not clear but may be related to decreased endothelial nitric oxide production. Patients receiving higher doses and prolonged treatment might require antihypertensive medications.\n\nBevacizumab can be used in the treatment of metastatic colon cancer, in combination with FOLFOX or FOLFIRI regimen. It can also be used in the treatment of non-small cell lung cancer.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient with uncontrolled diabetes is diagnosed to have diabetic macular edema. Which of the following anti-VEGF therapies is effective in the treatment of this condition?","o":["Bevacizumab","Ranibuzumab","Aflibercept","Ramucirumab"],"a":2,"e":"Aflibercept is more effective in diabetic macular edema in patients with worse baseline values of visual impairment when compared to bevacizumab and ranibizumab.\n\nAll of them are administered intravitreally. Clinical trials have shown the advantage of aflibercept over bevacizumab and ranibizumab in visual and anatomic terms at the end of one year.\n\nRamucirumab which is a monoclonal antibody against VEGFR is not indicated for diabetic macular edema.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient is referred to your center for the management of hepatocellular carcinoma. Imaging reveals a large lesion in the liver with multiple intrahepatic metastases. The tumor is found to be unresectable. Which of the following is considered as first-line treatment for this patient?","o":["Sunitinib","Sorafenib","Regorafenib","Thalidomide"],"a":1,"e":"Sorafenib is indicated as a first-line treatment for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma. It is an angiogenesis inhibitor.\n\nHepatocellular carcinoma management:\n\n• Resection, ablation, and other physical measures are the mainstay.\n\n• Only two drugs (angiogenesis inhibitors) are approved in this condition:\n\n• Sorafenib (first-line)\n\n• Regorafenib (second-line).\n\nSunitinib and thalidomide are not indicated in hepatocellular carcinoma.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Thalidomide should not be used in which of the following conditions?","o":["HIV-associated peripheral neuropathy","HIV-associated aphthous ulcers","Behcet syndrome","Erythema nodosum leprosum"],"a":0,"e":"Thalidomide use is associated with peripheral neuropathy and is usually avoided in HIV-associated peripheral neuropathy as it can worsen the condition.\n\nIt is being investigated as an orphan drug for:\n\n• Recurrent aphthous ulcers\n\n• GVHD\n\n• Behcet syndrome\n\n• primary brain malignancies\n\n• Kaposi's sarcoma\n\n• Crohn’s disease.\n\nIt is approved for use in multiple myeloma and erythema nodosum leprosum.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Use of lenalidomide over thalidomide would lead to the reduction of incidence of the following side effects, except ___________.","o":["Constipation","Sedation","Neuropathy","Bone marrow suppression"],"a":3,"e":"Lenalidomide, which is a congener of thalidomide, suppresses bone marrow and is associated with significant leukopenia.\n\nLenalidomide use is associated with a lesser incidence of sedation, constipation, and neuropathy when compared to thalidomide.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a proteasome inhibitor?","o":["Paclitaxel","Bortezomib","Cetuximab","Irinotecan"],"a":1,"e":"Bortezomib is a proteasome inhibitor.\n\nIt is a boron-containing anticancer drug that covalently binds to the proteasome and inhibits its proteolytic activity, thereby disrupting many intracellular signaling pathways. The most important of these is nuclear factor-κB (NF-κB) mediated signaling. It is indicated in the management of multiple myeloma.\n\nOption A: Paclitaxel is a taxane.\n\nOption C: Cetuximab is a monoclonal antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR.\n\nOption D: Irinotecan is a topoisomerase I inhibitor.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can be used for the treatment of a patient with hormone-responsive breast cancer?","o":["Adriamycin","Clomiphene citrate","Diethylstilbestrol","Tamoxifen"],"a":3,"e":"Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator that is indicated for hormone-responsive breast cancer.\n\nIt is approved for endocrine treatment of women with ER+ metastatic breast cancer or following primary tumor excision as adjuvant therapy.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing adjuvant radiotherapy for an oral malignancy is prescribed a radioprotective drug. Identify this drug.","o":["Cetuximab","Amifostine","Cisplatin","Gemcitabine"],"a":1,"e":"Amifostine is the radioprotective drug that has been prescribed to this patient.\n\nIt is an organic thiophosphate cytoprotective agent that is indicated for the following conditions:\n\n• Reduction of nephrotoxicity associated with cisplatin.\n\n• Reduction of xerostomia in patients with head and neck cancer undergoing postoperative irradiation.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is a cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) 4/6 inhibitor?","o":["Ruxolitinib","Palbociclib","Cobimetinib","Trametinib"],"a":1,"e":"Palbociclib is a cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) 4/6 inhibitor. It is approved for use in advanced or metastatic ER-positive HER2-negative breast cancer.\n\nRuxolitinib is an inhibitor of Janus associated kinases: JAK1 and JAK2.\n\nTramitenib and cobimetinib are inhibitors of serine-threonine kinases.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following inhibits V600E mutated BRAF?","o":["Vemurafenib","Dabrafenib","Both A and B","Ibrutinib"],"a":2,"e":"Vemurafenib and dabrafenib inhibit V600E mutated BRAF present in melanoma.\n\nBoth are indicated in the management of melanoma.\n\nThe name vemurafenib is derived from the target V600E mutated BRAF.\n\nIbrutinib is a Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is a hedgehog pathway inhibitor?","o":["Vemurafenib","Vismodegib","Olaparib","Neratinib"],"a":1,"e":"Vismodegib acts as a competitive antagonist of the SMO (smoothens hedgehog) receptor in the hedgehog signaling pathway.\n\nHedgehog signaling plays a key role in stem cell regulation and tissue regeneration. Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is associated with dysregulation of this pathway. Vismodegib is indicated in patients with metastatic or relapsed BCC.\n\nOption A: Vemurafenib is a BRAF kinase inhibitor.\n\nOption C: Olaparib is an inhibitor of PARP [Poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase] enzyme.\n\nOption D: Neratinib is an irreversible inhibitor of HER-2 and EGFR.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Evaluation of a patient with CLL showed evidence of 17p deletion. Which of the following is a BCL-2 inhibitor approved for use in this condition?","o":["Olaparib","Rucaparib","Venetoclax","Vismodegib"],"a":2,"e":"Venetoclax is a small-molecule inhibitor of BCL-2 that is approved for use in CLL with a 17p deletion.\n\nOverexpression of BCL-2 has been demonstrated in CLL cells where it mediates tumor cell survival. Venetoclax helps restore the process of apoptosis by binding directly to the BCL-2 protein, displacing proapoptotic proteins like BIM, triggering mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization and the activation of caspases.\n\nOlaparib and rucaparib are inhibitors of PARP [Poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase] enzymes.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Sipuleucel-T is the first vaccine approved for the treatment of cancer. This vaccine induces an immune response against which of the following?","o":["HER2 receptors","Prostatic acid phosphatase","Vascular endothelial growth factor","Vascular endothelial growth factor receptor"],"a":1,"e":"Sipuleucel-T is a cell-based approach to induce an immune response against prostatic acid phosphatase (PAP) that is commonly expressed in prostatic cancer. It is approved for use in minimally symptomatic, hormone-refractory, metastatic prostate cancer.\n\nSipuleucel-T preparation is done by isolating a patient's peripheral blood cells by leukapheresis and then exposing them to PAP-GM-CSF (which is a human recombinant protein). When these treated cells are infused to a patient, the APCs present in the blood produces antibodies directed against PAP.\n\nProstate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) is a specific prostate epithelial cell membrane antigen, showing increased expression in prostate cancers, which is also a potential target for monoclonal antibody therapy.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a histone deacetylase inhibitor?","o":["Vorinostat","Romidepsin","Panabinostat","Febuxostat"],"a":3,"e":"Febuxostat is a uric acid synthesis inhibitor that acts by inhibiting the enzyme xanthine oxidase and it is indicated in gout management.\n\nExamples of histone deacetylase inhibitors are:\n\n• Vorinostat - cutaneous T cell lymphoma\n\n• Belinostat - peripheral T cell lymphoma\n\n• Romidepsin - cutaneous/peripheral T cell lymphoma\n\n• Panobinostat - multiple myeloma","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman was diagnosed with melanoma. Sentinel lymph node biopsy showed malignant cells. Which of the following drugs used in this condition is a MEK inhibitor?","o":["Dabrafenib","Vemurafenib","Trametinib","Ipilimumab"],"a":2,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of metastatic melanoma and trametinib is used in this condition. It is a reversible inhibitor of the mitogen-activated extracellular signal-regulated kinase (MEK). It inhibits the serine-threonine kinase domain of BRAF V600E mutant of MEK.\n\nIt can be used as a single agent but it is more effective when used with BRAF inhibitors like dabrafenib.\n\nAdverse effects of this drug include:\n\n• Cutaneous rash\n\n• Acneiform dermatitis\n\n• Diarrhea\n\n• Fatigue\n\n• Nausea\n\n• Lymphedema.\n\n• Cardiomyopathy\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Hemorrhage\n\n• Interstitial lung disease\n\n• Ocular toxic effects.\n\nOptions A and B: Dabrafenib and vemurafenib are BRAF inhibitors used in metastatic melanoma.\n\nOption D: Ipilimumab is a human cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen 4 (CTLA-4) blocking antibody used in metastatic or unresectable melanoma.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 4-year-old child who was being evaluated for pancytopenia is diagnosed with acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Which of the following is the first gene therapy-based treatment approved for use in this condition?","o":["Trastuzumab","Tisagenlecleucel","Riociguat","Dinutuximab"],"a":1,"e":"Tisagenlecleucel is the first gene therapy-based treatment that is approved for use in acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).\n\nThe therapy involves reprogramming a patient’s own T-cells with a transgene encoding a chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) to identify and eliminate CD19-expressing malignant and normal cells.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 67-year-old man presented with fatigue and weight loss. On examination, pallor and splenomegaly were noted. Peripheral blood smear examination findings are shown in the image. Which of the following BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors is not a first-line agent for this condition?","o":["Imatinib","Dasatinib","Nilotinib","Bosutinib"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario and the peripheral smear findings (increased WBCs with myelocytes, metamyelocytes, and band forms), is suggestive of chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML). Bosutinib is approved for use in this condition only for resistant and/or intolerant cases and not in newly diagnosed cases.\n\nAgents that can be used as first-line for CML are:\n\n• Imatinib\n\n• Dasatinib\n\n• Nilotinib\n\nDasatinib and nilotinib can be used in newly diagnosed cases of CML, that are found to be imatinib-resistant.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7470297a361d4f2f98615f96cafa7386.JPEG"],"t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 63-year-old postmenopausal woman came to the OPD with a 12 cm adnexal mass. Her CA-125 levels were raised and her risk of malignancy index was calculated to be 250. PET scan revealed hepatic metastases. Which PARP inhibitor can you use in her treatment?","o":["Palbociclib","Rucaparib","Vorinostat","Romidepsin"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical scenario points towards a diagnosis of metastatic ovarian cancer. Rucaparib is a poly ADP-ribose polymerase/ PARP inhibitor approved for the treatment of advanced ovarian cancer.\n\nPARP is a nuclear protein that transfers ADP-ribose from NAD+ to target proteins and this plays an important role in the DNA damage response. Rucaparib and olaparib are PARP inhibitors.\n\nOption A: Palbociclib is a selective inhibitor of the cyclin-dependent kinases CDK4 and CDK6. It is used in the treatment of metastatic breast cancer.\n\nOptions C & D: Vorinostat and romidepsin are histone deacetylase inhibitors.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions has hydrogen peroxide been recently approved for?","o":["Psoriasis","Rosacea","Atopic eczema","Seborrheic keratoses"],"a":3,"e":"A topical solution of hydrogen peroxide has been approved for the treatment of raised seborrheic keratoses.\n\nHydrogen peroxide is a keratolytic agent. Its exact mechanism of action in the treatment of seborrheic keratoses is not fully understood. Supraphysiologic concentrations may cause oxidative damage and death to seborrheic keratosis cells.\n\nHydrogen peroxide is commonly used as a disinfectant to clean wounds. It can also be used as a mouthwash.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is apalutamide approved for?","o":["Breast cancer","Colon cancer","Prostate cancer","Gastric cancer"],"a":2,"e":"Apalutamide is an androgen receptor inhibitor indicated for the treatment of patients with non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman was diagnosed with melanoma. Sentinel lymph node biopsy showed malignant cells. Which among the following is a recombinant interleukin-2 preparation approved for use in this condition?","o":["Denileukin diftitox","Aldesleukin","Imatinib","Nilotinib"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical scenario is suggestive of metastatic melanoma. The recombinant interleukin-2 (IL-2) preparation that is approved for use in metastatic melanoma is aldesleukin. It acts as an IL-2 agonist and is also approved for use in metastatic renal cell cancer.\n\nDenileukin diftitox is an immunotoxin made from the recombinant combination of IL-2 and the catalytically active fragment of diphtheria toxin. It is approved for use in cutaneous T cell lymphoma.\n\nImatinib and nilotinib are a BCR/ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitor.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"},
 {"q":"Denileukin diftitox, a drug approved for the treatment of recurrent/refractory cutaneous T-cell lymphomas, is an immunotoxin made of:","o":["Fusion protein of IL-2 and pertussis toxin","Fusion protein of IL-2 and diptheria toxin","Monoclonal antibody against CD33","Monoclonal antibody against CD30"],"a":1,"e":"Denileukin diftitox is an immunotoxin made from the genetic recombination of IL-2 and the catalytically active fragment of diphtheria toxin.\n\nIt is indicated for advanced cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in patients with >20% of T cells expressing the surface marker CD25. After binding to the IL-2 receptors, it is internalized by endocytosis. The active fragment of diphtheria toxin then is released into the cytosol, where it inhibits protein synthesis through ADP ribosylation, leading to cell death.\n\nImmunotoxins are proteins that contain a toxin along with an antibody that binds specifically to target cells.\n\nAntibody-drug conjugates are complex molecules composed of an antibody linked to a biologically active cytotoxic (anticancer) drug. These antibody-targeted drug therapies are cell-specific to kill efficiently the target cells that bind and internalize the drug conjugate. Examples are:\n\n• Gemtuzumab ozogamicin - Monoclonal antibody against CD33 linked to a semisynthetic derivative of calicheamicin, a potent antitumor antibiotic. It was approved for the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia.\n\n• Brentuximab vedotin - Anti-CD30 IgG1 monoclonal antibody linked with the microtubule-disrupting agent MMAE. It is used for the treatment of Hodgkin and anaplastic lymphoma.\n\n• Ado-trastuzumab emtansine - It combines the HER2-targeted properties of trastuzumab with the antimicrotubule agent DM1 (derived from maytansine), allowing preferential intracellular drug delivery to HER2+ cells. It is used in the treatment of HER+ breast cancer.","t":"Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","c":"Cancer Drugs and Immunomodulators"}
];
